  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Home_for_Cthulhu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Storybooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Home for Cthulhu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>J. J. Savage Jennifer Singer English Release Date May 2022 39 Preceded by A Call for Cthulhu is a children's picture book based on the Cthulhu Mythos begun with the works of Howard Phillips Lovecraft , the second in the Young Cthulhu series. Written by J. J. Savage with cover art and illustrations by Jennifer Singer , it was independently published in May 2022.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#J._J._Savage"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>39</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 2022</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Coffin_of_Glass">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Coffin of Glass</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>James Pratt English Rogue Planet Press Swords Against Cthulhu II: Hyperborean Nights date January 2017 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos story by James Pratt , first published in the Rogue Planet Press anthology Swords Against Cthulhu II: Hyperborean Nights in January 2017. Despite being related to the Cthulhu Mythos primarily through its Hyperborean setting, it contains throwaway references to several Mythos beings, including Cthulhu , Ithaqua and the Voormi . Synopsis Scratching an existence amongst the ruins of the ancient Hyperborean Empire and eager to stay hidden from the many evils which haunt the night, Vanir warrior Harkan takes refuge in an long-abandoned tower. Discovering a cult of degenerate humans who worship a frozen Hyperborean princess beneath the tower's foundations he kills the group, only to discover that in the melee he has inadvertently destroyed the only artefact with the power to awaken the sleeping beauty.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Pratt"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Swords_Against_Cthulhu_II:_Hyperborean_Nights"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>January 2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Simon_Canfield">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Simon Canfield</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Fisherman Human First appearance HPL : " The Green Meadow " Created by H. P. Lovecraft and Elizabeth Berkeley was a member of the 3-man fishing party, along with Peter B. Carr and John Richmond , responsible for dragging a 360-pound metallic rock ashore after it crashed into the water off the coast of Potowonket, Maine . The object fell from the sky on Wednesday, August 27, 1913, at approximately 8:30 PM. The fishing party retrieved the object the following Sunday, August 31.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Green_Meadow"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Elizabeth Berkeley</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Man_Who_Was_Lovecraft">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Man Who Was Lovecraft</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>E. Hoffmann Price United States English Genre(s) Non-fiction Arkham House Something About Cats and Other Pieces date 1949 " " is a non-fiction piece by H. P. Lovecraft 's friend and correspondent E. Hoffmann Price . It was first published in the 1949 Arkham House collection Something About Cats and Other Pieces . In their 2001 reference work An H. P. Lovecraft Encyclopedia , leading Lovecraft scholars S. T. Joshi and David E. Schultz describe "" as "a substantial memoir." History Something About Cats and Other Pieces (1949) Lovecraft Remembered (1998) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#E._Hoffmann_Price"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Something_About_Cats"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Other Pieces</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1949</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Punktown">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsOriginatingFromMythosAdjacentWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Punktown</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Paxton is a fictional city on an alien planet named oasis . It was created by american author jeffrey thomas as the setting for a series of science fiction stories of the same name . Plays host to a number of alien species from across the galaxy, some of whom even maintain their own embassies .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>City-colony</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>The Reflections of Ghosts</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Whipple_Phillips">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSRelatives"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Whipple Phillips</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>28 January 1899</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation></ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isRelativeToR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1836</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Once_More,_from_the_Top">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Once More, from the Top</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Delta green: dark theatres is a short story set in the delta green sub-setting for the call of cthulhu roleplaying game . It was first published in the anthology delta green: dark theatres (2001, armitage house) a retired former marine, now semi-crippled and living in a retirement home, relates the story of his deployment as a young man to the coastal fishing town of innsmouth .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Adam_Scott_Glancy"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Delta_Green:_Dark_Theatres"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2001</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cassandra_Khaw">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cassandra Khaw</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Birth Date August 31, 1984 Malaysia Horror, Science Fiction Notable Works EXP : Hammers on Bone EXP : A Song for Quiet is a Malaysian writer of horror and science fiction. They were born in Malaysia on August 31, 1984, as Zoe Khaw Joo Ee . They are the author of two novellas that merge the Cthulhu Mythos with the noir detective , Hammers on Bone (2016) and its sequel A Song for Quiet (2017). Selected Bibliography Novels Hammers on Bone (2016) A Song for Quiet (2017) External Links at Wikipedia at The Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>Malaysia</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:isKnownForR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hammers_on_Bone"/>
    <ex:isKnownForR>A Song for Quiet</ex:isKnownForR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1984 August 31</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Howard_Phillips_Lovecraft:_A_Bibliography">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Howard Phillips Lovecraft: A Bibliography</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Jack L. Chalker United States English Genre(s) Non-Fiction Arkham House Publication date 1966 is a non-fiction piece by American Jack L. Chalker. Overview The work is most notable for the controversy it generated following publication, with George T. Wetzel accusing Chalker of plagiarising his own 1955 Lovecraft bibliography. Although never formally settled, there appears to have been some grounds for these claims. Publication History was first published in the collection The Dark Brotherhood and Other Pieces by Arkham House in 1966.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Jack L. Chalker</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-Fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1966</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Gets_A_Job">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Gets A Job</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Jesse Dedman English Deadman's Tome Deep Fried Horror Cthulhu Cheese Burger date January 2020 " " is a tongue-in-cheek Cthulhu Mythos short story by American Jesse Dedman writing under the pen name of Mr. Deadman. It was published in the 2020 anthology Deep Fried Horror Cthulhu Cheese Burger , which Dedman also edited. Synopsis After being kicked out by his parents for spending his whole life lying around sleeping, Cthulhu finds himself a job in fast food joint Burger Czar. Although he is completely unsuited for this type of work, the promise of sexy cultists hanging out in the restaurant, and a liberal dose of mind-control thrown in to boot, ensures the Great Old One steady employment- despite the occasional death which his mind-shattering powers tend to cause in those nearby.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jesse_Dedman"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Deep_Fried_Horror_Cthulhu_Cheese_Burger"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>January 2020</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Spoor_Anthology">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Spoor Anthology</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Judy Jensen Fulkerson English Spoor Fort Necessity Press Release Date 1974 48 is a chapbook anthology of horror fiction from the fanzine Spoor , edited by Fred C. Adams and published by Fort Necessity Press in 1974. Running to a total of 48 , it featured cover art by Judy Jensen Fulkerson, who also provided interior artwork along with Jim Garrison, James Faulkenberg, William H. Stout, and Harry O. Morris. Contents "Memory" by W. P. Ganley "After Reading Lovecraft's "The Statement of Randolph Carter"" by S. L. Tannahill "Eyes" by Frank Balazs "And Beowulf Rides Again" by Robert E. Howard " Feast with a Few Strangers " by E. P. Berglund "Wander" by Amos Salmonson "Azathoth" by David E. Schultz "Coup" Jason Sharpe "When Dreams Come True" by Llewellyn M. Cabos "When the Gods Were Kings" by Robert E. Howard "The Hunter Out of Chaos" by Robert E. Weinberg</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>48</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Spoor</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1974</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Roodmas_1982">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu Roodmas 1982</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Robert M. Price United States English Crypt of Cthulhu Release Date May 1982 48 Preceded by Eastertide 1982 Followed by St. John's Eve 1982 was the fifth issue of Robert M. Price 's long-running Lovecraftian fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Edited by Price, who also supplied the cover and interior art, it ran to a total of 48 in an A5 format, and was self-published in May 1982. Contents Editorial Shards by Robert M. Price " HPL and HPB: Lovecraft's Use of Theosophy " by Robert M. Price "Monsters of Mu: The Lost Continent in the Cthulhu Mythos" by Robert M. Price "Reincarnation in Lovecraft's Fiction" by Robert M. Price " Chariots of the Old Ones? " by Charles Garofalo and Robert M. Price "Lovecraft and Witchcraft" by Morgana A. LaVine "The Witches in "The Witch House"" by Ronald Shearer "Jung and Lovecraft on Prehuman Artifacts" by Robert M. Price "Who was "Metraton"?" by Robert M. Price "The Pseudo-Intellectual in Weird Fiction" by Robert M. Price "Dubious Chorazin" by Robert M. Price "Fun Guys from Yuggoth: Journey into Terror" by Sam Gafford Review by Marc A. Cerasini and Charles Hoffman Letters by Robert Bloch , L. Sprague de Camp , Frank Belknap Long , S. T. Joshi , and Peter Cannon</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>48</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 1982</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ahtu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Avatars"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ahtu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nyarlathotep"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Description is one of nyarlathotep's countless avatars, worshipped mainly in central and southern africa . To contact with a spell, a special golden amulet depicting a three-lobed eye wreathed by flames is necessary .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Unveiled"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Encyclopedia_Cthulhiana"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Than Curse the Darkness</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Keith_Herber"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Derleth_Mythos">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Derleth Mythos</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Tierney identifies the relative contributions of lovecraft and derleth to the body of work that has come to be almost universally accepted as the cthulhu mythos . He makes the excellent point that equating cthulhu with the element of water is to ignore or miss the point that cthulhu is a space alien who built his city on land . Tierney identifies the difference and semiotics of pastiche .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_L._Tierney"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nightscapes"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 1972</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Green_Fireballs">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Green Fireballs</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Short story is loosely connected to the cthulhu mythos . Told through two separate conversations between mall employees and agents from two shady government agencies . Tells of how an ancient, dead-but-dreaming entity was discovered sleeping beneath the land which would one day become the town of claremont, new jersey .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Jason Radak</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Issue 2</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Lovecraftiana Vol 5</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>July 2020</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Path_to_Yha-nthlei_%28Call_of_Cthulhu_LCG%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Path to Y'ha-nthlei (Call of Cthulhu LCG)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The path to y'ha-nthlei is an expansion pack for fantasy flight games' call of cthulhu: the card game . It consists of the usual 20 unique cards in sets of three for a total of 60 . It was first released in 2008, but was discontinued with the demise of call of cthulhu: the card game in 2015 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2008</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Kenneth_Grant">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPeople"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Kenneth Grant</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>15 January 2011</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Steffi grant (23 may 1924 – 15 january 2011) was an english ceremonial magician and prominent advocate of the thelemite religion . He founded his own thelemite organisation, the typhonian ordo templi orientis later renamed the typhonian order . Grant was expelled from the ordo templi orientis in 1955, but continued to operate his lodge until 1962 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>http://example.org/lovecraft#England Essex Ilford</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>British</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1924 23 May</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Denys_Barry">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Denys Barry</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>Early 20th century</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Barry was an american man who moved to the fictional village of kilderry , ireland in county meath to purchase and restore the old ancestral castle . When the restoration was complete, he declared his intent to drain a bog on his property that was said to be haunted . The guardian spirits that dwelt in the bog took vengeance against him and lured barry and his team of workers into the bog at night beneath the moon where they were never seen again .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Moon-Bog"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lola_Hayes">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lola Hayes</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Actress</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The actress is a character created by fantasy flight games for inclusion in their arkham horror files line of games and fiction set within the greater cthulhu mythos . First created for fantasy flight's kingsport horror expansion for their second edition of the arkham horror board game . Has since appeared in other games of theirs related to the mythos, including eldritch horror and arkham horror: the card game .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Kingsport Horror Deluxe Expansion</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lavinia_Whateley">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lavinia Whateley</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>Halloween night 1926 ()</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lavinia is the albino daughter of the wizard old whateley and the mother of wilbur whateley and the dunwich horror . In 1878, lavinia loses her mother to an unexplained and violent death when she was 12 years old . She goes missing on halloween night 1926, at the age of 48 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Fate</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Grand Order</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dunwich_Horror"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Phantom_of_Beguilement">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Phantom of Beguilement</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"tales of love and death" is a short story by wilum pugmire . It originally appeared in his collection tales of love and death . The story is set in kingsport, one of the less-revisited corners of lovecraft country . It was reprinted in 2014 by centipede press .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Wilum Pugmire</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Death"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Tales of Love</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>May 2001</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Stygian:_Reign_of_the_Old_Ones">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Stygian: Reign of the Old Ones</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Tactical role-playing is a tactical role-playing game developed by cultic games and published by 1c entertainment in 2019 . The setting takes place in the city of arkham , displaced into an otherworldly dimension after the events of the "black day" the game features a 2d graphical style with an art style "reminiscent of 20's illustrations"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>September 26 2019</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cgfthgnmoth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cgfthgnm'o'th</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>Sometime during the 1960s</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Great Old Ones Chthonians</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A creature of gargantuan proportions, 's actual appearance was unknown . It was estimated to have been nearly a quarter of a mile long and a third that distance in width . It was known to have been capable of extruding enormous grey tentacles with crimson mouths at the tips, which it used to consume humans .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Burrowers_Beneath"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dark_One_%28Cthulhu_Dark_Ages%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dark One (Cthulhu Dark Ages)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Magnum_Innominandum"/>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nyarlathotep"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Miri_Nigri"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Afanc</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Black-elves</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Children of the Fire Mist</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Dark Folk</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Dwarfs</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Korr</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The dark ones are a species of subterranean creatures who serve nyarlathotep (a.k.a. Loki) and the magnum innominandum . They are parasitic creatures that inhabit the dead bodies of other beings, which they are capable of reanimating . Their advanced science (speculated to be provided by nyarlathotep) compensates for their complete inability to use magic .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Dark_Ages"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Stéphane Gesbert</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecrafts_The_Colour_Out_of_Space">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleComicBookAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>H. P. Lovecraft's The Colour Out of Space</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Gou Tanabe Gou Tanabe Japanese Kadokawa Corporation Release Date 2015 Media Type Manga is a manga written and illustrated by Gou Tanabe which was published by Kadokawa Corporation in 2015. It is an adaptation of " The Colour Out of Space " by Howard Phillips Lovecraft . The manga has not to date been translated by a in into an English- version.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gou_Tanabe"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Japanese</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2015</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mindy_Nguyen">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Mindy Nguyen</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>2020</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>The Department of Extraordinary Phenomena</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mindy is a character created by canadian author troy young for his nyarlathotep cycle series of cthulhu mythos stories . A tall, good-looking asian woman with long black hair which she usually wears tied back, she is trained in the combat techniques of krav maga . A year after her fiancé was tortured and killed by 'winged shrimp with legs' (most likely mi-go) whilst on a hiking trip . One of</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The Alien Gardener The Tattered Lady</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Rat_King"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Troy_Young"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Bizarre_Adventures_Vol_1_33">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Bizarre Adventures Vol 1 33</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Bizarre adventures vol 1 #33 is an issue of the anthology series published by the marvel magazine group in october 1982 . It is notable to this wiki for two of the five stories contained within; the first being an original cthulhu mythos story . The second introducing a character who would later be incorporated into the mythos .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>5 October 1982</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cruelty">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cruelty</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"the nest of pain" is a short story by american author c.j. Henderson . It was first published in the compilation arkham detective agency in 2017 . The story is set in miskatonic university, an institution created by h.p. Lovecraft .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#C._J._Henderson"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror Short Fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Detective_Agency"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecrafts_Dunwich">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>H. P. Lovecraft's Dunwich</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Return to the forgotten village is a supplement for chaosium’s horror roleplaying game call of cthulhu . It focuses on the degenerate town of dunwich, massachusetts made famous in the novella the dunwich horror . Includes maps of both dunwich itself and the surrounding landscape, the town's history and inhabitants, details on several non-player characters .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Keith_Herber"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>192</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 2002</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Valley_Beyond_the_Stars">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Valley Beyond the Stars</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Charles Dixon Ernie Chan Gary Kwapisz English Marvel Comics Release Date September 1988 Media Type Comic book is a story in issue #152 of the 1st volume of Savage Sword of Conan written by Charles Dixon. Synopsis Conan of Cimmeria encounters an underground cult in Stygia dedicated to Yog-Sothoth .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Charles Dixon</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>September 1988</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Camp_Sunny">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Camp Sunny</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Paul Fricker Mike Mason English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Chaosium Release Date 2017 is a scenario supplement for the Call of Cthulhu horror roleplaying game. Written by Paul Fricker with Mike Mason , it was published as a free digital download by Chaosium in 2017. Overview External Links free download at Chaosium</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mike_Mason"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Paul_Fricker"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2017</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Two_Haikus">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Two Haikus</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Michael Balletti English Rogue Planet Press Lovecraftiana Vol 5, Issue 4 date 2021  " " is a Lovecraftian short work by Michael Balletti, first published in Vol 5, Issue 4 of the Lovecraftiana zine. As might be deduced from the title, it consists of a pair of haikus, a form of very short poetry which originated in Japan.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Michael Balletti</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Issue 4</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Lovecraftiana Vol 5</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2021</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sktai">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sk'tai</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu First appearance Nightmare's Disciple Created by Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. is Cthulhu 's third bride. ( EXP : Nightmare's Disciple )</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nightmares_Disciple"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Joseph S. Pulver. Sr.</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Worm_of_Urakhu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Worm of Urakhu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story first appeared in the 1988 issue of weirdbook (#23/24) it was reprinted in the simon of gitta collections the scroll of thoth ( chaosium, 1997) and sorcery against caesar ( pickman's press, 2020 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_L._Tierney"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Sorcery</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Sword</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Simon_of_Gitta"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>1998</ex:isPublishedAsW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Keeper_of_the_Key">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AugustDerlethWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Keeper of the Key</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The trail of cthulhu is a short story by the late american author august derleth . It was first published in the may 1951 issue of weird tales . It has since been included in numerous other collections, and has been translated into german, french and italian .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#August_Derleth"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Adventure</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>French</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>German</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Italian</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Trail_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>May 1951</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Spawn_of_the_Green_Abyss">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Spawn of the Green Abyss</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Deep Spawn</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Kraken Brood</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Monstrous Jellyfish</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Sea Slime</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The spawn are a fictional race expanded upon in 1994 for the call of cthulhu roleplaying game . They are heralds from outer space and came to this planet long ago to inhabit oceans and watery places . Some fishermen claim to have been taken down into the depths by the spawn .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>Gelatinous things with an elastic-rubber-like flesh</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>able to extend outward to feel the vibrations around it somewhat like a snake’s tongue. On land</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>belching it forth in a revolting fashion</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>it rolled or spread</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>larger than a man</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>plopping sounds as they move</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>rather than walked.</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>search for prey. They make wet</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>sending forth pseudopods or tentacles to taste the air</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>sucking in air</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>swell</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>their black-green flesh dripping a foul blue substance that seems to extrude like sweat. At the center is a sphincter-like orifice</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>they pulsate</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>which also appears to serve as a maw for feeding. Above the sphincter rises a worm-like tentacle</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#C._Hall_Thompson"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tomb_Herd">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tomb Herd</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sociocultural Characteristics Another dimension Real World Information First appearance EXP : " The " Created by Ramsey Campbell The is a collective of extra-dimensional beings that feast in crypts by possessing statues. They are described in the Necronomicon as creatures that exist beyond the threshold of space. They were first described by Ramsey Campbell in his short story " The ", which later became known as " The Church in High Street ". Trivia Interestingly, they are very similar in most respects to the Weeping Angels who appear in Doctor Who much, much later.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Tomb_Herd"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ramsey_Campbell"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hear_My_Call">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Hear My Call</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mark A. Mihalko English James Ward Kirk Publishing Lovecraft after Dark date November 2015 " " is a Cthulhu Mythos poem by American Mark A. Mihalko . It was published in the James Ward Kirk Publishing thematic anthology Lovecraft after Dark in November 2015. Synopsis The poem is written in a first-person narrative, and tells of a devotee to the Great Old Ones calling on sleeping Cthulhu to return in the face of unbelieving cultists. Behind the Mythos Amongst the Mythos elements made reference to in "" are the Necronomicon , Innsmouth and Dagon .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_A._Mihalko"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_after_Dark"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sngac">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>S'ngac</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>S'ngac the Violet Gas</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The violet gas is an alien character created by h. p. lovecraft . They first appeared unnamed in the short story " celephas " and are identified by name when they appear as a minor character in the novel the dream-quest of unknown kadath . The coloured gases are wise creatures which study the secrets of the universe .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dream-Quest_of_Unknown_Kadath"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Celepha%C3%AFs"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Experiment">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Experiment</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Frank Forte English Asylum Press Beyond Lovecraft  " " is a Lovecraftian short story by American Frank Forte . It was published in the collection Beyond Lovecraft by Asylum Press in September 2020. Synopsis A man who believes himself to be conducting an autopsy on a mutated human finds that he has been slipped experimental drugs which cause him to hallucinate his own murder at the hands of a monstrous being. Their test successful, the creators of the drug then haul him off for dissection.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Frank_Forte"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Beyond_Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tiamat_%28IDW_Comics%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tiamat (IDW Comics)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Tiamat is a goddess in the fictional universe of the ghostbusters comic by idw publishing . She is the sister and rival of gozer the destructor, whom she surpasses in power . She has a variety of powers including necromancy, warping reality, invading and influencing dreams, shapeshifting, illusions, speaking to the dead, extracting souls from other beings .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Spider_of_Leng">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Spider of Leng</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Leng Spider</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The spiders of leng are sentient cannibalistic creatures in the cthulhu mythos . They first appeared in lovecraft's novella the dream-quest of unknown kadath . The spiders are capable of spinning webs to disable their victims . They also possess a venomous bite of great potency .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dream-Quest_of_Unknown_Kadath"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Plain_of_Sound">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RamseyCampbellWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Plain of Sound</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story involves three university students, returning from severnford . They stumble across the titular plain of sound, a flat area with strange noises . Inside the house is a strange machine and a collection of occult books .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ramsey_Campbell"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Inhabitant_of_the_Lake"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Less Welcome Tenants</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1964</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Abominations_Of_Nephren-Ka">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Abominations Of Nephren-Ka</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Historywas published in the anthology horrors &amp; abominations: 24 tales of the cthulhu mythos in 2018 . The story mentions that evil came to the city of night through a strange artefact dubbed the eye of yuggoth .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Michael Sheehan. Jr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Abominations: 24 Tales of the Cthulhu Mythos</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Horrors</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 2018</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Silurian">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Silurian</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Sea Devils</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Earth Reptile</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Eocene</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The silurians ( adj : doctor who and the silurians ) are a fictional species created by malcolm hulke for the sci-fi television series doctor who . They are evolutionarily related to the aquatic sea devils, both species being collectively known as earth reptiles .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The Sea Devils</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Warriors of the Deep</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>Cold-blooded</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>Three eyes</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>Toothless mouth</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Doctor Who</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>the Silurians</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:usesLanguageC>Eocene language</ex:usesLanguageC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Malcolm Hulke</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
