  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Seabury_Quinn">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSFriendsAndAcquaintances"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Seabury Quinn</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1969 24 December 1969</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Prolific author of weird and detective tales to the pulps, notably a series of tales involving the psychic detective Jules de Grandin. He also edited Casket and Sunnyside and other trade publications for morticians.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Seabury grandin quinn was a government lawyer, journalist, and pulp magazine author . He was most famous for his stories of the occult detective, jules de grandin . The longest of the de grandin stories is the 1932 novel-length story the devil's bride .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA D.C. Washington</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Seabury Grandin Quinn</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>American</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Jerome Burke</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>A contemporary of H. P. Lovecraft</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1889 December 1889</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Children_of_Sthanee">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Children of Sthanee</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sthanee"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sthanee are vicious merfolk who are known to serve and revere the Great Old One Sthanee .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dreams_of_a_Diseased_Mind">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dreams of a Diseased Mind</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Steve Lines English Tenoka Press In Lovecraft's Shadow date March 2007 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos piece by the late , artist and editor Steve Lines . It was published in Ron Shiflet 's short-lived fanzine In Lovecraft's Shadow by Tenoka Press in March 2007. Overview Part Cthulhu Mythos story, part biography, "" reads as more akin to an 's description, the likes of which frequently precede published works. It gives a fictional account of Lines' childhood, which purportedly included encounters with a Polynesian Cthulhu cult and the government agency Delta Green , as well as sojourns in the Severn Valley and Innsmouth , before describing his real-world pursuits as an adult: mentions include his editorial work, musical achievements, and history as a .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Steve_Lines"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#In_Lovecrafts_Shadow"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 2007</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Cultus_Issue_11">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Periodicals"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Cultus Issue 11</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu Cultus #11 Various Cover Artist Tani Jantsang United States English P. Marsh Release Date March 1998 62 Preceded by Cthulhu Cultus #10 Followed by Cthulhu Cultus #12 Cthulhu Cultus #11 is an issue of sporadic American fanzine Cthulhu Cultus , edited by Tani Jantsang and James Ambuehl and published by P. Marsh in March 1998. Contents "Goddess" by Kenneth Silver "The Light Alone Leads to Disaster" by Casey Hughes "The Crystal from Out of Space" by P. J. Roberts "Ancient Feast" by Tracy Ambuehl "Attack of the Deep Ones" by Dan Ross "Revelation" by James Ambuehl "untitled ("HPL of Mythos fame...")" by Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. " People of the Monolith " (Part 5 of 7) by Crispin Burnham "Eviscerous Emanations" by Todd H. C. Fischer "Yhoundeh and Tsathoggua" by Ron Shiflet " The Chant of Dagon " by Ron Shiflet "A Core Unto Itself" by D. F. Lewis "The Steps at Siloth" by Michael Carter "Not One the Same" by J. B. Lee "The Book in the House" by Ron Shiflet "Beloved" by Kevin Eric Sheridan "Secret Cthulhu Cultus Message" by Tani Jantsang (as Anon)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>62</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>March 1998</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The crypt of cthulhu was a fanzine devoted to the writings of h. p. lovecraft . It was formally established in 1981 by robert m. price . Lovecraft scholarship was always a mainstay .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Second_Wish">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#BrianLumleyWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Second Wish</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Homage not only to h.p. Lovecraft but to r.e. Howard as well . Story first appeared in 1980 edition of new tales of the cthulhu mythos . Includes a macabre relic, a sinister old man, and a mummy .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#New_Tales_of_the_Cthulhu_Mythos"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1980</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>1976</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Autophagia">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Autophagia</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Fear &amp; infection in from the high seas is a third-party adventure supplement for chaosium's call of cthulhu roleplaying game . It contains a set of handouts as well as layout blueprints for the ship and a complete cast of non-player characters . The investigators must find out the cause of an inexplicable contagion which has taken root aboard a luxury passenger liner currently held under quarantine in new york .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tyler_Omichinski"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>36</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 2019</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Thing_from_the_Blasted_Heath">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#BrianLumleyWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Thing from the Blasted Heath</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"the caller of the black" was written in september 1967 "as an homage to hpl , and to the mythos" "the thing" of the title is a mutant shrub from the shores of the arkham reservoir . It wreaks horrific changes on the "thing" of the title .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Caller_of_The_Black"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1971</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>September 1967</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Plague_of_St._James_Infirmary">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Plague of St. James Infirmary</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>E. Keith Roller Ken Asamatsu Japanese (original) English (translation) " " is a short story by Ken Asamatsu , translated from the original Japanese by R. Keith Roller. The story first appeared in English translation in the anthology of Japanese Mythos writings, Night Voices, Night Journeys (Lairs of the Hidden Gods) . Synopsis In 1920s Chicago, Elliott Ness battles Al Capone as at the same time elemental forces of fire and water battle each other in the form of fire servants of Cthugha and water servants of Cthaat . Michael Leigh , a character originally created by Henry Kuttner , also makes an appearance. Behind the Mythos The pitting of fire against water in this story is a direct attempt to blend the kami elementals of Japanese mythology into the different concepts of the Mythos , in a manner quite similar to the August Derleth approach.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ken_Asamatsu"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Japanese</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Witch_Fire">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Witch Fire</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"closurebonanza" is a short story by american scott t. goudsward . It forms part of the arkham detective agency created by c.j. Henderson . Behind the mythoswas written at the request of brian m. sammons .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scott_T._Goudsward"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Fantasy Mystery Short Fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Detective_Agency"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Detective_Agency"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Nameless_City">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Nameless City</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lovecraft said that the story was based on a dream, which was in turn inspired by the last line of lord dunsany's story "the probable adventure of the three literary men" lovecraft drew on an already existing and vast literature of "lost city" stories .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Wolverine"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Tales_Vol._2">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Tales Vol. 2</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>various various English Boom! Studios Release Date March 2008 - March 2009 Media Type Comic book was a monthly comic book series that ran for a year, with stories in the Cthulhu Mythos that were written by various authors. Which was published in 2008-2009 by Boom! Studios. Issues and Stories Issue #1: The Eyes of Madness Issue #2: The Hiding Place Issue #3: Alimentary; My Dear Cthulhu Issue #4: In the Pi of the Beholder Issue #5: The Doorman Issue #6: Cthulhu House; A Stranger Comes to Night Hill; One of Those Days Issue #7: Empire of Ants; The Awakening Issue #8: The Elite; Theater of The Empty Eye Issue #9: The Zoo; It Must Have Been Something You Ate; Face of Competition Issue #10: Comeback Tour... Issue #11: Incorporation Issue #12: Arkham SVU; A Whistle For The Deep</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>March 2008 - March 2009</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Terrible_Old_Man">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Terrible Old Man</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The treeis a very short short story (less than 1,200 words) by h.p. Lovecraft . It's notable as the first story to make use of lovecraft's imaginary new england setting . The story focuses on three robbers who are "of that new and heterogeneous alien stock"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Tryout"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>July 1921</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Carter_%26_Lovecraft">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Carter &amp; Lovecraft</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Jonathan L. Howard English Thomas Dunne Books Release Date 2015 317 Followed by After the End of the World is a novel by Jonathan L. Howard , published in 2015 by Thomas Dunne Books, that combines the noir and Cthulhu Mythos genres. The story teams Daniel Carter, a former homicide detective turned PI, with Emily Lovecraft, a Providence bookstore owner and the last descendant of H. P. Lovecraft --whose works turn out to be far from fictional. It was followed by a sequel, After the End of the World .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jonathan_L._Howard"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>317 pages</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2015</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Worlds_of_H._P._Lovecraft:_The_Lurking_Fear">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleComicBookAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: The Lurking Fear</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft (original story) Steven Philip Jones (adaptation) Octavio Cariello United States English The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft Genre(s) Horror Caliber Comics Tome Press (imprint) Release Date 1997 59 Preceded by The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: The Alchemist Followed by The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: The Tomb is a comic book adaptation of H. P. Lovecraft 's short story " The Lurking Fear ". Overview Published by Caliber Comics under their Tome Press imprint in 1997, The Lurking Fear was adapted by Steven Philip Jones and featured artwork by Octavio Cariello . Running to a total of 59 in a softcover binding, the cover was full colour whilst the interiors were rendered in black and white; the original story was also included. It was later republished in the collection H. P. Lovecraft's Worlds: Volume 1 . Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Steven_Philip_Jones"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>59</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1997</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shaklatal">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CharactersIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Shaklatal</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Great Old Ones</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Amun</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Eye of Wicked Sight</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as The Eye of Wicked Sight Amun Great Old Ones First appearance EXP : Utatti Asfet Created by Owen Guthrie Toivo Luick is a fictional entity created by Owen Guthrie and Toivo Luick for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game. it made its first appearance in the 1996 scenario supplement Utatti Asfet , where the player characters are tasked with preventing its minions from awakening the creature Description A dark-skinned humanoid horror with tentacles sprouting from its head, and glowing red eyes, was worshiped by the earliest African civilizations as the god Amun. It is said to be the rival of Cthulhu .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Utatti_Asfet"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Owen Guthrie</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Toivo Luick</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Evan_Dicken">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Evan Dicken</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author United States United States http://www.evandicken.com/ is an American author who has written a handful of works set in and around the greater Cthulhu Mythos . In addition to these, he has also written extensively for gaming tie-in franchises, such as Legend of the Five Rings and Games Workshop 's Age of Sigmar . When not writing, Dicken works as a medical researcher at Ohio State University, from where he earned an M.A. in East Asian History, and is an avid tabletop roleplayer. Selected Works Short Fiction " Of the Fittest " (2015) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>United States</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://www.evandicken.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Children_of_Burma">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Children of Burma</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Colonel terusawa is a japanese army engineer who is part of the force invading burma in 1942 . He is tasked with taking control of former british airfield in the jungle and preparing it for occupation . When a patrol fails to return, a sentry notices lights from torches accompanied by an unearthly thumping sound . The next day, one of the men on watch catches a physically-degenerative native skulking on the edge of camp</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Stephen_Mark_Rainey"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Short Fiction Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2001</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>1999</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Thousand_Young">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Thousand Young</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A man who believes only sexual experience can grant him spiritual fulfilment succeeds in tracking down a group which professes the same . After completing a series of increasingly-depraved initiation rites, he is finally allowed to participate in one of their orgiastic rituals . When the lights are turned on, he finds that he has been tricked into engaging in congress with the outer god shub-niggurath .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_M._Price"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Eldritch Tales #18</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>January 1989</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Howard_Phillips_Lovecraft_%28fictional%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Howard Phillips Lovecraft (fictional)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Luveh-Keraphf"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Ech-Pi-El</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Horse Power Hateart</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Howard phillips lovecraft is a fictional character who has appeared in various conflicting accounts . He first appeared in 1948 in the poem " providence: two gentlemen meet at midnight " in 1979, he appeared as a character in the heavy metal magazine . Post-modern pastiches have accentuated his racialist outlook and in those works he has become a simplistic caricature .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Necronauts"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Necronomicon:_Book_of_the_Dead"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Fillmore_Shoggoth"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Three_Stooges_vs._Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Cast a Deadly Spell</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Fort: Prophet of the Unexplained</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Heavy Metal Magazine #197910 - Vol. 3</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Howard Lovecraft</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>No. 6</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Providence</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>To Mars</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Witch Hunt</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>the Frozen Kingdom</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>the Kingdom of Madness</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>the Undersea Kingdom</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Providence:_Two_Gentlemen_Meet_at_Midnight"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nightmares_%26_Tentacles:_13_Tales_Of_The_Cthulhu_Mythos">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nightmares &amp; Tentacles: 13 Tales Of The Cthulhu Mythos</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mark McLaughlin Michael Sheehan, Jr. English 256 is a collection of works by authors Mark McLaughlin and Michael Sheehan, Jr. Overview Nightmares &amp; Tentacles runs to a total of 256 and was released as a digital download on Amazon's Kindle platform. Contents Unless notes, all stories were written by both Mark McLaughlin and Michael Sheehan, Jr. "The Horror on the Superyacht" (Mark McLaughlin) " Shoggoth Apocalypse " " Stainless Steel Sarcophagus " "Pyramid of the Shoggoths" " The Prisoner of Carcosa " " Yuletide Mementos " " Horrors of the Trash Island " "The Idol in the Hedge Maze" "The Black-and-White House" "City of Living Shadows" "Hound-Dog McGee and the Ghost-Puncher Gang Meet the Blubbering Blasphemy in the Bed &amp; Breakfast of Madness" (Mark McLaughlin) "Just Another Afternoon in Arkham, Brought to You in Living Color" "All Abuzz" (Mark McLaughlin)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Michael Sheehan. Jr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>256</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Winfield_Scott_Lovecraft">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPeople"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Winfield Scott Lovecraft</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Winfield lovecraft was a traveling salesman for a manufacturer of silverware based in rhode island . In 1893, when howard was three, winfield became acutely psychotic in a chicago hotel room caused by neurological symptoms of syphilis . He was brought back to providence and placed in butler hospital, where he remained until his death in 1898 . Lovecraft was falsely informed that his father was comatose during this period .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasOccupationR>Blacksmith</ex:hasOccupationR>
    <ex:hasOccupationR>Travelling salesman</ex:hasOccupationR>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sarah_Susan_Phillips_Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasRelativeR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Azotharoth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OuterGods"/>
    <rdfs:label>Azotharoth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Azoth</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Azotheroth</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Dreaming God</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Dreaming One</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Azoth , or azotheroth , is an outer god who was created by writers gerry conway and roy thomas for marvel comics . It appeared in conan the barbarian: the horn of azoth (issue #59 of the 1st volume of marvel graphic novel) in april 1990 . The movie's main antagonist, dagoth , was renamed azoth in the graphic novel, among things to distance it from the movie .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horn_of_Azoth"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Roy_Thomas"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Gerry Conway</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nancy_Kilpatrick">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nancy Kilpatrick</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Nancy kilpatrick is a canadian writer and editor . She's best-known for her vampire stories . She has published 21 novels and more than 200 short stories .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Pennsylvania Philadelphia</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>Canadian</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://nancykilpatrick.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1946 May 6</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Worlds_of_H._P._Lovecraft_%28series%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleComicBookAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft (series)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The worlds of h. p. lovecraft was a series of comic books which adapted several of lovecraft's short stories into a more visual format . Published by caliber comics for the first five issues between 1993 and 1996 . The next four issues were released under caliber's tome press imprint from 1997 to 1998 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Steven_Philip_Jones"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Rapture_of_the_Deep_and_Other_Lovecraftian_Tales">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Rapture of the Deep and Other Lovecraftian Tales</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cody Goodfellow Cover Artist Rob Winfield United States English German Genre(s) Weird fiction Hippocampus Press Release Date April 2016  is a collection of Cthulhu Mythos stories by Cody Goodfellow , published in April 2016 by Hippocampus Press , who released an ebook edition two months later. " Lovecraftian fiction has comprised about half my fictional output since I began writing for money, twenty-two years ago," Goodfellow writes in an introduction to the volume: Describing his own approach to the Mythos, Goodfellow writes that he Publication History Since its debut in 2016, has been translated into German, released in October 2022 under the title Tiefenrausch ("Deep Rapture"), part of Festa's H. P. Lovecraft's Bibliothek des Schreckens series. Contents The stories included in Rapture of the Deep (with date of first publication) are: " The Anatomy Lesson " (2015) " König Feurio " (2015) " To Skin a Corpse " (2006) " In the Shadow of Swords " (2002) " Garden of the Gods " (2015) " Grinding Rock " (2005) " Rapture of the Deep " (2009) " Inside Uncle Sid " (2011) " Archons " (2016) " Broken Sleep " (2015) " Cahokia " (2005) " Swinging " (2016) Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cody_Goodfellow"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>German</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>April 2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
