  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Astronomicon_minorem">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Astronomicon minorem</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Khurt Khave English First United Church of Cthulhu Release Date August 2015 162 - DMT, Cthulhu and You: First United Church of Cthulhu is a book which purports to describe the doctrines of the First United Church of Cthulhu , a legally-recognised religion which takes its teachings from H. P. Lovecraft 's Cthulhu Mythos . Written by head priest Khurt Khave , it was published in August 2015. Overview Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Khurt_Khave"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>162</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 2015</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Department_of_Extraordinary_Phenomena">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Organisation"/>
    <rdfs:label>Department of Extraordinary Phenomena</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationO>Canadian government</ex:hasAffiliationO>
    <ex:hasAffiliationO>Various counterpart agencies worldwide</ex:hasAffiliationO>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The department of paranormal defence was created by troy young for his nyarlathotep cycle series of cthulhu mythos stories . It was founded in 1928 after two law enforcement officers and a scholar uncovered cult activity which resulted in the summoning of a malevolent being . In order to spare the lives and sanity of the innocent, the department recruits only from those individuals who have had prior encounters with the other .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasPurposeO>Paranormal defence</ex:hasPurposeO>
    <ex:hasTypeO>Government agency</ex:hasTypeO>
    <ex:isLocatedAtO>Canada</ex:isLocatedAtO>
    <ex:wasCreatedBynO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Troy_Young"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Franklin_Chase_Clark">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSRelatives"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Franklin Chase Clark</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>28 January 1886</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lovecraft’s maternal uncle and husband of Lillian Delora Phillips, whom he married on 10 April 1902. In 1869 Franklin received an A.B. from Brown University and in 1872 he received his M.D. from the College of Physicians and Surgeons in New York City. From this point until his death in 1915 he practiced medicine in Providence.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isRelativeToR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1807</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Stars_Are_Right%21">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Stars Are Right!</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Stars Are Right! Various Various English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Chaosium Release Date June 28, 1992 (5th edition) December 31, 2004 (6th edition) 128 (5th edition) 180 (6th edition) The Stars Are Right! is a scenario supplement for the Call of Cthulhu horror role-playing game. It was first published by Chaosium in 1992 for the 5th edition, and another version with two additional scenarios was released in 2004, as part of the 6th edition. Overview Contents "Love's Lonely Children" by Richard Watts "Nemo Solis Sapit" by John Tynes "This Fire Shall Kill" by André Bishop "The Professionals" by Fred Behrendt "Fractal Gods" by Steve Hatherly "The Gates of Delirium" by Gary Sumpter "The Music of the Spheres" by Kevin Ross "Darkest Calling" (6th edition only) "The Source and the End" (6th edition only) "When the Stars Came Right Again" by Steven C. Rasmussen &amp; D. H. Frew</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>128 (5th edition) 180 (6th edition)</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1992 (5th edition) 2004 (6th edition) December 31 June 28</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dogs">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Dogs</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A lonely man uses his library of esoteric books to create a device which allows him to peer into the earth's future . What he sees is a planet ravaged by the arrival of enormous floating abominations who have wiped out the human race . As he looks farther ahead, he sees the descendants of earth's dogs as they slowly evolve into something more intelligent .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jeffrey_Thomas"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dark_Rites_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 2014</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Documents_in_the_Case_of_Elizabeth_Akeley">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Documents in the Case of Elizabeth Akeley</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A federal agency investigates a cult headed by the great-granddaughter of a man who vanished under mysterious circumstances a half century earlier . Evidence suggests that the great-grandfather may be returning, improbably enough . It first appeared in the march 1982 issue of the magazine of fantasy &amp; science fiction .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_A._Lupoff"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Science Fiction</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The Magazine of Fantasy</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 1982</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Diary_of_Hannah_Greeley">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Diary of Hannah Greeley</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"the innsmouth covenant" is a short cthulhu mythos piece by american jennifer m. oaks . It was published with its parter digitally in the chapbook collection the brides of innsmouth . The text recounted from hannah greeley's diary is writing taken - for the most parts verbatim - from the companion story "the innsmouth covenant"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jennifer_M._Oaks"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Brides_of_Innsmouth"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dark_Equinox_and_Other_Tales_of_Lovecraftian_Horror">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dark Equinox and Other Tales of Lovecraftian Horror</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ann K. Schwader Cover Artist Lindsay Harper English Hippocampus Press Release Date 2015 260 is a collection of Lovecraftian fiction by Ann K. Schwader , published in 2015 by Hippocampus Press . It includes several stories featuring Schwader's recurring protagonist Cassie Barrett. Contents "Dark Equinox" (2014) "The Sweetness of Your Heart" (2001) "When the Stars Run Away" (2013) "Wings of Memory" "Her Beloved Son" (2013) "Custom Order" "Desert Mystery! Gas &amp; Go!" (2011) "Rehab" "Scream Saver" (2009) "The Water Lily Room" "The Death Verses of Yian-Ho" "Twenty Mile" (2002) "Experiencing the Other" (2003) "Paradigm Wish" "Night of the Piper" (2015) "The Wind-Caller"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ann_K._Schwader"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>260</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2015</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hyperborea_%28Role-Playing_Game%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Hyperborea (Role-Playing Game)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Hyperborea North Wind Adventures Publication Date 2012 Roleplaying game Dungeons &amp; Dragons Hyperborea is sword-and-sorcery table-top role-playing game based on Hyperborea as described or informed by the fantastic literature of Robert Ervin Howard , Howard Phillips Lovecraft , and Clark Ashton Smith . It uses the original Dungeons &amp; Dragons rules options from 1974, and is part of what has come to be called the Old School Revival (OSR) movement in table-top role-playing. It is published by North Wind Adventures. The first edition was released in 2012 and the game is now supported online and has print on demand versions of some of its earlier releases. In 2013, the first full year of its original release, it received nominations (but did not win) in the following competitions: North Texas RPG Con 2013 , Three Castles RPG Design Award ENnie Awards 2013 , Best Game ENnie Awards 2013 , Best Production Values ENnie Awards 2013 , Product of the Year The game made some effort to be faithful to the original source material in the works of the Lovecraft Circle authors but balance that with playability and more than passing reliance on the Marvel Comics conceptualisation of Conan the Barbarian from its many years of publishing what was essentially a pastiche of the original tales.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2012</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shadows_from_Rlyeh">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Shadows from R'lyeh</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>John Salonia Cover Artist John Salonia, Jr. English Release Date 2017 is a collection of Lovecraftian fiction by John Salonia. It was self-published in paperback and digital formats in 2017; an expanded edition was later released, featuring two essays previously published in the Lovecraft Annual , presented here as they were originally written. Overview Contents Original edition: "Mask of the Imago" "Darwin's Messenger" "The Thing in the Corner" "The Shameless City" "The Wake" "Ants' Nest" "Easter Island" "Clamheads" "The Warren" "Three Whispers in a Dead Man's Throat" Included in the expanded edition: "Cosmic Maenads and the Music of Madness: Lovecraft's Borrowings from the Greeks" "Essential Saltes: Lovecraft's Witchcraft"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>John Salonia</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2017</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Egypt">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsIncorporatedFromTheRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>Egypt</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Northeast africa nation has seen a degree of use in the works of h.p. Lovecraft . It is an extremely arid country consisting of vast swathes of desert . The majority of the population occupy the areas around the relatively fertile river valley .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>Extensive desert</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>Lengthy</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>fertile river valley</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Nation</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Northeast Africa</ex:isLocatedAtL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthalpa">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OuterGods"/>
    <rdfs:label>C'thalpa</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Outer_God"/>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Talpeur</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Internal One</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as The Internal One Relatives Shterot (offpring) Outer God Talpeur First appearance " Paris, Rêve ou Réalité " Created by Dominique Balczesak et al. ( The Internal One ) is an Outer God described as a huge mass of living, sentient magma, located in Earth’s mantle. She is the mother of the Great Old One Shterot and five other unnamed hideous children. She is served by a race of mole-like humanoid burrowers called the Talpeurs . References Call of Cthulhu RPG (French edition)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Paris</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Rêve ou Réalité</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Dominique Balczesak et al.</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Madame_Renee">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Madame Renee</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>The Arkham Detective Agency</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Brenda Goff</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as , is a character who appears in several of the arkham detective agency stories by c. j. henderson and others . Created by c. j. henderson for his first frank nardi story, the idea of fear .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Idea_of_Fear"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#C._J._Henderson"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Matthew_DiNardo">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Matthew DiNardo</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>Early 21st century</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Matthew is an aspiring young author, willing to say or do almost anything to get women to sleep with him . He is a devious character, willing to say or do almost anything to get women to sleep with him .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dagon"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Armand_Rosamilia"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Lady_in_Gray">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Lady in Gray</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Narrator's fiancée, a pale-skinned, grey-eyed young woman, dies in a plane crash . On the night of her funeral, the narrator dreams that miriam appears to him . He drowns in a strange sea made of a black viscous slime-like liquid . When he wakes up, he finds that his body is indeed covered in black slime .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Donald_Wandrei"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>French</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 1933</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Serpent_Person">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Serpent Person</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Serpent Folk</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Serpent Men</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Snake Men</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Valusian</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Serpent people are a nonhuman species which, although possessing somewhat humanoid limbs, strongly resembles snakes . They first dwelt in shuggon in mesozoic times, but when it was destroyed (circa 65 million b.c.e.) They moved first to elder valusia (the thurian continent), and then small pockets moved all over the world (america being a probable site)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The Shadow Out of Time The Diary of Alonzo Typer The Haunter of the Dark</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Nameless_City"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shadow_Kingdom"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_E._Howard"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Partridgeville">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Partridgeville</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Frank belknap long's fictional town is on a seacoast and near new york city . Town's financial district is built around central square . Town appears in " the space-eaters " (1928) and " the hounds of tindalos " (1929)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Hounds_of_Tindalos"/>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Town</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>United States East Coast</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Space-Eaters"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ravenstone_Sanitarium">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ravenstone Sanitarium</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>It provides game mechanics for dissociative identity disorder characters . It also adds a new rule and game mechanic, the personality check, modifying the standard and ubiquitous sanity check .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>John Gary Pettit</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2008</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Blasphemy_in_the_Canopic_Jar_%26_More_Tales_Of_The_Cthulhu_Mythos">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Blasphemy in the Canopic Jar &amp; More Tales Of The Cthulhu Mythos</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mark McLaughlin Michael Sheehan, Jr. English Genre(s) Horror Release Date September 2016 71 is a collection of short stories from authors Mark McLaughlin and Michael Sheehan, Jr. Overview Consisting of seven stories and running to a total of 71 , was released as a Kindle ebook in September 2016. Contents " The Blasphemy In The Canopic Jar " " Glove: A Tale Of The King In Yellow " " Mrs. Dakhamunzu " " The Pharaoh Of Pendleton Court " " The Final Door " " The Spawn Of Ghattambah " " Casino Carcosa "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Michael Sheehan. Jr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>71</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>September 2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#World_War_Cthulhu_%28Cubicle_7_Entertainment_anthology%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>World War Cthulhu (Cubicle 7 Entertainment anthology)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>World War Cthulhu Various Cover Artist Jon Hodgson English Genre(s) Lovecraftian fiction Cubicle 7 Entertainment Release Date December 2013 World War Cthulhu is a thematic anthology of Lovecraftian fiction produced by Cubicle 7 Entertainment as a tie-in work for their World War Cthulhu roleplaying game setting. Edited by Jonathan Oliver and featuring cover art by Jon Hodgson , it was published in December 2013 in a solely digital format . Overview Contents Introduction by Jonathan Oliver "The Aklo Intelligence" by James Lovegrove "Nightmares and the Price of Dreams" by Sarah Newton "Reign of Hell" by Paul Finch "The Hunger in the Flames" by Rebecca Levene "Baby-Steps to Oblivion" by T. P. Pike "The Death House" by John Llewellyn Probert "Now I Am Nothing" by Simon Bestwick "The Egyptian" by Robin D. Laws "Strange Bedfellows" by Greg Stolze "From Unquiet Waters" by Gaie Sebold "When I Knew Baseball" by Weston Ochse "The Scottish Patient" by Jonathan Green "The Mouse" by Archie Black "Watchers" by Lavie Tidhar Afterword by Dominic McDowall References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Lovecraftian fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>December 2013</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sea_Devil">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sea Devil</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Gerry Conway Mike Perkins English Marvel Comics Release Date May 2016 - Sept 2016 Media Type Comic book The is a 5-part story arc in from Carnage Vol. 2 #6 (March 23, 2016) to 10 (July 27, 2016), written by Gerry Conway and penciled by Mike Perkins . Synopsis The superhuman serial killer Carnage becomes involved with the Darkhold and searches for the Chthonic Island with the FBI and the Darkhold Redeemers on his trail. Links and references at the Marvel Database wiki</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Gerry Conway</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 2016 - Sept 2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Cultus_Issue_6">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Cultus Issue 6</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu Cultus #6 Various Cover Artist Tani Jantsang United States English P. Marsh Release Date January 1997 62 Preceded by Cthulhu Cultus #5 Followed by Cthulhu Cultus #7 Cthulhu Cultus #6 is an issue of sporadic American fanzine Cthulhu Cultus , edited by Tani Jantsang and James Ambuehl and published by P. Marsh in January 1997. Contents "Hungers" by Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. "Whiteout" by James Ambuehl and E. P. Berglund " Beavis and Butt-Head Read the Necronomicon " by James Ambuehl (as Mike Jury) "Dear Premier Fidel Castro" by Tani Jantsang (as Anon.) "The Price of a Book" by Laurence J. Cornford "A Letter from a Deconstructionist That Deconstructed" by Tani Jantsang (as Anon.) "A Missive from a Daughter of Dagon" by Tracy Ambuehl "Sea Change" by James Ambuehl "Gone Fishin'" by James Ambuehl "Introduction to "The Burrowers Beneath"" by Robert M. Price "The Burrowers Beneath" by Lin Carter "The Horror in Red Hook" by Tani Jantsang (as Anon.) "Watching Faye" by Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. "Feery's Original Notes" by Robert M. Price</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>62</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>January 1997</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gnoph-keh">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonSapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gnoph-keh</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Bear Monsters</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Nanruki</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Ohto</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Oon-T'keph</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>White Horns</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The gnophkeh are a race of beings who appear to inhabit cold places . Some tales speak of the creatures as earthly extensions of alien powers . Other tales speak of the creatures as earthly extensions of alien powers .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>A polar bear monstrosity</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>but other times it uses all its six limbs</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>covered in shaggy white hair. From its forehead rise a single horn</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>sharp teeth. It may walk upright on two legs or bear-like on four</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>while its great mouth was filled with wicked</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>with six-limbs</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>HPL The Horror in the Museum</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hazel_Heald"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Howard_Phillips_Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
