  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#When_He_Arrived">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>When He Arrived</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Norbert Gora English Rogue Planet Press Swords Against Cthulhu III: A New Dark Age date 2018 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos poem by Polish Norbert Gora . It was published in the Gavin Chappell -edited anthology Swords Against Cthulhu III: A New Dark Age by Rogue Planet Press in 2018. Synopsis The six verses tell of the coming of Hastur to Earth , and his subsequent destruction of the entire species, while simultaneously corrupting the biosphere.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Norbert_Gora"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Swords_Against_Cthulhu_III:_A_New_Dark_Age"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2018</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#History_of_the_Necronomicon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>History of the Necronomicon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lovecraft wrote it in 1927, largely for his own use, to keep his references consistent . It was published as a pamphlet in 1938, after lovecraft's death, by the rebel press . The account gives an original arabic title for the necronomicon, al-azif .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Keeper_of_the_Moon-Lens">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Avatars"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Keeper of the Moon-Lens</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Avatar of Shub-Niggurath</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The keeper is an avatar of shub-niggurath created by ramsey campbell . It is worshiped by the inhabitants of the english town of exham in the severn valley . The ancient romans worshiped it as the magna mater, while modern cultists seem to refer to it with male and female pronouns interchangeably .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isLocatedAtC>Inside a hill in Exham</ex:isLocatedAtC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Moon-Lens"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ramsey_Campbell"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Afterburn">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Afterburn</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A man recounts the devastation which occurred when his town was cursed by an infestation of flying, fire-breathing extra-dimensional entities . At story's conclusion, he reflects on whether the incursion was a one-off event, or the precursor to a full-scale invasion . Historywas published in vol 5, issue 1 of the lovecraftiana zine in 2020</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Carlton_Herzog"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Issue 1</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Lovecraftiana Vol 5</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2020</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mynoghra">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Mynoghra</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Great_Old_One"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>She-Daemon of the Shadows</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Created by the late american author edward p. berglund for his 1975 short story " wings in the night " is a cosmic entity capable of manifesting in a variety of forms, including that of a beautiful woman . Likes to forge pacts with mortals for an unnamed price, although usually this price is the life-essence of the other party .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Carpe Noctem Haiyore! Nyaruko-san</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Wings_in_the_Night"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Edward_P._Berglund"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Servile">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Servile</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu codex lammas 1997 is a short story by american jeffrey thomas . It was published in the lammas 1997 edition of the robert m. price -edited fanzine cthulhu codex by necronomicon press .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jeffrey_Thomas"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>German</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Codex_Lammas_1997"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>August 1997</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Anthony_M._Rud">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsInspirationsAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Anthony M. Rud</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>30 November 1942</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Anthony Melville Rud Pseudonym(s) Ray McGillivary Anson Piper Connection to the Mythos Possible Inspiration Birth Date 11 January 1893 Chicago, Illinois, USA Death Date 30 November 1942 New York City, New York, USA United States Horror Science Fiction Detective 𝐓 Anthony Melville Rud (11 January 1893 - 30 November 1942) was an American author and editor, several of whose works appeared in long-running pulp magazine Weird Tales ; his short story " Ooze " was published in the very first issue. This tale, of a creature secreted away in a house which it later emerges from and destroys, is considered a possible inspiration for H. P. Lovecraft 's short story " The Dunwich Horror ".</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Chicago Illinois</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Anthony Melville Rud</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Anson Piper</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Ray McGillivary</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Possible Inspiration</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>11 January 1893</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Re-Animator">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleFilmAndTelevisionAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Re-Animator</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Based on the episodic novella " herbert west--reanimator " written by dennis paoli, directed by stuart gordon, and produced by brian yuzna . It was the first film in the film series, and is still used as one of the benchmarks for appreciating and critiquing lovecraftian cinema.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasWriterW>http://example.org/lovecraft#Dennis_Paoli http://example.org/lovecraft#Stuart_Gordon http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft William J. Norris</ex:hasWriterW>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Herbert_West--Reanimator"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>18 October 1985</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Yqaa">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalLocations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>Y'qaa</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAccessibilityL>Underneath Mount Voormithadreth</ex:hasAccessibilityL>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesL>Cavern of Y'quaa</ex:hasAlternativeNamesL>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The abyss of , or the cavern of y'quaa is an underground realm inhabited by the primordial being ubbo-sathla . The name was coined by lin carter for his xothic legend cycle, while the first appeared unnamed in clark ashton smith's story " ubbo-sathla " . The alternate spelling y'quaa is used in the 2020 edition of the malleus monstrorum .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Descent_into_the_Abyss"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL>The Seven Geases (possibly)</ex:hasAppearanceL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ubbo-Sathla"/>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Abhoth (possibly)</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Interdimensional</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>The Horror in the Gallery (named)</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>Ubbo-Sathla (unnamed)</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Creeping_Kelp">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Creeping Kelp</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>157 is a cthulhu mythos novella by british author william meikle . It is intended as a cautionary tale on mankind's mistreatment of mother nature . A segment of the story was taken in its entirety and published in 2011 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#William_Meikle"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>157</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 2011</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Les_Fleurs_Empoisonn%C3%A9es">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CaitlinRKiernanWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Les Fleurs Empoisonnées</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"in the garden of poisonous flowers" is a novella by caitlin r. kiernan . It features their recurring character, teen albino monster-slayer dancy flammarion . Published in 2002, it was the second story about dancy after "threshold"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Caitlin_R._Kiernan"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dancy_Flammarion"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>2002</ex:isPublishedAsW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Church_of_Starry_Wisdom">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cults"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Organisation"/>
    <rdfs:label>Church of Starry Wisdom</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Haunter_of_the_Dark"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesO>The Celestial Providence sect</ex:hasAlternativeNamesO>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The , or starry wisdom cult, was founded in providence, rhode island circa 1844 by professor enoch bowen . The cult used a sacred relic known as the shining trapezohedron to summon the haunter of the dark, who demanded outrageous sacrifices in return for limitless knowledge of the universe . After run-ins with both local citizens and the municipal government, the church closed and 181 people left providence for unknown reasons .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNotableMemberO>Professor Enoch Bowen</ex:hasNotableMemberO>
    <ex:hasTypeO>Cult</ex:hasTypeO>
    <ex:isLocatedAtO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham"/>
    <ex:isLocatedAtO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Rhode_Island"/>
    <ex:isLocatedAtO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#San_Francisco"/>
    <ex:isLocatedAtO>Canada</ex:isLocatedAtO>
    <ex:isLocatedAtO>Chicago</ex:isLocatedAtO>
    <ex:isLocatedAtO>England (1880 to 1890)</ex:isLocatedAtO>
    <ex:isLocatedAtO>Equatorial Guinea (1970s)</ex:isLocatedAtO>
    <ex:isLocatedAtO>Illinois (-1871)</ex:isLocatedAtO>
    <ex:isLocatedAtO>Massachusetts (1920s)</ex:isLocatedAtO>
    <ex:isLocatedAtO>Providence</ex:isLocatedAtO>
    <ex:isLocatedAtO>Yorkshire</ex:isLocatedAtO>
    <ex:wasCreatedBynO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Great_Cthulhu_%28Sebastian_Greaves%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Great Cthulhu (Sebastian Greaves)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"great cthulhu " is a short mythos story by sebastian greaves . It is aimed at the young adult demographic . "great cthulhu" was published in the collection cthulhu mythos anthology .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sebastian_Greaves"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Cthulhu Mythos Anthology</ex:isPublishedAsW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Call_of_Distant_Shores">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Call of Distant Shores</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>David Niall Wilson English Macabre Ink Release Date January 2019 242 : Tales of Elder Gods &amp; Lovecraftian Horror is an anthology of Lovecraftian fiction by American David Niall Wilson . Running to a total of 242 , it was published in January 2019 by Macabre Ink. Overview Contents ’s Introduction "Glenn &amp; The Tart of Mortar Psycho Maine Tenants" "The Milk of Paradise" "Are You Lookin’ For Herb?" "Cockroach Suckers" "Darkness, and the Light" "Death, and His Brother Sleep" "Death Did Not Become Him" with Patricia Lee Macomber "From My Reflection, Darkly" "The Lost Wisdom of Instinct" "Rending the Veil" "The Hall of Captured Gods" "Anomaly" "" Reception American horror writer Al Sarrantonio speaks highly of the collection, stating "If you revere the ‘traditionalists’ of the horror field like I do — Lovecraft , Hugh B. Cave , Clark Ashton Smith , Manly Wade Wellman (who was one of my mentors, I’m proud to say) — then you’ll love these tales. Many of them would have had pride of place in any issue of Weird Tales in the 30s or 40s."</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#David_Niall_Wilson"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>242</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>January 2019</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Squire_Brewster">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Squire Brewster</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Human First appearance HPL : " The Tomb " Created by H. P. Lovecraft was a Boston man described by Jervas Dudley as rich and celebrated, and a maker of local history (though this is not elaborated upon). According to Jervas, the undertaker Goodman Simpson had robbed the recently dead body of the Squire, stealing various articles of clothing before burying him in 1711. Jervas acquired this information from spirits with whom he conversed during one of his nightly visits to a local cemetery. Jervas also stated that had not been fully deceased at the time of his presumed death, for he had "turned twice" in his coffin on the day after burial.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Tomb"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#What_Dwells_Beneath">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>What Dwells Beneath</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Gerry Conway Mike Perkins English Marvel Comics Release Date Oct, 2016 - March, 2017 Media Type Comic book is 6-part story arc in issues #11-16 of the 2nd volume of Carnage written by Gerry Conway. Synopsis The superhuman serial killer Carnage attempts to summon the Elder God , Chthon , to Earth, and a combined team of FBI and Darkhold Redeemers race to stop him.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Gerry Conway</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2017 2016 - March Oct</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Le_Novar">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Le Novar</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Nick Calvert</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as nick calvert millennia in the past . Created by author j. B. lee for his cthulhu mythos writings . Claims to be a cosmic time-travelling alien from xandra the asteroid planet . Currently occupies the body of an elderly black man named nick calvert .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#His_Hour_Upon_the_Stage"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#J._B._Lee"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#An_Item_of_Supporting_Evidence">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#BrianLumleyWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>An Item of Supporting Evidence</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Titus crow is taken to task by weird artist chandler davies for writing a piece of lovecraftian fiction that blatantly contradicts known history . Crow defends himself by producing an object that proves that his fiction is grounded in fact .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Titus_Crow"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Arkham_Collector"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>Summer 1970</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Adumbrali">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Adumbrali</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Creatures that appear as shadows that dwell in the abyss and send out seekers to capture souls for them to torture and devour . They are so named in the song of yste .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Abyss"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_A._W._Lowndes"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hchtelegoth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>H'chtelegoth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Great Tentacled God</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as The Great Tentacled God Great Old One First appearance " The Stranger &amp; " " Blood Red Moon " Created by Ran Cartwright (as R.S. Cartwright) is a Great Old One which resembled a greenish-brown trunk with a "crown" of tentacles, a row of multiple eyes and a couple of lateral grasping tentacles. References "The Stranger &amp; ", Ran Cartwright (1999) "Blood Red Moon", Ran Cartwright (2004)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Blood Red Moon</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Stranger"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ran_Cartwright"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Notes_on_Writing_Weird_Fiction">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Notes on Writing Weird Fiction</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft English Portuguese Italian French Spanish Genre(s) Non-fiction Amateur Correspondent date May 1937 " ", also published by August Derleth under the title " Notes on the Writing of Weird Fiction " , is a short non-fiction work by the late American H. P. Lovecraft . First published in the May-June 1937 edition of the Amateur Correspondent , it has been extensively reprinted in an variety of publications over the years, and has also been translated into Portuguese, Italian, French, and Spanish . References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>French</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Italian</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Portuguese</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Spanish</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Amateur Correspondent</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>May 1937</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#1920s_Investigators_Companion">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>1920s Investigator's Companion</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Keith Herber United States English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplay Game Supplement Chaosium Media Type Print PDF Call of Cthulhu: is a sourcebook for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game. It was first released as a paperback book, but is now only available in PDF format. Overview From the 's website: Contents Part One: The Roaring Twenties An Overview of the Jazz Age Chronology of the Twenties Part Two: Becoming an Investigator Playing an Investigator 1920s Occupations How to Create Your 1920s Investigator 1920s Skills Part Three: The Tools of the Trade Research &amp; Resources Newspapers from Around the Globe Consultants Transport &amp; Travel Equipment &amp; Arms 1920s Sample Gear &amp; Prices A Selection of Handguns Firearms Table Part Four: Words of Wisdom The Professional Investigator Tips From a Pro 1920s Forensic Arts Index of Professions Character Sheet (Front) Character Sheet (Back) Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Keith_Herber"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplay Game Supplement</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Pleasure_in_Madness">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Pleasure in Madness</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>" a walk in the shadows " is a short story by american c. j. henderson . It forms the final chapter in the arkham detective agency begun with " the idea of fear " the story is set after the events of the disastrous pabodie expedition described in at the mountains of madness . The primary antagonists of the story are the ancient shoggoths created by h.p. Lovecraft himself .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#C._J._Henderson"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Detective_Agency"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Grandma_Gilman">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Grandma Gilman</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Behind the mythos is a novelette by lovecraftian fiction author bryan whitehead . It is published digitally through amazon's kindle platform by 8sails press in january 2013 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Bryan Whitehead</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Lovecraftian fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>14 January 2013</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shlithneth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Shlithneth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>First appearance Avalon: The County of Somerset is an entity resembling a gigantic worm, with a mass of tentacles around its maw.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Avalon:_The_County_of_Somerset"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Return_of_the_Witch">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RamseyCampbellWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Return of the Witch</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The witch of the title is gladys shorrock, who in the late 1910s comes to live, with her son robert, at 7 victoria road in brichester's mercy hill neighbourhood . Norman owen, a novelist from lancashire, moves in, and suffers a dizzy spell, and finds a neon pentagram hanging from the ceiling . The house remains empty for some 35 years, when he suffers a bad dream, including one involving grave robbing .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ramsey_Campbell"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Inhabitant_of_the_Lake"/>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Less Welcome Tenants</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>1964</ex:isPublishedAsW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Swords_Against_Cthulhu_II:_Hyperborean_Nights">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Swords Against Cthulhu II: Hyperborean Nights</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Lissanne Lake English Genre(s) Sword-and-sorcery Rogue Planet Press Release Date January 2017 156 Preceded by Swords Against Cthulhu Followed by Swords Against Cthulhu III: A New Dark Age is a Cthulhu Mythos anthology focused on the sword-and-sorcery genre. Overview Running to a total of 156 , Swords Against Cthulhu II was edited by Gavin Chappell and published by Rogue Planet Press in January 2017. It features cover art by Lissanne Lake. Contents " Coeval with Nights and Chaos " by Charlie Walls " Castle of Circles " by Mike Lee " The Puppeteer " by Gary Budgen " A Sister's Love " by Stephen Hernandez " The Ink of the Slime Lord " by Jason Ray Carney " Fear of the Knight " by Carl Fox " The Flame in the Ice " by DJ Tyrer " Silence, Please " by Ernesto Canepa and Sergio Palumbo " Beneath the Temple of Outlandish Idols " by Gavin Chappell " The Last to Die " by Jason Scott Aiken " The Last Moments of Lomar " by Norbert Gora " When the Light Comes " by Mike VandeVenter " The Tale of Nanok the Merchant " by Thomas V. Powers " Coffin of Glass " by James Pratt " Annals of the South Part Two " by John C. Adams</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Sword-and-sorcery</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>156</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>January 2017</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Lightless_Beacon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Lightless Beacon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>When the lights went out is a beginner-level adventure module for chaosium ’s call of cthulhu roleplaying game . It is dedicated to the memory of chaosium founder greg stafford , who died in 2018 . Written by leigh carry with lynne hardy, is available as a free digital pdf download from their website .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Leigh_Carr"/>
