  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tengu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SpeciesIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tengu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>T'ien-kou</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>In 2005 they were first used by chaosium as a fictional race for the call of cthulhu role-playing game . They are an alien race of bird-like humanoids that have crow-like features, talons, shiny black wings, a beak and bird-like feet .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Secrets_of_Japan"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cryptic_Publications">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DefunctRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SmallPress"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cryptic Publications</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Current Status Presumed defunct March 1984 Robert M. Price United States is a small press publishing label by leading H. P. Lovecraft scholar Robert M. Price . Primarily used to publish Lovecraftian fiction and scholarly work in a fanzine format, it also released a handful of science and weird fiction works. Its first release, Risque Stories #1, was issued in March 1984; its last, Crypt of Cthulhu St. John's Eve 2022 , was released in July of that year. Selected Publications Crypt of Cthulhu (1984-2022) Revelations from Yuggoth (1987-1989) Tales of Lovecraftian Horror (1988-1999)</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Beast_of_Averoigne">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Beast of Averoigne</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>1369</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Abbot Theophile (host)</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The beast is an extraterrestrial creature, whose arrival coincided with the sight of a mysterious red comet in the year 1369, on the french province of averoigne . As an alien life form, the beast couldn't interact with anything on earth in its natural form . To be able to feed, it made itself tangible by inhabiting the body of a human host: the abbot theophile . Every night, the beast assumed control of the abbot and transformed him into a black</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Demonbane"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Beast_of_Averoigne"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Battle_that_Ended_the_Century">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Battle that Ended the Century</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The shadow out of time is a short story by h. p. lovecraft and r. h. barlow . It is a plethora of in-jokes and references to the members of the circle under their nicknames, alter egos or other humorous names . The story was originally privately circulated amongst the lovecraft circle .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#R._H._Barlow"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Humour</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Soul_of_Kephri">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Soul of Kephri</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Simon of gitta is a sword-and-sorcery story by richard l. tierney . It first appeared in the summer 1984 issue of space &amp; time (#66) the story is set in the spring of 34 a.d. in egypt, with a prelude on the italian island of capri .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_L._Tierney"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Space</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Summer 1984</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Time</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>June 1984</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Deadlands">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dimensions"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdfs:label>Deadlands</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Alternate Dimension Ruling Powers Dark Ones , shadow demon Notable Features mirror of material world but in shadow Shadow Demon, Human Souls (consumed or killed by evil) First appearance EXP : "Twist and Shout" (Ash vs Evil Dead, Season 3 Episode 7) Created by Caitlin Meares The is a fictional dimension in the Ash vs Evil Dead universe which first appeared in 2018 in the episode 7 of season 3, "Twist and Shout". Background The is the shadowy mirror version of the real world. Humans whom have been killed by Deadites or other evil beings or objects, end up in the where they are preyed upon by a shadow demon. It's where the Dark Ones were originally banished to from Earth.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>mirror of material world but in shadow</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Alternate Dimension</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Human Souls (consumed or killed by evil)</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Shadow Demon</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>Season 3 Episode 7)</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>Shout (Ash vs Evil Dead</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>Twist</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Victory">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#TechnologicalArtefacts"/>
    <rdfs:label>Victory</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Place of origin Liverpool, England Vehicle (Cargo ship) Transportation Real World Information H. P. Lovecraft First Appearance HPL : " The Temple " The was a British freighter torpedoed in the North Atlantic by the German submarine U-29 commanded by Karl Heinrich during the First World War. The ship was headed from New York to Liverpool and was sunk on the afternoon of June 18th, 1917. Survivors were allowed to leave the sinking ship in lifeboats and were then gunned down by Commander Heinrich. This incident was the catalyst for the supernatural events experienced by the crew of the submarine as told in the story " The Temple " by H. P. Lovecraft .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasFunctionA>Transportation</ex:hasFunctionA>
    <ex:hasPlaceOfOriginA>http://example.org/lovecraft#England Liverpool</ex:hasPlaceOfOriginA>
    <ex:hasTypeA>Vehicle (Cargo ship)</ex:hasTypeA>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnA rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Temple"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hazuth-Kleg">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Hazuth-Kleg</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A man named sliph approaches a locked gate looking for cathuria leaving from providence . He came across an elderly woman in black robes and a sack speaking of the stars . He ran off in fear and ends up following her advice to follow a star brighter than the others .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gary_Myers"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The House of the Worm</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1975</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sleep_of_the_Dead_%28Call_of_Cthulhu_LCG%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sleep of the Dead (Call of Cthulhu LCG)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sleep of the Dead Fantasy Flight Games Publication Date 2008 Living Card Game Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game Sleep of the Dead is an expansion pack for Fantasy Flight Games ' Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game . Overview The fifth Asylum Pack in the Dreamlands Cycle series of expansions, Sleep of the Dead contains 60 cards distributed in three sets of 20 unique cards. First released in 2008 and later revived through Fantasy Flights' in-house print-on-demand service, it was discontinued with the demise of Call of Cthulhu : The Card Game . Contents Sleep Therapist (Character; The Agency) Behind Bars (Event; The Agency) The Woman of His Dreams (Character; Miskatonic University ) Horrid Dreams (Event; Miskatonic University) Clover Club Executive (Character; Syndicate) Clover Club Cardroom, Parlor of Higher Stakes (Support; Syndicate) Princess Zura, The Flower of the Charnel Garden (Character; Cthulhu ) In an Unmarked Grave (Event; Cthulhu) Ward Phillips , Obsessed Recluse (Character; Hastur ) Dreams Within Dreams (Event; Hastur) Wandering Gug (Character; Yog-Sothoth ) Ascension to Zura (Event; Yog-Sothoth) Shuggob, Ghoul Sage (Character; Shub-Niggurath ) Mi-Go Dreams (Event; Shub-Niggurath) Inconspicuous Zoog (Character; Neutral) Basilisk (Character; Neutral) Richard Upton Pickman , Venerable Ghoul (Character; Neutral) Moon-Beast Captain (Character; Neutral) Moon-Beast Galley (Support; Neutral) The Sleep of the Dead (Event; Neutral) Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2008</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#M%C4%81na-Yood-Sush%C4%81%C4%AB">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Avatars"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Māna-Yood-Sushāī</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Chaosium is a creator deity who slumbers after creating the gods; if his drummer, skarl, ceases drumming for an instant, he would awaken and destroy the world . Created by lord dunsany in his anthology the gods of pegana .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Gods_of_Peg%C4%81na"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lord_Dunsany"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#August_Derleth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSFriendsAndAcquaintances"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsCoAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsCorrespondents"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>August Derleth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1971 July 4</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Author of weird tales and also a long series of regional and historical works set in his native Wisconsin. After Lovecraft’s death, he and Donald Wandrei founded the publishing firm of Arkham House to preserve Lovecraft’s work in book form. [The August Derleth Society is dedicated to preserving the memory of Derleth, Wisconsin’s most prolific author.]</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Derleth was a prolific writer in several genres, including historical fiction and detective fiction . Derleth was awarded a prestigious guggenheim fellowship in 1938 for his sac prairie saga . He was a contemporary and friend of h. p. lovecraft .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>August William Derleth</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>August W. Derleth</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Stephen Grenden</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Will Garth</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1909 February 24</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Lurker_From_the_Kelp_Forest">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Lurker From the Kelp Forest</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A man whose mother vanished in the waters of an island in the pacific ocean returns to the site to search for her corpse . He encounters a malevolent creature from the dreamlands which has been consuming the souls of unwary seamen and divers for unknown ages . After battling the creature and wounding it, he leaves the island and tries to ensure that nobody else will fall victim to it .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Michael Sheehan. Jr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Abominations: 24 Tales of the Cthulhu Mythos</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Horrors</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 2018</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Mountain_Walked_%28story%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CaitlinRKiernanWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Mountain Walked (story)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"a mountain walked" is a short story by caitlin r. kiernan . It first appeared in the september 2012 issue of sirenia digest . The title comes from a passage from h. p. lovecraft's "the call of cthulhu"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Caitlin_R._Kiernan"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sirenia_Digest"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>September 2012</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Afterlife_with_Archie">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crossovers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Afterlife with Archie</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Archie comic book is an ongoing series from archie comics that has 10 issues with 2 upcoming issues, which has been experiencing delays . By issue 6 sabrina has been admitted as a patient at a mental institution run by h. p. lovecraft .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Roberto Aguirre-Sacasa</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 2013 - present</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Garden">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Garden</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Magenta Nero English James Ward Kirk Publishing Lovecraft after Dark date November 2015  " " is a fairly long Lovecraftian poem by Magenta Nero . It was published in the James Ward Kirk Publishing thematic anthology Lovecraft after Dark in November 2015. Synopsis A first-person telling of how the unnamed narrator undergoes a horrific ordeal of pain and pleasure, one which brings their dead lover back to them, after a fashion…</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Magenta_Nero"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_after_Dark"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Acrewood">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Acrewood</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The call of poohthulhu is a lovecraftian short story by jude reid . Published in the april moon books anthology the call of poohthulhu . Bears little relation to a. a. milne's creations, barring a handful of re-used names .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Jude Reid</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Call_of_Poohthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>May 2022</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Queen_Of_The_Vultures">
