  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Michael_Fantina">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Michael Fantina</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Michael J. Fantina Connection to the Mythos Author Poet Birth Date 1 March 1946 Garrett, Indiana, United States United States Michael J. Fantina (1 March 1946 - present) is an American author and poet who has contributed a number of works to the greater Cthulhu Mythos begun with the works of Howard Phillips Lovecraft . These have been featured in publications such as the Edward P. Berglund -edited digital zine Nightscapes and the long-running fanzine Chronicles of the Cthulhu Codex . Selected Bibliography Short Fiction " The Ring of Azathoth " (2010) Poetry " Cthulhu " (1999) " Cthulhu Wakes " (1999) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>Indiana United States Garrett</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Michael J. Fantina</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Poet</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1 March 1946</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sunset">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sunset</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here H. P. Lovecraft United States English The Tryout date December 1917 " " is a short poem by the late American Howard Phillips Lovecraft . It was first published in the December 1917 edition of amateur press magazine The Tryout ; it has been reprinted numerous times in the years since, and has been included in an audio reading of Lovecraft's poetry . Synopsis As its title gives away, the three four-line verses of "" are an ode to the setting of the sun. History Beyond the Wall of Sleep (1943) Collected Poems (1963) The World of H. P. Lovecraft (1971) Fungi from Yuggoth and Other Poems (1971) The Californian: 1934-1938 (1977) The Ancient Track: The Complete Poetical Works of H. P. Lovecraft (2001, 2013) Fungi from Yuggoth: A Sonnet Cycle (2015) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Tryout"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 1917</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Fantasy_Fan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Fantasy Fan</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Charles d. hornig english monthly was a fan magazine published from 1933 to 1935 . It is remembered as the first outlet to publish " the other gods " (november 1933) and " from beyond " (february 1934) it was also the first publication to serialize the revised version of his supernatural horror in literature .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cliffourd_the_Big_Red_God">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Storybooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cliffourd the Big Red God</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft (original story) Kenneth Hite (adaptation) Andy Hopp English Genre(s) Children's Atlas Games Release Date July 2011 32 is a children's book, published by Atlas Games in 2011. It was written by Kenneth Hite and features illustrations by Andy Hopp. Part of the Mini Mythos series, the book is both an adaptation of H. P. Lovecraft 's " The Dunwich Horror " and a parody of Norman Bridwell's Clifford the Big Red Dog . Overview</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Kenneth_Hite"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Children's</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>32</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 2011</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Kuuko">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Kuuko</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Space Defense Agency</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthugha"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Cthuko</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Kūko</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Living Flame</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthugha is an extraterrestrial entity from the fictional universe of the haiyore! Nyaruko-san series . Was a childhood friend of nyaruko before they graduated from space school . Was the inspiration for the character " cthugha " from the cthulhu mythos .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Haiyore! Nyaruko-san Vol. 2</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Koin</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Manta Aisora</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Haunter_of_the_Dark">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Haunter of the Dark</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story takes place in providence, rhode island and revolves around the church of starry wisdom . The cult uses an ancient artifact known as the shining trapezohedron to summon a terrible being from the depths of time and space .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 1936</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Philip_Jermyn">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Philip Jermyn</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Royal Navy</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sir was the son of wade jermyn and the great-great-grandfather of arthur jermyn . Despite a strong physical resemblance to his father, his appearance and conduct were so coarse that he was universally shunned . He had a small frame but was very powerful and had incredible agility .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Facts Concerning the Late Arthur Jermyn</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>His Family</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Magah_Bird">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonSapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Magah Bird</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sociocultural Characteristics Resin groves in the Dreamlands Real World Information First appearance HPL : The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath Created by H. P. Lovecraft Magah birds , or simply magahs , are a species created by H. P. Lovecraft for his fantasy novel The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath . Description Native to the island of Oriab , in the Dreamlands , magah birds are brightly coloured creatures that nest in groves of resin trees, and sing in the mornings. Their bodies are prismatic, shining in seven different colours under the sun. ( HPL : The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath )</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dream-Quest_of_Unknown_Kadath"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Zarbi">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonSapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Zarbi</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Animus"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Vortis animus are an insectoid species on the planet vortis whom are often controlled by the animus . They are simple-minded creatures of little intelligence with no individuality . Their social structure is similar to that of earth's ants and they live in nests consisting of millions .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Web Planet</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Bill Strutton</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Circle_%28CthulhuTech%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cults"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Organisation"/>
    <rdfs:label>Circle (CthulhuTech)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationO>Nyarlathotep Chrysalis Corporation Children of Chaos</ex:hasAffiliationO>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Circle is a fictional organization in the cthulhutech pencil-and-paper roleplaying game by wildfire llc . It's members operate as assassins and spies for the chrysalis corporation . They are deeply involved in the black market in illegal magic trade . Doing business with them is dangerous as they are not trustworthy .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#M%C3%BCller">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Müller</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>1917 June</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Imperial German Navy</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>June, 1917 Alsace, France Navy Boatswain (Submariner) Imperial German Navy Human First appearance HPL : " The Temple " Created by H. P. Lovecraft Boatswain is a fictional character in the story " The Temple " by H. P. Lovecraft . He served as a boatswain aboard the German submarine U-29 under Lieutenant-Commander Karl Heinrich during the First World War. was an elderly man and described by Heinrich as a "superstitious Alsatian swine" (the region of Alsace, France being under German control during the time period of the story). During the supernatural events experienced by the crew, both and Seaman Zimmer went missing. They were presumed by Heinrich to have committed suicide by jumping overboard while the U-29 was surfaced, victims of the fear that overcame them.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Temple"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
