    <owl:inverseOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#isRealPersonMateOf"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_at_Martins_Beach">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Horror at Martin's Beach</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"the invisible monster" is a short story by h. p. lovecraft and sonia h. greene . It was written in 1922 and first published in weird tales (vol. 2, no. 4, 75–76, 83) a 50-foot-long creature was killed at sea after a lengthy 40-hour battle . Despite an extensive search, neither the wreck nor its cargo were ever found .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sonia_H._Greene"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Thing_in_the_Fridge">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Thing in the Fridge</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A constable in a rural english police station is called by the mayor's wife to deal with a monster in the fridge . When he arrives, he meets the mayor, and many other prominent local citizens, all part of a cult . They explain that the fridge is in fact a portal to the realm of great cthul</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Samuel Poots</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Shall Be</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Whispers from the Abyss Vol. 2: The Horrors That Are</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Monster_of_Voices:_Speaking_for_H._P._Lovecraft">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Monster of Voices: Speaking for H. P. Lovecraft</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Robert H. Waugh Cover Artist Philip Fuller English Genre(s) Non-fiction Hippocampus Press Release Date October 2011 384 is a collection of non-fiction works by H. P. Lovecraft scholar Robert H. Waugh. Running to a total of 384 and featuring cover art by Philip Fuller, it was published in a paperback format by Hippocampus Press in October 2011 . Overview Contents Introduction Further Sorties The Blasted Heath in “The Colour out of Space” Lovecraft and Lawrence on the Hidden Gods “The Rats in the Walls,” The Rats in the Trenches The Hounds of Hell, the Hounds of Heaven, and the Hounds of Earth The Ecstacies of “The Thing on the Doorstep,” “Medusa’s Coil,” and Other Erotic Studies Free Range Bloch and Leiber: The Siblings at War with Lovecraft, the Compound Ghost The Weird Historical Novel: Jonathan Strange &amp; Mr Norrell , The Case of Charles Dexter Ward , and Other Historical Ventures “Hey, Yew, Why Don’t Ye Say Somethin’?” Lovecraft’s Dramatic Monologues The Surreal and the Organic Imaginations: Lewis, Tolkien, and Lovecraft “An Exquisitely Low and Infinitely Distant Musical Note”: German Romantic Opera and Lovecraft’s Aesthetic and Practice Filiations and Affinities Lovecraft, a Citizen of Rome Lovecraft Speaks French, in a Manner of Speaking Lovecraft and O’Neill: The New England Haunts In Swan Point Cemetery Works Cited Index References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Robert H. Waugh</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>384</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 2011</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_House:_The_First_20_Years">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AugustDerlethWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Arkham House: The First 20 Years</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>August Derleth Cover Artist Frank Utpatel United States English Genre(s) Non-Fiction Arkham House Release Date 1959 Media Type Print 54 is a non-fiction work which, as it’s name might suggest, takes an in-depth look at the history of Arkham House 's first twenty years. Publication History Compiled by Lovecraft 's friend and contemporary August Derleth , was published in paperback format by Arkham House in 1959 in a run of only 815 copies. It has not been reprinted. The cover was designed by long-time Mythos artist and former correspondent of Lovecraft, Frank Utpatel . Trivia As can be seen from the front cover, was marketed with an RRP of only $1.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#August_Derleth"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-Fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>54</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1959</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nhll">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>N'h'll</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Void</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as The Void First appearance " Sweet Despise " ( EXP ) Created by Peter A. Worthy is a mysterious entity or force worshiped at least by one insane killer who escaped from Arkham Asylum . Information about is apparently only to be found in the Song of Yste . ( EXP : " Sweet Despise " (1997, Peter A. Worthy )</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sweet_Despise"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Peter_A._Worthy"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Out_of_the_Frying_Pan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Out of the Frying Pan</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Historywas first published in the anthology eldritch horrors: dark tales in 2009 . Appeared in the collection they thrive in darkness: mythos and madness in 2018 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Eldritch_Horrors:_Dark_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2009</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecrafter">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lovecrafter</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Overview is published as the official journal of the deutsche lovecraft gesellschaft e. v. (german lovecraft society) each issue contains essays, interviews, and reviews on the respective issue's focal topic . A significant section of the magazine features pen and paper content, including game materials and scenarios for either the call of cthulhu rpg or</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>German</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Nyarlathotep_Cycle:_The_Other_Omnibus">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Nyarlathotep Cycle: The Other Omnibus</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Troy Young English The Nyarlathotep Cycle Release Date June 2023 703 is a volume which collects the complete Nyarlathotep Cycle stories of Canadian Troy Young . Running to a total of 703 , it was self-published in a paperback format in June 2023. In addition to the core stories of the cycle, the book also contains two background stories, " The Happenings of December 13, 2012 " and " The Thing in the Customs House ", as well as two additional stories, "The Malign Rite" and "Knights of the Crescent, Knights of the Cross" . Overview Contents " It Came from the Sea " " It Slumbers Beneath the Ice " " It Lives in the Woods " " It Hides in the Village " " It Lurks in the Basement " " It Ends Where It Began " " The Rat King " " The Alien Gardener " " The Pyramid of the Black Pharaoh " " The Druid of Castle Rock " " The Viking's Doom " " The Tattered Lady " " The House at the Edge of Time and Space " " The Shade of Pierre Girault " " The Misplaced Masterpiece of Richard Upton Pickman " " The Curse of the Windsor Witch " " The Bride of the Yellow King " " In the Depths of R'lyeh " " The Happenings of December 13th, 2012 " " The Thing in the Customs House " "The Malign Rite" "Knights of the Crescent, Knights of the Cross" Gallery References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Troy_Young"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>703</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Nyarlathotep_Cycle"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>June 2023</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Kbaa_the_Serpent">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>K'baa the Serpent</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Relatives Family tree of Azathoth Yogash the Ghoul (parent) Ghoth the Burrower (spawn) H. P. Lovecraft (distant descendant) Offspring Ghoth the Burrower First appearance HPL : Selected Letters of H. P. Lovecraft 4.617 Created by H. P. Lovecraft is a mysterious character that is the descendant of Cthulhu and a distant ancestor of H. P. Lovecraft . It is unclear if there is any connection to Yig "The Father of Serpents". It is also unclear if has the tremendous power and abilities of other supernatural entities created by Lovecraft.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Selected Letters of H. P. Lovecraft 4.617</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Cultus_Issue_5">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Cultus Issue 5</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu Cultus #5 Various Cover Artist Tani Jantsang United States English P. Marsh Release Date January 1997 62 Preceded by Cthulhu Cultus #4 Followed by Cthulhu Cultus #6 Cthulhu Cultus #5 is an issue of sporadic American fanzine Cthulhu Cultus , edited by Tani Jantsang and James Ambuehl and published by P. Marsh in May 1996. Contents "Invocation from Beyond" by Crispin Burnham "Incident in Newark" by Tani Jantsang "Cthulhu's Island" by James Ambuehl and Arthur William Lloyd Breach "The Real Satchmo" uncredited " The Soul of the Devil-Bought " by Robert M. Price "Primogen" by Tani Jantsang "Behold the Wonders of the Cthonic World by Propaganda Minister of Yhe for Soft Intelligence" by Tani Jantsang "Shub-Niggurath Speaks" by Casey Ley</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>62</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>January 1997</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Yaggdytha">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ElderGods"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Yaggdytha</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Incandescent One</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as The Incandescent One Relatives Nodens (father) Vorvadoss (twin brother) First appearance EXP : " The Advent of Uvhash " Created by James Ambuehl ( The Incandescent One ) is twin brother of Vorvadoss , and first-born of Nodens , the Lord of the Abyss. is also associated with Bel Yarnak . It manifests as a great amorphous, incandescent ball of cyan living energy, spread itself into a web of giant talons of light. It is mentioned in the Book of Iod , The Celaeno Fragments , The Revelations of Glaaki , and an untitled work by Johannes Henricus Pott.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Advent_of_Uvhash"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Ambuehl"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Brown">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSRelatives"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>James Brown</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>23 November 1889</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Husband of Mary Lovecraft, whom he married on 2 March 1854. While in Ireland, James had two sons, Isaac and Adam, by Margaret Rountree, who died in 1836. James then moved to the United States, settling in Rochester by 1839, and finding work with the railroads. In 1846 he married Sarah Hurlburt, with whom he had four more children, Mary Jane, James, Saragh Anne (1851), and Saragh Anne (1853). When he finally met Mary Lovecraft, who was twenty-two years his junior, he was raising at least four children on his own.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isRelativeToR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1806</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Leng_%28short_story%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Leng (short story)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"leng" is a horror short story by marc laidlaw set in the mysterious plateau of leng . It was published in the 2009 anthology lovecraft unbound . Story consists of four entries in the narrator's journal documenting the second mycological survey of the leng plateau region .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Marc_Laidlaw"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>USA</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_Unbound"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2009</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ancient_One_%28Lovecraft_%26_Lumley%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ancient One (Lovecraft &amp; Lumley)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ancient One (Lovecraft &amp; Lumley) First appearance HPL : " The Diary of Alonzo Typer " Created by Howard Phillips Lovecraft and William Lumley An Ancient One is a humanoid that has squid-like features and exists only partly in the physical, material dimension.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Diary_of_Alonzo_Typer"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Howard_Phillips_Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#William_Lumley"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Zombie_Apocalypse">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Zombie Apocalypse</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>James Mendez Hodes Fred Hicks Kurt Komoda English Fate of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Evil Hat Productions Release Date 2022 32 is a timeline supplement for the Fate of Cthulhu horror roleplaying game. Written by James Mendez Hodes and featuring artwork by Fred Hicks and Kurt Komoda, it was published by Evil Hat Productions in a solely digital format in 2022 . Overview References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>James Mendez Hodes</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>32</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fate_of_Cthulhu"/>
