  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mortllgh">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Mortllgh</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Storm of Steel</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as Storm of Steel First appearance Kingdom of the Blind Created by Anthony Warren is an entity manifesting as a lustrous orb that floats at the center of a whirling vortex of razor-sharp, metallic-looking blades. Behind the Mythos is a fan fiction creation, listed in "The Encyclopedia Mythomagicka, Part 1". (source needed)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Kingdom of the Blind</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Anthony Warren</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Ithiliad">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Ithiliad</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The trojans inare half-breeds, some part deep one . Others the descendants of azathoth , shub-niggurath and other dark entities . The story was published in the thematic anthology world war cthulhu .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Christine_Morgan"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>August 2014</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Martense_Family">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtinctSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Martense Family</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Martense Kin</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Hairy men were once humans, but have devolved into a deformed cave-dwelling race due to decades of in-breeding . The only recorded martense to have left isolation was jan martense, who had joined the colonial army . In 1921, monster hunters came to catskill after sightings of the hairy men .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Worlds_of_H._P._Lovecraft:_The_Lurking_Fear"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Lurking_Fear"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_Remembered">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lovecraft Remembered</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>486 is a non-fiction collection edited by peter cannon and published by arkham house in 1998 . It contains the majority of the memoirs on howard phillips lovecraft from previous arkham house publications . Many of the memoirs are from those who were his friends or associates, including c. m. eddy, jr. , sonia h. greene , robert e. howard , clark ashton smith , frank belknap long , zealia bishop , hazel he</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-Fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>486</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1998</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gut_Shot_and_Coyote_Eaten">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gut Shot and Coyote Eaten</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Looking for darla is a short story by american author ron shiflet . It was first published in his collection looking for darla: stories of mythos noir in 2008 . The story is loosely connected to the cthulhu mythos .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Looking_for_Darla:_Stories_of_Mythos_Noir"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>September 2008</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Bruce_Brown">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Bruce Brown</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Comic book creator United States Cthulhu Mythos Notable Works Howard Lovecraft is an American comic book creator and author who has written a number of works set in the greater Cthulhu Mythos . Of these, perhaps the most notable to this wiki are his Howard Lovecraft graphic novels, a series which charts the adventures of a young H. P. Lovecraft . His Mythos works have to date been published exclusively through Arcana Studio. Selected Bibliography Comic Books " Carry Me Softly Into the Land of Dreams " (2010) Howard Lovecraft and the Frozen Kingdom (2010) Howard Lovecraft and the Undersea Kingdom (2012) Howard Lovecraft and the Kingdom of Madness (2013) Howard Lovecraft and the Three Kingdoms (2014) " Howard Lovecraft &amp; the Squid Kids " (2016) " Howard Lovecraft " (2018) Dr. Herbert West &amp; Astounding Tales of Medical Malpractice (2019) External Links at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Comic book creator</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isKnownForR>Howard Lovecraft</ex:isKnownForR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#David_A._Riley">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosInspiredAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>David A. Riley</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name David Andrew Riley Pseudonym(s) Ian Redfern Connection to the Mythos Author United Kingdom http://davidandrewriley.blogspot.com/  David Andrew Riley is a British author and editor who has written a number of stories set in the greater Cthulhu Mythos established with the works of Howard Phillips Lovecraft . These have appeared in collections of his own works such as The Lurkers in the Abyss and Other Tales of Terror and periodicals such as Lovecraftiana . Selected Bibliography " The Shadow by the Altar " (1983) " Lurkers " (2013) " Boat Trip " (2016) " The Psychic Investigator " External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>David Andrew Riley</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United Kingdom</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Ian Redfern</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://davidandrewriley.blogspot.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#For_Susannah">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>For Susannah</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Tom Lynch English Genre(s) Horror Ulthar Press Pickman's Gallery date 2018 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos story by American Tom Lynch . It was published in the Matthew Carpenter -edited anthology Pickman's Gallery by Ulthar Press in 2018. Synopsis A descendant of Richard Upton Pickman , himself a talented yet struggling artist, one evening meets a beautiful woman who inspires him to paint his greatest work ever. Creating a masterpiece which transcends time and space, he pours so much of himself into the canvas that it kills him, his aged body being discovered later, along with a note dedicating the piece to his muse.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tom_Lynch"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pickmans_Gallery"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2018</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Arkham_Sampler_Summer_1949">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AugustDerlethWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Periodicals"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Arkham Sampler Summer 1949</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Ronald Clyne United States English The Arkham Sampler Arkham House Release Date Summer 1949 100 Preceded by Spring 1949 Followed by Autumn 1949 was the sixth issue of short-lived magazine The Arkham Sampler . Edited by H. P. Lovecraft ’s friend and literary champion August Derleth and featuring cover art by Ronald Clyne, it ran to a total of 100 and was published by Arkham House . Contents "The One Who Waits" by Ray Bradbury "In the Year 2889" by Jules Verne "Hieroglyphics" by Vincent Starrett "Journey to the World Underground" (Part 1 of 2) by Lewis Holberg "Oblivion" by José-Maria de Heredia "The Door" by David H. Keller "Two Horsemen" by Vincent Starrett "The Giantess" by Charles Baudelaire "Lethe" by Charles Baudelaire "The Derleth Science-Fiction Collection" by Everett F. Bleiler "Ode to a Skylark" by Robert Bloch "More Caldecott" by Edward Wagenknecht "Poetry of Immortality" by John Haley ""American Dreams" and Utopias" by Everett F. Bleiler "Salem Again" by Robert Bloch "A Mixed Bag" by August Derleth Reviews by Everett F. Bleiler, Robert Bloch, Edward Wagenknecht, John Haley, August Derleth, and uncredited contributors External Links full issue online at the Internet Archive</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>100</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Arkham_Sampler"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>Summer 1949</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Satyr">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AveroigneCycle"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ClarkAshtonSmithWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Satyr</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Averoigne is a semi-historical french province . It first appeared in the july 1931 issue of la paree stories . It tells the story of an encounter with a nature spirit in the woods of averoigne .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Fantasy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Averoigne"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>La Paree Stories</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>July 1931</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkhams_Masters_of_Horror">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Arkham's Masters of Horror</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Harold Gauer Cover Artist Tony Patrick United States English Spanish Arkham House Release Date 2000 444 : A 60th Anniversary Anthology Retrospective of the First 30 Years of Arkham House is an anthology of horror and fantasy by the late American Peter Ruber . Running to a total of 444 , it was published in a hardcover limited run of 4,000 copies by Arkham House in 2000. A Spanish translation, titled Maestros del horror de Arkham House , was released by Valdemar in 2010 . Each story was prefaced by an introductory piece on the by Ruber, and attracted a degree of criticism from the community for the hostility displayed to some of the writers in these essays, which included the suggestions of H. P. Lovecraft being "a schizoid personality" and "a genuine crackpot" . Contents Foreword by Peter Ruber "Introduction: The "Un-Demonizing" of August Derleth" by Peter Ruber "Excerpts from the H. P. Lovecraft Letters to August Derleth" by H. P. Lovecraft "Prince Alcouz and the Magician" by Clark Ashton Smith "Man-Hunt" by Donald Wandrei "The Valley of the Lost" by Robert E. Howard "The Bat Is My Brother" by Robert Bloch "The Latch-Key" by H. Russell Wakefield "Dyak Reward" by Carl Jacobi "Sea-Tiger" by Henry S. Whitehead "The Dog-Eared God" by Frank Belknap Long, Jr. "The Beautiful Lady" by David H. Keller "Sweetheart from the Tomb" by E. Hoffmann Price "Wolf of the Steppes" by Greye La Spina "Rhythmic Formula" by Arthur J. Burks "The Small Assassin" by Ray Bradbury "George Is All Right" by Howard Wandrei "Something Old" by Mary Elizabeth Counselman "Property of the Ring" by John Ramsey Campbell "Bon Voyage, Michele" by Seabury Quinn "The Master of Cotswold" by Nelson Bond "Open Window" by Vincent Starrett "A Visitor from Outside" by August Derleth and Mark Schorer Gallery References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>444</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Spanish</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2000</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Ring_of_the_Demon_God">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Ring of the Demon God</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A small group of people use the power contained in a ring worn by of one of their number, the seal of nyarlathotep . The story culminates with a battle between nyarlathotep and a colossal entity known as the "dweller in the deep" nyarlathotep easily wins, before the group settle down to wait for rescue .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_Weinberg"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Nightscapes #1</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>June 1997</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mothers_Little_Helper">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Mother's Little Helper</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Amoral factory supervisor bud womack finds out a dirty little secret kept by one of his new staff . He attempts to blackmail the girl into embarking on a carnal relationship . However, what he has not counted on is that the girl is in fact a priestess of shub-niggurath .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Looking for Darla: Stories of Mythos Noir ]]</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>September 2008</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Till_A_the_Seas">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Till A' the Seas</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story consists of two parts: the first describes the events which took place on earth from a few thousand years after present to few millions years . The second part starts in a small village in the desert. Trying to reach the string, he falls into a well and dies .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#R._H._Barlow"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Science fiction short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pushing_Back">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Pushing Back</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story follows a detective and his interrogation of a man found covered in blood next to the body of his friend . As the interview progresses, it becomes apparent that the man has become infected by whatever entity killed his friend .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>J. C. Hemphill</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Whispers_from_the_Abyss"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2013</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Call">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Call</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Pete w. roncoli's flash fiction is published in the omnibus fall of cthulhu volume 3: the gray man by boom! Studios in december 2008 . It was one of two winning entries in a short story contest, the other having been submitted by roncoli's brother, david .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Pete W. Roncoli</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fall_of_Cthulhu_Volume_3:_The_Gray_Man"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 2008</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Horror_Fiction">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Horror Fiction</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Kieran Dacey Boylan English Rogue Planet Press Lovecraftiana Vol 5, Issue 2 date July 2020  " " is a Lovecraftian poem by Australian Kieran Dacey Boylan . It was published in Vol 5, Issue 2 of Lovecraftiana by Rogue Planet Press in July 2020. Synopsis The nine four-line verses describe a creature waiting just beyond the boundaries of reality, waiting to break through and destroy the human race.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Kieran_Dacey_Boylan"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Issue 2</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Lovecraftiana Vol 5</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>July 2020</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nhlathi">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>N'hlathi</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Real World Information First appearance EXP : The Transition of Titus Crow Created by Brian Lumley are a race of intelligent centipede aliens. Titus Crow sees their lair on Elysia ( EXP : The Transition of Titus Crow , Brian Lumley ).</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Transition_of_Titus_Crow"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
