  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Edward_M._Erdelac">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosInspiredAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Edward M. Erdelac</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Edward Michael Erdelac Connection to the Mythos Author Birth Date 28 September 1975 Hammond, Indiana, USA United States Horror Weird Fiction https://emerdelac.wordpress.com/  Edward Michael Erdelac is an American author, screenwriter and independent film director. He has written several stories set in the fictional Cthulhu Mythos inspired by the works of H. P. Lovecraft , and cites Stephen King , Richard Matheson, Robert E. Howard and Ambrose Bierce as some of his biggest influences in writing. Selected Bibliography Collections That at Which Dogs Howl and Other Lovecraftian Stories (2023) Short Fiction " Black Tallow " (2014) " The Boonieman " (2014) "The Unrepentables" (2015) "Beyond the Black Arcade" (2016) " The Allclear " (2017) " Head Like a Jar " (2022) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Indiana Hammond</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Edward Michael Erdelac</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://emerdelac.wordpress.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>28 September 1975</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#It_Came_from_Miskatonic_University">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>It Came from Miskatonic University</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Yves Tourigny Cover Artist Michael Bukowski English Broken Eye Books Release Date July 2020 238 Preceded by Welcome to Miskatonic University is an anthology of Lovecraftian fiction edited by C. Dombrowski and Scott Gable . Running to a total of 238 , it was published through Gable's small press Broken Eye Books in July 2020; paperback, hardcover and digital editions were all released. Overview Contents (Contents retrieved from Amazon's "Read sample" feature, 16 March 2024) "Identity Crisis" by Lynne Hardy "Fear of a Black Planet" by Tonya Liburd "Office Hours and After" by S.L. Edwards "Student Body" by Richard Lee Byers "Gills" by Jacqueline Bryk "Intermediate Yithian" by David Kammerzelt "Hashtag TPE" by Dawn Vogel "The Secret Trials of Oscar Bloom" by Chuck Regan "Mowbray's Museum" by Oliver Smith "The Librarian's Handbook" by Jennifer Brozek "Ordinary People" by Mary Berman "It Takes A Special Kind of Girl to Steal the Necronomicon" by Jill Hand "Between the Holes" by Dani Atkinson "The Kingdom of Is" by Matt Maxwell "The Last Observer" by Erica L. Satifka &amp; Rob McMonigal "My Miskatonic: A Who's Who of Arkham" by Matthew M. Bartlett</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>238</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 2020</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Insect_Philosopher">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Insect Philosopher</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sociocultural Characteristics The "fourth moon of Jupiter", possibly Callisto Real World Information First appearance HPL :" Beyond the Wall of Sleep " Created by H. P. Lovecraft The insect philosophers were a type of creature referenced by the late American author Howard Phillips Lovecraft in his short story " Beyond the Wall of Sleep "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Beyond_the_Wall_of_Sleep"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Uvhash">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Uvhash</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Blood-Mad God of the Void</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as The Blood-Mad God of the Void First appearance EXP : " The Advent of " Created by James Ambuehl , also known as The Blood Mad God of the Void , is a fictional entity created by American author James Ambuehl for his short Cthulhu Mythos story " The Advent of ". Description A gigantic, vampiric red mass of eyes and tentacles, is the archenemy of the being known as Gi-Hoveg . It dwells in the realm of Rhylkos, which is similar to the planet Mars , and it is said that whoever summons will meet with a terrible fate. seems to have affinities with the Star Vampires and is rumoured to have been one of the mad Roman Emperor Caligula's eldritch sponsors. History In the short story "The Advent of ", consumed the soul of a man who was attempting astral projection under the patronage of its arch-enemy Gi-Hoveg.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Advent_of_Uvhash"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Ambuehl"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gatsby_and_the_Great_Race">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gatsby and the Great Race</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Paul Fricker English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Chaosium Release Date October 2005 120 is a Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game scenario book written by Paul Fricker and published by Chaosium as part of their Monograph line of supplements. First released in October 2005 in a paperback format, it is currently out of print. Overview Taking place in a 1920s setting, is unusual for a roleplaying game scenario in that it is able to be played by a single small group, multiple groups playing individually with several keepers to keep the game coordinated, or a large group of up to 32 players. The plot involves the heir of a grand old mansion whose foolish actions have caught a group of partygoers in a Groundhog Day-style time loop. External Links at RPG.net review at DriveThruRPG Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Paul_Fricker"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>120</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 2005</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Note_on_Howard_P._Lovecrafts_Verse">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Note on Howard P. Lovecraft's Verse</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Rheinhart Kleiner United States English Genre(s) Non-fiction Arkham House Lovecraft Remembered Date of writing 1919 date 1998 " " is a short non-fiction piece by Howard Phillips Lovecraft correspondent Rheinhart Kleiner . Written in 1919, it did not see until 1998, making its first, and to date only, appearance in the 1998 Arkham House collection Lovecraft Remembered . References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Rheinhart_Kleiner"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_Remembered"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>1919</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Caligo_Accedendum_Tournament">
