  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Lammas_1984">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu Lammas 1984</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cover Artist Stephen Fabian United States English Crypt of Cthulhu Cryptic Publications Release Date August 1984 60 Preceded by St. John's Eve 1984 Followed by Michaelmas 1984 is the 24th issue of leading H. P. Lovecraft scholar Robert M. Price 's long-running Lovecraftian fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Edited by Price and featuring cover art by Stephen Fabian, it ran to a total of 60 in an A5 format and was published by Cryptic Publications in August 1984. This issue was a special dedicated to the late American author Richard L. Tierney , although Tierney had yet to pass at the time of publication. Contents Editorial Shards by Robert M. Price "The Howler in the Dark" by Richard L. Tierney " The Seed of the Star-God " by Richard L. Tierney "Simon of Gitta: A Chronology and Bibliography" by uncredited contributor Interview with Richard L. Tierney "The Nameless City" by Richard L. Tierney "Harag-Kolath" by Richard L. Tierney " The Derleth Mythos " by Richard L. Tierney Review by C. J. Henderson Letters by Robert Bloch , L. Sprague de Camp , Roger G. Knuth, Richard A. Frank, R. M. Calverley, and Steve Mariconda</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>60</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 1984</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Now_%28Call_of_Cthulhu%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Now (Call of Cthulhu)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu now is a sourcebook for chaosium's call of cthulhu horror roleplaying game . It was the first concerted attempt to update the basic game, set during the roaring twenties, to a contemporary setting . Both editions contain information on contemporary equipment, weaponry, skills, and scientific techniques . Although the books are long out of print, the original edition has been made available as a digital download .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1992 1987</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Whippoorwill">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonSapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SpeciesIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdfs:label>Whippoorwill</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Whippoorwills are a type of bird with a distinctive call and a feature of several stories set in the fictional cthulhu mythos . They were most notably present at the death of old whateley , his daughter lavinia , and his grandson wilbur . Behind the mythos lovecraft likely pulled this plot device from the native american superstition that whippoorwills were able to predict death .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chapelwaite"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Whippoorwills_in_the_Hills"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dunwich_Horror"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Ink_of_the_Slime_Lord">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Ink of the Slime Lord</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Short story first published in the 2017 rogue planet press anthology . Contains no direct references to the cthulhu mythos . Features lovecraftian entities in the form of cax, lord of slime, and the inmor .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Jason Ray Carney</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Swords_Against_Cthulhu_II:_Hyperborean_Nights"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>January 2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Spawn_of_the_Green_Abyss_%28story%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Spawn of the Green Abyss (story)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"spawn of the green abyss" is a short story by c. hall thompson . It is similar to the innsmouth leitmotif of sea dwelling monsters intertwining with humanity . A doctor falls in love with the daughter of a cursed former sailor . As the girl begins to hear the calling of her blood, she begs her husband to kill her .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#C._Hall_Thompson"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>German</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 1946</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Carl_Kolchak">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Carl Kolchak</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Kolchak is the protagonist of two made-for-television movies, one season of television episodes, a handful of novel and short story adaptations. And numerous comic books . Kolchak is the original creation of writer richard matheson, perhaps most famous for creating i am legend and other works of neogothic horror .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Richard Matheson</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Blackness_from_the_Stars">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Blackness from the Stars</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The blackness from the stars is an immobile blob of living, sentient darkness . It can enthrall nearby victims and make them slaves . Although intelligent, it speaks no known language and ignores attempts to communicate . Its only appearance is in chaosium's supplement king of chicago for their call of cthulhu rpg .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#King_of_Chicago"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gary_Sumpter"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Detective_Agency_%28organisation%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Organisation"/>
    <rdfs:label>Arkham Detective Agency (organisation)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Arkham detective agency is a fictional business created by american author c. j. henderson as a plot device for his arkham detective agency series of short stories . Former detectives frank nardi, tony balnco, mark berkenwald and a man whose name is only given as galtoni formed their own private investigations firm . In a bid to capitalise on the wealth of the local populace, the partners started to accept contracts with a more supernatural twist .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNotableMemberO>Frank Nardi Mark Berkenwald Tony Balnco (formerly)</ex:hasNotableMemberO>
    <ex:hasPurposeO>Business</ex:hasPurposeO>
    <ex:hasTypeO>Private Detective Agency</ex:hasTypeO>
    <ex:isLocatedAtO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham"/>
    <ex:isLocatedAtO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Massachusetts"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedBynO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#C._J._Henderson"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_St._Johns_Eve_1984">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu St. John's Eve 1984</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Peter H. Gilmore United States English Cryptic Publications Release Date June 1984 60 Preceded by Roodmas 1984 Followed by Lammas 1984 is the 23rd issue of leading H. P. Lovecraft scholar Robert M. Price 's long-running Lovecraftian fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Edited by Price and featuring cover art by Peter H. Gilmore, it ran to a total of 60 in an A5 format, and was published in June 1984 by Cryptic Publications . Interior art was provided by Price, Rodolfo A. Ferraresi, and Mike Mackenzie. This edition was a special devoted to essays and short fiction focusing on the various mystical grimoires which feature in the Cthulhu Mythos . Contents Editorial Shards by Robert M. Price "The Necronomicon, John Dee's Translation" by Frank Belknap Long "The Revelations of Glaaki" by Ramsey Campbell "Cthaat Aquadingen" by Brian Lumley "The Third Cryptical Book of Hsan" by Gary Myers "The Necronomicon: Concerning Them from Outside" by Lin Carter "The Book of Eibon: The Unbegotten Source" by Lin Carter "Confessions of the Mad Monk Clithanus: The Incantation of the Elder Sign" by Lin Carter "The Necronomicon: The Origin of a Spoof" by Colin Wilson "Preface to the Necronomicon" by L. Sprague de Camp "The Case of Simon's Necronomicon" by Robert C. Carey "Lovecraft's Necronomicon: An Introduction" by Robert M. Price "Reconstructing De Vermis Mysteriis" by Robert M. Price "Some Notes on the Eltdown Shards" by Robert M. Price "The Pnakotic Manuscripts: A Study" by Robert M. Price "Prehuman in Lovecraft" by Will Murray "Fun Guys from Yuggoth: From The Good News Necronomicon (Today's English Version)" by Steve Behrends Review by Robert M. Price Letters by Robert Bloch, L. Sprague de Camp, Brian Lumley, Patrice de G. Joubert, Richard L. Tierney , William Fulwiler , Darrell Schweitzer , Peter H. Gilmore, John Borkowski, Elmer R. Mudgett, Jeffrey Weinberger Trivia The cover features two lines of hieroglyphs, the top row of which reads "Crypt of Cthulhu", and the bottom row which reads "Great One, Serpent Demon, Terrible One" References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>60</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>June 1984</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Simon_Strantzas">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Simon Strantzas</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Birth Date 1972 Ontario, Canada Canada https://strantzas.com/ (born 1972) is a British Fantasy Award-nominated author of weird fiction from Ontario, Canada. He has written a number of works set in and inspired by both the Cthulhu Mythos and the Yellow Mythos . These have been included in anthologies such as Paula Guran 's New Cthulhu 2: More Recent Weird , Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. 's A Season in Carcosa , and S. T. Joshi 's Black Wings III and Black Wings IV . Strantzas has also been named, alongside Thomas Ligotti , Laird Barron , and John Langan, as one of the more notable authors of recent weird fiction by True Detective writer Nic Pizzolatto . Selected Bibliography Short Fiction "Beyond the Banks of the River Seine" (2012) "Thistle's Find" (2014) "On Ice" (2014) "In the Event of Death" (2015) External Links at Wikipedia at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>Canada Ontario</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>Canada</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://strantzas.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1972</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Steve_Lines">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Steve Lines</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>25 March 2021</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Pseudonym(s) Lucy Francis Haydon Atwood Prescott Connection to the Mythos Author Editor Artist Birth Date 1957 Death Date 25 March 2021 (1957 - 2021) was a prolific author, artist and editor who worked on many Cthulhu Mythos publications in all of these capacities. As an editor he frequently collaborated with John B. Ford , with whom he ran Rainfall Books . Lines occasionally wrote under the pseudonyms Lucy Francis and Haydon Atwood Prescott, although he appeared to use these exclusively for his poetry. Selected Bibliography Editor Inhabitants of Innsmouth (2006, with John B. Ford ) Tales of the Cthulhu Mythos: Cthulhu's Creatures (2007, with John B. Ford) Lovecraft's Disciples (2005-2020) E'ch Pi El (2006-2018) Author Dreams of a Diseased Mind (2000) " Dreams of a Diseased Mind " (2007) "Innsmouth Jewelery" (2006) "Song of the Deep One" (2006) Artwork Lovecraft's Disciples (2005-2016) Tales of Nephren Ka (2006) Inhabitants of Innsmouth (2006) Y'ha-nthlei Rising and Other Tales of the Deep Ones (2006) Tales of the Cthulhu Mythos: Cthulhu's Creatures (2007) In Lovecraft's Shadow (2007) See Also Images by External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Haydon Atwood Prescott</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Lucy Francis</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1957</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gordon_Walmsley">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gordon Walmsley</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Radcar Museum</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Professor , often known as of goole, is a fictional character created by brian lumley . Walmsley is an expert of translating ancient texts . In 1952, he studies a green figurine discovered in a yorkshire stream and dates it 10,000 years old .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Billys_Oak"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Heroes_and_Heroines_of_the_Shadow_War">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Heroes and Heroines of the Shadow War</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Oscar Rios Alberto Guerra Dan Smith English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Golden Goblin Press is a supplementary sourcebook for Chaosium 's Mythos roleplaying game Call of Cthulhu . Overview The investigators included are: Larcia Ayzebel, a congenial artisan Conri of Cait, a heroic auxiliary Sabina of Corduba, a quick-witted bathhouse attendant Malachi ben Zakai, a reliable bodyguard Vatinius Papaus, a noble centurion Tadius Lepidus, a kindhearted courier Livia the Dark, a charming courtesan Calvina Evodelia, an infamous entertainer Herminius Rusticus, a cunning finder Mennad the Mighty, a mighty gladiator Doreena Hermula, a mysterious huntress Atilius Calvus, an industrious laborer Tarquinius Sulla, an worldly merchant Olcia Nazarilla, a driven patrician Fadia Memorella, a secretive priestess Marcion of Corinthus, a learned scholar Drev of Narbo, a crafty slave Jacome of Toletum, a deadly slinger Curtius Fronto, a skilled surgeon Rosmunda of the Quadi, a confident thief Dulius Nasica, a brave vigilis Behind the Mythos Written by Oscar Rios with artwork provided by Alberto Guerra and Dan Smith, was published by licensed third-party content provider Golden Goblin Press .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Oscar_Rios"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Janaingo">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Janai'ngo</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Guardian</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Lobster of the Deep</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>the Key of the Watery Gates</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as Guardian and the Key of the Watery Gates The Lobster of the Deep First appearance EXP : "The Thing in the Library" Created by Crispin Burnham E. P. Berglund is a crustacean-like, tentacled, half-amorphous marine horror which serves Cthulhu , dwelling in the depths of the Bay of Rhiiklu, somewhere within the eastern coast of the United States .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Thing in the Library</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crispin_Burnham"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>E. P. Berglund</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#C._L._Moore">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsCoAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsCorrespondents"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>C. L. Moore</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1987 April 4</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Catherine lucille moore was an american science fiction and fantasy writer, who most often used the pen name . Her work paved the way for many other female speculative fiction writers . Her most famous story is " shambleau ", which was also her first professional sale .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>Indiana Indianapolis</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Catherine Lucille Moore</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>American</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1911 January 24</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
