  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Outsider">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Outsider</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A mysterious man who has been living alone in a castle for as long as he can remember decides to break free in search of human contact and light . Is one of lovecraft's most commonly reprinted works and is also one of the most popular stories ever to be published in weird tales . Combines horror, fantasy, and gothic fiction to create a nightmarish story, containing themes of loneliness, the abhuman, and the afterlife .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>April 1926</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>March-August 1921</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Battery">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Battery</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Two couple, the cejas and the stones, live peacefully in a cliff-side apartment building by the ocean . When one day a mysterious stranger arrives, the older couple reveal that they are guardians of a sort . Enlisting the help of the younger couple, the group awaken tsathoggua who slumbers beneath the rock, and the great old one contacts an even more powerful entity .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Michael_Shea"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Copping_Squid"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Other Mythos Tales</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2009</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shadow_Over_Innsmouth_%28Simon_Birks%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleComicBookAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Shadow Over Innsmouth (Simon Birks)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Shadow Over Innsmouth H. P. Lovecraft (original story) Simon Birks (adaptation) R. H. Stewart United Kingdom English Blue Fox Comics Release Date 2023 The Shadow Over Innsmouth is a comic book format adaptation of H. P. Lovecraft 's novella of the same name . Written for the format by Simon Birks and featuring artwork by R. H. Stewart, it was published by Blue Fox Comics in 2023; hardcover, paperback, and digital editions were all released. Overview Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Simon_Birks"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United Kingdom</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2023</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chuck_Buda">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Chuck Buda</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author New Jersey, USA United States https://www.authorchuckbuda.com/ is an American author and editor from Matawan, New Jersey, USA, who has written a number of stories set in the greater Cthulhu Mythos . At least two of these have been set in Armand Rosamilia 's Keyport milieu, a backdrop heavily influenced by H. P. Lovecraft 's Innsmouth . Buda and Rosamilia are regular collaborators, and the two co-host the Mando Method, a podcast addressing issues surrounding the process of writing and publishing . Selected Bibliography Short Fiction " The Terrible Old Man of Keyport " (2013) " Dark Waters of Sin " (2013) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA http://example.org/lovecraft#New_Jersey</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://www.authorchuckbuda.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Flying_Polyp">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Flying Polyp</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Aforplasm</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Bubbling Ones</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Flesh Twisters</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Floating Horrors</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>They Who Leave the Mark.</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Flying polyps are a race of extraterrestrial creatures . First appeared in h.p. Lovecraft's " the shadow out of time" rumours speak of the polyps also raising cities on three other planets .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>Unlike animals or plants</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>although they must walk</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>as where they went</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>cancerous with strange growths amidst multiple eyes</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>certain strange marks are left off a footprint consisting of five circular toe marks</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>despite a floating or hovering appearance</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>elements or portions seems to phase between visible</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>form a dark plastic flesh</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>invisible. No wings can be seen</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>jump</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>mouths. Semi-material</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>or crawl on invisible limbs</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>some 6 ft. in total lenght.</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>these entities twist</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shadow_Out_of_Time"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ogdru_Jahad">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ogdru Jahad</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ogdru_Hem"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Evil Winds</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Heralds of Pestilince</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Ogdru eb Jurhad</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Seven Gods of Chaos</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Beasts</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Dragon of Revelation</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Serpents</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Seven Who are One</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Windstorm</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Throne Bearers of Ereshigal</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The (aka, the dragons of revelation) are lovecraftian reptilian entities in the fictional universe of hellboy by dark horse comics . The are chaotic entities whose goal is subjugation and destruction. Beings whose appearance can induce madness .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Hellboy: Seed of Destruction</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mike_Mignola"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#River_Skai">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalLocations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>River Skai</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>River in the dreamlands originates on mount lerion . Flows through fertile plains, fields, and meadows, beautiful countryside, and peaceful farmland near the towns of ulthar, nir, and hatheg .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL>The Other Gods The Silver Key The Strange High House in the Mist The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath Through the Gates of the Silver Key</ex:hasAppearanceL>
    <ex:hasOriginL>Mount Lerion</ex:hasOriginL>
    <ex:hasTypeL>River</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dreamlands"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Cats_of_Ulthar"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dylan_Murphy">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dylan Murphy</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>Early 21st century</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>The Esoteric Order of Dagon</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A bald, heavyset man, dylan has a mean temper . He is the head of the keyport branch of the esoteric order of dagon and the eldest son of his family, at least the ninth to take the name of .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cabal"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Armand_Rosamilia"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Second_Cthulhu_Mythos_Megapack">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Second Cthulhu Mythos Megapack</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Shawn M. Garrett, editor Genre(s) Cthulhu Mythos Wildside Press Release Date November 1, 2019 485 Preceded by The Cthulhu Mythos Megapack is an ebook anthology published by Wildside Press in 2019, a follow-up to its Cthulhu Mythos Megapack (2012). The anthology features several of H. P. Lovecraft 's revision tales (including the overtly racist " Medusa's Coil ") and multiple contributions from Robert Bloch and Lin Carter . Most of the stories were written in the 1930s and '40s, with only five published since 1970. Contents "Introduction" by Shawn Garrett " Dreams of Yith " (poem) by H. P. Lovecraft and Duane W. Rimel (1934) " Out of the Aeons " by Hazel Heald and H. P. Lovecraft (1935) "Fishhead" by Irvin S. Cobb (1913) " When Chaugnar Wakes " (poem) by Frank Belknap Long (1932) The Mound by Zealia Bishop and H. P. Lovecraft (1940) " The Thing on the Roof " by Robert E. Howard (1932) " The Isle of Dark Magic " by Hugh B. Cave (1934) " The Secret in the Tomb " by Robert Bloch (1935) The Horror from the Hills by Frank Belknap Long (1931) "The Terrible Parchment" by Manly Wade Wellman (1937) " The Shambler from the Stars " by Robert Bloch (1935) " The Diary of Alonzo Typer " by H. P. Lovecraft and William Lumley (1938) " Hydra " by Henry Kuttner (1939) " The Suicide in the Study " by Robert Bloch (1935) "Marmok" (poem) by Emil Petaja (1940) "The Intruder" by Emil Petaja (1940) " Out of the Jar " by Charles R. Tanner (1941) "Skydrift" by Emil Petaja (1949) "Anonymous" by George T. Wetzel (1951) "Why Abdul Alhazred Went Mad" by D. R. Smith (1950) "Caer Sidhi" by George T. Wetzel (1954) " Dead of Night " by Lin Carter (1988) "Death of a Damned Good Man" by Avram Davidson (1991) " Medusa's Coil " by Zealia Bishop and H. P. Lovecraft (1939) " Perchance to Dream " by Lin Carter (1988) " The Winfield Heritance " by Lin Carter (1981) " The Challenge from Beyond " by Robert E. Howard, Frank Belknap Long, H. P. Lovecraft, A. Merritt , and C. L. Moore (1935) "The Last Horror Out of Arkham" by Darrell Schweitzer (1977) Trivia "Cthulhu" is misspelled as "Cthuhlu" on the book's cover.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Shawn M. Garrett</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>editor</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>485</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2019 November 1</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#To_a_Dreamer">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>To a Dreamer</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here H. P. Lovecraft United States English Genre(s) Horror Weird Tales Date of writing 1920 date November 1924 𝐓 " " is a Cthulhu Mythos poem by the late American Howard Phillips Lovecraft . Synopsis Consisting of six four-line stanzas which describe the narrator's encounter with a terrifying creature - seemingly masquerading as a man - previously seen only in his sleep: a denizen of the Dreamlands . History Written in 1920, "" did not see until it was featured in the November 1924 edition of pulp magazine Weird Tales , although it has appeared in a host of publications in the years since.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 1924</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>1920</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Red_Equinox">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SpectraFiles"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Red Equinox</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Becca philips is a disturbed young woman with a previously-unsuspected connection to dark forces . With the aid of her best friend and lover rafael moreno, adopted stray dog django, and spectra agent jason brooks, she thwarts the schemes of the old ones .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Douglas_Wynne"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>272</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>The SPECTRA Files</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>January 2015</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Count_Magnus">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Count Magnus</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story ofwas incorporated into the cthulhu mythos by brian lumley . Set around 1863, it describes the contents of a manuscript written by an english travel writer . The story describes the contents of a manuscript written by an english travel writer named wraxall .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#M._R._James"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Ghost-Stories of an Antiquity</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1904</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tcho-Tcho_people">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ServitorRaces"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tcho-Tcho people</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Tchortcha</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The are a group of tribes of near-human beings native to asia . Can be found in burma, the anderman islands, tibet, and malaysia . The name tcho means "destroyer" or "sorcerer"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The Horror in the Museum The Shadow Out of Time Black Man with a Horn When in Leng...</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Lair_of_the_Star-Spawn"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#August_Derleth"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_Schorer"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Idolaters_of_Cthulhu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Idolaters of Cthulhu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various English Alban Lake Publishing is an anthology of Lovecraftian fiction edited by H. David Blalock and published by Alban Lake Publishing. Overview Contents " Beyond The Wall Of Sleep " by H. P. Lovecraft PART THE FIRST: INCURSION "Fane of the Faceless God" by DJ Tyrer "Fatwa" by Amanda Hard "Slave To Unkind Gods" by Matthew Wilson "Names" by James Victor INTERMISSION "Sentry" by Herika R. Raymer PART THE SECOND: BATTLEGROUND "Sublime Architecture for the Proper Devotional Praise of Dagon" by Shenoa Carroll-Bradd "The Ones Who Remember" by Robert J. Krog "The Meat Junkies" by E. Dane Anderson "Breakwater" by Gregory L. Norris "Torn Asunder" by Michael Krog INTERMISSION "He Who Hesitates" by H. David Blalock PART THE THIRD: THE COMING "The Arms Of the Gods" by Jonathan Dubey "Cthulhu In Autumn" by Robin Wyatt Dunn "Dreaming Of A Darker Tomorrow" by Ben Stewart "Leap of Faith" by Tyree Campbell "Casual Blasphemies" by Harding McFadden "A Better View" by Brian Fatah Steele</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhus_aliases">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu's aliases</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Great Old One Cthulhu has gone by many names since its first appearance in Weird Tales magazine in 1928. While many of these are undoubtedly little more than alternate spellings for a name never intended to have been uttered by human vocal chords, a sizeable portion are entirely new monikers. This page offers a guide to the many and varied names of Cthulhu, arguably the most iconic of H. P. Lovecraft 's creations, and includes the avatars employed by this eldritch abomination. Lovecraft's Works Cthulhu (" The Call of Cthulhu ", 1928) Cthulhutl (" The Electric Executioner ", 1930) Clulu (" Winged Death ", 1934) Tulu ( The Mound , 1940) Lovecraft Circle Works Kthulhut (" Ubbo-Sathla ", 1933) Him Who Is to Come (" The House on Curwen Street ", 1944) Shooloo (" The House on Curwen Street ", 1944) Clooloo (" The Gorge Beyond Salapunco ", 1949) Kthulhu (" The Gorge Beyond Salapunco ", 1949) Kulu (" The Gorge Beyond Salapunco ", 1949) Him Who Lies Dreaming (" The Seal of R'lyeh ", 1957) Him Who Will Rise Again (" The Seal of R'lyeh ", 1957) Mark Dixon ( Strange Eons , 1978) Expanded Mythos B'Moth ( Call of Cthulhu , Ye Booke of Monstres II , 1995) Cathulhu ( The Real Ghostbusters , " The Collect Call of Cathulhu ", 1987) Chorazin ( Call of Cthulhu , Ye Booke of Monstres II , 1995) Cthulu ( The Grim Adventures of Billy &amp; Mandy , " Prank Call of Cthulu ", 2005) The Eternal Lord of Horror ( The Simpsons , "Treehouse of Horror XXIX", 2018) The Great C ( Demonbane , 2003-present) Herrscher of the Ocean ( Guns Girl Z , 2014-2021) Jyahnar ( Guns Girl Z , 2014-2021) Kathulhn (" The Guardian of the Book ", 1937) Kathulos (" The Strange Doom of Enos Harker ", 1997) Katsushika Hokusai ( Fate/Grand Order , 2015-present) Klulu (" The Strange Doom of Enos Harker ", 1997) Ktulu ( Metallica , "The Call of Ktulu", 1983) Kul ( Cthulhu Dark Ages , 2003) Kurumi Kuruya ( D.Y.N. Freaks , 2013) Kutulu ( The League of Extraordinary Gentlemen: Black Dossier , "On the Descent of the Gods", 2007) Kuzululu ( Housing Complex C , 2022) Leviathan ( Cthulhu Dark Ages , 2003) Lord of the Watery Abyss (" Out of the Ages ", 1975) Lotan ( Cthulhu Dark Ages , 2003) Reggie ( Census Issue 4 , 2023) Shagtanagotha ( The Shore , 2021) Spot ( Howard Lovecraft series , 2010-2018) Thu Thu Hmong ( Howard Lovecraft series , 2010-2018) Tiamat ( Cthulhu Dark Ages , 2003) Yamm ( Cthulhu Dark Ages , 2003) Unknown Source C'thlu C'thulhu Chullao Cighulu Cthullu Cuitiliú Cutlu The Great Dreamer The High Priest of the Great Old Ones Hlu-Hlu K'tulu Ka-Thoo-Loo Kathulu Khlul-Hliu Khulu Kulhu Kuthoolhew Kuthullhoo Kutunluu Nagji-ojingo Q'thulu The Sleeper of R'lyeh The Sleeping God The Star Spawn Tako-ika Thooloo Tornasuk Cthulhu Tu-Lu Xululú</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#De_Vermis_Mysteriis">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosBooksFictional"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdfs:label>De Vermis Mysteriis</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesA>Die geheimnisvollen Wurme</ex:hasAlternativeNamesA>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesA>Mysteries of the Worm</ex:hasAlternativeNamesA>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Hellboy is a fictional grimoire created by robert bloch, first mentioned in his cthulhu mythos story " the secret in the tomb " it is described as containing "spells and enchantments", particularly those that can summon strange entities . The last chapter of the book contains the formula for a drug that allows one to travel through space and time .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasFunctionA>Book</ex:hasFunctionA>
    <ex:hasPlaceOfOriginA>Brussels Duchy of Brabant</ex:hasPlaceOfOriginA>
    <ex:hasTypeA>Data Storage</ex:hasTypeA>
    <ex:hasTypeA>Transmission</ex:hasTypeA>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnA rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Secret_in_the_Tomb"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByA rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ludvig_Prinn"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Howard_Lovecraft_%28Arcana_Studios_series%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#StoryCycles"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Howard Lovecraft (Arcana Studios series)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Howard lovecraft is a series of cthulhu mythos comic books created by bruce brown and published by arcana studio . Despite the setting and names, many of the elements incorporated into the series have been modified to such an extent that they are nearly unrecognisable .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dreidel_of_Dread:_The_Very_Cthulhu_Hanukkah">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Storybooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dreidel of Dread: The Very Cthulhu Hanukkah</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here Alex Shvartsman English Genre(s) Children's Galaxy's Edge Magazine date September 2015 is a Cthulhu Mythos short story, originally written by Alex Shvartsman for the 16th issue of Galaxy's Edge Magazine . It was later published as a children's book by UFO Publishing in 2024, featuring illustrations by Tomeu Riera. Synopsis After being awoken from his eons-long slumber, Cthulhu initiates the apocalypse on the night before Hanukkah. Meanwhile, Hanukkah Henry sets his sights to make a great modern story for his holiday by saving the world from the incoming disaster.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Alex Shvartsman</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Children's</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Galaxy's Edge Magazine</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>September 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tales_of_Nyarlathotep">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CrossroadPress"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tales of Nyarlathotep</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various English Crossroad Press Release Date September 2023 407 is a thematic anthology of Lovecraftian fiction. Running to a total of 407 , it was published by Crossroad Press in September 2023; both paperback and digital editions were released. It is part of the Books of Cthulhu series. Overview Contents Foreword "The Black Pharaoh's Test" by CT Phipps "Cinderella and her Outer Godfather" by CT Phipps "Where the River Ends" by David J. West "The Apophis Sarcophagus" by David Hambling "Andrew Doran and the Curse of Nephren-Ka" by Matthew Davenport "Blackwood Relic" by Andrea Pearson "Cookies for the Gentleman" by CT Phipps "Coolidge and the Enchanted Dagger" by Andrea Pearson "In Its Shadow" by Eric Malikyte "Dream Math" by Matthew Davenport "The Siege of New Ulthar" by CT Phipps "All the Way Up" by David Niall Wilson "The Final Judgment" by CT Phipps Trivia "All the Way Up" contains the last appearance of Titus Crow , with permission granted by Brian Lumley to use the character.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>407</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>September 2023</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nemesis_%28Fall_of_Cthulhu%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nemesis (Fall of Cthulhu)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Hadron</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The D'harin</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Nemesis is a character created by michael alan nelson for his fall of cthulhu series of mythos comic books . He made his first appearance in the very first issue of fall of cthulhu , titled "harlot" although he played little part in the events of the main story arc, his character being fleshed out somewhat in the prequel spin-off fall of cthulhu volume 6: nemesis</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fall_of_Cthulhu_Volume_1:_The_Fugue"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>issue The Harlot</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Michael_Alan_Nelson"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Vincent_Chong">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Vincent Chong</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Illustrator Birth Date 9 April 1981 Stockport, United Kingdom United Kingdom Fantasy Science Fiction Horror Years Active 2004-present http://vincentchong-art.co.uk/ is a UK-based illustrator and graphic designer who specialises in the fantasy, science fiction and horror genres. He has won a World Fantasy Award and several British Fantasy Awards for his work, and has provided artwork for a number of authors, including Stephen King . Selected Work Covers World War Cthulhu (2014) Return of the Old Ones: Apocalyptic Lovecraftian Horror (2017) Tales from Arkham Sanitarium (2022)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>Stockport United Kingdom</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United Kingdom</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://vincentchong-art.co.uk/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:hasYearsActiveR>2004-present</ex:hasYearsActiveR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Illustrator</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>9 April 1981</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Aiueb_Gnshal">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OuterGods"/>
    <rdfs:label>Aiueb Gnshal</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Outer Gods</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Outer space Outer Gods First appearance EXP : " The Eyes Between the Worlds (RPG Module) " Created by Kevin T. McKinnon &amp; Dylan K. Sharpe (The Eyes Between Worlds) is a mysterious Outer God which has made his home in a long lost temple in Bhutan . He appears as a formless black void with seven orb-like eyes. It is mainly worshipped by Ghouls who worship him in a defiled cult spoken of in the Cambuluc Scrolls of the wizard Lang-Fu . Looking into the eyes of this horrid being, after a disgusting and awful ritual, will allow you to see into the Court of Azathoth . It is said that the Mongolian warlord Temujin , better known as Genghis Khan, had the favour of .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Eyes Between the Worlds (RPG Module)</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Dylan K. Sharpe</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Kevin T. McKinnon</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#On_Lovecraft_and_Life">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>On Lovecraft and Life</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>R. H. Barlow Cover Artist Jason C. Eckhardt United States English Genre(s) Non-fiction Necronomicon Press Release Date October 1992 25 is a non-fiction chapbook by Howard Phillips Lovecraft 's friend and correspondent R. H. Barlow . Overview Running to only 25 , collects two of Barlow's previously-unpublished works concerning his late friend, and includes an introduction by leading Lovecraft scholar and critic S. T. Joshi . It was published posthumously by Necronomicon Press in October 1992 with cover art supplied by Jason C. Eckhardt . Contents " " " Autobiography "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#R._H._Barlow"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>25</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 1992</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Mark_of_Yig">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Mark of Yig</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A brilliant man has an obsession with expanding the consciousness . Decides that the best way to do this is to suppress the base of the brain . Experiments go horribly wrong and results in the deaths of two orderlies . Decides that perhaps it would be better to enhance the reptilian part of the brain, and experiments on himself .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_M._Price"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The Black Book</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>August 2002</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shadow_Resurrection">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Shadow Resurrection</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lee Clark Zumpe David Hopewell English Rogue Planet Press Lovecraftiana Vol 5, Issue 3 date October 2020 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos story by authors Lee Clark Zumpe and David Hopewell . It was published in Vol 5, Issue 3 of the Lovecraftiana zine in October 2020. Synopsis The story follows the decades-long interaction of two families with the aquatic species known as Deep Ones , led by one "Mr. Dagon ". It simultaneously tells of the Deep Ones' efforts to annihilate the human race through "weaponised sex", an attempt to breed humanity out of existence through mass-mating with human females.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#David_Hopewell"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lee_Clark_Zumpe"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Issue 3</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Lovecraftiana Vol 5</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2020</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Darkman_Vs._Army_of_Darkness">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Darkman Vs. Army of Darkness</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Kurt Busiek, Roger Stern George Perez, James Fry III, Nick Bradshaw English Dynamite Entertainment Release Date Feb 2007 - May 2007 Media Type Comic book is a 4-issue mini series written by Kurt Busiek and Roger Stern in 2007 for Dynamite Entertainment. Synopsis Darkman's former lover Julie discovers the Necronomicon and out of curiosity reads it, unleashing the Deadites on Detroit. As citizens become possessed and the dead rise, Darkman and Ash Williams battle the Army of Darkness while trying to free the possessed Julie, now calling herself the Queen of Darkness.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Kurt Busiek</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Roger Stern</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>Feb 2007 - May 2007</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#George_Rogers">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>George Rogers</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>http://example.org/lovecraft#Rhan-Tegoth</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Rogers is the main antagonist of h. p. lovecraft's revision story " the horror in the museum " rogers runs a wax museum of horrors in the southwark borough of london . He insists that some of the figures on display are actual specimens of eldritch creatures.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_in_the_Museum"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Black_Wings_II">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Black Wings II</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>New Tales of Lovecraftian Horror S. T. Joshi (editor) Cover Artist Jason Van Hollander English Black Wings Genre(s) Lovecraftian Horror PS Publishing Release Date June 2012 321 Preceded by Black Wings: New Tales of Lovecraftian Horror Followed by Black Wings III : New Tales of Lovecraftian Horror is an anthology of original Lovecraftian horror , edited by S. T. Joshi and published by PS Publishing in 2012 as a sequel to Black Wings: New Tales of Lovecraftian Horror (2010). It was reprinted by Titan Books as Black Wings of Cthulhu 2: Eighteen Tales of Lovecraftian Horror in 2014. The anthology was followed by four more Black Wings volumes. Contents "Introduction" (essay) by S. T. Joshi "When Death Wakes Me to Myself" by John Shirley "View" by Tom Fletcher " Houndwife " by Caitlín R. Kiernan "King of Cat Swamp" by Jonathan Thomas "Dead Media" by Nick Mamatas "The Abject" by Richard Gavin "Dahlias" by Melanie Tem "Bloom" by John Langan "And the Sea Gave Up the Dead" by Jason C. Eckhardt "Casting Call" by Don Webb "The Clockwork King, the Queen of Glass, and the Man with the Hundred Knives" by Darrell Schweitzer "The Other Man" by Nicholas Royle "Waiting at the Crossroads Motel" by Steve Rasnic Tem "The Wilcox Remainder" by Brian Evenson "Correlated Discontents" by Rick Dakan "The Skinless Face" by Donald Tyson "The History of a Letter" by Jason V. Brock "Appointed" by Chet Williamson</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>S. T. Joshi (editor)</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Lovecraftian Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>321</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Black_Wings"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>June 2012</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Doom_that_Came_to_Dunfield">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Doom that Came to Dunfield</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Home from the sea: tales of lovecraftian terror is a short story by the british-born author . A think-tank for the british government develops what they believe to be the technology to render a battleship invisible . Upon arrival, the first test is a failure, with the machine only running for a few seconds before shutting down . Soon thereafter, the strange occurrences begin . The walls and decks of the ship become semi-liquid at random, swallowing</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#William_Meikle"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Home_From_The_Sea:_Tales_of_Lovecraftian_Terror"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>April 2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dunwich_Horror_and_Other_Weird_Tales">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Dunwich Horror and Other Weird Tales</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft English Council on Books in Wartime Release Date 1945 384 is a collection of H. P. Lovecraft stories published in 1945 as an Armed Services Edition for the entertainment of United States military personnel during World War II. The cover described the contents as "selected ghost stories". Contents "Introduction" " The Dunwich Horror " " In the Vault " " The Rats in the Walls " " Pickman's Model " " The Music of Erich Zann " " The Colour Out of Space " " The Outsider " " The Call of Cthulhu " " The Whisperer in Darkness " " The Shadow Over Innsmouth " " The Moon-Bog " " The Hound " External Links "Armed Services Editions" at Wikipedia "Armed Services Editions" at JimBooks.com</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>384</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1945</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shoggoth_Apocalypse_%26_More_Tales_Of_The_Cthulhu_Mythos">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Shoggoth Apocalypse &amp; More Tales Of The Cthulhu Mythos</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mark McLaughlin Michael Sheehan, Jr. English Genre(s) Horror Release Date 2017 68 is a collection of short stories by authors Mark McLaughlin and Michael Sheehan, Jr. Overview Consisting of five stories and running to a total of 68 , is a digital-only collection available through Amazon Kindle. It was released in 2017. Contents " The Curse Of The Tillinghasts " " In The Chapel Of Ghattambah " " This Pulsing Mass That Serves As My Heart " " The Colossus In The Catacombs " " Shoggoth Apocalypse "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Michael Sheehan. Jr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>68</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2017</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Ambrel">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>James Ambrel</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Author Human First appearance EXP : " The Star-Seed " EXP : " De Marigny's Clock " Created by James Ambuehl is a self-insert character of prolific Cthulhu Mythos author and editor James Ambuehl . Overview Although little is known of Ambrel, he appears to be an author specialising in occult and paranormal literature. It is possible that he lives in, or near to, Braving, Minnesota . Fictional Bibliography Tales of Braving and its Insidious Environs ( EXP : " The Star-Seed ") Ritualistic Practices in the Cult of Byagoona ( EXP : " De Marigny's Clock ")</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>De Marigny's Clock</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Star-Seed"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Ambuehl"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Call_of_Cthulhu_Supplements">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Call of Cthulhu Supplements</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Chaosium's call of cthulhu supplements include: 1920s investigator's companion, alone against the dark, alone against the static, alone against the tide, alone against the wendigo and many more . The supplements are published by chaosium .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Suicide_in_the_Study">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Suicide in the Study</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story is notable as the first appearance of the mythos tome cultes des goules . The count's name is a play on august derleth 's name, which has misled some into thinking that derleth was the originator . The story is also the first mention of luveh-keraph , a fictional priest of bast from ancient egypt --whose name is of course based on h. p. lovecraft .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_Bloch"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Lovecraftian Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Damballah">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Damballah</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>He was created in 1975 by roy . Thomas, gerry conway, and john buscema and made his first appearance in issue #152 of the 1st volume of the . Avengers comic book series . When in spirit form, can travel to earth where it manifests as a humanoid serpent or a huge snake .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Avengers Vol 1 #152</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Roy_Thomas"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Gerry Conway</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>John Buscema</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tuomas_Saloranta">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tuomas Saloranta</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Pseudonym(s) Johannes Sohlman Stig-Peter Lund Connection to the Mythos Mythos Contributor Birth Date 1977 Finnish (born 1977) is a Finnish author who has written several short horror stories which take place in the Cthulhu Mythos . One of his most notable short stories was 2012's Mahtavat Ammoiset (Finnish for Great Ancient One or Great Old One ) which features concepts from both the mythos and H. P. Lovecraft 's real life. He has used the pseudonyms Johannes Sohlman and Stig-Peter Lund. Works Keväistä kauhua lenkkipolulla (Translation: Spring Horror on the jogging track ) from 2009 Mahtavat Ammoiset (Translation: Great Old Ones ) from 2012 Pyörätuolimummo (Translation: Wheelchair-grandma ) from 2011 Sammakkoprinssi (Translation: Frog Prince ) from 2012 Viimeinen kaukopartiomies (Translation: The Last Guerrilla ) from 2011 Rontit from 2011 Kari Tenho Väyrysen tapaus (Translation: The Case of Kari Tenho Värynen ) from 2012 Länsimetro viivästyy (Translation: The Western Metro is delayed ) from 2011 Äidin talossa (Translation: In the house of Mother ) from 2009 Kaksi kesäyötä Bodominjärvellä (Translation: Two nights at Lake Bodom ) from 2009</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>Finnish</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Johannes Sohlman Stig-Peter Lund</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Mythos Contributor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1977</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Life_for_Humanitys_Sake">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Life for Humanity's Sake</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here H. P. Lovecraft United States English Genre(s) Non-fiction Necronomicon Press Uncollected Prose &amp; Poetry Volume 2 Date of writing October 1920 date July 1980 " " is a short non-fiction work by the late American Howard Phillips Lovecraft . Written in October 1920, it was first published in the Necronomicon Press collection Uncollected Prose &amp; Poetry Volume 2 in July 1980 . History Uncollected Prose &amp; Poetry Volume 2 (1980) Miscellaneous Writings (1995) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Poetry Volume 2</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Uncollected Prose</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>July 1980</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>October 1920</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Peril_of_Liberated_Objects,_or_the_Voyeurs_Seduction">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CaitlinRKiernanWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Peril of Liberated Objects, or the Voyeur's Seduction</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Caitlin R. Kiernan English Subterranean Press Confessions of a Five-Chambered Heart date July 2012 "The Peril of Liberated Objects, or the Voyeur’s Seduction" is a short story by Caitlin R. Kiernan that first appeared in their collection Confessions of a Five-Chambered Heart ( Subterranean Press , 2012). It tells the story of an unnamed book that escaped from the Dreamlands that "reads the fondest nightmares and most repressed appetites of those who gaze into it and subsequently shows them what they have always longed to see." History The story later appeared in Kiernan's collection Houses Under the Sea: Mythos Tales (Subterranean Press, 2019).</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Caitlin_R._Kiernan"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Confessions of a Five-Chambered Heart</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>July 2012</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Return_of_the_Sorcerer">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ClarkAshtonSmithWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Return of the Sorcerer</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story is set in the fictional cthulhu mythos . It was first published in 1931, then received an ebook issue with wildside press's the cthulhu mythos megapack in 2012 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror Short Fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Strange Tales of Mystery</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Terror</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>September 1931</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
