  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ramsey_Campbells_Goatswood_and_Less_Pleasant_Places">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ramsey Campbell's Goatswood and Less Pleasant Places</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Scott David Aniolowski Gary Sumpter Et al United States English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Chaosium Release Date July 2001 244 is a sourcebook for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game. Written by Scott David Aniolowski , Gary Sumpter , et al, it was published in a 244-page paperback format in July 2001; it is currently out of print. Overview The book includes eight playable scenarios, plot hooks, spells and artefacts, and a bestiary, as well as a look into the people and places of the Severn Valley. Scenarios "The Watcher Out of Time" "Cross My Heart, Hope to Die" "Unpleasant Dreams" "Blessed Be" "Silent Scream" "Of Dreams and Dark Waters" "Third Time's The Charm" "Gothic" Trivia The title of the book is a nod to Ramsey Campbell's first published collection, The Inhabitant of the Lake and Less Welcome Tenants . Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gary_Sumpter"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scott_David_Aniolowski"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Et al</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>244</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 2001</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Cthulhu_Stories_of_Robert_E._Howard">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RobertEHowardWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Cthulhu Stories of Robert E. Howard</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Robert E. Howard Cover Artist Sofyan Syarief English Genre(s) Cthulhu Mythos WordFire Press Release Date 2019 is a collection of Howard 's Cthulhu Mythos -related fiction, published by WordFire Press in 2019. The stories are compiled by Scott Lee and presented with a foreword by Paul Di Filippo . Contents "Tentacles, Terrible Tomes, and Broadswords" foreword by Paul Di Filippo " The Shadow Kingdom " (1929) Skull-Face (1929) " The Children of the Night " (1931) " The Gods of Bal-Sagoth " (1931) " The Black Stone " (1931) " People of the Dark " (1932) " Worms of the Earth " (1932) " The Thing on the Roof " (1932) " The Haunter of the Ring " (1934) " The Challenge from Beyond " (1935) (by C.L. Moore , A. Merritt , H.P. Lovecraft , Robert E. Howard &amp; Frank Belknap Long ) " The Fire of Asshurbanipal " (1936)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_E._Howard"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2019</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#David_Barker">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>David Barker</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Editor Horror is an author, poet and one-time editor. Overview Specialising in short horror fiction, has made a number of contributions to the Cthulhu Mythos inspired by the works of H. P. Lovecraft . In addition, he was also the founder of Lovecraftian horror fanzine Midnight Shambler , and although he departed as editor after only two issues, he continued to contribute a column called Ye Olde Lemurian ; after Midnight Shambler ceased publication, Barker took the column to The Audient Void , another small press horror zine. Selected Works Editor Midnight Shambler (1988) Short Fiction "The Stone of Ubbo-Sathla" (1997) "Old Lovecraftians in the Park" (2018) Poetry "Out of Arkham Hills" (2016) "Innsmouth Shanty" (2017) "Altar of Yig" (2019)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#L._Sprague_de_Camp">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>L. Sprague de Camp</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>De camp is credited with the first usage of "extraterrestrial" as a noun with the meaning of "alien life" he also coined the abbreviation "e.t." In his article "design for life" de camp worked for many years editing and writing fiction in continuation of robert e. howard's conan cycle .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Han">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Han</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Yig"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Dark One</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as The Dark One Yig First appearance CIRCLE : " The Shambler from the Stars " Created by Robert Bloch is a fictional entity created by the late American author Robert Bloch for his 1935 short story " The Shambler from the Stars ." Overview Little is known of the enigmatic entity known as . Composed of a freezing, howling mist, it is bound to Yig 's worship. He guards a gigantic endless maze, which holds a relic inside that belongs to Yig. He is also associated with Byatis as part of one of the three "gods of divination".</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shambler_from_the_Stars"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_Bloch"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tracy_Ambuehl">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tracy Ambuehl</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Poet Weird fiction is an author and poet who has contributed a number of works to the Cthulhu Mythos begun with the works of H. P. Lovecraft . Although unknown at this time, a relationship to or with American author James Ambuehl , with whom she has written several works, seems likely. Selected Works Short Fiction "Beast of Love" (1995, with James Ambuehl ) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Poet</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chamber_of_Chills_Vol_1_3">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleComicBookAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Chamber of Chills Vol 1 3</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Chamber of Chills Vol 1 #3 United States English Marvel Comics Release Date December 5, 1972 Media Type Comic book Chamber of Chills Vol 1 #3 is an issue of Marvel Comics ' horror anthology series. It features an adaptation of Robert E. Howard 's short story " The Thing on the Roof ", written by Roy Thomas and illustrated by Frank Bunner. External Links at the Marvel Database wiki</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1972 December 5</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#J._Todd_Kingrea">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Critics"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>J. Todd Kingrea</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Artist Games designer United States United States https://www.jtoddkingrea.net/ is an American author, pastor, film critic, artist, and games designer. His first published works were articles and story modules for the now-defunct Call of Cthulhu fanzine The Unspeakable Oath , followed by contributions to official Call of Cthulhu works such as Ramsey Campbell's Goatswood and Less Pleasant Places . He has since branched out into the world of short fiction and novels, and his stories have been published in anthologies including Made in Goatswood and Return to Lovecraft Country . Selected Works Short Fiction "Cross My Heart, Hope to Die" (1995) Gaming Supplements Dead Reckonings (1998, contributor) Ramsey Campbell's Goatswood and Less Pleasant Places (2001, contributor) The Resurrected III: Out of the Vault (2002, contributor) Artwork Creatures &amp; Cultists (1990, card game) Crypt of Cthulhu (1994, Lammas issue) See Also Images By External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>United States</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://www.jtoddkingrea.net/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Uzuldaroum">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Uzuldaroum</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAccessibilityL>Road</ex:hasAccessibilityL>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Hyperborea is a city created by the late american author and lovecraft contemporary clark ashton smith for his hyperborean cycle series of fantasy fiction . It was built after the citizens of commoriom were forced to abandon their former home, became the new capital of hyperborea in the days of that great civilisation .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL>At the Mountains of Madness The Testament of Athammaus</ex:hasAppearanceL>
    <ex:hasTypeL>City-state</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Humans</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hyperborea"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Tale_of_Satampra_Zeiros"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dark_Brotherhood">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AugustDerlethWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Dark Brotherhood</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft &amp; August Derleth United States English Genre(s) Horror Arkham House Publication date 1966 " " is a short story by H. P. Lovecraft and August Derleth . One of the controversial " posthumous collaborations " between the two, it was in fact written almost entirely by the latter, based on a fragment by the former. Synopsis A pair of friends whose habit is to explore the city by night meet a mysterious stranger who appears to share their enjoyment of after-dark walks. As their relationship develops, the woman is beguiled by the stranger's charm while the man uncovers a cult who are scheming to draw something deadly to Earth. Publication History "" was first published in the collection and Other Pieces by Arkham House in 1966.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#August_Derleth"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1966</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Trans-Galactic_Empire">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Organisation"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OrganisationsOriginatingFromMythosInspiredWorks"/>
    <rdfs:label>Trans-Galactic Empire</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationO>The Dweller Beneath</ex:hasAffiliationO>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The (tge) is a totalitarian regime in the three galaxies, which is controlled from the shadows by an incredibly powerful colossal alien intelligence . Each of the kreeghor emperors has been chosen by the dweller beneath and bestowed with superhuman powers . The elite of the empire are the royals, a super powered caste of kreeghor . Serving in the war machine are conscripts and volunteers whom come from all of the</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeO>Intergalactic empire</ex:hasTypeO>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Can_Such_Things_Still_Be%3F:_Complete_Literary_Guide_to_the_King_in_Yellow">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Can Such Things Still Be?: Complete Literary Guide to the King in Yellow</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Can such things still be? - complete literary guide to the king in yellow is a collection of cthulhu mythos -related works . It was compiled by khurt khave and published by the first united church of cthulhu in september 2016 . Despite its title, it is not a book of literary criticism, but for the most part a collection of previously-published material .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>135</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>September 2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Evil_Dead_2:_Revenge_of_Dracula">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Evil Dead 2: Revenge of Dracula</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Scott Peterson Jacob Bascle, Horacio Domingues English Space Goat Publishing Release Date 2017 Media Type Comic book is a one-shot comic book written by Scott Peterson in 2017 which was published by Space Goat Publishing. Synopsis Dracula is revived by the Ex-Mortis , and Ash Williams tries to send him back to the grave. Only for Dracula to reveal to him that the book revived a greater threat, one that forces the two to join forces against it.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Scott Peterson</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2017</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#I_Saw_the_Light">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>I Saw the Light</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Is a short story by american and games designer greg stolze . First published in the lovecraft -inspired anthology whispers from the abyss .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Greg_Stolze"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Shall Be</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Whispers from the Abyss Vol. 2: The Horrors That Are</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraftiana_Vol_1,_Issue_2">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Periodicals"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lovecraftiana Vol 1, Issue 2</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Gav Roachdown Antoni Espinosa Pahko Khannea SunTzu Cover Artist Gav Roachdown English Rogue Planet Press Release Date July 2016 38  is an issue of the periodical Lovecraftiana , edited by Gavin Chappell and published by Rogue Planet Press in July 2016. Contents "Strange Days Indeed" by John C Adams "Diplomacy" by Gary Budgen "A Muezzin from the Tower of Darkness" by Gavin Chappell "The Swelling" by DJ Tyrer The Darkest of the Hillside Thickets (Review) by Stephen Hernandez "The Sweetness of the Drainage" by Norbert Gora</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>38</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#N_%28anthology%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>N (anthology)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>N Various Cover Artist Simon Brake English Stygian Fox Release Date May 2021 165  N is a thematic anthology of Lovecraftian fiction released by British small-press Stygian Fox in May 2021; it has to date only been issued in a digital format. It was edited by Simon Brake, who also provided the cover art, and focuses on themes and concepts related to the Outer God Nyarlathotep . Overview Contents "The Innsmouth Girl" by Glynn Owen Barrass "Cult of the Black Swine" by Tim Curran "Hate" by Adam Gauntlett "The Yuggoth Club" by John Linwood Grant "The Voice on the Line" by Jo Kreil "Nyarlathotapp" by John Houlihan " He Shall Put on the Semblance of Man " by Jeffrey Thomas "Waking from the Deepest Dream" by Rhian Waller "I Am the Last" by Eygló Karlsdóttir</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>165</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 2021</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Wonderland:_Asylum">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Wonderland: Asylum</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Patrick Shand Tony Brescini English Zenescope Entertainment Release Date Jan 2014-May 2014 Media Type Comic book is a five-issue comic book mini-series written by Patrick Shand in 2014 which was published by Zenescope Entertainment. Synopsis Deep within Alice Liddle 's broken mind, lies the Jabberwocky who hungers to break free and unleash his evil on the rest of the world.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Patrick Shand</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>Jan 2014-May 2014</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Red_Eye_of_Azathoth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Red Eye of Azathoth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Malcolm McClinton English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Open Design LLC Release Date 2011 is a scenario supplement for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu cosmic horror roleplaying game. It was published by Open Design LLC in 2011 . Overview Contents Prologue "That Which is Dead Shall Refuse to Lie" "The Silence of Thousands Shall Quell the Refrain" "Fires of Hatred Defile the Sky" "Lost Shall Be Those Bearing Souls Split in Twain" "And Madness Shall Rise to Devour the West" Design Team Written by: Chad Bowser , Tim Connors, Eileen Connors, Michael Furlanetto, Andi Newton, and Ted Reed Artwork by: Malcolm McClinton, Edward Reed, Cynthia Sheppard, Hugo Solis, Herwin Wielink, Callie Winters, and Kikuchi Yosai Production staff: Wolfgang Baur and John D. Rateliff Gallery References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2011</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_In_The_Water_Tower_%26_Five_More_Tales_Of_The_Cthulhu_Mythos">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Horror In The Water Tower &amp; Five More Tales Of The Cthulhu Mythos</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mark McLaughlin Michael Sheehan, Jr. English Genre(s) Horror Release Date March 2016 59 is a collection of short stories from authors Mark McLaughlin and Michael Sheehan, Jr. Overview Running to a total of 59 , was released in a digital format in March 2016. Two of the stories are continuations of H. P. Lovecraft 's 1933 short story " The Dreams in the Witch House ". Contents " The Horror in the Water Tower " " The Last Witch-House " " The Hungry One Must Feed " " The Slimy Ones " " Uncle Caesar " " Coins From Y'ha-nthlei "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Michael Sheehan. Jr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>59</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>March 2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Letters_to_Lovecraft">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Letters to Lovecraft</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Daniel Solis English Stone Skin Press ( Pelgrane Press imprint) Release Date December 2014 280 : Eighteen Whispers to the Darkness is an anthology of horror fiction in which each story uses a quote from H. P. Lovecraft 's " Supernatural Horror in Literature " as a prompt. Edited by Jesse Bullington , it was published by Pelgrane Press through their Stone Skin Press imprint in December 2014; both print and digital editions were released. Overview Contents Introduction by Jesse Bullington "Past Reno" by Brian Evenson "Only Unity Saves the Damned" by Nadia Bulkin "_____" by Paul Tremblay "Allochthon" by Livia Llewellyn "Doc's Story" by Stephen Graham Jones "The Lonely Wood" by Tim Lebbon "Help Me" by Cameron Pierce "Glimmer in the Darkness" by Asamatsu Ken "The Order of the Haunted Wood" by Jeffrey Ford "Only the Dead and the Moonstruck" by Angela Slatter "That Place" by Gemma Files "The Horror at Castle at the Cumberland" by Chesya Burke "Lovecrafting" by Orrin Grey "One Last Meal, Before the End" by David Yale Ardanuy "There Has Been a Fire" by Kirsten Alene "The Trees" by Robin D. Laws "Food From the Clouds" by Molly Tanzer "The Semi-Finished Basement" by Nick Mamatas</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>280</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>December 2014</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Hastur_Cycle">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Hastur Cycle</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu mythos: 13 tales that created and define dread hastur, the king in yellow, nighted yuggoth, and dire carcosa is a thematic anthology by chaosium as part of their cthulhu cycle of fiction . Each of the stories had previously published and features a short introduction by editor robert m. price . A second revised edition was issued in june 2006, with all new cover art by h. e. fass</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Cycle"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 1993</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#World_War_Cthulhu:_Cold_War">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>World War Cthulhu: Cold War</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various English Call of Cthulhu (Role-Playing Game) Genre(s) Roleplaying game Cubicle 7 Entertainment Release Date 2016 is a period setting supplement for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu horror roleplaying game. Featuring contributions from a wide range of games designers, it was published by third-party content provider Cubicle 7 Entertainment in 2016 . Overview Designers Scott Dorward Martin Dougherty Jason Durall Paul Fricker Andrew Kenrick Mike Mason Matt Nixon Nick Robinson Matthew Sanderson Ken Spencer Alasdair Stuart References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Call_of_Cthulhu_%28Role-Playing_Game%29"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fred_Behrendt">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Fred Behrendt</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Games designer Author is a games designer and occasional author. He has written material for several of Chaosium 's Call Of Cthulhu roleplaying game supplements, as well as at least two short stories inspired by the works of H. P. Lovecraft . Behrendt's story "Beauty" appeared in the anthology Made in Goatswood: New Tales of Horror in the Severn Valley (1995); his "In the Times After" was included in Return to Lovecraft Country (1997). Selected Works Gaming Supplements Blood Brothers (1990, contributor) External Links at The Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Vigilant">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#TechnologicalArtefacts"/>
    <rdfs:label>Vigilant</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Place of origin Valparaíso, Chile Used by The Morrison Co. Vehicle Goods transportation Real World Information H. P. Lovecraft First Appearance HPL : " The Call of Cthulhu " The is a freighter owned by the Morrison Co. which originated in Valparaíso, Chile. It appears in H. P. Lovecraft 's seminal short story " The Call of Cthulhu ". History En route to Sydney , the crew of the sights the yacht Alert , populated by two remaining members of the schooner Emma , Gustaf Johansen and William Briden , the latter of whom has been dead for several days. tows Alert to Sydney, where the yacht is sold for commercial use.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasFunctionA>Goods transportation</ex:hasFunctionA>
    <ex:hasPlaceOfOriginA>Chile Valparaíso</ex:hasPlaceOfOriginA>
    <ex:hasTypeA>Vehicle</ex:hasTypeA>
    <ex:isUsedByA>The Morrison Co.</ex:isUsedByA>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnA rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Call_of_Cthulhu"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Paul_Wade-Williams">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Paul Wade-Williams</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Games Designer United Kingdom Paul "Wiggy" Wade-Williams is a British role-playing game designer and co-founder of Triple Ace Games. Known for his prolific work on Shane Lacy Hensley's Savage Worlds and Jeff Combos' Ubiquity , he has also written for Cubicle 7 Entertainment 's Cthulhu Britannica line of Call of Cthulhu supplements. Selected Works Gaming Supplements Avalon: The County of Somerset (2010) Leagues of Cthulhu (2017) Leagues of Cthulhu: Cthulhu Codicil (2019) Leagues of Cthulhu: Guide to Cumbria (2019) Leagues of Cthulhu: Guide to Afghanistan (2020)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United Kingdom</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games Designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Garuda_Bird">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonSapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Garuda Bird</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Garuda birds are a species of extra-dimensional avian which feature in the call of cthulhu roleplaying game . They reside in the dreamlands and occasionally cross over into tibet's western regions .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Secrets_of_Tibet"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Jason Williams</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Screams_from_Within_%28Call_of_Cthulhu_LCG%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Screams from Within (Call of Cthulhu LCG)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Screams from Within Fantasy Flight Games Publication Date 2010 Living Card Game Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game Screams from Within is an expansion pack for Fantasy Flight Games ' Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game . Overview The fifth Asylum Pack in The Yuggoth Contract Cycle series of expansions, Screams from Within contains 60 cards in the standard division of three sets of 20 unique cards. First released in 2010, it was discontinued with the demise of Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game . Contents Border Patrol Guard (Character; The Agency) Police Headquarters (Support; The Agency) Sledgehammer (Support; The Agency) River of Serpents (Support; Cthulhu ) Yig , Father of Serpents (Character; Cthulhu) Dangerous Inmate (Character; Hastur ) Thing from Nightmare (Character; Hastur) Cultist Journal Sketches (Support; Miskatonic University ) Professor Smith, Alone With His Secrets (Character; Miskatonic University) M.U. Museum of Natural History (Support; Miskatonic University) Archaeological Dig Site (Support; Miskatonic University) Losing the Tail (Conspiracy; Neutral) Riding Shotgun (Event; Neutral) Chupacabra (Character; Shub-Niggurath ) Feeding Time (Event; Shub-Niggurath) Kidnapping 101 (Event; Syndicate) A Sight for Sore Eyes (Event; Syndicate) Blind Fighting (Event; Syndicate) Yog-Sothoth , Lord of Time and Space (Character; Yog-Sothoth) Spiritual Guidance (Event; Yog-Sothoth) Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2010</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Zorro:_Rise_of_the_Old_Gods">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crossovers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Zorro: Rise of the Old Gods</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Jason Pell Michael William Kaluta, Puis Calzada English American Mythology Productions Release Date Sept 2019 - January 2020 Media Type Comic book is a 4-part miniseries written by Jason Pell which in 2019 was published by American Mythology Productions. The story features the Great Old One Cthulhu and his army of Deep Ones . Synopsis</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Jason Pell</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>Sept 2019 - January 2020</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Haunter_of_the_Dark">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Avatars"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Haunter of the Dark</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Worshiped by the Church of Starry Wisdom</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>the Cult of the Sand Bat</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Father of All Bats</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The haunter is an avatar of nyarlathotep who appears in the homonymous story by h. p. lovecraft . He is worshiped by the church of starry wisdom and the cult of the sand bat . His body is composed of a corrosive substance capable of dissolving human skin on contact .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shadow_from_the_Steeple"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Haunter_of_the_Dark"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Lovecraft_eZine">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Audio"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SmallPress"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Lovecraft eZine</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Current Status Active 2011 Mike Davis Lovecraftian horror https://lovecraftzine.com/ is a Lovecraftian community and resource which operates a weekly podcast and micro-press, as well as hosting free fiction. It was by Mike Davis in 2011. Press Press published a regular digital magazine between February 2011 and January 2016; the press has since moved to Kindle and paperback publishing, but still offers the magazine's original run for free on the . The press has published work by many contemporary Cthulhu Mythos authors, including Pete Rawlik , Mike Minnis , William Meikle , Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. , Michael Cisco , Jeffrey Thomas , and many others. Selected Output Periodicals (2011-2016) Anthologies Autumn Cthulhu (2016) Lovecraftian Tales: Stories of Weird Fiction and Cosmic Horror (2017) Collections 2011 Megapack (2011) 2012 Megapack (2013) Presents Making the Cut (2014) The King in Yellow Tales: Volume 1 (2015, Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. ) The Peaslee Papers: A Lovecraftian Chronicle (2017, Pete Rawlik ) Carnacki: The Edinburgh Townhouse and Other Stories (2017, William Meikle ) Rules for Monsters (2021, Michael Minnis ) Novels Whispers (2016, Kristin Dearborn) Nightmare's Disciple (2016, Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. ) Non-Fiction The Lurking Chronology: A Timeline of the Derleth Mythos (2015, Pete Rawlik ) External Links Press at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://lovecraftzine.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraftiana">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lovecraftiana</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Gavin Chappell English Rogue Planet Press 2016 https://horrifiedpress.wordpress.com/  : the Magazine of Eldritch Horror is a fanzine published by Rogue Planet Press , an imprint of Horrified Press . It features traditional Mythos works such as Lovecraftian short stories, poetry and art. appears at least annually with some intermittently published issues bearing a numerical index as well as yearly volumes named for Candlemas, Lammas and so on. The zine is known for including stories from established contemporary weird fiction authors, as well as showcasing works from new or more obscure talent. List of Issues (incomplete) Vol 1 Issue 1 (2016) Issue 2 (2016) Issue 3 (2016) Issue 4 (2017) Vol 2 Issue 1 (2017) Issue 2 (2017) Issue 3 (2017) Issue 4 (2018) Vol 3 Issue 1 (2018) Issue 2 (2018) Issue 3 (2018) Issue 4 (2019) Vol 4 Issue 2 (2019) Vol 5 Issue 1 (2020) Issue 2 (2020) Issue 3 (2020) Issue 4 (2021) Vol 6 Issue 3 (2021) Vol 8 Issue 2 (2023) Collections The Best of (2023)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ulthar_%28Elder_God%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ElderGods"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ulthar (Elder God)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Elder Gods</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Uldar</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Ultharathotep</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ulthar Also known as Uldar, Ultharathotep Elder Gods First appearance EXP : " The Sussex Manuscript " Created by Fred L. Pelton Ulthar (Uldar, Ultharathotep) is a deity that was sent to Earth to keep watch over the Great Old Ones .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Sussex_Manuscript"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fred_L._Pelton"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gloon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gloon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Great Old Ones</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Glhuun</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Master of the Temple</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Corrupter of Flesh</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The corrupter of flesh , master of the temple is a fictional deity created by the late american author h. p. lovecraft for his short story " the temple " description is described as great limacene or slug-like abomination, though it hides its true nature through the glamour of a beautiful statue resembling the greek god dionysus .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The Infernal History of the Ivybridge Twins</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Temple"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Zushakon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Zushakon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Bringer of Darkness</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Lord of Darkness</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Old Night</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Dark Silent One</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Zu-Che-Quon</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Zulchaquon</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The name is also given as zul-che-quon (by west coast indian tribes) and (in mu and xinian) is an alternative spelling of zulchequon used by the elder hierophant yogmosh-voth in mu and it is also used in the book of iod . Logic suggests that is the "father" of cyäegha .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The Descent into the Abyss Dead of Night</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Bells_of_Horror"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Henry_Kuttner"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books_of_Cthulhu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CrossroadPress"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Books of Cthulhu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The series is a dedicated anthology series by multiple authors and published by crossroad press . Each volume is dedicated to a different mythos creature or concept and are usually linked by some larger story thread or object . The stories are notably on the pulpier vein with the authors frequently going into action horror .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Byron_Craft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#David_Conyers"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#David_Hambling"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#David_Niall_Wilson"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Matthew_Davenport"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tim_Mendees"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Andrea Pearson</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>C.T. Phipps</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>David J. West</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Eric Malikyte</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>John DeLaughter</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Action Adventure</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cosmic Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Fantasy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Post-Apocalypse</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Urban Fantasy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Strangers_Die_Every_Day">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Strangers Die Every Day</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Historyis a short story by american author cody goodfellow . It was published in the thematic anthology return of the old ones: apocalyptic lovecraftian horror in 2017 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cody_Goodfellow"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
