  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Fleece_of_Yaggar">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Fleece of Yaggar</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story tells of how, after the golden-fleeced offspring of shub-niggurath , yaggar, is killed, the vengeful god summons the being known as xathagorra to exact his revenge .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>F. C. Adams</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Translations_from_the_Book_of_Yng"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Nightscapes #9</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>September 1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Littlest_Lovecraft:_The_Shadow_Over_Innsmouth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Storybooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Littlest Lovecraft: The Shadow Over Innsmouth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft (original story) Tro Rex (adaptation) Eyona Bella English Genre(s) Children's Esoteric Order of Publishing Release Date October 1, 2015 64 is a children's book, published by the Esoteric Order of Publishing in 2015. It was written by Tro Rex and illustrated by Eyona Bella, as an adaptation of H. P. Lovecraft 's The Shadow Over Innsmouth . Overview</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Tro Rex</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Children's</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>64</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2015 October 1</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Celestial">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Celestial</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The celestials are a cosmic race of "space gods" in the fictional universe of marvel by marvel . The celestials experiment on lifeforms, creating sub-species with superhuman powers . They implant eggs / embryos in the cores of many worlds, which take may take millennia to gestate .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ancient:_A_Keyport_Cthulhu_Tale">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ancient: A Keyport Cthulhu Tale</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Armand Rosamilia United States English Keyport Cthulhu Genre(s) Weird fiction Rimfyre Books Release Date July 2012 27 is a Cthulhu Mythos chapbook by American Armand Rosamilia . Overview First released by Rimfyre Books in July 2012 through Amazon's digital Kindle platform, contains two short stories (one co-written with Rosamilia's daughter Katelynn) and runs to a total of 27 . Contents " Ancient " " Cthulhunicorn " (with Katelynn Rosamilia)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Armand_Rosamilia"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>27</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Keyport_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 2012</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Darkest_of_the_Hillside_Thickets">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosInspiredWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Darkest of the Hillside Thickets</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The band was formed in 1992 by lead singer and main songwriter toren atkinson and lead guitarist warren banks . Members live in british columbia's vancouver, abbotsford, and chilliwack . Their music blends melodious punk and rock .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Kenneth_Sterling">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsCoAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Kenneth Sterling</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1995</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Kenneth sterling was an american science fiction author best known for his collaboration with lovecraft in the short story " in the walls of eryx " he was a young man when when he first crossed paths with lovecraft in 1934 . The story was rewritten and published after lovecraft's death in the weird tales of october 1939 . As of 1952, sterling had become a physician and was involved in cancer research .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>American</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1920</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu:_The_Mythos_and_Kindred_Horrors">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RobertEHowardWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu: The Mythos and Kindred Horrors</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Robert E. Howard ; David Drake, ed. Cover Artist Steve Hickman English Genre(s) Cthulhu Mythos Baen Books Release Date May 1987 247 is a collection of writings by Robert E. Howard , edited by David Drake and published by Baen Books in 1987. It was the first compilation of Howard's work to focus on his contributions to the Cthulhu Mythos . The volume includes three of Howard's poems, including one--"Silence Falls of Mecca's Walls"--published for the first time therein. Contents "Introduction" (by David Drake) "Arkham" (poem) (1932) " The Black Stone " (1931) " The Fire of Asshurbanipal " (1936) " The Thing on the Roof " (1932) " Dig Me No Grave " (1937) "Silence Falls on Mecca's Walls" (poem) (1987) " The Valley of the Worm " (1934) " The Shadow of the Beast" (1977) " Old Garfield's Heart " (1933) " People of the Dark " (1932) " Worms of the Earth " (1932) " Pigeons from Hell " (1938) "An Open Window" (poem) (1932)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_E._Howard"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>David Drake</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>ed.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>247</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 1987</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Big_Book_of_Cults">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Big Book of Cults</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Overview details six very different cults which can be found in the world of the cthulhu mythos in the modern age . The organisations include: the church of a thousand tomorrows, neo-nazis from the uk and the golden chalice, a cult of pleasure .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Ben Counter</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>100</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2005</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Time_to_Harvest">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Time to Harvest</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Brian M. Sammons Glynn Owen Barrass Mike Mason Lynne Hardy Various Cover Artist Caleb Cleveland United States English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Chaosium Release Date 2021 336 is a campaign supplement for the Call of Cthulhu horror roleplaying game written by Brian M. Sammons , Glynn Owen Barrass , Mike Mason , and Lynne Hardy , it was published by Chaosium in 2021. Cover art was supplied by Caleb Cleveland , with interior work from Dan Barker, Alberto Bontempi, Alicia Cardell, Cleveland, Emanuele Desiati, Fifa Finsdottir , Victor Leza, Pat Loboyko, Ernest Mason, Loïc Muzy , Alessandro Patria, Lee Simpson, April Solomon, Mali Ware . Overview Gallery References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_M._Sammons"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Glynn_Owen_Barrass"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lynne_Hardy"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mike_Mason"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>336</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2021</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Nights:_Tales_of_Mythos_Noir">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Arkham Nights: Tales of Mythos Noir</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Glynn Owen Barrass Ron Shiflet Cover Artist M. Wayne Miller United States English Genre(s) Horror Noir Celeano Press Release Date September 2016 is a collection of Cthulhu Mythos short stories by Glynn Owen Barrass and Ron Shiflet . Overview First published by Celeano Press in September 2016 with cover art by M. Wayne Miller , Arkham Nights assembles a series of stories involving their gritty detectives Barnes and Towers as they confront supernatural forces lurking just out of sight of the daytime world. The stories are, as the title might suggest, an amalgamation of the noir fiction genre with elements of the Cthulhu Mythos. Contents "Cthulhu's Big Sleep" "A Man Called West" "Big Boss" "The Lady in Yellow" "Redemption" "The Kingsport Desk" "The Glass Jaw" "Skin Flick"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Glynn_Owen_Barrass"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Noir</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>September 2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gemini_Rising">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gemini Rising</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Brian McNaughton English Genre(s) Cthulhu Mythos Release Date 1977 256 is a Cthulhu Mythos novel by Brian McNaughton , originally published in 1977 by Carlyle Books as Satan's Love Child . A revised version was released under McNaughton's preferred title in 2000 by Wildside Press. Synopsis When a reporter is assigned to cover cultists that appear in her neck of New Jersey , it stirs buried memories of her own teenage encounter with a cult.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_McNaughton"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>256</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1977</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Orphanage_at_Trellaree">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Orphanage at Trellaree</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The howling terror and other lovecraftian horror stories was first published in 2022 by weird house press . It is a short story set in the greater cthulhu mythos . The accompanying artwork is a black-and-white painting by k. l. turner .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tony_Richards"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Other Lovecraftian Horror Stories</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The Howling Terror</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2022</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Leprechaun">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SpeciesIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdfs:label>Leprechaun</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sociocultural Characteristics Ireland Real World Information First appearance CIRCLE : The Moon Pool Created by A. Merritt Leprechauns are a folkloric species of small humanoids which inhabit Ireland. In the Mythos Larry O'Keefe once saw a in Carntogher Woods, describing it as a small man dressed in green, about as tall as a man's knee. He later met another in the underground realm of Muria. ( CIRCLE : The Moon Pool ) A passage from the Necronomicon claims that when the Great Old Ones rise again, the Spawn of Shub-Niggurath will "take dominion over all wood nymphs , satyrs , leprechauns, and the Little People ". ( AWD : " The House on Curwen Street ", " The Keeper of the Key ")</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Moon_Pool"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>A. Merritt</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chasing_Shadows">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Chasing Shadows</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. United States English Publication date April 1998 " " is a short story by Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. Synopsis Characters from Robert W. Chambers ' fictional play The King in Yellow pursue each other on a dark and stormy night. Characters Thane Cassilda The King in Yellow , an Avatar of Hastur . Publication History First featured in the March-April 1998 edition of Al-Azif , "" was reprinted in Nightscapes in July that year. It was included in Pulver's own anthology Blood Will Have Its Season in 2009 and the collection The King in Yellow Tales, Volume I in 2015.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Joseph S. Pulver. Sr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>April 1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#CthulhuPunk">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>CthulhuPunk</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The game is published by steve jackson games and uses the gurps system . It combines the call of cthulhu and cyberworld , a cyberpunk game released earlier in 1993 by steve jackson games . Setting is set in a dystopian cyberpunk future in which the united states is no longer the land of the free but a dictatorship .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Chris W. McCubbin</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cyberpunk</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Sci-Fi</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>role-playing game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>GURPS</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ancient_Ones_II">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ancient Ones II</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>90 is a short anthology of lovecraftian stories . Edited by jesse dedman and published by deadman's tome in march 2018 . Despite being titled 90 , a first part does not appear to have ever been published .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>90</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>March 2018</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pigman">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Pigman</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>'pickman's gallery' is a short story by american author max d. stanton . It was first published in the anthology pickman's gallery by ulthar press in 2018 . 'pickman's gallery' is about a struggling art gallery owner who attempts to obtain works from a sociopathic 'art-terrorist'</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Max_D._Stanton"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pickmans_Gallery"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2018</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Black_Pool">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Black Pool</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A young heiress in post-wwii britain uses her vast fortune to indulge in her every whim and desire, searching for a way to lose herself in sensation . Despite her wealth, she is unable to find what she is looking for and eventually becomes jaded with life . Travelling to iceland, she eventually locates a lodge for the rich and decadent, and is introduced to their secret .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>James Grim Desborough</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_after_Dark"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_Stories_of_Robert_E._Howard">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RobertEHowardWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Horror Stories of Robert E. Howard</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Robert E. Howard English Genre(s) Horror Del Rey/Ballantine Release Date July 29, 2008 518 is a collection of Howard 's weird fiction, including many of his contributions to the Cthulhu Mythos , published by Del Rey/Ballantine in 2008. Contents "In the Forest of Villefère" (1925) "A Song of the Werewolf Folk" (1987) "Wolfshead" (1926) "Up, John Kane!" (1977) "Remembrance" (1928 "The Dream Snake" (1928) "Sea Curse" (1928) "The Moor Ghost" (1929) "Moon Mockery" (1929) " The Little People " (1970) "Dead Man’s Hate" (1930) "The Tavern" (1970) "Rattle of Bones" (1929) "The Fear That Follows" (1970) "The Spirit of Tom Molyneaux" (1929) "Casonetto’s Last Song" (1973) "The Touch of Death" (1930) "Out of the Deep" (1967) "A Legend of Faring Town" (1975) "Restless Waters" (1974) "The Shadow of the Beast" (1977) "The Dead Slaver’s Tale" (1973) " Dermod’s Bane " (1967) "The Hills of the Dead" (1930) " Dig Me No Grave " (1937) "The Song of a Mad Minstrel" (1931) " The Children of the Night " (1931) "Musings" (1971) " The Black Stone " (1931) " The Thing on the Roof " (1932) "The Dweller in Dark Valley" (1965) "The Horror from the Mound" (1932) "A Dull Sound as of Knocking" (2007) " People of the Dark " (1932) "Delenda Est" (1968) " The Cairn on the Headland " (1933) " Worms of the Earth " (1932) "The Symbol" (1976) "The Valley of the Lost" (1967) " The Hoofed Thing " (1970) "The Noseless Horror" (1970) "The Dwellers Under the Tomb" (1976) "An Open Window" (1932) "The House of Arabu" (1952) "The Man on the Ground" (1933) " Old Garfield’s Heart " (1933) "Kelly the Conjure-Man" (1964) "Black Canaan" (1936) "To a Woman" (1933) "One Who Comes at Eventide" (1933) " The Haunter of the Ring " (1934) "Pigeons from Hell" (1938) "The Dead Remember" (1936) " The Fire of Asshurbanipal " (1936) "Fragment" (1937) "Which Will Scarcely Be Understood" (1937) "Golnor the Ape" (1985) "Spectres in the Dark" (1985) " The House " (2003) " Untitled Fragment " (1967)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_E._Howard"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>518</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2008 July 29</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#John_Goodrich">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Critics"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>John Goodrich</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Vermont, USA United States Horror Fantasy Science Fiction Non-Fiction http://qusoor.com/index.htm is an American author of science fiction, horror, fantasy and literary criticism. He has written a number of pieces set within the Cthulhu Mythos established by H. P. Lovecraft and others. Selected Bibliography Short Stories " The Dark Horse " (2014) " Tribute Band " (2017)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA http://example.org/lovecraft#Vermont</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://qusoor.com/index.htm</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dunwich_Horror_%282008_film%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleFilmAndTelevisionAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Dunwich Horror (2008 film)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Dunwich Horror Based on HPL : " The Dunwich Horror " Leigh Scott Dean Stockwell Jeffrey Combs Release date 2008 Running time 91 minutes United States English The Dunwich Horror is a 2008 horror film directed by Leigh Scott, and an adaptation of the H. P. Lovecraft short story from which it takes its name. It stars Dean Stockwell in the role of Dr. Henry Armitage ; in 1970, Stockwell had starred in another remake of " The Dunwich Horror ", having portrayed Wilbur Whateley , a role taken on in this film by Mythos screen regular Jeffrey Combs . A critical failure, the film has received poor reviews from the general public, holding only a 3.6/10 rating on IMDb . Trivia Despite the promotional poster showing the film's title as H. P. Lovecraft's Witches: The Dunwich Horror , this does not appear to have ever been used. References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dunwich_Horror"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2008</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Abigail_Foreman">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Abigail Foreman</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Miskatonic_University"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Abigail is a librarian at miskatonic university . Joined forces with sailor silas to uncover a plot by the folks of innsmouth to awaken a dread entity and thwart it . Appearances has appeared in several cthulhu mythos contributions by fantasy flight .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fantasy_Flight_Games"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nxla">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nxla</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Soul Stealer</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Cold</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>the Darkness</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The harvester of souls is an alien intelligence of such power, that even the splugorth fear him . It establishes footholds on worlds where mortals worship power and/or death, accepting it as their master . Once he physically enters the world, his minions are given immortality, though they can still be injured and killed . The only way to force him back to his home dimension is to destroy most of his physical body, killing or liberating at least half of the soulless xomb</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Rifts"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Palladium Games</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fall_of_Cthulhu_Volume_6:_Nemesis">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Fall of Cthulhu Volume 6: Nemesis</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Preceded by fall of cthulhu volume 5: apocalypse is a comic book omnibus collecting issues 1-4 of michael alan nelson 's nemesis arc . It was originally published between march and july 2009, and later collected as a graphic novel in november of that year . Despite being billed as the conclusion to fall of cthulhu, the story has virtually nothing to do with the main .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Michael_Alan_Nelson"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>110</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fall_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>November 2009 March-July 2009</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pickman%E2%80%99s_Madonna">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CaitlinRKiernanWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Pickman’s Madonna</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story's title refers to an idol of a ghoulish goddess, first noted by humans in the 5th century . It was later rediscovered in a cave in arabia . The story was reprinted (revised and in part) in cherry bomb, a novel kiernan wrote under the name kathleen tierney .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Caitlin_R._Kiernan"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sirenia_Digest"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2013</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#William_Yorick">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>William Yorick</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Gravedigger</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as arkham gravedigger unknown, possibly actor human . Created by fantasy flight games as part of their arkham horror files line of cthulhu mythos -related games and fiction .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Eldritch Horror Arkham Horror: The Card Game Mansions of Madness</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Innsmouth Horror Deluxe Expansion</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fantasy_Flight_Games"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tilyic">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ti'lyic</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sub-aquatic Metropolis Deep Ones First appearance EXP : " Interlude at the Bridge " Created by Franklyn Searight is a Deep One city introduced by Franklyn Searight in the story " Interlude at the Bridge " (1980)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Sub-aquatic Metropolis</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Deep Ones</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>Interlude at the Bridge</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Wonderland:_Through_the_Looking_Glass">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Wonderland: Through the Looking Glass</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Raven Gregory, Patrick Shand Alessandra Miracolo English Zenescope Entertainment Release Date Oct 2013 - Jan 2014 Media Type Comic book is a five-issue comic book mini-series written by Raven Gregory and Patrick Shand in 2013-2014 which was published by Zenescope Entertainment. Synopsis The story of Wonderland 's most feared being, the Mad Hatter. How Johnny Liddle came to be the Mad Hatter.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Patrick Shand</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Raven Gregory</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>Oct 2013 - Jan 2014</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Byagoona">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Avatars"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Byagoona</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Powers and Abilities Necromancy Relatives Lu-Kthu (Parent) First appearance " The Bane of " Created by James Ambuehl ( The Faceless One ) is a Great Old One and the offspring of Lu-Kthu . He arrived on Earth during the reign of the Atlaus civilization. During their reign he was revered as a god of the dead and reanimated the deceased to sustain itself on their life force. It is theorised that is not its own entity but is, in fact, an avatar of Nyarlathotep , though this is unconfirmed. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Bane of Byagoona</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Ambuehl"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tina_%28Keyport_Cthulhu%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tina (Keyport Cthulhu)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Tina (last name unrevealed, possibly cecil) is a character created by american author armand rosamilia for his keyport cthulhu series of mythos tales . She is devoutly religious as a result of her upbringing as the daughter of a catholic minister .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dagon"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Armand_Rosamilia"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dunwich_Horror_%281970_film%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleFilmAndTelevisionAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Dunwich Horror (1970 film)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Dunwich Horror Based on HPL : " The Dunwich Horror " Daniel Haller Release date 14 January 1970 Running time 90 minutes United States English The Dunwich Horror is a 1970 American horror film directed by Daniel Haller , and a loose adaptation of the H. P. Lovecraft short story from which it takes its name. It stars Dean Stockwell in the role of Wilbur Whateley ; in 2009, Stockwell would star in another remake of " The Dunwich Horror ", this time portraying Dr. Henry Armitage . A box office flop, the film has received low-to-middling reviews, holding a 29% score on Rotten Tomatoes and 5.4/10 on IMDb . Cast Dean Stockwell as Wilbur Whateley Sandra Dee as Nancy Wagner Ed Begley as Dr. Henry Armitage Lloyd Bochner as Dr. Cory Sam Jaffe as Old Whateley Joanne Moore Jordan as Lavinia Whateley External Links The Dunwich Horror at Wikipedia Gallery References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dunwich_Horror"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>14 January 1970</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Maker_of_Gargoyles">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AveroigneCycle"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ClarkAshtonSmithWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Maker of Gargoyles</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here Clark Ashton Smith English Weird Tales date August 1932 𝐓 "" is a short story by Clark Ashton Smith , one of his stories set in Averoigne , a fictionalized province of France. It first published in the August 1932 issue of Weird Tales . The story centers on Blaise Reynard, a master stonecarver of Vyones , who carves two gargoyles so lifelike that they begin attacking residents. The story has been credited with introducing the idea of gargoyles as monsters rather than mere architectural decoration.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>August 1932</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Thing_that_Devours_and_Dissolves">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Thing that Devours and Dissolves</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Devourer Sociocultural Characteristics Extra-dimensional Real World Information First appearance " From Beyond " Created by Howard Phillips Lovecraft</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#From_Beyond"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tales_of_the_Cthulhu_Mythos:_Cthulhus_Creatures">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tales of the Cthulhu Mythos: Cthulhu's Creatures</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Steve Lines United States English Rainfall Books and JnJ Publications Release Date November 2007 Media Type Softcover Print 230 is an anthology of short Cthulhu Mythos works. Overview Containing a total of 19 short stories and 6 poems, was published by Rainfall Books and JnJ Publications in November 2007. It was edited by John B. Ford and Steve Lines , with Lines also providing the cover artwork. Running to 230 , it was limited to 100 copies, each signed and numbered. Contents Introduction - Weird Tomes &amp; Lightning Rods by Simon Clark " The Night Music of Oakdeene " by Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. " Objects from the Gilman-Waite Collection " by Ann K. Schwader "The Image Dissector" Simon Clark "Ms. Found in a Steel Bottle" by Simon Whitechapel "Far Sight" by Kevin L. O'Brien "Re-Quest Denied" Stanley C. Sargent " I Married a Shoggoth " by Jeffrey Thomas "The Questioning of the Azathothian Priest" by C. J. Henderson " When in Leng... " by Ron Shiflet "Signs &amp; Signals" by John Shire " The Horror in the Genizah " by Robert M. Price "Welcome to Goatshead" by Tim Curran "Felicity" by Susan McAdam " Beneath Evil Skies " by Phillip A. Ellis "The Marsh House" by Franklyn Searight " Innsmouth Harvest " by Ran Cartwright "Eclipse" by Joel Lane "The Prisoner" by Joel Lane "The Ring of Azathoth" by Michael Fantina "Crawling Terrors from Sho-Beth Mein" by C. D. Allen " Episode in an Arkham Pool Hall " by James Ambuehl "A Vision of Carcosa" John B. Ford and Steve Lines "Chancellor Town at Dark" by Brian Leno "The Passing of Cassilda" by Richard L. Tierney "Apocalypse" by Richard L. Tierney</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>230</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>November 2007</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Those_of_the_Air">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Those of the Air</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Behind the mythos is a short story by american and literary critic darrell schweitzer in collaboration with artist and jason van hollander . The story takes place on the day an estranged son returns to his family ten years after leaving . As he reminisces about his childhood, it is revealed that the family are descended from the whateleys of dunwich . The curse of that lineage has followed them down the years along with a copy of the necromicon .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Darrell_Schweitzer"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jason_Van_Hollander"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Short Fiction Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1994</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Zoth-Ommog">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Zoth-Ommog</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Great Old Ones</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Dweller in the Depths Lord of the Abyss Zatamaga Hommogah Z'otomogo Zatamagwa Sothamogha Z'mog Sothmogg Zhmog-yaa</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A planet in the xoth system and the third son of cthulhu . Created by lin carter and featured in the xothic legend cycle .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Xothic_Legend_Cycle"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lin_Carter"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Zothique">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>Zothique</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Clark ashton smith created a far future continent in a series of short stories . It is also the title of the cycle of tales which take place there . The cycle belongs to the fantasy genre, and more precisely to the dying earth subgenre .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL>The Tomb-Spawn The Dark Eidolon The Master of the Crabs The Dweller in Darkness '</ex:hasAppearanceL>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Continent</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Planet Earth</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>The Empire of the Necromancers</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Book_of_Hastur">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Book of Hastur</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various English Books of Cthulhu Crossroad Press Release Date Upcoming is an upcoming anthology of Lovecraftian fiction from Crossroad Press . Slated for a release on 24 September 2024, it forms part of the press's ongoing Books of Cthulhu . Overview References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>Upcoming</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Temujin">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Temujin</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Genghis Khan</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Gedei khan is a warlord that sought the favor of aiueb gnshal, better known as genghis khan . His descendants extended the mongol empire across most of eurasia by conquering or creating vassal states in all of modern-day china, korea, the caucasus, central asia, and substantial portions of eastern and southwest asia . Genghis khan is also credited with bringing the silk road under one cohesive political environment .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Eyes Between the Worlds (RPG Module)</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Dylan K. Sharpe</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Kevin T. McKinnon</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Glenn_Rahman">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Glenn Rahman</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Glenn arthur rahman (born 1949) is an author and games designer . He co-created "the first successful transference of h. p. lovecraft ’s style of supernatural literature into a modern role-playing format" rahman has written a number of stories for a variety of genres .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Glenn Arthur Rahman</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Arthur Rahman</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Glen Rahman</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Glenn A. Rahman</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Glenn Arther Rahman</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Glenn Arthur Rahman</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>J. A. McKraken</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>James A. McKraken</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasRelativeR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Philip_J._Rahman"/>
    <ex:hasYearsActiveR>1977-</ex:hasYearsActiveR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Game Designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1949</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dwarf">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dwarf</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Imp</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a fictional demon in the prime reality of the . Marvel universe of comics who was created in 1992 by . Chris cooper and richard case . It delivers pages of the darkhold in black envelopes to people, sometimes giving them little to no inkling of what it contains .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>1992</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Ghost Rider Vol 3 #28</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Chris Cooper</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Richard Case</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Kathulos_%28Marvel%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Kathulos (Marvel)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shuma-Gorath"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Kulthas</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Kulthas of the Green Planet  Kathulos of the Eternal Lives</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>the Living Planet  Kathulos the Slave-Seer</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Kathulos was a fictional character by marvel comics and first appeared in marvel premiere #8 (may, 1973), written by gardner fox and jim starlin . Both names were created by robert e. howard : the atlantean sorcerer kathulos ( circle : " skull-face ") and the evil deity kulthas ( circle : " the curse of the golden skull ").</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>ADJ</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Marvel Comics : Marvel Premiere #8</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Curse of the Golden Skull (mention)</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>ADJ</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Jim Starlin</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Marvel Comics : Gardner Fox</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Robert E. Howard (mention)</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Descendant">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Descendant</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"in the vaultcool air" is a story fragment by american horror fiction writer h. p. lovecraft . It was first published in the journal leaves in 1938, after lovecraft's death . The details of the character evoke two of lovecraft's favorite british writers .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Slorg">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Slorg</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Real World Information First appearance The Wizard of Lemuria Created by Lin Carter Slorgs are woman-headed snake creatures inhabiting Lemuria .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Wizard_of_Lemuria"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lin_Carter"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Investigators_of_Arkham_Horror">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Investigators of Arkham Horror</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various United States English Arkham Horror Files Fantasy Flight Games Release Date December 2016 (Pre-order) February 2017 Media Type Hardcover Print 264 is a reference book for Fantasy Flight Games ' Arkham Horror Files range of Mythos -related products. Description A 264-page full-colour hardback reference book featuring more in-depth information on the Arkham Horror investigators than can be found in the various games, Investigators includes 52 short stories, with each one focussing on a different character but forming a narrative when read as a whole. A pre-order premium was also available for a limited time, which contained the same book along with an exclusive gaming mat, four art prints - including one of Daisy Walker - and three cards for Arkham Horror: The Card Game , featuring investigator Marie Lambeau . Publication History was first published by Fantasy Flight Games in February 2017. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>264</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Arkham Horror Files</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>February 2017 December 2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_End_of_the_Story">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AveroigneCycle"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ClarkAshtonSmithWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The End of the Story</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story concerns a young law student named christophe morand, who stays at the abbey of perigon in averoigne . There, he reads a manuscript about a knight who was seduced by a lamia who lives in the ruins of the chateau des faussesflammes . Reading the story, he becomes similarly obsessed and seems to meet the same fate .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Fantasy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>May 1930</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Zakarion">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalLocations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Zakarion</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Dreamlands City Humans First appearance HPL : " Ex Oblivione " Created by H. P. Lovecraft is a city in the Dreamlands and a repository for much of the lore pertaining to the lands of dream. This lore was written down by sages who once dwelt there, sages who were "too wise ever to be born in the waking world." ( HPL : " Ex Oblivione ")</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>City</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Humans</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dreamlands"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ex_Oblivione"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cordelia_Amanda_Lovecraft">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSRelatives"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cordelia Amanda Lovecraft</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>31 October 1896</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Daughter of George Lovecraft and Helen Allgood, she only lived to be 12 days old.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isRelativeToR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1812</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nush_the_Eternal">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nush the Eternal</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Relatives Tsathoggua (grandparent) Clark Ashton Smith (possible descendant) First appearance HPL : " Selected Letters of H. P. Lovecraft " 4.617 Created by H. P. Lovecraft is an entity only mentioned in H. P. Lovecraft 's letter to James F. Morton as a descendant of Tsathoggua .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Selected Letters of H. P. Lovecraft 4.617</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shoggoth_Lord">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Shoggoth Lord</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Shoggoth lords are an evolved variety of shoggoth created by michael shea for his short story " fat face ". They were later expanded on by chaosium for their call of cthulhu roleplaying game . They resemble most shoggoths, but are both more intelligent and have the ability to mimic the human form .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Call of Cthulhu</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fat_Face"/>
    <ex:usesLanguageC>English</ex:usesLanguageC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Michael_Shea"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Shop_in_Go-by_Street">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Shop in Go-by Street</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"idle days on the yann" is a sequel to "idle days on the yann" it has been cited as a key inspiration for h.p. Lovecraft's conception of cthulhu . The narrator wants to return to the yann to see his friends from the ship bird of the river .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lord_Dunsany"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Tales of Three Hemispheres</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 1911</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Winfield_Heritance">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Winfield Heritance</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Weird tales #3 date 1981 is a short story by lin carter . It originally appeared in the 1981 anthology weird tales #3 . It is part of carter's xothic legend cycle .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lin_Carter"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Weird Tales #3</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1981</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Presentation">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Presentation</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A chance encounter with a prophet of cthulhu kickstarts a movement amongst the artistic community of san francisco . The prophet gains influence in the city, and the artists are hired to paint an enormous mural, one which opens into a portal to another dimension . When the portal closes, another commission is made, and the community begins to realise what they have initiated .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Michael_Shea"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Allen K's Inhuman Magazine #3</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 2005</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Joseph_Curwen">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Joseph Curwen</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>1771 April 12</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Curwen is depicted as a successful merchant, shipping magnate, slave trader, and highly accomplished sorcerer . He was killed again in 1928, presumably for good, by dr. willett, using his own sorcery . He was resurrected through necromancy by his great-great-great-grandson charles dexter ward, whom he closely resembles .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCharacterMateC>Eliza Tillinghast</ex:hasCharacterMateC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Case_of_Charles_Dexter_Ward"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Worm_in_the_Wood">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Worm in the Wood</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story is told by a narrator who is trying to dissuade someone from venturing into the wood in the vicinity of arkham . No one has claimed the land after the death of the last owners, but the locals take care to maintain the fence around the wood in good condition .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dimensional_Shambler">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dimensional Shambler</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lurkers"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Dark Gatherers</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Excoleoptera</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Insects from the Void</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Shambling Things</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Dimensional shamblers are a race of monsters from a different dimension . First appeared in h.p. Lovecraft's story " the horror in the museum " . Rumours suggests that these entities occasionally serve the outer gods and great old ones .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>Hulking extra-dimensional entities</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>ape-like mammal. In places</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>insect</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>rugose. The eyes appear as black points of light set in a rudimentary head. The mouth is a misshapen hole. Large talon-like clawed appendages protrude from the arms.</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>seemingly outlandish combinations of crustacean</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>their hide is like thick plates of armor</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>while the rest of the skin is loose</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_in_the_Museum"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Hunters from Beyond (possibly)</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hazel_Heald"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Howard_Phillips_Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
