  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cold_Warning">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cold Warning</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Scott David Aniolowski Reuben Dodd Cover Artist Reuben Dodd English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Golden Goblin Press Release Date 2017 30 is a scenario supplement for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu horror roleplaying game. Written by veteran designer Scott David Aniolowski , it was published by third-party content provider Golden Goblin Press in 2017 ; both print and digital editions were released . Overview Synopsis The story involves the players going in search of a woman who has been abducted by her husband's brother and used a vessel for the spawn of Ithaqua . Stranded in a hunting lodge beset by a powerful blizzard, the group must face not only the blistering cold, but also a pack of vicious Wendigo and, ultimately, the Great Old One itself. Design Team Written by: Scott Aniolowski Artwork by: Reuben Dodd and Stephanie McAlea Production staff: Tim McGonagle, Oscar Rios , and Mark Shireman Gallery References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scott_David_Aniolowski"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>30</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2017</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Arkham_Sampler_Winter_1948">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AugustDerlethWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Periodicals"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Arkham Sampler Winter 1948</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Ronald Clyne United States English The Arkham Sampler Arkham House Release Date Winter 1948 100 Preceded by Autumn 1948 Followed by Spring 1949 The Arkham Sampler]] . Edited by August Derleth and featuring cover art by Ronald Clyne, it ran to a total of 100 . Contents "Messrs. Turkes and Talbot" by H. Russell Wakefield "History and Chronology of the Necronomicon Together with Some Pertinent Paragraphs" by August Derleth and H. P. Lovecraft "Lamia" by Clark Ashton Smith "The Nameless Wraith" by Clark Ashton Smith "The City of Destruction" by Clark Ashton Smith "Introduction: Strange Ports of Call" by August Derleth "A Little Anthology" by Malcolm Ferguson "Mara" by Stephen Grendon "A Hornbook for Witches" by Leah Bodine Drake "Checklist: The Carvings of Clark Ashton Smith" by Clark Ashton Smith The Dream Quest of Unknown Kadath (Part 1 of 4) by H. P. Lovecraft "Two Novels and an Anthology" by August Derleth "From the Fan Presses" by August Derleth "The Shasta Checklist" by August Derleth "Through a Glass, Darkly" by Robert Bloch "A Thorne Off the Old Smith" by Robert Bloch "Three Anthologies" by John Haley Reviews by August Derleth, Robert Bloch, John Haley, and uncredited contributors External Links full issue online at the Internet Archive</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>100</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Arkham_Sampler"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>Winter 1948</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Freddy_Krueger">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Freddy Krueger</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Springwood Slasher</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Adj is a slasher in the fictional nightmare on elm street universe, who was created in 1984 and has been the franchise's antagonist . He has appeared in comic book series by marvel comics , innovation publishing, trident comics, avatar press, and most recently wildstorm productions .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Freddy vs. Jason vs. Ash Freddy vs. Jason vs. Ash: The Nightmare Warriors</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>A Nightmare on Elm Street</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Wes Craven</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Cultus_Issue_14">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Periodicals"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Cultus Issue 14</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu Cultus #14 Various Cover Artist Tani Jantsang United States English P. Marsh Release Date January 1999 62 Preceded by Cthulhu Cultus #13 Followed by Cthulhu Cultus #15 Cthulhu Cultus #14 is an issue of sporadic American fanzine Cthulhu Cultus , edited by Tani Jantsang and James Ambuehl and published by P. Marsh in January 1999. Contents "Lovecraftian Deep Thoughts" by Steve Bastasch "More Lovecraftian Deep Thoughts" by James Ambuehl "The Gemstone" by P. J. Roberts "Half a Sixpence" by D. F. Lewis "Evil After Midnight" by P. J. Roberts "Ancient Beings" by P. J. Roberts "A House of Haunts" by P. J. Roberts "Imposing Stone Structures" by P. J. Roberts "Insect-Like Creatures" by P. J. Roberts "Night Floaters" by P. J. Roberts "Whipping Boy" by G. W. Thomas "The Forgotten" by G. W. Thomas "The Prodigal" by Andy Nunez " Folks of Innsmouth: The Fish Dealer " by Franklyn Searight " The Statement of Randolph Carter: Lovecraftian Cliff Notes for Ech-Pi-El's " by Franklyn Searight "Road Rage" by Kenneth J. Beattie "Cthulhu's Camaro" by James Ambuehl " The Dark Man's Assassin " by Ron Shiflet "The Nameless City (A Haiku Cycle)" by G. W. Thomas " An Arkham Witch " by Ron Shiflet "The Man in Cell Number Thirty-Three" by Ray Wallace "Next Stop" by David Fabricant "Subway Terror" by James Ambuehl "The Temple in the Matrix" by Tim Jones "The Inmate" by James Ambuehl "Tcho-Tcho's" by Tracy Ambuehl</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>62</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>January 1999</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
