  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Proto-Shoggoth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Proto-Shoggoth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The proto-shoggoths are a subspecies of the shoggoth . They are thought to have originated from the plasma remains of ubbo-sathla . In "the asylum", a scenario included in the call of cthulhu supplement, a doctor began creating an army of proto-shoggoths .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Autophagia"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>EXP</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Other Tales</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The Asylum</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#At_the_Mountains_of_Madness"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Foul_One_Lives_To_Destroy:_A_Tale_Of_The_Dreamlands">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Foul One Lives To Destroy: A Tale Of The Dreamlands</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mark McLaughlin English Genre(s) Horror The House of the Ocelot &amp; More Lovecraftian Nightmares date 2019 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos story by Mark McLaughlin . Synopsis After lying imprisoned beneath the sands of the Dreamlands for a thousand years, the Foul One, an Avatar of Nyarlathotep , is freed and returns to the city of Xambolei to take its vengeance on the descendants of those who turned their backs on its worship so many years ago. Characters The Foul One , an avatar of the Outer God Nyarlathotep. Chabao , a Xamboleian trader with a penchant for strong wine. History "" was published in the 2019 collection The House of the Ocelot &amp; More Lovecraftian Nightmares .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>More Lovecraftian Nightmares</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The House of the Ocelot</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2019</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Star_Vampire">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonSapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Star Vampire</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Shambler from the Stars</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Star vampires are a fictional extraterrestrial species in the cthulhu mythos . They are voracious blood suckers, becoming visible once their bodies are full of the victim's blood, a vivid red colour . Their form is like that of a jellyfish with neither head nor any visible facial features apart from its huge maw .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Malleus Monstrorum Demonbane</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shambler_from_the_Stars"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_Bloch"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Deputy_Dingby">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Deputy Dingby</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Arkham County Sheriff's Department</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ire of the void is a character who features in the arkham horror files series of games and fiction . He was a member of the arkham county sheriff's department .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Feeders_from_Within"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ire_of_the_Void"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Arkham Horror Second Edition</ex:hasAppearanceC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Demonbane">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosFilmAndTelevision"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Demonbane</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The series is set in arkham city and involves magick users who use a grimoire in human form to become a magius and pilot a deus machina . Some of 's parts are named timaeus and critias, which have the ability to distort time and space . Master therion, grandmaster of the black lodge and son of yog-sothoth, is the main antagonist .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>Japan</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Japanese</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2003 – present</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Return_of_Hastur">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AugustDerlethWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Return of Hastur</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>August Derleth United States English Genre(s) Horror Weird Tales date March 1939 𝐓 is a short story by the late American August Derleth . Synopsis To be added History "" was first published in Weird Tales in March 1939, later in The Mask of Cthulhu (1958), and again in The Hastur Cycle (1998). Behind the Mythos In "" Derleth ties Hastur deeply into the Cthulhu Mythos as a Great Old One and introduces the controversial Elemental Theory of the Great Old Ones. The story assumes that the writings of H. P. Lovecraft and Robert W. Chambers exist in the world in which the story takes place, and implies a connection between Hastur and Shub-Niggurath . Set in Arkham , it also ties the Shoggoth's cry of Tekeli-li into the Yellow Mythos .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#August_Derleth"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 1939</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Peter_B._Carr">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Peter B. Carr</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Fisherman Human First appearance HPL : " The Green Meadow " Created by H. P. Lovecraft and Elizabeth Berkeley was a member of the 3-man fishing party, along with John Richmond and Simon Canfield , responsible for dragging a 360-pound metallic rock ashore after it crashed into the water off the coast of Potowonket, Maine . The object fell from the sky on Wednesday, August 27, 1913, at approximately 8:30 PM. The fishing party retrieved the object the following Sunday, August 31. ( HPL : " The Green Meadow ")</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Green_Meadow"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Elizabeth Berkeley</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Garden_of_Fear">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#JamesAllisonCycle"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RobertEHowardWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Garden of Fear</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The winged black man is a sadistic last survivor of a race which once ruled the world . The story is the second instalment of the james allison cycle . It was first published in marvel tales #2 (july-august 1934)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_E._Howard"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Fantasy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Marvel Tales #2</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>July 1934</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#North_by_Due_North">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>North by Due North</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story was first published in the deadman's tome anthology ancient ones ii . It offers a glimpse into the life of the unnamed narrator, a well-connected american man on a mission to thwart the plans of the old ones .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>David M. Hoenig</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ancient_Ones_II"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 2018</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Yuletide_1983">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu Yuletide 1983</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Robert M. Price United States English Crypt of Cthulhu Release Date December 1983 44 Preceded by Hallowmas 1983 Followed by Candlemas 1984 was the eighteenth issue of Robert M. Price 's long-running Lovecraftian fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Edited by leading H. P. Lovecraft scholar Price, who also provided the cover art, it ran to a total of 44 in an A5 format and was self-published in December 1983. Contents Editorial Shards by Robert M. Price "The Cthulhuers" by John Strysik "H. P. Lovecraft As I Knew Him" by Duane Rimel "The Lurker in the VCR: More H. P. Lovecraft on the Screen" by Denise Dumars "Lovecraft's Influence on Stephen King" by Sam Gafford "The Scales of Horror" by Bert Atsma "Obed and Obadiah Marsh" by Tani Jantsang ""Lovecraftianity" and the Pagan Revival" by Robert M. Price "The Cthulhu Controversy" by Edward DeGeorge "Locating Lovecraft" by Ralph E. Vaughan "Lovecraft and the Mythos in Stephen King" by Robert M. Price "The Not-So-Great Race" by John A. Buettner "Mearle Prout and "The House of the Worm"" by Will Murray "More Limericks from Yuggoth" by Lin Carter "Fun Gals from Yuggoth: Apunkalypse" by Carolyn Lee Boyd "Advice to the Lovecraft-Lorn" by Donna Death "Reviews by Steven Mariconda and Robert M. Price Letter by L. Sprague de Camp</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>44</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>December 1983</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Keepers_Companion">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Keeper's Companion</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Keith Herber Various others Paul Carrick Various others English Chaosium Release Date 2000-2002 , subtitled Blasphemous Knowledge, Forbidden Secrets, and Handy Information , is a 2-volume supplement book series by Chaosium for their Call of Cthulhu RPG . It complied and added various types of useful information for Keepers from previous supplements, scenarios, and campaigns. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Keith_Herber"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various others</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2000-2002</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ammutseba">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ammutseba</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Devourer of Stars</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as the devourer of stars , is a great old one in the cthulhu mythos . First appeared in the short story " lost stars " by ann k. schwader . First appeared in chaosium 's supplement secrets of kenya for their call of cthulhu horror roleplaying game .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Lost Stars</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ann_K._Schwader"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ssshaa">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sss'haa</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Yig"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Yig Serpent Person First appearance EXP : " The Horror in the Gallery " Created by Lin Carter is a fictional character created by Lin Carter for his Cthulhu Mythos fiction. It is the first and oldest of all the Serpent People , considered the prototype of the entire , who leads them in the service of their god Yig . ( EXP : " The Descent into the Abyss ", " The Horror in the Gallery ", " The Vengeance of Yig ")</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_in_the_Gallery"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lin_Carter"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecrafts_Monsters">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lovecraft's Monsters</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various John Coulthard Cover Artist John Coulthard English German Tachyon Publications Release Date March 2014 is an anthology of Cthulhu Mythos fiction edited by Ellen Datlow and published by Tachyon Publications in March 2014. In September 2020, Festa released a German- version. Overview Contents Foreword by Stefan Dziemianowicz Introduction by Ellen Datlow " Only the End of the World Again " by Neil Gaiman "Bulldozer" by Laird Barron "Red Goat Black Goat" by Nadia Bulkin "The Same Deep Waters as You" by Brian Hodge "A Quarter to Three" by Kim Newman "The Dappled Thing" by William Browning Spencer "Inelastic Collisions" by Elizabeth Bear "Remnants" by Fred Chappell " Love Is Forbidden, We Croak and Howl " by Caitlín R. Kiernan "The Sect of the Idiot" by Thomas Ligotti "Jar of Salts" by Gemma Files "Black as the Pit, from Pole to Pole" Steven Utley and Howard Waldrop "Waiting at the Crossroads Motel" by Steve Rasnic Tem "I've Come to Talk with You Again" by Karl Edward Wagner "The Bleeding Shadow" by Joe R. Lansdale "That of Which We Speak When We Speak of the Unspeakable" by Nick Mamatas "Haruspicy" by Gemma Files "Children of the Fang" by John Langan Monster Index Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>German</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>March 2014</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sea_Devil_%28species%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sea Devil (species)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Dagon ( EXP )</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Silurians ( ADJ )</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Earth Reptile</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sea Devil Sociocultural Characteristics Also known as Earth Reptile Earth (prehistory) Oceanic Silurians ( ADJ ) Dagon ( EXP ) Physical Characteristics Tall stature Beaked face Webbed feet Green blood Real World Information First appearance ADJ : The Sea Devils ADJ : Warriors of the Deep Created by Malcolm Hulke The Sea Devils are a fictional species of sapient aquatic reptiles which evolved in prehistoric Earth . Like their land-based relatives, the Silurians , the Sea Devils were created by Malcolm Hulke for the sci-fi television series Doctor Who . The Sea Devils are known to worship Dagon , an entity that the Doctor identifies as one of the Great Old Ones . ( EXP : All-Consuming Fire ) External links Sea Devil at the Doctor Who wiki</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Warriors of the Deep</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>Beaked face</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>Green blood</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>Tall stature</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>Webbed feet</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Sea Devils</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Malcolm Hulke</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Atlach-Nacha">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Atlach-Nacha</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Spinner in Darkness</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Spider God</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The spinner in darkness is a fictional deity created by . Clark ashton smith for his short story " the seven geases "; it has since been referenced and elaborated upon by . August derleth , colin wilson , brian lumley , lin carter , laurence j. cornford , james ambuehl , ran cartwright and many others .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Atlachnaphobia"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Pathfinder Adventure Path #109 Strange Aeons</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:isLocatedAtC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mount_Voormithadreth"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Seven_Geases"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Appleton">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Brian Appleton</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Games designer Editor United States Roleplaying games is an American games designer who has had a hand in several Cthulhu Mythos books, primarily for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu horror roleplaying game. His work, which focuses towards the editing side of gaming material production, has been issued through third-party content provider Pagan Publishing , with whom he worked as one of the company's earliest staff editors . Selected Works Periodicals The Unspeakable Oath (1997-2001, contributor) Gaming Supplements Courting Madness (1992, production staff) Coming Full Circle (1995, production staff) Mortal Coils (1998, "assembled by", editor) The Resurrected III: Out of the Vault (2002, "assembled by", editor) Final Flight (2008, editor) The Mysteries of Mesoamerica (2008, contributor, developer, editor) External Links at Goodreads at RPGGeek References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Alan_Bligh">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Alan Bligh</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>26 May 2017</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Games Designer Death Date 26 May 2017 was an author and games designer perhaps best known for being the Lead Writer and Game Designer for Games Workshop 's high-end subsidiary Forge World, although he has also produced content for Fantasy Flight Games and Chaosium . He died on 26 May 2017 after a brief battle with cancer. Selected Works Fiction Dance of the Damned (2011) The Hungering God (2014) Gaming Supplements Cthulhu Britannica (2009, contributor) Dead Light (2013) Dead Light &amp; Other Dark Turns (2020)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games Designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Ambassador_from_Ulthar">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Ambassador from Ulthar</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The testament of cthulhu date 2018is a short story by authors mark mclaughlin and michael sheehan, jr. it was first seen in print in the self-published digital collection the testament of cthulhu in 2018 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Michael Sheehan. Jr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Testament_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2018</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Genuine_Article">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Genuine Article</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Nightscapesis a short story by mythos j. b. lee . A woman researching new age philosophy uncovers a copy of the necromicon . It attempts to force her to recite a series of incantations which will bring about the return of yog-sothoth .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#J._B._Lee"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nightscapes"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Emil_Petaja">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSFriendsAndAcquaintances"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Emil Petaja</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>2000</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Science fiction fan and late associate of Lovecraft’s; later a prolific author and editor.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1915</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#%28S%29Exit">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>(S)Exit</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>S)Exit Davie Yates English James Ward Kirk Publishing Lovecraft after Dark date November 2015  " (S)Exit " is a Lovecraftian short story by Davie Yates. It was published in the James Ward Kirk Publishing thematic anthology Lovecraft after Dark in November 2015. Synopsis Presented in a jarring combination of present tense, flashback, and flash-forward, "(S)Exit" tells the life story of a sex addict, from his first experience, to marriage and the birth of his child, to how the addiction costs him everything. It culminates with the unnamed narrator meeting a mysterious woman who offers him sweet oblivion through the tender mercies of unmentionable creatures, an offer which he accepts.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Davie Yates</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_after_Dark"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tales_of_the_Caribbean">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tales of the Caribbean</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various United States English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Golden Goblin Press Release Date December 2016 ) is a scenario sourcebook for Chaosium 's Mythos roleplaying game Call of Cthulhu . Description Contents "The Devil Cuts In" by Phredd Groves , set in Montserrat "Toil in the Fields" by Jo Kreil, set in Haiti "Crimson Eyes &amp; Azure Pools" by Jason Williams, set in the Bahamas "Wrath of the Sulfurer" by Dave Sokolowski, set in St. Vincent and the Grenadines "Black as Pitch at Midnight" by Oscar Rios , set in Trinidad "Servant of God" by Jeff Moeller , set in Cuba "Night Forms a Cover for Sinners" by Oscar Rios, set in Puerto Rico Publication History was published in December 2016 by licensed third-party content provider Folden Goblin Press. It was edited by Oscar Rios and Lisa Padol . Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>December 2016 )</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Martins_Beach">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Martin's Beach</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAccessibilityL>Coastal wharf</ex:hasAccessibilityL>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A fictional beach resort used by h. p. lovecraft and sonia greene . It appears as the venue for the display of a huge sea monster . It also appears as the scene of the revenge of the creature's still-more enormous parent .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>Wavecrest Inn</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Beach Resort</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Massachusetts"/>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Unknown</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>possibly near Gloucester</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_at_Martins_Beach"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Cleaners_Tale">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Cleaner's Tale</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"pickman's model" is a short story by british author tom johnstone . It was published in the thematic anthology pickman's gallery by ulthar press in 2018 . It tells the story of an immigrant cleaning lady at a research facility . She overhears two researchers discussing an old photograph of a creature .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Tom Johnstone</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pickmans_Gallery"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2018</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Incident_at_Dagon_Wells">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Incident at Dagon Wells</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A trio of outlaws on the run from hostile natives take refuge in the abandoned outpost of dagon wells . What they do not realise is that the waystation lies on the site of a breach between worlds . As night falls and creatures begin to emerge from beneath the earth, the three become part of a repeating cycle of events .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Western</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Frontier_Cthulhu:_Ancient_Horrors_in_the_New_World"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2007</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Providence:_Two_Gentlemen_Meet_at_Midnight">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AugustDerlethWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Providence: Two Gentlemen Meet at Midnight</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Autumn 1948is a short cthulhu mythos poem by the late american and friend of lovecraft , august derleth . It was first published in the autumn 1948 edition of defunct magazine the arkham sampler . The poem would seem to have inspired at least the germ of a story in derleth, one which may have gone on to be developed in his "posthumous collaborationthe dark brotherhood"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#August_Derleth"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Arkham_Sampler_Autumn_1948"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>Autumn 1948</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gramma">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gramma</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The short story was first published in spring 1984 in weirdbook #19. it also appeared in the 1985 collection skeleton crew . It concerns a boy who must look after his bedridden grandmother while his mother is away .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Stephen_King"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Weirdbook 19</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 1984</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#In_Search_of_Lake_Monsters">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>In Search of Lake Monsters</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A film crew decides to produce a documentary on the mythical creature known as gla'aki despite the protestations of professor langdon rivenbark . When a trio of divers, sent to film the bottom of the lake said to contain the creature, vanish mid-broadcast, the production is shut down . However, the event has caused a deep psychological change in the professor, and the conclusion sees him enter the lake to commune with its inhabitants .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_M._Price"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Children_of_Glaaki"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dreams_of_Lovecraftian_Horror">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dreams of Lovecraftian Horror</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>W. H. Pugmire Cover Artist Stanley C. Sargent United States English Mythos Books Release Date December 1999 91 is a collection of Lovecraftian fiction by the late American W. H. Pugmire , released a few months before his death in 1999. Featuring an introduction and cover art by Stanley C. Sargent , it ran to a total of 91 and was published by Mythos Books in December 1999 . Contents Introduction by Stanley C. Sargent "Beyond the Realm of Dream" "Graffito Flow" "The Hour of Their Appetite" "Cool Mist" "Dust to Dust" "Necronomicon" "Gates Closed by Darkness" " The Baleful God " "Drink the Moon" "The Thing in the Glen: The Final Manuscript of Desmond Peters" "The Audient Void" "The Hungry Place" "A Piece of Stone" "The Totem Pole" References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#W._H._Pugmire"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>91</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>December 1999</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Michael_Szymanski">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Michael Szymanski</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Games Designer Author Horror is a traditional games designer, author, and editor known for his work on Chaosium 's seminal roleplaying Mythos game Call of Cthulhu . His short fiction works have appeared in anthologies including Made in Goatswood (1995, Chaosium) , Horror for the Holidays (2011, Miskatonic River Press ) , and Edge of Sundown (2015, Chaosium) . Selected Bibliography Short Fiction "Random Access" (1995, as Michael G. Szymanski) "Keeping Christmas" (2011) "Son of the Wild Moon" (2015) Gaming Supplements Cthulhu Now (1987, 1992, contributor) Cthulhu Classics (1989, contributor, "The Temple of the Moon" scenario with Scott David Aniolowski ) Lurking Fears (1990, with Scott David Aniolowski) Blood Brothers (1990, contributor) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at RPGGeek at Goodreads References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games Designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dunwich_Horror_%28Lovecraft_Illustrated%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Dunwich Horror (Lovecraft Illustrated)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Dunwich Horror Various Pete Von Sholly Cover Artist Pete Von Sholly English Lovecraft Illustrated PS Publishing Release Date June 2014 112 Preceded by The Dreams in the Witch House Followed by The Shadow Out of Time The Dunwich Horror is a chapbook anthology which features a Pete Von Sholly -illustrated version of H. P. Lovecraft 's " The Dunwich Horror " as its centrepiece, with a handful of accompanying non-fiction pieces regarding the story. Edited by Lovecraft scholar S. T. Joshi , it was published in a hardcover format by PS Publishing as part of their Lovecraft Illustrated . Overview Contents Introduction by S. T. Joshi Foreword by Pete Von Sholly " The Dunwich Horror " by H. P. Lovecraft "The Mythic Hero Archetype in "The Dunwich Horror"" by Donald R. Burleson "Call Me Wizard Whateley: Echoes of Moby-Dick in "The Dunwich Horror"" by Peter Cannon "Lustcraft" by W. H. Pugmire Gallery References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>112</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Lovecraft Illustrated</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>June 2014</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Elder_Sign_%28Game%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Elder Sign (Game)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Elder sign is a cooperative cards-and-dice game for up to eight players . The objective of the game is to collect a predetermined number of elder sign tokens . A digital version of the game was also released, titled elder sign: omens .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2011</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cydathria">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalLocations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cydathria</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Dreamlands Country Humans First appearance HPL : " The Doom That Came to Sarnath " HPL : " The Quest of Iranon " Created by H. P. Lovecraft is a region in the Dreamlands briefly mentioned in " The Doom That Came to Sarnath " by H. P. Lovecraft . Nuts and spices from Cydathrian groves were considered delicacies in Sarnath .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Quest_of_Iranon"/>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Country</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Humans</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dreamlands"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Doom_That_Came_to_Sarnath"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dark_Forest">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Dark Forest</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Chitin Proctor Stephanie McAlea Cover Artist Stephanie McAlea English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Stygian Fox Release Date 2019 74 is a Modern Era scenario supplement for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu horror roleplaying game. Written by Chitin Proctor , featuring artwork by Stephanie McAlea , and published by third-party content provider Stygian Fox , it was released in 2019; both softcover and digital editions were made available . Overview External Links at RPGGeek Review of at Reviews from R'lyeh Gallery References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chitin_Proctor"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>74</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2019</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Other_God">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Other God</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The other gods are a group of higher deities that protect the gods of earth . They are identified as the outer gods in the expanded mythos . The role-playing game call of cthulhu acknowledges two meanings of the term "other gods"</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Bizaamo">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Bizaamo</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Artificial Life Form</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Life Form of Planet Bizaamo</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The bizaamos are a kaiju in the fictional universe of the ultraman tiga tv series . They were created by extraterrestrials to save their homeworld from extinction . Due to their ability of self-reproduction, the bizaamos wiped out all life on the planet, reducing it to a dead world .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ultraman_Tiga"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Hideyuki Kawakami</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Tsugumi Kitaura</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecrafts_The_Dunwich_Horror_and_Other_Stories">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleFilmAndTelevisionAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>H. P. Lovecraft's The Dunwich Horror and Other Stories</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Based on " The Picture in the House " " The Dunwich Horror " " The Festival " Ryo Shinagawa H. P. Lovecraft (original stories) Ryo Shinigawa (screenplay) Production Company Toei Animation Release date 28 August 2007 Running time 46 minutes Japan Japanese is Japanese- anthology film that adapts stories by H. P. Lovecraft in claymation format. Overview Consisting of three claymation segments based on the short stories " The Picture in the House ", " The Dunwich Horror ", and " The Festival ", is a Japanese production from Toei Animation, a subsidiary of Japanese multimedia giant Toei Company. Released in August 2007, it was screenwritten and directed by Ryo Shinagawa, and runs to a total of 46 minutes. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>Japan</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasWriterW>http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft Ryo Shinigawa</ex:hasWriterW>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dunwich_Horror"/>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Festival"/>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Picture_in_the_House"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Japanese</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>28 August 2007</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Black_Wings_IV">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Black Wings IV</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Tales of lovecraftian horror is an anthology of lovecraftian horror stories edited by s.t. Joshi . It was originally published in hardback by ps publishing in 2015 . Titan books released the book in paperback in 2016, under the title black wings of cthulhu 4: seventeen tales of lovecraftian horror .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>S. T. Joshi (editor)</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>339</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Black_Wings"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 2015</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Titus_Crow_%28series%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#BrianLumleyWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Titus Crow (series)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The titus crow series is a cthulhu mythos story arc by noted british author brian lumley . It comprises six novels and eleven short stories featuring the eponymous protagonist and those allied with him against the forces of the great old ones . The books are heavily influenced by the interpretation of the mythos forwarded by august derleth .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United Kingdom</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror Cosmic Horror Action Adventure Fantasy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Saves_Christmas">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Saves Christmas</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Zeboyd Games Publication Date December 2019 Retro RPG is a retro-style RPG video game from indie developer Zeboyd Games . A tongue-in-cheek parody of the Cthulhu Mythos , it was published in December 2019 for Windows. Overview Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 2019</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Rapture_of_the_Deep">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Rapture of the Deep</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ingrid is an employee of a corporation based in hong kong, moscow, and san francisco . She has the ability to see through the another's eyes . She and a psychic operative investigate a mysterious phenomenon in the marianas .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cody_Goodfellow"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Dark Discoveries</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>Fall 2009</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
