  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#King_Conan:_The_Scarlet_Citadel">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleComicBookAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>King Conan: The Scarlet Citadel</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Timothy Truman Tomás Giorello English Dark Horse Comics Release Date Feb 2012 Media Type Comic book is a 4-issue mini-series, an adaptation of Robert E. Howard 's story of the same name by Timothy Truman which was published by Dark Horse Comics in 2012. Synopsis King Conan of Aquilonia finds himself in the citadel of Pelias, a sorcerer in Koth and the Old One Satha are among the dangers he faces.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Timothy Truman</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>Feb 2012</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Xhamen-Dor">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Xhamen-Dor</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Great_Old_One"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Inmost Blot</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Star Seed</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A monstrous hybrid of fibrous, twisted hair-like fungal tendrils and toadstool-like growths which emerges from a mass of bones or a great corpse . Has various incarnations and forms it has taken, one of which is that of a serpentine dragon . Originated in carcosa, from the residue left behind by hastur which fused with the remnants of the minds and souls of those consumed by carcosa .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Wake of the Watcher</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>James Jacobs</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Charles_Le_Sorcier">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Charles Le Sorcier</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Count antoine de c-, would have been the last member of the line to be assassinated by le sorcier if he were successful . However he failed in the assassination attempt and ended up being burned alive by count antoine .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Alchemist"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ghouls_of_the_Miskatonic">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ArkhamHorrorFiction"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ghouls of the Miskatonic</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Behind the mythos is the first book in the dark waters trilogy by graham mcneill . It is part of fantasy flight games 'arkham horror fiction line of mythos -related literature .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Graham_McNeill"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror Mystery</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>329</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>The Dark Waters Trilogy</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dchichi">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dchichi</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Elder Gods of Elysia</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The are a race of bird-like creatures created by brian lumley as part of his titus crow series of novels . Known to inhabit a floating mountain-island in elysia powered by antigravity discs . Each of these eggs is spoken to and taught as if it were a thinking, sentient being .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Transition_of_Titus_Crow"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Martin_S._Warnes">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Martin S. Warnes</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Cthulhu Mythos is an author, most notable for his 1980 short story "The Black Tome of Alsophocus," a story written from H. P. Lovecraft 's 1933 fragment " The Book ." In 2020, the competed story was adapted into a short film of the same name . Selected Bibliography Short Fiction "The Black Tome of Alsophocus" (1980) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads at iMDB References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#C_is_for_Cthulhu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Storybooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>C is for Cthulhu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Jason Ciaramella Greg Murphy English Genre(s) Children's Educational ComixTribe : The Lovecraft Alphabet Book is a children's educational book which takes its cue from H. P. Lovecraft 's Cthulhu Mythos . Overview Written by Jason Ciaramella and featuring digital illustrations by Greg Murphy, contains 26 Mythos-related images each representing a letter of the alphabet. It was published in 2016 by ComixTribe in both board book and digital formats. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jason_Ciaramella"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Children's</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Educational</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#I_Love_You,_Hastur">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>I Love You, Hastur</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Norbert Gora English James Ward Kirk Publishing Lovecraft after Dark date November 2015 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos poem by Polish Norbert Gora . It was first seen in the anthology Lovecraft after Dark by James Ward Kirk Publishing in November 2015. Overview The six three-line verses describe the terrible seduction of a beautiful woman by the Great Old One Hastur .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Norbert_Gora"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_after_Dark"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Harry_Houdini">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AlterEgosCharactersIncorporatedFromTheRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsCorrespondents"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Harry Houdini</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1926 October 31</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Hungarian-american illusionist and stunt performer, noted for sensational escape acts . First attracted notice in vaudeville in the us and then as "handcuff houdini" on a tour of europe . In 1904, thousands watched as he tried to escape from special handcuffs commissioned by the daily mirror .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>Austria-Hungary Budapest</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasYearsActiveR>1891–1926</ex:hasYearsActiveR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1874 March 24</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Return_of_the_Old_Ones">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Return of the Old Ones</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Apocalyptic lovecraftian horror is a collection of thematically-linked short stories . Edited by brian m. sammons, the book is divided into three sections . Each section focuses on an aspect of the cthulhu mythos .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror Anthology</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>304</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Simon_of_Gitta">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Simon of Gitta</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Simon is an ex-gladiator turned sorcerer, using his skills with sword and sorcery alike . He first appeared in "the ring of set", published in swords against darkness #1 in 1977 . Tierney based on the biblical figure of simon magus, inspired by jack palance .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>1985) The Gardens of Lucullus (with Glenn Rahman</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>1985) The Throne of Achamoth (with Robert M. Price</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>2001) The Drums of Chaos (2008) The Secret of Nephren-Ka (by Robert M. Price</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>2018)</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The Sword of Spartacus (1978) The Fire of Mazda (May-Jun 1984) The Seed of the Star–God (1984) The Blade of the Slayer (1986) The Soul of Kephri (Sum 1984) The Ring of Set (1977) The Worm of Urakhu (1988) The Curse of the Crocodile (1987) The Treasure of Horemkhu (1987) The Scroll of Thoth (1977) The Dragons of Mons Fractus (Spr 1984) The Pillars of Melkarth (1990) The Wedding of Sheila-Na-Gog (with Glenn Rahman</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Sword_of_Spartacus"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_L._Tierney"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Herbert_West:_Reanimator_%28Nick_OGorman%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleComicBookAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Herbert West: Reanimator (Nick O'Gorman)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Herbert West: Reanimator H. P. Lovecraft (original story) Nick O'Gorman (adaptation) Nick O'Gorman Cover Artist Nick O'Gorman English Target Destroyed Comics Release Date 2022 Herbert West: Reanimator is a comic book-format adaptation of the H. P. Lovecraft short story of the same name by Canadian artist Nick O'Gorman . It was published through his independent label Target Destroyed Comics in 2022 after obtaining funding through a successful Kickstarter campaign . The book was later included in the omnibus collection HPL Vol. 1: Comic Adaptations of the Works of HP Lovecraft , also published by Target Destroyed. References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nick_OGorman"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2022</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Peddler%E2%80%99s_Tale,_or_Isobel%E2%80%99s_Revenge">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CaitlinRKiernanWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Peddler’s Tale, or Isobel’s Revenge</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A short story by caitlin r. kiernan first appeared in the december 2013 issue of sirena digest . It serves as a sequel to "pickman's madonna" the story was republished in the mammoth book of cthulhu and the red brain .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Caitlin_R._Kiernan"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sirenia_Digest"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 2013</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ash_Williams">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ash Williams</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ash is a hero in the fictional evil dead and army of darkness universes . Created in 1981 by sam raimi for his film the evil dead . Has battled demons, deadites, sorcerers, vampires, werewolves, aliens, and super-powered beings .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Evil Dead</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sam_Raimi"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Nameless_Offspring">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ClarkAshtonSmithWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Nameless Offspring</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Story first appeared in the june 1932 issue of strange tales of mystery and terror . It is a short story by writer clark ashton smith . Story is about a woman who dies after giving birth to a monstrous child . It is believed to belong to the cthulhu mythos .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Strange Tales of Mystery</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Terror</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>June 1932</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#BrianLumleyWorks">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Candlemas_1986">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu Candlemas 1986</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Various Cover Artist Chris Gross United States English Crypt of Cthulhu Cryptic Publications Release Date February 1986 64 Preceded by Yuletide 1985 Followed by Eastertide 1986 is the 37th issue of long-running fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Running to a total of 64 , it was edited by Robert M. Price , featured cover art by Chris Gross, interior art by Gross, Lance Brown, Pierre D. Lacroix, Darrell Schweitzer , and Nicholas Roerich, and was published in an A5 format by Cryptic Publications in February 1986 . Contents Editorial Shards by Robert M. Price "The Structure of Lovecraft's Longer Narratives" by S. T. Joshi "Lovecraft's Cosmic History" by Robert M. Price "H. P. Lovecraft and The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath" by S. T. Joshi "Real World Links in The Dream-Quest" by Ralph E. Vaughan "Do Shoggoths Lurk in The Case of Charles Dexter Ward?" by Will Murray "The Sleep of Reason Produces Monsters: An Unbridled Romp with Charles Dexter Ward Through the Minds of Francisco Goya and C. G. Jung" by Carolyn Lee Boyd "A Note on Nicholas Roerich" by Ben P. Indick "Further Notes on the Eltdown Shards" by Edward P. Berglund Reviews by Steven Mariconda and Daniel Gobbett Letters by A. Beaufort, Darrell Schweitzer , Kevin A. Ross , Steven Mariconda, Rodolfo A. Ferraresi, Charles Garofalo , Robert Bloch , and Lin Carter References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>64</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>February 1986</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Doyle_Jeffries">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Doyle Jeffries</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>The Arkham Advertiser</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Doyle Jefferies (frequent mispelling)</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Arkham horror first appeared in the arkham horror second edition expansion the king in yellow as a blight, a character which imparts negative effects onto the players .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ire_of_the_Void"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_King_in_Yellow"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fantasy_Flight_Games"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Travellers_by_Night">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AugustDerlethWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Travellers by Night</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist James Dietrich United States English Genre(s) Horror Anthology Arkham House Release Date 1964 Media Type Print is a collection of short horror stories edited by August Derleth . It is notable for containing the first printing of " The Horror from the Middle Span ", a " posthumous collaboration " between Derleth and H. P. Lovecraft . Behind the Mythos First published in a run of 2,486 copies in 1964 by Derleth's specialist Arkham House , contains a total of 14 stories, none of which had been previously published. Contents "The Cicerones", by Robert Aickman "Episode on Cain Street", by Joseph Payne Brennan "The Cellars", by J. Ramsey Campbell "The Man Who Rode the Trains", by Paul A. Carter "A Handful of Silver", by Mary Elizabeth Counselman "Denkirch", by David Drake "The Wild Man of the Sea", by William Hope Hodgson "The Unpleasantness at Carver House", by Carl Jacobi "The Terror of Anerley House School", by Margery Lawrence " The Horror From the Middle Span ", by H. P. Lovecraft and August Derleth "Not There", by John Metcalfe "Family Tree", by Frank D. Thayer, Jr. "Death of a Bumblebee", by H. Russell Wakefield "The Crater", by Donald Wandrei</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror Anthology</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1964</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mortal_Coils">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
