    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mordiggian"/>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_Upton_Pickman"/>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Baoht Z'uqqa-mogg</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Corpse Eaters</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Dark Kin</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Fiends</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Ghûls</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Grave Lurkers</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Grave Robbers</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Tomb Dwellers</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ghouls are a species of cannibalistic humanoids featured in several of h. p. lovecraft's works, where they feed principally (but not exclusively) on the bodies of the dead . They are first mentioned in the short story " the hound ", although ambiguously, as the word might be intended as just a synonym for "graverobber" ghouls are first patently depicted in the short story " pickman's model ", where the e</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Beneath Evil Skies Love Is Forbidden</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Hallowe'en in a Suburb The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath The Nameless Offspring The Dark Eidolon Strange Eons</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Howl</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>We Croak</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pickmans_Model"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Hound (possibly)</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Cultus_Issue_9">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Cultus Issue 9</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu Cultus #9 Various Cover Artist Tani Jantsang United States English P. Marsh Release Date January 1998 62 Preceded by Cthulhu Cultus #8 Followed by Cthulhu Cultus #10 Cthulhu Cultus #9 is an issue of sporadic American fanzine Cthulhu Cultus , edited by Tani Jantsang and James Ambuehl and published by P. Marsh in January 1998. Contents " People of the Monolith " (Part 3 of 7) by Crispin Burnham " Folks of Innsmouth " by Franklyn Searight "Passiflora" by D. F. Lewis " Innsmouth Harvest " by R. S. Cartwright "Select Offer Marsh Family Associates" by Tani Jantsang (as Anon.) "The Sign of the Yellow Servitor" by Robert Bee " Atlachnaphobia " by James Ambuehl "The Lay of Renfield" by Philip Marsh "The Statue" by Sean Rodgers "Subaqueous Dreams" by Tracy Ambuehl "Down on the Farm" by Tracy Ambuehl "I Am Nyarlathotep!" by P. J. Roberts "Clean this Leonides: Haggopian's Third Wife" by Tani Jantsang and Philip Obed Marsh "The Uninvited" by James Ambuehl</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>62</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>January 1998</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ghadamon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ghadamon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Great Old Ones</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Seed of Azathoth</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>'s minions are large black cone-like fish with huge jaws and heads that taper back sharply to a narrow snake-like tail . It emits and absorbs a mix of noxious gas and slime through an ever shifting cycle of pustules and orifices . Despite having no established cult, its attempted incursion into the waking world was supported by the deep ones .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecrafts_Dreamlands"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Keith_Herber"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Innsmouth_Harvest">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Innsmouth Harvest</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ran cartwright (as r. s. cartwright) first published in 1997 edition of nightscapes zine . First reprinted in the collections innsmouth tales - 2000 (2000), y'ha-nthlei rising and other tales of the deep ones (2006), and of gods &amp; aliens (2011) .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ran_Cartwright"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 1997</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Michaelmas_1989">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu Michaelmas 1989</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Bruce W. Timm H. E. Fassl Cover Artist Daryl Hutchinson United States English Crypt of Cthulhu Cryptic Publications Release Date September 1989 72 Preceded by Lammas 1989 Followed by Hallowmas 1989 was the 67th issue of Robert M. Price 's long-running Lovecraftian fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Edited by Price and running to a total of 72 in an A5 format, it was published by Cryptic Publications in September 1989. Cover art was by Daryl Hutchinson, with interior work provided by Bruce W. Timm and H. E. Fassl . This issue is notable as being a special edition dedicated to the late British John Glasby (though Glasby had not yet passed on at the time of its publication). Contents Editorial Shards: The Glasby Revelation by Mike Ashley "The Ring of the Hyades" by John S. Glasby " Drawn from Life " by John S. Glasby " The Old One " by John S. Glasby "The Dweller in Darkness" by John S. Glasby Reviews by Stefan Dziemianowicz and Robert M. Price Letters by J. Brawley, Robert A. W. Lowndes , Donald R. Burleson , James Rockhill, Paul R. Wilson, Chet Williamson, Robert Bloch , Marc A. Michaud , Louis A. Irmo, Stefan Dziemianowicz</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>72</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>September 1989</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hazel_Heald">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsCoAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Hazel Heald</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1961 February 4</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Hazel drake heald was an american horror and weird fiction author from somerville, massachusetts . She is best known as a revision client of lovecraft .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>American</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1896 April 6</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Underwater">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosFilmAndTelevision"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Underwater</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Filming finished in may 2017, due to issues with production company 20th century fox it did not receive a cinematic release until january 2020 . The film has received generally middling to mediocre reviews and was a box office flop .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>10 January 2020</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Michael_Shea">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Michael Shea</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>16 February 2014</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Michael shea was an american author who wrote in the horror, fantasy and science fiction genres . He won two world fantasy awards, and was nominated for a hugo award, two nebula awards, and the british fantasy society's august derleth award . Much of his lovecraftian work is set in his home town of los angeles, california .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA http://example.org/lovecraft#California Los Angeles</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://www.michaelsheaauthor.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:hasYearsActiveR>1974-2014</ex:hasYearsActiveR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>3 July 1946</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sesqua_Valley">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sesqua Valley</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a fictional locale created by the late american author w. h. pugmire for many of his cthulhu mythos stories . Jeffrey thomas has also written at least one short story, " through obscure glass ", based in the setting . Notable features sesqua town a reasonably-sized urban settlement complete with its own business district .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Geographical region</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Continental United States</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Pacific Northwest</ex:isLocatedAtL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ghattambah">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ghattambah</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Hungry One</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Insect-God</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The insect-god is a being created by mark mclaughlin and michael sheehan, jr. for their cthulhu mythos stories . Described as being exists outside of time and space, and is said to be able to gift those it deems worthy with fantastic powers . The high priest of 's human cult, azmael mondrago, has shown no compunction in destroying those who trespass within its chapel .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Michael Sheehan. Jr.</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Owned">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Owned</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>T. S. Woolard English James Ward Kirk Publishing Lovecraft after Dark date November 2015 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos poem by T. S. Woolard. It was published in the James Ward Kirk Publishing anthology Lovecraft after Dark in November 2015. Synopsis The thirteen lines describe Great Cthulhu ’s seduction of a woman, the first in what is promised to become many.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>T. S. Woolard</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_after_Dark"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fred_C._Adams">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Fred C. Adams</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Fred Calvin Adams, Jr. Pseudonym(s) F. C. Adams Jason Sharpe Connection to the Mythos Author Editor Birth Date 1949 Fred Calvin Adams, Jr. (born 1949) is an author and editor who has worked on several Cthulhu Mythos works in a career spanning decades. One of his most notable contributions to the Mythos is his Translations from the Book of Yng series, five linked short stories published in the Edward P. Berglund -edited online zine Nightscapes in 1998. Selected Bibliography Editor Spoor Anthology (1974) Short Fiction " The Son of Misharga " (1998) " The Temple of Bones " (1998) " The Debt of the Summoner " (1998) " The Punishment of Igharta " (1998) " The Fleece of Yaggar " (1998) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Fred Calvin Adams. Jr.</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>F. C. Adams</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Jason Sharpe</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1949</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brown_University">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsIncorporatedFromTheRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Brown University</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Providence , rhode island educational institute is an ivy league college located in providence , rhode island . It was lovecraft's ambition to attend brown to study astronomy; his failure to graduate from high school was a source of</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Educational institute</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Rhode_Island"/>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Providence</ex:isLocatedAtL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Yoth-Tlaggon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Yoth-Tlaggon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The name was mentioned in a letter from howard phillips lovecraft to clark ashton smith . Ken asamatsu depicted it as a slug-like being covered with wrinkles and scales, and endowed with innumerable eyes .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Mask of Yoth-Tlaggon</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ken_Asamatsu"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Thought_He_Was_a_Goner">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Thought He Was a Goner</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story is a scene from west's childhood, as the future mad scientist accidentally reanimates his first corpse, a schoolyard bully . The title is a line from the song "the cat came back," about a man whose cat keeps coming back despite all his attempts to get rid of it . "sharp as a tack, smart as a whip, cleverest student i’ve ever been ... but insolent," says a teacher .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Christine_Morgan"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Legacy_of_the_Reanimator"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2015 December 4</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Among_the_pictures_are_these:">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RamseyCampbellWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Among the pictures are these:</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Among the pictures are these: is a short essay written by ramsey campbell and included in his 1985 collection of short horror fiction cold print . Written in 1973, the essay did not see until 1981 in the march 16th edition of nyctalops magazine . It was later reprinted in the 1987 and 1993 reprints by tor books and headline, respectively .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ramsey_Campbell"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United Kingdom</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Essay</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Cold Print</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Nyctalops</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Nyctalops</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>16th March 1981</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>1973</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jesse_Bullington">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Jesse Bullington</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Pseudonym(s) Alex Marshall Connection to the Mythos Editor Birth Date 14 April 1982 Pennsylvania, USA United States Historical fantasy https://jessebullington.com/ (born 14 April 1982) is an American historical fantasy novelist and occasional editor based in Boulder, Colorado. His editorial credits include the Stone Skin Press anthologies Letters to Lovecraft and Swords v. Cthulhu (the latter in partnership with Molly Tanzer ). In April 2014, he gave a reading of his work at the H. P. Lovecraft Film Festival in Portland, Oregon . Selected Bibliography Editor Letters to Lovecraft (2014) Swords v. Cthulhu (2016, with Molly Tanzer ) External Links at Wikipedia at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads Alex Marshall at Goodreads References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Pennsylvania</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Alex Marshall</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://jessebullington.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>14 April 1982</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft:_A_Look_Behind_the_Cthulhu_Mythos">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lovecraft: A Look Behind the Cthulhu Mythos</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lovecraft: a look behind the "cthulhu mythos" is a 1972 non-fiction book written by lin carter . He attempted to define what was and was not "part of" the mythos . His conclusions are wildly out of step with accepted wisdom today .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lin_Carter"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1972</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Tales_February_2009">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Periodicals"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Arkham Tales February 2009</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Jean-Marc Velladier English Cold Fusion Media Empire Release Date February 2009 104 Preceded by Arkham Tales November 2008 Followed by Arkham Tales May 2009 was the second issue of Arkham Tales: A Magazine of Weird Fiction , a short-lived quarterly magazine edited by Nathan Shumate . It featured cover art by Jean-Marc Velladier. Contents Editorial: The Future Belongs to the Long Tail by Nathan Shumate "Mi Morena" by K. S. Clay "What Friends Are For" by Dev Jarrett "The Night Guest" by Jason Hardy "Cthulhu's Nightmare" by Bric Barnes "Compositions in Gratitude and Sorrow" by Bret Tallman "A Curse in Any " by Matt Finucane "Uncle Eric's Leather Bound Tale" by Catherine J. Gardner "Some Nutcase" by John Jasper Owens "Drown" by K. S. Conlon "The Key Ring" by Garrett Carcatella "Weather" by Diana Payne Contributor Notes</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>104</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>February 2009</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cosmic_Abstract">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cosmic Abstract</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The cosmic abstracts are eldritch cosmic entities of the fictional universe of marvel by marvel comics . They are witnesses to universal and multiversal events. Guiding, shaping, protecting, destroying, and reshaping universes . They are so alien, that when the spacefaring team of superheroes found themselves on a they perceived them as being on a checkerboard like planet .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Drashi_Khendup">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Drashi Khendup</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Artist United States Horror is a freelance American artist and illustrator who has provided work for several Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game sourcebooks, perhaps most notably the thematic handout supplement Arkham Sanitarium (1997, Chaosium ), which was largely created by Khendup. Selected Works Cover Art Arkham Sanitarium (1997) Interior Art In the Shadows (1995) Arkham Sanitarium (1997) Secrets (1997, maps) Unseen Masters (2001) External Links at Goodreads at ArtStation References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Temple_of_Bones">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Temple of Bones</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"the debt of the summoner" is a pseudo-factual cthulhu mythos work by and editor f. c. adams . It is the second in his translations from the book of yng . The translation tells of a city named ikhyr, built by the worshippers of hastur .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>F. C. Adams</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Translations_from_the_Book_of_Yng"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Nightscapes #9</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>September 1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Kassogtha">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Kassogtha</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Great Old Ones</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>K'ssuggtha</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Leviathan of Disease</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Ochre Demon</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Coiled One</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu's sister and is depicted as the mother of nctosa and nctolhu . This old one seems to be more like some cosmic parasite, able to merge or join with other significant entities for periods of time . This old one is rarely heard of or encountered in mythos lore .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCharacterMateC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:hasCharacterMateC>Rynvyk</ex:hasCharacterMateC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nightmares_Disciple"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Joseph S. Pulver. Sr.</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jane_Ann_Place">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSRelatives"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Jane Ann Place</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>28 January 1899</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation></ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isRelativeToR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1836</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Christopher_Smith_Adair">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Christopher Smith Adair</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Games Designer United States Horror https://csmithadair.com/ is an American games designer and editor perhaps best known for his contributions to Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu horror roleplaying game, both through Chaosium themselves and third-party content providers. Selected Bibliography Gaming Supplements New Tales of the Miskatonic Valley (2008, scenario designer) Atomic-Age Cthulhu (2013, scenario designer) Doors to Darkness (2016, scenario designer) An Inner Darkness (scenario designer)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://csmithadair.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games Designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Tales:_Legends_of_the_Haunted_City">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Arkham Tales: Legends of the Haunted City</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Arkham Tales Various United States English Genre(s) Horror Chaosium Release Date July 2006 Media Type Print 288 , also known as Arkham Tales: New Terrors Threaten Arkham , is an anthology of Cthulhu Mythos stories. Overview First published by Chaosium in July 2006, Arkham Tales was edited by William Jones , contains a total of 17 short stories and runs to 288 . The works contained within are all thematically linked by the city of Arkham created by H. P. Lovecraft . Contents Introduction to Arkham Tales by William Jones "Mysterious Dan's Legacy" by Matthew Baugh "Vaughn's Diary" by Robert Vaughn "The Orb" by Tony Campbell "The Nether Collection" by Cody Goodfellow "Worms" by Pat Harrigan " They Thrive in Darkness " by Ron Shiflet "What Sorrows May Come" by Lee Clark Zumpe "Arkham Pets" by James Ambuehl "Small Ghost" by Michael Minnis "Burnt Tea" by Michael Dziesinski "Arkham Rain" by John Goodrich "Regrowth" by David Conyers " The Idea of Fear " by C. J. Henderson "Disconnected" by Brian M. Sammons "The Lady in the Grove" by Scott Lette "On Leave to Arkham" by Bill Bilstad "Geometry of the Soul" by Jason Andrew</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>288</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 2006</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Woven_Offspring">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Woven Offspring</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story is loosely connected to the cthulhu mythos through its setting of the sesqua valley . It was first published in issue #9 of lore magazine in 1998 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#W._H._Pugmire"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Lore #4</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Miscellaneous_Impressions_of_H.P.L.">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Miscellaneous Impressions of H.P.L.</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>" miscellaneous impressions of h.p.l. " Is a short non-fiction work by marian f. bonner . It was first published in the scholarly collection rhode island on lovecraft in 1945, where it was mistakenly credited to marian f. barner</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Marion F. Bonner</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Rhode_Island_on_Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1945</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#John_H._Crowe_III">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>John H. Crowe III</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Pseudonym(s) John Crowe John H. Crowe John Crowe III Connection to the Mythos Games designer Editor United States Roleplaying games is an American games designer and one-time editor who has produced a number of Cthulhu Mythos -related works, primarily for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu horror roleplaying game. His works have been published through third-party content provider Pagan Publishing , including in their Call of Cthulhu -dedicated roleplaying fanzine The Unspeakable Oath , an issue of which Crowe also edited. Selected Works Editor The Unspeakable Oath (2001) Gaming Supplements Courting Madness (1992, contributor) The Weapons Compendium (1993) 1920s Investigators' Companion (1993, with Keith Herber ) The Resurrected Volume Two: Of Keys &amp; Gates (1994, with Christian Klepac and John Tynes ) Walker in the Wastes (1994) Coming Full Circle (1995) The Realm of Shadows (1997) Mortal Coils (1998, contributor) The Resurrected III: Out of the Vault (2002, contributor) Final Flight (2008) The Mysteries of Mesoamerica (2009, contributor) Bumps in the Night (2012) External Links at Wikipedia at RPGGeek</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>John Crowe</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>John Crowe III</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>John H. Crowe</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gervasio_Gallardo">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gervasio Gallardo</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Artist Birth Date 5 June 1934 Barcelona, Spain Spanish (born 5 June 1934) is a prolific Spanish artist working in the science fiction and fantasy genres. During his career he has provided cover art and interior illustration for many publications, including Ballantine's Adult Fantasy Series , who published a series of Lovecraft 's works both individually and in boxed set formats. Selected Works Cover Art The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath (1970) Fungi from Yuggoth and Other Poems (1971) The Doom that Came to Sarnath (1971) The Survivor and Others (1971) The World of H. P. Lovecraft (1971) See Also Images by External Links at Wikipedia at The Internet Speculative Fiction Database</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>Barcelona Spain</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>Spanish</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>5 June 1934</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dark_Mans_Assassin">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Dark Man's Assassin</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A man who hears murderous voices in his head is recruited by his brother to perform a service for the evil entity known as the dark man . However, the voices persuade the man that this would be an incredibly bad idea, and he double crosses both his brother and the dark man .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>April 2008</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Yog-Sothoth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OuterGods"/>
    <rdfs:label>Yog-Sothoth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Outer Gods Fractal Creatures</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Crouch End )</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Lurker at the Threshold The Key</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>the Gate The Beyond One Opener of the Way The All-in-One The One-in-All All-Encompassing Yogge-Sothothe Yok-Zothoth Yog-Sototl Iog-Sotôt Yogsoggoth ( Jerusalem's Lot</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The nameless mist is a fictional cosmic entity and outer god created by h. p. lovecraft . He is the progenitor of cthulhu , hastur the unspeakable, and the ancestor of the voorhees . He is a core part of the cthulhu mythos, which lovecraft would refer facetiously as "yog-sothery"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The Last Test History of the Necronomicon The Dunwich Horror The Electric Executioner The Whisperer in Darkness At the Mountains of Madness The Horror in the Museum Through the Gates of the Silver Key The Haunter of the Dark The Secret of Kralitz Dig Me No Grave The Druid of Castle Rock</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasCharacterMateC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lavinia_Whateley"/>
    <ex:hasCharacterMateC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shub-Niggurath"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Case_of_Charles_Dexter_Ward"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Charlene_Hines">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Charlene Hines</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>2019 in the South Pacific</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>The Department of Extraordinary Phenomena</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Charlene is a character created by author troy young for his nyarlathotep cycle series of cthulhu mythos stories . At the time of her introduction, she is serving a lengthy jail sentence for murder . On one mission, she encountered the spirit of long-dead sorcerer pierre girault which briefly possessed her .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shade_of_Pierre_Girault"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Troy_Young"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#En_Sabah_Nur">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>En Sabah Nur</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Apocalypse is an ancient mutant, one of the oldest on earth, who has been around for over 3000 years . He has clashed many times with the x-men and their allies, and his agents have fought other heroes . He was created in 1986 and has been one of its main supervillains, an a-list villain with a few comic book series .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#X-Men:_Apocalypse_vs_Dracula"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Tapped Out</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Boby Layton</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Jackson Guice</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Louise Simonson</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ithaqua_%28story%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AugustDerlethWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ithaqua (story)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"ithaqua" is a short story by august derleth, written in 1933 . It first appeared in the february 1941 issue of strange stories . The story further develops the entity ithaqua, introduced in "the thing that walked on the wind"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#August_Derleth"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Strange_Stories"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>February 1941</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>1933</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Sinking_City">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Sinking City</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A lone yithian takes control of a human from the present and transports him into the distant past, using his body to infiltrate the great city . Although not making it as far as the centre, the yithian detonates its displacement bomb, ripping a chunk from r'lyeh and causing it to sink . It contributes to the expanded cthulhu mythos by providing a story about events behind the original mythos story of cthulh</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Konstantine_Paradias"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>August 2014</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Beyond_the_Mountains_of_Madness">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Beyond the Mountains of Madness</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Robert M. Price , ed. Cover Artist Steven Gilberts English Genre(s) Cthulhu Mythos Celaeno Press Release Date April 30, 2015 291 is an anthology of Cthulhu Mythos stories related to H. P. Lovecraft 's novella At the Mountains of Madness . It was edited by Robert M. Price and published by Celaeno Press in 2015. Contents "Introduction" (essay) by Robert M. Price "The City at the Two Magnetic Poles" by Glynn Owen Barrass "The Second Wave of Fear" by Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. "Second Death" by Pierre V. Comtois (2015) "Beneath the Mountains of Madness" by Pete Rawlik "The Continent of Madness" by Ken Asamatsu "Gedney" by Laurence J. Cornford " The Pleasure in Madness " by C. J. Henderson "A Biting Cold" by Brian M. Sammons " Garden of the Gods " by Cody Goodfellow "The Danforth Project" by Stephen Mark Rainey "Tekeli-Li!" by Edward Morris " Static " by Will Murray " Into the Black " by William Meikle "In Amundsen's Tent" by John Martin Leahy (1928)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_M._Price"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>ed.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>291</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2015 April 30</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft:_His_Life_and_Work">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>H. P. Lovecraft: His Life and Work</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Kenneth W. Faig, Jr. S. T. Joshi United States English Genre(s) Non-fiction Ohio University Press H. P. Lovecraft: Four Decades of Criticism date 1980 " " is a short non-fiction work by Kenneth W. Faig, Jr. and S. T. Joshi . Overview Running to around twenty pages in length, "" was published in the collection of essays H. P. Lovecraft: Four Decades of Criticism , which was also edited by Joshi, by Ohio University Press in 1980.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#S._T._Joshi"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Kenneth W. Faig. Jr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft:_Four_Decades_of_Criticism"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1980</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robin_D._Laws">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Robin D. Laws</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Games designer Author Editor Birth Date 14 October 1964 Orillia, Ontario, Canada Canada http://robin-d-laws.blogspot.com/ (born 14 October 1964) is a Canadian author, games designer, and editor. The creator of the popular GUMSHOE system used in several Cthulhu Mythos roleplaying games, his gaming credits include both core and supplementary material for Trail of Cthulhu , Dungeons &amp; Dragons , Cthulhu Confidential , The Yellow King and the World of Darkness universe, while his writing credits include a collection of Yellow Mythos short fiction, as well as tie-in pieces for the Warhammer tabletop miniatures games and Pathfinder roleplaying game. Selected Bibliography Editor Shotguns v. Cthulhu (2012) Collections New Tales of the Yellow Sign (2012) Novels The Missing and the Lost (2019, The Technician ) Fifth Imperative (2022, The Technician ) Games Cthulhu Confidential (2017, with Chris Spivey and Ruth Tillman ) High Voltage Kill The House Up in the Hills Even Death Can Die Shadows Over Filmland (2009, with Kenneth Hite ) The Armitage Files (2010) Stunning Eldritch Tales Dreamhounds of Paris The Yellow King Absinthe in Carcosa (2020) Black Star Magic Legions of Carcosa External Links at Wikipedia at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>Canada Ontario Orillia</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>Canada</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://robin-d-laws.blogspot.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>14 October 1964</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#DJ_Tyrer">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>DJ Tyrer</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>David-john tyrer is a prolific poet, short story writer, indie publishing house founder, and occasional artist . His work has appeared in anthologies such as swords against cthulhu , swords against cthulhu ii: hyperborean nights , corporate cthulhu , the averoigne legacy .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>David-John Tyrer</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United Kingdom</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://djtyrer.blogspot.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
