  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jahrhundertsommer">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Jahrhundertsommer</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>is one of the adventures provided in the hardcover sourcebook Berlin: The Wicked City published in January 2002 by Chaosium for the Call of Cthulhu RPG . It is written by Jan Christoph Steines. Synopsis The adventure is set in 1925 in post-World War I Berlin. The city is licentious, steeped in sex and the occult and turbulent with cross currents of extremist politics. It features a version of the pagan god Swarog , which in this case is a Great Old One , as would be expected.</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Yekubian">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Yekubian</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Being of Yekub</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Inhabitant of Yekub</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Yekubians are a fictional alien species created by h. p. lovecraft , robert e. howard , frank belknap long , abraham merritt , and c. l. moore . Chaosium incorporated them into its call of cthulhu roleplaying game in the bestiary book ye booke of monstres . They are a race of worm-creatures whom invade worlds and exchange their minds with those of the natives .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Challenge_from_Beyond"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Abraham_Merritt"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#C._L._Moore"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Frank_Belknap_Long"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Howard_Phillips_Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_E._Howard"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Leigh_Blackmore">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Critics"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scholars"/>
    <rdfs:label>Leigh Blackmore</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Leigh David Blackmore Connection to the Mythos Scholar Poet Author Birth Date Flub Sydney, Australia Australian https://ahrasitra.wordpress.com/ Leigh David Blackmore (born 30 June 1959) is an Australian author, poet, critic, editor and occultist who has written numerous works in and about the greater Cthulhu Mythos , including bibliographies of Brian Lumley and H. P. Lovecraft himself. His short works have appeared in periodicals such as The Arkham Sampler , Shoggoth , and Lovecraft Annual , and anthologies including Eldritch Horrors: Dark Tales (2009, H. Harksen Productions). Blackmore has cited H. P. Lovecraft, Clark Ashton Smith , Edgar Rice Burroughs , Robert Bloch , Frank Belknap Long , Donald Wandrei , Ramsey Campbell , and Lin Carter as amongst his influences . Selected Works Poetry "Pickman's Models" (1986) "The Statement of Randolph Carter" (1986) "Ubbo-Sathla" (1987) "Lovecraftian Sonnets" (1993) "In a Sequestered Churchyard Where Once Lovecraft Walked" (2010) Non-Fiction H. P. Lovecraft and Lovecraft Criticism: An Annotated Bibliography, Supplement, 1980-1984 (1985, with S. T. Joshi ) External Links at Wikipedia at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>http://example.org/lovecraft#Australia Sydney</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Leigh David Blackmore</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>Australian</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://ahrasitra.wordpress.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Poet</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Scholar</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>Flub</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tom_Lynch">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tom Lynch</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Games Designer Author United States Roleplaying games Horror fiction is an American author and games designer who has worked on a number of books for Chaosium 's popular Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game, as well as writing several separate Mythos tales. His Lovecraftian fiction has appeared in anthologies such as The Dark Rites of Cthulhu , The Children of Gla'aki , and Arkham Detective Agency . Lynch was also the co-founder, with Keith Herber , of the now-defunct Miskatonic River Press , an independent publisher established in 2008 which specialised in Lovecraftian gaming material and fiction. Selected Bibliography Short Fiction " Of Circles and Rings " (2014) The Doom That Came to Providence (2015, round-robin, contributor) "Lines in the Sand" (2015) "Crossing the Line" (2016) " Scion of Chaahk " (2017) " Reeling Back " (2017) Roleplaying Games &amp; Supplements New Tales of the Miskatonic Valley (2009) Atomic-Age Cthulhu (2013) Island of Ignorance: The Third Cthulhu Companion (2013, contributor) Tales of the Sleepless City (2013, contributor) Doors to Darkness (2016) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games Designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lynne_Jamneck">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scholars"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lynne Jamneck</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Editor Birth Date 7 December 1975 South Africa Horror Erotica http://lynnejamneckdiaries.blogspot.com/ (born 7 December 1975) is a South African-born author and editor who has produced a number of Lovecraftian works in both of these capacities. Her fiction has appeared in anthologies such as Black Wings V and Black Wings VI , and periodicals such as H. P. Lovecraft's Magazine of Horror and Lovecraft Annual , while her editorial credits include Dreams From the Witch House , an anthology Lovecraftian fiction by female authors, and Gothic Lovecraft , which was produced in partnership with leading H. P. Lovecraft scholar S. T. Joshi . Jamneck also holds an MA in English Literature from Auckland University, New Zealand. Selected Works Editor Dreams From the Witch House (2015) Gothic Lovecraft (2016, with S. T. Joshi ) Non-Fiction " Tekeli-li! Disturbing Language in Edgar Allan Poe and H. P. Lovecraft " (2012) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>South Africa</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://lynnejamneckdiaries.blogspot.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>7 December 1975</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Lies_Dreaming">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Lies Dreaming</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here Ron Shiflet United States English Publication date March 1997 " " is a short poem by American Ron Shiflet . Synopsis The two verses are a cautionary tale against the power of the Great Old One Cthulhu , and a warning to stay vigilant against his insidious power. Publication History "" was first published in the zine Cthulhu Cultus #8 in March 1997. It later appeared in contemporary zines Nightscapes (September 1997) and Dark Legacy #3 (March 1997).</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 1997</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
