  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Deep_One">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ServitorRaces"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Deep One</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Mother Hydra Dagon Cult of Dagon Cthulhu</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Fish Folks</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Kelpie</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Mermaids</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Merrows</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Nommo</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Rusalki</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Scaled Ones</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Sea Demon</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Sea People</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Sirens</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Those From Below</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Deep ones are a fictional species of ocean-dwelling humanoids, created by h. p. lovecraft for his story " dagon " . The deep ones are presented as worshipping the cthulhu mythos entity father dagon and his consort mother hydra . They are also known as fish folks, kelpie, mermaids, merrows, nommo, rusalki, sea people, scaled ones, sirens, those from</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Discovery_of_the_Ghooric_Zone"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Strange_Eons"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Deep_Ones"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_in_the_Gallery"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Seal_of_Rlyeh"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Thing_on_the_Doorstep"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Trail_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Innsmouth Harvest Neonomicon</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The Frog (possibly)</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>Humanoid fish-frog entities</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>appearing fish- or frog-like</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>hands. Their heads vary in disposition</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>skin is a grayish-green to blue. Shiny</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>slippery</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>sometime rugose</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>their scaled</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>thin-lipped mouths possessing shark-like teeth. On their necks may be present palpitating gills.</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>webbed feets</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>wide</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>with fine-like ridges on their backs</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>with prodigious bulging eyes</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shadow_Over_Innsmouth"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Dagon (unnamed)</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#John_Pelan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Critics"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosInspiredAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scholars"/>
    <rdfs:label>John Pelan</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>12 April 2021</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name John C. Pelan Connection to the Mythos Author Editor Scholar/critic Publisher Birth Date 19 July 1957 Seattle, Washington, USA Death Date 12 April 2021 Albuquerque, New Mexico, USA United States Science fiction Weird fiction Horror  John C. Pelan (19 July 1957 - 12 April 2021 ) was an American author, editor, scholar/critic, and small-press publisher who had a hand in numerous works of Lovecraftian and Cthulhu Mythos fiction over the course of a career which spanned over 40 years, both on his own and in collaboration with others. Pelan's first novel, The Colour Out of Darkness (1998, Sideshow Press), was heavily influenced by H. P. Lovecraft 's 1927 short story " The Colour Out of Space ". His editorial credits include the anthologies The Children of Cthulhu , Shadows Over Baker Street (2002 and 2003 respectively, Del Ray/Ballantine), and The Last Continent: New Tales of Zothique (1999, Shadowlands Press), as well as collections by Fritz Leiber , Michael Shea , and James P. Blaylock, while his fiction has appeared in anthologies such as Black Wings IV (2015, PS Publishing ) and Robert M. Price 's long-running fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Selected Bibliography Editor The Last Continent: New Tales of Zothique (1999) The Children of Cthulhu (2002, with Benjamin Adams ) Shadows Over Baker Street (2003, with Michael Reaves) Author The Colour Out of Darkness (1998) "That's the Story of My Life" (2002, with Benjamin Adams ) "The Mystery of the Worm" (2003) "Contact" (2015, with Stephen Mark Rainey ) External Links at Wikipedia at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Washington Seattle</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>John C. Pelan</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Publisher</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Scholar</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>critic</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>19 July 1957</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Feeder_from_Within_%28entity%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonSapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Feeder from Within (entity)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Tsathoggua (possibly)</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Feeder from Within Sociocultural Characteristics Tsathoggua (possibly) Real World Information First appearance EXP : Feeders from Within Created by Peter J. Evans The Feeders from Within were the titular creatures from the 2013 novel Feeders from Within by Peter J. Evans . Description What the Feeders actually looked like is unknown, but they were stated to be parasitic entities which caused a slow, painful death in their host creature over a period of months, all the while broadcasting infectious thoughts of psychic insanity to others in the vicinity. They may have been servants of the Great Old One Tsathoggua , as they were said to dwell with him in N'kai . Feeders from Within ( EXP ) As part of an unspecified plot, alien Mi-Go Mr. Stone and a number of Silver Twilight Lodge members travelled to N'kai with the intent of "harvesting" the Feeders from Within. The group were foiled by the combined efforts of Mark Harrigan , Diana Stanley and Carolyn Fern .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Feeders_from_Within"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Peter_J._Evans"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Providence_Pals">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPeople"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Providence Pals</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. p. lovecraft the were a group of enthusiasts who shared their mutual interest during the latter part of the 20th century . They were so named for their periodical meetings in providence, rhode island . The group was formed by the members to allow them visit and re-visit sites related to lovecraft's life and works in like-minded company .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Xaligha">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Avatars"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Xa'ligha</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_King_in_Yellow"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Master of Twisted Sound</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Demon of Dissonance</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as Master of Twisted Sound The Demon of Dissonance Current location Dissonance (possible pocket dimension) The King in Yellow First appearance " Correlated Contents " Created by James Ambuehl , the The Demon of Dissonance and The Master of Twisted Sound , appears to be an avatar of Tru'nembra or of Rla Tfuthla, assuming that they are not the same creature. dwells in Dissonance, whatever that cryptic phrase alludes to. "The Xa'lugha Tree" is mentioned as connected with the Lake of Hali and itself acts in concert with the King in Yellow at times. There might be a connection to the Gulf of S'glhuo .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isLocatedAtC>Dissonance (possible pocket dimension)</ex:isLocatedAtC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Correlated_Contents"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Ambuehl"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lee_Clark_Zumpe">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosInspiredAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lee Clark Zumpe</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author St. Petersburg, Florida, USA United States Years Active Late 90s-present https://www.leeclarkzumpe.com/  is an American author and journalist. He has been writing both fiction and poetry for over 20 years, while still holding down a job as a columnist for the Tampa Bay newspapers. Selected Bibliography Short Stories "What Sorrows May Come" (2006) "The Terror of Emmett's Cove" (2009) " A Feast of Death " (2014) "The Temple of Iald-T'qurhoth" (2015) " Dreams of the Docent " (2015) "Shadows Upon the Matanzas" (2016) " Beneath Cayuga's Churning Waves " (2017) " Echo of a Distant Scream " (2017) " The Shadow Resurrection " (2020, with David Hopewell ) " Back to the Black Bog " (2022) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Florida St. Petersburg</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://www.leeclarkzumpe.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:hasYearsActiveR>Late 90s-present</ex:hasYearsActiveR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Haiyoru%21_Nyaruani:_Remember_My_Mr._Lovecraft">
