  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Philip_Jos%C3%A9_Farmer">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Philip José Farmer</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>25 February 2009</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Birth Date 26 January 1918 North Terra Haute, Indiana, USA Death Date 25 February 2009 Peoria, Illinois, USA American Science Fiction Years Active 1946-2009 http://www.philipjosefarmer.com/ (January 26, 1918 – February 25, 2009) was an award-winning American science fiction and fantasy writer, known for his pioneering use of sexual and religious themes in his works. He is perhaps best known for his World of Tiers and Riverworld series . He has written extensively in the worlds of other authors, expanding on the fictional worlds of characters such as Phineas Fogg, Tarzan of Greystoke, Sherlock Holmes, Doc Savage, and The Wizard of Oz's Dorothy . Farmer's 1979 short story " The Freshman " explored the dark side of Miskatonic University and delved deeper into the fictional history of Arkham as a whole . Selected Bibliography Short Fiction " The Freshman " (1979) External Links at Wikipedia at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Indiana North Terra Haute</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>American</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://www.philipjosefarmer.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:hasYearsActiveR>1946-2009</ex:hasYearsActiveR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>26 January 1918</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Daniel_Howard_Jr.">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSRelatives"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Daniel Howard Jr.</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>14 December 1870</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Husband of Betsey Phillips, whom he married 24 September 1809. Daniel was, like his father before him, Town Clerk of Foster, Rhode Island, serving in this capacity for 25 years. During this same time he represented the town of Foster in the Rhode Island House of Representatives, served as a justice of the Court of Common Pleas, and was an associate justice of the Rhode Island Supreme Court. After the death of Betsey Phillips, he married Lurana Wilbur (11 March 1815–18 August 1908) on 21 April 1851.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isRelativeToR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1817</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nightscapes_March_1999">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nightscapes March 1999</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various English Edward P. Berglund Release Date March 1999 Preceded by September 1998 Followed by August 1999 is the tenth issue of the long-running but sporadically-released fanzine Nightscapes . It was edited by the late American and Lovecraft scholar Edward P. Berglund . Contents In addition to the written material listed below, this issue included artwork by Jay Brandt, Nicole Morris, Si Morris and Toren G. Atkinson. Fiction "The Curiosity" by Michael Carter "Them" by Risto Remes "Cthulhu War: Genesis" by Michael Carter "The Statement of Jackson Palmer" by Thomas V. Powers "Necromancer's Legacy" by Peter F. Guenther "The Project" by Jeff Miller "The Isle of Chor-Tal" by Ron Shiflet "Goddess" by Kenneth Silver "Primogen" by Tani Jantsang Non-Fiction "Great Old One Humanity" by Patrick Crocker " A Lovecraft Movie/Television Show to Be Proud of " by Patrick Crocker Parodies "The Really Ugly, Nasty Thing from Hell" by L. Sprague de Carter "The Waiting Doom" by James Ambuehl and Steven Marc Harris Poetry " Lovecraftian Cliff Notes for Ech-Pi-El's: Cool Air " by Franklyn Searight "The Sentinels of Time" by Phillips A. Ellis "The Dark Planet" by Phillips A. Ellis " Tentacled Dreams " by Ron Shiflet " Summoning the Dream-Sender " by Ron Shiflet External Links full issue free online at the Nightscapes website</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>March 1999</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
