    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Peter Slader</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>New York State ( Catskill Mountains ) Human First appearance HPL : " Beyond the Wall of Sleep " Created by H. P. Lovecraft was a neighbor of Joe Slater , both of whom lived in the same community of hill dwellers in the Catskill Mountains of New York State . During one of Joe Slater's uncontrollable manic episodes, Peter attempted to restrain him and was brutally killed. Joe, who was horrified at what he had done after returning to his senses, was tried for murder, found not guilty by reason of insanity, and was confined to a mental hospital . ( HPL : " Beyond the Wall of Sleep ")</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Beyond_the_Wall_of_Sleep"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Resurrectionists">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#TheSalemHawleySeries"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Resurrectionists</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Followed by borne of the deep is a cthulhu mythos novella by american michael patrick hicks . It was first published by high fever books, hicks's own small press label . The story is based around the real-world 1788 doctor's riots in new york city .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Michael_Patrick_Hicks"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>French</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Salem Hawley</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>June 2019</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Cultus_Issue_17">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Periodicals"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Cultus Issue 17</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu Cultus #17 Various Cover Artist Tani Jantsang United States English P. Marsh Release Date January 2000 56 Preceded by Cthulhu Cultus #16 Followed by Cthulhu Cultus #18 Cthulhu Cultus #17 is an issue of sporadic American fanzine Cthulhu Cultus , edited by Tani Jantsang and James Ambuehl and published by P. Marsh in January 2000. Contents "The Unholy Attendant" by P. J. Roberts " Seduced " by Ron Shiflet "Azathoth" by Ron Shiflet " Azathoth Awakening " by R. S. Cartwright "There Are Worst Thungs Than Mere Evil ..." by Andrew Nellis "Cthulhu, You Fat Bastard!" by D. Ian Rogers "Lives of the Rich and Squamous" by John H. Toon "An Incident in a Small Yorkshire Village" by Nick Lancaster "Amphibians" by Duane Pesice "The Thing from the Mound" by Peter F. Guenther "The City in the Sands" by James Ambuehl "Valediction (Part One): Hunter" by R. A. Strong "Dunwick" by Ron Shiflet</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>56</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>January 2000</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Avatars"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CharactersIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdfs:label>Pan</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Great_God_Pan"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Created by arthur machen is an ancient greek god often associated with pastoral scenery, music and fertility . In the call of cthulhu roleplaying game by chaosium, is depicted as an avatar of shub-niggurath .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Tree"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Great_God_Pan"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arthur_Machen"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tim_Curran">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tim Curran</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Pseudonym(s) Jack Hamlyn Connection to the Mythos Author Michigan, United States United States Horror http://www.corpseking.com/ is an American horror fiction author who comes from upper Michigan. He has written a number of stories based in the Cthulhu Mythos inspired by the works of H. P. Lovecraft and many others; his short fiction has appeared in anthologies such as World War Cthulhu , The Children of Gla'aki , and Return of the Old Ones (2014, 2017, and 2017, respectively, all published by Dark Regions Press ). Selected Bibliography Collections Morbid Anatomy (2013) Novels/Novellas The Hive (2005) The Spawning (2010) Morbid Anatomy (2011) Short Fiction "Charnel House" (2005) "Welcome to Goatshead" (2007) " The Procyon Project " (2014) " Night of the Hopfrog " (2017) " Scratching from the Outer Darkness " (2017) External Links at Wikipedia at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>United States Michigan</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Jack Hamlyn</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://www.corpseking.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Corpse_Candles">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Corpse Candles</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Detective grange is given a glimpse into the world of the occult when he is called to attend an incident where an elderly man apparently self-combusted . What he uncovers is a decades-long feud between two brothers, each of whom worship a seperate deity: one a being named only as n, the other likely cthulhu . The story concludes with the death of both brothers, each having summoned lesser minions of their patrons to destroy the other</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jeffrey_Thomas"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>German</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Codex_Hallowmas_1998"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Time_Loopers">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CrossroadPress"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Time Loopers</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Four tales from a time war is an anthology of lovecraftian fiction . It was published in july 2020 by mystique press . It was updated and re-released with new content in july 2023 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Byron_Craft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#David_Hambling"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Matthew_Davenport"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>C.T. Phipps</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>John DeLaughter</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>264</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 2020</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#K._A._Opperman">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosInspiredAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>K. A. Opperman</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Birth Date 12 September 1987 California, USA United States Weird Fiction Fantasy  Kyle A. Opperman is an American poet and author of weird fiction and fantasy. He has written several works around the Cthulhu Mythos first established by H. P. Lovecraft and others. Selected Bibliography Poems " Daemonic Nathicana " (2019)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA http://example.org/lovecraft#California</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>12 September 1987</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lrogg">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Avatars"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lrogg</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>L'gy'hxians</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>In the role-playing game call of cthulhu, it is identified as an avatar of nyarlathotep . Its cult is regarded as comparatively agreeable, as confers benefits to its believers . Its enemies are executed by having acid poured into their brains .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Insects_from_Shaggai"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ramsey_Campbell"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Blupe">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonSapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ServitorRaces"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Blupe</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Blupes are translucent creatures that float through the air and glow faintly in the dark . They can be summoned to both the waking world and the dreamlands . While harmless to most species, contact with a can be fatal to some forms of life, such as the fire vampires and the minions of karakal .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecrafts_Dreamlands"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sandy_Petersen"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dispersing_Vibrato">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dispersing Vibrato</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>산란 비브라토) is a community-created but fully licensed supplement for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu RPG . It is a creation of Korean fans of the system and setting.</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gene_Wolfe">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gene Wolfe</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>14 April 2014</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Gene Rodman Wolfe Connection to the Mythos Author Birth Date 7 May 1931 New York City, New York, USA Death Date 14 April 2014 Peoria, Illinois, USA United States Fantasy Science fiction Gene Rodman Wolfe (7 May 1931 - 14 April 2014) was a prolific American author widely regarded as one of the finest writers of his day, winning several awards and receiving nominations for many more. Although his works largely focus of the science fiction and fantasy genres, he also wrote a number of Lovecraftian pieces which were included in anthologies such as Lovecraft's Legacy (1990, Tor), Cthulhu 2000: A Lovecraftian Anthology (1995, Arkham House ), and The Book of Cthulhu (2011, Night Shade Press). External Links at Wikipedia at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads at WolfeWiki</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>New York USA http://example.org/lovecraft#New_York_City</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Gene Rodman Wolfe</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>7 May 1931</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_2000:_A_Lovecraftian_Anthology">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu 2000: A Lovecraftian Anthology</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>416 is an anthology of lovecraftian short stories with a more contemporary leaning than h. p. lovecraft 's original writings . Edited by jim turner, it was published by arkham house in 1995 and runs to 416 . In january 2004, a german- edition was released by bastei lübbe under the title spur der schatten .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>416</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1995</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hiram">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Hiram</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Servant Human First appearance HPL : " The Tomb " Created by H. P. Lovecraft is the elderly servant of Jervas Dudley in the short story " The Tomb " by H. P. Lovecraft . Jervas has stated that he was always fond of , describing him as a simple man but extremely loyal, and who shares Jervas' love of churchyards. After Jervas was confined to an asylum , investigated the abandoned burial vault of the Hyde family discovered by Jervas, and confirmed enough of his story so that Jervas was convinced of his own sanity.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Tomb"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Destroyer_of_Worlds">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Destroyer of Worlds</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Matt Ruff English Release Date Upcoming Preceded by Lovecraft Country : A Return to Lovecraft Country is a novel by Matt Ruff , and a sequel to his Lovecraft Country (2016). It was released by HarperCollins in February 2023.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Matt_Ruff"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>Upcoming</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cloudbeast">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonSapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cloudbeast</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The cloudbeasts are beings from the dreamlands . According to the manual, they are beings whose shape is reminiscent of a living cloud . They can grow and wane numerous times throughout their lives .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#S._Petersens_Field_Guide_to_Creatures_of_the_Dreamlands"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sandy_Petersen"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lord_of_the_Worms">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#BrianLumleyWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lord of the Worms</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"the caller of the black" is a cthulhu mythos story by brian lumley . It was written in 1982, and first published in weirdbook 17 in 1983 . A young occult investigator, titus crow, finds himself in a battle of wits and will .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Titus_Crow"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Weirdbook 17</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1983</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>1982</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Kill_Those_Damn_Cats">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Kill Those Damn Cats</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various English First United Church of Cthulhu Release Date July 2016 100 : Cats of Ulthar Lovecraftian Anthology is a thematic anthology of Lovecraftian fiction. It was published by the First United Church of Cthulhu through CreateSpace in July 2016, and was edited by that organisation's head priest, Khurt Khave . Both physical and digital copies were released. Overview Contents "Wilful Creatures" by DJ Tyrer "Nix's Night About Town" by Nicholas Diak "The Daughters of Bastet" by James Pratt "Dot" by Mark P. Steele "A Story for the Zoogs" by William J. Wallace "Rise, Cats, Rise" by S. L. Kerns "The Dark Thicket" by Jason Scott Aiken "The Uninvited Guest" by Rohit Sawant "In These Dark Woods" by J. M. Heluk "The Law of Unintended Consequences" by Kevin Wetmore "Thimble Hearts and Tamarind Eyes" by Kris Dikeman "Jangling the Silver Keys" by Khurt Khave Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>100</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shathak">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Shathak</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Chushax</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Death Reborn</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Mistress of the Abyssal Slime</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Zishaik</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as Chushax Zishaik Mistress of the Abyssal Slime Death Reborn Relatives Zvilpogghua (offspring) Tsathoggua First appearance CIRCLE : Selected Letters of Clark Ashton Smith 189 Created by Clark Ashton Smith is the wife of Tsathoggua and the mother of Zvilpogghua . The being mated with Tsathoggua to produce Zvilpogghua on the planet Yaksh according to the Parchments of Pnom . Also called Chushax and Zishaik in other texts. Notes (and variants Chushax and Zishaik) were created by Clark Ashton Smith , but not elaborated upon. Laurence J. Cornford correlated some elements.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCharacterMateC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tsathoggua"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Selected Letters of Clark Ashton Smith 189</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
  </rdf:Description>
