    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>H. P. Lovecraft: A Critical Study</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>243 is a non-fiction work by scholar and critic donald r. burleson . It was first published in september 1983 by greenwood press . It has not been reissued .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Donald_R._Burleson"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>243</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>September 1983</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Confessions_of_Clithanus">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosBooksFictional"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdfs:label>Confessions of Clithanus</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesA>The Confessions of the Mad Monk Clithanus ( The Advent of Uvhash )</ex:hasAlternativeNamesA>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Also known as The Confessions of the Mad Monk Clithanus ( EXP : " The Advent of Uvhash ") Clithanus Unknown, likely Italy Real World Information First appearance AWD : " Something from Out There " AWD : " Passing of Eric Holm " AWD : " The Horror from the Depths " Created by August Derleth The is an occult text created by August Derleth for his short Cthulhu Mythos story " Something from Out There ". Overview Penned by a Christian monk named Clithanus in Rome sometime in the late 4th/early 5th century, not much is known about the Confessions . However, it has been stated to contain information on subterranean catacombs beneath Hydestall Cathedral, England, and the oceanic horrors which lurk beneath the waves thereabouts. It also tells of an encounter which Clithanus and Saint Augustine of Hippo had with the titular "Something" from Derleth's short story, during which the entity was defeated and sealed away. " Something from Out There " (1951, August Derleth ) " The Advent of Uvhash " (1996, James Ambuehl )</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceA rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Passing_of_Eric_Holm"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceA rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_from_the_Depths"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnA rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Something_from_Out_There"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByA rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#August_Derleth"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Howard_V._Brown">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Howard V. Brown</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1945</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Howard vachel brown (1878 - 1945) was an american science fiction artist . He provided the original illustrations for h. p. lovecraft's novellas at the mountains of madness and the shadow out of time . He also illustrated stories by frank belknap long , donald wandrei , and others .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Howard Vachel Brown</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1878</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sweet_Dreams_Cthulhu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Storybooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sweet Dreams Cthulhu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A Lovecraftian Bedtime Book Jason Ciaramella Greg Murphy United States English C is for Cthulhu Genre(s) Children's ComixTribe Release Date January 1, 2017 24 : A Lovecraftian Bedtime Book is a children's book based on H. P. Lovecraft 's Cthulhu Mythos . Overview The Night Before Christmas consists of 24 digitally-rendered images by artist Greg Murphy, was written by Jason Ciaramella , and was published by ComixTribe in 2017. Both board book and digital editions were issued. The plot involves Howard Lovecraft helping his friend Cthulhu overcome his nighttime fears so he can go back to sleep.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jason_Ciaramella"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Children's</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>24</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#C_is_for_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2017 January 1</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Universe_That_Lovecraft_Built">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Universe That Lovecraft Built</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here Doug McGarvey, Jr. English Nightscapes #15 date May 2003  " " is a Cthulhu Mythos poem by Doug McGarvey, Jr. Synopsis The five progressively-shorter verses show the 's appreciation for the fictional universe created by H. P. Lovecraft when compared to the creations of other world-builders, including Lewis Carroll's Alice's Adventures , L. Frank Baum's Oz and J. R. R. Tolkien's Middle Earth . History "" was published in the online Mythos zine Nightscapes in May 2003.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Doug McGarvey. Jr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Nightscapes #15</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>May 2003</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Troid">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Troid</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Troids are a fictional race in marvel comics, which were created in 1993 by chris cooper . They are a race of immaterial parasitic beings created by elder god chthon . They are present in most humans, feeding on the fear and humiliation felt by their hosts .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Midnight Sons Unlimited Vol 1 2</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Chris Cooper</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Carcosa">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalLocations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsOriginatingFromMythosAdjacentWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Carcosa</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The city of is a mystical ancient city created by ambrose bierce for his short story " an inhabitant of " . It is a central element of the horror fantasy story cycle the king in yellow , by robert w. chambers . It is the home of hastur, who resides in the monstrous lake of hali .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_W._Chambers"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_King_in_Yellow"/>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hastur"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ambrose_Bierce"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#An_Inhabitant_of_Carcosa"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Innsmouth_Clay">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AugustDerlethWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Innsmouth Clay</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A 1971 short story by august derleth, one of his " posthumous collaborations " with h. p. lovecraft . The story involves a sculptor named jeffrey corey who rents a cottage on the massachusetts coast south of innsmouth, a town where his distant relatives live . Corey, in his late 30s, is described as "not ill-favored in looks": six feet tall with "fine, fresh skin", and "very strong blue eyes" with a "pier</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#August_Derleth"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dark_Things"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1971</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Seeker">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Seeker</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>The Adumbrali</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sociocultural Characteristics Extra-dimensional The Adumbrali Notable members Dureen Real World Information First appearance CIRCLE : " The Abyss " Created by Robert A. W. Lowndes Seekers are a type of extradimensional creature which feature in the Cthulhu Mythos . Description Created by the malevolent Adumbrali , Seekers are employed by their parent race to travel across dimensions and bring them living beings to torture and ultimately kill. To aid them in this task, they are able to change their shape to match that of the indigenous species, and possess extensive mental abilities which enable them to mesmerise other beings and transport them to their masters' dimension. Known Seekers Dureen , a which disguised itself as a physically-perfect human male before taking its prey, a young university student who dabbled in the occult ( CIRCLE : " The Abyss "). Behind the Mythos Seekers were created by American author Robert A. W. Lowndes for his short story "The Abyss" in April 1941.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Abyss"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_A._W._Lowndes"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Doom_That_Came_to_Providence">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Doom That Came to Providence</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Nick Gucker United States English Genre(s) Horror Black Comedy LASC Press Release Date August 2015 Media Type Print 66 is a round-robin book exclusive to NecronomiCon 2015. It was edited by Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. with cover art by Nick Gucker . Synopsis A series of bizarre events at the WaterFire Providence event are related through 22 chapters of eyewitness accounts, from police reports to photographs to newspaper articles. Publication History was published in limited numbers by LASC Press in August 2015, and was distributed exclusively at NecronomiCon 2015. Contributors Ramsey Campbell Selena Chambers Todd Chicoine Michael Cisco Sam Cowan Mike Davis Sam Gafford Lois Gresh Michael Griffin Nick Gucker Niels Hobbs Tom Lynch Anya Martin Daniel Mills Gage Prentiss Robert M. Price Pete Rawlik Jayaprakash Satyamurthy Ann K. Schwader Justin Steele Jeffrey Thomas Scott Thomas Damien Angelica Walters</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Black Comedy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>66</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 2015</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Gorge_Beyond_Salapunco">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AugustDerlethWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Gorge Beyond Salapunco</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"the testament of claiborne boyd" is a short story by august derleth . It was written in 1946, but not published until the march 1949 issue of weird tales . Derleth complained of the difficulty of writing a story complete in itself that also serves as a transition between two parts of a novel .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#August_Derleth"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Trail_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 1949</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Roland_Franklyn">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Roland Franklyn</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>1967</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>An unnamed cult in Middle England</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Franklyn is a fictional character created by ramsey campbell for his short story . He is considered part of the expanded cthulhu mythos . He was the leader of a cult in brichester, england, in the mid-1960s .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Franklyn_Paragraphs"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ramsey_Campbell"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Othuum">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Othuum</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Great Old Ones</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Oceanic Horror</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Created by brian lumley ( the oceanic horror ) is a great old one from the expanded cthulhu mythos . First mentioned in brian lumley's short story " rising with surtsey ", and was later incorporated into the call of cthulhu role-playing game .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Rising_with_Surtsey"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gavin_Chappell">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gavin Chappell</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Editor Author England, United Kingdom United Kingdom https://www.smashwords.com/profile/view/schlock  is prolific British author and editor who has worked on a number of works related to the the works of Howard Phillips Lovecraft and the greater Cthulhu Mythos . Many of these have been published through small-press outfit Rogue Planet Press . Born in the north of England, Chappell currently lives near Liverpool. Selected Bibliography Editor Lovecraftiana (2016-present) Swords Against Cthulhu (2015) Swords Against Cthulhu II: Hyperborean Nights (2017) Swords Against Cthulhu III: A New Dark Age (2018) Author Sinbad and the Great Old Ones (2016) "A Muezzin from the Tower of Darkness" (2015) " Beneath the Temple of Outlandish Idols " (2017) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>http://example.org/lovecraft#England United Kingdom</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United Kingdom</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://www.smashwords.com/profile/view/schlock</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Swinging">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Swinging</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The mind-swapping, time-jumping tragic love story is a sequel to h.p. Lovecraft's " the shadow out of time" the story follows a young woman who learned the mindswapping technique from her father . Her father, who is possessed by a yithian, is repossessed in 2002 "to stop me telling the world about them"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cody_Goodfellow"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2016 April 29</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cold_War,_Yellow_Fever">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cold War, Yellow Fever</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story follows a group of american, soviet and cuban operatives . They attempt to neutralise an incursion of the yellow sign which appears to be transmitted over the airwaves . Although the group are successful, they must pay a heavy price for their actions .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Pete Rawlik</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Espionage</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#World_War_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>August 2014</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Final_Door">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Final Door</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A middle-aged man, bored with his life at home falls for the exotic femme fatale living across the hall who introduces him to worlds and times beyond his imagining . After encouraging his wife to drink herself to death, he spends increasing amounts of time with the woman, whose power causes him to de-age until, during a trip to the caverns beneath the sphinx of giza, a terrible accident occurs .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Michael Sheehan. Jr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Fantasy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Blasphemy_In_The_Canopic_Jar"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>More Tales Of The Cthulhu Mythos</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 2016</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Blood_Brigade">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cults"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Organisation"/>
    <rdfs:label>Blood Brigade</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Disciples_of_Deaths_Shadow"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a fictional organization in the cthulhutech pencil-and-paper roleplaying game by wildfire llc . It's lunatic members spread like the plague, literally society's cockroaches . It recruits its members from the disillusioned and depressed .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeO>Terrorist network</ex:hasTypeO>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dark_Cell">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dark Cell</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A highly-specialised cia operative recruits a former arms dealer when he learns that an irish republican army cell plans to summon a dark young . The two manage to stop the creature, but at a price . Historywas published in the war-themed anthology world war cthulhu .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_M._Sammons"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Glynn_Owen_Barrass"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#World_War_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>August 2014</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dagon_%28song%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleAudioAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dagon (song)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"dagon" (russian "аон") is a song by russian horror-punk band " korol i shut " ("орол и ут") the lyrics of the song were written by andrei knyazev, based on the story of the same name and the shadow over innsmouth by h.p. Lovecraft .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Korol i Shut</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>Russia</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror-punk</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Punk-rock</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Russian</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Ten' klouna (Тень клоуна)</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2008 November 20</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft:_An_American_Allegory">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lovecraft: An American Allegory</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Donald R. Burleson United States English Genre(s) Non-fiction Hippocampus Press Release Date July 2015 260 in a collection of non-fiction works by American and H. P. Lovecraft scholar Donald R. Burleson . Running to a total of 260 , it was published in a paperback format by Hippocampus Press in July 2015. Overview Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Donald_R._Burleson"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>260</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 2015</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Aldebaran">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsIncorporatedFromTheRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsOriginatingFromMythosAdjacentWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Stars"/>
    <rdfs:label>Aldebaran</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The eye of the bull lies in the constellation of taurus and is said to be the star of the great old one hastur . Appears in the short story " polaris " by h. p. lovecraft .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL>The King in Yellow Polaris The Festival The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath</ex:hasAppearanceL>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Star</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Northern sky (Taurus)</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#An_Inhabitant_of_Carcosa"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mansions_of_Madness">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Mansions of Madness</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cooperative, adventure, horror, mystery is a board game designed by corey konieczka and first published by fantasy flight games in 2011 . A second edition was released in august 2016 . The game involves a group of players taking on the roles of investigators who must explore a map and solve a predetermined mystery .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2011</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Friedrich_von_Junzt">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Friedrich von Junzt</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>1840</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Von junzt is a fictional character created by robert e. howard for his short story " the black stone " von junzt is incorporated into the cthulhu mythos by h.p. Lovecraft and others . Von junzt was a "german eccentric" who "spent his entire life...delving into forbidden subjects"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The Horror in the Museum Out of the Aeons The Thing on the Doorstep The Shadow Out of Time The Haunter of the Dark The Children of the Night The Thing on the Roof Schrödinger's Cat Trilogy</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Black_Stone"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_E._Howard"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_in_the_Museum:_Collected_Short_Stories_Volume_Two">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Horror in the Museum: Collected Short Stories Volume Two</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft Cover Artist Nichola Trayler-Barbrook United Kingdom English Genre(s) Fantasy, Horror, Science fiction short stories Wordsworth Editions Limited Release Date 2010 Media Type Print (Paperback) xiii, 450 pp Preceded by The Whisperer in Darkness: Collected Stories Volume One Followed by The Haunter of the Dark: Collected Short Stories Volume Three is a collection of fantasy, horror and science fiction short stories by the American H. P. Lovecraft . Contents The Horror in the Museum : Collected Short Stories Volume Two contains these texts: " The Green Meadow " (with Elizabeth Berkeley ) " Poetry and the Gods " " The Crawling Chaos " (with Elizabeth Berkeley) " The Horror at Martin's Beach " " Imprisoned with the Pharaohs " " Two Black Bottles " " The Thing in the Moonlight " " The Last Test " " The Curse of Yig " " The Electric Executioner " " The Mound " " Medusa's Coil " " The Trap " " The Man of Stone " " The Horror in the Museum " " Winged Death " " Out of the Aeons " " The Horror in the Burying-Ground " " Till A' the Seas " " The Disinterment " " The Diary of Alonzo Typer " " Within the Walls of Eryx " " The Night Ocean "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United Kingdom</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Fantasy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Science fiction short stories</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>450 pp xiii</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2010</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sglhuoan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>S'glhuoan</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Trunembra"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>S'glhuoans are a fictional alien race invented by ramsey campbell in his 1964 short story " the plain of sound " they were expanded upon by chaosium in 1995 for the call of cthulhu roleplaying game . They exist in a universe in which every world and inhabitant is made up of sound . They are shown to have the ability to mentally project messages which humans pick up as dreams, even across dimensions .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Call of Cthulhu</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Plain_of_Sound"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ramsey_Campbell"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Caller_of_The_Black">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#BrianLumleyWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Caller of The Black</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Titus crow is an occult investigator with an extensive library of arcane lore . He lives near london in a place called blowne house, a "sprawling bungalow" he is described as resembling a thinner bela lugosi .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Titus_Crow"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The Caller of the Black</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1971</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>August 1967</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#isLocatedAtA">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label>isLocatedAtA</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ermengarde_Stubbs">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ermengarde Stubbs</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Ermengarde S. Van Itty</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Ethyl Ermengarde Stubbs</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Maude Van Itty</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as ethyl ermengarde s. van itty hogton, vt . She is the daughter of a farmer and alcohol smuggler . After the prohibition, her father asked her to drop her first name as it made him thirsty .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sweet_Ermengarde"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Grey_Eagle">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Grey Eagle</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Chief is a character created by . H. p. lovecraft and featured in two stories that he wrote for . Zealia bishop . He has a minor role in " the curse of yig ", and a comparatively small but important role in . The mound . In 1928, his age was estimated as being close to 150 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Mound"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Curse_of_Yig"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Miskatonic_High_1:_The_City_of_Silence">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Miskatonic High 1: The City of Silence</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Miskatonic high: the city of silence is the first issue in the miskatonic high of young adult comic books . Written by mike shea and featuring cover art by ryan mendoza . Published by danger dads in november 2021 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Mike Shea</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Miskatonic_High"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>November 2021</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lobon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lobon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Great Ones</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Lobon of the Sacred Spear</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as of the Sacred Spear Great Ones First appearance HPL : " The Doom That Came to Sarnath " Created by H. P. Lovecraft , also known as of the Sacred Spear is one of the Great Ones , the gods of Earth that reside in Kadath . He appears as an ivy-crowned youth bearing a spear. ( EXP : " The Crawler of Pnoth " Mike Minnis ) was one of the chief gods worshipped in doomed Sarnath together with Tamash and Zo-Kalar . All three were depicted in Sarnath as graceful bearded men. ( HPL : " The Doom That Came to Sarnath ") Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Doom_That_Came_to_Sarnath"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Deep_Down_Under:_Volume_1">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Deep Down Under: Volume 1</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Australia English Cthulhu Deep Down Under Release Date February 2015 Followed by Cthulhu Deep Down Under: Volume 2 is an anthology of Lovecraftian fiction written exclusively by Australian authors. The first in the Cthulhu Deep Down Under , it was edited by Steve Proposch , Christopher Sequiera and Bryce Stevens , and was first published by Horror Australis in February 2015. This edition featured a matching art plate for most of the stories, with each piece being supplied by a different artist Contents First Edition Contents Introduction by Ramsey Campbell "Vanguard" by Aaron Sterns "The Dog Pit" by Jason Fischer "Ortensia and Osvaldo" by Lucy Sussex "Dagon's Forge" by Don Boyd "The Elder Things" by B. Michael Radburn "In the Drawback" by Kaaron Warren "An Incident at Portsea, 1967" by Jason Nahrung "Depth Lurker" by G. N. Braun "Where the Madmen Meet" by T. S. P. Sweeney "Pest Control" by Steven Paulsen "Untitled short fiction" by Steve Kilbey "Lady of the Swamp" by Janeen Webb "The Thing in the Bidet" by William Tevelein "Dreamgirl" by Stephen Dedman "They Are Impatient" by Maurice Xanthos "The Pit" by Bill Congreve "The Wake in the Witch House" by Francis Payne "Darkness Beyond" by Jason Franks "The Island in the Swamp" by Jo Hannes "Impossible Object" by David Conyers "Haunting Matilda" by Dmetri Kakmi "The Return of..." by Christopher Sequeira "The Seamounts of Vaalua Tuva" by David Kuraria "The Black Lake's Fatal Flood" by Robert Hood Second Edition Introduction by Ramsey Campbell "A Pearl Beyond Price" "Haunting Matilda" by Dmetri Kakmi "Wife to Mr Lovecraft" by Lucy Sussex "Darkness Beyond" by Jason Franks "The Diesel Pool" by Kaaron Warren* "Dreamgirl" by Stephen Dedman "The Thing in the Bidet" by William Tevelein "The Return of..." by Christopher Sequeira "The Elder Things" by B. Michael Radburn "Vanguard" by Aaron Sterns Publication History After its first appearance, was reissued by IFWG Publishing Australia in 2017 in a drastically reduced edition, with a new cover, the original 25 stories being reduced to only 11- two of those being new to this edition - and the interior artwork removed in its entirety; both paperback and digital versions of this virtually-unrecognisable "lite" edition were released. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Australia"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Cthulhu Deep Down Under</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>February 2015</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#An_Evening_at_Mount_Baldy">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>An Evening at Mount Baldy</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mark Brandon Allen English James Ward Kirk Publishing Lovecraft after Dark date November 2015  " " is a Lovecraftian short story by American Mark Brandon Allen . It was published in the thematic James Ward Kirk Publishing anthology Lovecraft after Dark in November 2015. Synopsis Two curious men, investigating the catacombs revealed by a sinkhole, encounter a bizarre and malevolent creature lurking within. The being kills the men, but not before violating them in a most horrible way first.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_Brandon_Allen"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_after_Dark"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#S._T._Joshi/Bibliography">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scholars"/>
    <rdfs:label>S. T. Joshi/Bibliography</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>This page aims to provide a comprehensive bibliography of the H. P. Lovecraft -related works of scholar and critic S. T. Joshi . Author Non-Fiction: Collections and Studies H. P. Lovecraft and Lovecraft Criticism: An Annotated Bibliography (1981) An Index to the Selected Letters of H. P. Lovecraft (1980; 2nd ed, 1991) H. P. Lovecraft (Starmont Reader's Guide) (1982) H. P. Lovecraft and Lovecraft Criticism: An Annotated Bibliography: Supplement, 1980-84 (1984, with Leigh Blackmore) Selected Papers on Lovecraft (1989) H. P. Lovecraft: The Decline of the West (1990) H. P. Lovecraft in the Argosy (1994) H. P. Lovecraft: A Life (1996) A Subtler Magick: The Writings and Philosophy of H. P. Lovecraft (1996) Sixty Years of Arkham House: A History and Bibliography (1999) An H. P. Lovecraft Encyclopedia (2001, with David E. Schultz ) A Dreamer and a Visionary: H. P. Lovecraft in His Time (2001) Primal Sources: Essays on H. P. Lovecraft (2003) The Rise and Fall of the Cthulhu Mythos (2008) H. P. Lovecraft: A Comprehensive Bibliography (2009) I Am Providence: The Life and Times of H. P. Lovecraft (2010) A Weird Writer in Our Midst: Early Criticism of H. P. Lovecraft (2010) H. P. Lovecraft: The Early Stories (2019) Dawnward Spire, Lonely Hill: The Letters of H. P. Lovecraft and Clark Ashton Smith: 1932-1937 Volume 2 (2020, with David E. Schultz) Non-Fiction: Essays " H. P. Lovecraft: His Life and Work " (1980, with Kenneth W. Faig, Jr.) " Lovecraft Criticism: A Study " (1980) " A Chronology of Selected Works by H. P. Lovecraft " (1980) " A Look at Lovecraft's Letters " (1982) " Lovecraft's Other Planets " (1982) " Lovecraft's Revisions: How Much of Them Did He Write? " (1983) " The Structure of Lovecraft's Longer Narratives " (1986) " Lovecraft's Alien Civilisations: A Political Interpretation " (1989) Editor Periodicals Lovecraft Studies (1979-2004) Lovecraft Annual (2007-present). Anthologies &amp; Collections Uncollected Prose and Poetry by H. P. Lovecraft (3 volumes, 1978–1982, with Marc A. Michaud ) H. P. Lovecraft in "The Eyrie" (1979, with Marc A. Michaud) H. P. Lovecraft: Four Decades of Criticism (1980) Uncollected Prose &amp; Poetry Volume 2 (1980, with Marc A. Michaud) The Private Life of H. P. Lovecraft by Sonia H. Davis (1985, 1993) Uncollected Letters (1986) An Epicure in the Terrible: A Centennial Anthology of Essays in Honor of H. P. Lovecraft (1991, with David E. Schultz ) The H. P. Lovecraft Centennial Conference: Proceedings (1991) H. P. Lovecraft in the Argosy (1994) Caverns Measureless to Man: 18 Memoirs of Lovecraft (1996) The Annotated H. P. Lovecraft (1997) More Annotated H. P. Lovecraft (1999, with Peter H. Cannon ) The Call of Cthulhu and Other Weird Stories (1999) Lord of a Visible World: An Autobiography in Letters (2000) The Annotated Supernatural Horror in Literature (2000) The Ancient Track: The Complete Poetical Works of H. P. Lovecraft (2001) The Thing on the Doorstep and Other Weird Stories (2001) Lovecraft's Library: A Catalogue (revised &amp; enlarged ed., 2002) Collected Essays (5 vols, 2004–2007) The Dreams in the Witch House and Other Weird Stories (2005) O Fortunate Floridian: H. P. Lovecraft's Letters to Robert H. Barlow (2007, with David E. Schultz) Essential Solitude: The Letters of H. P. Lovecraft and August Derleth (2 volumes, 2008, with David E. Schultz) A Means to Freedom: The Letters of H. P. Lovecraft and Robert E. Howard (2 volumes, 2009, with David E. Schultz and Rusty Burke) Copping Squid and Other Mythos Tales (2009) Against Religion (2010) Black Wings: New Tales of Lovecraftian Horror (2010) A Weird Writer in Our Midst: Early Criticism of H. P. Lovecraft (2010) The Crawling Chaos and Others: The Annotated Revisions and Collaborations of H. P. Lovecraft Volume 1 (2011) Medusa's Coil and Others: The Annotated Revisions and Collaborations of H. P. Lovecraft Volume 2 (2012) Black Wings II: New Tales of Lovecraftian Horror (2012) A Mountain Walked (2014) The Madness of Cthulhu (2014) Black Wings III: New Tales of Lovecraftian Horror (2014) The Madness of Cthulhu: Volume Two (2015) Black Wings IV: New Tales of Lovecraftian Horror (2015) The Shadow Out of Time (2015) The Shadow Over Innsmouth (2015) Black Wings V: New Tales of Lovecraftian Horror (2016) Gothic Lovecraft (2016, with Lynne Jamneck ) Demiurge: The Complete Cthulhu Mythos Tales of Michael Shea (2017) The Red Brain: Great Tales of the Cthulhu Mythos (2017) Black Wings VI: New Tales of Lovecraftian Horror (2012) Translator Lovecraft: A Study in the Fantastic by Maurice Lévy (1988)</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Lamp_of_Alhazred">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AugustDerlethWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Lamp of Alhazred</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>August Derleth Appears in The Watchers Out of Time and Others Arkham House The Magazine of Fantasy and Science Fiction date October 1957 ← Previous Next → " The Shadow Out of Space " " The Shuttered Room " "" is a short story, first published in the October 1957 issue of The Magazine of Fantasy and Science Fiction as a "posthumous collaboration" between August Derleth and H. P. Lovecraft . While most such "collaborations" were entirely written by Derleth based on fragmentary story ideas from Lovecraft, "" incorporates text from Lovecraft's final and unfinished piece of correspondence recounting hiking Neutaconkanut Hill in Rhode Island from Lovecraft's final and unfinished piece of correspondence. ( HPL : Selected Letters of H. P. Lovecraft 5.930) External Link "" complete text at Librarium Cthulhuvius</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#August_Derleth"/>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Others</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>The Watchers Out of Time</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Science Fiction</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The Magazine of Fantasy</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 1957</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#T._E._Grau">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>T. E. Grau</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Theodore edward grau is an american author who writes primarily horror fiction . His works have been translated into french, spanish, japanese and italian . He has been nominated for both the shirley jackson award and the bram stoker award .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Ames Iowa</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Theodore Edward Grau III</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://cosmicomicon.blogspot.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://tegrau.com/media/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>8 January 1972</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tulush">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tulush</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tulushuggua"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sociocultural Characteristics Possibly Florida Tulushuggua Real World Information " Sinkhole " Created by Michael LaBossiere The are a race of humanoid eel-like beings that serve under Tulushuggua deep within the flooded caves of Florida. They appear and are described in the third-party Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game module " Sinkhole " by Michael LaBossiere . They are described as being blind creatures resembling a cross between an eel and a squid, with two strange bumps on their heads where their sonar echo location organs are located.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sinkhole"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Michael_LaBossiere"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Adolf_Hitler">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Avatars"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdfs:label>Adolf Hitler</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>1945 April 30</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Nazi Germany</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Hitler was a devotee of the occult and a follower of the tibetan mystic teppa tsanpo . After his suicide in 1945, he was reincarnated as a japanese man named hirata and went on to become the head of the majority party in the japanese parliament .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Kthulhu_Reich"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Asamatsu Ken</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Howard_Phillips_Lovecraft_%28Donald_A._Wollheim%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Howard Phillips Lovecraft (Donald A. Wollheim)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Howard Phillips Lovecraft Donald A. Wollheim United States English Genre(s) Non-fiction Operation: Phantasy: The Best from The Phantagraph Date of writing May 1937 date 1967 " Howard Phillips Lovecraft " is a tribute piece written by and publisher Donald A. Wollheim for the late American H. P. Lovecraft . Although dated May 1937, a mere two months after Lovecraft's death, it does not appear to have been published until its appearance in Operation: Phantasy: The Best from The Phantagraph thirty years later in 1967 , although this discrepancy may simply be due to to the fact that Wollheim was himself the publisher, and included the piece in one of his magazines without credit. References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Donald_A._Wollheim"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Operation: Phantasy: The Best from The Phantagraph</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1967</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>May 1937</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Implan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalLocations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>Implan</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Dreamlands Country Humans First appearance HPL : " The Doom That Came to Sarnath " Created by H. P. Lovecraft is a region in the Dreamlands briefly mentioned in " The Doom That Came to Sarnath " by H. P. Lovecraft . The meat of young goats from the distant hills of was considered a delicacy in Sarnath .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Country</ex:hasTypeL>
