  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Give_Me_That_Old_Time_Religion">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Give Me That Old Time Religion</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A hillbilly con-artist who has made a living preaching his faux religion across the country finds a true calling before returning to his roots . There, he enacts a sacred ritual designed to awaken the great old one cthulhu . But he is grievously injured in the process .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Lee Finney</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Whispers_from_the_Abyss"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2014</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Call_of_Cthulhu_%28J._Scott_Vanlester%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleComicBookAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Call of Cthulhu (J. Scott Vanlester)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Call of Cthulhu H. P. Lovecraft J. Scott Vanlester United States English Lovecraft Comics Release Date September 2022 137 The Call of Cthulhu is a sanitised comic book-format adaptation of the H. P. Lovecraft work of the same name by American artist and J. Scott Vanlester . It was self-published in September 2022; both hardcover and digital editions were released. The artwork is rendered entirely in black and white with a heavy leaning towards line drawings and a highly minimalist presentation. Overview</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>137</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Lovecraft Comics</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>September 2022</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Braving,_Minnesota">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>Braving, Minnesota</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAccessibilityL>Road</ex:hasAccessibilityL>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>United States Road Created by James Ambuehl , is a fictional created by author James Ambuehl to be the focal point of a number of his Cthulhu Mythos works. Points of Note Royceton University A centre of learning which contains an extensive occult library. The Braving Bulletin The local newspaper whose ace reporter is a man named James Joseph "J.J." Hanley . Environs Laren A small town not far from Braving which once played host to one of the Star-Seeds of Ei'lor . Appearances " The Advent of Uvhash " (1996) " The Star-Seed " (1996) " Atlachnaphobia " (1997) " The Terror of Toad Lake " (1997) " Correlated Contents " (1998) " Wrath of the Wind-Walker " (1998) " One Rainy Afternoon Spent in the Company of Dr. Thulask " (2005) " The Farmhouse " Trivia A book on the paranormal named Tales of Braving and its Insidious Environs was penned by one James Ambrel , a clear nod to the setting's creator James Ambuehl.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>United States</ex:isLocatedAtL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nathaniel_Hawthorne">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsInspirationsAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nathaniel Hawthorne</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>19 May 1864</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Inspiration Birth Date 4 July 1804 Salem , Massachusetts, USA Death Date 19 May 1864 Plymouth, New Hampshire, USA United States (4 July 1804 - 19 May 1864) was an American author, many of whose works, like those of H. P. Lovecraft , centre around his native New England . Lovecraft praised his novel The House of the Seven Gables as "New England's greatest contribution to weird literature" in the essay " Supernatural Horror in Literature ", and also discussed Hawthorne's role in the genre. According to An H. P. Lovecraft Encyclopedia , compiled by leading scholars S. T. Joshi and David E. Schultz , several of Lovecraft's stories drew inspiration from those of Hawthorne. These include: " The Dreams in the Witch-House " (the unfinished novel Septimius Felton ) The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath ( The Marble Faun , " The Great Stone Face ") " The Case of Charles Dexter Ward " ( The House of the Seven Gables ) " The Outsider " (" Fragments from the Journal of a Solitary Man ") The writings of Lovecraft's friend and contemporary Robert E. Howard have also been favourably compared to Hawthorne's work.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA http://example.org/lovecraft#Massachusetts http://example.org/lovecraft#Salem</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Inspiration</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>4 July 1804</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dreamlands">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dimensions"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dreamlands</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAccessibilityL>(To humans) Only in dreams</ex:hasAccessibilityL>
    <ex:hasAccessibilityL>or transcendental states</ex:hasAccessibilityL>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesL>The Lands of Dream</ex:hasAlternativeNamesL>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The or the dream realm are a setting created by h. p. lovecraft for a series of stories, known as the dream cycle . It is a vast, alternate dimension or universe, with lands, seas, creatures, civilisations, and an extensive underworld . The dreamers are able to enter the world only in dreams, or transcendental states . There are numerous races, civilisations, and beings existing in the that do not exist in the waking universe .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL>The White Ship The Doom That Came to Sarnath The Cats of Ulthar Celephaïs To a Dreamer The Other Gods The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath The Maker of Gods In 'Ygiroth In the Vale of Pnath The Clock of Dreams The Summons of Nuguth-Yug The Viking's Doom</ex:hasAppearanceL>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>Gardens of Yin The Eastern Desert Pool of Night Mnar Thul Cuppar-Nombo Mountains of Madness Cydathria Oriab Sarrub Khem The Stony Desert Yann Lomar Zobna Ooth-Nargai Kaar Bodies of water Twilight Sea Southern Sea Cerenerian Sea Middle Ocean</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Alternate Dimension</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Humans Gugs Zoogs Moon-beasts Cats</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Polaris"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Arrival_Of_Our_New_Master:_A_Lovecraftian_Armageddon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Arrival Of Our New Master: A Lovecraftian Armageddon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mark McLaughlin English Genre(s) Horror The House of the Ocelot &amp; More Lovecraftian Nightmares date 2019 " " is a Cthulhu Mythos poem by Mark McLaughlin . Synopsis The verses describe the rise of an avatar of Nyarlathotep named Caliban Storm who orchestrated the final hours of the human race, only to be defeated by an unlikely coalition of the cats of Ulthar and Night-gaunts . Characters Caliban Storm , a genetically-engineered avatar of Nyarlathotep. Penny Blanchard , a former homeless woman and humankind's last hope. History "" was published in the collection The House of the Ocelot &amp; More Lovecraftian Nightmares in 2019.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>More Lovecraftian Nightmares</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The House of the Ocelot</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2019</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#One_Below_All">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>One Below All</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a multiversal counterpart of the multiversal one-above-all . Resides in the deepest part of hell called the "below-place" spawns entities known as qlippoths and disgorges legions of demonic monsters .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Legacy_of_Erich_Zann_and_Other_Tales_of_the_Cthulhu_Mythos">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Legacy of Erich Zann and Other Tales of the Cthulhu Mythos</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The collection is a thematic one in that all of the stories are written as sequels to original h.p. Lovecraft titles . Contains two novellas and two short stories .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Stableford"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Short Fiction Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>209</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>April 2012</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Companion">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Companion</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Yurek Chodak Morgan Conrad Alan K. Crandall Gene Day William Hamblin Keith Herber Chris Marrinan Sandy Petersen Mark Pettigrew Glenn Rahman John Sullivan Richard L. Tierney Lynn Willis Gene Day Chris Marrinan Lisa A. Free Cover Artist Chris Marrinan United States English Chaosium Release Date 1983 Media Type Print Digital (PDF) 68 The is a Call of Cthulhu supplement from roleplaying games manufacturer Chaosium . Overview A compilation of articles from a wide variety of writers, the contains additional skills, details on prison systems of the 1930s world, an expansion of the Sanity condition, a selection of gods and monsters, and three ready-to-play scenarios. Along with poetry by H. P. Lovecraft himself, it features cover art by Chris Marrinan . Publication History First released in 1983, the has since gone out of print, but is currently available as a digital PDF download direct from Chaosium.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Glenn_Rahman"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Keith_Herber"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lynn_Willis"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_L._Tierney"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sandy_Petersen"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Alan K. Crandall</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Chris Marrinan</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Gene Day</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>John Sullivan</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Mark Pettigrew</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Morgan Conrad</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>William Hamblin</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Yurek Chodak</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>68</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1983</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Azathi">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Azathi</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Children of Azathoth</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The are a species of extra-dimensional entities created by the late british author brian lumley for his 1989 cthulhu mythos novel . They cause nuclear and atomic reactions and are instantly annihilated upon their birth . According to legend, they made a pact which when fulfilled would lead to the destruction of the multiverse and the return of the</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Elysia"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Zehirete">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ElderGods"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Zehirete</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Elder Gods</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Holy Womb of Light</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Pure</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as the pure and holy womb of light is a fictional deity created by joseph s. pulver for his novel nightmare's disciple .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCharacterMateC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nodens"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nightmares_Disciple"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Joseph S. Pulver</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Child_of_Shugoran">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Child of Shugoran</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nyarlathotep"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sociocultural Characteristics Nyarlathotep Real World Information First appearance EXP : " Black Man with a Horn " Created by T. E. D. Klein The Children of Shugoran are a fictional race created by American author T. E. D. Klein for his short story " Black Man with a Horn ", and later expanded upon by Chaosium for their Call of Cthulhu horror roleplaying game. Background The Children of Shugoran are smaller, less physically-powerful copies of Shugoran , an avatar of Nyarlathotep . Those followers of Nyarlathotep who have performed particularly well may be given a as a familiar of sorts.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Black_Man_with_a_Horn"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#T._E._D._Klein"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_and_the_Literature_of_Cosmic_Fear">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lovecraft and the 'Literature of Cosmic Fear'</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lovecraft and the 'Literature of Cosmic Fear' Stephen Jones English Genre(s) Non-fiction Barnes &amp; Noble Books H. P. Lovecraft's Book of Horror date November 1993 " Lovecraft and the 'Literature of Cosmic Fear' " is a short non-fiction piece by British and editor Stephen Jones . It was first published as the closing work in the anthology H. P. Lovecraft's Book of Horror in November 1993, edited by Jones and Dave Carson .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Stephen_Jones"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecrafts_Book_of_Horror"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 1993</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ktynga">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ktynga</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesA>Norby's Comet</ex:hasAlternativeNamesA>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as norby's comet ( circle : " the fire vampires " ) is a planetoid which serves as cosmic transportation for the entity known as fthaggua in donald wandrei 's 1933 cthulhu mythos story . First observed by human astronomer gustav norby on 7th july 2321, around five light-years from the earth .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasFunctionA>Starship</ex:hasFunctionA>
    <ex:hasFunctionA>dwelling</ex:hasFunctionA>
    <ex:hasPlaceOfOriginA>Extrasolar</ex:hasPlaceOfOriginA>
    <ex:hasTypeA>Celestial body</ex:hasTypeA>
    <ex:isUsedByA rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fthaggua"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnA rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Fire_Vampires"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByA>Presumably Fthaggua</ex:wasCreatedByA>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dark_Circus">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dimensions"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsOriginatingFromMythosAdjacentWorks"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dark Circus</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAccessibilityL>Segonax (well)</ex:hasAccessibilityL>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Alternate Dimension Ruling Powers Gods of Ragnarok Segonax (well) Gods of Ragnarok First appearance ADJ : "The Greatest Show in the Galaxy" Created by Stephen Wyatt The is a dimension created by the BBC in 1988 for Doctor Who . Background The is the Gods of Ragnarok's dimension, which appears as an arena consisting of grey stone slabs that have inscriptions. Entrance to it is usually through a well on Segonax, though they can also create a bridge for others to enter the Circus. Gladiators are lured to the , where the Gods feed on the entertainment. External links at the Doctor Who wiki</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Alternate Dimension</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gods_of_Ragnarok"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>The Greatest Show in the Galaxy</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pond_Street">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Pond Street</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Street in fictional town of bolton, massachusetts mentioned in the story " herbert west--reanimator " by h. p. lovecraft . Street is named after a street in the fictional town of bolton .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Street</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Humans</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Bolton"/>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Massachusetts"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Herbert_West--Reanimator"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Black_Book_Issue_1_%28May_2002%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Black Book Issue 1 (May 2002)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Black Book Issue 1 Various Cover Artist Daniel Alan Ross English The Black Book Release Date May 2002 Followed by Issue 2 The Black Book Issue 1 was the inaugural issue of short-lived Lovecraftian fanzine The Black Book . Edited by Peter Worthy and featuring cover art by Daniel Alan Ross, it was self-published published in a digital format in May 2002. Contents " The Isle of Dark Magic " by Hugh B. Cave "The Mirror" by Susan McAdam "The Elephant God of Leng" by Robert M. Price "That Horrid Necronomicon" by Franklyn Searight "The Demon" by Glenn Rahman "Lovecraft and "The Evil Dead"" by Stanley C. Sargent "To Chorazin" by Mark Francis "The Madness Out of Time" by E. P. Berglund and Lew Cabos "Column: Strange Selections" by Peter A. Worthy "Darkest Mother" by Ann K. Schwader "Things to Do in Kingsport When You're Dreaming" by John Tynes "Forge Park" by Jeffrey Thomas "A Funeral for Mr. Mittens" by Mike Minnis</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>The Black Book</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 2002</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Llewellyn_M._Cabos">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Llewellyn M. Cabos</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lew cabos was born in honolulu, hawaii, on 26 juin 1954 . All of his lovecraftian stories were written when he was in his teens and early 20s . All of his professional art was done under the pen name lew cabos .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Hawaii Honolulu</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Lew Cabos</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>26 June 1954</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_St._Johns_Eve_1983">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu St. John's Eve 1983</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Albrecht Dürer United States English Crypt of Cthulhu Release Date June 1983 52 Preceded by Roodmas 1983 Followed by Lammas 1983 is the fourteenth issue of Robert M. Price 's long-running Lovecraftian fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Edited by Price, it ran to a total of 52 in an A5 format and was self-published in June 1983. The cover art is credited to Albrecht Dürer, a prominent German Renaissance artist . Contents Editorial Shards by Robert M. Price "Baring-Gould and the Ghouls" by Steve Mariconda "Lovecraft's Ghouls" by Will Murray "Has Kadath Been Sighted?" by Richard L. Tierney "Swamp Call" by Brian Lumley "A Letter on the Lovecraft Mythos" by Pierre de Caprona "Contra Capronam" by Lin Carter "Lovecraft As I Seem to Remember Him" by F. Gumby Kalem "Making a Friend of Horror" by Charles Hoffman "Crypt-o-Cthulhu-Gram" (puzzle game) by Carol Selby "Some Stray Bits of Lovecraftiana" by Darrell Schweitzer "Fantasy Gaming for Lovecraftians: The Call of Cthulhu" by Sam Gafford "Fun Guys from Yuggoth: On Reading Lovecraft" by C. J. Henderson "Advice to the Lovecraft-Lorn" by Donna Death Reviews by Ronald Shearer, S. T. Joshi , and Scott D. Briggs Letters by Karl Edward Wagner , Gahan Wilson , Fritz Leiber , Frank Belknap Long , L. Sprague de Camp , Ben Indick, S. T. Joshi, Patrice de G. Joubert, William Fulwiler, and Ronald Shearer References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>52</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>June 1983</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ruthanna_Emrys">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ruthanna Emrys</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>American Science Fiction Fantasy is an American science fiction and fantasy writer, best known for her Innsmouth Legacy series of novels, which reimagines the Deep Ones from H. P. Lovecraft 's " The Shadow Over Innsmouth " as sympathetic protagonists, victims of racism , genocide, and internment. She also writes, with Anne M. Pillsworth , The Lovecraft Reread blog series at Tor.com. Books The Innsmouth Legacy The Litany of Earth (2014) Winter Tide (2017) Deep Roots (2018) Other Books Imperfect Commentaries (2019) The Half-Built Garden (2022) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>American</ex:hasNationalityR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Californian:_1934-1938">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Californian: 1934-1938</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft English Necronomicon Press Release Date 1977 67 is a collection of works focussing on H. P. Lovecraft 's contributions to the amateur press magazine The Californian . Running to a total of 67 , it was published by Necronomicon Press in a limited run of 1,000 copies in 1977, and includes a handful of works dedicated to Lovecraft by those who knew him . Contents " August " " Providence " " Sunset " " Some Notes on Interplanetary Fiction " " Phaeton " " Death " " To the American Flag " " Till A' the Seas ", with R. H. Barlow Foreword () by Hyman Bradofsky Foreword () by Dirk W. Mosig " The Homes and Shrines of Poe " " Heritage or Modernism - Common Sense in Art Forms " " Howard Phillips Lovecraft " by Rheinhart Kleiner " Yet Still We Mourn (dedicated to the memory of HPL) " by Frank Earle Schermerhorn " Mrs Miniter - Estimates and Recollections " References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>67</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1977</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Zar">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalLocations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>Zar</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Dreamlands First appearance HPL : " The White Ship " Created by H. P. Lovecraft is a fictional coastal region in the Dreamlands of Earth and home to "the dreams and thoughts of beauty that come to men once and then are forgotten". Description Its green shore is bright and beautiful with verdant tree-studded terraces rising up from the sea. The temples have gleaming white roofs and colonnades and stand amoungst a beautiful landscape of "forms and fantasies more splendid than any I had ever known; the visions of young poets who died in want before the world could learn of what they had seen and dreamed". However those who go there can never return home.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>The Dreamlands</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_White_Ship"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Cultus_Issue_1">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Cultus Issue 1</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu Cultus #1 Various Cover Artist Tani Jantsang United States English P. Marsh Release Date April 1995 58 Followed by Cthulhu Cultus #2 Cthulhu Cultus #1 is an issue of sporadic American fanzine Cthulhu Cultus , edited by Tani Jantsang and James Ambuehl and published by P. Marsh in April 1995. Contents "The Statement of Richard Daniel Upton" by J. Gerber, Tani Jantsang and C. Ley "The Marshes Since the Civil War" by Frances A. Marsh "Heterodox Churches in Innsmouth" by Philip Marsh "When Cthulhu Rises from R'lyeh" by Philip Marsh "Cthulhu's on the Rise" by Tani Jantsang and Philip Marsh "Cthulhu" by Tani Jantsang "Lullaby" by Tani Jantsang (as Anon.) "Innsmouth Rap Song" by Wayne Hicks, Tani Jantsang and Philip Marsh "The Victory Hymn of Other Nations" by Casey Ley " The Transition of Zadok Allen " by Robert M. Price "When You Walk by the Sea" by Philip Marsh "Innsmouth by the Sea" by Tani Jantsang "Misty Cthulhu" by Philip Marsh "The Forsaken Merman" by Matthew Arnold "Reverie" by Matthew Arnold "Dover Beach" by Matthew Arnold "Isolation" by Matthew Arnold "A True Deep One Story" by Tani Jantsang "Old Tale" by Tani Jantsang "We Part Only to Meet Again" by Tani Jantsang and E. A. Poe "Somebody Loves Him" by Tani Jantsang "The Case of the Old Lady" by Wayne Hicks "Dear Mr. Wuth..." uncredited "Polynesian Religion &amp; People (Polynesian Religion - Their Oral Doctrines / The Polynesians: Oceanic Nomads)" by Tani Jantsang "On "Unda", HPL's Poem "Bride of the Sea"" by Tani Jantsang and Casey Ley "The Bride of the Sea" by H. P. Lovecraft "Psyche and Eros" by Wayne Hill</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>58</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>April 1995</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shadow_%28Supernatural%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Shadow (Supernatural)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The shadow is an "ancient cosmic entity" older than god who exhibits great destructive power when awakened from eternal sleep . Like cthulhu or lord dunsany 's mana-yood-sushai , its normal state is eternal sleep . The shadow is the oldest entity in the supernatural cosmos .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Konstantine_Paradias">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Konstantine Paradias</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Editor Athens, Greece Horror http://shapescapes.blogspot.com/ is a Greek author and occasional editor who has written and had a hand in several Cthulhu Mythos works. His short fiction has been published in anthologies such as Whispers from the Abyss Vol. 2: The Horrors That Are &amp; Shall Be (2013), World War Cthulhu (2014), The Revelations of Glaaki (2015), Return of the Old Ones (2017), and Arkham Detective Agency (2017). Selected Bibliography Editor Cthulhu Detective: A C. J. Henderson Tribute (, with David Conyers and David Kernot ) Short Fiction " Echoes in Porcelain " (2013) " The Sinking City " (2014) "A Plague of Wounds" (2015) " Cult of Panacea " (2017) " Howling Synchronicities " (2017) " Miskatonic Contradance " (2017) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>Athens Greece</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://shapescapes.blogspot.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_New_King_in_Town">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A New King in Town</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A small-time criminal unexpectedly finds himself in possession of a copy of the dreaded play the king in yellow . After reading the work, a change begins to come over him and although his body is no longer his own, he kills cleveland's former underworld boss .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The Yellow Sign #3</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>June 2011</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Camside">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Camside</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Town created by raamsey campbell is a fictional town in the severn valley of england . It appears in many of his stories to contrast its relative normalcy with the weirdness going on around it . The occultist who ropes kevin gillson into a disastrous summoning of the outer god daoloth lives in a block of flats in town .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Town</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#England"/>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>United Kingdom</ex:isLocatedAtL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shaggai">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Planets"/>
    <rdfs:label>Shaggai</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesL>Chag-Hai</ex:hasAlternativeNamesL>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>'chag-hai' is a planet orbiting a binary system with green suns . It was home to insects called the 'shans' and a mysterious being called 'the worm that gnaws in the night' according to robert blake, it is in the depths of the cosmos, even deeper than yuggoth . Due to the peculiar gravitational forces at play in 's binary system, only two planets were able to form there: and</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Insects_from_Shaggai"/>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Planet</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Worm_that_Gnaws_in_the_Night"/>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Baoht Z'uqqa-mogg</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Shans</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Andromeda Galaxy</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>The Haunter of the Dark (mentioned)</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dean_Stockwell">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPeople"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dean Stockwell</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>7 November 2021</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Robert Connection to the Mythos Actor Birth Date 5 March 1936 Los Angeles, California, USA Death Date 7 November 2021 Whangarei, New Zealand United States Robert (5 March 1936 - 7 November 2021) was a prolific American film and television actor whose career spanned over sixty years. Although he had been acting for 35 years by the time of its release, he is perhaps best remembered for his role as Admiral Albert "Al" Calavicci in the popular science fiction drama series Quantum Leap ; he also appeared in the television adaptation of Expanded Mythos author Stephen King 's The Langoliers . Stockwell starred in two adaptations of the H. P. Lovecraft short story " The Dunwich Horror ", nearly forty years apart, playing very different characters in each: in the 1970 adaptation, he portrayed the malevolent Wilbur Whateley , while in the 2008 production he was instead protagonist Dr. Henry Armitage . Selected Filmography The Dunwich Horror (1970, Wilbur Whateley ) The Dunwich Horror (2008, Dr. Henry Armitage ) External Links at Wikipedia at IMDb Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA http://example.org/lovecraft#California Los Angeles</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Robert Dean Stockwell</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Actor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>5 March 1936</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Miskatonic_High_Volume_1">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Miskatonic High Volume 1</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mike Shea Ryan Mendoza Cover Artist Ryan Mendoza English Miskatonic High Danger Dads Release Date November 2021 176 is a collection of the first six issues of one of Mike Shea and Ryan Mendoza's Cthulhu Mythos comic book Miskatonic High . Released by Danger Dads in November 2021, it includes an all-new cover drawn by Mendoza, as well as a bonus 8-page story. Both trade paperback and digital editions were released. Contents The City of Silence The Rats in the Orchard Ghost Stories Crash Missing Pieces Monster's Ball</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Mike Shea</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>176</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Miskatonic_High"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>November 2021</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Brian Lumley</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>January 2024</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Barbara anne (silky) lumley was an english horror-fiction writer . He was a major contributor to the cthulhu mythos, inventing a number of new great old ones , elder gods , grimoires , and mythos locations . He joined the army in 1958, and was well into a 22-year career there when he began corresponding with august derleth .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>http://example.org/lovecraft#England County Durham Horden</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>British</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1937 December 2</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Forever_Azathoth_and_Other_Horrors">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Forever Azathoth and Other Horrors</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Peter Cannon Cover Artist Jason Eckhardt English Tartarus Press Release Date July 1999 234 is an anthology of Lovecraftian fiction by American and H. P. Lovecraft scholar Peter Cannon . Running to a total of 234 in a hardcover format, it was published by Tartarus Press in July 1997. Cover art was supplied by Jason Eckhardt , and it included an introduction by Steven J. Mariconda . Contents Introduction by Steven J. Mariconda "Forever Azathoth" "Azathoth in Arkham" "The Revenge of Azathoth" "The House of Azathoth" "Azathoth in Analysis" "Bride of Azathoth" "Son of Azathoth" "The Partridgeville Papers" "The Letters of Halpin Chalmers" "Last Flight" "It Was the Day of the Deep One" "The Hound of the Partridgevilles" "Parodies or Pastiches?" "The Undercliffe Sentences" "Tender is the Night-Gaunt" "Scream for Jeeves" "Cats, Rats, and Bertie Wooster" "Something Foetid" "The Rummy Affair of Young Charlie"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Peter_Cannon"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>234</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 1999</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_White_Ship">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The White Ship</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A lighthouse keeper named basil elton engages in a peculiar fantasy in which a robed, bearded man is piloting a mystical white ship . They travel past zar, a green land where "dwell all the dreams and thoughts of beauty that come once and then are forgotten" they then pass akariel, the huge carven gate of thalarion, where dwell frightful demons . They finally settle in sona-nyl, the "land of</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Cultus_Issue_10">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Cultus Issue 10</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu Cultus #10 Various Cover Artist Tani Jantsang United States English P. Marsh Release Date February 1998 62 Preceded by Cthulhu Cultus #9 Followed by Cthulhu Cultus #11 Cthulhu Cultus #10 is an issue of sporadic American fanzine Cthulhu Cultus , edited by Tani Jantsang and James Ambuehl and published by P. Marsh in February 1998. Contents "The Watchers at the Portal" by P. J. Roberts "My Cthulhu Story" by G. W. Thomas "The Tunnels" by Sean Rodgers " The Changeling " by Ron Shiflet "Arkham House" by Tracy Ambuehl "Politically Correct" by Hadrian Wayne Hicks " People of the Monolith " (Part 4 of 7) by Crispin Burnham "The Unforgotten Merman" by Casey Ley "Servants of the Coils" by Kevin Eric Sheridan "Would You Rather Swing on a Star" by Lady Fish and P. Marsh</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>62</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>February 1998</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Julius_Schwartz">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsCorrespondents"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Julius Schwartz</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>8 February 2004</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Schwartz was born in the bronx, new york city, the child of jewish romanian immigrants . He founded the fanzine time traveller with forrest j. ackerman and mort weisinger . He sold two of lovecraft's stories, at the mountains of madness and " the shadow out of time ", to astounding stories .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>United States http://example.org/lovecraft#New_York_City</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Lovecraft's agent</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>19 June 1915</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Acceleratrix">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Acceleratrix</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Is a lovecraftian short story by british glynn owen barrass . First published in vol 5, issue 4 of the lovecraftiana zine in 2021 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Glynn_Owen_Barrass"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Issue 4</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Lovecraftiana Vol 5</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2021</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Aldebaran_Apocalypse">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Aldebaran Apocalypse</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>" is the fourth and final of his king in yellow stories . Was independently published in both print and digital formats in may 2019 . Has also been included in the june 2019 collection brian barr's king in yellow .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Barr"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>May 2019</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cy_Morgan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dreamers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cy Morgan</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cy is an average american man who as an adult studied at arkham 's miskatonic university . He previously lived with and was engaged to a woman named jordan, but their relationship ended with her supernaturally-motivated suicide . He made his debut appearance in the first issue in 2007 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fall_of_Cthulhu_Volume_1:_The_Fugue"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>issue Harlot</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Michael_Alan_Nelson"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arachnid">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Arachnid</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sociocultural Characteristics Future Earth Subterranean Real World Information First appearance HPL : The Shadow Out of Time Created by H. P. Lovecraft The denizens of Earth's last age are a fictional species created by H. P. Lovecraft for his novella The Shadow Out of Time . Description Nothing is known about this species except that they inhabit Earth in a distant future, long after the extinction of humanity and of the coleopteran race that followed it. By the time of the arachnids, the Earth will be a cold, inhospitable planet, and the natives will be forced to move to underground burrows to survive. At least one member of this species was abducted by the Great Race of Yith and was met by Nathaniel Wingate Peaslee . ( HPL : The Shadow Out of Time )</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shadow_Out_of_Time"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#ArkhamHorrorFiction">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mary_Full%3F">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSRelatives"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Mary Full?</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>14 August 1864</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lovecraft’s paternal great-grandmother. Mary was both the wife and cousin of Joseph S. Lovecraft. She gave birth to six children: John Full, William, Joseph Jr., George, Aaron, and Mary.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isRelativeToR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1782</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Lurking_Chronology:_A_Timeline_of_the_Derleth_Mythos">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Lurking Chronology: A Timeline of the Derleth Mythos</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Pete Rawlik Cover Artist Steve Santiago English The Lovecraft eZine Press Release Date August 2015 44 is a scholarly work by American Pete Rawlik . Running to a total of 44 , it was published by The Lovecraft eZine Press in August 2015; both paperback and digital editions were released. Overview References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Pete Rawlik</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>44</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 2015</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Sorcerers_Jewel">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Sorcerer's Jewel</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A story by robert bloch that first appeared in the february 1939 issue of strange stories . It concerns the photographer david niles, who is obsessed with dream worlds . The narrator hits on the idea of lithomancy--"divination by the use of jewels"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_Bloch"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Strange_Stories"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>February 1939</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Echo_of_a_Distant_Scream">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Echo of a Distant Scream</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"il segno gialloclosure" is a short story by american lee clark zumpe . It was written by request from author brian m. sammons as a piece involving the fictional character of private eye frank nardi .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lee_Clark_Zumpe"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Mystery</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Detective_Agency"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Summoning_of_the_Old_Ones">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Summoning of the Old Ones</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A three-part lovecraftian tale is a short collection of lovecraftian fiction by american author jeffrey thomas . Set in the dystopian sci-fi setting of punktown, the stories form a continuous narrative . Despite being the last in the trilogy, "wunderwaffe" was in fact the first to be published .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jeffrey_Thomas"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Science fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Punktown"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>December 2019</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_in_the_Museum_%28DART_production%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleAudioAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Horror in the Museum (DART production)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Horror in the Museum (DART production) Based on " The Horror in the Museum " Running time 70 minutes (approx.) United States English The Horror in the Museum is a Dark Adventure Radio Theatre production by the H. P. Lovecraft Historical Society , adapting the story of the same name . Overview Running to approximately 70 minutes, The Horror in the Museum is an audio drama produced in the style of a 1930s radio broadcast. It is available in both physical compact disc and digital download formats, with both versions including a series of thematic props linked to the story: a ticket to the titular wax museum, a photograph of Alaskan ruins, a page from the Pnakotic Fragments , and the (fictional) front page from a newspaper of the time. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_in_the_Museum"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Yuletide_1989">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu Yuletide 1989</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Bruce W. Timm United States English Crypt of Cthulhu Cryptic Publications Release Date December 1989 52 Preceded by Hallowmas 1989 Followed by Candlemas 1990 was the 69th issue of Robert M. Price 's long-running Lovecraftian fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Edited by Price with cover art by Bruce W. Timm, it ran to 52 in an A5 format, and was published by Cryptic Publications in December 1989. Contents Editorial Shards by Robert M. Price "Terror Wears Yellow" by Lin Carter "A Bottle of Djinn" by Lin Carter "Sweet Tooth" by Lin Carter "The Bell in the Tower" by Lin Carter and H. P. Lovecraft "The Strange Doom of Enos Harker" by Lin Carter "Nameless Gods and Entities: Robert E. Howard's Contributions to the Cthulhu Mythos" by Lin Carter "The Great Old Ones" by Lin Carter Letters by Robert Bloch , Albert Roger, Frank Philipp, Jeff Leach, Douglas S. Vaughan, Darrell Schweitzer , and Stefan R. Dziemianowicz Reviews by Stefan Dziemianowicz</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>52</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>December 1989</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Does_Love_Forgive%3F">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Does Love Forgive?</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Does love forgive? Is a scenario supplement for the call of cthulhu horror roleplaying game . Written by lynne hardy , airis kamiska, anna maria mazur, it featured cover and interior art by marcin lesniak . First released in polish by black mon</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lynne_Hardy"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Airis Kamińska</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Anna Maria Mazur</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>Poland</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>64</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Polish</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2020</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Littlest_Lovecraft:_The_Dreamlands_Collection">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Storybooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Littlest Lovecraft: The Dreamlands Collection</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft (original stories) Tro Rex (adaptations) Eyona Bella English Genre(s) Children's Esoteric Order of Publishing Release Date October 1, 2018 61 is a children's book, published by the Esoteric Order of Publishing in 2018. It was written by Tro Rex and illustrated by Eyona Bella, featuring adaptations of three stories from H. P. Lovecraft 's Dream Cycle . Overview</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Tro Rex</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Children's</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>61</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2018 October 1</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_E._Howard">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsCoAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsCorrespondents"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Robert E. Howard</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1936 June 11th</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Robert ervin howard was an american pulp writer of fantasy, horror, historical adventure . He is best known for creating the character of conan the barbarian . He was a member of "the lovecraft circle," a group of like-minded writers and friends .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1906 January 22nd</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Wade">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>James Wade</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1 August 1983</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Wade was an american -born writer and composer who lived much of his adult life in south korea . He wrote a number of stories set in the greater cthulhu mythos . His symphonic and chamber music compositions were performed in numerous countries .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Illinois Granite City</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>5 January 1930</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Veltroch">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Planets"/>
    <rdfs:label>Veltroch</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Fomalhaut system Planet Veltrochni First appearance EXP : White Darkness Created by David A. McIntee is a fictional planet created by David A. McIntee for his 1993 novel White Darkness . Description orbits the star Fomalhaut , about seven parsecs from the Solar System . Artifacts depicting the Great Old Ones have been found on the planet, at least one of which was dated to at least 3 billion years ago. ( EXP : White Darkness ) Nowadays, is inhabited by a sapient species known as the Veltrochni. ( EXP : The Dark Path ) External links at the Doctor Who wiki</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Planet</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Veltrochni</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Fomalhaut system</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#White_Darkness"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Grim_Investigations">
