  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nightscapes_August_1999">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nightscapes August 1999</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various English Edward P. Berglund Release Date August 1999 Preceded by March 1999 Followed by December 1999 is the eleventh issue of the sporadically-released online fanzine Nightscapes . It was edited by the later American and Lovecraft scholar Edward P. Berglund . Contents In addition to the written material listed below, this issue also included artwork by Nicole Morris, Mike Stagg, Alan M. Clark and Simon Barber. Fiction "Bethia" by Tom Liberman "The Jungle's Black God" by Joseph A. McCullough V "Condemned" by Adrian Kleinbergen "Island Paradise" by J. R. Boos and Graeme Stevens "Photo Essay" by Michael Minnis " With Eyes That See " by J. B. Lee "Rosa's Day" by Brendan Carson " When the Stars Are Right " by William Meikle "Mythos Variations" by David C. Kopaska-Merkel Non-Fiction "An Interview With World Fantasy Award Finalist Brian McNaughton" by Jeff VanderMeer "Why Lovecraft Still Matters" by Don Webb "Artists of the Cthulhu Mythos IV: Simon Barber" by G. W. Thomas Parodies "Kn-Kn-Knocking on Cthulhu's Door" by Daniel William Gonzales by David Kopaska-Merkel "Our Glowworld's Lump" by Doyle Netzly Poetry " Lovecraftian Cliff Notes for Ech-Pi-El's: The Thing on the Doorstep " by Franklyn Searight "Nightscape" by kåren " The Feaster From Afar " by Ron Shiflet " On a Beach Near Innsmouth " by Ron Shiflet " Mythos Haiku " by David C. Kopaska-Merkel "The Old Ritual" by Roy Corey " Scarlet Obeisance " by Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. " Cthulhu " by Michael Fantina " Cthulhu Wakes " by Michael Fantina Reviews By James Ambuehl and Edward P. Berglund External Links full issue free online at the Nightscapes website</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 1999</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Samantha_Lee_Howe">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Samantha Lee Howe</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Pseudonym(s) Sam Stone Connection to the Mythos Author Birth Date 15 October 1965 Manchester, England, UK United Kingdom Horror Fantasy Thriller Years Active 2007-present http://sam-stone.blogspot.com/ https://samanthaleehowe.co.uk/ , who goes by the pen name of Sam Stone for her horror and fantasy works, is an English author and editor. She has written numerous stories set in the Cthulhu Mythos inspired by the works of H. P. Lovecraft and many others. Her Lovecraftian short fiction has appeared in anthologies such as Return of the Old Ones , The Dark Rites of Cthulhu , Heroes of Red Hook , Atomic-Age Cthulhu , Eldritch Chrome , Through a Mythos Darkly , Tails of Terror , and Summer of Lovecraft , as well as in her own collection Cthulhu and Other Monsters . Stone has cited authors including Tanith Lee and Stephen King as among her inspirations . Selected Bibliography Collections Cthulhu and Other Monsters (2017) Short Fiction "Sonar City" (2013) " The Vessel " (2014) "Fall Out" (2015) "The Book of the Gods" (2015) "The Gold of Roatán" (2016) "Tell Me No Lies" (2016) "Food" (2017) "Of Gods and Blood" (2017) "The Rift" (2017) " Breaking Point " (2017) External Links Sam Stone at Wikipedia Sam Stone at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database Sam Stone at Goodreads References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>http://example.org/lovecraft#England UK Manchester</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United Kingdom</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Sam Stone</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://sam-stone.blogspot.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://samanthaleehowe.co.uk/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:hasYearsActiveR>2007-present</ex:hasYearsActiveR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>15 October 1965</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Armageddon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Armageddon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Background is one of the chaos lords which ruled the previous universe . In the last years of the 20th century, there was a battle between lady death and one of the last chaos lords, genocide . In 1999 during the war on earth between hell, lady death, and heaven, arrived.</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft_%28band%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPeople"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>H. P. Lovecraft (band)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. p. lovecraft was an american psychedelic rock band named for horror writer . The band's repertoire included two songs inspired by lovecraft stories . The band formed around folk singer/songwriter, and classically trained keyboardist, dave michaels .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
