  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Edmond_Hamilton">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsInspirationsAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Edmond Hamilton</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1 February 1977</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Edmond Moore Hamilton Birth Date 21 October 1904 Youngstown, Ohio, USA Death Date 1 February 1977 Lancaster, California , USA United States 𝐓 Edmond Moore Hamilton (21 October 1904-1 February 1977) was a prolific American science fiction, horror, and fantasy author active throughout the middle of the 20th century . He was one of the most frequent contributors to pulp magazine Weird Tales , having a total of 79 of his stories published within its pages. In addition to his prose work, Hamilton also wrote early stories for comic book superhero properties such as Superman and Batman . Hamilton's first published short story " The Monster-God of Mamurth " ( Weird Tales , August 1926) was cited by author Kenneth Sterling as at least a partial inspiration for his original draft of " In the Walls of Eryx ," before it was presented to H. P. Lovecraft for revision . Selected Bibliography Short Fiction " The Monster-God of Mamurth " (1926) External Links at Wikipedia at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Ohio Youngstown</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Edmond Moore Hamilton</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>21 October 1904</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Commonplace_Book">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Commonplace Book</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Notebook of 221 entries for characters and stories written by h. p. lovecraft between late 1919 - 1937 date 1938 . First 201 entries were typed by barlow from the original notebook in may 1934 with lovecraft annotating previous and writing new ideas in the typed copy . Lovecraft's handwritten additions are put between square brackets .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Commonplace Book Employed by the Late H. P. Lovecraft</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The Notes</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1938</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>1919 - 1937</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Thule">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>Thule</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ultima was first mentioned by pytheas, the first documented representative of the mediterranean world to visit the british isles . The roman poet vergil refers in his georgics to ultima --"farthest"--which became an expression for any place beyond the known world . The name was applied to ancient lands in the fiction of lovecraft circle writers robert e. howard and clark ashton smith .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Kingdom</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Pre-human Ancestors Voormi</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Earth"/>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Far north</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>western hemisphere</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>The Double Shadow</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>
    <ex:wasDestroyedOnL>End of the Hyperborean Age</ex:wasDestroyedOnL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dagon_October-December_1989">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dagon October-December 1989</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various United Kingdom English Dagon Press Release Date October 1989 58 was the twenty-sixth issue of the now-defunct Lovecraftian fanzine Dagon , billed as a D. F. Lewis special. It was edited by the late British and occasional Carl T. Ford . Contents (incomplete) Editorial by Carl T. Ford "Crawling at You" by uncredited "Etepsed" by Peter F. Jeffery " Etepsed Egnis " by D. F. Lewis "Brewing Up with Des Lewis" by Mark F. Samuels "Mort au Monde" by D. F. Lewis "Down to the Boots" by D. F. Lewis "Daughters" by D. F. Lewis "The Beach Hut" by D. F. Lewis "Beyond the Bookcase" by D. F. Lewis "Sponge and China Tea" by D. F. Lewis "Wall Pack" by D. F. Lewis "Wiles" by D. F. Lewis "A D.F. Lewis Bibliography" by D. F. Lewis Reviews Letters</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United Kingdom</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>58</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 1989</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#In_Which_We_Discover_the_101st_Acre">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>In Which We Discover the 101st Acre</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The call of poohthulhu was published in the anthology the call of poohthulhu in may 2022 . The story blends the world of winnie-the-pooh with lovecraftian horror .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Robert P. Ottone</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Call_of_Poohthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>May 2022</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#John_Kirowan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>John Kirowan</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Professor is a fictional character from robert e. howard 's contributions to the cthulhu mythos . He is often partnered with the character john conrad , to the extent that these stories are often referred to under the group title conrad &amp; kirowan . His ancestor, sir michael kirowan was a medieval knight famous for killing a particularly fierce and notorious villain .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Children_of_the_Night"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_E._Howard"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Michaelmas_1985">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu Michaelmas 1985</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Jason C. Eckhardt United States English Crypt of Cthulhu Cryptic Publications Release Date September 1985 68 Preceded by Lammas 1985 Followed by Hallowmas 1985 was the 34th issue of Robert M. Price 's long-running Lovecraftian fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Edited by Price with cover art provided by Jason C. Eckhardt , it ran to a total of 68 in an A5 format and was published by Cryptic Publications in September 1985. The issue was a special devoted to the works of the Providence Pals , an informal group of H. P. Lovecraft scholars and enthusiasts who were active from the late 1970s through to the 1990s. Contents Editorial Shards by Robert M. Price "The Recurring Doom" by S. T. Joshi "The Last Supper" by Donald R. Burleson "An Echo of Pipes" by Jason C. Eckhardt "In the Dead of the Night" by Marc A. Michaud "Blood Atonement" by Robert M. Price "The Madness Out of Space" by Peter H. Cannon Reviews by Dabney Hoskins Letters by L. Sprague de Camp , Ben P. Indick, S. T. Joshi, Richard L. Tierney , Will Murray , Eric Kramer, Rodolfo A. Ferraresi, John Betancourt , Randy Palmer, Michael J. Lotus, Richard A. Zotara, Michael F. Albertini, and Carlos Waldraven</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>68</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>September 1985</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Unseen_Masters">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Unseen Masters</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>United states english consists of three "mini-campaigns" set in modern day new york . It was written by bruce ballon with cover art by matt harpold and runs to a total of 216 pages .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Bruce_Ballon"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>216</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>[Call of Cthulhu (Role-Playing Game)</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2001</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_God_of_Dark_Laughter">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The God of Dark Laughter</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Michael Chabon English Genre(s) Cthulhu Mythos The New Yorker date April 2001 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos story by American Michael Chabon, first published in The New Yorker in April 2001 . Synopsis A bereaved husband and father is involved in the investigation of a murder in his capacity as district attorney. The ritualistic killing, that of a circus clown, leads him to uncover the existence of two warring cults, each of which follow different gods and possess polarised outlooks on the nature of the universe. Behind the Mythos The narrator's research leads him to a book on the occult written by Friedrich von Junzt , best known as the of the blasphemous tome the Unaussprechlichen Kulten . History The New Yorker (2001) The Year's Best Fantasy &amp; Horror: Fifteenth Annual Collection (2002, St. Martin's Griffin) Feeling Very Strange (2006, Tachyon Publications) American Fantastic Tales (2009, The Library of America) Sympathy for the Devil (2010, Night Shade Books) The Weird: A Compendium of Strange and Dark Stories (2012, Tor/SFBC) The Book of Cthulhu II (2012, 2022, Night Shade Books) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Michael Chabon</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The New Yorker</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>April 2001</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Re-Animator:_The_Musical">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Re-Animator: The Musical</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The musical is based on the low-budget 1985 horror movie re-animator . It was inspired in part by the musical horror-comedy little shop of horrors . Lyrics were provided by mark nutter, while stuart gordon, dennis paoli and william j. norris wrote the script .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United Kingdom</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasWriterW>http://example.org/lovecraft#Dennis_Paoli http://example.org/lovecraft#Stuart_Gordon Mark Nutter William J. Norris</ex:hasWriterW>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Re-Animator"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>March 2011</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Guardian_of_the_Book">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Guardian of the Book</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu mythos short story appeared in 1937 issue of weird tales . Narrated by a doctor who haunts used bookstores out of a "desire to peruse those so-called ‘forbidden books’ which speak of the unthinkable things of evil" story included in the collection tales of the lovecraft mythos .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Henry_Hasse"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 1937</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Blasphemies_%26_Revelations">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Blasphemies &amp; Revelations</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Robert M. Price Cover Artist Daryl Hutchison Pixaby English Mythos Books Release Date November 2008 Media Type Print Ebook 515 (574) is an anthology of works by Robert M. Price , which contains a mix of Mythos and non-Mythos stories from across his lengthy career. Overview Containing a total of 43 short stories and novelettes, runs to a total of 515 (later 574). First published in November 2008 by Mythos Books , a second edition was released by Exham Priory in September 2019. It is available in both physical print and ebook formats. Contents Introduction: "Fumbler at the Latch" "'Twas the Night" (non-Mythos) " Beneath the Tombstone " " Saucers from Yaddith " " Black Eons " (with Robert E. Howard ) "An Antique Coffin" (non-Mythos) " Wilbur Whateley Waiting " "Ashes to Ashes" (non-Mythos) "Midnight Mass" (non-Mythos) " The Deprogrammer " " A Thousand Young " " The Dweller in the Pot " " Exham Priory " " The Round Tower " " The Strange Fate of Alonzo Typer " " Behold, I Stand at the Door and Knock " " The Beard of Byatis " " Down in Limbo " " The Transition of Zadok Allen " " Under the Mound " " Young Goodwife Doten " " The Soul of the Devil-Bought " " Dope War of the Black Tong " " The Tree-House " (with W. H. Pugmire ) " Aquadingen " " Annotations for the Book of Night " " The Burrower Beneath " " Feery's Original Notes " " The Green Decay " " The Incubus of Atlantis " "The Shunpike" "The Strange Doom of Enos Harker" (with Lin Carter ) " The Transition of Abdul Alhazred " " Wrath of the Wind-Walker " (with James Ambuehl ) " The Thing from the Trenches " "From the Pits of Elder Blasphemy" (with Hugh B. Cave) "The Ghoul's Tale" " The Elephant God of Leng " " The Horror in the Genizah " " The Mark of Yig " " Acute Spiritual Fear " "The Devil's Steps" (although set in Ramsey Campbell 's Brichester University , this story contains no mythos elements) " A Mate for the Mutilator " "The Prying Investigations of Edwin M. Lillibridge" Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_M._Price"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>515</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>November 2008</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gzxtyos">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gzxtyos</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Mate of Othuyeg</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as Mate of Othuyeg First appearance EXP : "People of the Monolith: Stone of Death" Created by Crispin Burnham is the consort of Othuyeg , likely similar to her bridegroom.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>People of the Monolith: Stone of Death</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
