  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Mammoth_Book_of_Cthulhu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Mammoth Book of Cthulhu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Tim McDonagh English Robinson Release Date April 2016 476 is a collection of Lovecraftian fiction edited by Paula Guran and published by Robinson in April 2016. Both trade paperback and digital editions were released. Overview Contents "The Future Eats Everything" by Don Webb "A Clutch" by Laird Barron "I Believe That We Will Win" by Nadia Bulkin "The Sea Inside" by Amanda Downum "Those Who Watch" by Ruthanna Emrys "Deep Eden" by Richard Gavin "In the Sacred Cave" by Lois H. Gresh "In Syllables of Elder Seas" by Lisa L. Hannett "It's All the Same Road in the End" by Brian Hodge "The Peddler's Tale, or, Isobel's Revenge" by Caitlín R. Kiernan "Outside the House, Watching for the Crows" by John Langan "Falcon-and-Sparrows" by Yoon Ha Lee "In the Ruins of Mohenjo-Daro" by Usman T. Malik "The Cthulhu Navy Wife" by Sandra McDonald "Caro in Carno" by Helen Marshall "Legacy of Salt" by Silvia Moreno-Garcia "Backbite" by Norman Partridge "A Shadow of Thine Own Design" by W. H. Pugmire "Variations on Lovecraftian Themes" by Veronica Schanoes "An Open Letter to Mister Edgar Allan Poe, from a Fervent Admirer" by Michael Shea "Just Beyond the Trailer Park" by John Shirley "Alexandra Lost" by Simon Strantzas "Umbilicus" by Damien Angelica Walters "I Do Not Count the Hours" by Michael Wehunt "I Dress My Lover in Yellow" by A. C. Wise</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>476</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>April 2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Hallowmas_2000">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu Hallowmas 2000</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Crypt of Cthulhu’’ Hallowmas 2000 Various Allen Koszowski Cover Artist D. L. Hutchinson United States English Mythos Books Release Date November 2000 48 Preceded by Lammas 2000 Followed by Eastertide 2001 is the 106th issue of Robert M. Price 's long-running fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Edited by Price, it ran to a total of 48 in an A5 format and was published by Mythos Books in November 2000. Cover art was supplied by D. L. Hutchinson with interior work by Allen Koszowski . Contents Editorial Shards by Robert M. Price " Herbert West--Reincarnated " by Rod Heather, Brian McNaughton , Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. , Robert M. Price, C. J. Henderson , and Michael Cisco "Notes from a Snug Room" by T. G. Cockcroft " Scarlet Obeisance " by Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. "The Club Bore" by Darrell Schweitzer "Resignation Accepted" by Darrell Schweitzer "The Raconteur" by Darrell Schweitzer "A Three-Lobed, Burning Enigma" by Darrell Schweitzer "The New Member" by Darrell Schweitzer "Unauthorized Withdrawal" by Darrell Schweitzer "The Comedian" by Darrell Schweitzer "Insanity" by Robert A. Lovely "Ancients" by Robert A. Lovely " Lovecraftian Cliff Notes " by Frank Searight "Camptown Zendo" by R. L. Tierney Reviews by Fred Blosser</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>48</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>November 2000</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Innsmouth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Innsmouth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAccessibilityL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Newburyport"/>
    <ex:hasAccessibilityL>Bus Route between Arkham</ex:hasAccessibilityL>
    <ex:hasAccessibilityL>Train line to Rowley (Closed)</ex:hasAccessibilityL>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A seaside town in essex county, massachusetts, run by the esoteric order of dagon and their deep one allies until their destruction in 1928 . It has appeared in numerous cthulhu mythos stories written by various authors . The town lacked a public library or a chamber of commerce by 1927 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL>Fungi from Yuggoth The Dreams in the Witch-House The Thing on the Doorstep The Trail of Cthulhu The Seal of R'lyeh The Innsmouth Heritage Innsmouth Harvest</ex:hasAppearanceL>
    <ex:hasOriginL>1643</ex:hasOriginL>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Harbour</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Deep One hybrids</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Humans</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Massachusetts"/>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Essex County</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shadow_Over_Innsmouth"/>
    <ex:wasDestroyedOnL>1928</ex:wasDestroyedOnL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lisa_Padol">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Critics"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lisa Padol</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Editor Games designer Roleplaying games Years Active 1991-present is a games designer, occasional critic, and staff editor for independent third-party content producer Golden Goblin Press . She has also worked for other small press gaming companies including Arc Dream Publishing and Pelgrane Press , and her gaming credits include a number of supplements for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu horror roleplaying game, as well as contributions to Bayt al Azif , a sporadically-released almanac focused on gaming in the Cthulhu Mythos , and Alarums and Excursions , a long-running amateur press roleplaying magazine. Selected Bibliography Editor Island of Ignorance: The Third Cthulhu Companion (2013) Tales of the Caribbean (2016) Terror of Octobernomicon (unknown date) Author Bayt al Azif (2019-2023, contributor) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads at RPGGeek References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasYearsActiveR>1991-present</ex:hasYearsActiveR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Miskatonic_%28comic%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Miskatonic (comic)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>137 miskatonic is a five issue cthulhu mythos comic book written by mark sable and illustrated by giogio pontrelli . It was published by aftershock comics individually before being compiled into a trade paperback . It had a 51-page sequel with the same name .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Mark Sable</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>137 pages</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Miskatonic</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2021 (TPB) July 7th</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPL_and_HPB:_Lovecrafts_Use_of_Theosophy">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>HPL and HPB: Lovecraft's Use of Theosophy</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here Robert M. Price United States English Genre(s) Non-fiction Crypt of Cthulhu Roodmas 1982 date May 1982 " " is a non-fiction work by leading American H. P. Lovecraft scholar Robert M. Price . It was first published in the Roodmas 1982 edition of Price's long-running Lovecraftian fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . The work explores the debt of inspiration owed by Lovecraft to the belief system of Theosophy, an esoteric religion largely compiled and popularised by Helena Blavatsky in the late 19th century . References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_M._Price"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Roodmas_1982"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>May 1982</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Northot">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Northot</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Forgotten God</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Thing That Should Not Be</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as The Forgotten God The Thing That Should Not Be EXP : Night Terrors EXP : Le Retour de is a mysterious entity related to Yog-Sothoth , Shub-Niggurath , and possibly even to Azathoth . It manifests as a faun-like humanoid with color-changing hair or as a glowing halo of unknown color. The faun-like traits may be a hint of its lineage due to both it and Shub-Niggurath's ungulate-like features of the goat and deer respectively.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Le Retour de Northot</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Night Terrors</ex:hasAppearanceC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Detective_Tales">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Arkham Detective Tales</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Gareth Ryder-Hanrahan Jérôme Huguenin Cover Artist Jérôme Huguenin English Trail of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Pelgrane Press Release Date 2009 (first edition) 2010 (second edition) is a scenario supplement for the Trail of Cthulhu horror roleplaying game. Written by Gareth Ryder-Hanrahan and featuring artwork from Jérôme Huguenin, it was published by Pelgrane Press in 2009 ; both print and digital editions were made available. In 2010, an extended edition including updated rules and an additional scenario was released . Overview Gallery References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gareth_Ryder-Hanrahan"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
