  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jennifer_Singer">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Jennifer Singer</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Artist writes and illustrates children's books, as well as university review cases and books on childcare. She is most notable to this wiki for the artwork she provided for J. J. Savage 's Young Cthulhu series of toddler's picture books, published through Amazon. Selected Works Cover Art A Call for Cthulhu A Home for Cthulhu (2022) Interior Art A Call for Cthulhu A Home for Cthulhu (2022) See Also Images by External Links at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Glimpse_of_H._P._L.">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Glimpse of H. P. L.</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A Glimpse of H. P. L. Mary V. Dana United States English Genre(s) Non-fiction Grant-Hadley Rhode Island on Lovecraft date 1945 " A Glimpse of H. P. L. " is a short non-fiction piece by American Mary V. Dana. It was first published in the short scholarly collection Rhode Island on Lovecraft in 1945. Since its first appearance it has been translated into German for Festa's H. P. Lovecraft's Bibliothek des Schreckens series. History Rhode Island on Lovecraft (1945, 1946) Lovecraft Remembered (1998) Namenlose Kulte (2006)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Mary V. Dana</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Rhode_Island_on_Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1945</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tales_of_the_Miskatonic_Valley">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tales of the Miskatonic Valley</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist John T. Snyder United States English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Chaosium Release Date November 1991 : Uncovering Forbidden Secrets in Lovecraft is a scenario supplement for the Call of Cthulhu horror roleplaying game. Written by an ensemble team, it was published by Chaosium in November 1991 . Overview Contents "Freak Show" "Regiment of Dread" "A Painted Smile" "The Watcher in the Valley" "Fade to Grey" "The Trail of Yig" Publication History Since its first release, has been translated into French where it was published by Descartes Editeur under the title Contes de la vallée du Miskatonic . Design Team Written by: Scott Aniolowski , Geoff Gillan, Keith Herber , Erik Herber, Kevin Ross , Richard Watts , and Todd A. Woods Artwork by: Gus DiZerega, Charlie Krank, Petra Pino, Blair Reynolds , Tony Santos, John T. Snyder, and Carol Triplett Production Staff: Les Brooks, Keith Herber, Amanda J. Lee, and Anne G. Merritt Gallery References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>November 1991</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Alchemist">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Alchemist</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story is recounted by the protagonist, count antoine de c- . Antoine's noble ancestor was responsible for the death of a dark wizard . The wizard's son swore revenge on antoine and all his descendants .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 1916</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>1908</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Beast_in_the_Cave">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Beast in the Cave</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The picture (nonextant), the alchemist is a short story by american horror fiction writer . It was first published in the june 1918 issue of the amateur press journal the vagrant . Popular culture sapthuran's ep (2007) is based upon the short story .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Very_Hungry_Cthulhupillar">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Storybooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Very Hungry Cthulhupillar</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ben Mund Ben Mund English Genre(s) Children's Signal Fire Studios LLC Release Date February 4, 2015 32 is a children's book, published by Signal Fire Studios LLC in 2015. It was written and illustrated by Ben Mund, as a Cthulhu -themed parody of Eric Carle's The Very Hungry Caterpillar . Synopsis The story begins on a Sunday morning when the stars were almost right, leading to a cthulhupillar crawling out of his tomb in R'lyeh . The cthulhupillar spends the whole week eating through numerous artifacts and entities from the Cthulhu Mythos , until he got too full and went back to rest. A few strange eons later, the cthulhupillar woke up again to eat the entire world.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Ben Mund</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Children's</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>32</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2015 February 4</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ron Shiflet</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ronald "ron" shiflet is an american writer of fiction and poetry . He has worked with other mythos authors including james ambuehl , glynn owen barrass and ran cartwright . A number of his works are set in and around tenoka , east texas, a region created by the author himself for this purpose .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Texas</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Ron Shifuretto</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>ロン・シフレット</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Yellow_Sign">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Yellow Sign</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A short story in chambers' 1895 collection is connected to a play called "the king in yellow" the story concerns an artist named scott and his favorite model, tessie reardon . The play has a disastrous effect on those who read it .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraftiana_Vol_2,_Issue_3">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Periodicals"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lovecraftiana Vol 2, Issue 3</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Sofyan Syarief Jens Heimdahl Ernő Juhász Cover Artist Nottsuo English Rogue Planet Press Release Date October 2017 52  is an issue of the periodical Lovecraftiana , edited by Gavin Chappell and published by Rogue Planet Press in October 2017. Contents "The Voyage of the Jericho" by Max D Stanton "The Bride of Cthulhu" by Neal Privett "Philadelphia" by Paul Lubaczewski "What the Sea Saw" by David J Wing "The House in the Woods" by Nate Ealy "Maggona Beach" by David B Harrington "A Prayer to Ghroth" and "The Hymn of Basatan" by Allen Mackey</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>52</ex:hasPageCountW>
