  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Far_Realm">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dimensions"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalLocations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsOriginatingFromMythosInspiredWorks"/>
    <rdfs:label>Far Realm</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a plane of madness, which is very far from the material plane . Consists of layers that are very thin from a mile in thickness at most to an inch at the thinnest . The rulers of the are the uvuudaums, whose presence would make most creatures insane .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Book_of_Kyog">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosBooksFictional"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdfs:label>Book of K'yog</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Unknown The is a fictional Cthulhu Mythos grimoire created by the late British John Glasby . The book's is unknown in the fictional Cthulhu Mythos . It is doubtful whether any copies of it exist in the present day. Appearances The Book of K’yog was referenced in John Glasby's short story " The Old One " through an unnamed volume which discusses its contents.</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chig">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Chig</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Yarkdao"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Unknown, possibly Tond Yarkdao First appearance " The Inhabitant of the Lake " Created by Ramsey Campbell is an entity first made mention of in The Revelations of Glaaki , which appears to have been its only reference to date ( EXP : " The Inhabitant of the Lake "). It is apparently some form of deity worshipped by the alien yarkdao , whose Priests of undertake devotional rituals lasting three-and-quarter years in length.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Inhabitant_of_the_Lake"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ramsey_Campbell"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Beneath_Cayugas_Churning_Waves">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Beneath Cayuga's Churning Waves</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cultists of gla'aki inadvertently summoned the creature to a subterranean chamber in upstate new york in the 1800s . As journalist tisha stumbles across the story, she finds that it has already begun to awaken and assert its will on those nearby . Only tisha and one member of the tv crew survive the encounter .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lee_Clark_Zumpe"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Children_of_Glaaki"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Hound_%28short_story%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Hound (short story)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"the hound" is a short story written by h. p. lovecraft in 1922 . It was published in the february 1924 issue of weird tales . Lovecraft later dismissed it as "a dead dog" and "a piece of junk" it contains the first mention of lovecraft's fictional text the necronomicon .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>February 1924</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Alan_Moore">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Alan Moore</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Comic book creator Birth Date 18 November 1953 Northampton, England United Kingdom https://alanmooreworld.blogspot.com/ (born 18 November 1953) is a prolific British comic book creator and prose writer whose work has at times explored the Cthulhu Mythos. For the comic book genre, he has written several works set in and around the Cthulhu Mythos , including the hugely popular linked series Neonomicon and Providence . His League of Extraordinary Gentlemen series has also incorporated the Mythos in a supporting role. Moore has written short stories connected to the Mythos, including "The Courtyard", whose adaptation in comic book format was the inspiration for Providence . Selected Bibliography Comic Books 's The Courtyard (2003) 's Yuggoth Cultures and Other Growths (2007) Neonomicon (2010-2011) Providence (2015-2017) Prose "The Courtyard" (1994) "Recognition" (1995) "Zaman's Hill" (1995) External Links at Wikipedia at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>http://example.org/lovecraft#England Northampton</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United Kingdom</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://alanmooreworld.blogspot.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Comic book creator</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>18 November 1953</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Parson_Clark">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Parson Clark</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Massachusetts Church parson Human First appearance HPL : " The Picture in the House " Created by H. P. Lovecraft was a church parson mentioned in the story " The Picture in the House " by H. P. Lovecraft . When the unnamed old man from the story obtained a copy of the book Regnum Congo , was one of those who helped him read some of the Latin passages. The parson died by drowning in a pond, but the circumstances surrounding this are not explained.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Picture_in_the_House"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_in_the_Museum">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Horror in the Museum</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. p. lovecraft ghostwrote for somerville, ma writer hazel heald in october 1932, one of five which he did so . Story concerns the relationship between stephen jones and george rogers , the owner of a private wax museum specialising in the grotesque .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hazel_Heald"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Howard_Phillips_Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
  </rdf:Description>
