  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Of_Circles_and_Rings">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Of Circles and Rings</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Pete jones, an up-and-coming martial artist, thinks he has found someone who can teach him a new method of fighting which will make him unbeatable . Unbeknownst to pete, his "teacher" is actually a millennia-old cultist of yog-sothoth . His god requires sacrifice to continue granting</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tom_Lynch"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dark_Rites_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 2014</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dreaming_in_Rlyeh_Issue_1">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dreaming in R'lyeh Issue 1</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various English C. Brut Release Date January 2003 60 Followed by Dreaming in R'lyeh Issue 2 was the first issue of short-lived Lovecraftian fanzine Dreaming in R'lyeh . Edited by C. Brut, it was independently published in January 2003. Contents "Azathoth Awakened" by R. S. Cartwright " Screaming Crawler " by David Conyers "Fumgoids" by Jim Gruetzmacher Kammer " When the Stars Are Ripe " by Stanley C. Sargent "The Chlorine Crypt" by Cassius Baron "Le Reflet dans le Mur" by Cedric Sagne "Re-Quest Denied" by Stanley C. Sargent " The Bones of the Old Ones " by Jeffrey Thomas "The Shallow Sea" by Cassius Baron "Revisiting Dark Valley Destiny" by Gary Romeo "The Elephant in the Tower" by Gary Romeo "A Shrewdness of Apes" by C. Brut</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>60</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>January 2003</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#White_Feather">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>White Feather</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A privateer recalls a time when he and his crew encountered a hamlet populated by deep one hybrids . The men put the village to the torch, and later his ship was boarded by deep one and all but the captain slaughtered . The privateer later returns to the waters outside the destroyed habitation and waits for the deep ones to once again come aboard . The story ends with a statement that the deep ones had subsequently begun to prepare themselves for a war with those</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#T._E._Grau"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2014</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraftian_Covens">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lovecraftian Covens</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Franklyn Searight English H. Harksen Productions (unconfirmed) Release Date September 2015 238 is a collection of horror fiction by the late American Franklyn Searight . Running to a total of 238 in a paperback format, it was released in September 2015, and is currently (as of September 2016) available through Lulu's print-on-demand service. Although uncertain, it appears to have been published by H. Harksen Productions. Overview</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Franklyn_Searight"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>238</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>September 2015</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dwellers_in_the_Depths">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosBooksFictional"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dwellers in the Depths</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Gaston Le Fe English Real World Information First appearance CIRCLE : " The Aquarium " Created by Carl Jacobi is a fictional book created by Carl Jacobi , part of a set of books dealing with undersea horrors that are mentioned in his 1962 short story " The Aquarium ". Jacobi says that the book was written "by Gaston Le Fe who, the foreword stated quite blandly, had died insane." The other eldritch aquatic books named by Jacobi are Hydrophinnae and Unter Zee Kulten ; all three texts are frequently mentioned by Brian Lumley to indicate an unwholesome interest in oceanic secrets, in such stories as " The Cypress Shell ," " Rising with Surtsey ", " Dagon's Bell ", " De Marigny's Clock ", " Lord of the Worms ", and " The House of the Temple ".</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnA rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Aquarium"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByA rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Carl_Jacobi"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Rex_Murphy">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Rex Murphy</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>The Arkham Advertiser</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Reporter</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The reporter is a fictional character created by fantasy flight games . He appears in their interpretation of h. p. lovecraft's cthulhu mythos .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Kingsport Horror Deluxe Expansion</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shoggoths_Old_Peculiar">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Shoggoth's Old Peculiar</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Story first appeared in print in 1998 in the mammoth book of comic fantasy . It was nominated for the world fantasy award for best short fiction . Story takes its name from the beer which cultists drink together .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Neil_Gaiman"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Dutch</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>French</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Portuguese</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The Mammoth Book of Comic Fantasy</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_United_Co-operative">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AmateurPress"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Periodicals"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The United Co-operative</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>United amateur press association irregular 1918 final publication 1921 was an amateur press journal in 1918 by h. p. lovecraft . The journal published lovecraft's prose poem " memory " and lovecraft and jackson's collaboration " the crawling chaos " the publication was to promote lovecraft's idea of cooperative papers .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robot_Yeti">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ArtefactsOriginatingFromMythosAdjacentWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#TechnologicalArtefacts"/>
    <rdfs:label>Robot Yeti</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The abominable snowmen are robots created and used by the great intelligence on earth . Were deployed by the great intelligence in tibet, where they formed its footsoldiers during a convoluted plan to conquer earth . Armed with fangs and claws, and possessed augmented durability and strength .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeA>Weapon</ex:hasTypeA>
    <ex:isUsedByA>The Great Intelligence</ex:isUsedByA>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnA>The Abominable Snowmen</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnA>
    <ex:wasCreatedByA>The Great Intelligence</ex:wasCreatedByA>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Rise_of_Hastur">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Rise of Hastur</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Kira Magrann Lara Turner Evil Hat Productions Publication Date 2021 Roleplaying game Fate is a timeline supplement for the Fate of Cthulhu supernatural horror roleplaying game. Written by Kira Magrann and Lara Turner , it was published in a digital format by Evil Hat Productions in 2021 . Overview References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2021</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Wheaton_Phillips">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSRelatives"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>James Wheaton Phillips</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>29 August 1852</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation></ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isRelativeToR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1803</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Knaa">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>K'naa</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A province of the lost continent of mu that housed several ancient, pre-human ruins . Most of the information comes from around 173,148 bc, the year of the red moon, during the reign of king thabon . The cult of shub-niggurath headed by high preist t'yog from the copper temple .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Muvians"/>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Earth"/>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mu"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Out_of_the_Aeons"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Terror_in_Venice_%28Call_of_Cthulhu_LCG%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Terror in Venice (Call of Cthulhu LCG)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Terror in venice is a deluxe expansion for fantasy flight games' call of cthulhu: the card game . It focuses on the setting (1930s venice) and on expanding a previously unused game mechanic, that of night and day . As with all call of cthulhu: the card game game sets, terror in venice was discontinued with the introduction of arkham horror: the card game .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2013</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Rllyeh">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>R'llyeh</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesL>Seventh World of the Ancients</ex:hasAlternativeNamesL>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesL>The Underwater City of the Aqueos</ex:hasAlternativeNamesL>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesL>the Aqueos City</ex:hasAlternativeNamesL>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesL>the First Atlantis</ex:hasAlternativeNamesL>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The underwater city of the aqueos is a fictional location in the prime reality of the marvel universe by marvel comics . It is a clear pastiche of r'lyeh . It is a race of aquatic vampires, formerly aliens .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>eldritch writings</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>elritch ruins</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasTypeL>underwater city</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Aqueos (vampires)</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>formerly Ancient things</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>ADJ</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>Marvel Comics : Namor: The First Mutant Vol 1 3| Namor: The First Mutant #3 (2010)</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ad_Criticos:_Liber_Secundus">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ad Criticos: Liber Secundus</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft United States English The Argosy date February 1914 " " is a poem by the late American Howard Phillips Lovecraft . It was first published as a letter to the editor in the pulp magazine The Argosy in February 1914 ; it did not see print again until its inclusion in the 1980 Necronomicon Press collection Uncollected Prose &amp; Poetry Volume 2 . History The Argosy (1914) Uncollected Prose &amp; Poetry Volume 2 (1980) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
