  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Solace_of_the_Farther_Moon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Solace of the Farther Moon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Allan Rozinski English Wildside Press Weirdbook Annual 2 date February 2019  " " is a poem by Allan Rozinski . Synopsis An alien species living on the dark side of the moon speculate what’s will happen to them once humanity has used up the Earth 's resources. History "" was published in the Wildside Press anthology Weirdbook Annual 2 in 2019.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Allan_Rozinski"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Weirdbook Annual 2</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>February 2019</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Hunt">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Hunt</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A man named alvin doyle goes to the small town of monk's hollow to kill his cousin, will benson . Doyle is next in line for benson's grandfather's inheritance . The story is set in the fictional town of monk's hollow from kuttner's earlier tale, " the frog "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Henry_Kuttner"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Strange_Stories"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>June 1939</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Valley_of_Banof">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalLocations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>Valley of Banof</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Dreamlands ( Lomar ) Valley First appearance HPL : " Polaris " Created by H. P. Lovecraft The is a fictional created by the late American author H. P. Lovecraft for his short story " Polaris ". Overview A valley in the far northern kingdom of Lomar in the Dreamlands , the can be seen at a distance from the Watchtower of Thapnen in the city of Olathoë . ( HPL : " Polaris ")</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Valley</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Dreamlands ( Lomar )</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Polaris"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Danvers_State_Hospital">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsIncorporatedFromTheRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Danvers State Hospital</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Hospital complex was mostly demolished in 2006 to make way for a housing development . Derelict hospital was used as the set of the 2001 horror film session 9 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shadow_Over_Innsmouth"/>
    <ex:hasOriginL>2006</ex:hasOriginL>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Healthcare facility</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Massachusetts"/>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Danvers</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pickmans_Model"/>
    <ex:wasDestroyedOnL>2006</ex:wasDestroyedOnL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Raabe">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Raabe</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>1917 June 28th</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Imperial German Navy</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>June 28th, 1917 Germany Navy Engineer (Submariner) Imperial German Navy Human First appearance HPL : " The Temple " Created by H. P. Lovecraft Engineer is a fictional character created by the late American author H. P. Lovecraft for his short story " The Temple ". Overview As an engineer aboard the German submarine U-29, served under Lieutenant-Commander Karl Heinrich during the First World War. During the course of the supernatural events experienced by the crew, and fellow engineer Schneider were both killed by an explosion in the engine room.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Temple"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#To_Bare_Ones_Soul">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>To Bare One's Soul</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ron Shiflet English Genre(s) Horror Book of Dark Wisdom #7 date October 2005  " " is a short story by American Ron Shiflet . Synopsis A madman with a talent for writing stories about the horrors he has seen torments one of his irritating coworkers for a time, before summoning forth a pack of extraterrestrial abominations to consume him. Characters Loyd Bloom , a man who has stolen his deceased brother's writings and passed them off as his own. Neil Paine , a man cursed with well-concealed insanity and a sadistic nature. History "" was published in Book of Dark Wisdom #7 in October 2005 and later in the anthology They Thrive in Darkness: Mythos and Madness in 2018.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Book of Dark Wisdom #7</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2005</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mythos_Collector_Spring_2003">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Mythos Collector Spring 2003</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Allen Koszowski English Mythos Collector Dark Tree Press Release Date May 2003 56 Preceded by Fall 2002 Followed by Winter 2004 was the fifth edition of the sporadically-released fanzine Mythos Collector . Edited by Brian Lingard, it featured cover art by Allen Koszowski , and was published by Dark Tree Press in May 2003 . Contents First Words by Brian Lingard Interview with Graham Masterton by James R. Cain "The Birth" by Paul Pinn "The Terror at Woodruff" by Michael Penncavage "The Blood of Alhazred" by Lee Clark Zumpe Interview with C. J. Henderson by Brian Lingard "Mythos Collecting in the U.K." by Keith Woodhams "Artist Changeling" by W. Paul Ganley "Were They Minions of Cthulhu?" by W. Paul Ganley Auction Watch Reviews Mythos Gallery (artwork by Mike Todd) Contributor Bios References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>56</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mythos_Collector"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 2003</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Terror_of_Emmett%E2%80%99s_Cove">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Terror of Emmett’s Cove</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"the terror of emmett's cove " is a short cthulhu mythos story by american lee clark zumpe . Two people on a mission to purchase a copy of the pnakotic manuscripts discover that the writings are in fact alive . The writings have possessed the body of a local man and begun calling forth terrifying entities .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lee_Clark_Zumpe"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Lovecraft's Disciples #13</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 2009</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ilythla">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ilyth'la</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Offspring of Cthulhu and Nyarlathotep Relatives Cthulhu (creator) Nyarlathotep (creator) First appearance EXP : The Encyclopedia Cthulhiana is a woman created by Cthulhu and Nyarlathotep who founded her own cult among humans in prehistoric Virklyu . According to The Sussex Manuscript , many cultists consider themselves her children. She is often identified with the Hebrew demon Lilith . ( EXP : The Encyclopedia Cthulhiana )</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Encyclopedia_Cthulhiana"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#hasBrotherR">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#SymmetricProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label>hasBrotherR</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#On_the_Threshold">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>On the Threshold</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ominous realities: the anthology of dark speculative horrors is a short story by lovecraftian author william meikle . Two scientists attempting to access other dimensions tear a hole in reality which allows something to come through . After they remove their system protections, an eldritch guardian banishes the thing back to its own world . The description of the entity which breaks through from another dimension is virtually identical to that of a being from two of meikle'</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#William_Meikle"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Ominous Realities: The Anthology of Dark Speculative Horrors</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 2013</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Haunter_of_the_Dark_%281975_comic%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleComicBookAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Haunter of the Dark (1975 comic)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Haunter of the Dark H. P. Lovecraft Norberto Buscaglia Alberto Breccia Argentina Spanish Genre(s) Horror Release Date December 1975 The Haunter of the Dark is a comic book-format adaptation of the H. P. Lovecraft short story of the same name by Argentinian artist Alberto Breccia . Adapted by Norberto Buscaglia, it was published in the Spanish- volume Los mitos de Cthulhu in December 1975. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Norberto Buscaglia</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>Argentina</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Spanish</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>December 1975</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Hallowmas_1986">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu Hallowmas 1986</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cover Artist Allen Koszowski United States English Crypt of Cthulhu Cryptic Publications Release Date November 1986 72 Preceded by Michaelmas 1986 Followed by Medusa and Other Poems by H. P. Lovecraft is the 43rd issue of long-running fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Running to a total of 80 , it was edited by Robert M. Price , featured cover art by Allen Koszowski , and was published in an A5 format by Cryptic Publications in November 1986 . Subtitled "The Tomb Herd and Others", the issue was a special, its contents devoted primarily to the works of Ramsey Campbell , a contemporary British Cthulhu Mythos writer who would later go on to become one of the U.K.'s most respected horror authors. Contents Editorial Shards by Robert M. Price " The Tomb Herd " by Ramsey Campbell "The Other Name of Azathoth" by uncredited "The Face in the Desert" by Ramsey Campbell " A Madness from the Vaults I " by Ramsey Campbell "The Tower from Yuggoth" by Ramsey Campbell " The Plain of Sound " by Ramsey Campbell " The Return of the Witch " by Ramsey Campbell " The Mine on Yuggoth " by Ramsey Campbell " The Stone on the Island " by Ramsey Campbell " A Madness from the Vaults II " by Ramsey Campbell Letters by Virgil Utter, Vic Ghidalia, L. Sprague de Camp , Lin Carter , Robert Bloch , and Charles Garofalo References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>72</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>November 1986</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Art_of_H._P._Lovecrafts_Cthulhu_Mythos">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ArkhamHorrorFiction"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Art of H. P. Lovecraft's Cthulhu Mythos</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>United States English Fantasy Flight Games Release Date October 2006 192 is a coffee table-format art book collecting the best imagery from Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu and Fantasy Flight Games ' Arkham Horror lines of Cthulhu Mythos material. Edited by Pat Harrigan and Brian Wood, it was published by Fantasy Flight Games in October 2006 in a hardcover format, and ran to a total of 192 . Overview</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>192</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 2006</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Foulness_Island_Vanishings">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Foulness Island Vanishings</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Glynn Owen Barrass United Kingdom English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Horror Stygian Fox Release Date 2020 84 : A Corrupting Infiltration in a Time of War is a scenario sourcebook for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game. Overview Containing a complete stand-alone scenario including all necessary non-player characters, handouts and maps, was written by prolific Cthulhu Mythos Glynn Owen Barrass . Published by third-party content creator Stygian Fox in 2010, it runs to a total of 84 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Glynn_Owen_Barrass"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United Kingdom</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>84</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2020</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Graboid">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonSapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Graboid</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Tu Lung</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sociocultural Characteristics Also known as Tu Lung ("Earth Dragon") Presumed to be relic Earth species Dependent on life-cycle stage Physical Characteristics Average height Varies dependent on life-cycle stage Average length Varies dependent on life-cycle stage Average mass Varies dependent on life-cycle stage Real World Information First appearance INFL : Tremors (1990) Created by S.S. Wilson Brent Maddock Ron Underwood  The is a fictional arthropod species featured as the main antagonist in the Tremors film series and related media. The has a metamorphic insect-like life cycle with several different forms at different stages of its life, including a small flying version, an immense adult version very similar to the Cthonians , and various subspecies. In stories set in the 19th Century it was revealed that the Chinese name for this species is Tu Lung ("Earth Dragon").</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Tremors (1990)</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Brent Maddock</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Ron Underwood</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>S.S. Wilson</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#World_War_Cthulhu:_The_Darkest_Hour">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>World War Cthulhu: The Darkest Hour</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Various English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Cubicle 7 Entertainment Release Date January 2013 213 is a setting supplement for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game. Written by a team of designers, it runs to a total of 213 , and was published by third-party content provider Cubicle 7 Entertainment in January 2013; both hardcover and digital editions were released. Overview External Links Review of at RPG.net</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>213</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>January 2013</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Herbert_West_--_Reanimator_%28DART_production%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleAudioAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Herbert West -- Reanimator (DART production)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Herbert west--reanimator is an audio production created by the h. p. lovecraft historical society as part of their dark adventure radio theatre series . As is the norm with dark adventure productions, a set of thematic props is available to complement the ambience .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Herbert_West--Reanimator"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Xol">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Xol</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Will of the thousands is one of the five worm gods in destiny series of computer games . He was worshipped by the high priest nokris prior to his death and defection . He helped orchestrate the rise of oryx, the taken king, savathûn, the witch-queen, and xivu arath, god of war .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Archons_%28story%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Archons (story)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Archons Cody Goodfellow English Appears in Rapture of the Deep Hippocampus Press Publication date April 29, 2016 "Archons" is a short story by Cody Goodfellow that first appeared in Rapture of the Deep and Other Lovecraftian Tales ( Hippocampus Press , 2016). It has also been translated for the German- collection Tiefenrausch (Festa, 2022). Overview Goodfellow uses the Iraq War as the setting for a Cthulhu Mythos story that examines the roots of human violence. The plot involves a secret war within the war, between a for-profit private military operation whose roots go back to the Crusades and a sect of snake worshippers who are "older than the dinosaurs." The story's protagonist, a combat-hardened Marine, is recruited by the former and gradually comes to identify with the latter.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cody_Goodfellow"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Rapture_of_the_Deep"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2016 April 29</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#To_Mars_and_Providence">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crossovers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>To Mars and Providence</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story is set in 1898, at the beginning of the martian invasion of earth . It was written by don webb and published in the 1996 anthology war of the worlds: global dispatches . It serves as a crossover between h.p. Lovecraft 's cthulhu mythos and h.g. Wells 's war of the worlds .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Don_Webb"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>War of the Worlds: Global Dispatches</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>June 1996</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fungi_from_Yuggoth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Fungi from Yuggoth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"medusa's coil" is a sonnet sequence by supernatural horror writer h. p. lovecraft . It concerns a person who obtains an ancient book of esoteric knowledge that allows one to travel to other planets and strange parts of the universe . The title is a term for the mi-go , an alien race the narrator encounters, which are fungoid beings resembling crustaceans .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>27 December 1929 – 4 January 1930</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Marginalia">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AugustDerlethWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Marginalia</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>X, 377 pp is a collection of fantasy, horror, science fiction short stories, essays, biography and poetry by and about the american h. p. lovecraft . It was released in 1944 and was the third collection of lovecraft's work published by arkham house . 2,035 copies were printed. The dust-jacket art is a reproduction of virgil finlay's illustration for lovecraft's story " the shunned house .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Fantasy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>biography</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>poetry</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>science fiction short fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>377 pp x</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1944</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brotherhood_of_the_Black_Lotus">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cults"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Organisation"/>
    <rdfs:label>Brotherhood of the Black Lotus</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hastur"/>
    <ex:hasAffiliationO>Tcho-Tcho</ex:hasAffiliationO>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The brotherhood was founded in 6th century bc . They are caretakers of the forbidden sutra and its learned masters have a lineage that goes all the way back to the emerald lama (an avatar of hastur) their goal is to bring about the age of corruption and the end of mankind's dominance on earth .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isLocatedAtO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#China"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Zoey_Samaras">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Zoey Samaras</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Chef</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The chef is a character who appears in several of fantasy flight games' products based on the expanded cthulhu mythos . She first appeared as an investigator for the second edition expansion of lovecraftian adventure board game arkham horror . A plastic miniature of the character was released in 2010 as part of their arkham horror premium figures release, but this is no longer in production .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Innsmouth Horror Deluxe Expansion</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#hasAppearanceC">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label>hasAppearanceC</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Arkham_Alphabet_Book">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Arkham Alphabet Book</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Darrell Schweitzer Allen Koszowski Cover Artist Allen Koszowski English Zadok Allen Release Date 2006 28 : Being a Compilation of Life's Lessons in Rhyme for Squamous Spawn is a chapbook collection of works by American Darrell Schweitzer . It was published by Zadok Allen in 2006 and featured cover and interior artwork by Allen Koszowski . Contents It should be noted that this list of contents is likely incomplete. "" interior artwork by Allen Koszowski "Introduction, to the Parents by Zubulon Whateley III" by Darrell Schweitzer "" by Darrell Schweitzer (variant of "A Dunwich School Primer")</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Darrell_Schweitzer"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>28</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2006</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Henry_S_Whitehead">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSFriendsAndAcquaintances"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Henry S Whitehead</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1932</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Episcopal clergyman and accomplished author of weird and adventure tales for the pulp magazines, many of them set in the Caribbean. Lovecraft corresponded with him and visited him in Dunedin, Florida, in 1931.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1882</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Evil_Things_in_Cold_Midnight">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Evil Things in Cold Midnight</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Matthew Wilson English James Ward Kirk Publishing Lovecraft after Dark date November 2015  " " is a Lovecraftian poem by and poet Matthew Wilson . It was published in the James Ward Kirk Publishing thematic anthology Lovecraft after Dark in November 2015. Synopsis The four four-line verses tell of how a man mates with the dark and destructive creature which killed his wife, and how the two populate the surrounding lands with their evil offspring.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Matthew_Wilson"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_after_Dark"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nhimbaloth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OuterGods"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nhimbaloth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Outer_God"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Empty Death</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The empty death is an outer god created by paizo for the pathfinder roleplaying game in 2016 . Has no form but her passage is thought to be revealed by telltale marks .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>In Search of Sanity</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>James Jacob</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Yamath">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Yamath</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Yama</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as Yama First appearance " The Wizard of Lemuria " Created by Lin Carter , the Lord of the Flames ( EXP : The Wizard of Lemuria , Lin Carter ; " The Pillars of Melkarth ", Richard Tierney ), is the Great Old One worshipped in ancient Lemuria in Patanga , City of the Flame, one of the three primal lords of Lemuria and an aspect of the Triple God of Chaos . References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Wizard_of_Lemuria"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lin_Carter"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Black_Goat_of_the_Woods_%28Arkham_Horror_Expansion%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ArkhamHorror"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Black Goat of the Woods (Arkham Horror Expansion)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The black goat of the woods was the fifth expansion pack for fantasy flight games' second edition of lovecraftian adventure board game arkham horror . It included an additional 90 investigator and 88 ancient one cards to diversify games of arkham horror, and a new herald, the black goat of the woods . The expansion also introduced two new optional mechanics to change how the core game plays, and a difficulty level selector system .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>June 2008</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ivy_Town">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ivy Town</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAccessibilityL>Land</ex:hasAccessibilityL>
    <ex:hasAccessibilityL>aircrat</ex:hasAccessibilityL>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Earth city is a fictional city in the dc universe which first appeared in 1961 in issue #34 of the 1st volume of the showcase comic series . It was established in 1708 by a group that broke away from the protestant town of calvin city . In the late 20th century, ray palmer a brilliant physicist discovered white dwarf star matter which he used to discover how to shrink himself down to a subatomic size .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>Buildings</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>Ivy University</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>roads</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasTypeL>City</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mnagalah"/>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>The Atom</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>humans</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Earth"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>Showcase Vol 1 34</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Becoming">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Becoming</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A young man has the magical ability to "become" the characters he reads about in books . He suffers a gruesome fate when an accident leaves him unable to leave the character he has become . The story was first published in the january 1998 edition of nightscapes .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>January 1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Never_Meet_Again">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Never Meet Again</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Adam Gauntlett Stephanie McAlea Cover Artist Stephanie McAlea United Kingdom English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Stygian Fox Release Date 2021 18 is a short scenario supplement for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu period sub-game Cthulhu Dark Ages . Overview Containing a complete one-off scenario including handouts and maps, was written by Adam Gauntlett, edited by Chitin Proctor , and featured artwork provided by Stephanie McAlea . It was published by third-party content provider Stygian Fox in 2021 and runs to a total of 18 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Adam Gauntlett</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United Kingdom</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>18</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2021</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Unknown_City_in_the_Ocean">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Unknown City in the Ocean</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft English Genre(s) Non-fiction Donald M. Grant To Quebec and the Stars Date of writing 1934 date 1976 " " is a short non-fiction work by the late American Howard Phillips Lovecraft . Although it was written in 1934, it did not see until its inclusion in L. Sprague de Camp 's 1976 collection To Quebec and the Stars . History To Quebec and the Stars (1976) Collected Essays Volume 4: Travel (2006) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>To Quebec</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>the Stars</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1976</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>1934</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Soul_of_the_Devil-Bought">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Soul of the Devil-Bought</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Anton zarnak, his manservant, and a student investigate a pair of men who have recently inherited their uncle's estate . What they find is that hiram stokely, the uncle, has in fact used black magic fuelled by the blood of humans to extend his life . After a fierce fight with a yugg and stokely himself, the three emerge victorious, and burn stokely's mansion to the ground .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_M._Price"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Mystery</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Anton_Zarnak"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Cthulhu Cultus #5</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>May 1996</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Lammas_1986">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu Lammas 1986</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cover Artist Robert H. Knox United States English Crypt of Cthulhu Cryptic Publications Release Date August 1986 72 Preceded by St. John's Eve Followed by Michaelmas 1986 is the 41st issue of long-running fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Running to a total of 72 , it was edited by Robert M. Price , featured cover art by Robert H. Knox, and was published in an A5 format by Cryptic Publications in August 1986 . The issue was a special, its contents devoted entirely to the fiction of the late American author Henry Kuttner , a friend of H. P. Lovecraft 's who wrote numerous short stories set in the Cthulhu Mythos . Contents Editorial Shards by Robert M. Price "The Secret of Kralitz" by Henry Kuttner "The Eater of Souls" by Henry Kuttner "The Salem Horror" by Henry Kuttner "The Jest of Droom-Avista" by Henry Kuttner "The Invaders" by Henry Kuttner "The Bells of Horror" by Henry Kuttner "Hydra" by Henry Kuttner "The Hunt" by Henry Kuttner Trivia Although this issue was the 41st in the , the front cover incorrectly identifies it as being the 42nd . References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>72</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 1986</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Statement_of_Randi_Carter">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleFilmAndTelevisionAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Statement of Randi Carter</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>United states english is a short film based on the h. p. lovecraft story " the statement of randolph carter ". Randi carter gives a statement to police officer thomas f. malone on the reasons behind her lover harley warren 's disappearance .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Statement_of_Randolph_Carter"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>26 August 2012</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
