  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_New_Lovecraft_Circle">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The New Lovecraft Circle</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>First edition was published by fedogan &amp; bremer in 1996, featuring cover art by gahan wilson . In april 2004, a second edition was released by del rey/ballantine, this time with the cover supplied by john jude palencar . It collects work from the second generation of lovecraftian writers, serving as a sequel of sorts to price's tales of the lovecraft mythos .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1996</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Walk_on_the_Weird_Side">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Walk on the Weird Side</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Nick Gucker United States English Genre(s) Horror Fantasy LASC Press Release Date August 2017 Media Type Print 254 is an anthology of all-new work edited by Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. Overview A collection of weird fiction and poetry in the fantasy and horror genres, was released at NecronomiCon 2017 and is also available through their website. It is a non-profit publication, with all proceeds going to NecronomiCon Providence and the Lovecraft Arts &amp; Sciences Council . It was published by LASC Press with cover art by Nick Gucker , and runs to 254 . Contents "Empire Down" by Nadia Bulkin "140 x 76 (A Tour of Griffith Park)" by S. P. Miskowski "A Sound from the Earth" by Kristi DeMeester "The Two-Wheel System" by Matthew M. Bartlett "Haunted Innsmouth" by Ann K. Schwader "Everyone Gathers at Haystack Rock" by Michael Griffin "Eidolon Realty, LLC" by Craig L. Gidney "As Unbreakable as the World" by Farah Rose Smith "The Final Days of Der Zirkus LAvenza" by Peter Rawlik "Daemonolatry" by Ashley Dioses "A Sea of Snow and Frost" by Ashley Dioses "Goodnight Kookaburra" by Daniel Braum "Divine Providence" by Nathan Carson "A Little Delta of Filth" by Jon Padgett "Lambda 580" by Rebecca J. Allred "The Fear Seeker" by Alistair Rennie "Night Gaunts, Too (On reading sonnet XX in H.P. Lovecraft's *Fungi from Yuggoth* cycle)" by Starry Wizdom "The Bannister" by Rhys Hughes "Eouem Chumkpaa" by John Claude Smith "Hollow King" by Ashley Dioses "The Loved One (Being Among the Thousand Forms of Nyarlathotep)" by Michael Wehunt The Loved One (Being Among the Thousand Forms of Nyarlathotep) by Michael Bukowski "The Godchildren" by Anna Tambour "The Anthroparian Integration Technique" by Christopher Slatsky "The Red Gryphon" by Scott Thomas "Stolen" by Lynda E. Rucker "Release" by Tom Lynch "He Opens a Window" by Cody Goodfellow "This Is Love" by Robert Levy "The Night of Maya" by Jayaprakash Satyamurthy "ID" by Philip Fracassi "Tomb-Feasters" by Ann K. Schwader "Impossible to Feign" by Maura McHugh</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Fantasy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>254</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 2017</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Behold,_I_Stand_at_the_Door_and_Knock">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Behold, I Stand at the Door and Knock</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A young man who fancies himself something of an occultist jumps at the chance to buy an edition of the revelations of glaaki from a door-to-door salesman . But once he begins to read, he realises that he has been tricked . He must now become a herald for the dark power or face a dire punishment .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_M._Price"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu Fiction</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhus_Heirs"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 1994</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Black_Brat_of_Dunwich">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dunwich"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Black Brat of Dunwich</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Retells the story of lovecraft's " the dunwich horror " from a different perspective . Paints wilbur whateley in a far more sympathetic light, and henry armitage as the story's villain . Ends with the revelation that armitage had used his knowledge of magic not to destroy the horror, but to allow it to slip beneath the earth to join its kin .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Stanley_C._Sargent"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Cthulhu Codex #10</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>April 1997</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_Beneath_the_Surface_%28Call_of_Cthulhu_LCG%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Horror Beneath the Surface (Call of Cthulhu LCG)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The horror beneath the surface is an expansion pack for fantasy flight games' call of cthulhu: the card game . It contains a total of 60 cards in three sets of 20 unique cards . First released in 2008, it was discontinued with the demise of call of cthulhu: the card game in 2015 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2008</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Meadow_Hill">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>Meadow Hill</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The hill is a landmark in the fictional town of arkham , massachusetts, invented by h. p. lovecraft and mentioned repeatedly in his stories . First mentioned in lovecraft's story " herbert west--re-animator ", written in 1921-22 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL>The Unamable The Colour Out of Space The Dreams in the Witch-House</ex:hasAppearanceL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham"/>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Massachusetts"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Herbert_West--Reanimator"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hnarqu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Hnarqu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Great Old Ones</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Great One</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as The Great One Cthulhu (sibling) Great Old Ones First appearance EXP : " Tentacles " Created by Peter F. Guenther ( The Great One ) a lesser brother of the great Cthulhu manifesting as a titanic mouth surrounded by innumerable tentacles, similar to a great sea anemone. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCharacterMateC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tentacles"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Peter_F._Guenther"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Necronauts">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Necronauts</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Horror comic book date 2000-2001 was serialised in 2000 ad magazine . Features four historical characters: harry houdini, h. p. lovecraft, and sir arthur conan doyle, teaming up against the forces of the great old ones .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Frazer Irving</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Gordon Rennie</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United Kingdom</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>2000 AD</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2000-2001</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Invaders">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Invaders</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Tale tells of horror writer michael hayward who accidentally unleashes monstrosities from a forgotten age upon the earth . First published in the debut issue of strange stories in february 1939, has since been reprinted several times and has also been translated into italian .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Henry_Kuttner"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English br&gt;Italian</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Strange_Stories"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>February 1939</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Transition_of_Juan_Romero">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Transition of Juan Romero</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The white ship is a short story by american horror fiction writer h.p. Lovecraft . The story involves a mine that uncovers a very deep chasm, too deep for any sounding lines to hit bottom . The narrator and one of the mine's workers, a mexican called juan romero, venture inside the mine, drawn against their will by a mysterious rhythmical throbbing in the ground . The night after the discovery of the abyss the</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Marginalia"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#All_Things_Come">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>All Things Come</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here Stanley C. Sargent United States English Publication date August 1997 " " is a very short Cthulhu Mythos story by American Stanley C. Sargent . Synopsis Great Cthulhu awakens from his slumber only to find that the human race have destroyed themselves and reduced the earth to a barren wasteland while he slept. Characters Cthulhu , a Great Old One Publication History "" was first published in the August 1997 edition of Nightscapes , and was later included in the collection of Sargent's work The Taint of Lovecraft by Mythos Books in 2002. Trivia The tag line for "" is " Sometimes it just doesn’t pay to get up in the morning. "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Stanley_C._Sargent"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>August 1997</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Beavis_and_Butt-Head_Read_the_Necronomicon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crossovers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Beavis and Butt-Head Read the Necronomicon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu cultus #6 date 1997is a very short cthulhu mythos story by james ambuehl, writing under the pen name mike jury . Sees mtv's iconic knuckleheads, having somehow come across a copy of the necronomicon, deciding to indulge in a spot of spellcasting .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Mike Jury</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Humour</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Cthulhu Cultus #6</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1997</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Bimini_Islands">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsIncorporatedFromTheRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>Bimini Islands</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAccessibilityL>Air</ex:hasAccessibilityL>
    <ex:hasAccessibilityL>Sea</ex:hasAccessibilityL>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The bimini glyphs are an island chain in the north atlantic east of florida . They feature in stories of the fictional cthulhu mythos . The islands are located near the bahamas in the west indies .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>The Bimini Glyphs</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>The Bimini Road</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>The Bimini Wall</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Island Chain</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>The Bahamas</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>the West Indies</ex:isLocatedAtL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Blemmyes">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ServitorRaces"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SpeciesIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdfs:label>Blemmyes</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Acephali</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Akephalos</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Headless Horsemen</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sociocultural Characteristics Also known as , Akephalos, Headless Horsemen, Acephali Real World Information First appearance EXP : Cthulhu Invictus The are part of Greco-Roman mythology, the names given to them meaning Headless Men . Chaosium first used them in 2009 for the Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game. Background The are children of Y'golonac , whom during the times of the Romans were found in the lands of Kush, Numidia, Egypt, and Africa Proconsularis. It's a nomadic race of warriors, that expanded the cult of Y'golonac and sacrificed those that resisted. Behind the Mythos Similar creatures named Bhlemphroims inhabit the planet Saturn in Clark Ashton Smith 's Hyperborea story " The Door to Saturn ".</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Invictus"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Lurking_Fear_%28DART_production%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleAudioAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Lurking Fear (DART production)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Lurking Fear Based on HPL : " The Lurking Fear " Production Company The H. P. Lovecraft Historical Society Running time 70 minutes (approx.) United States English The Lurking Fear is a Dark Adventure Radio Theatre production by the H. P. Lovecraft Historical Society , an adaptation of the H. P. Lovecraft short story of the same name . Overview As with all DART productions, The Lurking Fear is produced in the style of a 1930s radio drama. Running to a total of 70 minutes, it comes complete with a series of period-authentic thematic props to enhance the immersive experience for the listener. These include: An Albany Evening News article regarding the gruesome Lefferts Corners deaths Arthur Munroe's press card A map of the Catskill Mountains A medical examiner's report on one of the bodies A handwritten letter from Jan Martense to Johnathan Gifford (pre-order exclusive) Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Lurking_Fear"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Annie_Knowby">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Annie Knowby</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Background is a hero in the fictional army of darkness universe . Created by sam raimi, she was created in 1987 by raimi . She is a simulacrum of evil ash who killed her parents .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Evil Dead 2 (movie)</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sam_Raimi"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Casefile:_Arkham-_Nightmare_on_the_Canvas">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Casefile: Arkham- Nightmare on the Canvas</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Her blood runs cold is a graphic novel which acts as a follow-up to h. p. lovecraft 's " pickman's model " written by josh finney , illustrated by patrick mcevoy and edited by kat rocha . It was published by 01publishing in 2016; both softcover and digital download editions were released . Nightmare on the canvas is littered with references to many of lovecraft's works .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Josh_Finney"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>February 2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Advent_of_Uvhash">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Advent of Uvhash</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The advent of uhvash is a short story by american and editor james ambuehl . It was first published in the anthology mythos tales &amp; others in 1996 . It later appeared in midnight shamer #9 in august 1998 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Ambuehl"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1996</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tindalos">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dimensions"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalLocations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tindalos</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAccessibilityL>Magical gateways</ex:hasAccessibilityL>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The hounds of is a fictional dimension in the cthulhu mythos . It was first mentioned by american author frank belknap long in his 1929 short story " the hounds of ."</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dimension"/>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Dholes</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Hounds of Tindalos</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Satyrs</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Extra-dimensional</ex:isLocatedAtL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ygirothian">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ServitorRaces"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>'Ygirothian</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nyarlathotep"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Beast-man</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>'ygirothians are a fictional species created by walter c. debill, jr. for his short story "in 'ygiroth" 'ygirothians are a humanoid species which inhabit the city of 'ygiroth . They hunt with stone spears and wear necklaces made of wolves' teeth .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>In 'Ygiroth</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Walter C. DeBill. Jr.</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Adam_Scott_Glancy">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Adam Scott Glancy</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Games Designer United States Horror is an American traditional games designer, author and former attorney. He is perhaps best known for designing the Delta Green setting for Call of Cthulhu with John Scott Tynes and Dennis Detwiller , although he has written numerous other works set in the Cthulhu Mythos , including gaming supplements and fiction. In 2001 he was appointed president of the Mythos-focussed games publisher Pagan Publishing . Glancy has also been a guest attendee at the H. P. Lovecraft Film Festival every year since 2013 . Selected Bibliography Short Fiction " Once More, from the Top " (2001) Games Delta Green (2016, with Dennis Detwiller and John Scott Tynes ) Gaming Supplements Delta Green (1997, with Dennis Detwiller and John Scott Tynes ) Delta Green: Countdown (1999, with Dennis Detwiller and John Scott Tynes) Final Flight (2008, art director) External Links at Wikipedia at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads at RPGGeek References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games Designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecrafts_Other_Planets">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lovecraft's Other Planets</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>S. T. Joshi United States English Genre(s) Non-fiction Crypt of Cthulhu #4 date April 1982 " " is a non-fiction work by noted H. P. Lovecraft scholar S. T. Joshi . History "" was first published in issue #4 of Lovecraftian fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu in April 1982 . It was later included in the collection of Joshi's own works Selected Papers on Lovecraft by Necronomicon Press in June 1989.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#S._T._Joshi"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Crypt of Cthulhu #4</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>April 1982</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Philip_J._Rahman">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Philip J. Rahman</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>2011 July 23</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Philip john rahman was co-founder of fedogan &amp; bremer . Publishing house won a world fantasy award in 1998 . He was married to lynne holden from 1986 until 2005, and married his second wife in 2006 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Minnesota Wabasha</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasChildrenR>Clark Howard Rahman</ex:hasChildrenR>
    <ex:hasChildrenR>Dana Madden</ex:hasChildrenR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Philip John Rahman</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>American</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasRelativeR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Glenn_Rahman"/>
    <ex:hasSpouseR>Diane Landen (2006)</ex:hasSpouseR>
    <ex:hasSpouseR>Lynne Holden (1986-2005)</ex:hasSpouseR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Publisher</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1952 February 18</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Vibur">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Vibur</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Great_Old_One"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Thing From Beyond</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A 12 ft tall great old one from another plane of existence which has been on earth for a half-dozen millennia . Growing on its fur is a blue fungus-like substance which is dangerous to humans . A third die within 2 weeks while the majority continue to be transformed by the blue fungus as it locks the consciousness in never ending pain .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ye_Booke_of_Monstres_II"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scott_David_Aniolowski"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shout_/_Kill_/_Revel_/_Repeat">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Shout / Kill / Revel / Repeat</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Historywas published in the thematic anthology return of the old ones: apocalyptic lovecraftian horror in 2017 . It was later included in the collection of his own works shout kill revel repeat in 2019 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scott_R._Jones"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Science fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Return of the Old Ones: Apocalyptic Lovecraftian Horror</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_House_of_Haon-Dor">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ClarkAshtonSmithWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HyperboreanCycle"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The House of Haon-Dor</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The sorcerer's apprentices is a story fragment by the late american and poet clark ashton smith . It was apparently intended to form part of his hyperborean cycle of cthulhu mythos -related fiction . It was first published in james ambuehl 's 1998 anthology the sorcerer's apprentices: new tales in the tradition of clark ashton .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>French</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hyperborean_Cycle"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The Sorcerer's Apprentices: New Tales in the Tradition of Clark Ashton Smith</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Fantastic_Poetry">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Fantastic Poetry</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>63 is a collection of works by the late american h.p. Lovecraft . Contains a selection of lovecraft's poems, runs to 63 including an introduction by scholar and critic s.t. Joshi . It was published by necronomicon press in july 1990 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>63</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 1990</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Resurrected">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleFilmAndTelevisionAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Resurrected</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Based on, rather than a direct adaptation of, the strange case of charles dexter ward by h. p. lovecraft . Plot borrows from the lovecraft original, and follows some of the concepts in the original story . Film features a contemporary (1991) private detective character who takes on a mythos related case .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Case_of_Charles_Dexter_Ward"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1991</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Survivor">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AugustDerlethWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Survivor</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>August Derleth Howard Phillips Lovecraft United States English Genre(s) Weird fiction Weird Tales date July 1954 𝐓 "" is a short story by August Derleth based on an idea from H. P. Lovecraft , billed as one of the controversial " posthumous collaborations " between the two writers. It initially appeared in the July 1954 issue of Weird Tales , and then was the title story of a collection of Derleth/Lovecraft "collaborations" released by Arkham House in 1957. Synopsis The story concerns the efforts of antiquarian Alijah Atwood to investigate the history of the Carriere House on Benefit Street in Providence , Rhode Island, which Atwood is renting. The owner of the house, Jean-Francois Charriere , a surgeon who supposedly died in 1927, was born in Bayonne, France, in 1636 after having achieved near-immortality by turning himself into a kind of human/reptile hybrid. External Link "" complete text at Librarium Cthulhuvius</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#August_Derleth"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Howard_Phillips_Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>July 1954</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Book_of_Azathoth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosBooksFictional"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdfs:label>Book of Azathoth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The , or the Black Book , is described as a "book of prodigious size" and is signed in blood by those going to serve the throne of Azathoth at the centre of ultimate chaos. The Black Man seems responsible for persuading humans like Keziah Mason and Walter Gilman to sign. ( HPL : " The Dreams in the Witch-House ")</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nick_Gucker">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nick Gucker</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Pseudonym(s) Nick "The Hat" Gucker Connection to the Mythos Artist Horror 2012 Pickman’s Apprentice Iron Artist https://nickthehatart.com/ is an artist who has illustrated several works for the Cthulhu Mythos inspired by the works of H. P. Lovecraft . He displayed a selection of his Lovecraftian works at the Providence, Rhode Island Art Club during NecronomiCon 2013, and in the same year supplied the artwork for the H. P. Lovecraft Film Festival in Portland. His artwork has also appeared in periodicals including The Lovecraft eZine . Selected Works Cover Art The Doom That Came to Providence (2015) Walk on the Weird Side (2017) Interior Art The Lovecraft eZine (2012) See Also Images by External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Nick The Hat Gucker</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://nickthehatart.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#isSpouseOfR">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#FunctionalProperty"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
