  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mythos_Collector_Winter_2002">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Mythos Collector Winter 2002</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Ken Withrow English Mythos Collector Dark Tree Press Release Date February 2002 52 Preceded by Summer 2001 Followed by Summer 2002 was the second edition of the sporadically-released fanzine Mythos Collector . Edited by Brian Lingard, it featured cover art by Ken Withrow, and was published by Dark Tree Press in February 2002 . Contents First Words by Brian Lingard Interview with Steven Philip Jones by Brian Lingard "The One Below" by David Sakmyster "The Call of Resin" by Shawn Scarber "I Am Nyarlathotep" by P. J. Roberts Lovecraft Comic Price Guide, Part 2 by Brian Lingard Auction Watch Reviews Mythos Gallery (artwork by James R. Cain) Contributor Bios References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>52</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mythos_Collector"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>February 2002</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Threshold_of_Fantasy_1_%28March_1982%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Threshold of Fantasy 1 (March 1982)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Threshold of Fantasy #1 Various Cover Artist Dan Day English Fandom Unlimited Enterprises Release Date March 1982 64 Threshold of Fantasy #1 was the first and only issue of the horror fanzine Threshold of Fantasy . Edited by Randall D. Larson and featuring cover art by Dan Day, it ran to a total of 64 and was published by Fandom Unlimited Enterprises in March 1982. The issue contains a number of Cthulhu Mythos and Lovecraftian works, many which have never been seen in print since. Contents "Joseph Payne Brennan: Old Cities and the Stream of Time" interview of Joseph Payne Brennan by Randall D. Larson "Come, Death" by David C. Smith "Glimpses of Cthulhu" by Neal Wilgus "Lefty Meets Cthulhu" by Neal Wilgus "Cthulhu Blues" by Neal Wilgus "Unreasoned Seasons" by Neal Wilgus "You Remind Me of a Man" by Neal Wilgus "An Invitation" by Neal Wilgus "Cthulhooligans" by Neal Wilgus "The Crack Collector" by Albert J. Manachino "Cafe Berlin" by Diana Thatcher "Now We Offer Roses" by Billy Wolfenbarger "It Lies Beneath the Ocean Floor" by Jim Pianfetti "The Last Day of Illilium" by Frank L. Balazs "The Eyes" by Billy Wolfenbarger " Dr. Dexter " by Llewellyn M. Cabos "The Night They Came" by Jessica Amanda Salmonson "The Bad Man" by Phillip C. Heath</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>64</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>March 1982</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tales_of_Nephren_Ka">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tales of Nephren Ka</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ran Cartwright Cover Artist Steve Lines United States English Rainfall Books Release Date 2006 Media Type Chapbook 37 is a chapbook collection of short Cthulhu Mythos stories by American Ran Cartwright . Overview Centring on Nephren-Ka , one of the many avatars of Nyarlathotep , contains four stories. It was first published by Rainfall Books in 2006 with cover artwork provided by Steve Lines , and runs to a total of 37 . Contents " The Circle " " The Chronicler " " The Coming " " The Candidate "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ran_Cartwright"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>37</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2006</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Caedmon_Records">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Publishers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Caedmon Records</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Parent Company HarperCollins Current Status Active (as Caedmon Audio) 1952 United States New York, United States Spoken word is an American recording label which specialises in spoken-word and other literary productions. It was in New York in 1952 and remains active today under the name of Caedmon Audio. During the 1970s, released three readings of Howard Phillips Lovecraft works, the vocal talents for which were supplied by British actor David McCallum . Selected Releases The Rats in the Walls (1973) The Dunwich Horror (1976) The Haunter of the Dark (1979)</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Christmas_Carnage">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Christmas Carnage</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A mentally-unstable man returns to his home at christmas time to find the ghost of his great aunt carol awaiting him . She tells him that if they are to be together, he must claim a toll in blood, one which will cost many, many lives . When the police break down his door the following morning, they find the man in bed, covered in blood, and embracing the corpse of a woman dead for over a century .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_S._Dorr"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Cthulhu Christmas Special</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Other Lovecraftian Yuletide Tales</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ghostbusters">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Organisation"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OrganisationsOriginatingFromMythosAdjacentWorks"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ghostbusters</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The are a fictional team of paranormal investigators created by dan aykroyd and harold ramis for the 1984 supernatural comedy film . The success of the film led to the creation of sequels, video games, animated series, comic books, and spin-offs .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNotableMemberO>Egon Spengler</ex:hasNotableMemberO>
    <ex:hasNotableMemberO>Peter Venkman</ex:hasNotableMemberO>
    <ex:hasNotableMemberO>Ray Stantz</ex:hasNotableMemberO>
    <ex:hasNotableMemberO>Winston Zeddemore</ex:hasNotableMemberO>
    <ex:hasPurposeO>Capturing ghosts for hire</ex:hasPurposeO>
    <ex:hasTypeO>Company</ex:hasTypeO>
    <ex:isLocatedAtO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#New_York_City"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedBynO>Dan Aykroyd</ex:wasCreatedBynO>
    <ex:wasCreatedBynO>Harold Ramis</ex:wasCreatedBynO>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shantak">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonSapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ServitorRaces"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Shantak</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Shantak-bird</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The shantaks are a fictional species created by h. p. lovecraft in his dreamlands novel the dream-quest of unknown kadath . Lovecraft depicts them as a flying bird-like creature larger than elephants, with heads resembling those of horses . They are allied with nyarlathotep , and fear the night-gaunt minions of nodens .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Distant Worlds</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Pathfinder RPG Bestiary 2 Pathfinder</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dream-Quest_of_Unknown_Kadath"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Tower_of_Dreams">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Tower of Dreams</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mike Adamson English Rogue Planet Press Lovecraftiana Vol 5, Issue 2 date July 2020  " " is a Lovecraftian short story by English-Australian Mike Adamson. It was published in Vol 5, Issue 2 of Lovecraftiana by Rogue Planet Press in July 2020. Synopsis A farmer wakes every night from his dreams covered in sweat. After one particularly horrifying dream where he is told by a trio of terrible beings that his land is doomed, he resolves to see out the summer, make what he can from the harvest, then take his family away in an attempt to avoid the coming apocalypse.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Mike Adamson</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Issue 2</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Lovecraftiana Vol 5</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>July 2020</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Charles_Wain">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsIncorporatedFromTheRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Stars"/>
    <rdfs:label>Charles' Wain</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Northern sky (Ursa Major) Asterism HPL : " Polaris " (or Charles' Wagon ) is an older name for the Big Dipper , a group (asterism) of seven bright stars in the constellation Ursa Major in the northern sky. is mentioned in the story " Polaris " by H. P. Lovecraft , where the unnamed narrator observes the Wagon as it "lumbers up from behind the vapour-soaked swamp trees that sway in the night wind." Polaris, the current northern pole star, can be located by extending an imaginary straight line through Merak and Dubhe, the two stars in the front of the bowl of the Big Dipper, making it useful for celestial navigation.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Polaris"/>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Asterism</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Northern sky (Ursa Major)</ex:isLocatedAtL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dark_Man">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Avatars"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dark Man</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nyarlathotep"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a character created by american author ron shiflet for his cthulhu mythos fiction . Made his first appearance in the short story " the 's assassin ", published in the april 1998 edition of edward p. berglund's online fanzine nightscapes .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dark_Mans_Assassin"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Back_to_the_Black_Bog">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Back to the Black Bog</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The call of poohthulhu was published in the anthology the call of poohthulhu in may 2022 . It was written by american lee clark zumpe and is based on a short story by h.p. Lovecraft .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lee_Clark_Zumpe"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Call_of_Poohthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>May 2022</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Blind_Idiot_Godhead">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Blind Idiot Godhead</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu mythos anthologyis a short story by sebastian greaves . Character is an old man who researches the entities of the cthulhu mythos . After his death, reality undoes his very existence, beginning with his granddaughter</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sebastian_Greaves"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Cthulhu Mythos Anthology</ex:isPublishedAsW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Polaris">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Polaris</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>''is one of lovecraft's most autobiographical stories, reflecting his feelings of guilt, frustration, and uselessness during world war one . Lovecraft remarked on the peculiar similarity of the story's style to that of lord dunsany, whose work he would not read for another year . ''is one of lovecraft's most autobiographical stories, reflecting his feelings of guilt, frustration, and uselessness .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Thousand-and-Third_Tale_of_Scheherazade">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CaitlinRKiernanWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Thousand-and-Third Tale of Scheherazade</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story first appeared in the january 2009 issue of sirenia digest . It was later reprinted in kiernan's collection confessions of a five-chambered heart . The unnamed changeling somewhat resembles soldier, from daughter of hounds .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Caitlin_R._Kiernan"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sirenia_Digest"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>January 2009</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Tsathoggua_Cycle">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Tsathoggua Cycle</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Disputed (see text) English Call of Cthulhu Fiction Genre(s) Horror Chaosium Release Date 2005 220 : Terror Tales of the Toad God is a thematic anthology of Cthulhu Mythos stories released as part of Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu Fiction line of literature. Overview Edited by Robert M. Price and published by Chaosium, is focused on the Great Old One Tsathoggua created by Clark Ashton Smith , runs to a total of 220 and contains 14 short stories, the lion's share of which are by Smith himself. The cover art credit is unclear, as the title page states it to be the work of Harry Fassl , whilst the copyright page assigns the work to Mark Achilles White . Contents " From the Parchments of Pnom " by Clark Ashton Smith " The Seven Geases " by Clark Ashton Smith " The Testament of Athammaus " by Clark Ashton Smith " The Tale of Satampra Zeiros " by Clark Ashton Smith " The Theft of Thirty-Nine Girdles " by Clark Ashton Smith "Shadow of the Sleeping God" by James Ambuehl "The Curse of the Toad" by Loay Hall and Terry Dale "Dark Swamp" by James Anderson " The Old One " by John Glasby "The Oracle of Sadoqua" by Ron Hilger "Horror Show" by Gary Myers " The Tale of Toad Loop " by Stanley C. Sargent "The Crawling Kingdom" by Rod Heather "The Resurrection of Kzadool-Ra" by Henry J. Vester, III References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>220</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu Fiction</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2005</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ash_and_the_League_of_Light">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ash and the League of Light</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mike Raicht Scott Cohn English Dynamite Entertainment Release Date July 2009- April 2010 Media Type Comic book is a 6-issue story arc in issues #22-27 of the 2nd volume of the Army of Darkness . it was written by Mike Raicht in 2009-2010 and published by Dynamite Entertainment. Synopsis Ash travels around the world, recruiting the Protectors of the various continents to help him defeat Hell's Prophet before the Deadites bring about the Apocalypse. Unknown to him, those same Protectors have been informed that he carries Hell's Prophet and to save the world, they will have to kill him.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Mike Raicht</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 2009- April 2010</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Prom_Night,_or_Whateley_Family_Happiness">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Prom Night, or Whateley Family Happiness</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>G. Large English Rogue Planet Press Lovecraftiana Vol 5, Issue 2 date July 2020 " " is a tongue-in-cheek Cthulhu Mythos story by G. Large. It was published in the Lammas Eve edition of the Lovecraftiana zine by Rogue Planet Press in July 2020. Synopsis Told in the style of a frenetic sitcom episode, amidst the chaos of everyday life on the farm, young Eliza Whateley tries to get ready for her mystery prom date while trying to keep her clan quiet so as not to make a bad impression. The hazards include: constantly-growing brother Wilbur desperate to kill his mother Lavinia - a harlot intent on engaging in congress with Yog-Sothoth and his friends; a grandfather slowly losing his sanity and reciting passages from Favourite Sayings from the Necronomicon ; younger brother Little Peaslee, a boy intent on declaring war on the Yithians who have inhabited the bodies of the neighbours; and the antics of H. W. , another brother who spends his days in the basement tinkering with corpses. When finally Eliza's date does arrive, he is revealed to be Bob the Shoggoth , a lecherous creature intent on carnal relations with the girl.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>G. Large</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Issue 2</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Lovecraftiana Vol 5</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>July 2020</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Master_of_the_Crabs">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ClarkAshtonSmithWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Master of the Crabs</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Wtis a short story by clarkashton smith set in his world of zothique . It was originally published in weird tales in 1948 . The story shows what a civilized wizard does to earn a living in a settled area .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Karakal">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Karakal</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Great Ones</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Karakal of Flames</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as of Flames Great Ones First appearance EXP : H. P. Lovecraft's Dreamlands Created by Sandy Petersen , also known as of Flames is one of the Great Ones , the gods of Earth that reside in Kadath . He might also be the same being as Zo-Kalar .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecrafts_Dreamlands"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sandy_Petersen"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Bolton_Worsted_Mills">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Bolton Worsted Mills</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Bolton , massachusetts factories created by the late american author howard phillips lovecraft for his 1922 short story " herbert west--reanimator " the mills were located near arkham (worsted refers to a of cloth) the immigrant workers were described as a rough and tumble bunch, and enjoyed competing in secret boxing matches since the sport was outlawed in bolton .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Factories</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Bolton"/>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Massachusetts"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Herbert_West--Reanimator"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#hasTypeL">
