    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1957</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Rats_in_the_Walls_%28Caedmon_Records%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleAudioAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Rats in the Walls (Caedmon Records)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Rats in the Walls Based on " The Rats in the Walls " David McCallum Production Company Caedmon Records Release date 1973 Running time 55 minutes (approx.) English The Rats in the Walls is an audio adaptation of H. P. Lovecraft 's short story of the same name . Overview Narrated by screen actor David McCallum , The Rats in the Walls runs to a total of 55 minutes. It was released on vinyl LP by Caedmon Records (now Caedmon Audio), a label specialising in spoken word and other literary content, in 1973. External Links The Rats in the Walls , available to listen at the Internet Archive.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Rats_in_the_Walls"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1973</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pegasus_Press">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Publishers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Pegasus Press</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Pegasus spiele verlags - und medienvertriebsgesellschaft mbh was founded in 1993 . The hessian enterprise has established itself not only as a distributor, but also as a publisher of popular games . Today more than 5.000 games and toys are distributed through its commercial partners worldwide .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Pegasus Spiele Verlags</ex:hasFullNameR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Laurence_J._Cornford">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Laurence J. Cornford</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Artist Birth Date 1967 Worcester, England English Horror Speculative History Science Fiction is an author and occasional artist who has written a number of short stories expanding on the Cthulhu Mythos inspired by the works of H. P. Lovecraft . His prose has been published in periodicals such as Cthulhu Codex , Nightscapes , Cthulhu Cultus , Crypt of Cthulhu , The Black Book , and Dark Legacy , as well as anthologies such as Beyond the Mountains of Madness and The Book of Eibon , the latter of which he also contributed interior artwork to. Selected Works Short Stories "The Fear of Liqoimkh" (1997) "The Price of a Book" (1997) " The Doom of Enos Harker " (1997) with Lin Carter " The Worm in the Wood " (1997) " The Return of Rhan-Tegoth " (1998) "The Alkahest: The History of Enoycla the Alchemist" (2002) "The Demon of the Ring" (2002) "The Epistles of Eibon" (2002) with Robert M. Price "The Face from Below: The History of Pnom the Exoercist" (2002) "The Haunting of Uthnor" (2002) "The Offspring of the Tomb" (2002) "The Sphinx of Abormis: The History of the Wizard Hormagor" (2002) "Utressor" (2002) with Clark Ashton Smith and Richard L. Tierney "Gedney" (2015) Interior Art The Book of Eibon (2002) Exterior Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>http://example.org/lovecraft#England Worcester</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>English</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1967</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#D._B._Spitzer">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>D. B. Spitzer</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Subject Podcaster Portland, Oregon, USA United States Horror Weird Fiction https://pgttcm.com/ is an American podcaster whose work focuses on the Cthulhu Mythos of H. P. Lovecraft and other weird fiction. He has also written at least one poem on the subject, and has been a guest speaker over several years at the H. P. Lovecraft Film Festival in his hometown of Portland, Oregon . Selected Bibliography Poems " The Shadow of Azathoth Is Your Galaxy " (2019) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Oregon Portland</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://pgttcm.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Subject Podcaster</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jenova">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Jenova</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Calamity from the skies is an extraterrestrial being which appears feminine but whose gender is ambiguous . 2000 years ago she landed on gaia in a meteor, resulting in a large wound being created in the northern part of the planet . The shinra megacorporation discovered her and with her cells they created super-soldiers . The renegade super soldier sephiroth would come to believe that was his mother and attempt to destroy humanity .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Necronomicon_Ex-Mortis_%28Evil_Dead%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ArtefactsOriginatingFromMythosInspiredWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosBooksFictional"/>
    <rdfs:label>Necronomicon Ex-Mortis (Evil Dead)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesA>Naturom Demonto Book of the Dead</ex:hasAlternativeNamesA>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The necronomicon ex-mortis is a fictional ancient manuscript containing prophecies, funerary rituals, and passages for resurrecting demons . It was created by a race of beings called the "dark ones" as a comprehensive guide to the supernatural for their exclusive use . However, the book eventually fell into human hands and has since been transferred from one owner to another . The book made its first appearance in the 1981 horror-comedy film the</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceA>Evil Dead franchise</ex:hasAppearanceA>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Edlers_Erotic_Corps">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Edler's Erotic Corps</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>David S. Pointer English James Ward Kirk Publishing Lovecraft after Dark date November 2015  " " is a short Lovecraftian poem by American David S. Pointer . It was included in the James Ward Kirk Publishing thematic anthology Lovecraft after Dark in November 2015. Synopsis The twelve lines describe a carnal encounter between two inhuman creatures in a science-fiction setting.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#David_S._Pointer"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_after_Dark"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
