    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Death on the Arkham Express</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The arkham detective is a novella by american and screenwriter byron craft . It was self-published in may 2019; both paperback and digital editions were released .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Byron_Craft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Arkham_Detective"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 2019</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Testament_Of_Cthulhu:_Tales_Of_Weird_Fantasy_%26_Horror">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Testament Of Cthulhu: Tales Of Weird Fantasy &amp; Horror</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mark McLaughlin Michael Sheehan, Jr. English Release Date 2018 63 is a collection of short Cthulhu Mythos stories by Mark McLaughlin and Michael Sheehan, Jr. Overview Containing five stories and running to a total of 63 , was released in 2018 on Amazon's digital Kindle platform. Three of the stories were later included in the 2018 collection Horrors &amp; Abominations: 24 Tales of the Cthulhu Mythos . Contents " The Ambassador from Ulthar " " Sea-Green Gown With Gold Buttons " " The Foul One Lives To Destroy: A Tale Of The Dreamlands " " The Sea-Spider " " The Testament of Cthulhu "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Michael Sheehan. Jr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>63</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2018</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Turua">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Turua</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Father of Swamps</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Bayou Plant God</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as Father of Swamps The Bayou Plant God Current location The swamps of Florida First appearance EXP : Utatti Asfet Created by Owen Guthrie and Toivo Luick is a fictional creature created by Owen Guthrie and Toivo Luick for their 1996 Call of Cthulhu supplement Utatti Asfet . Overview A fungoid entity with tendrils and tentacles that haunts the swamp lands of Florida, bears a certain resemblance to the Great Old One known as the Green God .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isLocatedAtC>The swamps of Florida</ex:isLocatedAtC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Utatti_Asfet"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Owen Guthrie</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Toivo Luick</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Truth_About_Mars">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Truth About Mars</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft English Genre(s) Non-fiction Donald M. Grant To Quebec and the Stars Date of writing 1917 date 1976 " " is a short non-fiction work by the late American Howard Phillips Lovecraft . Although it was written in 1917, it did not see until its inclusion in L. Sprague de Camp 's 1976 collection To Quebec and the Stars . It does not appear to have been republished in the years since . References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>To Quebec</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>the Stars</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1976</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>1917</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Cultus_Issue_7">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Cultus Issue 7</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu Cultus #7 Various Cover Artist Tani Jantsang United States English P. Marsh Release Date February 1997 62 Preceded by Cthulhu Cultus #6 Followed by Cthulhu Cultus #8 Cthulhu Cultus #7 is an issue of sporadic American fanzine Cthulhu Cultus , edited by Tani Jantsang and James Ambuehl and published by P. Marsh in February 1997. Contents "Configuration Mythos" by Kenneth Silver " People of the Monolith " (Part 1 of 7) by Crispin Burnham " The Doom of Enos Harker " by Lin Carter and Laurence J. Cornford "Alive" by Peter A. Worthy "The Acolyte" by Lee Clark "The Temple at Karnak" by Lee Clark "R'lyeh" by Lee Clark "At Night I Play" by Chad Hensley "Nothing Personal" by T. J. Phillips "The War Wake" by D. F. Lewis " The Worm in the Wood " by Laurence J. Cornford "Sha'no'mai" by Mik Clarke "Innsmouth Rules!" by Ron Shiflet "A Collector of Literature" by Craig Sernotti "Ancient One II" by Chad Hensley "The Last Survivor" by Sean Rodgers "The Disappearance of Mordos" by Boyd E. Pearson</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>62</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>February 1997</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Evil_Dead_2:_Revenge_of_Evil_Ed">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Evil Dead 2: Revenge of Evil Ed</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ian Edginton Vincenzo Riccardi, Chris Summers, Drew Johnson, Guy Major English Space Goat Publications Release Date Feb 2017 - May 2017 Media Type Comic book is a 2-issue mini series written by Ian Edginton in 2017 for Dynamite Entertainment. Synopsis Ash Williams is tricked into going to Las Vegas, believing he won the Employee of the Month award. Only to encounter Evil Ed who brought along Kandarian Demons, Rasputin, The Mummy, Dracula , Bin Laden, and Hitler.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Ian Edginton</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>Feb 2017 - May 2017</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Horrified_Press">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SmallPress"/>
    <rdfs:label>Horrified Press</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Current Status Active Horror Science Fiction Fantasy Rogue Planet Press https://horrifiedpress.wordpress.com/ is a small umbrella publisher which operates several labels, all of which operate in the horror, science fictions and fantasy genres. While themselves have produced a small number of Lovecraftian anthologies, this type of release is usually left to one of their incorporated labels, Rogue Planet Press . Selected Publications The Fall of Cthulhu (2015) The Fall of Cthulhu Volume II (2015)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://horrifiedpress.wordpress.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Idea_of_Fear">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Idea of Fear</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Crueltyis a short horror mystery short fiction by the late american c.j. Henderson . Private investigator frank nardi and psychic madame renee conduct an investigation into an old house on behalf of its new owners . Something evil is lurking in its walls .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#C._J._Henderson"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror Mystery Short Fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Detective_Agency"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Leigh_Carr">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Leigh Carr</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Games designer Roleplaying games is a games designer based in Vienna, Austria who has worked on a number of supplements for Chaosium 's seminal horror roleplaying game, Call of Cthulhu . These include the scenario expansions Gateways to Terror and The Lightless Beacon (both 2019, the latter with Call of Cthulhu veteran Lynne Hardy ). Selected Works Gaming Supplements The Haunted Clubhouse (2017) Gateways to Terror (2019, with Jon Hook and Todd Gardiner) The Lightless Beacon (2018, with Lynne Hardy ) Crimson Carnivale (unknown year) External Links at RPGGeek at LinkedIn References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Last_Night_I_Dreamed_of_Cthulhu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Last Night I Dreamed of Cthulhu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>TS S. Fulk English Genre(s) Cthulhu Mythos Rogue Planet Press Lovecraftiana date Unknown " " is a Cthulhu Mythos poem by TS S. Fulk, first published in Gavin Chappell 's Lovecraftiana horror magazine (date unknown, Rogue Planet Press ). In 2023, it was voted by the fanbase on social media platform Facebook to be included in the anthology The Best of Lovecraftiana (2023, Rogue Planet Press) . Synopsis The sixteen lines describe how the unnamed narrator dreamt of the Great Old One Cthulhu sitting on his carven throne, and the subsequent realisation that they would gladly serve the mighty being. History Lovecraftiana (date and issue unknown, Rogue Planet Press ) The Best of Lovecraftiana (2023, Rogue Planet Press) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>TS S. Fulk</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraftiana"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>Unknown</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tales_Out_of_Dunwich">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tales Out of Dunwich</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Philip Fuller United States English Genre(s) Weird fiction Hippocampus Press Release Date October 2005 302 is a thematic Cthulhu Mythos anthology. Overview Edited by Lovecraft scholar and Robert M. Price and published by Hippocampus Press in October 2005, contains ten stories and runs to a total of 302 . As the title might give away, its theme is the backwoods town of Dunwich created by the late American horror H. P. Lovecraft , and contains works from both his contemporaries and more recent writers. Also included is the novel The Thing in the Woods by Margery Williams , a story which some have suggested as an inspiration for Lovecraft's " The Dunwich Horror " . Contents The Thing in the Woods by Margery Williams "The Mark of the Monster" by Jack Williamson "The Thing from Lovers' Lane" by Nancy A. Collins "Acute Spiritual Fear" by Robert M. Price " The Black Brat of Dunwich " by Stanley C. Sargent "The Dunwitch Lodger" by Brian McNaughton " The Doom That Came to Dunwich " by Richard A. Lupoff "The Dunwich Gate" by Don D'Ammassa "The N-Scale Horror" by Gerard E. Giannattasio "Dunwich Dreams, Dunwich Screams" by Eddy C. Bertin References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>302</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 2005</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Tale_of_the_Reanimator">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MediaAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Tale of the Reanimator</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>'is an episode of the children's horror anthology television series are you afraid of the dark? , written by kenny davis and first aired in 2000 . Inspired by h.p. Lovecraft's short story 'herbert west--reanimator'</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2000 April 30</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhus_Minions">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu's Minions</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The unnamed narrator is a hardboiled detective in 1930s arkham . Assigned to solve the case of a series of murders in which victims have their faces ripped off . He discovers the perpetrators are a breed of monstrous creatures known as pilot demons . Their function is to pave the road for the return of their masters, the old ones .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Byron_Craft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>September 2015</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Light_a_Candle,_Curse_the_Darkness">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Light a Candle, Curse the Darkness</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Behind the mythoswas written by paula e. stiles on request by editor brian m. sammons . The story is set in the fictional town of arkham, a major focus of many mythos stories . When disturbed by something he sees, frank nardi makes the elder sign to ward himself against evil .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Paula_R._Stiles"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Detective_Agency"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Tales_November_2008">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Periodicals"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Arkham Tales November 2008</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Ivan Green English Cold Fusion Media Empire Release Date November 2008 99 Followed by Arkham Tales February 2009 was the first issue of Arkham Tales: A Magazine of Weird Fiction , a short-lived quarterly magazine edited by Nathan Shumate . It featured cover art by Ivan Green. Contents Editorial: Genesis by Nathan Shumate "Talking About Chet Baker" by Derek Rutherford " Pickman's Progeny: The Horrid Truth Below " by Robert Masterson "The House on the Hill of Stars" by Jeffery Scott Sims "MageRider" by Mike W. Barr "Market Values" by Jenny Schwartz "Animal Rights" by Steve Calvert "Night Frights" by Benjamin W. Olson "Until Death, I Eat" by Scott Bastedo Contributor Notes</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>99</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>November 2008</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#My_Friend_Fishfinger_by_Daisy,_Age_7">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>My Friend Fishfinger by Daisy, Age 7</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>David Tallerman English Genre(s) Weird fiction Andromeda Spaceways Inflight Magazine #30 date August 2007 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos flash fiction piece by English David Tallerman. It made its first appearance in issue #30 of Andromeda Spaceways Inflight Magazine in August 2007 . Synopsis Written from the perspective of a 7-year-old girl, the story tells of Daisy's friendship with a new girl at her school named Samantha, who the other girls call Fishfinger. Samantha and her parents, who have recently moved from Innsmouth , want Daisy to go on holiday with them to their home town, where they have promised to introduce her to someone called Dagon … History Andromeda Spaceways Inflight Magazine #30 (2007) Whispers from the Abyss (2013) The Sign in the Moonlight and Other Stories (2016) Cast of Wonders #262 (2017) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>David Tallerman</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Andromeda Spaceways Inflight Magazine #30</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>August 2007</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Causality_Revelation">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Causality Revelation</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Glynn Owen Barrass English Genre(s) Horror Dark Regions Press Return of the Old Ones: Apocalyptic Lovecraftian Horror date 2017 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos story by British Glynn Owen Barrass . Synopsis A computer programmer, acting under the influence of the minions of Cthulhu alongside several thousand of his online contemporaries, creates a memetic virus which infects a significant portion of humankind, in turn causing the return of the Old Ones, widespread madness, and the fall of humankind . Characters Lloyd , a gifted computer specialist. Sarah / The Woman , Lloyd's girlfriend, in reality an otherworldly creature which has infiltrated his life in order to direct his actions. History "" was published in the Brian M. Sammons -edited anthology Return of the Old Ones: Apocalyptic Lovecraftian Horror in 2017.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Glynn_Owen_Barrass"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Return of the Old Ones: Apocalyptic Lovecraftian Horror</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Black_Man">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Avatars"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Black Man</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Avatar of Nyarlathotep</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The main mission of the is to recruit acolytes by having them sign the book of azathoth with their blood . To earn acolytes, this nyarlathotep avatar enlists the help of the witch keziah mason and brown jenkin .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Man_of_Stone"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dreams_in_the_Witch-House"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#I_Married_a_Shoggoth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>I Married a Shoggoth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Jeffrey Thomas English German Necronomicon Press Midnight Shambler date April 1997 is a short Cthulhu Mythos story by Jeffrey Thomas , first published in Midnight Shambler No.5 in April 1997. Synopsis Despite its apparently humorous title it is not a parody work and is played straight. A man uses a Shoggoth able to assume a convincing human form as his ...companion. History Midnight Shambler (1997) Unholy Dimensions (2005) Lovecraft's Disciples (2006) Tales of the Cthulhu Mythos: Cthulhu's Creatures (2007) Geschichten aus dem Cthulhu-Mythos (2012, German-) H. P. Lovecrafts Necronomicon (2018)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jeffrey_Thomas"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>German</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Midnight_Shambler"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>April 1997</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Comte_dErlette">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Comte d'Erlette</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Francois-Honore Balfour</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Author of the "infamous" and "ghastly" cultes des goules . His actual name is francois-honore balfour . Inspired by ancestral form of mythos author august derleth 's name . Lovecraft uses the name in two 1935 stories .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Out_of_the_Jar"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Haunter_of_the_Dark"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shadow_Out_of_Time"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Suicide_in_the_Study"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_Bloch"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mary_Lovecraft">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSRelatives"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Mary Lovecraft</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>24 February 1907</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Wife of James Brown, whom she married on 2 March 1854. She gave birth to nine children: James Eliot, George Henry, William Lovecraft, Frederick Joseph, Robert Bell, Henry Donovan, Frank Clifford, Edward Fulton, and Aaron Lovecraft. All but Robert Bell died before seven years of age.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isRelativeToR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1828</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Strident_Caller">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Strident Caller</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Is a short story by american laird barron, first published in the collection of lovecraftian fiction whispers from the abyss vol. 2: the horrors that are &amp; shall be .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Laird_Barron"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Shall Be</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Whispers from the Abyss Vol. 2: The Horrors That Are</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Kandarian_Demon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Kandarian Demon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a fictional demon created by sam raimi in 1981 for the movie, " the evil dead ", though it would not appear in physical form till 1987 in the sequel, evil dead 2 . It is able to turn nearly anything into a deadite, including plants, humans, and inanimate objects .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Physical: Evil Dead 2</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Unseen: The Evil Dead</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sam_Raimi"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Out_of_the_Jar">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Out of the Jar</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A collector of curiosities named james franklin denning acquires an ancient jar sealed with a six-pointed star and decorated with strange symbols . Occultist edward barnes halpin becomes fascinated by the jar, judging from the seal that it must have belonged to the legendary king solomon . From the damaged jar oozes a mass of luminous bluish fluid that forms itself into a tall creature, vaguely humanoid, with an eyeless</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Charles_R._Tanner"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Stirring Science Stories</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>February 1941</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shakespeare_vs._Cthulhu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Shakespeare vs. Cthulhu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Malcolm Barter Kev Crossley Tony Hough Russ Nicholson English Snowbooks Release Date August 2016 416 is an anthology of Lovecraftian fiction edited by Jonathan Green and published by Snowbooks in August 2016. Both paperback and hardcover editions were released. Overview Contents "That Way Madness Lies" introduction by Jonathan Green "Star-Crossed" by Jonathan Oliver "A Madness Most Discreet" by Michael Carroll "Something Rotten" by Adrian Tchaikovsky "Once More Unto the Breach" by C. L. Werner "A Tiger's Heart, A Player's Hide" by Josh Reynolds "What Dreams May Come" by Nimue Brown "The "Iä"s of March" by Andy Lane "The Undiscovered Country" by Ian Edginton "The Suns of York" by Adrian Chamberlin "A Reckoning" by Guy Haley "Exit, Pursued by...?" by James Lovegrove "The King in Yellow Stockings" by Ed Fortune "The Terrors of the Earth" by Pat Kelleher "Exeunt" by John Reppion "Something Wicked This Way Comes" by Graham McNeill "#Tempest" by Jan Siegel</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>416</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Armageddon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CrossroadPress"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Armageddon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The tower of zhaal is the first novel in the cult of cthulhu series by c.t. Phipps . The story follows john henry booth and his companion, mercury takahashi, after they are exiled from their post-apocalyptic city-state new arkham . He survives the destruction of his squad of new arkham rangers only to be branded a monster due to the suspicious nature of his loss . Having nothing</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>C.T. Phipps</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror Modern Fantasy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Father_Michael">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Father Michael</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>South Church Parish</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Unknown, possibly Arkham Pastor South Church Parish Human First appearance Arkham Horror Second Edition Core Set The King in Yellow Ire of the Void Created by Fantasy Flight Games is a character who appears in several of Fantasy Flight Games ' Arkham Horror Files series of Cthulhu Mythos -related games and fiction. He is the pastor at Arkham 's South Church, and works alongside investigator character Sister Mary . Arkham Horror ( EXP ) first appeared as a Blight card in the Arkham Horror Second Edition expansion set The King in Yellow, although he was mentioned on Sister Mary's Investigator card from the Core Set . Ire of the Void ( EXP ) In the novella by Richard Lee Byers , made a cameo appearance when the protagonists, Professors Norman Withers and Claus Schmidt , visited South Church as part of their research.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The King in Yellow Ire of the Void</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Horror_Second_Edition_Core_Set"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fantasy_Flight_Games"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Oriab">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalLocations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>Oriab</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The dreamlands is an extra-dimensional land mass created by h. p. lovecraft . It is a large island located in the southern sea of the dreamlands . The northern side of the island is made up of sheer crags over a valley of lava . The southern side is made up of sheer crags over a valley of caves .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Extra-dimensional land mass</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>The Dreamlands</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dream-Quest_of_Unknown_Kadath"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Phosphorescent_Monster">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Phosphorescent Monster</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Wenelians</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Glowing Giants</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sociocultural Characteristics Also known as Glowing Giants Yundu (unconfirmed) Wenelians Physical Characteristics Average height 30 feet Skin colour Luminous green Real World Information First appearance EXP : H. P. Lovecraft's Dreamlands Created by Phil Frances Phosphorescent Monsters are a species of creature developed for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu Mythos roleplaying game. Description Relatively unintelligent beasts of the Dreamlands , Phosphorescent Monsters are nevertheless dangerous beings which take their name from the eerie green glow which they emit. Standing a full thirty feet tall at the shoulder, they possess wide, gaping mouths which are capable of sucking up smaller creatures and a pair of large bat-like wings which allow them to fly through space. The Phosphorescent Monsters are frequently employed as mounts and working beasts by the piratical Wenelians , and they presumably originated on the same planet, Yundu . Behind the Mythos Phosphorescent Monsters were created by Phil Frances for the Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game supplement H. P. Lovecraft's Dreamlands in 1986.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecrafts_Dreamlands"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Phil Frances</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Foundling">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Foundling</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A recently-bereaved woman travels to a site where an abortive magical ritual once took place . What she finds is a small part of a great old one , severed when the ritual ended . She uses her body as a birthing shell for its new form .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tim_Waggoner"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Nightscapes #3</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>September 1997</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaugnar_Faugn">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Chaugnar Faugn</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Great Old Ones</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_from_the_Hills"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Caug-Narfagn</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Elephant God</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Feeder</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Chaugnar is a vampiric elephant-like humanoid horror with a leech-like mouth on the end of his trunk . When hungry, he moves very quickly for his size and uses his trunk to drain the blood from his victims, hence his nickname, "the feeder" created by frank belknap long (horror from the hills) is a vampiric elephant-like humanoid horror with a leech-like mouth on the end of his trunk</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_in_the_Museum"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#When_Chaugnar_Wakes"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_from_the_Hills"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Frank_Belknap_Long"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Arkham_Sampler_Spring_1949">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AugustDerlethWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Periodicals"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Arkham Sampler Spring 1949</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Ronald Clyne United States English The Arkham Sampler Arkham House Release Date Spring 1949 100 Preceded by Winter 1948 Followed by Summer 1949 was the fifth issue of the short-lived magazine The Arkham Sampler . Edited by H. P. Lovecraft ’s friend and contemporary August Derleth and featuring cover art by Ronald Clyne, it ran to a total of 100 and was published by Arkham House . Contents "The Root of Ampoi" by Clark Ashton Smith "Fragment" by Vincent Starrett ""The Mummy!"" by Everett Bleiler "Sed Non Satiata" by Charles Baudelaire "Lovecraft and the Stars" by E. Hoffmann Price "The Saints of Four-Mile Water" by Leah Bodine Drake "Technical Slip" by John Beynon Harris "The Last American" by J. A. Mitchell "Full Circle" by Vincent Starrett "The Realm of Redonda" by August Derleth ""Gougou"" by P. Schuyler Miller "Characterization in Imaginative Literature" by Jack C. Miske "Jamesian Spectres" by August Derleth "Two Bibliographies" by Everett Bleiler "The Devil and Miss Barker" by Leah Bodine Drake "Cristina" by Joseph L. McNamera "An Arkham Quartet" by Edward Wagenknecht "Messrs. Sturgeon, Williamson &amp; DeCamp" by August Derleth Reviews by Leah Bodine Drake, Joseph L. McNamera, Edward Wagenknecht, August Derleth, Everett Bleiler, and uncredited contributors External Links full issue online at the Internet Archive</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>100</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Arkham_Sampler"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>Spring 1949</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Joe_Czanek">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Joe Czanek</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The terrible old man is a fictional character created by the late american author h. p. lovecraft for his 1921 short story " the terrible old man " . He was a literary representation of the animosity felt by lovecraft towards the non-english immigrants of the time, who he believed were polluting the genetic pool of the united states .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Terrible_Old_Man"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Neothelid">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Neothelid</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shub-Niggurath"/>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Dark Tapestry</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Neothelids are gargantuan creatures with powerful psionic abilities . They can teleport on the battlefield, communicate with others telepathically, move objects telekinetically, and fly without wings . Their minds are strong enough to overwhelm weaker minds, sometimes fatally, and influence those with weak wills .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pathfinder"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>D</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Into the Darklands</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Illithiad</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Wizards of the Coast</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ballylough">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ballylough</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ireland Village Humans First appearance HPL : " The Moon-Bog " Created by H. P. Lovecraft is a fictional village in County Meath, Ireland . It is located near the village of Kilderry and is mentioned in the story " The Moon-Bog " by H. P. Lovecraft . was probably based on the real world Ballinlough, also in Meath.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Village</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Humans</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ireland"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Moon-Bog"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#NGabthoth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>N'Gabthoth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shuma-Gorath"/>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Serpent-Men of Starkesboro</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Sea-Bed Shambler</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Shambler</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Shambler from the Sea</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Frank brunner is a fictional character created by marvel comics . He first appeared in a comic book series called 'marvel premiere #6' he was a great old one or a lesser old one who had been sleeping in a cavern in the ocean near starkesboro for an unknown amount of time . When he woke from his slumber, he headed toward the serpent men of starksboro, to feed on the human sacrifices which they</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Marvel Premiere #6</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Frank Brunner</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Gardner Fox</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Lovecraft_Library_Volume_2:_The_Call_of_Cthulhu_and_Other_Mythos_Tales">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Lovecraft Library Volume 2: The Call of Cthulhu and Other Mythos Tales</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft United States English IDW Publishing Release Date July 3, 2012 232 is a collection of H. P. Lovecraft 's stories, featuring illustrations by Sam Shearon. It was published by IDW Publishing in 2012 as a follow-up to Horror Out of Arkham . Contents Introduction by Robert Weinberg " The Call of Cthulhu " " The Haunter of the Dark " " The Shunned House " " The Diary of Alonzo Typer " " The Festival " The Shadow Over Innsmouth</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>232</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2012 July 3</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Xexanoth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OuterGods"/>
    <rdfs:label>Xexanoth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Outer Gods</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Outer Gods First appearance CIRCLE : " The Chain of Aforgomon " Created by Clark Ashton Smith is an Outer God known for being the bane of the time god Aforgomon , the latter entity might be an avatar of the All-in-One himself, Yog-Sothoth .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Chain_of_Aforgomon"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Devils_Children">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Devil's Children</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>David Conyers David Conley David Witteveen Jesper Myrfors Cover Artist Anson Maddocks English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Pagan Publishing Release Date 1993 is a scenario supplement for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu horror roleplaying game. Written by David Conyers , David Conley, and David Witteveen, it was published by third-party content provider Pagan Publishing in 1993 . Overview Design Team Writers: David Conyers , David Godley, and David Witteveen Artists: Dennis Detwiller , David Godley, Anson Maddocks, and Jesper Myrfors Production Staff: Jeff Barber and John Scott Tynes Gallery References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#David_Conyers"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>David Conley</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>David Witteveen</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1993</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Worlds_of_H._P._Lovecraft:_Beyond_the_Wall_of_Sleep">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleComicBookAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: Beyond the Wall of Sleep</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft (original story) Steven Philip Jones (adaptation) Octavio Cariello United States English The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft Genre(s) Weird fiction Caliber Comics Tome Press (imprint) Release Date 1998 55 Preceded by The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: The Tomb Followed by The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: The Shadow Over Innsmouth is a comic book adaptation of the Howard Phillips Lovecraft short story of the same name . Overview Adapted for the comic book format by Steven Philip Jones and illustrated by Octavio Cariello , Beyond the Wall of Sleep was published by Caliber Comics under their Tome Press imprint in 1998. Running to a total of 55 , it features a full-colour cover with black-and-white interiors. It was later collected in the omnibus H. P. Lovecraft's Worlds: Volume 1 along with the three other stories from the Tome Press run. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Steven_Philip_Jones"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>55</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1998</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Transition_of_Zadok_Allen">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Transition of Zadok Allen</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu cultus #1 is a short story by american and lovecraft scholar robert m. price . Tells the story of how zadok allen was not in fact killed by the deep one s during the events of "the shadow over innsmouth ", but was relieved of his mortal coil and permitted to join them as an immortal being in their sub-aquatic city .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_M._Price"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Cthulhu Cultus #1</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>April 1995</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hypnos_%28short_story%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Hypnos (short story)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Hypnos is a first-person narrative written from the perspective of an unnamed character living in kent and later london, england . The narrator, a sculptor, recounts meeting a mysterious man in a railway station . Soon the narrator is off on a foray with his friend, travelling through a void that he explains is beyond human sensation .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The National Amateur</ex:isPublishedAsW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Arkham_Evil">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Arkham Evil</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Basic role-playing is a scenario supplement for chaosium's horror roleplaying game call of cthulhu . It was first published by theatre of the mind enterprises for the first edition of call of cthulhu in 1983 . The three scenarios consist of: "into the throat of the beast": the group must investigate the mysterious disappearance of miners in pennsylvania .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1983</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Celery_at_the_Threshold">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Celery at the Threshold</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The short story crosses the world of the cthulhu mythos with a. a. milne's series of children's stories featuring winnie-the-pooh . It was published in the anthology the call of poohthulhu by april moon books in may 2022 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#John_Linwood_Grant"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Call_of_Poohthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>May 2022</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Makoto_Yamada">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Makoto Yamada</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Dr. Japan Occultist Human First appearance CIRCLE : " The Black Kiss " Created by Robert Bloch and Henry Kuttner Dr. is a character created by Robert Bloch and Henry Kuttner for their short story " The Black Kiss ". Biography Dr. is an occult investigator, and a friend and associate of Michael Leigh , although he claims that Leigh's knowledge far exceeds his own. Yamada believes that legends about werewolves and other instances of therianthropy are based on historical accounts of sorcerers who could temporarily transfer their minds to the bodies of animals, as doing so is easier than initiating mind-transference with humans . He is described as "a small, slender man," with his "thin face sallow beneath a stiff thatch of gray hair...covered with a fine-spun web of wrinkles." ( CIRCLE : " The Black Kiss ")</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Black_Kiss"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Henry_Kuttner"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_Bloch"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Alala">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Alala</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Herald of S'glhuo</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as Herald of S'glhuo Gulf of S'glhuo First appearance ADJ : " The White People " EXP : " The Plain of Sound " EXP : " Secrets of Kenya " Created by Arthur Machen ( Herald of S'glhuo ) is an entity who is composed of living sound native to the Gulf of S'glhuo . He manifests as a huge, monstrous being. He is served and worshipped by the Denizens of S'glhuo , who are composed of the same substance. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Secrets_of_Kenya"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Plain_of_Sound"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_White_People"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arthur_Machen"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Return_of_Rhan-Tegoth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Return of Rhan-Tegoth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story is a sequel to lovecraft's short story " the horror in the museum ", featuring the monstrous rhan-tegoth . The piece was briefly exhibited in a london museum in the 1920s, but removed due to public outcry . At night, british artist daniel sykes dreams of a mountainous landscape and a dark city .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Laurence_J._Cornford"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nightscapes"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>April 1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Digital_Pinball:_Necronomicon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Digital Pinball: Necronomicon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>KAZe Publication Date 1996 Digital sports Sega Saturn  was a 1996 video game for the now-defunct Sega Saturn games console. Overview As its name indicates, consists of a series of three digital pinball tables which draw their inspiration from the works of H. P. Lovecraft . It was a Japan-exclusive release, although all text and vocal effects were included in English. Trivia Tracks included on the musical score include " Arkham Multiball", " Cult of the Bloody Tongue Multiball", " Dreamlands Multiball", " Keeper of the Silver Gate " and " Moon Beast ". Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1996</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pete_Ivalu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Pete Ivalu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>Circa 2018</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>The Canadian Army Rangers</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>The Department of Extraordinary Phenomena</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sergeant is a character created by troy young for his nyarlathotep cycle series of cthulhu mythos short stories . He is a 6-year veteran in the canadian army rangers, an organisation purposed with maintaining a military presence in the arctic circle . He was of inuk (inuit) descent, a trait which aided him during his deployment in the tundra .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#It_Slumbers_Beneath_the_Ice"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Troy_Young"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Stragglers_from_Carrhae">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Stragglers from Carrhae</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Historywas first published in the dark regions press anthology world war cthulhu . It was included in the collection of darrell schweitzer’s own works awaiting strange gods by fedogan &amp; bremer in 2015 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Darrell_Schweitzer"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#World_War_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>August 2014</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_3_%28Jack_Ryan%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu 3 (Jack Ryan)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Crypt of Cthulhu 3 Jack Ryan United States English Release Date 2023 91 Crypt of Cthulhu 3 is a collection of horror fiction by American Jack Ryan , consisting of five short stories tied together by a framing narrative. Running to a total of 91 , it was independently published in 2023; paperback, hardcover, and digital editions were all released. Overview</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jack_Ryan"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>91</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2023</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Queen_of_Clubs">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Queen of Clubs</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a character in the fictional grimm fairy tales pastiche universe, who first appeared in 2013 in issue #13 of wonderland . The character was created by patrick shand &amp; raven gregory .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Wonderland v1 #13</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Wonderland Vol 1 13</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Patrick Shand</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Raven Gregory</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Doom_That_Came_to_Sarnath">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Doom That Came to Sarnath</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"the statement of randolph carter" is an early short story by h. p. lovecraft . It is written in a mythic/fairy tale style and is associated with his dream cycle . It was first published in the scot , a scottish amateur fiction magazine, in june 1920 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Fantasy short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Codex_November_1996">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Codex November 1996</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Robert H. Knox English Necronomicon Press Release Date November 1996 40 Preceded by August 1996 Followed by Eastertide 1997 was the ninth issue of the Necronomicon Press fanzine Cthulhu Codex . Running to a total of 40 , it was edited by H. P. Lovecraft scholar Robert M. Price and featured cover art by Robert H. Knox. Contents Editorial: From the Scriptorium by Robert M. Price "In the of Earth" by Mark Francis "The Wizard of Leng" by Frederick Stansfield "Family Portraits" by Darrell Schweitzer "Live Bait" by Stanley C. Sargent "Chaos" by Chris Forbes "Mother of Serpents" by Gary Myers "Chaos, My Love" by William Scheinman "A Brush of Mammoth Wings" by Chad Hensley "Of Stonehenge &amp; Star-Myths" by Ann K. Schwader</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>40</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>November 1996</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Hallowmas_1984">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu Hallowmas 1984</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Allen Koszowski United States English Crypt of Cthulhu Cryptic Publications Release Date November 1984 56 Preceded by Michaelmas 1984 Followed by Untold Tales by Clark Ashton Smith is the 26th issue of leading H. P. Lovecraft scholar Robert M. Price 's long-running Lovecraftian fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Edited by Price and featuring cover art by Allen Koszowski , it ran to a total of 56 in an A5 format and was published by Cryptic Publications in November 1984. This issue was a special devoted to the works of the late American and friend of Lovecraft’s, Clark Ashton Smith . Contents Editorial Shards by Robert M. Price "Clark Ashton Smith and his World of Fantasy" by Jean Marigny "The Last Hieroglyphs: Smith's Lost or Unpublished Fiction" by Steve Behrends "The Clark Ashton Smythos" by Will Murray "An Annotated Chronology of Smith's Fiction" by Steve Behrends "The Feaster from the Stars" by Lin Carter and Clark Ashton Smith "CAS and Divers Hands: Ideas of Lovecraft and Others in Smith's Fiction" by Steve Behrends "The Price-Smith Collaborations" by Will Murray "The History of Averoigne?" by Glenn Rahman "Susran" by Lin Carter "Advice to the Lovecraft-Lorn" by Donna Death Review by Marc A. Cerasini Letters by Peter Cannon , Frank Belknap Long , Brian Lumley , Robert Bloch , Carl Budka, Allen Koszowski , Dan Gobbett, Steve Behrends, Jeff Newman, James Ambuehl , David A. Kraklow, Darrell Schweitzer , Ben Indick, and S. T. Joshi</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>56</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>November 1984</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Delta_Green_%28supplement%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Delta Green (supplement)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Delta green is a setting supplement for chaosium's call of cthulhu horror roleplaying game . It was published by third-party content provider pagan publishing in 1997 . The setting involves players taking on the roles of agents of the shadowy government organisation from which it takes its name .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Adam_Scott_Glancy"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dennis_Detwiller"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>John Scott Tynes</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1997</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mu%C3%B1oz">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Muñoz</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>1905 ( reanimated ) 1923 (permanent death)</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Doctor is a fictional character created by the late american author h. p. lovecraft . He made his first appearance in the 1928 short story " cool air " . A spanish physician of "striking intelligence and superior blood and breeding", he calls himself "the bitterest of sworn enemies to death"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cool_Air"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Charles_W.">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSFriendsAndAcquaintances"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Charles W.</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1940</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Longtime amateur journalist, editor of the Tryout, and friend and correspondent of Lovecraft.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1852</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Face">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Face</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A young and beautiful married woman has a nightmare about a hideous and terrifying face . This same face had appeared to her ten years earlier in a recurring nightmare, telling her that it will return for her when she is older . She tells her husband about her strange nightmares .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#E._F._Benson"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>French</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>German</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Hutchinson's Magazine</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>February 1924</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Marvel_Premiere_%28Doctor_Strange%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Marvel Premiere (Doctor Strange)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Marvel Premiere (Doctor Strange) Steve Englehart, Frank Brunner Frank Brunner, Ernie Chan, Crusty Bunkers English Marvel Comics Release Date July, 1972 - March, 1974 Media Type Comic book Marvel Premiere was a 61 issue series that ran from 1972-1981, which from 1972-1974 ran 12 contiguous issues (#3-14) starring Doctor Stephen Strange . Synopsis Doctor Strange defends Earth from Shuma-Gorath , the lord of the Great Old Ones .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Frank Brunner</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Steve Englehart</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 1974 1972 - March</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chariots_of_the_Old_Ones%3F">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Chariots of the Old Ones?</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Chariots of the Old Ones? Robert M. Price Charles Garofalo United States English Genre(s) Non-fiction Crypt of Cthulhu Roodmas 1982 date May 1982 " Chariots of the Old Ones? " is a non-fiction essay by leading H. P. Lovecraft scholar Robert M. Price in partnership with Charles Garofalo . First published in the Roodmas 1982 edition of Price's long-running Lovecraftian fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu , it later appeared in the 1992 Starmont House anthology Black Forbidden Things . References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Charles_Garofalo"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_M._Price"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
