    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2024</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Door_to_Saturn">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ClarkAshtonSmithWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HyperboreanCycle"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Door to Saturn</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The sorcerer eibon is declared a heretic by morghi, the high priest of yhoundeh . He uses a magical artefact gifted to him by zhothaqquah to flee to cykranosh . However, morghi follows him through the portal and the two form an uneasy truce . As they explore cykranosh, they encounter strange creatures and realise there is no way for them to ever return home .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Dutch</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>French</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>German</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Hungarian</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Italian</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Portuguese</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Spanish</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>January 1932</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Aklo_%28band%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ContentCreators"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Aklo (band)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>AKLO Connection to the Mythos Music creators Ambient https://web.archive.org/web/20080303093844/http://www.aklo.net/index.htm AKLO is an experimental music project inspired by the works of H. P. Lovecraft and the Cthulhu Mythos . "The ideology of AKLO is that the unexplored sonic potentialities of the Cthulhu Mythos are as limitless as its literary ones," the band's stated. An allied project, Tillinghast Laboratories, "examines the Lovecraft mythos from a modern perspective, exploring its connection with the dystopian nightmares of our techno-industrial age." It has released the albums Sensitive Document (2009) and DO NOT EXECUTE (2017). Albums Beyond Madness (2005) Lurking Fear 04:56 Rue d'Auseil 04:31 Brain Cylinder 05:36 Swamp Cult 04:14 Exham Priory 04:50 Tillinghast's Notebook 05:22 Yuggoth 05:00 Red Hook Alley 05:08 Nyarlathotep 06:02 R'lyeh 04:53 The Unnamable (2006) The Dee Translation 06:03 Antarctica 06:06 Asenath 05:14 Eryx 05:21 The Tomb 05:43 Yith 05:21 Captain Marsh 06:27 Alhazred's Hymn 04:44 Shadows of Time 05:35 Eulogy for Humanity 05:46 Titan Blue (2013) Starry Wisdom 05:45 Witch House 05:47 Leng 06:02 4Mi-go Surgery 05:03 Festival 06:30 Near Algol 05:10 Nameless City 06:29 Pickman's Studio 05:36 Silver Key 06:24 Azathoth 05:38 Singles "St. John's Private Museum" (22:41) (2018) "Draining the Moon​-​Bog" (15:12) (2018) "Blasted Heath Inquest" (11:12) (2018) "The Wall of Sleep" (21:28) (2018) "He" (21:03) (2019) External Link AKLO at Bandcamp References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://web.archive.org/web/20080303093844/http://www.aklo.net/index.htm</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Music creators</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Of_the_Doom_of_Irem">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Of the Doom of Irem</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here Luis G. Abbadie English Nightscapes #9 date September 1998 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos work by Mexican Luis F. Abbadie. Overview The piece is written as a factual translation of a portion of the Necronomicon (although it is stated to have come from a Latin fragment, itself translated into Spanish and then English). The excerpt concerns the building and subsequent destruction of the city of Irem , first mentioned in the Cthulhu Mythos in the short story " The Nameless City " ( HPL ), but with a number of major differences; for example, Irem is stated to have been built by Shaddad (similar to the Encyclopedia Britannica entry of the builder, a man named Shedad) as opposed to the inhabitants described by Lovecraft . History "" was published in the online Mythos zine Nightscapes in September 1998.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Luis G. Abbadie</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Nightscapes #9</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>September 1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Orocoran">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Orocoran</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Black Ichor</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Orocorans are the most numerous indigenous creatures on aucturn . They reproduce by sharing genetic information with their tongues . Although they cannot speak, they understand the languages spoken in the planet .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Starfinder Roleplaying Game Core Rulebook</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Adam Daigle</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Amanda Hamon Kunz</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Crystal Frasier</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>F. Wesley Schneider</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Isabelle Lee</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>James L. Sutter</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Jason Keeley</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Joe Pasini</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>John Compton</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Jon Keith</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Josh Vogt</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Lyz Liddell</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Mark Moreland</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Owen K.C. Stephens</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Robert G. McCreary</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Steve Kenson</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sarah_Monette">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sarah Monette</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sarah elizabeth monette is a writer of fantasy, horror, and science fiction . She co-wrote three novels in the iskryne series with elizabeth bear . Her novel the goblin emperor won the 2015 locus award for best fantasy novel .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>United States Tennessee Oak Ridge</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Sarah Elizabeth Monette</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Kathleen Addison</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://sarahmonette.com</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1974 November 25</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hydra_%28mythology%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Hydra (mythology)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The hydra of the mythos is a creature inspired by the lernean hydra of greek mythology . It takes the form of a many headed serpent that harbors a bitter hatred for surface dwellers, spitting acid and poisonous venom at unsuspecting travelers . In comics the lernean hydra was a descendant of the elder god set , sired by set's granddaughter and mother of many prominent mythological monstrous figures .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dunwich_Horror"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_United_Amateur">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Periodicals"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The United Amateur</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>William h. greenfield english 1901 was the official publication of the press association . Lovecraft contributed numerous nonfiction pieces to it from 1915 through 1922 . Lovecraft's first published piece of fiction was " a reminiscence of dr. samuel johnson "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#World_War_Cthulhu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>World War Cthulhu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>360 : a collection of lovecraftian war stories is a cthulhu mythos anthology, first published in 2014 . Edited by glynn owen barrass and brian m. sammons, with cover art by vincent chong . Also features 22 interior illustrations by m. wayne miller .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>War</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>360</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 2014</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Color_That_Stole_Christmas">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Color That Stole Christmas</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Into the black: tales of lovecraftian terror was published in may 2019 . Contains no direct references to either lovecraft or the greater cthulhu mythos . Premise is that of an alien lifeform falling to earth and corrupting everything it comes into contact with .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#William_Meikle"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Into_the_Black:_Tales_of_Lovecraftian_Terror"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>May 2019</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Call_of_Lovecraft">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Call of Lovecraft</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Billy Tackett English Evil Jester Press Release Date June 2012 346 is an anthology of Lovecraftian fiction edited by Gregory L. Norris and published by Evil Jester Press in June 2012. Both paperback and digital editions were released. Overview Contents (incomplete) " Cold Print " by Ramsey Campbell "That Place" by Scott T. Goudsward "The Colour of the Deep" by William Meikle "Blood Pine" by Carol MacAllister "The Tentacle" by John F. D. Taff "Magnus the Magnificent" by Roxanne Dent "The Vessel" by Geoffrey James "The Shed" by H. David Blalock "Endless Hunger" by Karen Dent "I Luv RT" by John B. Rosenman "The Winds of Gobekli Tepe" by James Ravan "Legend" by Jacqueline Seewald "The Clearing" by Derek Neville</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>346</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>June 2012</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dystopia_as_Utopia:_Howard_Phillips_Lovecraft_and_the_Unknown_Content_of_American_Horror_Literature">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dystopia as Utopia: Howard Phillips Lovecraft and the Unknown Content of American Horror Literature</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Paul Buhle United States English Genre(s) Non-fiction Ohio University Press H. P. Lovecraft: Four Decades of Criticism date 1980 " Dystopia and Utopia: Howard Phillips Lovecraft and the Unknown Content of American Horror Literature " is a short non-fiction piece by American Paul Buhle. It was published in the S. T. Joshi -edited collection H. P. Lovecraft: Four Decades of Criticism in 1980.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Paul Buhle</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft:_Four_Decades_of_Criticism"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1980</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shadow_by_the_Altar">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Shadow by the Altar</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Short story first published in the spring 1983 edition of dark horizons . Tells of small-time criminal stephen tibbets who is hired to break into the house of wealthy barlow and pour a vial of liquid over a specific symbol . When he arrives to complete his task, he realises that confined within the sigil is a terrifying entity made from living shadow .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#David_A._Riley"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Dark Horizons Spring 1983</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1983</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Canis_Mysterium">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Canis Mysterium</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>32 : a scenario with bite is an adventure supplement for the call of cthulhu horror roleplaying game . It was written by scott haartman and edited by meghan mclean with cover art supplied by colin nitta and interior work by eric york .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Scott Haartman</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>32</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 2013</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Innsmouth_Look_%28Byron_Craft%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Innsmouth Look (Byron Craft)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The innsmouth look is a novella by american and filmmaker byron craft . It was independently published through createspace in september 2016, and features cover art by eric lofgren . The second in his arkham detective , it was independently published through createspace in september 2016 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Byron_Craft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Arkham_Detective"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>September 2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jonathon_Hobart_Stane">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Jonathon Hobart Stane</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>1926</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Stane was a character who appeared in the novella ire of the void by richard lee byers . He was born to an upper-class arkham family sometime in the late nineteenth century and had inherited his parents considerable wealth . When the united states joined the great war in 1917, stane was one of the first in arkham to sign up and spent a final night with his fiancée barbara before being shipped out . Unfortunately for the pair, they were attacked by a hound of tinda</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCharacterMateC>Barbara</ex:hasCharacterMateC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>( EXP ): Ire of the Void</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_Lee_Byers"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ludvig_Prinn">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ludvig Prinn</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>Late 15th early 16th century</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Prinn was captured during the ninth crusade in 1271 and held captive in syria, where he learned much from the sorcerers of that country . He later travelled through libya and egypt, before being burned at the stake in brussels for apparent human sacrifice . Prinn is the author of the occult text de vermis mysteriis , which would go on to appear in a host of mythos stories .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The Shadow Out of Time The Diary of Alonzo Typer The Haunter of the Dark The Shambler from the Stars The Brood of Bubastis Fane of the Black Pharaoh Batman: The Doom That Came to Gotham</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Secret_in_the_Tomb"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_Bloch"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Terror_From_the_Skies">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Terror From the Skies</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>144 is a campaign supplement for chaosium 's call of cthulhu roleplaying game . Contains a complete ten-part campaign set around a covert invasion of the earth by the extraterrestrial shan . Incorporates actual historic events into its storyline to add to its authenticity .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Colin Hart</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>144</ex:hasPageCountW>
