  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#David_Niall_Wilson">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Publishers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>David Niall Wilson</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Author of "of dark things and midnight planes" and "the call of distant shores" cites edgar allan poe , stephen king , and neil gaiman as influences . Founded crossroad press, a publishing house for horror and science fiction .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Illinois Clay County</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>American</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://www.davidniallwilson.com</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1959</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Mandrakes">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AveroigneCycle"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ClarkAshtonSmithWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Mandrakes</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>It tells the story of a married couple, both sorcerers, who make a living selling love-philtres . When he kills her out of jealousy, she returns as a mandrake root and exposes his crime . It first appeared in the february 1933 issue of weird tales .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Fantasy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Averoigne"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>February 1933</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Despised_Pastoral">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Despised Pastoral</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here H. P. Lovecraft United States English Genre(s) Non-fiction The Conservative , 4, No 1 date July 2018 " " is a short non-fiction piece by the late American Howard Phillips Lovecraft . It was first published in The Conservative , 4, No 1 in July 1918 . Synopsis In the "", Lovecraft laments his perceived attitude of poets of the time to look down on the pastoral, a type of poetry which extoll the virtues of a rural lifestyle and the countryside in general. He also expresses his fondness for such works. History (Note: this history is compiled from listings given on The H. P. Lovecraft Archive and the Internet Speculative Fiction Database ) The Conservative , 4, No 1 (1918) Something About Cats and Other Pieces (1949) A Winter Wish and Other Poems (1977) Collected Essays, Volume 2: Literary Criticism (2004) Complete Collection of H. P. Lovecraft (2014) In the Mind of H. P. Lovecraft (2020) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>4</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>No 1</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The Conservative</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>July 2018</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Yugg">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ServitorRaces"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Yugg</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ubb"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Yuggs, which may be extraterrestrial in , resemble enormous white flatworms . They have mouths full of horn-like teeth and a half dozen tentacles which can be up to 2 ft long . They have unusual characteristics, such as the ability to shoot organic darts into humans to transmit genetic information .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Out_of_the_Ages"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lin_Carter"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Deborah_Walker">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Deborah Walker</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Pseudonym(s) Kelda Crich Debs Walker Connection to the Mythos Author United Kingdom http://deborahwalkersbibliography.blogspot.com/p/welcome.html  is a prolific London-based author and poet who has written several stories inspired by the works of Howard Phillips Lovecraft . The majority of her horror output is released under the pen name of Kelda Crich . Selected Bibliography " Baby Rhyme Time: Youngsters Enjoy Initiation at Innsmouth Public Library " (2009) " Stone City, Old as Immeasurable Time " (2013) External Links author profile at The Internet Speculative Fiction Database</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United Kingdom</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Debs Walker</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Kelda Crich</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://deborahwalkersbibliography.blogspot.com/p/welcome.html</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shadows_of_Yog-Sothoth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
