  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fabulous_creature">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SpeciesIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdfs:label>Fabulous creature</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Fabulous creatures consist of one of the three main categories employed by the role-playing game Call of Cthulhu to classify the fictional and legendary species of the setting. Unlike the other two categories ( independent races and servitor races ), fabulous creatures are found almost exclusively in the Dreamlands . However, it should be noted that not every Dreamlands creature belongs to this group. Fabulous creatures Basilisks Buopoths Butterfly-Dragons Carnivorous fish Cloudbeasts Fireworms Goblins Growleywogs Lamp-Efts Magah Birds Manticores Nightriders Primitive Snouters Razortongues Scabfish Shades Slime Molds Sloblubikiks Sluggocs Vooniths Winged Snakes Witch Trees See also Independent race Servitor race</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Exorcism_of_Iagsat">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Exorcism of Iagsat</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. English Publication date 1998 " " is a poem by American-born Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. Synopsis Presented as an instructional, "" details the incantations and inscriptions necessary to banish a being named Iagsat . This includes invoking the power of several Great Old Ones and Outer Gods . Publication History "" was published in Nightscapes in 1998, and again in Chronicles of the Cthulhu Codex in 2000. It later appeared in the Robert M. Price -edited anthology The Book of Eibon in 2002, and in Pulver's own collection SIN &amp; Ashes in 2010.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Joseph S. Pulver. Sr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Weapons_Compendium">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Weapons Compendium</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>John H. Crowe III English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Pagan Publishing Release Date 1993 is a mechanics supplement for Chaosium ’s horror roleplaying game Call of Cthulhu . Written by John H. Crowe III , it was published by third-party content provider Pagan Publishing in 1993 . Overview offers a comprehensive look at various weaponry available to player characters and NPCs in any time period from 1793 to 1993 . Some of the information contained in ("Automatic Weapons for Call of Cthulhu" and "American Shotguns 1860-1940") had been previously included in Pagan Publishing's gaming magazine The Unspeakable Oath (in issue numbers 2 and 4, respectively) . References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#John_H._Crowe_III"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1993</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Joseph_Lovecraft_Jr.">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSRelatives"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Joseph Lovecraft Jr.</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>7 November 1879</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Joseph arrived in Rochester from Devonshire on 24 May 1831. After working as a cooper, he opened a manufacturing company, W &amp;amp; J Lovecraft, with his brother, William. He married his cousin, Elizabeth Lovecraft, on 10 October 1839. He began Joseph Lovecraft &amp;amp; Son, his own barrel manufacturing firm, in 1856, but financial hardships pushed him to move to Indianapolis from 1869 to 1871, when he moved back to Rochester.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isRelativeToR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1811</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nephren-Ka">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Avatars"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nephren-Ka</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Became Avatar of Nyarlathotep</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Nophru-Ka</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Black Pharaoh</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"the black pharaoh" (also called nophru-ka ) is a fictional pharaoh of ancient egypt, invented by h. p. lovecraft . He is depicted as a worshipper of the outer god nyarlathotep . Later versions of the cthulhu mythos presented him as an avatar of nyarlathotep himself .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Haunter_of_the_Dark"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Fane of the Black Pharaoh The Shadow from the Steeple</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Outsider"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#True_Detective">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosAdjacentFilmAndTelevision"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>True Detective</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Production Company HBO Release date 12 January 2014 United States English is an HBO anthology series in the hard-boiled detective genre. Its first season, which aired in 2014, contained references to the King in Yellow stories of Robert W. Chambers . The character of Rustin Cohle, played by Matthew McConaughey, is based in part on the philosophies of Thomas Ligotti .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>12 January 2014</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Carl_T._Ford">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonFictionAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Carl T. Ford</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>13 November 2017</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Editor Birth Date Unknown Death Date 13 November 2017 Lovecraftian horror British Fantasy (? - 13 November 2017) was a British Fantasy Award-winning editor, publisher, and occasional author who specialised in the Lovecraftian . Perhaps best known for being the driving force behind gaming/weird fiction fanzine Dagon , he was also a contributor to Robert M. Price 's long-running fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Ford's short fiction appeared in periodicals such as Tales After Dark , and anthologies including The Fourth Black Book of Horror (2009, Mortbury Press). Selected Bibliography Editor Dagon (1985-1990) Author " Cthaat Aquadingen: A Guide to Further Research " (1987) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database tribute at Chaosium tribute at Wormwoodiana</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>Unknown</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Randolph_Carter">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dreamers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Randolph Carter</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Miskatonic_University"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Randolph is a fictional character in the cthulhu mythos . Created by h. p. lovecraft is a semi-autobiographical character .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The Statement of Randolph Carter (1919) The Unnamable (1923) The Silver Key (1926) The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath (1926-1927) The Case of Charles Dexter Ward (1927) Through the Gates of the Silver Key (1933) Out of the Aeons (1933) The Clock of Dreams (1978) Allan</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>the Sundered Veil (1999)</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Statement_of_Randolph_Carter"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Elysia_%28novel%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#BrianLumleyWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CrossroadPress"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Elysia (novel)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Elysia is a cthulhu mythos novel by brian lumley . It is the final novel in his of titus crow books . In the moons of borea was first published in 1989 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
