  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Arkham_Sampler_Autumn_1948">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AugustDerlethWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Periodicals"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Arkham Sampler Autumn 1948</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Ronald Clyne United States English The Arkham Sampler Arkham House 100 Preceded by Summer 1948 Followed by Winter 1948 was the third issue of the short-lived magazine The Arkham Sampler . Edited by August Derleth , it ran to a a total of 100 and featured cover art by Ronald Clyne. Contents "The Sign" by Lord Dunsany " Providence: Two Gentlemen Meet at Midnight " by August Derleth "A Note on Aubrey Beardsley" by Malcolm Ferguson "Only to One Returned" by Clark Ashton Smith "A Spell Useful Near Water" by Peter Viereck "Nut Bush Farm" by Mrs. J. H. Riddell "The Unknown Land" by Leah Bodine Drake The Dream Quest of Unknown Kadath (Part 4 of 4) by H. P. Lovecraft "Change of Heart" by Robert Bloch "Anterior Life" by Charles Baudelaire "The Machen Collection" by August Derleth "Books of Magical Lore" by August Derleth "John Campbell's Stories" by John Haley "The World Is My Idea" by Robert Bloch "A Cosmic Novel" by Clark Ashton Smith Reviews by August Derleth, John Haley, Robert Bloch, Clark Ashton Smith, and uncredited External Links full issue at the Internet Archive</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>100</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Arkham_Sampler"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Under_the_Mound">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Under the Mound</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The challenge from below is the first chapter in the multi- miniseries . It forms the first chapter in the multi- miniseries the challenge from below . It was first published in lore #3 in december 1995 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_M._Price"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Challenge_from_Below"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Lore #3</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 1995</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#In_the_Haunted_Darkness">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>In the Haunted Darkness</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Behind the mythos is a short story by michael collings set to the backdrop of h.p. Lovecraft's fictional cthulhu mythos . The narrator is plagued by debilitating tinnitus . He manages to project his consciousness outside of his body, travelling through space to the outer fringes of the solar system . There, an unseen - and innately evil - presence tells him that</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Michael_R._Collings"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Short Fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Veerla">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Avatars"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Veerla</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Outer_God"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>V'lera</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The , or v'lera , are a fictional species which feature in the call of cthulhu roleplaying game scenario "dead of night" they are thought of as spirits or as creatures from somewhere outside .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Unveiled"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Keith_Herber"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Night_Ocean">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Night Ocean</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>" in the walls of eryx " with kenneth sterling is a short story first published in the californian vol. 4, no. 3 (winter 1936) it is about a man who spends most of a year at a small coastal town . A series of deaths occurs, disappearances of swimmers including those strong and skilled enough for such deaths to be most unlikely .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Howard_Phillips_Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#R._H._Barlow"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fractal_Yog-Sothoth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Avatars"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Fractal Yog-Sothoth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Yog-Sothoth"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Lord of the Fractal Realm</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is an avatar of the outer god yog-sothoth created by chaosium for their call of cthulhu mythos roleplaying game . It appears as an agglomeration of coruscating rainbow-coloured geometric shapes surrounding a central mass of tentacles . When its bizarre dimension is accessed, the immediately forces its way through the user's now-destroyed computer screen .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ye_Booke_of_Monstres_II"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scott_David_Aniolowski"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hyperborea:_The_Tale_of_Satampra_Zeiros">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ClarkAshtonSmithWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleComicBookAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Hyperborea: The Tale of Satampra Zeiros</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Clark Ashton Smith United States English Media type Graphic Novel Publication date 2004 , is a comic book adaptation of Clark Ashton Smith's short story The Tale of Satampra Zeiros . Released in 2004, it is illustrated by American artist Jason Thompson and includes a map of Hyperborea along with additional artwork.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2004</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ponape">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsIncorporatedFromTheRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ponape</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesL>Pohnpei</ex:hasAlternativeNamesL>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Pohnpei is a real-world island in the pacific ocean . The island has been incorporated into the cthulhu mythos, particularly in the works of august derleth and lin carter . The ruins of the ruined city of nan madol are a unesco world heritage site .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>Ruined city of Nan Madol</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Island</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pacific_Ocean"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shadow_Over_Innsmouth"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shadow_in_the_Attic">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AugustDerlethWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Shadow in the Attic</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story first appeared in the over the edge collection over the edge in 1964 . It involves the narrator, adam duncan, inheriting a 200-year-old house in arkham . The story is unusual in that it features a strong, appealing female character in rhoda prentiss .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#August_Derleth"/>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Over the Edge</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>1964</ex:isPublishedAsW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Last_Rites">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Last Rites</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Two miskatonic university faculty members independently come to the conclusion that the events related by disgraced pabodie expedition leader william dyer were in fact true . As the last of the guardians dies, the men are able to seal a portal leading to the ruins of their former city . But as the ultimate price is paid, one is forced to pay the ultimate price .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Gael Baudino</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The Madness of Cthulhu: Volume 1</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2014</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jormungand">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CharactersIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Jormungand</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Asgardians (Norse Gods)</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Midgard Serpent</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>the World Serpent</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The midgard serpent is a dragon from norse mythology, better known as the world serpent . The marvel adaptation was created in 1966 by stan lee and jack kirby . Making its first appearance in issue #127 of the 1st volume of the thor comic book series .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Thor Vol 1 #127</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Jack Kirby</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Stan Lee</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dreams_and_Fancies_%28collection%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dreams and Fancies (collection)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Dreams and Fancies H. P. Lovecraft Cover Artist Richard Taylor United States English Arkham House Release Date 1962 174 Dreams and Fancies is a collection of the fantastical works of the late American Howard Phillips Lovecraft . Running to a total of 174 , it featured cover art by Richard Taylor and was published in a hardcover format by Arkham House in 1962; it has not been reprinted . Contents Introduction by August Derleth " Dreams and Fancies " " Recapture " " Night-Gaunts " " Memory " " The Statement of Randolph Carter " " Celephais " " The Doom That Came to Sarnath " " Nyarlathotep " " The Evil Clergyman " " The Thing in the Moonlight " with Jack Chapman Miske The Shadow Out of Time Trivia The book's colophon gives the print run length as 2,000 copies; as usual, Lovecraft scholar S. T. Joshi disputes this, giving the number as 2,030 . References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>174</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1962</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#An_American_to_Mother_England">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>An American to Mother England</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here H. P. Lovecraft United States English Poesy No.7 date January 1917 " " is a poem by the late American H. P. Lovecraft , first published in the periodical Poesy No.7 in January 1916 . As the title implies, it is an ode to the of England , a place to which Lovecraft felt a strong cultural and historic connection. History Poesy No.7 (1916) A Winter Wish and Other Poems (1977) Medusa and Other Poems by H. P. Lovecraft (1986) The Ancient Track: The Complete Poetical Works of H. P. Lovecraft (2001, 2013) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Poesy No.7</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>January 1917</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Andrew_Doran_%28series%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#StoryCycles"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Andrew Doran (series)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Andrew Doran Matthew Davenport United States English Release Date 2013-2021 The Andrew Doran series is a Cthulhu Mythos story cycle by American Matthew Davenport , begun with The Statement of Andrew Doran in 2013. Over its run, the series has spawned novels, short stories, and omnibus collections. Premise The series focuses on protagonist Andrew Doran , an archaeologist with knowledge of the occult and a deep seated hatred towards the creatures which periodically invade our world, as he attempts to put a halt to the various incursions of monstrous creatures and evil cults which are thrown his way. Books The Adventures of Andrew Doran: Box Set (2015) Andrew Doran Omnibus: Volume 1 (2021) The Statement of Andrew Doran (2013) Andrew Doran at the Mountains of Madness (2018) Andrew Doran and the Scroll of Nightmares (2021) Miskatonic University: Elder Gods 101 (2022, spin off) "The Early Adventures of Andrew Doran" (2014) "Andrew Doran and the Obsidian Key" (2018) "The Forever Gate" (2021) "Andrew Doran and the Journey to the Serpent Temple" (2021) "Andrew Doran and the Crawling Caves" (2023)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Matthew_Davenport"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2013-2021</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mand_Ybula">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>M'and Ybula</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Undying Leaders</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The are a fictional race created by chaosium in 2013 for the call of cthulhu roleplaying game . They are intelligent and powerful cephalopod humanoids created by the deep ones to protect the imprisoned star spawn . As they age at a far slower rate than any other lifeform on earth, the eldest of them possess great intellect and powerful spells .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Secrets_of_Tibet"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Jason Williams</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Glorious_Return_Of_Herbert_West">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Glorious Return Of Herbert West</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The house of the ocelot &amp; more lovecraftian nightmares date 2019is a short horror story by mark mclaughlin and michael sheehan, jr. it is a direct sequel to " shoggoth apocalypse " by the same authors .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Michael Sheehan. Jr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>More Lovecraftian Nightmares</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The House of the Ocelot</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2019</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Ancient_Track_2">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Ancient Track 2</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Ancient Track #2 Various Cover Artist Allen Koszowski English Release Date October 1999 72 Preceded by October 1998 The Ancient Track #2 was the second and final issue in the short-lived Lovecraftian fanzine The Ancient Track . Running to a total of 72 , it was edited by Melanie E. Fischer and Todd H. C. Fischer . Featuring cover art by Allen Koszowski , it was independently published in October 1999. Contents "Barrows and Stones" short story by Glen Rahman and Richard L. Tierney "Where the Moon Is Always Gibbous" poem by Alan D. Peschke "The Only Crop Visible" poem by William P. Robertson "The Circle of Tsau Hsu" short story by Adrian Kleinbergen and G. W. Thomas "Among the Ghouls" short story by David Barker "A Song of Madness and of Death" short story by Todd H. C. Fischer " Lovecraft's Vision " poem by M. E. F. "The Door" (1999) short story by C. J. Henderson "The Black Box" short story by Melanie E. Fischer "The Last Halloween" short story by Ron Shiflet</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>72</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 1999</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Master_of_the_Monolith">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Master of the Monolith</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>1526</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a large toad-like creature, believed to be a remnant from prehuman eras . It was worshiped by the people of xuthltan (later known as stregoicavar) in the middle ages, it was worshiped by the people of xuthltan, who abducted young women and children to offer as sacrifices to it . When hungary was invaded by the forces of suleiman the magnificent in 1526, the turkish</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Black_Stone"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_E._Howard"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nith">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nith</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ulthar Notary Human First appearance HPL : " The Cats of Ulthar " Created by H. P. Lovecraft is a resident of the town of Ulthar where he works as a notary (a legal professional who witnesses the signing of documents). He was one of the town burgesses who helped pass the law that made it illegal to kill cats . ( HPL : " The Cats of Ulthar ") The plain of is also the name of a location within the blue-litten subterranean realm of K'n-yan . ( HPL : The Mound )</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Cats_of_Ulthar"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Murmurs_of_Evil_%28Call_of_Cthulhu_LCG%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Murmurs of Evil (Call of Cthulhu LCG)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Murmurs of Evil Fantasy Flight Games Publication Date 2010 Living Card Game Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game Murmurs of Evil is an expansion pack for Fantasy Flight Games ' Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game . Overview The second Asylum Pack in The Yuggoth Contract Cycle series of expansions, Murmurs of Evil contains 60 cards in the standard division of three sets of 20 unique cards. First released in 2010, it was discontinued with the demise of Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game . Contents Machete (Support; The Agency) Apprentice Monster Hunter (Character; The Agency) Shrine to Yig , Worship of an Ancient Evil (Support; Cthulhu ) Ravenous Piranhas (Character; Cthulhu) Feathered Serpent (Character; Cthulhu) Obsessive Playwright (Character; Hastur ) Harbinger of Insanity (Character; Hastur) Erin Moirai, Biology Student (Character; Miskatonic University ) Searching the Reserves (Event; Miskatonic University) The Painted Corpse (Conspiracy; Neutral) Tattoo Parlor (Support; Neutral) Crowbar (Support; Neutral) Mi-Go Caretaker (Character; Shub-Niggurath ) Specimen Room (Support; Shub-Niggurath) Mi-Go Brain Cylinder (Support; Shub-Niggurath) Veronica Frost, Elegant but Deadly (Character; Syndicate) Tattoo Artist (Character; Syndicate) Trophy Room (Support; Syndicate) Crazed Shoggoth (Character; Yog-Sothoth) Cursed Skull (Support; Yog-Sothoth )</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2010</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
