    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Miskatonic Missives</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2022</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Aaron_Lovecraft">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSRelatives"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Aaron Lovecraft</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>14 December 1870</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Aaron arrived in Rochester from Devonshire in 1830. He began working as a tailor and in 1841 started working with men’s clotheir B.P. Robinson &amp;amp; Co., eventually becoming a foreman in 1849. He married Althea Veazie in 1842. Aaron was acquainted with the founders of the Western Union Telegraph Co. and was an early investor in that company. In 1861 he purchased B.P. Robinson &amp;amp; Co., renaming the company Lovecraft &amp;amp; Goodrich, after himself and his partner, Frederick Goodrich. In the following years the company prospered and Aaron became one of Rochester’s best respected and admired employers.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isRelativeToR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1817</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brown_Jenkin">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Brown Jenkin</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Keziah_Mason"/>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nyarlathotep"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Keziah mason nyarlathotep is a rat-like creature with a human-like face . He is a familiar to the witch keziah mason . Popular culture has been showcased as a creature in a number of films and television shows .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dreams_in_the_Witch-House"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Eva">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Eva</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Daughter of Dracula</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Daughter of the Dragon</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as daughter of the dragon, daughter of dracula powers and abilities swordsmanship, empathy, super strength, healing, super speed, longevity, stamina, weapon master, super hearing, vampirism, super smell and super sight vampire hunter, monster hunter human/vampire first appearance in army of darkness vol 1 10 . Created by james kuhoric in 2006 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Army_of_Darkness:_Ash_Vs._The_Classic_Monsters"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Army_of_Darkness:_Furious_Road"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Prophecy"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Army of Darkness Vol 1 10</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>James Kuhoric</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Lammas_1989">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu Lammas 1989</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Crypt of Cthulhu’’ Lammas 1989 Various Bruce W. Timm Cover Artist Allen Koszowski United States English Crypt of Cthulhu Cryptic Publications Release Date August 1989 56 Preceded by St. John's Eve 1989 Followed by Michaelmas 1989 is the 66th issue of Robert M. Price 's long-running Lovecraftian fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Edited by Price, it featured cover art by Allen Koszowski with interior work from Bruce W. Timm, ran to a total of 56 , and was published by Cryptic Publications in August 1989. Contents Editorial Shards by Robert M. Price "The Door" by Michael Storm "The Frenzied Farmhouse" by Stefan Grabinski "Lovecraft and the Death of Tragedy" by Donald R. Burleson, Ph.D. "Lovecraft's Best Supernatural Stories" by David E. Schultz "Essential Salts" by John Dorfman ""The Burrowers Beneath" by Fritz Leiber" by Edward P. Berglund "H. P. Lovecraft and the Cabala" by David F. Goodwin "E. R. B. and H. P. L." by William Fulwiler "Henry Akeley" by Marie Lazzari "Alain Resnais' "Providence"" by John Brower "A Conversation with E. Hoffman Price" by the Staff of Fungi Magazine "Why (Not) Deconstruct Lovecraft?" by Donald R. Burleson, Ph.D. Reviews by Stefan Dziemianowicz Letters by Ramsey Campbell , Louis A. Irmo, Stacy Clark, Michael Cisco , and Dennis Lien</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>56</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 1989</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Lies_Dreaming_%28anthology%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Lies Dreaming (anthology)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu Lies Dreaming Various Cover Artist Lee Joyner English Ghostwoods Books Release Date February 2016 388 Cthulhu Lies Dreaming: Twenty-three tales of the Weird and Cosmic is an anthology of Lovecraftian fiction published by the Ghostwoods Books small press. Edited by Salomé Jones , and featuring cover art by Lee Joyner, it runs to a total of 388 and was released in February 2016; both paperback and digital editions were released . Overview Contents Foreword by Ken Hite "Nikukinchaku" by Matthew J. Hockey "Babatunde" by Ayobami Leeman Kessler "The Myth of Proof" by Greg Stolze "Service" by Lynnea Glasser "The Star that is not a Star" by Lucy Brady "August Lokken" by Yma Johnson "Wake My Lord" by M. S. Swift "Puddles" by Thord D. Hedengren "Sometimes, the Void Stares Back" by Marc Reichardt "Beyond the Shore" by Lynn Hardy "Bleak Mathematics" by Brian Fatah Steele "Father of Dread" by Matthew Chabin "He Sees You in His Dreams" by Samuel Morningstar "Isophase Light" by Daniel Marc Chant "Icebound" by Morris Kenyon "Seven Nights in a Sleep Clinic" by Saul Quint "Mykes Reach" by William Couper "Notes for a Life of Nightmares" by Pete Rawlik "Offspring" by Evey Brett "Out on Route 22" by E. Dane Anderson "The Red Brick Building" by Mike Davis "The Lullaby of Erich Zann" by G. K. Lomax "Cymothoa Cthulhii" by Gethin A. Lynes A Quick Note from Ghostwoods Books Contributor Bios Gallery References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>388</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>February 2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Trail_of_the_Loathsome_Slime">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Trail of the Loathsome Slime</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Marcus L. Rowland Brian Williams Cover Artist Brian Williams English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Games Workshop Release Date 1985 16 is a contemporary scenario supplement for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game, written by Marcus L. Rowland and issued by then-small press Games Workshop in 1985. Little more than a booklet, it ran to a total of 16 , and also included player handouts and a handful of new skills for investigators to employ . The base material from which was developed first appeared in Games Workshop's hobby magazine White Dwarf #43 in June 1983 . Trivia The title of the book appears to be a pun playing on the title of the song "The Trail of the Lonesome Pine" which was popularised by Laurel and Hardy in 1937 in the movie Way Out West , or perhaps the 1908 novel by John Fox Jr. which inspired it . External Links Review of at RPG.net Review of at Reviews from R'lyeh References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Marcus_L._Rowland"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>16</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1985</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Strange_Adventures_of_H._P._Lovecraft">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Strange Adventures of H. P. Lovecraft</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mac Carter Tony Salmons English Image Comics Release Date April-Nov 2009 Media Type Comic book The Strange Adventures of H.P. Lovecraft is a 4-issue comic book mini-series written by Mac Carter and Tony Salmons in 2009, which was published by Image Comics. Synopsis An ancient curse turns H. P. Lovecraft 's nightmares into reality, turning him into a god of destruction. He is the only one that can battle the forces of evil that his mind unleashed on the world.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Mac Carter</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>April-Nov 2009</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
