  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Darkhold_Redeemers">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DefunctOrganisationsFictional"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Organisation"/>
    <rdfs:label>Darkhold Redeemers</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Midnight_Sons"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>General Information team Midnight Sons Notable Members Victoria Montesi Real World Information First appearance Ghost Rider Vol 3 28, 1992 Darkhold: Pages from the Book of Sins , Midnight Massacre , Sea Devil , What Dwells Beneath Created by Andy Kubert, Howard Mackie The were a fictional team in the prime reality of the Marvel universe of Marvel Comics . Background The was a short-lived team of humans and mystics led by Victoria Montesi that operated for a few years. It dedicated itself to fighting the Elder God Chthon by stopping others from using the pages of the Darkhold and minimizing the effects once someone used one of the pages. Though the team eventually disbanded after taking major losses, the surviving members continued the fight against the Old Ones, with Victoria Montesi joining the Midnight Sons.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNotableMemberO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Victoria_Montesi"/>
    <ex:hasTypeO>team</ex:hasTypeO>
    <ex:wasCreatedBynO>Andy Kubert</ex:wasCreatedBynO>
    <ex:wasCreatedBynO>Howard Mackie</ex:wasCreatedBynO>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Darkhold">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosBooksFictional"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdfs:label>Darkhold</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The , also known as the shiatra book of the damned, is a fictional grimoire which first appeared in comics in the 4th issue of the 1st volume of marvel spotlight in 1972 . It is said to have predicted the appearance of a natural witch, known as the scarlet witch . Certain pages contain spells for summoning demons and monsters or hold imprisoned demons (e.g. Wish-demon)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnA>Issue 4</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnA>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnA>Marvel Spotlight Vol 1</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnA>
    <ex:wasCreatedByA>Gerry Conway</ex:wasCreatedByA>
    <ex:wasCreatedByA>Mike Ploog</ex:wasCreatedByA>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_House_at_the_End_of_the_Street">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The House at the End of the Street</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The unnamed narrator recalls all of the horrific urban legends associated with an ancient abandoned house . As his mind degrades, he attempts suicide, and as his conscience fades black he wonders if his will become the latest story associated with the terrible old house .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Frank_Forte"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Beyond_Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>September 2020</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#When_Chaugnar_Wakes">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>When Chaugnar Wakes</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Frank Belknap Long United States English Genre(s) Verse Weird Tales date September 1932 𝐓 is a very short Cthulhu Mythos poem by Frank Belknap Long . Synopsis As its name might suggest, concerns the potential events which may occur upon the waking of elephantine Great Old One Chaugnar Faugn , and the devastation he could wreak. History First published in the September 1932 issue of Weird Tales magazine, was later issued in 1978 as a chapbook with a very limited run of only 80 copies.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Frank_Belknap_Long"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Verse</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>September 1932</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Secrets_of_Tibet">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Secrets of Tibet</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Jason Williams Lee Simpson Dean Engleheart Cover Artist Caleb Cleveland United States English Genre(s) Roleplaying game Chaosium Release Date 2013 168 is a regional sourcebook for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game. Overview Containing chapters on the history, culture, religion, and everyday life in Tibet, as well as a unique bestiary and three thematic scenarios to introduce investigators to the , was first published in 2013. It was written by Jason Williams with artwork supplied by Caleb Cleveland , Lee Simpson and Dean Engleheart and runs to a total of 168 . Although physical copies have since been discontinued, it is still available as a digital PDF download. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Jason Williams</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>168</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2013</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Candlemas_1985">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu Candlemas 1985</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Jason C. Eckhardt Cover Artist Stephen Fabian United States English Crypt of Cthulhu Cryptic Publications Release Date February 1985 56 Preceded by Saturnalia and Other Poems by H. P. Lovecraft Followed by Eastertide 1985 is the 29th issue of leading H. P. Lovecraft scholar Robert M. Price 's long-running Lovecraftian fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Edited by Price with a cover by Stephen E. Fabian, it featured interior panels by Jason C. Eckhardt , ran to a total of 56 in an A5 format, and was published by Cryptic Publications in February 1985 . Unusually for earlier editions of Crypt of Cthulhu , aside from the reviews and letters section, this was billed an all-fiction issue, with Eckhardt providing an accompanying image for each story, Contents Editorial Shards by Robert M. Price "The Vault Beneath the Mosque" by Lin Carter "The Gods of Drinen" by Gary Myers "The Waiting Dark" by Eddy C. Bertin " The Wedding of Sheila-Na-Gog " by Glenn Rahman and Richard L. Tierney "Goodbye, Joe" by Duane W. Rimel "I Am Your Shadow" by Clark Ashton Smith Review by Robert M. Price "Truth Is Stranger Than Lovecraft" by Richard Zotara Letters by Ramsey Campbell , John Rectenwald, Richard L. Tierney, Laurence Bush, Steve Behrends , Fritz Leiber , Karl Edward Wagner , Darrell Schweitzer , and Richard A. Zotara References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>56</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>February 1985</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shadows_of_the_Deep">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Shadows of the Deep</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A. Fabrizio English Genre(s) Cthulhu Mythos " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos story which takes its cue from H. P. Lovecraft 's novella The Shadow Over Innsmouth . Written by A. Fabrizio, it was published through Amazon's digital Kindle platform (date unknown). Synopsis In the years following his Uncle Robert 's disappearance, a man begins to develop an affinity with the ocean while undergoing strange physical changes. Slowly retreating from mainstream society, he finds acceptance amongst the local dock workers and the dour folks of Innsmouth . As his body metamorphoses, he eventually takes to the ocean, and assumes his birthright amongst the Deep Ones and reunites with his vanished uncle, now acting as the High Priest of the Esoteric Order of Dagon .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>A. Fabrizio</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Achtung%21_Cthulhu:_The_Secret_War">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Achtung! Cthulhu: The Secret War</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Kevin Wilson Modiphius Entertainment Publication Date N/A Board game is a cooperative board game spun off from the Achtung! Cthulhu horror roleplaying game, designed by Kevin Wilson and featuring artwork by Hennig Ludvigsen Although announced by Modiphius Entertainment in 2016, with online comments by Wilson citing workload as a factor and promising further development that year , and the launching an appeal for online play testers in January 2021 , as of March 2024 the game appears to have remained unreleased. Overview References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>Unknown</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#People_of_the_Dark">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RobertEHowardWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>People of the Dark</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story is the first appearance of the famous character conan . It centers on a love triangle between the narrator, john o'brien, and an englishman, richard brent . The narrator slips and hits his head, giving him a vision of a past life when he was conan of the reavers .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_E._Howard"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Strange Tales of Mystery</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Terror</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>June 1932</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crystal_of_the_Elder_Things">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#TechnologicalArtefacts"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crystal of the Elder Things</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The crystals of the elder things are fictional artefacts in the cthulhu mythos . They were created by chaosium for their call of cthulhu role-playing game . They are described as containers constructed to harness power . Only the elder things have the knowledge and ability to imbue them with magical power, but other creatures may access the power inside .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasFunctionA>Power storage</ex:hasFunctionA>
    <ex:isUsedByA>Elder Things</ex:isUsedByA>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnA rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Call_of_Cthulhu_%28Role-Playing_Game%29"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByA>Elder Things</ex:wasCreatedByA>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lament_for_the_Vanished_Spider">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lament for the Vanished Spider</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft United States English Arkham House Something About Cats and Other Pieces date January 1920 " " is a poem by the late American Howard Phillips Lovecraft , first published in the January 1920 edition of the National Amateur Press Association The Tryout . History (Note: this history is largely compiled from information found at the ""'s entry on the Internet Speculative Fiction Database ) The Tryout (1920) Something About Cats and Other Pieces (1949) Collected Poems (1963) The World of H. P. Lovecraft (1971) Fungi from Yuggoth and Other Poems (1971) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Something_About_Cats"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Other Pieces</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>January 1920</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Lammas_1983">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu Lammas 1983</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Jason C. Eckhardt United States English Crypt of Cthulhu Release Date August 1983 40 Preceded by St. John's Eve 1983 Followed by Michaelmas 1983 is the fifteenth issue of Robert M. Price 's long-running Lovecraftian fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Edited by Price and featuring cover art by Jason C. Eckhardt , it ran to a total of 40 in an A5 format, and was self-published in August 1983. Contents Editorial Shards by Robert M. Price "Some Comments on the Dunsany-Lovecraft Influence" by Donald R. Burleson "The Dream World and the Real World in Lovecraft" by S. T. Joshi LTentacles in Dreamland: Cthulhu Mythos Elements in the Dunsanian Stories" by Will Murray ""The Other Gods" and the Four Who Entered Paradise" by Robert M. Price "Pombo and "The Other Gods"" by Robert Schwartz "On "Polaris"" by S. T. Joshi "The Horror of "Polaris"" by Ralph E. Vaughan "Something About the Cats of Ulthar" by Jason C. Eckhardt "Fun Guys from Yuggoth: What Lovecraft and I Have Done to Each Other" by Peter H. Cannon "Advice to the Lovecraft-Lorn" by Donna Death Reviews by Robert M. Price and Mollie L. Burleson Letters by Ramsey Campbell, Robert Finnan, and David E. Schultz</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>40</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 1983</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Silver_Twilight_Lodge">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Organisation"/>
    <rdfs:label>Silver Twilight Lodge</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a shadowy occult organisation created by sandy petersen for his shadows of yog-sothoth campaign in 1982 . It has since become something of a staple in fantasy flight games ' arkham horror files range of cthulhu mythos -based content . Originally conceived as something of a morally ambiguous cult whose aims were somewhat unclear .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNotableMemberO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Carl_Sanford"/>
    <ex:hasNotableMemberO>Diana Stanley ( Feeders from Within )</ex:hasNotableMemberO>
    <ex:hasNotableMemberO>Phillip Avery ( Feeders from Within )</ex:hasNotableMemberO>
    <ex:hasNotableMemberO>Samuel Nightingale ( Feeders from Within ).</ex:hasNotableMemberO>
    <ex:hasNotableMemberO>the Lodge's high priest</ex:hasNotableMemberO>
    <ex:isLocatedAtO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedBynO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sandy_Petersen"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Swami_Chandraputra">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Swami Chandraputra</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Randolph_Carter"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Alias used by randolph carter after his mind is trapped in an alien body . Appears in h. p. lovecraft's stories " through the gates of the silver key " and " out of the aeons "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Out_of_the_Aeons"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Through_the_Gates_of_the_Silver_Key"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#E._Hoffmann_Price"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Army_of_Darkness/Xena:_Forever..._And_A_Day">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Army of Darkness/Xena: Forever... And A Day</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Scott Lobdell Elliot Fernandez English Dynamite Entertainment Release Date Oct 2016 - March 2017 Media Type Comic book Army of Darkness/Xena: Forever...And A Day is a six-issue comic book mini series written by Scott Lobdell in 2016-2017 which was published by Dynamite Entertainment. Synopsis An ancient ruthless foe shatters Xena 's army, forcing her to summon from the future the only ally who can help her save the world, Ash Williams .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Scott Lobdell</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>Oct 2016 - March 2017</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#William_J._Dowdell">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSFriendsAndAcquaintances"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>William J. Dowdell</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1953</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Amateur journalist who abruptly resigned as president of the National Amateur Press Assocation in late 1922, leading the executive judges to appoint Lovecraft as interim president.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1898</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecrafts_The_Tomb">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleComicBookAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lovecraft's The Tomb</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>J. Scott Vanlester J. Scott Vanlester United States English Lovecraft Comics Release Date October 2023 108 is a comic book-format adaptation of the H. P. Lovecraft short story from which it takes its name by American artist and J. Scott Vanlester . Part of his Lovecraft Comics , it was self-published in October 2023; both paperback and digital editions were released. The artwork is rendered entirely in black and white with a strong leaning towards line drawings and a highly minimalist presentation. Overview</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#J._Scott_Vanlester"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>108</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Lovecraft Comics</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 2023</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Die,_Monster,_Die%21_%281965_film%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleFilmAndTelevisionAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Die, Monster, Die! (1965 film)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Die, monster, die! Is a 1965 horror film based on the plot of h.p. Lovecraft's the colour out of space . A radioactive meteorite is used to mutate plant and animal life, with horrific consequences to his subjects and to his family . The monster attacks steve and susan, but falls from a balcony and bursts into flame when he hits the floor . The film received mixed reviews, with some critics calling it a "textbook example of the</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United Kingdom United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasWriterW>Jerry Sohl</ex:hasWriterW>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Colour_Out_of_Space"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>27 October 1965 (US) 20 February 1966 (UK)</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Rocco_Ignacio">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Rocco Ignacio</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>The Catholic Church</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Father rocco is a character created by american author armand rosamilia for his keyport cthulhu series of mythos stories . Despite the esoteric order of dagon 's attempts to run him out of town and his flock's loss of faith, he himself remains a devout servant of his lord .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Barren"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Armand_Rosamilia"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Barbara_Crampton">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPeople"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Barbara Crampton</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Actress Birth Date 27 December 1958 Levittown, New York, USA United States (born 27 December 1958) is an award-winning American actress and producer who has appeared in a number of movies adapted from the fiction of H. P. Lovecraft ; the majority of these productions were filmed with director Stuart Gordon , screenwriter Dennis Paoli , and fellow screen Mythos regular Jeffrey Combs . Most of her early roles, and a large number of her later ones, are in the horror and science fiction genres, Crampton having made her name in the low-budget movie industry, Selected Filmography Actress Re-Animator (1985) From Beyond (1986) Pulse Pounders (1988) Castle Freak (1995) Sacrifice (2020) Psycho Re-Animated (2021) Suitable Flesh (2023) Producer Castle Freak (2020) Suitable Flesh (2023) Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>New York USA Levittown</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Actress</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>27 December 1958</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Carl_Ferdinand_Strauch">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSFriendsAndAcquaintances"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Carl Ferdinand Strauch</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1989</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Friend of Harry Brobst and correspondent of Lovecraft. He later became a distinguished professor and critic.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1908</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Earth_%28Marvels_Prime_Reality%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsOriginatingFromMythosAdjacentWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Planets"/>
    <rdfs:label>Earth (Marvel's Prime Reality)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The earth is the primary setting in the prime reality (earth-616) within the fictional universe . There have existed eldritch entities since before life began . There are over a hundred nations, multiple faiths, several human sub-species billions of humans .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>multiple faiths</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>over a hundred nations</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>several human sub-species</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Planet</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>billions of humans</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Prime Reality (the mainstream universe)</ex:isLocatedAtL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Tale_of_Nanok_the_Merchant">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Tale of Nanok the Merchant</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Thomas V. Powers English Rogue Planet Press Swords Against Cthulhu II: Hyperborean Nights date January 2017 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos story by Thomas V. Powers . It was published in the Gavin Chappell -edited anthology Swords Against Cthulhu II: Hyperborean Nights , by Rogue Planet Press in 2017. Synopsis Avaricious Hyperborean merchant Nanok, his trusty business partner and swordsman Tochran, and ambitious magician Uhlmhaas gather together an expedition to search for a copy of the fabled Book of Eibon , rumoured to be hidden along with a fabulous stash of treasure. Travelling to a remote island on their quest, the party encounter and defeat a cult devoted to Great Cthulhu , the guardians of the book and its hoard of valuables. Unfortunately for them, during the confrontation the book is destroyed, and they are forced to abandon their stolen treasure on the return voyage when they are attacked by wave upon wave of aggressive fish-men .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Thomas_V._Powers"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Swords_Against_Cthulhu_II:_Hyperborean_Nights"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>January 2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Strange_Eons">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Strange Eons</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>194; 249 is a 1978 novel by robert bloch . It serves as an homage to the cthulhu mythos stories of h.p. Lovecraft . The book has been translated into french, spanish, italian, and german .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_Bloch"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>194 249 (paperback)</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1978 (1979 paperback)</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Die_Xothic-Legenden">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Die Xothic-Legenden</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lin Carter Cover Artist Babbarammdass Germany German H. P. Lovecrafts Bibliothek des Schreckens Festa Release Date October 2004 317 is a German- translation of Lin Carter 's The Xothic Legend Cycle: The Complete Mythos Fiction of Lin Carter . It was published in a hardcover format by Festa in October 2004 as part of their H. P. Lovecrafts Bibliothek des Schreckens . Contents "Xothische Legenden: Das Grauen aus der Zeit" introduction by Robert M. Price (translation of "Xothic Romance: Terrors Out of Time") "Die rote Opfergabe" (translation of " The Red Offering ") "Der Bewohner der Gruft" (translation of " The Dweller in the Tomb ") "Das Ding in der Tiefe" (translation of "The Thing in the Pit") "Aus der Tiefe der Zeit" by Lin Carter (translation of " Out of the Ages ") "Der Schrecken in der Galerie" (translation of " The Horror in the Gallery ") "Der Winfield-Nachlass" (translation of " The Winfield Heritance ") "Vielleicht ein Traum" (translation of " Perchance to Dream ") "Das seltsame Manuskript aus den Wäldern von Vermont" (translation of " Strange Manuscript Found in the Vermont Woods ") "Etwas im Mondlicht" (translation of " Something in the Moonlight ") "Die Fischer von Draußen" (translation of " The Fishers from Outside ") "Hinter der Maske" (translation of " Behind the Mask ") "Die Glocke im Turm" (with H. P. Lovecraft , translation of "The Bell in the Tower")</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lin_Carter"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>Germany</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>317</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>German</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecrafts_Bibliothek_des_Schreckens"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 2004</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Iod">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Iod</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Great Old Ones</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Dimension Prowler</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Hunter of Souls</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Shining Hunter</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Shining Pursuer</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Source</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Torphonios</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Vediovis</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>the Dweller beyond Phlegethon</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>the River of Flame</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as the shining hunter the shining pursuer torphonios vediovis, the dweller beyond phlegethon, the river of flame . Created by the late american author henry kuttner for his short story " the secret of kralitz "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The Invaders The Hunt Malleus Monstrorum</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Secret_of_Kralitz"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Henry_Kuttner"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nightscapes_May_2000">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nightscapes May 2000</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>This is the thirteenth issue of sporadically-released online fanzine nightscapes . It was edited by the late american and lovecraft scholar edward p. berglund . This issue also featured artwork by jason hardy, glenn roberts, joseph morales, jeff remmer, iain k. peake, kenneth waters and dave carson .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 2000</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Reanimators">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Reanimators</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Pete rawlik originally intended to write a novel mixing many aspects of lovecraft's fiction, with herbert west as a central character . However, his devotion to established timelines meant that he was unable to pursue this line of writing . Instead, he created a new character: dr. stuart hartwell . The novel was followed by a sequel of sorts, the lovecraftian mash-up the weird company .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Pete Rawlik</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>254</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>June 2013</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shoggoth_Issue_1_%28June_1992%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Shoggoth Issue 1 (June 1992)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Shoggoth Issue 1 Various Cover Artist Charles Spiteri English Shoggoth Release Date June 1992 84 Followed by Issue 2 Shoggoth Issue 1 was the first issue of Chris A. Masters ' short lived Lovecraftian fanzine Shoggoth . Self-published in June 1992, it ran to a total of 84 in an A5 format, and featured cover art by Charles Spiteri. Contents "The Shadow Out of Space and Time" by David Tansey "On the Rim of the Unknown: A Visit with Frank and Lyda Belknap Long" interview by Leigh Blackmore "The Deal" by Denise Dumars "The Old One" by D. F. Lewis "Cthulhu on Mars" by Todd Mecklem " Blasphemous Tomes: A Guide to Lovecraftiana in the Small Press " by Leigh Blackmore, C. A. Masters , and B. J. Stevens "Shadow from the Past" by Tim Richards "Creature from the Deep Terrorizes Beach in Rio" by Sandra Lee</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>84</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shoggoth"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>June 1992</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hyman_Bradofsky">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSFriendsAndAcquaintances"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Hyman Bradofsky</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>2002</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Amateur journalist, editor of the Californian, and president (1935–1936) and official editor (1936) of the National Amateur Press Association.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1906</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sam_Gafford">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Critics"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonFictionAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sam Gafford</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>20 July 2019</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>American author, editor and occasional artist who specialised in the horror, non-fiction and weird fiction genres . In 1982, a 20-year-old gafford was invited to providence, rhode island by leading h.p. Lovecraft scholar s.t. Joshi . In 2015, he would publish a collection of their works titled providence pals: memories and miscellany .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA http://example.org/lovecraft#Massachusetts Attleboro</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Critic</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1962</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nightscapes_October_1997">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nightscapes October 1997</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The fourth issue of long-running online fanzine nightscapes . Edited by the late american and lovecraft scholar edward p. berglund . Some of the works listed below are included as part of a sampler for the anthology hpl .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 1997</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Steve_Behrends">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scholars"/>
    <rdfs:label>Steve Behrends</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Literary scholar, editor, and occasional author who writes primarily on the fantasy and weird fiction genres . His main focus is the works of h. p. lovecraft 's correspondent and friend, clark ashton smith . Edited the collection clark ashton smith: letters to h.p. Lovecraft (1987)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Scholar</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isKnownForR>Clark Ashton Smith: Letters to H.P. Lovecraft</ex:isKnownForR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1959</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#CaitlinRKiernanWorks">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Between_and_Beyond">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Between and Beyond</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Astronomer at miskatonic university discovers a very special telescope lense apparently created by something not human . Using its vast power of magnification, he sees the multitude of horrors lurking in our universe before committing suicide .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Ricardo Madeira</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Nightscapes #1</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>June 1997</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Signs_of_Carcosa">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Signs of Carcosa</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Corey Konieczka Nikki Valens Fantasy Flight Games Publication Date 2016 Board game Eldritch Horror is an expansion set for the Eldritch Horror Cthulhu Mythos board game. Designed by Corey Konieczka and Nikki Valens and featuring artwork by Cristi Balanescu, it was published by Fantasy Flight Games in 2016 . Overview External Links "Madness Rising" at the Fantasy Flight Games website Gallery References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2016</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Christmas_Day">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Christmas Day</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu christmas special and other lovecraftian yuletide tales was published in the anthology cthulhu christmas special and other lovecraftian yuletide tales in november 2017 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DJ_Tyrer"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Cthulhu Christmas Special</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Other Lovecraftian Yuletide Tales</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Weird_of_Avoosl_Wuthoqquan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ClarkAshtonSmithWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HyperboreanCycle"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Weird of Avoosl Wuthoqquan</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>First printed in weird tales in june 1932, it is the third tale in his hyperborean cycle fantasy . The story features the avaricious money-lender chasing a pair of ill-gotten emeralds that mysteriously rolled away from his possession . Inside this cavern he finds a great trove of precious gems, as well as its monstrous owner .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hyperborean_Cycle"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>June 1932</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lucifer_%28Fall_of_Cthulhu%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dreamers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lucifer (Fall of Cthulhu)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lucifer is a character created by american comic book creator michael alan nelson for the gray man story arc of his fall of cthulhu series . She is a pretty young woman in her late teens or early twenties who lived her early life as an orphan thief on the streets of brazil . Under her mentor's tutelage, she gained a degree of mystical knowledge, including how to use protective wards and travel to the dreamlands .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Fall of Cthulhu Volume 3: The Gray Man</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>issue 11</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Michael_Alan_Nelson"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Isles_of_Nariel">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalLocations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>Isles of Nariel</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Middle Ocean Islands Humans First appearance HPL : " The Doom That Came to Sarnath " Created by H. P. Lovecraft The form a fictional archipelago created by the late American author Howard Phillips Lovecraft for his 1920 short story " The Doom That Came to Sarnath ." Overview A group of islands located in the Middle Ocean in the Dreamlands , peacocks from the islands were considered a delicacy in Sarnath . ( HPL : " The Doom That Came to Sarnath ")</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Islands</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Humans</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Middle_Ocean"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Doom_That_Came_to_Sarnath"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Abholos">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Abholos</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Great Old Ones</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Devourer in the Mist</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as Devourer in the Mist Relatives Tsathoggua (possible brother) Cthulhu (possible parent) Great Old Ones First appearance EXP : " Devourers In The Mist (RPG adventure) " Created by Robin Laws (aka Devourer in the Mist ) is a great grey, festering glob of endless malevolence. According to one account he is a product of Ghisguth and Zstylzhemghi mating , which gave birth to Tsathoggua . This made more of a self-willed placenta that had taken form in-and-of-itself, rather than a brother or cousin of the others. According to another account, is a spawn of Cthulhu , born from his bile and tears. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Devourers In The Mist (RPG adventure)</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Robin Laws</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Kn-yanian">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>K'n-yanian</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Yig"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Old One</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>People of K'n-yan</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>K'n-yanians, also known as the people of k'n-yan, are immortal aliens who live in the mysterious underground city of k'n-yan . They are believed to have descended from the ancient north and south american settlers, having moved to k'n-yan after the sinking of atlantis . Due to their isolation, they lost part of their culture, including their knowledge of magic, but are still great dreamers . They possess great mental powers such as the ability</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Mound"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Caliber_Comics">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Publishers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Caliber Comics</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Current Status Active 1989 Gary Reed United States Wayne County, Michigan https://calibercomics.com/ is an American independent publisher best known for its comic book output, but which also publishes a small amount of written content. Overview Established in 1989 by Gary Reed, is still publishing as of October 2022, despite a lengthy hiatus between 2000 and 2015, and Reed's death in 2016. Caliber's primary interest to this wiki is its Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft series by Steven Philip Jones , in which he has adapted a number of Lovecraft 's stories into the comic book format. Caliber also publishes a certain amount of prose work under the label Caliber Books; these works are usually published in both paperback and digital editions. Selected Publications Collections H. P. Lovecraft's Reanimator Tales (2016) Novels Lovecraftian: The Shipwright Circle (2019, Steven Philip Jones ) The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: Arthur Jermyn (1993) The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: Dagon (1993) The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: The Music of Erich Zann (1993) The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: The Picture in the House (1993) The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: The Statement of Randolph Carter (1996) The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: The Alchemist (1997) The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: The Lurking Fear (1997) The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: The Tomb (1997) The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: Beyond the Wall of Sleep (1998) H. P. Lovecraft's Worlds: Volume 1 (omnibus) H. P. Lovecraft's Worlds: Volume 2 (omnibus)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://calibercomics.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#White_ape">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>White ape</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"white apes" are a species created by h. p. lovecraft for his short story . They are habitually bipedal, have a roughly similar frame as anatomically modern humans . The wife and mate of arthur jermyn's great-great-great grandfather, sir wade jermyn, was a . Their child, sir philip jermyn, went on to have his own family and children .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Facts Concerning the Late Arthur Jermyn</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>His Family</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_%282007_film%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleFilmAndTelevisionAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu (2007 film)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu is a 2007 film loosely inspired by h.p. Lovecraft's the shadow over innsmouth . It is notable for having a gay protagonist, exploring lgbtq themes seriously for the first time in a film with a cult backdrop . It premiered at the seattle film festival on 14 june 2007 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shadow_Over_Innsmouth"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>14 June 2007</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hunter_from_Beyond">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Hunter from Beyond</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dimensional_Shambler"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"the hunters from beyond" are an alien species of interdimensional predators . They feed on the minds of living prey, converting their victims into mindless automatons . Their hunting method consists of using a form of mesmerism to tempt victims into their own realm .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Hunters_from_Beyond"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shoggoths_in_Bloom">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Shoggoths in Bloom</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The shoggoths of the title are the creatures introduced in h. p. lovecraft's at the mountains of madness . The story is set in 1938 in the fictional town of passamaquoddy, maine . It focuses on professor paul harding, a black academic who is studying the little known life-cycle of the shoggoth .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Elizabeth_Bear"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Science Fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Asimov's Science Fiction</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 2008</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Zebulon_Whateley">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Zebulon Whateley</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Zebulon is a fictional character in the cthulhu mythos . First mentioned by h. p. lovecraft in " the dunwich horror " . Later appears in " the shuttered room ", written by august derleth .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dunwich_Horror"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Tree_of_Azathoth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CrossroadPress"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Tree of Azathoth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu armageddon is the third book in the cthulhu mythos series . It was written by c.t. Phipps and was published in 2023 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>CT Phipps</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Action adventure</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Post-apocalypse</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Armageddon"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Joan_C._Stanley">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Joan C. Stanley</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>2016 October 16</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Joan carol stanley was an attorney and the author of ex libris miskatonici . She was diagnosed with rheumatoid arthritis in her early 20s . As a lawyer, she worked as both a public defender and as an assistant us attorney .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>http://example.org/lovecraft#Boston http://example.org/lovecraft#Massachusetts</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Joan Carol Stanley</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:isKnownForR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ex_Libris_Miskatonici"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1945 May 24</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Translations_from_the_Book_of_Yng">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#StoryCycles"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Translations from the Book of Yng</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The individual works "the debt of the summoner,the fleece of yaggar" and "the punishment of igharta" were published in 1973 . Later all the stories were printed in the online zine nightscapes in 1998 . The stories are each presented as a translation from an occult text named the book of yng .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>F. C. Adams</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nightscapes"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>September 1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Gods_of_Earth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Gods of Earth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>This is a fantasy short story by gary myers set in the cthulhu mythos . It is notable for incorporating elements from the woks of lord dunsany . It was published in the 1975 anthology nameless places .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gary_Myers"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>USA</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Fantasy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Nameless Places</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1975</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Under_A_Winters_Snow">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Under A Winter's Snow</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Nathan Ross et al English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Stygian Fox Release Date February 2020 20 is a short scenario supplement for Chaosium 's Mythos roleplaying game Call of Cthulhu . Overview Consisting of a complete one-off scenario set in North Dakota with all of the necessary handouts and maps, also contains a new monster and spell. Running to a total of 20 , it was published by third-party content creator Stygian Fox in February 2020.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Nathan Ross et al</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>20</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>February 2020</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Deep_Down_Under:_Volume_2">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Deep Down Under: Volume 2</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Australia English IFWG Publishing Australia Release Date August 2018 213 Preceded by Cthulhu Deep Down Under: Volume 1 Followed by Cthulhu Deep Down Under: Volume 3 is an anthology of Lovecraftian fiction edited by Steve Proposch , Christopher Sequeira , and Bryce Stevens . Running to a total of 213 , it features interior artwork by Andrew J. McKiernan and David Heinrich, and was published by IFWG Publishing Australia in August 2018; both paperback and digital editions were released. Overview Contents Introduction by Peter Rawlik "Sleeping Dogs" by Kirstyn McDermott "Time and Tide" by Robert Hood "Dead End Town" by Lee Murray "Slither" by Jason Nahrung "The Pit" by Bill Congreve "The Island in the Swamp" by J. Scherpenhuizen "Melbourne Calling" by Silvia Brown "Where the Madmen Meet" by T. S. P. Sweeney "Depth Lurker" by Geoff Brown "The Seamounts of Vaalua Tuva" by David Kuraria Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Australia"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>213</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 2018</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Bloodborne">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Bloodborne</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Is an action role-playing game (rpg) released exclusively for the playstation 4 in march 2015 . The game is a spiritual successor to fromsoftware's dark souls series . Combat is fast-paced and aggressive, encouraging players to take the initiative rather than adopting a defensive playstyle .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecrafts_Cthulhu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleComicBookAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>H. P. Lovecraft's Cthulhu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Terry Collins Paul Davis Robert Lewis Don Heck English Millennium Publications Release Date Dec 1991 - May 1992 Media Type Comic book H.P. Lovecraft's Cthulhu was a comic book series that was published in 1991-1992 by Millennium Publications. It was an adaptation of " The Whisperer in Darkness " by Terry Collins and Paul Davis, that ran for 3 issues and was cancelled before completion.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Paul Davis</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Terry Collins</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>Dec 1991 - May 1992</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sleepers">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Organisation"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sleepers</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The are a fictional organization in the cthulhutech pencil-and-paper roleplaying game by wildfire llc . They are a benevolent organization whose members walk the dreams of men . Once they roamed the dreamlands, but ever since it has been cut off or possibly consumed by a great old one .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_House_of_Cthulhu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#BrianLumleyWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The House of Cthulhu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Dreaded zar-thule the conqueror and his fleet of dragonships set sail towards the island of arlyeh to loot its treasures . They have heard about the island from a priest named voth vehm, who revealed the information under torture . They are surprised to find that there are no guards or even a human population, except for an old man whose skin is covered by some sort of fungus .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Fantasy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Whispers #1</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>July 1973</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Guide_to_Charleston,_South_Carolina">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Guide to Charleston, South Carolina</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Hh H. P. Lovecraft United States English Genre(s) Non-fiction Arkham House Marginalia Date of writing 1936 date 1944 " " is a mid-length non-fiction piece by the late American Howard Phillips Lovecraft . Written in 1936, it did not see until after Lovecraft's death in 1937, making its first, and to date only, appearance in print in the 1944 Arkham Horror collection Marginalia . References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Hh H. P. Lovecraft</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Marginalia"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1944</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>1936</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#UtUlls-HrHer">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ut'Ulls-Hr'Her</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Great Old Ones</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Black Glory of Creation</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Great Horned Mother</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>'s true origins are unknown, although it's been speculated that she has a connection to shub-niggurath , possibly being the spawn or an avatar of the outer god . Others have theorized that she may be the twin sister of glaaki .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nightmares_Disciple"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Joseph S. Pulver</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shade_of_Pierre_Girault">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Shade of Pierre Girault</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The other: encounters with the cthulhu mythos book 3 was published in 2021 . It forms the second episode in book 2 of his nyarlathotep cycle . It is a short story about the forces of the cthulhu mythos . It was written by canadian troy young .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Troy_Young"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Nyarlathotep_Cycle"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Other:_Encounters_with_the_Cthulhu_Mythos_Book_3"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2021</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lusstghaa">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dimensions"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsOriginatingFromMythosInspiredWorks"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lusst'ghaa</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The realm of was once home to a humanoid species who could manipulate essence . The god-king iauv'abrarc sought a way to eternal happiness, which he believed could be achieved through sexual ecstasy . Lauv'abrarc proposed surgeries on all of his people to change their bodies into beings who felt nothing but lust and perpetual orgasm . The god-king performed the change upon himself, but left a fraction of his old</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL>Lust Universe franchise</ex:hasAppearanceL>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>The Chamber of Change The Chamber of Trinity Xu'thrar</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Extra-dimensional plane of existence Hedonistic realm</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>The Changed The Enthralled Vorh'n M'Nag</ex:isInhabitedByL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Esch">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Esch</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Elder Gods of Elysia</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Dchichi is an extraterrestrial avian creature who appears in brian lumley's titus crow series of novels . First appeared in 1975 novel the transition of titus crow .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Transition_of_Titus_Crow"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Seduced">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Seduced</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Historywas first published in mythos-dedicated fanzine al-azif #2 in march 1998 . It has since appeared in the anthologies eldritch blue: love &amp; sex in the cthulhu mythos (2004) and they thrive in darkness (2018) .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Al-Azif</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ernst_Kant">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ernst Kant</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Baron is a fictional character created by brian lumley as a recurring background element in his cthulhu mythos stories . He is first mentioned in lumley's story " the mirror of nitocris " (1971) kant is said to be the father of occultist joachim feery --though feery was "illegitimate"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Mirror_of_Nitocris"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#John_Carpenter">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPeople"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>John Carpenter</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name John Howard Carpenter Connection to the Mythos Film director Birth Date 16 January 1948 Carthage, New York, USA United States https://theofficialjohncarpenter.com/  John Howard Carpenter (born 16 January 1948) is an American film director, many of whose films have developed a strong cult following. Several of his works have intersected the topic of this wiki, from The Thing (1982), the aliens from which were retroactively incorporated into the Cthulhu Mythos by Chaosium for their Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game, to In the Mouth of Madness (1996), which features a theme of insanity very similar to that present in Lovecraft 's works, and derived its name from At the Mountains of Madness . Selected Filmography The Thing (1982) In the Mouth of Madness (1996)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>New York USA Carthage</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>John Howard Carpenter</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://theofficialjohncarpenter.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Film director</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>16 January 1948</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pallid_Dancer">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Pallid Dancer</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hastur"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Courtiers of the King in Yellow</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Masked Ones</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The King's Demons</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Yellow Horrors</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Pallid dancers are miniature humanoid entities that dance and caper like ballerinas . They were created by games designer chad bowser for chaosium's call of cthulhu role-playing game . They made their first appearance in the 2006 monster manual malleus monstrorum .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Malleus_Monstrorum"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Chad J. Bowser</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Danny_OBannion">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Danny O'Bannion</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>The Syndicate</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Arkham , Massachusetts Offspring Naomi O'Bannion (daughter) Criminal The Syndicate Human Created by Fantasy Flight Games is a character created by Fantasy Flight Games for their Arkham Horror Files series of Cthulhu Mythos -related releases. Although primarily appearing the the now-discontinued Call of Cthulhu Living Card Game, he has also been referenced in other media, such as Peter J. Evans ' franchise tie-in novel Feeders from Within . Overview is a major player in the shady underworld of 1928 Arkham , heading the organised crime coalition dubbed The Syndicate, a powerful group with an interest in every illegal activity in town. Conducting much of his business from the Clover Club, a shady den of iniquity, his group is at constant war with the Sheldon gang, bootleggers plying their trade in opposition to the Syndicate. O'Bannion has a daughter, a beautiful young woman named Naomi , who also works for the Syndicate.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fantasy_Flight_Games"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Warder_of_Knowledge">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Warder of Knowledge</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story is notable for its explication of the eltdown shards . They were pre-human records invented by searight in his story " the sealed casket " and elaborated upon by h. p. lovecraft in the round-robin story " the challenge from beyond "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_F._Searight"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tales_of_the_Lovecraft_Mythos"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1992</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>1935</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_Cocktails:_Elixirs_%26_Libations_from_the_Lore_of_H._P._Lovecraft">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lovecraft Cocktails: Elixirs &amp; Libations from the Lore of H. P. Lovecraft</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lovecraft cocktails is a humorous horror fiction recipe guide based on h.p. Lovecraft's cthulhu mythos . The book is published by the countryman press, copyrighted by red duke games, llc . The book is designed as an accompanying "bar book" supplementing the recipes found in the original necronomnomnomnom .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Mike Slater</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>parody</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>recipe book</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_as_I_Knew_Him">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lovecraft as I Knew Him</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sonia H. Greene United States English Genre(s) Non-fiction Arkham House Something About Cats and Other Pieces date 1949 " " is a short (12-13 pages ) memoir of H. P. Lovecraft written by his former wife Sonia H. Greene . It was first published in the Arkham House collection Something About Cats and Other Pieces in 1949. History Something About Cats and Other Pieces (1949) Lovecraft Remembered (1998) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sonia_H._Greene"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Something_About_Cats"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Other Pieces</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1949</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Revelations_from_Yuggoth_2">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Revelations from Yuggoth 2</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Revelations from Yuggoth #2 Various Cover Artist Allen Koszowski English Revelations from Yuggoth Cryptic Publications Release Date May 1988 52 Preceded by Revelations from Yuggoth #1 Followed by Revelations from Yuggoth #3 Revelations from Yuggoth #2 was an issue of Shawn Ramsey 's short-lived Lovecraftian fanzine Revelations from Yuggoth . Edited by Ramsey with cover art by Allen Koszowski , it ran to 52 in an A5 format and was published by Cryptic Publications in May 1988. Contents Introduction by Shawn Ramsey "Satan's Servants Introduction" by Robert Bloch "Satan's Servants" by Robert Bloch "The Watcher at the Door" by Henry Kuttner "The Abyss" by Robert A. W. Lowndes "Headcase" by Garrie Hall "The Hungry Place" by W. H. Pugmire "Slumber" by Robert E. Howard "R. E. H." by R. H. Barlow "The Singing Dead" by Jessica Amanda Salmonson "Shadows of Chapultepec" by Alice I'Anson "Necrofile" by H. J. Vester "Review of "The Outsider, The Rats in the Walls, The Dunwich Horror", old radio dramatizations from Radio Showcase" by H. J. Vester "Other Views from Yuggoth" by uncredited Letters by Robert Bloch, Dave Stall, Peter Smith, R. A. W. Lowndes , Robert H. Knox, and Mark Raney</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>52</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Revelations_from_Yuggoth"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 1988</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tesla_vs_Lovecraft">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tesla vs Lovecraft</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Top-down shooter is a top-down shooter inspired by the creatures of the cthulhu mythos . The player controls nikola tesla as he tries to secure the wardenclyffe tower from h. p. lovecraft and his hordes of eldritch creatures . The player will battle with various creatures from the cthulhu mythos as well as some new</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2018 26 Jan</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Alone_Against_the_Static">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Alone Against the Static</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>B. W. Holland Nicholas Grey Andrey Fetisov Pat Loboyko Cover Artist Nicholas Grey English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Chaosium Release Date 2023 104 is a scenario supplement book for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game. Written by B. W. Holland, it was published in a 104-page hardcover format in 2023. Overview is a solo-play adventure which includes two pre-generated characters for quick-start play, as well as statistics for a new Cthulhu Mythos entity and a new mechanic for keeping track of gameplay. Unusually for a Call of Cthulhu scenario, it is set in the 1990s time period. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>B. W. Holland</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>104</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2023</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Atishfishan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pastiches"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Atishfishan</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The asylum diaries: arkham date may 2019is a contemporary cthulhu mythos story by australian brian craddock . It was published in the first issue of short-lived horror periodical the asylum files by oscillate wildly press in may 2019 . The reporter and his lover travel to an archaeological site in pakistan to cover the proceedings . They discover an ancient fortification beneath which sleeps an army of vicious, intelligent beings which belch fire and</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Brian Craddock</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Asylum_Diaries:_Arkham"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>May 2019</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPL_1920">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleComicBookAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>HPL 1920</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A collection of comic book-format adaptations of h. p. lovecraft short stories by canadian artist nick o'gorman . It was published through his independent label target destroyed comics in 2020 . The book was later included in the omnibus collection hpl vol. 1: comic adaptations of the works of hp lovecraft .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nick_OGorman"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2020</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arthur_S._Koki">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scholars"/>
    <rdfs:label>Arthur S. Koki</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1989</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Koki, whose parents were born in albania, wrote an honor's thesis on lovecraft at clark university in 1960 . His master's thesis was based on extensive original research, including interviews with lovecraft correspondents . The paper was a significant source for l. sprague de camp's lovecraft: a biography .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Scholar</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1937</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Marble_Faun">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Marble Faun</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Nathaniel Hawthorne United States English Ticknor and Fields Release Date February 1860 ; or The Romance of Monte Beni is a gothic romance novel by the late American Nathaniel Hawthorne . First published in 1860, it has been cited by Lovecraft scholars S. T. Joshi and David E. Schultz as one of the influences behind The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath . External Links at Wikipedia References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nathaniel_Hawthorne"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>February 1860</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_White_Ship:_A_Psychological_Odyssey">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>"The White Ship": A Psychological Odyssey</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The White Ship": A Psychological Odyssey Dirk W. Mosig English Genre(s) Non-fiction Whispers #5 date November 1974 " "The White Ship": A Psychological Odyssey " is a short non-fiction piece by critic Dirk W. Mosig . As it’s title suggests, it is a focus study of Lovecraft's short story " The White Ship ". Overview First published in Whispers #5 in November 1974, "A Psychological Odyssey" was included in the S. T. Joshi -edited critical collection H. P. Lovecraft: Four Decades of Criticism in 1980.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dirk_W._Mosig"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Whispers #5</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 1974</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Eibon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dreamers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Eibon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"the unfathomable" was the most powerful sorcerer in the ancient land of hyperborea . Born in the city of iqqua, the son of the kingdom's chief archivist, he was taken in by the warlock zylac as an apprentice . Later accused of heresy by morghi the inquisitor, the high priest of the goddess yhoundeh .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#In_the_Vale_of_Pnath"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shaggai"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The Demon of the Ring</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Door_to_Saturn"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Carlton_Herzog">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosInspiredAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Carlton Herzog</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author United States United States Weird fiction  is an American author and former flight dispatcher for the United States Air Force. He has published a number of short stories inspired by the works of Howard Phillips Lovecraft ; these have been included in publications such as the Lovecraftiana zine. Selected Works " Afterburn " (2020) " The Thing In the Jar " (2020) External Links at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>United States</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Achtung%21_Cthulhu_Tactics">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Achtung! Cthulhu Tactics</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A turn-based tactical video game which takes place in modiphius entertainment's achtung! Cthulhu historical horror setting . The player takes control of a squad of four special forces operatives who must fight the forces of the cthulhu mythos behind enemy lines . The main issues the game draws criticism over are the repetitive level designs .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2018</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sacrifice">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MediaAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sacrifice</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Is a 2020 lovecraftian horror film based on a short story by paul kane . It stars screen mythos stalwart barbara crampton as the lead antagonist . A man who was brought to america as a young child returns to his norwegian family home after his mother's death, accompanied by his heavily-pregnant wife . Despite remembering nothing of his childhood, he is quickly introduced to a local cult which worships a sleeping sub</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United Kingdom</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isBasedOnW>Men of the Cloth</ex:isBasedOnW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>22 October 2020</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecrafts_Kingsport">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>H. P. Lovecraft's Kingsport</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Kevin Ross et al United States English Call of Cthulhu Chaosium Release Date May 2008 150 is a background and scenario supplement for the Call of Cthulhu horror roleplaying game, focusing on the fictional town of Kingsport in Lovecraft . Written by Kevin Ross et al, it runs to a total of 150 and was published by Chaosium in May 2008. It is currently out of print. Overview Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Kevin Ross et al</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>150</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 2008</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Roby_Rathbun">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSRelatives"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Roby Rathbun</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>28 January 1899</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lovecraft’s maternal great-grandmother.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isRelativeToR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1836</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Leviathan_%28Hellraiser%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Leviathan (Hellraiser)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Cenobites</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The leviathan is a lovecraftian-like eldritch god from the fictional universe of the hellraiser movies and comics . The entity has existed for numerous millennia, ruling over hell (also known as the labyrinth) and the cenobites . It is the creator of the cenobites, whom come in many shapes and forms .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Hellbound: Hellraiser II</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Clive Barker</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Evil_Dead_2:_Dark_Ones_Rising">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Evil Dead 2: Dark Ones Rising</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Frank Hannah Oscar Bazaldua, Carlos Eduardo English Space Goat Publications Release Date Oct 2016 - Dec 2016 Media Type Comic book is a 3-issue mini series written by Frank Hannah in 2016 and published by Space Goat Publications. Back from the Mirror Dimension , Annie Knowby and a simulacrum Ash Williams find a sinkhole in the middle of a road. From which a Dark One climbs out of it, absorbing the souls of those around it. Aiding them in the battle against the Dark One are the souls of voodoo sorcerers.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Frank Hannah</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>Oct 2016 - Dec 2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Hilger">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonFictionAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ron Hilger</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Editor California, USA United States is an American non-fiction author specialising in the works of the late author and H. P. Lovecraft 's friend and contemporary Clark Ashton Smith . In addition to his factual output, he has also written the occasional work of fiction, and has edited several collections of Smith's works and at least one issue of a Lovecraftian fanzine, The Cthulhu Codex . Selected Works Editor The Cthulhu Codex #6 (1994) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA http://example.org/lovecraft#California</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Molly_Tanzer">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Molly Tanzer</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Birth Date October 29, 1981 Marietta, Georgia, USA American Horror Fantasy Science Fiction http://mollytanzer.com/ (born October 29, 1981) is an American writer of horror, fantasy, and science fiction whose work has repeatedly appeared in anthologies of Cthulhu Mythos fiction. She also edited (with Jesse Bullington ) the anthology Swords v. Cthulhu ( Stone Skin Press , 2016). Selected Bibliography Editor Swords v. Cthulhu (2016, with Jesse Bullington ) Fiction "The Infernal History of the Ivybridge Twins" (2011) "A Pretty Mouth" (2012) "A Spotted Trouble at Dolor-on-the Downs" (2012) "Damnatio Memoriae" (2012) " The Hour of the Tortoise " (2012) "Go, Go, Go, Said the Byakhee" (2011) "All This for the Greater Glory of the 7th and 329th Children of the Black Goat of the Woods" (2012) " Herbert West in Love " (2013) "The Curse of the Old Ones" (2015, with Jesse Bullington ) "The Thing on the Cheerleading Squad" (2015) "Cognac, Communism, and Cocaine" (2017, with Nick Mamatas ) External Links at Wikipedia at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Georgia Marietta</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>American</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://mollytanzer.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1981 October 29</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
