  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Halpin_Chalmers">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Halpin Chalmers</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>3 July 1928</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Manhattan_Museum_of_Fine_Arts"/>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Miskatonic_University"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Chalmers is a fictional character created by frank belknap long in the 1929 short story " the hounds of tindalos " he is notable for being the first victim in literature of the titular creatures . In the story, he is depicted as a journalist and "the author of several recondite books on occult themes" he describes himself as "a rebel, a champion of originality and lost causes"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Hounds_of_Tindalos"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Frank_Belknap_Long"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Balm_of_Sperrgebiet_Is_the_Krokodil">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Balm of Sperrgebiet Is the Krokodil</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Steve Berman English Simian Publishing Apotheosis: Stories of Human Survival After the Rise of the Elder Gods date October 2015 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos story by American Steve Berman. It was published in the Jason Andrew -edited thematic anthology Flub by Simian Publishing in October 2015. Synopsis After an unspecified collapse of civilisation, a group of men take up residence in an abandoned town located in the Namib desert, killing those who stumble across their new home. When a group arrive muttering the name of something called " Ahtu ", the group attempt to kill them, but are in turn attacked by the vicious parasites growing within their victims. After successfully fending off the beasts, most of the group sets off to seek answers about Ahtu, leaving one to grub out an existence in the derelict town, a changed man.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Steve Berman</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Apotheosis:_Stories_of_Human_Survival_After_the_Rise_of_the_Elder_Gods"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pickmans_Muse">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Pickman's Muse</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Pickman's gallery is a short story by american author k. h. vaughn . It was published in the anthology pickman's gallery by ulthar press in 2018 . The story charts the development of young artist pickman .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>K. H. Vaughn</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pickmans_Gallery"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2018</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hziulquoigmnzhah">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Hziulquoigmnzhah</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The God of Cykranosh</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Ziulquag-Manzah</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as The God of Cykranosh, Ziulquag-Manzah Unknown, perhaps Xoth Current location Cykranosh (Saturn) Relatives Cxaxukluth (father) Ghisguth (brother) Tsathoggua (nephew) Scathach (daughter) Ysbaddaden (son) Klosmiebhyx First appearance CIRCLE : " The Door to Saturn " Created by Clark Ashton Smith (also Ziulquaz-Manzah) is a being created by Clark Ashton Smith for his short story " The Door to Saturn ". Overview In "The Door to Saturn", is described as having: It is slow-moving, and shows no real interest in either helping or hindering the sorcerer Eibon or his pursuer, the zealot Morghi. Currently residing on Cykranosh , the planet known as Saturn in the modern day, the Parchments of Pnom tell of Hzuilquoigmnzhah being the "son" of Cxaxukluth , "brother" to Ghisguth and "uncle" to Tsathoggua . Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCharacterMateC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Klosmiebhyx"/>
    <ex:isLocatedAtC>Cykranosh</ex:isLocatedAtC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Door_to_Saturn"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Cult">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cults"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Organisation"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Cult</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationO>Cthulhu The White polypous thing</ex:hasAffiliationO>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesO>The Cult of Cthulhu</ex:hasAlternativeNamesO>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The , alternately referred to as the cult of cthulhu, are the mortal worshippers of the great old one cthulhu . They believe that their worship will one day result in the return of cthulhu, and that when he awakens they will be able to throw all notions of morality and compassion aside . Small "cells" of the cult exist in remote parts of the world; many of their members are seamen who travel from port to port</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNotableMemberO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Castro"/>
    <ex:hasPurposeO>To speed the awakening of Great Cthulhu</ex:hasPurposeO>
    <ex:hasTypeO>Cult</ex:hasTypeO>
    <ex:isLocatedAtO>Worldwide</ex:isLocatedAtO>
    <ex:wasCreatedBynO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Doom_That_Came_to_Dunwich_%28collection%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Doom That Came to Dunwich (collection)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Doom That Came to Dunwich Richard A. Lupoff United States English Venture Press Release Date October 2017 224  The Doom That Came to Dunwich (which boasts two different taglines: Selected Stories of Lovecraftian Mystery… and Weird Mysteries of the Cthulhu Mythos ) is a collection of short stories by the late American Richard A. Lupoff . Overview Running to a total of 224 , The Doom That Came to Dunwich was published in October 2017 by Venture Press. It has also received a digital ebook release. Contents " The Doom That Came to Dunwich " (1996) " The Secret of the Sahara " (2005) " The Turret " (1995) " The Peltonville Horror " (2004) " The Devil's Hop Yard " (1978) " Documents in the Case of Elizabeth Akeley " (1982) " Brackish Waters " (2005) " The Adventure of the Voorish Sign " (2003) " Nothing Personal " (2010)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_A._Lupoff"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>224</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 2017</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Larva_of_the_Other_Gods">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OuterGods"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Larva of the Other Gods</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sociocultural Characteristics Court of Azathoth Outer Space Real World Information First appearance HPL : The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath Created by H. P. Lovecraft The Larvae of the Other Gods (sometimes referred to as the Larvae of the Outer Gods) are a type of cosmic being which made their first appearance in H. P. Lovecraft's 1943 novella The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath . Overview The Larvae are the product (or perhaps offspring) of the beings which revel in the Court of Azathoth, created when portions of the Outer Gods slough off and drift away into the cosmos. These entities vary enormously in every aspect, including intelligence: some are mindless, some possess their own goals and desires . For some unknown reason, the space surrounding Earth's Dreamlands plays host to an unusually high concentration of these creatures . Gallery References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dream-Quest_of_Unknown_Kadath"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Francis_Wayland_Thurston">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Francis Wayland Thurston</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Thurston was a bostonian anthropologist, the grandnephew of george gammell angell and the sole heir and executor of his estate . While going through the late professor angell's papers, he discovered the secret of the cthulhu cult . Thurston's name appears only in the story's subtitle, which originally appeared in weird tales .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Call_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Labyrinth_of_Sleep">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Labyrinth of Sleep</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Is a short story by orrin grey, first published in the anthology future lovecraft in november 2011 . Despite containing no direct references to lovecraft's work, does contain some nods to earlier works .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Orrin_Grey"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Future_Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2011</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Outposts">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Outposts</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Historywas first published in the anthology further tales of cthulhu invictus . It was also included in the 2019 collection of meikle's own works into the black: tales of lovecraftian terror .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#William_Meikle"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Further_Tales_of_Cthulhu_Invictus"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Istasha">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Istasha</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Mistress of Darkness</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as Mistress of Darkness First appearance " The Stalker in the Snows " Created by James Ambuehl is a fictional entity created by American author James Ambuehl for his 1997 short Cthulhu Mythos story " The Stalker in the Snows ". Overview A cat deity similar to Bast but more vicious, 's sister is the sylvan Lythalia .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Stalker_in_the_Snows"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Ambuehl"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dakini">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dakini</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Khandromas</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>She Who Moves In Space</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The are mystical creatures whose enlightened form is that of a beautiful human female . The unenlightened form is the flesh-eating demon, khandromas, which can also be summoned and bound by sorcerers .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Secrets_of_Tibet"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Jason Williams</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Penelope_Love">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Penelope Love</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Games Designer Author Birth Date 1965 Box Hill, Melbourne, Australia Australian Horror (born 1965) is an Australian games designer and author who has written extensively for Chaosium 's horror roleplaying game Call of Cthulhu . In addition, she has also written a small number of short fiction works as tie-in pieces for the ; these have appeared in anthologies such as Tales of Cthulhu Invictus , Further Tales of Cthulhu Invictus (2015 and 2017 respectively, Golden Goblin Press ), Made in Goatswood (1995, Chaosium) and She Walks in Shadows (2015, Innsmouth Free Press ). Selected Bibliography Short Fiction "Unseen" (1995) "Magnum Innominandum" (2015) Gaming Material Fatal Experiments (1990, contributor) Terror Australis First Edition (1991) Horror on the Orient Express First Edition (1991, contributor) Blood Brothers 2 (1992, contributor) Sacraments of Evil (1993, contributor) Horror of the Orient Express Second Edition (2014, contributor) Reign of Terror (2017, contributor) Horror of the Orient Express Second Edition (2014, contributor) De Horrore Cosmico (2015, contributor) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads at AustLit</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>http://example.org/lovecraft#Australia Box Hill Melbourne</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>Australian</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games Designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1965</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ngha-Kthun">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>N'gha-Kthun</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hastur"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>N'gah-Kthun</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as N'gah-Kthun Hastur Mi-Go First appearance HPL : The Whisperer in Darkness Created by H. P. Lovecraft N'gah-Kthun is a name coined by H. P. Lovecraft for his novella The Whisperer in Darkness , in which it is heard as part of a fragmentary conversation between the Mi-Go and their allies. The name appears with the variant spelling in August Derleth 's short story " The Return of Hastur ", where it is part of a list of beings, places, and concepts that Paul Tuttle learned about while studying occult grimoires . In " The Horror in the Gallery ", by Lin Carter , is at last identified as one of the Lesser Old Ones , and the leader of all the Mi-Go who serve Hastur .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Whisperer_in_Darkness"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Fire_of_Mazda">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Fire of Mazda</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Simon of gitta is enmeshed in a plot to kill the roman emperor by provoking the wrath of the persian fire god atar against him . The ritual involves the invocation of azathoth , identified with the persian demon azdahak and the gnostic entity achamoth . The story was first published in the may/june 1984 issue of orion's child .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_L._Tierney"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Orion's Child #1</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>May 1984</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Library_of_St_John_the_Beheaded">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Library of St John the Beheaded</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>St Giles Rookery, Holborn, London Library First appearance EXP : All-Consuming Fire Created by Andy Lane The is a secret library of forbidden texts in the fictional universe of Doctor Who . It is owned by the Catholic Church and contains items they deemed unwise for the general public to know about. As well as evidence of alien activity and lost works by writers Plato, Aristophanes and Shakespeare it also contains the following Mythos books: De Vermis Mysteriis ( EXP : All-Consuming Fire ) The Eltdown Shards ( EXP : Millennial Rites ) The Pnakotic Manuscripts ( EXP : Millennial Rites ) The Book of Eibon ( EXP : Millennial Rites ) Lies and Lives of Yog-Sothoth ( EXP : Millennial Rites ) Behind the Mythos Although the Library is fictional, St Giles is a real in London which was infamous for its slum rookery. External links at the Doctor Who wiki</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Library</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Holborn</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>London</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>St Giles Rookery</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#All-Consuming_Fire"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Snake_Fever">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Snake Fever</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A cowboy in the wild west takes refuge from a hunting party at an abandoned homestead said by the natives to be cursed land . While there, he recalls the story of the previous occupants, who made the mistake of killing a nest of strangely-coloured snakes . The head of the household developed a deep-seated psychosis, causing him to believe that his family were transforming into serpent-like creatures .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dark_Legacy"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>April 2004</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Atlantis">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>Atlantis</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Continent is a legendary lost continent, usually supposed to have been located in the north atlantic ocean . It was believed to have hosted an ancient civilization that was destroyed and sunk beneath the ocean in a cataclysmic event . The myth of was incorporated as a background element by h. p. lovecraft and other cthulhu mythos authors .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Continent</ex:hasTypeL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft:_Against_the_World,_Against_Life">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>H. P. Lovecraft: Against the World, Against Life</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Michel Houellebecq France French English Portuguese Genre(s) None-fiction Éditions du Rocher Release Date 1991 (originally published as H. P. Lovecraft : Contre le monde, contre la vie ) is study of H. P. Lovecraft and his oeuvre by French Michel Houellebecq. First published by Éditions du Rocher in 1991, it has since been reprinted many times by many different publishing houses, and has also been translated into both English and Portuguese. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Michel Houellebecq</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#France"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>None-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>French</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Portuguese</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1991</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jacqueline_Fine">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Jacqueline Fine</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Psychic</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The psychic is a character created by fantasy flight games . She is part of their arkham horror files line of cthulhu mythos gaming material . Behind the mythos was first introduced in the supplement dunwich horror .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Dunwich Horror</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fantasy_Flight_Games"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Koth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Koth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Hyboria city is a fictional city first mentioned in the conrad &amp; kirowan stories of the late american author robert e. howard . It has also been referenced in howard's conan series of sword and sorcery tales, and further expanded upon in this setting by marvel comics . Despite howard presenting as a city, the waters have been somewhat muddied through mentioning it in the same context as such beings as cthulhu and yog-soth</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>City</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Hyboria</ex:isLocatedAtL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Next_Stop:_Rlyeh">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Next Stop: R'lyeh</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Shadrick Beechem English Cthulhu Christmas Special and Other Lovecraftian Yuletide Tales date November 2017 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos story by Shadrick Beechem, published in the Jesse Dedman and William C. Marchese collection Cthulhu Christmas Special and Other Lovecraftian Yuletide Tales in November 2017. Synopsis The mighty cruise ship Pride of the Sea finds itself lost and without power in a violent Pacific Ocean storm, its passengers and crew subjected to terrible dreams of doom and destruction throughout this ordeal. As the storm clears and the maintenance team inspect the damage, they find that the propellers are fouled with what at first appears to be an enormous rope, but is soon recognised for what it truly is: a colossal tentacle. As the crew desperately try to cut the tentacle loose and escape, the tentacle's owner, Great Cthulhu himself, rises from the depths and sends the ship and everyone aboard to a watery grave.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Shadrick Beechem</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Cthulhu Christmas Special</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Other Lovecraftian Yuletide Tales</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecrafts_Disciples">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lovecraft's Disciples</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>John B. Ford &amp; Steve Lines English Rainfall Books Irregular December 2005 Final Publication October 2020 was a relatively long-running fanzine devoted to works inspired by H. P. Lovecraft and the Cthulhu Mythos . Overview Running for a total of 42 issues between December 2005 and October 2020, Lovecraft's Discipes was edited by John B. Ford and Steve Lines , with Lines also providing artwork for many of the covers. It was published by Rainfall Books , a publishing house with a long history of Mythos-related releases. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ymnar">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OuterGods"/>
    <rdfs:label>'Ymnar</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Ngyr-Korath</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Seducer of Earthly Intelligence</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Dark Stalker</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ymnar Also known as The Dark Stalker, Seducer of Earthly Intelligence Spawn of Ngyr-Korath Ngyr-Korath First appearance " He Who Comes at the Noontime " Created by Walter C. DeBill Jr. ' Ymnar ( The Dark Stalker or Seducer of Earthly intelligences ) is a shape-shifting entity spawned by the Outer God Ngyr-Korath to serve him only. It may grant great powers to whoever chooses to serve it and its master, but its final aim is the destruction of all sentient and intelligent life in the Cosmos. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>He Who Comes at the Noontime</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Walter C. DeBill Jr.</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Darkness">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OuterGods"/>
    <rdfs:label>Darkness</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Outer Gods</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Lord Darkness</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Magnum Tenebrosum</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Unnamed Darkness</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The nameless mist was identified with the magnum innominandum by some later writers, notably lin carter and the call of cthulhu rpg . The nameless mist was identified with the nameless mist by some later writers, notably lin carter and the call of cthulhu rpg .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Selected Letters of H. P. Lovecraft II</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Following_in_Pickmans_Footsteps_%E2%80%A6">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Following in Pickman's Footsteps …</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Following in Pickman's Footsteps … Full story online here James Ambuehl United States English Genre(s) Horror Nightscapes #16 date October 2006 " Following in Pickman's Footsteps … " is a short Cthulhu Mythos poem by American James Ambuehl . Overview The three verses describe a man's struggle as he devolves into a monstrous ghoul in the manner of Richard Upton Pickman from the H. P. Lovecraft short story " Pickman's Model ". History "Following in Pickman's Footsteps …" was published in issue 16 of the online Mythos zine Nightscapes in October 2006.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Ambuehl"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Nightscapes #16</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2006</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Knights_Head">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Knight's Head</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>England Tavern First appearance HPL : " Facts Concerning the Late Arthur Jermyn and His Family " Created by H. P. Lovecraft The is a tavern in England where Sir Wade Jermyn was often found drinking after returning home from his frequent explorations in Africa. At the he related wild tales of the things he discovered on that dark continent, tales that led many people to question his sanity. ( HPL : " Facts Concerning the Late Arthur Jermyn and His Family ")</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Tavern</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#England"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>Facts Concerning the Late Arthur Jermyn</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>His Family</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dark_Legacy">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dark Legacy</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ron Shiflet (1998-1999) Ran Cartwright (2000-2001) Lee Clark Zumpe (2004-2005) English Irregular August 1998 Final Publication June 2005 was a sporadically-released Lovecraftian fanzine with a focus on works set in and around the greater Cthulhu Mythos . Overview Running for 14 issues between 1998 and 2005 (with issue #12 appearing to have never been published, resulting in the final issue being numbered 15), was released on an irregular basis. Edited by Ron Shiflet (1998-1999), Ran Cartwright (2000-2001), and Lee Clark Zumpe (2004-2005), each issue ran to 58 pages. In 2015, Cartwright compiled a collection titled - The Poetry , which gathered all of the poetry from the fanzine's run, while 2016 saw the release of Volume 1 and Volume 2 The Best of . Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Arkham_Sampler">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AugustDerlethWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Periodicals"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Arkham Sampler</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>August Derleth English Arkham House Quarterly Spring 1948 Final Publication Winter 1949 was a short-lived American magazine published quarterly by Arkham House which ran for eight issues between 1948 and 1949. Focusing on the fantasy and horror genres, included a mix of fiction, non-fiction, and poetry, and contained pieces by Lovecraft and his contemporaries, as well as more recent writers. All issues were edited by August Derleth . Contributors included H. P. Lovecraft, August Derleth, Clark Ashton Smith , Robert Bloch , Fritz Leiber , George T. Wetzel , Rheinhart Kleiner , C. M. Eddy, Jr. , Samuel Loveman , Ray Bradbury, Frank Belknap Long , E. Hoffman Price , John Wyndham, Jules Verne, A. E. van Vogt, and many others. Issues 1948 Spring Summer Autumn Winter 1949 Spring Summer Autumn Winter External Links at Wikipedia at The Internet Speculative Fiction Database</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Demon_Lord_of_the_Dark_Valley">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Demon Lord of the Dark Valley</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The was a demonic being in koth which ruled over the dark valley . It seized the mortal dancer timara, and violated her . She gave birth to twin sons and apparently fell in love with the monster .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
