    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Delta Green: The Role-Playing Game</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Delta green is a stand-alone cthulhu mythos roleplaying game published by arc dream publishing . It updates the original call of cthulhu supplement delta green from the 1990s into a post-911 environment . The game uses a modified version of chaosium's basic role-playing (brp), allowing it to be used in conjunction with many call of cthulhu supplements .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>27 April 2016</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Visage_of_Great_Truths">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Visage of Great Truths</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>" the sacred house" is the first of four stories set in the yellow mythos . It was first published in the anthology within stranger aeons in 2016 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Barr"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Within_Stranger_Aeons"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>21 May 2016</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dhole">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dhole</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Bholes</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Death Worms</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Dol</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Great Eater</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>K'ole</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Maw of Death</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Planet Devourers</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Dholes, also called bholes, are huge, slimy worm-like creatures, at least several hundred feet long . They are not native to earth and none seem to have been brought here for more than brief periods . Theories include magical summoning to "swim" through space .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath Through the Gates of the Silver Key Dreams in the House of Weir Ire of the Void</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>20 ft. thick</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>Gigantic worms reaching over 600 ft. in length</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>hidden beneath a segmented aperture</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>their skin is armor-clad with thick chitinous-like sections. At one end is the feasting mouth</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>which closes right when burrowing below</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The White People (possibly)</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Arthur Machen (Dôls)</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>H. P. Lovecraft (Dholes)</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sarnath_Centre">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sarnath Centre</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Tony Daly English Genre(s) Weird fiction Rogue Planet Press Lovecraftiana Vol 8, Issue 2 date July 2023 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos poem by Tony Daly. It was published in the Gavin Chappell -edited zine Lovecraftiana , Vol 8, Issue 2 by Rogue Planet Press in July 2023 . Synopsis The eighteen lines offer a brief exploration of the doomed city of Sarnath , from its lofty towers, to its verdant gardens, to its lowest slums, as well as the moral burden weighed upon its people as a result of their actions against the Beings of Ib . Behind the Mythos Despite the text containing no direct references to the Cthulhu Mythos or the writings of H. P. Lovecraft , the poem's title combined with the content makes it clear that the piece was intended to refer to the short story " The Doom That Came to Sarnath ". References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Tony Daly</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Issue 2</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Lovecraftiana Vol 8</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>July 2023</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Matthew_Wilson">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosInspiredAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Matthew Wilson</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Poet Weird fiction  is an author and poet who has written a handful of works about and inspired by those of Howard Phillips Lovecraft . These have been published in anthologies such as Lovecraft after Dark (2015, James Ward Kirk Publishing) and zines including Lovecraftiana ( Rogue Planet Press ). Selected Works Poetry " Evil Things in Cold Midnight " (2015) " Lurker of the Woods " (2021) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Poet</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cobra_Crown">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MysticalArtefacts"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cobra Crown</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a fictional item in the expanded cthulhu mythos . Was created by l. sprague de camp and . Lin carter . First appeared in their conan novel conan the buccaneer . In 1971 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasFunctionA>Magical mind-control</ex:hasFunctionA>
    <ex:hasPlaceOfOriginA>Valusia</ex:hasPlaceOfOriginA>
    <ex:hasTypeA>Mystical artefact</ex:hasTypeA>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnA>Conan the Buccaneer</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnA>
    <ex:wasCreatedByA>The Serpent Men</ex:wasCreatedByA>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Arkham_Collector">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AugustDerlethWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Periodicals"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Arkham Collector</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>August Derleth English Arkham House Semiannual Summer 1967 Final Publication Summer 1971 was a magazine published by Arkham House from 1967 until 1971. It mostly published essays, reviews, and poetry, but also published some original fiction, including the first published works by Brian Lumley and Alan Dean Foster . The entire run of ten issues was bound in hard cover and published as , Vol. 1 , in 1971. Selected Contents Essays "Colin Wilson" by August Derleth (Summer 1967) "The Art of Pastiche" by August Derleth (Summer 1967) "Notes on the Writing of Weird Fiction" by H. P. Lovecraft (Winter 1968) "Hugo Gernsback" by August Derleth (Winter 1968) "Cthulhu in Celluloid" by Ramsey Campbell (Summer 1968) "Memories of Lovecraft: I" by Sonia Greene (Winter 1969) "Memories of Lovecraft: II" by Helen V. Sully (Winter 1969) "Lovecraft on Love" by H. P. Lovecraft (Winter 1971) "The Mass Media Horror" by James Wade (Summer 1971) Fiction " The Cyprus Shell " by Brian Lumley (Summer 1968) " 'Till A' the Seas' " by R. H. Barlow and H. P. Lovecraft (Winter 1969) " The Thing in the Moonlight " by H. P. Lovecraft and Brian Lumley (Winter 1969) "Under the Eaves" by Lin Carter (Summer 1969) "A Darker Shadow Over Innsmouth" by James Wade (Summer 1969) " Billy's Oak " by Brian Lumley (Winter 1970) "The House of the Worm" by Gary Myers (Summer 1970) "Miss McWhortle's Weird" by Donald A. Wollheim (Summer 1970) " An Item of Supporting Evidence " by Brian Lumley (Summer 1970) "The Cocomacaque" by Carl Jacobi (Winter 1971) " Yohk the Necromancer " by Gary Myers (Winter 1971) "Passing of a Dreamer" by Gary Myers (Spring 1971) " The Doom of Yakthoob " by Lin Carter (Summer 1971) "Notes Concerning a Green Box" by Alan Dean Foster (Summer 1971) Poetry "Graveyard in April" by Joseph Payne Brennan (Summer 1967) "Tintagel" by L. Sprague de Camp (Summer 1967) "Down Endless Years" by Joseph Payne Brennan (Winter (1968) "H. P. Lovecraft" by William Fagan (Winter (1968) "The Lemurienne" by Clark Ashton Smith (Summer 1968) "Shadow on the Wall" by Duane W. Rimel (Summer 1969) "Ghosts" by L. Sprague de Camp (Summer 1969) "Carcosa" by Richard L. Tierney (Summer 1969) "Poe's Lake • poem by Joseph Payne Brennan (Winter 1970) "To Clark Ashton Smith" by Lin Carter (Winter 1970) "The Wendigo" by Richard L. Tierney (Winter 1970) "Moonlight" by Clark Ashton Smith (Summer 1970) "All Hallows' Eve" by Lin Carter (Summer 1970) "To Howard Phillips Lovecraft" by Clark Ashton Smith (Winter 1971) "Black Thirst" by Lin Carter (Winter 1971) "Enough" by Brian Lumley (Winter 1971) "K'n-yan" by Walter C. DeBill, Jr. (Winter 1971) "Summer Night" by Joseph Payne Brennan (Winter 1971) "Strange Flowers Bloom" by Duane W. Rimel (Winter 1971) "To Great Cthulhu" by Richard L. Tierney (Spring 1971) "May-Eve" by Steve Eng (Spring 1971) "In Pressure-Pounded Chasms" by Brian Lumley (Spring 1971) "Night Sounds" by Walter C. DeBill, Jr. (Summer 1971) External Links Title: at Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Wikipedia</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Those_Dreadful_Eltdown_Shards">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Those Dreadful Eltdown Shards</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Allen Koszowski English H. Harksen Productions Release Date August 2016 259 is an anthology of Lovecraftian horror focusing on the fictional artefacts the Eltdown Shards . Edited by Franklyn Searight , whose father Richard was the creator of the Shards , it was published by H. Harksen Productions in August 2016. Cover art was supplied by Allen Koszowski . Overview Contents , introduction by Franklyn Searight " The Sealed Casket " by Richard F. Searight " The Warder of Knowledge " by Richard F. Searight " The Challenge from Beyond " by Robert E. Howard , Frank Belknap Long , H. P. Lovecraft , A. Merritt , and C. L. Moore " The Diary of Alonzo Typer " by H. P. Lovecraft and William Lumley " The Shadow Out of Time " by H. P. Lovecraft "The Coming of Ouran-Atun" by Franklyn Searight and Richard F. Searight "The Horror from the Shards" by Franklyn Searight and Richard F. Searight "Mists of Death" by Franklyn Searight and Richard F. Searight "Seized by the Warder" by Franklyn Searight "Admonition from the Past" by Franklyn Searight and Richard F. Searight</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>259</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nightscapes_July_1998">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nightscapes July 1998</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various English Edward P. Berglund Release Date July 1998 Preceded by April 1998 Followed by September 1998 is the eighth issue of the long-running online fanzine Nightscapes . It was edited by the late American and Lovecraft scholar Edward P. Berglund . Contents In addition to the written material listed below, this edition also featured artwork from Darren Fisher, Toren Atkinson, Jason Thompson , John Burridge, and Suzanne E. Miller. Three of these images were custom-designed cards for Chaosium 's Mythos card game, based around W. H. Pugmire 's Sesqua Valley setting, with one featuring Pugmire himself. Fiction "Maastricht Chalk" by Mike Minnis "Chasing Shadows" by Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. "Carpenter's Hammer" by Adrian Kleinbergen "The Life and Death of Zebros Santiago" by Peter Levi "The Missive Of Thaddeus" by Sean Rodgers "Foundations" by Forrest Lancaster "Night of Samhain" by Carlos Orsi Marinho, translated by Ricardo Madeira "The Hungry Man" by Tim Waggoner "The Owls" by Alan Peschke Pseudo-Factual Material "Notes on Cthulhu" by Thomas V. Powers Non-Fiction "Conan and the Cthulhu Mythos" by G. W. Thomas "The Origin of Lovecraft's 'Black Magic' Quote" by David E. Schultz "Artists of the Cthulhu Mythos II: Toren Atkinson" by G. W. Thomas "An Interview with Aaron Vanek" by Toren Atkinson Games "Gathering Darkness: A Keynote Address" by Evan Franke (gaming nonfiction) "Children of the Yellow Lake" by Carlos Orsi Martinho ( Call of Cthulhu scenario) Parodies " Collect Call of Cthulhu " by Keith Allen Daniels Poetry " Folks of Innsmouth: Matt Eliot " by Franklyn Searight " From Beyond " by Adrian M. Kleinbergen " Algol " by Adrian M. Kleinbergen " Eibon and the Lord of Bala-Norath " by Ron Shiflet " When Old Gods Awaken " by Ron Shiflet Reviews By Nicole Lindroos and Edward P. Berglund External Links full issue free online at the Nightscapes website</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 1998</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Zamacona_y_Nu%C3%B1ez">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Zamacona y Nuñez</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>The Catholic Church Coronado's Expedition</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Pánfilo de is a fictional character who appears in "the mound" he is a spanish adventurer who uncovered the hidden land of k'n-yan . He was one of the few selected for a last-ditch attempt to discover something of worth .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Mound"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Roodmas_1989">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu Roodmas 1989</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Jason C. Eckhardt United States English Cryptic Publications Release Date May 1989 42 Preceded by Eastertide 1989 Followed by St. John's Eve 1989 is the 64th issue of Robert M. Price 's long-running Lovecraftian fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Edited by Price and featuring cover art by Jason C. Eckhardt , it was published by Cryptic Publications in May 1989. It ran to a total of 42 in an A5 format. Contents Editorial Shards by Robert M. Price " Herbert West—Reanimated " by Donald R. Burleson , Peter H. Cannon , Charles Hoffman, Will Murray , and Robert M. Price Reviews by Robert M. Price and Stefan Dziemianowicz "Hark Hecate!" by Richard L. Tierney "Baa, Baa, Black Goat" by Richard L. Tierney "Urgent" by Darrell Schweitzer Letters by Robert H. Knox, Darrell Schweitzer, Douglas Anderson, Stefan Dziemianowicz , Dan Gobbett, W. Paul Ganley, Tani Jantsang , Robert H. Daugherty, Darrell Schweitzer, and Donald R. Burleson</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>42</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 1989</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Hour_of_the_Tortoise">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Hour of the Tortoise</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Molly Tanzer English The Ivybridge Twins Lazy Fascist Press A Pretty Mouth date October 2012 " " is a Lovecraftian short story by American Molly Tanzer , which forms part of her Ivybridge Twins of Cthulhu Mythos fiction. It was first published in the collection of her works A Pretty Mouth (2012, Lazy Fascist Press) . Synopsis An illegitimate scion of the Calipash family falls foul of a pair of evil twins upon her return to the family home in the nineteenth century. Imprisoned in the family mausoleum with only a mute supernatural entity as company, she realises that she is not long for this world… History A Pretty Mouth (2012, Lazy Fascist Press) The Book of Cthulhu II (2012, 2022, Night Shade Books) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Molly_Tanzer"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>The Ivybridge Twins</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>A Pretty Mouth</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2012</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Trenchcoat_Brigade">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Trenchcoat Brigade</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The trenchcoat brigade is a 4-part miniseries written by john ney rieber . The plot involves the titular group of crime fighters and investigators trying to prevent a dystopian future . The book is set in a dystopian future in which all life on earth was consumed by m'nagalah .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>John Ney Rieber</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>March - June 1999</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Unfinished_Business">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Unfinished Business</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Historywas first published in the anthology hardboiled cthulhu in july 2006 . It later appeared in lovecraft's disciples #12 in may 2008 . It was included in the collection they thrive in darkness: mythos and madness in 2018 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hardboiled_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>May 2006</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Daisy_Walker">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Daisy Walker</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Librarian</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The librarian is a fictional character from the expanded cthulhu mythos . She was created by fantasy flight games as part of their arkham horror files range . She first appeared in the kingsport horror expansion for arkham horror .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Kingsport Horror Deluxe Expansion</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Despair">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Despair</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here H. P. Lovecraft United States English (original) French Pine Cones date June 1919 " " is a poem by the late American Howard Phillips Lovecraft . First published in the June 1919 edition of amateur press Pine Cones , it has since been reprinted numerous times, and has also been translated into French . Synopsis The feeling of hopelessness is evoked in five eight-line verses. History Pine Cones (1919) Beyond the Wall of Sleep (1943) Collected Poems (1963) The World of H. P. Lovecraft (1971) Fungi from Yuggoth and Other Poems (1971, 2016) The Fantastic Poetry (1990) The Ancient Track: The Complete Poetical Works of H. P. Lovecraft (2001, 2013) Eldritch Tales: A Miscellany of the Macabre (2011, 2020) Essais - Correspondance - Poésie - Révisions (2021, French-) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>French</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Pine Cones</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>June 1919</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Doel">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Doel</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Space Eaters (possibly)</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Doels are a fictional species from another dimension created by frank belknap long . It's been speculated that they might be the same as the space eaters . The name is also evocative of the dholes, which are massive predators that inhabit yaddith and the dreamlands in lovecraft's fiction .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Whisperer_in_Darkness"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Hounds_of_Tindalos"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Space-Eaters"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Frank_Belknap_Long"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Deep_Ones">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Deep Ones</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Historywas first published in tales of the cthulhu mythos by arkham house in 1969 . It has since been reprinted several times, and has also been translated into french .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Wade"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>French</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tales_of_the_Cthulhu_Mythos"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1969</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Kevin_Ross">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Kevin Ross</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Games Designer Author Editor Birth Date January 19, 1962 United States Horror Kevin A. Ross (born January 19, 1962) is an American games designer, writer, and editor who has been writing Cthulhu Mythos -related material for over thirty years. Overview Kick-starting his career by sending original Call of Cthulhu material to Chaosium ’s Cthulhu line editor Sandy Petersen , was assisted in this venture by the patronage of now-deceased Keith Herber , another long-time games designer. Although Ross has spent the lion's share of his time devoted to Call of Cthulhu , he has also produced fantasy content in the form of the Conan roleplaying game from Modiphius Entertainment , and has written a small number of fictional works based within the Mythos. Selected Bibliography Editor Dead But Dreaming (2002, with Keith Herber ) Dead but Dreaming 2 (2011) Author "The Music of the Spheres" (1995) "Dark Heart" (2011) "El Dia De Los Muertos" (2011) Games The Great Old Ones (1989) Blood Brothers (1990, contributor) Tales of the Miskatonic Valley (1991, contributor) Blood Brothers 2 (1992, contributor) Courting Madness (1992, contributor) Dead Reckonings (1998, contributor) Escape from Innsmouth (1992) The Resurrected III: Out of the Vault (2002, contributor) New Tales of the Miskatonic Valley (2008) H. P. Lovecraft's Kingsport (2008)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games Designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>January 19 1962</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Reloaded">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Reloaded</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>David Conyers Cover Artist Tithi Luadthong English Harrison Peel Genre(s) Cthulhu Mythos David Conyers Release Date June 22, 2021 258 Followed by Cthulhu Resurgent is a collection of Cthulhu Mythos stories by David Conyers , featuring the recurring character Harrison Peel --the first in a of three such compilations. It was self-published by Conyers in 2021. Contents "A Rambling Introduction, of Sorts..." (essay) by Peter Clines "Forward" ( essay) by David Conyers "Made of Meat" (2005) "Driven Underground" (2013) "Impossible Object" (2005) "False Containment" (2005) "Tears in Yellow" (2021) "The Weaponized Puzzle" (2013) "Weapons Grade" (2021) "The Elder Codex" (2014)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#David_Conyers"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>258</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Harrison_Peel"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2021 June 22</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shane_Ivey">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Shane Ivey</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Games designer Editor United States Roleplaying games is an American games designer whose output has included numerous contributions to Cthulhu Mythos works. His gaming credits include the core rulebook for the Delta Green stand-alone horror roleplaying game as well as a large number of its supplements, and several books for Cubicle 7 Entertainment 's Tolkenian fantasy The One Ring Roleplaying Game . Ivey is also the co-founder, with Dennis Detwiller , of Arc Dream Publishing , through which they publish Delta Green and the resurrected roleplaying magazine The Unspeakable Oath , for which he is the editor-in-chief. He also worked for the now-defunct Pagan Publishing , several of whose works Ivey and Detwiller have continued through Arc Dream. Selected Works Roleplaying Games Delta Green: Need to Know (2016) External Links at Wikipedia at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Colin_Wilson">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosAdjacentAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Colin Wilson</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>2013 December 5</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Betty troop, joy stout was an english writer who wrote horror and science fiction . He also wrote philosophy, especially philosophy in the gurdjieff and existentialist traditions . His works are more overtly sexual and explicitly violent than those of earlier writers .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>http://example.org/lovecraft#England Leicester Leicestershire</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>British</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasSpouseR>Betty Troop</ex:hasSpouseR>
    <ex:hasSpouseR>Joy Stewart</ex:hasSpouseR>
    <ex:isKnownForR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Return_of_the_Lloigor"/>
    <ex:isKnownForR>The Mind Parasites</ex:isKnownForR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1931 June 26</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Some_Notes_on_Cthulhuian_Pseudobiblia">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Some Notes on Cthulhuian Pseudobiblia</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Edward S. Lauterbach English Genre(s) Non-fiction Ohio University Press H. P. Lovecraft: Four Decades of Criticism date 1980 " " is a short non-fiction piece by poet and writer Edward S. Lauterbach. History H. P. Lovecraft: Four Decades of Criticism (1980, 1981)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Edward S. Lauterbach</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft:_Four_Decades_of_Criticism"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1980</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tick-Tock_Man">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Avatars"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tick-Tock Man</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Revered by currents related to the New World Order</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>technology in general.</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Mechanical Man</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The or mechanical man is an avatar of nyarlathotep . It is related to currents related to the "new world order" and technology in general . The size, construction, and exact appearance of the avatar's machine varies with time and place .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhus_Daughters">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu's Daughters</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>She Walks in Shadows Various Sarah K. Diesel English Innsmouth Free Press Prime Books Release Date October 2015 She Walks in Shadows is a collection of Cthulhu Mythos fiction by female writers, edited by Silvia Moreno-Garcia and Paula R. Stiles and published by Innsmouth Free Press in October 2015. It was later published as : Stories of Lovecraftian Horror by Innsmouth Free Press in April 2016, under which title it won the World Fantasy Award for Best Anthology. Contents "Ammutseba Rising" by Ann K. Schwader "Turn On the Light" by Penelope Love "Bring the Moon to Me" by Amelia Gorman "Violet is the Color of Your Energy" by Nadia Bulkin "De Deabus Minoribus Exterioris Theomagicae" by Jilly Dreadful "Lavinia's Wood" by Angela Slatter "The Adventurer's Wife" by Premee Mohamed "Lockbox" by E. Catherine Tobler "Hairwork" by Gemma Files "The Thing on the Cheerleading Squad" by Molly Tanzer "Body to Body to Body" by Selena Chambers "Magna Mater" by Arinn Dembo "Chosen" by Lyndsey Holder "Bitter Perfume" by Laura Blackwell "Eight Seconds" by Pandora Hope "The Eye of Juno" by Eugenie Mora "Cthulhu of the Dead Sea" by Inkeri Kontro "Notes Found in a Decommissioned Asylum, December 1961" by Sharon Mock "The Cypress God" by Rodopi Sisamis "When She Quickens" by Mary Turzillo "Queen of a New America" by Wendy N. Wagner "The Opera Singer" by Priya Sridhar "Shub-Niggurath's Witnesses" by Valerie Valdes "Provenance" by Benjanun Sriduangkaew "The Head of T'la-yub" by Nelly Geraldine García-Rosas (translated by Silvia Moreno-Garcia ) Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 2015</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#That_Which_Consumes_%28Call_of_Cthulhu_LCG%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>That Which Consumes (Call of Cthulhu LCG)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>First released in 2010, it was discontinued in 2015 with the demise of call of cthulhu: the card game . That which consumes is divided into the standard format of three duplicate sets of 20 unique cards for a total of 60 . The cards contained are divided between the existing factions .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2010</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#It_Lives_in_the_Woods">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>It Lives in the Woods</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>It is the third instalment in the nyarlathotep cycle by canadian troy young . Inspector joe mills investigates the ritualistic slaying of an entire family on a remote canadian island .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Troy_Young"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Nyarlathotep_Cycle"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Other:_Encounters_with_the_Cthulhu_Mythos_Book_1"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2020</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#hasUncleOrAunt">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
