  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sandy_Petersen">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sandy Petersen</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Carl petersen is the principal designer of the chaosium role-playing game call of cthulhu and drove its development for decades . After separating from chaosium he continued to write and edit mythos related game material such as the malleus monstrorum . He has now returned to chaosium in the role of senior consultant and advisor with input into design .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Missouri St. Louis</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Carl Sanford Joslyn Sandy Petersen</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>16 September 1955</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#William_Hope_Hodgson">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsInspirationsAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosAdjacentAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>William Hope Hodgson</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>17 19 April 1918</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Inspiration Birth Date 15 November 1877 Essex, England Death Date 17/19 April 1918 Ypres, France Great Britain Science Fiction Fantasy (15 November 1877 – 19 April 1918) was a prolific British author who wrote in a number of genres. His works are known to have inspired the likes of H. P. Lovecraft , Clark Ashton Smith and Henry S. Whitehead , and he was responsible for the creation of supernatural detective Thomas Carnacki , a character who has been expanded on for many years by many authors. Hodgson was killed in action during the First World War at the fourth battle of Ypres, April 1918. Selected Bibliography Novels The House on the Borderland (1908) Short Fiction " The Hog " (1947) Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>http://example.org/lovecraft#England Essex</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>Great Britain</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Inspiration</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>15 November 1877</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Devourer_of_the_Dead">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Devourer of the Dead</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is an eldritch abomination from the fictional universe of Marvel by Marvel Comics . Background The is a monstrous abomination , which lived in the base of a giant pyramid in the Stygian city of Luxur during the Hyborian Age . It was encountered by Conan and his Zembabwean sorcerer ally Zula, whom were easily overpowered by the abomination. They lit their barge on fire, and sent it into the Devourer, while they escaped with the sarcophagus.</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_%28Festa%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu (Festa)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu H. P. Lovecraft Germany German H. P. Lovecrafts Bibliothek des Schreckens Festa Release Date April 2009 335 Cthulhu is a German- collection of the works of late American Howard Phillips Lovecraft ; also included is a retrospective on Lovecraft by H. Warner Munn. Running to a total of 335 , the book was published by Festa as part of their H. P. Lovecrafts Bibliothek des Schreckens in April 2009; it was released in a hardcover-only edition . Contents "Er" (" He ") "Der Alchimist" (" The Alchemist ") "Die Straße" (" The Street ") "Die lauernde Furcht" (" The Lurking Fear ") "Der Übergang des Juan Romero" (" The Transition of Juan Romero ") "Das Mond-Moor" (" The Moon-Bog ") "Die Aussage des Randolph Carter" (" The Statement of Randolph Carter ") "Der silberne Schlüssel" (" The Silver Key ") "Durch die Tore des silbernen Schlüssels" (" Through the Gates of the Silver Key ") Die Traumsuche nach dem unbekannten Kadath ( The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath ) "Wohnsitze und Gedenkstätten Poes" (" Homes and Shrines of Poe ") "H. P. L. - Eine Erinnerung" by H. Warner Munn (" H.P.L.: A Reminiscence ") References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>Germany</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>335</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>German</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecrafts_Bibliothek_des_Schreckens"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>April 2009</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Aquadingen">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Aquadingen</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Occultist's apprentice occultist travels to the banks of the rhine in order to use its power to make himself rich . Using a passage from the book, he summons what he believes to be a water nymph, and is captivated by its charms . His mentor, alive and well, takes up the book and reads a spell from its pages, before dragging him down to the depths .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_M._Price"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Blasphemies</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Revelations</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2008</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Thomas_Lee">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Thomas Lee</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>The Cult of Glaaki</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Unknown, likely early 1800s Brichester , Great Britain Cultist The Cult of Glaaki Human First appearance EXP : " The Inhabitant of the Lake " Created by Ramsey Campbell is a character created by British horror author Ramsey Campbell for his short story " The Inhabitant of the Lake ". Overview some time before 1865, was a Brichester resident who, as an adult, became a servant of the Great Old One Glaaki , and the leader of his cult . In this capacity Lee commissioned a row of houses, later known as Lakeside Terrace , in a remote part of the Severn Valley next to the body of water in which his master dwells. He and his fellow cult members were responsible for transcribing the volumes known as the Revelations of Glaaki . Having succumbed to the Green Decay, Lee is no longer able to tolerate the touch of sunlight, and now appears to spend his days inside one of the many stone sarcophagi which dot the landscape surrounding Lakeside Terrace.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Inhabitant_of_the_Lake"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ramsey_Campbell"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Torn">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Torn</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here Natalie Cthullia (pseudonym, real name unknown) English Edward P. Berglund Nightscapes September 1998 date September 1998 " " is a humorous Cthulhu Mythos piece by an unknown using the pseudonym Natalie Cthullia. It was published in the September 1998 edition of Edward P. Berglund 's long-running online fanzine Nightscapes . Overview The piece is a parody of the pop song "" made famous in the English-speaking world by Australian singer Natalie Imbruglia in 1997 , The lyrics have been modified to incorporate elements of the Cthulhu Mythos , such as Shoggoths , Keziah Mason , and Brown Jenkin . References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Natalie Cthullia</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nightscapes_September_1998"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>September 1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Dream_of_Blood">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosComicBookAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Dream of Blood</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>L. Sprague de Camp &amp; Lin Carter (original story) Roy Thomas (adaptation) John Buscema Tony DeZuniga English Marvel Comics Release Date May 1979 Media Type Comic book " " is a Cthulhu Mythos comic book featuring Robert E. Howard 's fantasy character Conan . It is an adaptation of the first chapter of L. Sprague de Camp and Lin Carter 's sword-and-sorcery novel Conan the Buccaneer . Synopsis The pirate Conan finds himself on the Nameless Isle in the Western Ocean, home to a temple to Tsathoggua , one of the Great Old Ones . Behind the Mythos "" was adapted to the comic book format by Roy Thomas and featured artwork by John Buscema and Tony DeZuniga. It was published in issue #40 of The Savage Sword of Conan by Marvel Comics in May 1979.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#L._Sprague_de_Camp"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lin_Carter"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Roy_Thomas"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 1979</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Night_Music_of_Oakdeene">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Night Music of Oakdeene</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The horror at oakdeene is a short story by joseph s.pulver, sr . Written in may 1985, it was not published until may 1998 . It was later included in the anthology tales of the cthulhu mythos: cthulhu's creatures .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Joseph S. Pulver. Sr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Al-Azif</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>May 1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>May 1985</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chimera">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Chimera</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cultists plan to use the outer god yog-sothoth to open a gateway which will bring about the return of the old ones . An abused arkham youth enters a sleep-study program . Discovers that he is the herald of these beings by virtue of his bloodline . The boy takes up his birthright as a pair of enormous gates on a pacific island open .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sam_Gafford"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Return of the Old Ones: Apocalyptic Lovecraftian Horror</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nyaruko">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Avatars"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nyaruko</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Space Defense Agency</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nyarlathotep"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Crawling_Chaos"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as nyarlathotep is an extraterrestrial entity from the fictional universe of the haiyore! -san series . Was sent to earth to protect mahiro yasaka, a human boy that she fell in love with . Was the inspiration for the character " nyarlathotep " from h. p. lovecraft 's cthulhu mythos .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCharacterMateC>Mahiro Yasaka</ex:hasCharacterMateC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Haiyore! Nyaruko-san Vol. 1</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Koin</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Manta Aisora</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Salome_Gray">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Salome Gray</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Queen_of_Hearts"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as Queen of Hearts Powers and Abilities Street Smart Intellect Agility Unarmed Combat Skilled Motorcyclist Tattoo Artist Hero Monarch Human First appearance ADJ : Grimm Fairy Tales Presents: Call of Wonderland Vol 1 2 Created by Dan Wickline was created by Dan Wickline in 2012, first appearing in issue #2 of the 1st volume of Grimm Fairy Tales: Call of Wonderland by Zenescope Entertainment. Background is a tattoo artist that runs a tattoo parlor called Anarchy Ink. She and her friend Julie Sands were inadvertently drawn into an attempt by the forces of Wonderland to invade Earth. They vanquished their foes and returned to Earth, but were soon drawn back into Wonderland by the power of the Queen of Spades, who needed them to fill the role of the Queen of Hearts in Wonderland. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Grimm Fairy Tales Presents: Call of Wonderland Vol 1 2</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Dan Wickline</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tiamat_%28Fate/Grand_Order%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tiamat (Fate/Grand Order)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Tiamat (aka, beast ii and titan) is a cosmic entity from the fictional universe of the fate/grand order mobile game . She is the goddess of chaos, whom randomly designed life . Tiamat is a being who doesn't speak any human language .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dweller-in-Darkness">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dweller-in-Darkness</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Fear Lords</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>He Who Dwells in Darkness</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a demon, allegedly sired by cthulhu himself, hailing from the dimension everinnye . Like nightmare, the dweller draws strength and sustenance from the fear of living beings . In a previous universe, he caused a rupture in the m'kraan crystal with the aim of destroying the universe . The phoenix force intervened telepathically, thwarting the dweller's plan .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isLocatedAtC>Halls of Fear dimensions</ex:isLocatedAtC>
    <ex:isLocatedAtC>formerly Everinnye dimension</ex:isLocatedAtC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Gerry Conway</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Rich Buckler</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Miskatonic_River_Press">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DefunctRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SmallPress"/>
    <rdfs:label>Miskatonic River Press</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Current Status Defunct 2008 Tom Lynch Keith Herber United States Lovecraftian fiction Roleplaying games was an American independent publisher whose focus was Lovecraftian fiction and roleplaying game material. Overview Established in 2008 by Tom Lynch and Keith Herber , continued publishing until around 2014 , when Lynch (who had continued to run the company after Herber's death in 2009) announced its closure, citing lack of time as well as punishing shipping costs leading to loss of overseas sales as the culprits . Designers and authors who have provided material for include Oscar Rios , Scott David Aniolowski , Stephanie McAlea , Badger McInnes, Fred Behrendt , Kevin Ross , Gary Sumpter , Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. , W. H. Pugmire , and many others. Selected Publications Fiction Dead but Dreaming (2008) Dead but Dreaming 2 (2011) The Strange Dark One: Tales of Nyarlathotep (2012) A Season in Carcosa (2012) Deepest, Darkest Eden (2013) Gaming Material The Legacy of Arrius Lurco (2011) External Links at RPGGeek.com References</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Britannia_%26_Beyond">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Britannia &amp; Beyond</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Stuart Boon &amp; friends English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Golden Goblin Press Release Date September 27, 2023 is a licenced supplement for the 7th edition of Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu role-playing game, published by Golden Goblin Press in 2023. Overview is a setting guide detailing the Roman Province of Britannia and the barbarian lands of Caledonia (Scotland) and Hibernia (Ireland) for Cthulhu Invictus . Available in both PDF and print (softcover and hardcover) editions, it was written by Stuart Boon with William Adcock, Fred Norton, Oscar Rios and Richard Watts .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Stuart_Boon"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>friends</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2023 September 27</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Army_of_Darkness/Xena:_Why_Not%3F">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Army of Darkness/Xena: Why Not?</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Army of Darkness/Xena: Why Not? John Layman Miguel Montenegro English Dynamite Entertainment Release Date May 2008 - June 2008 Media Type Comic book Army Darkness/Xena: Why Not? is a four-issue comic book mini series written by John Layman in 2008 which was published by Dynamite Entertainment. Synopsis Ash Williams is transported thousands of years into the past, where he joins Xena and Gabrielle, as they battle the evil of the Necronomicon and a group of fairies controlled by an evil little Ash.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>John Layman</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 2008 - June 2008</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Comte_Godfrey_de_C--">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Comte Godfrey de C--</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Comte godfrey de c-- (no surname given) was a french count in the story " the alchemist " by h.p. Lovecraft . He was the son of comte henri , the father of robert , and an ancestor of comte antoine . When the young godfrey went missing, the blame was put on michel mauvais , an alchemist and black magician .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Alchemist"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cold_Print_%28Short_Story_Collection%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RamseyCampbellWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cold Print (Short Story Collection)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cold Print Ramsey Campbell English Genre(s) Horror Scream/Press (First Edition) Tor Books (Second Edition) Headline (Third Edition) Media type Print Publication date 1985 (First Edition) 1987 (Second Edition) 1993 (Third Edition) Cold Print , by British horror fiction Ramsey Campbell , is a collection of short stories either set in the expanded Cthulhu Mythos , which contain story elements linking them to the larger universe, or whose subject matter is in the same vein. It includes most of the stories found in Campbell's earlier collection The Inhabitant of the Lake and Less Welcome Tenants , with a number of additional stories added to the contents. Publication History First published in 1985 by Scream/Press, Cold Print saw a re-release in 1987 by Tor Books, and then an expanded edition featuring the three stories missing from The Inhabitant of the Lake and Less Welcome Tenants along with three other tales, issued by Headline in 1993. Contents " The Church in High Street " (1962) " The Room in the Castle " (1964) " The Horror from the Bridge " (1964) " The Insects from Shaggai " (1964) " The Render of the Veils " (1964) " The Inhabitant of the Lake " (1964) " The Will of Stanley Brooke " (1964) " The Moon-Lens " (1964) " Before the Storm " (1980) " Cold Print " (1969) " Among the pictures are these: " (1980) " The Tugging " (1976) " The Faces at Pine Dunes " (1980) " Blacked Out " (1984) " The Voice of the Beach " (1982) Included in the expanded edition: " The Plain of Sound " (1964) " The Return of the Witch " (1964) " The Mine on Yuggoth " (1964) " The Stone on the Island " (1964) " The Franklyn Paragraphs " (1973) " A Madness from the Vaults " (1972) Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ramsey_Campbell"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1985 (First Edition) 1987 (Second Edition) 1993 (Third Edition)</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Now">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Arkham Now</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Brian Courtemanche Matt Sanbourne David Grilla Andy Hopp Marco Morte Joe Rossomanno Cover Artist David Grilla United States English Call of Cthulhu Chaosium Release Date February 2010 Media Type Print Digital (PDF) 180 : Revisiting the Legend-Haunted City is a sourcebook for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game. Overview Containing information which allows a Keeper to present the city of Arkham as it exists in the modern day, includes information on all of the major regions of Lovecraft 's iconic city along with three playable scenarios set in the contemporary age. It was written by Brian Courtemanche and Matt Sanbourne with cover art by David Grilla and runs to a total of 180 . Chapters Introduction Welcome to Arkham Town Directory Northside Downtown East-Town Merchant Section Rivertown Miskatonic University Campus French Hill Uptown The Outskirts Lonely Hearts (Taste Great) Lost in a Book I Did What The Virgin Asked Publication History was first published in February 2010 and is available in both physical and digital (PDF) formats. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Courtemanche"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Matt Sanbourne</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>180</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>February 2010</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chapman_house">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Chapman house</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The house was located beyond meadow hill in an isolated position away from roads and other houses . For this reason herbert west and his friend used the house as a clandestine laboratory for their experiments involving the reanimation of the dead .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>House</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Herbert_West"/>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham"/>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Massachusetts"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Herbert_West--Reanimator"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Great_Intelligence_Institute">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DefunctOrganisationsFictional"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Organisation"/>
    <rdfs:label>Great Intelligence Institute</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationO>Great Intelligence</ex:hasAffiliationO>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The institute was founded in london during the 19th century by walter simeon . It was established with the goal of creating a physical form for the great intelligence . Took the institute decades to achieve its goal, resulting in the rising of snowmen on christmas eve, 1892 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNotableMemberO>Dr Walter Simeon</ex:hasNotableMemberO>
    <ex:hasPurposeO>To create a physical form for the Great Intelligence</ex:hasPurposeO>
    <ex:isLocatedAtO>2 Bloomsbury Lane</ex:isLocatedAtO>
    <ex:isLocatedAtO>London</ex:isLocatedAtO>
    <ex:wasCreatedBynO>Steven Moffat</ex:wasCreatedBynO>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ryleh">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Planets"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ry'leh</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAccessibilityL>Gate opened by chanting a certain spell</ex:hasAccessibilityL>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A fictional alien planet created by andy lane for his novel all-consuming fire . It is the location where the supposed " azathoth " has been imprisoned . The name of this planet is an obvious reference to the sunken city in which cthulhu and his spawn are imprisoned in the works of h.p. Lovecraft . The chanting of specific spells can open up a gateway between the area of known as the plain of leng and the earth</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>Purple ground</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>Solid ice sky</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Planet</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Balloon-like animals</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Carnivorous plants</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Tripedal giant rats</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#All-Consuming_Fire"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Terrors_%28Richard_A._Lupoff%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Terrors (Richard A. Lupoff)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Terrors is a collection of works by the late american richard a. lupoff . Contains both stories set in the cthulhu mythos and ones inspired by the works works of h.p. Lovecraft .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_A._Lupoff"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>360</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2005</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Wilbur_Whateley_Waiting">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dunwich"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Wilbur Whateley Waiting</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Revelations from yuggoth #1 was first published in the fanzine revelations from yuggoth #1 in november 1987 . It was later published in the anthologies the dunwich cycle: where the old gods wait (november 1995) and blasphemies &amp; revelations (november 2008)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_M._Price"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 1987</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ann_K._Schwader">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ann K. Schwader</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>In 2018 she was named a grand master poet by the science fiction poetry association, and she is a prolific writer of cthulhu mythos-related material . Her poetry and prose fiction has been published in several collections, including dark equinox and other tales of lovecraftian horror .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Wyoming</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Marie Kennedy Schwader</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://schwader.net/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Poet</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>23 February 1960</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ashcan_Pete">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>"Ashcan" Pete</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Drifter</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Pete washburne is a fictional character created by fantasy flight games for their arkham horror files line of cthulhu mythos content . He made his first appearance in the core set of arkham horror second edition .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isLocatedAtC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham"/>
    <ex:isLocatedAtC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Massachusetts"/>
    <ex:isLocatedAtC>USA</ex:isLocatedAtC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Horror_Second_Edition_Core_Set"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Kevin_Wilson"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_Launius"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Colder_War">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Colder War</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story originally appeared in spectrum sf no. 3 in 2000 . It explores the consequences of a follow-up to the expedition in h.p. Lovecraft's at the mountains of madness . The story has similarity to the later stross novel the atrocity archives .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Charles_Stross"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Spectrum SF</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>July 2000</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fear_of_the_Knight">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Fear of the Knight</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Carl Fox English Rogue Planet Press Swords Against Cthulhu II: Hyperborean Nights date January 2017 " " is a short story by Carl Fox , first published in the Rogue Planet Press anthology Swords Against Cthulhu II: Hyperborean Nights in 2017. Synopsis A recluse offers hospitality to a knight of an ancient order who is on his way to a gathering of his peers. When, shortly after, the knight returns horribly aged and disfigured, he recounts the story of a disastrous encounter with the disciples of Cthulhu , and how he barely escaped with his life. The story concludes with the knight passing and the hermit becoming aware that the cultists of Cthulhu are now watching him. Writing down the knight's tale, his existence passes from recorded history.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Carl_Fox"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Swords_Against_Cthulhu_II:_Hyperborean_Nights"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>January 2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Wamp">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Wamp</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sociocultural Characteristics Spawned in dead cities Dreamlands Real World Information First appearance HPL : The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath Created by H. P. Lovecraft Wamps are a species created by H. P. Lovecraft for his fantasy novel The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath . Description Wamps are web-footed creatures that are "spawned" in dead cities and roam the graveyards of the upper regions of the Dreamlands . The Ghouls prefer to avoid these areas and leave them to the Wamps ( HPL : The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath ), possibly implying some territorial rivalry between the two species. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dream-Quest_of_Unknown_Kadath"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Tindalos_Asset">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CaitlinRKiernanWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Tindalos Asset</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>174 preceded by black helicopters is a novella by caitlin r. kiernan . It focuses on a former assassin for an occult secret agency . The story draws on the work of h.p. Lovecraft and other mythos writers .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Caitlin_R._Kiernan"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>174</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tinfoil_Dossier"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2020 October 13</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tree-Man_of_Mbwa">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ServitorRaces"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tree-Man of M'bwa</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>M'bwa</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>The God of the Red Flux</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The tree-men of m'bwa are once-human creatures created by donald wandrei for his short story of the same name . They were the unfortunate victims of an evil undead being named m'bwa, an entity in service to the alien god of the red flux . They were forced to drink an elixir which would allow him to work on them uninterrupted, amputating their legs and grafting what remained onto the stumps of trees . These living humans would then slowly</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isLocatedAtC>A single valley in the Central African rainforests</ex:isLocatedAtC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Tree Men of M'bwa</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Donald_Wandrei"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mythos_Collector_Winter_2004">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Mythos Collector Winter 2004</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Allen Koszowski English Mythos Collector Dark Tree Press Release Date December 2004 56 Preceded by Spring 2003 was the sixth and final edition of the sporadically-released fanzine Mythos Collector . Edited by Brian Lingard, it featured cover art by Allen Koszowski , and was published by Dark Tree Press in December 2004 . Contents First Words by Brian Lingard "That Hideous Face" by Daniel J. Bishop "Festival in a Forbidden City" by Tim Curran "The Unholy Attendant" by Phil Roberts "On Yuggoth" by Lee Clark Zumpe Interview with Peter Scartabello by Kevin Dole 2 "Lovecraftian Audio" by Brian Lingard Auction Watch Reviews Contributor Bios References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>56</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mythos_Collector"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>December 2004</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ylla">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Y'lla</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Master of the Seas</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as Master of the Seas First appearance EXP : " The Deep-Lord Awakens " Created by James Ambuehl is the tentacled Sea-Worm who shares the sea with his sibling the bi-sexual Vhuzompha . It is a monstrous, barrel-shaped sea worm with tentacles and a lamprey-like mouth. , the titanic tentacled Sea-Worm, dwells within his sunken citadel of K'hraa beneath the waves.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Deep-Lord Awakens</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Ambuehl"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Whispering_Shadows">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Whispering Shadows</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Abomination of the is an eldritch abomination from the fictional universe of marvel by marvel comics . It inhabited an eldritch fortress in the black kingdoms of the hyborian age . While he slept a slaver band of 40 stygians took shelter in the fortress . The ghosts merged and used their lifeforces to create a physical body of numerous malformed heads, limbs, and tentacle-like appendages .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Hallowmas_1981">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu Hallowmas 1981</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Crypt of Cthulhu Michaelmas 1981 Cover Artist Robert M. Price United States English Crypt of Cthulhu Release Date November 1981 28 Followed by Yuletide 1981 was the inaugural issue of Robert M. Price 's long-running fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Edited by Price, who also provided the cover art, it ran to a total of 28 in an A5 format and was independently published in November 1981. The issue was reprinted in 1984. Contents Editorial Shards by Robert M. Price "Lovecraft's Concept of Blasphemy" by Robert M. Price "The Fun Guys from Yuggoth" by uncredited "Mail-Call of Cthulhu" by uncredited "Describing de Scribes" by uncredited Reviews by uncredited contributors</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>28</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>November 1981</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Miskatonic_Five">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Miskatonic Five</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>17 is a scenario supplement for chaosium's call of cthulhu horror roleplaying game . Written by matthew sincic and alex artang, and featuring artwork by andrew sincic . Published in a digital-only format through drivethrurpg as part of their miskatonic repository licensed player-driven initiative .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Alex Artang</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Matthew Sincic</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>17</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>DriveThruRPG</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Mindhouse">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Mindhouse</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>In a letter to her estranged sister, a woman incarcerated in a mental health facility tells how she has been cured of her acute schizophrenia by an unconventional doctor . The writer of the letter knows that the insanity which is being siphoned away by the doctor's procedures will one day spell the end of humanity .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Christine_Morgan"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dark_Rites_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 2014</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sligguth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sligguth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shuma-Gorath"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Mighty Scaled One</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Great One</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as The Great One Mighty Scaled One Relatives Father: Set Siblings: Dragon of the Moon , Yamata no Orochi , Ishiti , Phorcys , Tartessus , Damballah Offspring: Serpent Men , Dragon Kings Shuma-Gorath First appearance Marvel Premiere Vol 1 #5 Created by Ivy Wesley Gardner Fox is a fictional character from Marvel Comics . He was created in 1972 by Ivy Wesley and Gardner Fox, making his first appearance in issue #5 of the first volume of the Marvel Premier comic book series. Background was the offspring of the Elder God Set , who possessed superhuman durability and strength. He drained a victim's life force by generating a magical fungus which rapidly grew over the body. He was in the service of Shuma-Gorath , lord of the Great Old Ones .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Marvel Premiere Vol 1 #5</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Gardner Fox</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Ivy Wesley</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#H.P.L.:_A_Reminiscence">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>H.P.L.: A Reminiscence</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. Warner Munn English (original) German Genre(s) Non-fiction Whispers date 1976-1979 " " is a short non-fiction piece by the late American H. Warner Munn. Oddly, the first part of the work was included in the December 1976 edition of Whispers , but the magazine did not publish the second part until October 1979. It has since been reprinted a handful of times, including a translated version in a German- collection . History Whispers (1976-1979) Lovecraft Remembered (1998) Conversations with the Weird Tales Circle (2009) Cthulhu (2009, German collection) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>H. Warner Munn</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>German</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Whispers</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1976-1979</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lois_H._Gresh">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lois H. Gresh</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Lois Harriet Gresh Connection to the Mythos Author Editor Birth Date March 1956 Lois Harriet Gresh is an author and editor whose works fall primarily into the science fiction, weird fiction, and fantasy genres. She has written numerous Lovecraftian and Cthulhu Mythos stories, including some which cross over the world of Sherlock Holmes with that of H. P. Lovecraft . In addition to these, she has also written television tie-ins for franchises such as The X-Files , and has co-authored a non-fiction book on the Star Trek universe. Selected Works Editor Innsmouth Nightmares (2015) Author Cult of the Dead and Other Weird and Lovecraftian Tales (2015) " Devil's Bathtub " (2014) External Links at Wikipedia at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Lois Harriet Gresh</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>March 1956</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Justin_Geoffrey">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Justin Geoffrey</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Geoffrey came from a family of merchants with no interest in art or poetry . As a child, he went to sleep beside a long-abandoned, sinister-looking farmhouse . He was remembered by the villagers of stregoicavar as acting in an odd manner .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Thing_on_the_Roof"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Black_Stone"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_E._Howard"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Severnford">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Severnford</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Is a fictional community located on the real-world river severn, almost directly northwest of brichester . It's introduced in campbell's short story " the room in the castle ", and is described as a dull place to visit . In " potential ", a setting in which "the streets were lit by gas-lamps, reflected flickering in windows set in dark moist stone"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Town</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Severn_Valley"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Room_in_the_Castle"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Great_God_Pan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Great God Pan</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Arthur machen english and the inmost light is a horror novella telling of misogyny, miscegenation, rape and the crossing over of two worlds . The story is that of a woman violated by an extra-dimensional entity. She then gives birth to a daughter who is in fact the incarnated form of something from beyond . It is an experimental mix of science fiction and goth</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arthur_Machen"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The Inmost Light</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1894</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Litany_of_Earth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Litany of Earth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story centers on aphra marsh, a woman from innsmouth with deep one ancestry . After 17 years as a prisoner, she moves to san francisco . At the bookstore, she encounters a customer who asks for books by the likes of al-hazred and prinn . He seeks to recruit her to help the bureau investigate what are referred to as aeonists--worshippers of cthulhu and the</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ruthanna_Emrys"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>The Innsmouth Legacy</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2014</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Birth_of_Madness">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Birth of Madness</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Dave Franchini Renzo Rodriguez English Zenescope Entertainment Release Date May 2017 Media Type Comic book is a one-shot comic book written by Dave Franchini in 2017 which was published by Zenescope Entertainment. Synopsis Once upon a time, Wonderland was a peaceful realm of dreams. This is the story of how the Old One turned it into the realm of nightmares.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Dave Franchini</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 2017</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Nightmare_In_Delapore_Tower">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Nightmare In Delapore Tower</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A writer checks in to a high-rise hotel in order to finish his latest novel . While there, he discovers a dark secret: the delapores have been kidnapping people and eating their flesh . Although the evil lisa sends the rats to kill the writer her kindly yet mentally-deficient brother is able to turn the tables .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Michael Sheehan. Jr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Abominations: 24 Tales of the Cthulhu Mythos</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Horrors</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 2018</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Tale_of_Toad_Loop">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Tale of Toad Loop</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A man incarcerated in a sanatorium relates a tale from his youth . The story concerns the arrival of a man from innsmouth named mulltree who proceeds to buy up a parcel of land including toad loop . After he marries a local girl by the name of pritchy and puts her in the family way, he is witnessed summoning forth a dark entity .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Stanley_C._Sargent"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>January 1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Benjamin_Adams">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Benjamin Adams</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Editor Birth Date 1966 United States Horror Science fiction Bram Stoker Award (nominee) https://web.archive.org/web/20130424092450/http://www.sff.net/people/ben_adams/ (archive) (born 1966) is a Bram Stoker Award-nominated American author and editor based in Phoenix, Arizona, who has had a hand in a number of Lovecraftian and Cthulhu Mythos works. He has written tie-in pieces for the popular British science fiction franchise Doctor Who , as well as the Mythos-set roleplaying game Delta Green . Adams' Lovecraftian output includes editorial duties on the anthology The Children of Cthulhu (2002, Del Ray/Ballantine, with John Pelan ), and his short fiction has appeared in anthologies such as Singers of Strange Songs (1997, Chaosium ) and Delta Green: Dark Theatres (2001, Armitage House ), as well as the fanzine Cthulhu Codex . Selected Bibliography Editor The Children of Cthulhu (2002, with John Pelan ) Short Fiction "Second Movement" (1996) "Not to Force the Rhymes" (1997) "The Pain Eater" (1997) "Frank the Cnidarian" (1997) "Good Night, Bach Ma, Good-Bye" (2001) "That's the Story of My Life" (2002, with John Pelan ) "The Last Temptation of Ricky Perez" (2003) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://web.archive.org/web/20130424092450/http://www.sff.net/people/ben_adams/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1966</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Imagon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Imagon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Is a lovecraftian german- novel by michael marrak which takes its cue from h. p. lovecraft 's 1936 novella at the mountains of madness . It was first published by festa in a hardcover format as part of their h. p. lovecraft's bibliothek des schreckens</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Michael_Marrak"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>Germany</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>German</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecrafts_Bibliothek_des_Schreckens"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 2002</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#What_the_Moon_Brings">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>What the Moon Brings</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Story was first published in the national amateur in may 1923 . It is shorter than most of lovecraft's other short stories, and is essentially a fragment . It is based on one of lovecraft's dreams (a common technique of his)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The National Amateur</ex:isPublishedAsW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Challengers_of_the_Unknown">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Challengers of the Unknown</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>M'nagalah is a long-running comic series which had a two-part story featuring m'nagalah in issues #82-83 . The series was written by gerry conway and published by dc comics in 1977 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Gerry Conway</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>November 1977 (Issue #83) September 1977 (Issue #82)</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Edward_J._Lazare">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSFriendsAndAcquaintances"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Edward J. Lazare</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1991</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Friend of Hart Crane and Samuel Loveman; he later became a longtime editor of American Book-Prices Current (1940–1965).</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1904</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#D.Y.N._Freaks">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>D.Y.N. Freaks</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Jin haganeya soratokumo is a manga series written by jin haganeya . It is a spinoff of the demonbane series by nitroplus . Chronologically takes place after the novel zanma taisen demonbane .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Jin Haganeya</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Japanese</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 2013 - June 2014</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Scroll_of_Thoth_%28collection%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Scroll of Thoth (collection)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Scroll of Thoth (collection) Richard L. Tierney ( Robert M. Price , editor) Cover Artist H. E. Fassl English Genre(s) Cthulhu Mythos /Sword &amp; Sorcery Chaosium Release Date September 1997 369 The Scroll of Thoth: Simon Magus and the Great Old Ones is a collection of Richard L. Tierney 's Simon of Gitta stories published by Chaosium in 1997. Simon is a sword-and-sorcery hero who battles both Mythos horrors and the Roman Empire. The collection includes extensive commentary by editor Robert M. Price on the religious and mythological themes incorporated by Tierney into his tales. Contents "Introduction: Sword of the Avatar" (essay by Robert M. Price) " The Sword of Spartacus " (1978) " The Fire of Mazda " (1984) " The Seed of the Star-God " (1984) " The Blade of the Slayer " (1986) " The Soul of Kephri " (1984) " The Ring of Set " (1977) " The Worm of Urakhu " (1988) " The Curse of the Crocodile " (1987) " The Treasure of Horemkhu " (1987) " The Scroll of Thoth " (1977) " The Dragons of Mons Fractus " (1984) " The Pillars of Melkarth " (1990)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Richard L. Tierney ( Robert M. Price</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>editor)</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Sorcery</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Sword</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>369</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>September 1997</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Xiclotlan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonSapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Xiclotlan</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Xiclotlans are a carnivorous race of low intellect and great strength . They were enslaved by the insects of shaggai and used for labor until they left the planet . In 1994, chaosium incorporated them into the roleplaying game call of cthulhu .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Insects_from_Shaggai"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ramsey_Campbell"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Abdul_Alhazred">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Abdul Alhazred</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>731 ( AWD ) 738 ( HPL )</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Mad Arab The Mad Poet ( The Nameless City ) Abdool Al-Hazred ( The Case of Charles Dexter Ward )</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Alhazred is a recurring character in the works of h. p. lovecraft . He is the author of the fabled necronomicon , an occult text containing knowledge from beyond the earth . The term "mad arab" in reference to alhazred is always capitalized and used as a title .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The Hound The Festival The Descendant The Case of Charles Dexter Ward The Last Test History of the Necronomicon The Dunwich Horror The Whisperer in Darkness At the Mountains of Madness The Dreams in the Witch-House Through the Gates of the Silver Key Out of the Aeons The Thing on the Doorstep The Shadow Out of Time The Were-Snake The Nameless Offspring Black Lotus The Abyss The Keeper of the Key</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Nameless_City"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dr._Barnard">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dr. Barnard</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Albany, New York Psychiatrist Human First appearance HPL : " Beyond the Wall of Sleep " Created by H. P. Lovecraft is a psychiatrist from Albany, New York who examined and questioned Joe Slater after Slater was arrested for the murder of Peter Slader . ( HPL : " Beyond the Wall of Sleep ")</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Beyond_the_Wall_of_Sleep"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Xoxiigghua">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Xoxiigghua</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Great Old Ones</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Great Old Ones First appearance EXP : "Une Ombre Couleur Sépia (RPG adventure)" is a three-eyed, octopoid, parasitic entity trapped inside a Central American mountain range.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Une Ombre Couleur Sépia (RPG adventure)</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#All-Consuming_Fire">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crossovers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>All-Consuming Fire</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The novel is a three-way crossover between the worlds of the three franchises . The bulk of the narrative is presented as a journal written by dr. john watson . Some chapters are taken from the journal instead of watson's, and some brief interludes are narrated by ace .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Andy Lane</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United Kingdom</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Science fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>New Adventures ( Doctor Who )</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1994</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
