  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Jewels_of_Arachne">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Jewels of Arachne</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Maria Mitchell English James Ward Kirk Publishing Lovecraft after Dark date November 2015 " " is a Cthulhu Mythos poem by American Maria Mitchell . It was first published in the James Ward Kirk thematic anthology Lovecraft after Dark in November 2015. Synopsis In the land of Leng , a being known as Arachne is imprisoned for an unspeakable crime. She is later freed by the Great Old One Cthulhu and the two mate, producing an army of unspeakable offspring. History Lovecraft after Dark (2015) Buried (2018)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Maria_Mitchell"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_after_Dark"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Prank_Call_of_Cthulu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crossovers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosFilmAndTelevision"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Prank Call of Cthulu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The grim adventures of billy &amp; mandy is the sixth episode of the fourth season of the animated comedy series . It pits the protagonists against a monster named "cthulhu" without the second "h" in the episode . The story was conceived by series creators, along with alex almaguer, and mike diederich .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2005 October 21</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Reminiscence_of_Dr._Samuel_Johnson">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Reminiscence of Dr. Samuel Johnson</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"dagonsweet ermengarde" is a short story written in 1917 by horror fiction writer h. p. lovecraft . The story is a spoof of lovecraft's antiquarian affectations . It was first published in the september 1917 issue of the united amateur .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Yuletide_1987">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu Yuletide 1987</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Guy Cowlishaw United States English Crypt of Cthulhu Cryptic Publications Release Date December 1987 64 Preceded by Hallowmas 1987 Followed by Candlemas 1988 is the 52nd issue of leading H. P. Lovecraft scholar Robert M. Price 's long-running fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Edited by Price and featuring cover art by Guy Cowlishaw, it ran to a total of 64 in an A5 format, it was published by Cryptic Publications in December 1987 . The issue was a special devoted to fiction featuring a literary incarnation of H. P. Lovecraft . Contents Editorial Shards by Robert M. Price "The Volume Out of Print" by Jim Cort "Midnight in Providence" by Charles Garofalo "Howard Lovecraft and the Terror from Beyond" by Robert M. Eber "The Man Who Collected Lovecraft" by Philip Weber "Christmas with Uncle Lovecraft" by Bruce J. Balfour "Lovecraft As a Character in Lovecraftian Fiction" by Robert M. Price "From the Vaults of Yoh-Vombis" by Lin Carter "The Jabberwock" by Lin Carter "The Yeti" by Lin Carter "The Keeper at the Crypt" by Carl T. Ford "The Magazine Mythos, 1986-1987" by Stefan Dziemianowicz ""Dancehall" Church Razed" by Marc A. Cerasini Reviews by Stefan Dziemianowicz and Robert M. Price Letters by Shawn Ramsey , Meldrum Hawkins, Frank Belknap Long , Dick Fawcett, Tom Rathgeber, Darrell Schweitzer , Robert Bloch , Allen Koszowski , Charles Garofalo, Stefan Dziemianowicz, James Rockhill, David E. Schultz , Lin Carter, and Tani Jantsang References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>64</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>December 1987</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecrafts_At_the_Mountains_of_Madness_for_Beginning_Readers">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Storybooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>H. P. Lovecraft's At the Mountains of Madness for Beginning Readers</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft (original story) R. J. Ivankovic (adaptation) R. J. Ivankovic English Genre(s) Children's Chaosium Release Date December 14, 2024 142 is a children's book, published by Chaosium in 2024. It was written and illustrated by R. J. Ivankovic as an adaptation of H. P. Lovecraft 's At the Mountains of Madness , done in the style of a Dr. Seuss book. Overview</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>R. J. Ivankovic</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Children's</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>142</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2024 December 14</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Laird_Barron">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Laird Barron</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Laird Samuel Barron Connection to the Mythos Author Birth Date 1970 Palmer, Alaska, USA United States Years Active 2001 - present https://lairdbarron.wordpress.com/ Laird Samuel Barron (born 5 March 1970) is an American author, poet and occasional editor. A former fisherman, his professional career began in 2001, and he has since contributed numerous works to the Cthulhu Mythos inspired by the writings of H. P. Lovecraft and many others. Selected Bibliography Short Fiction " Strident Caller " (2015) External Links at Wikipedia at The Internet Speculative Fiction Database</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Alaska Palmer</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Laird Samuel Barron</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://lairdbarron.wordpress.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:hasYearsActiveR>2001 - present</ex:hasYearsActiveR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1970</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#William_Channing_Webb">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>William Channing Webb</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>American Anthropology Society</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Professor of anthropology at Princeton University American Anthropology Society Human First appearance HPL : " The Call of Cthulhu " Created by H. P. Lovecraft is a professor of anthropology at Princeton University and "an explorer of no slight note." When Inspector John Raymond Legrasse conferred with a meeting of the American Anthropology Society about the Cthulhu Cult, Professor Webb was the only member of the assembly to be familiar with an idol found during the raid and the ritualistic chants used by the cult, based on his investigation of a "singular tribe or cult of degenerate Esquimaux" he encountered "high up on the West Greenland coast" in 1860. ( HPL : " The Call of Cthulhu ")</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Call_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fireworm">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Fireworm</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sociocultural Characteristics Forest of Parg, the Dreamlands Woodland Physical Characteristics Average length 6 to 40 feet Skin colour Armoured Real World Information First appearance EXP : H. P. Lovecraft's Dreamlands EXP : S. Petersen's Field Guide to Creatures of the Dreamlands Created by Sandy Petersen Fireworms are a species of creature which feature in the Call of Cthulhu Mythos -based roleplaying game. Overview Originating in the Parg forests of the extra-dimensional realm known as the Dreamlands , Fireworms are a species of large armoured annelid whose biology seems to revolve around the utilisation of extremely high temperatures, emitting occasional puffs of smoke from their body and even possessing the ability to burst into flames on command. They have a fifty-year breeding cycle, and during these periods infest the land in a frenzy of destruction before seemingly disappearing for a full decade, possibly entering a state of hibernation or simply hiding away while their young gestate. Behind the Mythos Fireworms were created by Sandy Petersen for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game supplement H. P. Lovecraft's Dreamlands in 1986. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#S._Petersens_Field_Guide_to_Creatures_of_the_Dreamlands"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecrafts_Dreamlands"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sandy_Petersen"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mob_Hit">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Mob Hit</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here Boyd E. Pearson English Edward P. Berglund Nightscapes Nightscapes December 1997 date December 1997 " " is a humorous Cthulhu Mythos poem by Boyd E. Pearson . It was published in the December 1997 edition of Edward P. Berglund 's online fanzine Nightscapes . Synopsis The three eight-line verses toy with the idea of mob hitmen who are also cultists of the supernatural entities Dagon , Nyarlathotep , and Yog-Sothoth .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Boyd_E._Pearson"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Nightscapes Nightscapes December 1997</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 1997</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Vhoorl">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Planets"/>
    <rdfs:label>Vhoorl</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Twenty-Third Nebula Planet First appearance CIRCLE : " The Guardian of the Book " Created by Henry Hasse is a planet in the "Twenty-Third Nebula" ( CIRCLE : " The Guardian of the Book ") and supposed birthplace of Cthulhu ( EXP : " The Horror in the Gallery "). Background In "The Horror in the Gallery", by Lin Carter , it is claimed that Cthulhu is the offspring of Yog-Sothoth , who mated with a female denizen of the planet . However, this information contradicts the genealogical charts created by H. P. Lovecraft and Clark Ashton Smith , in which Cthulhu is the offspring of Nug (in Lovecraft's version) or Cxaxukluth (in Smith's version). In Other Media In the Transformers multiverse, is a neutral planet in the Dark Nebula. ( ADJ : Transformers Animated: The AllSpark Almanac II )</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Planet</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>The Twenty-Third Nebula</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Guardian_of_the_Book"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jabberwocky">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CharactersIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Jabberwocky</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is based on the nonsense poem written by lewis carroll in 1871 about the killing of a creature which was called the "jabberwock" it was included in his story through the looking-glass, and what alice found there . In 2007, zenescope entertainment created a great old one called the , which first appeared in issue #16 of the 1st volume of the grimm fairy tales comic series .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Demonbane"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Grimm Fairy Tales Vol 1 16</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Through the Looking-Glass</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>What Alice Found There</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Lewis Carroll</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gou_Tanabe">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gou Tanabe</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Manga creator known for adapting the stories of h.p. Lovecraft into comic book format . Manga has been translated into english, french, german, spanish and italian . Lovecraft's characters "lose all hope and appetite for life," creator says .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>Japan</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>Japanese</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Oscar_Rios">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Oscar Rios</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Games Designer Author Horror Fantasy is an author, editor and games designer. Overview Although has written both fantasy and Lovecraftian fiction, his largest output to date has been for Chaosium 's seminal Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game, for which he has supplied material for all of the different time periods which that game supports. In addition to working for Chaosium, Rios has also written material for third-party content providers such as Stygian Fox and Golden Goblin Press , the latter of which he is the president. Selected Works Editor Island of Ignorance: The Third Cthulhu Companion (2013) Tales of the Crescent City (2014, with Lisa Padol) Heroes of Red Hook (2016, with Brian M. Sammons ) Further Tales of Cthulhu Invictus (2017, with Brian M. Sammons) Cold Warning (2017) Children of Lovecraft Country (2020, with Brian M. Sammons) An Eldritch Legacy (2020, with Brian M. Sammons) Tales of Cthulhu Invictus: Britannia (2020, with Brian M. Sammons) Short Fiction "Men and Women" (2016) Gaming Supplements Extrico Tabula (2008) The Legacy of Arrius Lurco (2011) Atomic-Age Cthulhu (2013) Island of Ignorance: The Third Cthulhu Companion (2013, contributor) Tales of the Sleepless City (2013, contributor) Tales of the Crescent City (2014) Ripples from Carcosa (2014) De Horrore Cosmico (2015, contributor) Tales of the Caribbean (2016, editor, contributor) Fronti Nulla Fides (2019, contributor) An Inner Darkness (2020) The Eldritch New England Holiday Collection (2021) New Tales of the Miskatonic Valley - An Anthology of Call of Cthulhu Scenarios set in the 1920s (2008) More Adventures in Arkham Country (2010) The Mystery of April Snow An Inner Darkness (2020, contributor) Heroes and Heroines of Britannia Heroes and Heroines of the Shadow War Britannia &amp; Beyond (2023)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games Designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#At_the_Mountains_of_Madness_and_Other_Novels">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AugustDerlethWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>At the Mountains of Madness and Other Novels</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft United States English Genre(s) Horror Arkham House Release Date 1964 Media Type Print is an anthology of stories by H. P. Lovecraft . Publication History First released in 1964 by Arkham House , collects eight of Lovecraft’s works together with an introduction by friend and contemporary August Derleth , with this edition receiving four reprints. It was later revised in 1986 by S. T. Joshi , with the new edition replacing Derleth's introduction with two new ones, one by Joshi himself and another by Jim Turner . This version, titled the "corrected printing" was reissued a further four times. Contents At the Mountains of Madness The Case of Charles Dexter Ward " The Shunned House " " The Dreams in the Witch-House " " The Statement of Randolph Carter " " The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath " " The Silver Key " " Through the Gates of the Silver Key " Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1964</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Harrison_Marsh">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Harrison Marsh</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Harrison is a character created by american author armand rosellia for his keyport cthulhu series of mythos tales . Although he comes from innsmouth stock, he is considered of "weak blood" by those of the esoteric order of dagon .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCharacterMateC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nicole_Marsh"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ancient"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Armand_Rosamilia"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Kenneth_Hite">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonFictionAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Kenneth Hite</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Games designer Author Birth Date 15 September 1965 Tulsa, Oklahoma, USA United States https://princeofcairo.livejournal.com/ 𝐓 (born 15 September 1965) is an American author and games designer who has written numerous works set in and around the Cthulhu Mythos . Perhaps best known for his roleplaying game material, which has won him a number of awards from the community, his credits include Pelgrane Press 's Trail of Cthulhu , and White Wolf Publishing's Vampire: the Masquerade . His Cthulhu Mythos fiction has appeared in anthologies such as Cthulhurotica (2011, Dagan Books) and Shotguns v. Cthulhu (2012, Pelgrane Press), and from 2007 to 2012 he wrote a column for the resurrected pulp magazine Weird Tales . Selected Works Short Fiction "Infernal Devices" (2012) Children's Books The Mini Mythos series: Where the Deep Ones Are (2008) The Antarctic Express (2009) Cliffourd the Big Red God (2011) Goodnight Azathoth (2015) Games Trail of Cthulhu (2008) Gaming Supplements Achtung! Cthulhu: Keeper's Guide to the Secret War (2013, contributor) Rough Magicks (2009) Shadows Over Filmland (2009, with Robin D. Laws ) Mythos Expeditions (2018) Non-Fiction " Cthulhu's Polymorphous Perversity " (2011) Miscellaneous Stealing Cthulhu (2011, annotations) External Links at Wikipedia at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads at RPGGeek</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Oklahoma Tulsa</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://princeofcairo.livejournal.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>15 September 1965</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Skull-Face">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RobertEHowardWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Skull-Face</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Wt is a fantasy novella by robert e. howard, originally serialized in weird tales in 1929 . The story shares several themes with " the call of cthulhu ", and the character has been incorporated into the expanded mythos .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_E._Howard"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 1929</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#X-Men:_Soul_Killer">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>X-Men: Soul Killer</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Richard Lee Byers Leonardo Manco Cover Artist Richard Corben English Genre(s) Superhero Berkley Release Date February 1, 1999 Media Type Paperback Novel is a mass market Marvel universe tie-in novel written by American Richard Lee Byers . Featuring cover art by Richard Corben and an interior plate by Leonardo Manco, it was published in a paperback format in February 1999 by Berkley Boulevard , and has yet to be re-released . Synopsis The X-Men , Excalibur, and Dracula join forces to stop the demon sorcerer Belasco and his army of vampires and demons before they bring about the return of the N'Garai . References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_Lee_Byers"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Superhero</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1999 February 1</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#In_the_Vale_of_Pnath">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>In the Vale of Pnath</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>In hyperborea, the wizard eibon tries to obtain the ingredients for the powerful ygthar elixir that he learned about from the scrolls of zon mezzamalech . But he cannot complete the recipe without the enigmatic "glund fluid" the nature of which is unknown to him .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lin_Carter"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1975</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fisher_from_Outside">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ServitorRaces"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Fisher from Outside</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Groth-Golka"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Dark Flyers</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Demon Birds</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Devil Skewers</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Pterodactyls</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Slaughterers</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The fishers from outside are entities that serve the great old one groth-golka . They are sometimes confused with shantaks and even prehistoric pterosaurs . Cultists of groth-golka regard the fishers as the manifestation of their god's will .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Winged Death The Horror from the Hills</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>Single eye</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>Single leg</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>Wings</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>foot</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Outpost"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dendrrah">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OuterGods"/>
    <rdfs:label>D'endrrah</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Divinity</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The divinity is a shape shifting outer god created by pierre zaplotny for "la terza bocca di " on the licensed call of cthulhu roleplaying game website tentacules.net . Often manifests in the form of a gigantic squid-like monster or a cloud of heavy, smoky, organic matter with millions of teeth .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>La Terza Bocca di D'endrrah</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Pierre Zaplotny</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lauvabrarc">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lauv'abrarc</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Lusst'ghaa's Trinity</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The God-King The Lustful God</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The god-king of lusst'ghaa, also known as the lustful god, is the god-king of lusst'ghaa . He is deeply tied to the eldritch energies of lusst'ghaa as one of the trinity and revered by the cult of ecstasy . It is a symbol of the universe's core themes of desire, transformation, and the thin line between ecstasy and horror</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Lust Universe franchise</ex:hasAppearanceC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Fall_of_Cthulhu_Volume_II">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Fall of Cthulhu Volume II</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various English Horrified Press Release Date June 2017 184 Preceded by The Fall of Cthulhu is an anthology of Lovecraftian fiction edited by Douglas Draa and released in June 2015. Unusually, it was published by Horrified Press , the parent company of specialty imprint Rogue Planet Press , who normally issue their Lovecraftian releases through this label. Overview Contents "The Heart of Heka" by Craig E. Sawyer "The Guards of the Stone" by Carl Thomas Fox "Crawling Chaos Theory" by Kevin Wetmore "From Cronus to Cthulhu" by John Kaneicki "Innocence and Blood" by Kevin Henry "Legacy" by Josh Phillips "Perchance to Sleep Perchance to Dream" by Owen Morgan "Dreams of Dead Gods" by Matthew Wilson "Scary World" by Spencer Carvalho "Showdown at Port Innsmouth" by Phil Morgan "Strange and Distant Shores" by James Pratt "The Condo Over in Innsmouth" by Stephen R. Wilk "The Crimson Trail" by L. W. Underwood "The End of the Others" by Charie D. La Marr "The Eye of Cthulhu" by Neal Privett "From Beyond Came the Key" by Eric Tarango "The Shoggulators" by Benjamin Sperduto "Trach's Death Ray" by Benjamin Welton "The Rain in Eksley" by Brian Hamilton "The Summoning" by Jason S. Aiken "The Speaker" by Taylor Foreman-Niko "Mosaic" by Mark Sims "The City of Stars" by Robert Trska "The Crossing of the United" by Jacob Farley Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>184</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>June 2017</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Moon-Lens">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RamseyCampbellWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Moon-Lens</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ramsey Campbell United Kingdom English (original) German (translation) Genre(s) Weird fiction Arkham House The Inhabitant of the Lake and Less Welcome Tenants date 1964 is a short Cthulhu Mythos story by British Ramsey Campbell . It was first published in the 1964 Arkham House collection The Inhabitant of the Lake and Less Welcome Tenants . Synopsis When Roy Leakey visits Exham in pursuit of his passion - second-hand books - he is trapped by a cult devoted to Shub-Niggurath . After a horrifying experience in the Outer God 's temple, he finds himself transformed both physically and mentally, with none able to help him ease his pain. History The Inhabitant of the Lake and Less Welcome Tenants (1964, 2011, 2013, 2018) Cold Print (1985, 1987, 1993) The Shub-Niggurath Cycle: Tales of the Black Goat With a Thousand Young (1994) Der Wahnsinn aus der Gruft (2014, German- collection)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ramsey_Campbell"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United Kingdom</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>German</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Inhabitant_of_the_Lake"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Less Welcome Tenants</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1964</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Horror_Fiction">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Arkham Horror Fiction</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A series of novels, short stories and art books set in the arkham horror iteration of lovecraft's cthulhu mythos . Began as a handful of novel-length publications of fiction . Has since expanded in scope to encompass an ongoing series of hardback novellas . Each comes with a sixteen-page full-colour insert containing visualisations of some of the various artefacts, locations and equipment employed by the character .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fantasy_Flight_Games">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Publishers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Fantasy Flight Games</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Fantasy flight is a relatively new games developer and publisher from the united states . Their primary interest to this wiki is their arkham horror files line of products, which deal with games and fiction related to h.p. Lovecraft's cthulhu mythos . In 2004 they secured the publication rights for a re-release of the 1987 game of lovecraftian monster-hunting arkham horror .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dagon_January-March_1989">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dagon January-March 1989</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various United Kingdom English Dagon Press Release Date January 1989 58 is the twenty-fourth issue of the now-defunct Lovecraftian fanzine Dagon . It was edited by the late British and occasional Carl T. Ford . Contents (incomplete) Editorial by Carl T. Ford "Crawling at You" by uncredited "Lovecraft in the Comic Books" by Randy M. Wadkins "The Red Brain's Trust" by Peter F. Jeffery "S.T. Joshi: Re-editor" interview with S. T. Joshi by Will Murray "Wild Beasts: A Call of Cthulhu Scenario" by Penelope Love "Digory Smalls" by D. F. Lewis Reviews Letters</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United Kingdom</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>58</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>January 1989</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Evil_Clergyman">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Evil Clergyman</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The horror in the burying-ground is an excerpt from a letter written by american horror fiction writer h. p. lovecraft in 1933 . The story was later adapted into the unreleased 1987 anthology film pulse pounders . The story was published in the april 1939 issue of weird tales as a short story .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_Alive%21">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lovecraft Alive!</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lovecraft Alive! John Shirley Cover Artist Harry O. Morris English Hippocampus Press Release Date August 2016 251 Lovecraft Alive! is a collection of works by American John Shirley . It was published by Hippocampus Press in August 2016, in both paperback and digital editions. Overview Contents "How Deep the Taste of Love" (1993) "Buried in the Sky" (2006) "Those Who Came to Dagon" (2007) "When Death Wakes Me to Myself" (2012) "At Home with Azathoth" (2014) " The Witness in Darkness " (2014) "Windows Underwater" (2015) "The Rime of the Cosmic Mariner" (2016) "The Holy Grace of Cthulhu" (2016) "Broken on the Wheel of Time" (2016)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#John_Shirley"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>251</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Doctor">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Doctor</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Doctor Who John Smith Jane Smith Sarah Jane Smith A Madman with a Box Ancient One Doktor of TARDIS Sir Doctor of TARDIS Dark Lord Bringer of Darkness Lazarus Star Traveller Fate's Accomplice Time's Champion Life's Champion Doctor Know-All Dalek Killer Evergreen Man Time Lord Victorious The Professional The Umbrella Man The Scottish Doctor Theta Sigma The Oncoming Storm The Destroyer of Worlds The Traveller from Beyond Time The Warrior The Lord of Death The Predator of the Daleks The Deathbringer The Great Scourge The Living Death The Executioner The Renegade The Doctor of War The Butcher of Skull Moon The Final Judgement The Cosmic Hobo The Comedian The Great Healer The Time Warrior The Bohemian</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>He Whose Name Dare Not Be Mentioned The Destroyer of Skaro The Evil Renegade The Prophet The Great Destroyer The Heathen The Hero The Imp of the Pandorica The Beast of Trenzalore The Demon The Stranger The Great Destruction of the Universe The Zonewalker The Shadow-thief The Trickster of Time The Vessel of The Final Darkness The Roaring Winds of Righteousness The Shadow of the Valeyard The Last Tree of Garsennon The Uniter of the Thirteen Worlds of the Hoomis Collective The Smiter of the Frangian Simulacra The Aathkeeper of Valnis The Mighty Lord of TAR-DIS</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Wanderer The Evil One The Wizard The Boggle-Eyed Demon The Dark One</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Doctor of tardis is the main alias of a space and time traveling alien, the protagonist in the fictional doctor who universe, who was created by sydney newman in 1963 . He is a mysterious alien scientist and renegade time lord who has vowed to do no harm and to help others . He is a time traveler, scientist, adventurer, soldier, explorer time lord .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>White Darkness All-Consuming Fire</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>An Unearthly Child</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Sydney Newman</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Paramental">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Paramental</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Paramentals are elemental spirits which manifest through inanimate materials such as glass, concrete and steel. They are described in the novella Our Lady of Darkness by Fritz Leiber . Paramentals are not friendly, and to become aware of them is not a good thing. They are described as "about midway in nature between the atomic bomb and the archetypes of the collective unconscious." Notable Individuals The antagonist in Our Lady of Darkness goes by the name of " Taffy ".</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraftiana_Vol_2,_Issue_2">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Periodicals"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lovecraftiana Vol 2, Issue 2</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Gwabryel English Rogue Planet Press Release Date July 2017 60  is an issue of the periodical Lovecraftiana , edited by Gavin Chappell and published by Rogue Planet Press in July 2017. Contents "In Darkness" by Lee Glenwright "Realm of Nazaro" by Dean Wirth "With Strange Aeons" by Mike Adamson "The Lost Poems of Pnakotus" by Mathias Jansson "Pthfthf The Not-So-Great One" by Columbkill Noonan "The Order" by Colt Leasure "County Library" by David B Harrington "Dreams of Dead Men" by Matthew Wilson</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>60</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 2017</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Narrathoth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Narrathoth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Created by robert silverberg is a fictional extradimensional entity created by silverberg for his short story " demons of cthulhu ". Description is an entirely white creature covered in scales. It is larger than a man, and has a bulging head with a single three-lobed eye. It describes itself as "one of the lurkers at the threshold of the universe", and as one of the followers of cthulhu .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Demons_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_Silverberg"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Book_of_Ending">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ArtefactsOriginatingFromMythosAdjacentWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosBooksFictional"/>
    <rdfs:label>Book of Ending</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesA>The Book of Beginning</ex:hasAlternativeNamesA>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The book is a fictional eldritch book created by vertigo comics for their miniseries the trenchcoat brigade . It was held by the ukrainian leshy whose city was protected by the "eye of the world", which hid them from the world .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnA rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Trenchcoat_Brigade"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Diabolique">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Diabolique</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A demon/human hybrid that has ties to both the darkhold and the cult of the darkhold . She is constantly seeking out surrogate parents, who she later kills when she decides to move on to a new set of parents . At full power, she can open dimensional portals, manipulate and absorb hellfire, tear apart victims, mentally control others, and practice sympathetic magic (e.g. Voodoo dolls)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>1993</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Darkhold: Pages from the Book of Sins Vol 1 9</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Chris Cooper</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Rurik Tyler</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Venusian_%28In_the_Walls_of_Eryx%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Venusian (In the Walls of Eryx)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Man-lizards</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Venusians are a reptilian alien race that live in cities and towers in the planets thick jungles . They are said to communicate telepathically with their four chest tentacles . Despite being initially peaceful they now defend their orbs with swords and poison darts .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>Tapir-like snouts</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#In_the_Walls_of_Eryx"/>
    <ex:usesLanguageC>They communicate telepathically with their four chest tentacles</ex:usesLanguageC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Charles_Fort">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AlterEgosCharactersIncorporatedFromTheRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsInspirationsAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonFictionAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Charles Fort</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>3 May 1932</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Charles hoy fort was an american author famous for his non-fiction books that document unexplained phenomena . His works dealt upon the subject of extraterrestrial visitors and undiscovered animals . His influence was such that the word "fortean" has become virtually synonymous with the unknown .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>New York USA Albany</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Charles Hoy Fort</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>6 August 1874</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Troll">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ServitorRaces"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Troll</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Real World Information First appearance EXP : " Nightmare in Norway " Created by Marcus L. Rowland Trolls are a semi-human species from Norse mythology which were used by Chaosium for the Call of Cthulhu role-playing game. Description Trolls are a dwarfish species relative to humans that dwell in glacial caverns. Their society is tribal and worships Tsathoggua . Some Trolls are granted the ability to summon blizzards by said god.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nightmare_in_Norway"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Marcus_L._Rowland"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Sorcery_of_Aphlar">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Sorcery of Aphlar</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The wizard aphlar is exiled from the city of bel-haz-en for spending too much time alone . He moves to the mountains and becomes a cave-dwelling hermit, but continues to study the scrolls he brought with him . One day, seven assassins arrive, only to find the cave empty, with no sign of aphlar .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Duane_W._Rimel"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Fantasy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Fantasy_Fan"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 1934</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Apocalypse_Machine">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Apocalypse Machine</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Graham Walmsley Cover Artist Alessandro Alaia English Trail of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Pelgrane Press Release Date September 2011 72 is a gamebook supplement for the Cthulhu Mythos roleplaying game Trail of Cthulhu . Written by Graham Walmsley , it runs to a total of 72 and was published digitally by Pelgrane Press in September 2011. The contents were also included in the collection Cthulhu Apocalypse , also by Pelgrane, in 2015. Overview</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Graham_Walmsley"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>72</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Trail_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>September 2011</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Polaris_%28star%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsIncorporatedFromTheRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Stars"/>
    <rdfs:label>Polaris (star)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesL>North Star</ex:hasAlternativeNamesL>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesL>Pole Star</ex:hasAlternativeNamesL>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Polaris is a star in the northern circumpolar constellation ursa minor . Polaris is the brightest star in the constellation, and is also known as the north star or pole star . Its position is less than 1° away from the north celestial pole of rotation .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Polaris"/>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Star</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Northern sky (Ursa Minor)</ex:isLocatedAtL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Creature_in_the_Waxworks:_Tales_of_Weird_%26_Lovecraftian_Horror">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Creature in the Waxworks: Tales of Weird &amp; Lovecraftian Horror</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mark McLaughlin Michael Sheehan, Jr. English Genre(s) Horror Release Date 2017 78 is a collection of short stories by authors Mark McLaughlin and Michael Sheehan, Jr. Overview Consisting of six stories and running to a total of 78 , is a digital-only collection available through Amazon's Kindle platform. It was published in 2017. Contents " The Surprising Sweetness Of Their Blood " " The Creature In The Waxworks " "Nobody Goes To Paw Paw Town Any More" (non-Lovecraftian mythological horror) " The Gateway to Carcosa " "Aunt Paloma" (non-Lovecraftian monster horror " The Glorious Return Of Herbert West "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Michael Sheehan. Jr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>78</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2017</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Erlik_Khan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CharactersIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Erlik Khan</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shuma-Gorath"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Lord of the Thirty Infernos</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Peter b. gillis is a fictional character in the marvel universe who first appeared in 1987 in issue #6 of the 2nd volume of the strange tales comic book series . He was one of the agents of shuma gorath , the lord of the great old ones .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Strange Tales Vol. 2 #6</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Peter B. Gillis</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Terry Shoemaker</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Daily_Grind">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Daily Grind</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>J. Childs-Biddle English Simian Publishing Apotheosis: Stories of Human Survival After the Rise of the Elder Gods date October 2015 " " is a Lovecraftian short story by J. Childs-Biddle. It was published in the Jason Andrew -edited thematic anthology Apotheosis: Stories of Human Survival After the Rise of the Elder Gods by Simian Publishing in October 2015. Synopsis The story charts a day in the life of a psychiatrist who treats people who have glimpsed what lies beneath the veneer of reality which coats our world. What nobody knows is that she has an extensive knowledge about that dark truth, and now must work for the forces which lurk just out of sight to keep the masses ignorant and compliant, for if she does not, a horrible fate awaits her and her loved ones.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>J. Childs-Biddle</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Apotheosis:_Stories_of_Human_Survival_After_the_Rise_of_the_Elder_Gods"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#KaajhKaalbh">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OuterGods"/>
    <rdfs:label>Kaajh'Kaalbh</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Azathoth"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Chaos azathoth is a servant of the outer god azathoth . First appeared in philip josé farmer 's 1998 short story " the freshman " summoned by black magic and has no true stable form .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Freshman"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Philip_Jos%C3%A9_Farmer"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Matt_Ruff">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Matt Ruff</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Matthew theron ruff is a writer of genre fiction . He is the author of lovecraft country (2016) and its sequel, the destroyer of worlds: a return to lovecraft country (2023) he attended stuyvesant high school, where one of his teachers was the writer .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>http://example.org/lovecraft#New_York_City</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Matthew Theron Ruff</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://bymattruff.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isKnownForR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_Country"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1965 September 8</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hallowed_Rock">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Hallowed Rock</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Franklyn Searight English Tenoka Press In Lovecraft's Shadow date March 2007 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos story by the late American Franklyn Searight . It was published in the first issue of Ron Shiflet 's short-lived Lovecraftian fanzine In Lovecraft's Shadow by Tenoka Press in March 2007. Synopsis The story is told from the point of view of a sacrificial altar, located somewhere deep in the backwoods, which has long been used as a place of worship by the cult of Nyarlathotep , and has become sentient due to the release of so much dark energy. After a period of long and lonely disuse, the rock perceives to its delight the return of the cultists with a fresh victim; it’s elation is short lived however, as the cultists notice a small tree in the shape of a cross which has begun to grow from the rock. Believing this to be an ill omen, the group departs the clearing, leaving the rock to its eternal solitude.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Franklyn_Searight"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#In_Lovecrafts_Shadow"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 2007</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#What_Songs_We_Sing">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>What Songs We Sing</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>L. K. Whyte English Simian Publishing Apotheosis: Stories of Human Survival After the Rise of the Elder Gods date October 2015 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos story by L. K. Whyte. It was included in the thematic anthology Apotheosis: Stories of Human Survival After the Rise of the Elder Gods by Simian Publishing in October 2015. Synopsis In a future where humankind lives in a series of shanty-towns and are kept in servitude through the use of a mind-altering drug, Ikere meets a mysterious and beautiful woman named Jonny who belongs to no shanty. Jonny persuades Ikere to stop taking the drug, an action which leads her to join a resistance movement dedicated to fighting back against the Earth's malevolent masters. Mythos Connections When wandering the cemetery outside her shanty, at one point Ikere worries that she has disturbed "one of the Mi-Goh , perhaps, or a nesting byahkee ."</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>L. K. Whyte</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Apotheosis:_Stories_of_Human_Survival_After_the_Rise_of_the_Elder_Gods"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#In_Memoriam:_Howard_Phillips_Lovecraft:_Recollections,_Appreciations,_Estimates">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>In Memoriam: Howard Phillips Lovecraft: Recollections, Appreciations, Estimates</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>W. Paul Cook English Genre(s) Non-fiction Driftwind Press Release Date 1941 75 , which was also published under the title An Appreciation of H. P. Lovecraft , is a medium-length non-fiction piece by the late American W. Paul Cook . Running to a total of 75 , it was first published in an extremely limited run of 94 copies by Driftwind Press in 1941. Publication History (1941) Beyond the Wall of Sleep (1943) Lovecraft Remembered (1998)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#W._Paul_Cook"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>75</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1941</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lost_Soul">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lost Soul</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story's title refers not only to those who were transported to alien dimensions, but to the mental state of one of the main characters, meredith . It was published in the mythos books collection unholy dimensions in october 2005 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jeffrey_Thomas"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Unholy_Dimensions"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2005</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_M._Price">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scholars"/>
    <rdfs:label>Robert M. Price</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Robert mcnair price is an american theologian, writer, editor and artist . Price is known for arguing against the existence of a historical jesus . He is editor of the journal crypt of cthulhu and of a series of anthologies .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Jackson Mississippi</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Robert McNair Price</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Henry Akeley</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://robertmprice.mindvendor.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1954 July 7</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Daikos">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalLocations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Daikos</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Dreamlands ( Lomar ) City Humans First appearance HPL : " Polaris " Created by H. P. Lovecraft is a city in the far northern kingdom of Lomar in the Dreamlands . It was sacked by the invading hordes of the Inutos , leaving the way open for their assault on the city of Olathoë . ( HPL : " Polaris ")</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>City</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Humans</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Dreamlands ( Lomar )</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Polaris"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_after_Dark">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lovecraft after Dark</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Gideon van de Swaluw Cover Artist Stephen Cooney English James Ward Kirk Publishing Release Date November 2015 172 is a thematic anthology of Lovecraftian fiction edited by Roger Cowin and James Ward Kirk. It was released under Kirk's own publishing label in November 2015, with both print and digital copies being made available. Synopsis Contents " Flame of Cthulhu " by Josh Brown " A Gift from the Stars " by Sheldon Woodbury " I Love You, Hastur " by Norbert Gora " Under the Keeper of the Key " by Jaap Boekestein " Inscrutable Alien " by Richard King Perkins, II " Follower from the Depths " by Richard King Perkins, II " Madeline Marsh's Midlife Crisis " by K. Z. Morano " Owned " by T. S. Woolard " Erotinomicon " by Alex S. Johnson " Claire " by A. Henry Keene " Sea Memory " by Maria Mitchell " Evil Things in Cold Midnight " by Matthew Wilson " Edler's Erotic Corps " by David S. Pointer " Sybarites " by Doug Rinaldi " The Abbess of Erebus " by David Schütz, II " The Black Pool " by James 'Grim' Desborough " Tangled " by A. Henry Keene " Faster, Vampussy ... Kill, Kill! " by Alex S. Johnson " The Garden " by Magenta Nero " (S)Exit " by Davie Yates " Masdevallia " by Mark Brandon Allen " The Jewels of Arachne " Maria Mitchell " An Evening at Mount Baldy " Mark Brandon Allen " The Origin of Original Sin " by Ruth Sabath Rosenthal " Cold Air " by Sean Hoade " Hear My Call " by Mark A. Mihalko " Medusa's Harem " by Magenta Nero " Herbert West and the Mammaries of Madness " by Dixie Pinoit Behind the Mythos Despite the publishers claims, neither the "obscene romance produced the human / elder god hybrid, Wilbur Whateley" nor how "the Black Goat of the Wood to have a thousand young" are explored in any of the stories contained within.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>172</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>November 2015</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dagon_September-December_1988">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dagon September-December 1988</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various United Kingdom English Carl T. Ford Release Date September 1988 82 was an issue of the now-defunct Lovecraftian fanzine Dagon . Although billed as a "Thomas Ligotti Special Double Issue", it contained only three of Ligotti 's works. It was edited by the late British and occasional Carl T. Ford . Contents (incomplete) "Autumn Horror" by Thomas Ligotti "The Library of Byzantium" by Thomas Ligotti "Oneiric Horror" by Thomas Ligotti</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United Kingdom</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>82</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>September 1988</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mysteries_of_the_Worm_%28anthology%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Mysteries of the Worm (anthology)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mysteries of the worm: early tales of the cthulhu mythos is a collection of robert bloch 's cthulhu mythos fiction . It first appeared in 1981 in an edition edited by lin carter, published by zebra books . It was later reissued in a revised and expanded second edition, edited by robert m. price and published by chaosium in 1993 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_Bloch"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>263</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 1993</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Blood_Brothers_2">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Blood Brothers 2</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Lee Gibbons United States English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Chaosium Release Date 1992 is a scenario supplement for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game. Written by an ensemble team of games designers and featuring artwork by Lee Gibbons , it was published in a paperback format in 1992. Overview As with its predecessor Blood Brothers , the book contains a selection of scenarios (9 in total) which take their inspiration from B-movies. The stories included are all stand-alone adventures and come complete with monster statistics, NPCs, and pre-generated characters to facilitate quick-start play. Scenarios "Nightmare in Silence" "Chateau of Blood" "An Alien Kicked Sand In My Face" "Alive and Kicking" "El Tigre, y la Pirámede de Destrucci--n" "The Evil Gun" "Dead on Arrival 2" "Carnival Knowledge" "Simply Red" Contributors Geoff Gillan Kathy Ho Marcus L. Rowland John B. Monroe Scott David Aniolowski Lynn Willis Penelope Love Mark Grundy Kevin A. Ross Steve Kluskens Richard Watts External Links at RPG.net (includes book details and reviews) Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1992</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mike_Mason">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Mike Mason</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Games designer United Kingdom Notable Works Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game (7th edition) is a leading British games designer and small press publisher best known for his work on the roleplaying games Warhammer 40,000: Dark Heresy by Fantasy Flight Games and Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu . He is the current creative director for the latter. Overview Selected Works Core Games Call of Cthulhu (7th edition, 2016) Gaming Supplements Ramsey Campbell's Goatswood and Less Pleasant Places (2001) Pulp Cthulhu (2016) Call of Cthulhu Keeper Screen (2016, possibly other editions) The Derelict (2016, contributor) The Grand Grimoire of Cthulhu Mythos Magic (2017) Camp Sunny (2017, with Paul Fricker ) Alone Against the Dark (2018) Malleus Monstrorum (2020, with Scott David Aniolowski and Paul Fricker) A Time to Harvest (2021) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United Kingdom</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isKnownForR>Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game</ex:isKnownForR>
  </rdf:Description>
