    <rdfs:label>More Annotated H. P. Lovecraft</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft (stories) Peter Cannon (non-fiction) S. T. Joshi (non-fiction) United States English Release Date August 1998 312 is a book of stories by Lovecraft with insights and critique from Peter Cannon and S. T. Joshi . Publication History Dell (August 1999) Turtleback Books (October 1999) Dell/SFBC (January 2008)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Peter_Cannon"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#S._T._Joshi"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>312</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 1998</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Garden_of_the_Gods">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Garden of the Gods</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cody Goodfellow English Genre(s) Cthulhu Mythos Appears in Beyond the Mountains of Madness Celaeno Press Publication date April 30, 2015 "" is a short story by Cody Goodfellow that first appeared in Beyond the Mountains of Madness (Celaeno Press, 2015). Set in the Peruvian Andes and at the Yanacocha gold strip mine there, it tells the story of the awakening of an Elder Thing known as the Scientist after 100 million years, and the efforts of US special forces soldier Lt. Keith “BS” Purcell and a Quechua Indian possessed by a Yithian to stop it--and each other. Publication History After its appearance in Beyond the Mountains of Madness (2015), "" was reprinted in Goodfellow's collection Rapture of the Deep and Other Lovecraftian Tales ( Hippocampus Press , 2016). References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cody_Goodfellow"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Beyond_the_Mountains_of_Madness"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2015 April 30</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jinn">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CharactersIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Jinn</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The (singular: jinni) are part of pre-islamic arabia's mythology . They were introduced by chaosium as a fictional race for the call of cthulhu rpg . They are a race of beings from another dimension who were brought to earth by taranushi, an outer god .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Out of the Jar Cthulhu Invictus</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shambler_from_the_Stars"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Zytl_Qae">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Zy'tl Q'ae</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Death-Vines of Xiclotl</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The are a fictional race created by ramsey campbell for his cthulhu mythos short story " the insects from shaggai ". Their name originates from chaosium 's roleplaying game call of cthulhu .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Insects_from_Shaggai"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ramsey_Campbell"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Volgna-Gath">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Volgna-Gath</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Great Old Ones</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Guardian of the Knowledge</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Keeper of Secrets</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as The Keeper of Secrets The Guardian of the Knowledge Great Old Ones First appearance EXP : " The Sculpture " Created by James Ambuehl , also called The Keeper of the Secrets and The Guardian of the Knowledge , is a slimy shape-shifting mass. It can be summoned with mud and the blood of the invoker. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Sculpture</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Ambuehl"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Toadface">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Toadface</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Behind the mythos is a short story by mark mclaughlin set in h.p. Lovecraft's cthulhu mythos . It was first printed in mclaughlin's short story collection motivational shrieker .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Short Fiction Horror Black Comedy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Motivational Shrieker (2004) The Cthulhu Mythos Megapack (2012)</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>2004</ex:isPublishedAsW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#He_Knew">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>He Knew</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here James Ambuehl United States English The Arkham Sampler V3 #4 date 1986 " " is a poem by American James Ambuehl . Synopsis Each of the five verses comprises a short comment on one of H. P. Lovecraft 's most famous works: " The Colour Out of Space ", " The Call of Cthulhu ", " The Dunwich Horror ", The Shadow Over Innsmouth and At the Mountains of Madness are all included, in that order. Each verse ends with the line "Lovecraft knew." History The Arkham Sampler V3 #4 (1986) Imelod #15 (October 1999) Nightscapes (May 2000)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Ambuehl"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The Arkham Sampler V3 #4</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1986</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_House_on_the_Borderland">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The House on the Borderland</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>300 is a novel by english author william hope hodgson . It left a marked impression on many authors of the time, not least among them h.p. Lovecraft and clark ashton smith .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#William_Hope_Hodgson"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Fantasy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Science Fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>300</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1908</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Worlds_of_H._P._Lovecraft:_Dagon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleComicBookAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: Dagon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft (original story) Steven Philip Jones (adaptation) Sergio Cariello United States English The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft Genre(s) Weird fiction Caliber Comics Release Date 1993 Preceded by The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: Arthur Jermyn Followed by The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: The Music of Erich Zann is a comic book adaptation of the Howard Phillips Lovecraft short story of the same name . Overview Released as the second instalment of Caliber Comics ' The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft , Dagon was adapted to the format by Steven Philip Jones and featured artwork by Sergio Cariello. It was issued in a softcover format with a full-colour cover and black and white interiors. Dagon was later included in the collection H. P. Lovecraft's Worlds: Volume 2 . Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Steven_Philip_Jones"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1993</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gods_of_the_Deep">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosFilmAndTelevision"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gods of the Deep</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A new deep sea trench is discovered at the bottom of the atlantic ocean . Avaricious executives demand a tissue sample, revealing the existence of a colossal sleeping creature . As the entity begins to awaken, insanity starts to claim the minds of the crew, and the sample begins to grow . The film has received mostly negative reviews .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United Kingdom</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasWriterW>Charlie Steeds</ex:hasWriterW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>6 February 2024</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Strange_Dark_One:_Tales_of_Nyarlathotep">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Strange Dark One: Tales of Nyarlathotep</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>W. H. Pugmire Jeffrey Thomas Cover Artist Jeffrey Thomas English Miskatonic River Press Release Date November 2012 162 is a thematic collection of Lovecraftian fiction by the late American W. H. Pugmire . First published in paperback and digital editions by Miskatonic River Press in November 2012, a hardcover release was issued in July of the following year. Cover art and illustrations were provided by Pugmire's friend and co- Jeffrey Thomas . Overview Contents "The Strange Dark One" "Immortal Remains" "Past the Gates of Deepest Dreaming" "One Last Theft" " The Hands That Reek and Smoke " "The Audient Void" "Some Bacchante of Irem" "To See Beyond"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#W._H._Pugmire"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>162</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>November 2012</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Marble_from_the_Void">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Marble from the Void</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Steven J. Alvarez English Rogue Planet Press Lovecraftiana Vol 5, Issue 4 date 2021  " " is a Lovecraftian short story by Steven J. Alvarez, first published in Vol 5, Issue 4 of the Lovecraftiana zine in 2021. Synopsis An astronaut performing maintenance work on his ship in the orbit of Saturn encounters a derelict craft. Aboard, he disturbs an entity which opens a portal to another realm, allowing its true form to emerge and unleash its voracious appetites on the galaxy. Trivia Despite never being explicitly named, the entity's description as a collection of glowing spheres locked out of our dimension along with the narrator's reference to it being "both the gate and its key" would suggest that it is intended to be the Outer God Yog-Sothoth .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Steven J. Alvarez</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Issue 4</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Lovecraftiana Vol 5</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2021</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraftiana_Vol_4,_Issue_2">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Periodicals"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lovecraftiana Vol 4, Issue 2</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Dean Wirth Cover Artist Borja Pindado English Rogue Planet Press Release Date July 2019 116  is an issue of the Rogue Planet Press periodical Lovecraftiana , published in July 2019. It was edited by Gavin Chappell , featuring cover art by Borja Pindado and interior art from Dean Wirth . Contents 'Incense of Irem" by Lee Clark Zumpe "Lovecraft's Pets" by Matthew Wilson "Cthulhu Chicks" by Ronald Linson "The Book" by Jacob Jones-Goldstein "The Colour of Deceit" by William Robert Webb, III "The King in the Emerald Cloak" by Kieran Judge "The Last Resort" by Dean Wirth "The Lost Archivist" by David Kenney "The Crimson Sin" by J. R. Young "The Bat" by Sandro D. Fossemò "The Horror on the Buffet Table" by R. C. Mulhare "The Orphanage of Lowell Street" by Sergio Palumbo "Late Night Encounter" by Greg Fewer</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>116</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 2019</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lupine_One">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lupine One</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ngirrthlu"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ngirrth'lu Lupine Ones are the names of the werewolf-like servants that serve the entity known as Ngirrth'lu .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ram_Singh">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ram Singh</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>First half of the 20th century</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Anton_Zarnak"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Akbar Singh</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as akbar singh unknown, likely late 19th/early 20th century . A large yet cultured man of prodigious strength. He is a sikh and wears a dastar (turban) in india, anton zarnak saved from a were-tiger (the feline equivalent of a werewolf) in india, he met his death in service to the accomplished mystic .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lin_Carter"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cult_of_the_Black_Sun">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cults"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Organisation"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cult of the Black Sun</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Die schwarze sonne (the black sun) was founded by the sorcerer reinhardt weissler in 1921 . The cult is a sorcerer heavy society, whose sorcerers call on terrible gods and encourage casualties on their side to please their gods . Their ultimate goal is to bring to the material realm (earth) the black sun from the dreamlands .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dirge_of_Reason">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ArkhamHorrorFiction"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Dirge of Reason</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Behind the mythos is a hardback novella written by graeme davis . Published by fantasy flight games under their arkham horror files imprint . Contains a fifteen page full-colour insert featuring extras such as artwork of the items and locations featured in the story .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Graeme_Davis"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror Mystery Action Adventure</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>95 (plus 15-page insert)</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Horror_Fiction"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>22 February 2018</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cat_of_Sadoqua">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cat of Sadoqua</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Real World Information First appearance CIRCLE : The Oracle of Sadoqua Created by Clark Ashton Smith The Cats of Sadoqua are black feline servants of the Great Old One Tsathoggua ( EXP : " The Wedding of Sheila-Na-Gog ", R. L. Tierney &amp; G. A. Rahman ) or "Sadoqua" as he is known in Averonia ( CIRCLE : " The Oracle of Sadoqua ", Clark Ashton Smith ).</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Oracle of Sadoqua</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tuama">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tuama</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A professor's husband dies as a result of researching the fourth dimension . She recruits a graduate student and travels to a remote island to continue his work . What the professor has not told her student is that the guardian to the fourth dimension needs a sacrifice .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#L._F._Falconer"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Weirdbook Annual 2</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>February 2019</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Scroll_of_Morloc">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ClarkAshtonSmithWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Scroll of Morloc</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A rare example of a mythos story in which no humans appear (although they are mentioned) a voormi shaman named yhemog is infuriated after his seventh failed attempt to be elected high-priest . He decides to get revenge on his own people by trespassing into their temple .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lin_Carter"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Fantastic</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 1975</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tsath">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tsath</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAccessibilityL>Via the ruined suburb of L'thaa</ex:hasAccessibilityL>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Named after the once-worshipped tsathoggua , it is the last occupied city in the underground realm of k'n-yan . The gn'agn of the supreme tribunal and the council of executives operate from the frescoed halls of a gold-and-copper palace behind a gardened and fountained park.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>The Men of Tsath</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Mound"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nightscapes_August_2001">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nightscapes August 2001</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various English Edward P. Berglund Release Date August 2001 Preceded by May 2000 Followed by May 2003 is the fourteenth issue of the sporadically-released online fanzine Nightscapes . It was edited by the late American and Lovecraft scholar Edward P. Berglund . Contents In addition the the written content listed below, this issue features artwork by Suzanne E. Miller, Andrea Bonazzi, Jeff Remmer, Garth Hill, and Chris Cotgrove. Fiction "Masters of Terror" by James Ambuehl and Laurence J. Cornford "The Faceless One" by David A. Oakes "The Key of Shub-Niggurath" by Fred S. Lubnow and Steve Maschuck "The Girl Who Walked in Circles" by Mike Minnis "The Day the Circus Came to Chicora" by Philip I. Jones " The Creature from the Fog " by Ron Shiflet "Goat's Milk" by J. R. Boos "Nyarlathotep" by Kenneth Silver "The Inner Dark" by Duane Pesice "How Darkness Came to Carcosa" by Galad Elflandsson Non-Fiction "An Open Letter to Robert Bloch" by Garth Mindfeather Hill "Tales of the Blair Witch Mythos"by Michael D. Winkle Games "Wake the Dead" by Matt Sanborn ( Call of Cthulhu scenario) Parodies "The Big Moment" by Tim Waggoner "Great Cthulhu" by David Cunnius Poetry " Lovecraftian Cliff Notes for Ech-Pi-El's: The Nameless City " by Franklyn Searight " Folks of Innsmouth: Used Car Dealer " by Franklyn Searight "Night Terrors" by Michael Fantina " Thirteen Ways of Looking at Cthulhu " by Mark Francis External Links full issue free online at the Nightscapes website</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 2001</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pickmans_Gallery">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Pickman's Gallery</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Fuseli English Ulthar Press Release Date 2018 214  is a thematic anthology of original Cthulhu Mythos fiction centred on H. P. Lovecraft 's character Richard Upton Pickman . Edited by Matthew Carpenter , it was published by Ulthar Press in 2018. Both paperback and digital editions were released. Overview Contents " One Night South of the Border " by Paul McNamee " The Studies of Dr. Reid " by Peter Rawlik " The Ghulistan Affair " by Sam Inabinet " Pigman " by Max D. Stanton " A Creak in the Floor " by Victoria Dalpe " Pickman's Model " by Max D. Stanton " For Susannah " by Tom Lynch " The Ghoul's Portrait " by Joshua Reynolds " A Pickman Original " by Logan Noble " Pickman's Muse " by K. H. Vaughn " Eigenspace X " by Mike Chinn " The Medium and the Message " by L. C. von Hessen " Beyond the Veil of Pretty Pink Lies " by Rebecca J. Allred " Pickman's Model Kit " by Cliff Biggers " The Pickman Revival " by Steven M. Vance " The Cleaner's Tale " by Tom Johnstone " A Photograph From Life " by Robert M. Price Trivia The cover of features a painting by late Swiss artist Henry Fuseli ; Fusili's works were an influence on H. P. Lovecraft.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>214</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2018</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Achtung%21_Cthulhu:_Guide_to_the_Pacific_Front">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Achtung! Cthulhu: Guide to the Pacific Front</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Dim Martin Cover Artist Dim Martin English Call of Cthulhu ( Achtung! Cthulhu ) Genre(s) Roleplaying game Modiphius Entertainment Release Date 2014 is a background supplement for the Achtung! Cthulhu sub-setting of Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu horror roleplaying game. Written by Dave Blewer, Bill Bodden, Alex Bund, Adam Crossingham, and Lynne Hardy , it was published by Modiphius Entertainment in 2014 . Overview Design Team Writers: Dave Blewer, Bill Bodden, Alex Bund, Lynne Hardy Additional material: Adam Crossingham Editor: Lynne Hardy Artwork: Dim Martin Graphic design, layout &amp; cartography: Michal E. Cross Production &amp; art direction: Chris Birch &amp; Lynne Hardy Gallery References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2014</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#She_Wore_a_Trench_Coat">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>She Wore a Trench Coat</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>" those folk belowclear the air" is a short story by american don webb . It forms part of the arkham detective agency, but has little to do with the agency . Beat cop travis mazoni is assigned to surveillance by a shadowy government department after he witnesses a bizarre death on the street . He uncovers a link to the "church of the transformations", which is in reality a cult devoted to the great old one eihort .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Don_Webb"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Detective_Agency"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft:_The_Early_Stories">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>H. P. Lovecraft: The Early Stories</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft Various Cover Artist David Hartman United States English Genre(s) Weird fiction Caliber Comics Release Date July 2019 110 is a collection of short stories by the late American Howard Phillips Lovecraft . Overview Containing nine of Lovecraft's works along with all-new accompanying artwork, was edited by Lovecraft scholar S. T. Joshi and runs to a total of 110 . It was published by Caliber Comics in July 2019 in a softcover format; whilst its cover was in full colour, the interiors were rendered in black and white. Contents Introduction by Steven Philip Jones " The Alchemist " " The Tomb " " Dagon " " Beyond the Wall of Sleep " " The Statement of Randolph Carter " " Arthur Jermyn " " The Picture in the House " " The Music of Erich Zann " " The Lurking Fear " Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>110</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 2019</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Trouver_Objet_Cach%C3%A9">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Trouver Objet Caché</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>often abbreviated ToC , is a 2003 registered French association centered around the Call of Cthulhu RPG. Its website Tentacules.net include scenarios which originate from French magazines, such as the one which coined the name Nyog' Sothep . Some of its website creations include Olkoth Nssu-Ghahnb Ugga-Naach External links Official website Official registry entry (FR)</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Re-Animator_%28novelisation%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Re-Animator (novelisation)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Re-Animator Jeff Rovin English Pocket Books Release Date May 1987 223 Re-Animator is a novelisation of the 1985 Stuart Gordon science fiction horror-comedy film of the same name , itself based on " Herbert West--Reanimator ". Written by Jeff Rovin, it was first released in May 1987 by Pocket Books, a subdivision of publishing giant Simon &amp; Schuster; it has since been reissued numerous times, including in audiobook and ebook formats . Overview Gallery References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Jeff Rovin</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>223</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 1987</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cabal">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cabal</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"barrendagon" is a short story by american author armand rosamilia . It forms the third part of his keyport cthulhu story cycle .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Armand_Rosamilia"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Keyport_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 2012</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Gathering">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#BrianLumleyWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Gathering</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu mythos novel by brian lumley . Published as part of his earth, air, fire &amp; water collection . Represents the element of fire .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Earth"/>
