  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Edward_Morris">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Edward Morris</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Edward Robert Morris, Jr Connection to the Mythos Mythos Contributor Birth Date 8 October 1975 Goldsboro, North Carolina, United States United States Edward R. Morris is an American author of science fiction, fantasy and horror. He has made several contributions to the expanded Cthulhu Mythos inspired by H. P. Lovecraft . Selected Bibliography Short Stories " Magna Mater " (2014) " The Last Night on Earth " (2017) " Family Tradition " (2017) " I Want to Break Free " (2017)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>United States North Carolina Goldsboro</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Edward Robert Morris Jr</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Mythos Contributor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>8 October 1975</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Angelo_Ricci">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Angelo Ricci</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Petty criminal Human First appearance HPL : " The Terrible Old Man " Created by H. P. Lovecraft is a fictional character created by the late American author H. P. Lovecraft for his 1921 short story " The Terrible Old Man ". He was a literary representation of the animosity felt by Lovecraft towards the non-white immigrants of the time, who he believed were polluting the genetic pool of the United States. Overview In Lovecraft's story Ricci, along with fellow criminals Joe Czanek and Manuel Silva , intended to rob the titular Terrible Old Man of Kingsport , an elderly recluse reputed to be extremely wealthy. What they did not bargain on was the man's true nature, and the gang's corpses were found washed ashore, horribly mutilated, shortly afterwards. Ricci was one of the more thuggish of the trio, appearing entirely willing to cause the old man any bodily harm necessary to persuade him to give up the location of his fortune.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Terrible_Old_Man"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mhithrha">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OuterGods"/>
    <rdfs:label>Mh'ithrha</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Hounds_of_Tindalos"/>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Outer Gods</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Outer Gods The Hounds of Tindalos First appearance EXP : Unseen Masters Created by Bruce Ballon , also known as The Arch-Lord of Tindalos , is a fictional entity created by Bruce Ballon for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu role-playing game. Description The master of the Hounds of Tindalos , is the mightiest member of that strange species. Although invisible to the human eye, its physical form is said to be that of a gargantuan wolf, somewhat resembling the beast Fenrir from Norse mythology. has been engaged in a war with Yog-Sothoth for all of eternity; stories of this conflict are the stuff of legend in the Cthulhu Mythos , and the fact that it has not been annihilated by such a being gives a hint to the true extent of 's power. ( EXP : Unseen Masters )</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Unseen_Masters"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Bruce_Ballon"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#River_Narg">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalLocations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>River Narg</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cathuria River First appearance HPL : " The White Ship " Created by H. P. Lovecraft The is a sacred river that flows through the fabled land of Cathuria in the legends of those who live in the Dreamlands . On the green and flowery mountains of Cathuria stand temples of pink marble with silver fountains in their courtyards that flow with the scented and musical waters of the Narg. The river is said to be home to colorful varieties of fish not found outside Cathuria. ( HPL : " The White Ship ")</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>River</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cathuria"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_White_Ship"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Basil_Copper">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Basil Copper</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>3 April 2013</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Basil Frederick Albert Copper Pseudonym(s) Basil Cooper Lee Falk Connection to the Mythos Author Birth Date 5 February 1924 London, England, UK Death Date 3 April 2013 United Kingdom Fantasy Horror Mystery Basil Frederick Albert Copper (5 February 1924-3 April 2013) was an English author and editor. Overview Beginning his career as a journalist and newspaper editor, decided to become a professional author around 1970. He established long-lived relationships with both August Derleth and Arkham House , who published many of his works, perhaps the most famous being his continuation of Derleth's Solar Pons series of Arthur Conan Doyle pastiches; Copper not only wrote more short works, but also edited a collection of Derleth's original Pons stories, although the changes he made were poorly received by the fanbase. In addition to his other stories, and as might be expected by his close ties to Derleth and Arkham House, Copper also wrote several short stories set in the Cthulhu Mythos established by the late H. P. Lovecraft . Selected Bibliography Short Fiction " Shaft Number 247 " (1980) " Beyond the Reef " (1994) " Voices in the Water " (2005)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>http://example.org/lovecraft#England London UK</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Basil Frederick Albert Copper</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United Kingdom</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Basil Cooper</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Lee Falk</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>5 February 1924</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Bryan_Thao_Worra">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosInspiredAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Bryan Thao Worra</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Worra is an award-winning author of laotian descent . He has been recognized by the loft literary center, the minnesota state arts board and the national endowment for the arts .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>Laos Vientiane</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://thaoworra.blogspot.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1 January 1973</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Vile_Fungus">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonSapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Vile Fungus</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mi-Go"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sociocultural Characteristics Yuggoth (presumed) Mi-Go Real World Information First appearance EXP : Beschwörungen Created by Michael Siefener The is as a kind of parasitic life form first appearing in Michael Siefener 's 1995 novelette Beschwörungen . In the 2006 edition of the Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game supplement Malleus Monstrorum , it is mentioned that the fungi originated from Yuggoth and spread through the cosmos with the Mi-Go . The can infect any organism it comes into contact with, mutating the living flesh into fungal tissue. Note The fungi's infection is very similar to William Hope Hodgson 's 1907 short story "The Voice in the Night".</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Beschwörungen</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Michael Siefener</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Plateau_of_Sung">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>Plateau of Sung</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Burma First appearance AWD : " The Lair of the Star-Spawn " Created by August Derleth and Mark Schorer The is a plateau in Burma on which the Tcho-Tcho People are centered. The city of Alaozar stood on the Isle of Stars in the Lake of Dread, hidden from common view by a barrier of trees (if they were trees). The gods Lloigor and Zhar are situated here. Sometimes called the Sung Region, the area is shunned by the non-Tcho-Tcho natives, and few men have penetrated the jungles to approach it. It is sometimes confused with the Plateau of Leng .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Burma</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Lair_of_the_Star-Spawn"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Steve_Rasnic_Tem">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Steve Rasnic Tem</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Birth Date 14 September 1950 Jonesville, Virginia, USA United States https://stevetem.com/ (born 14 September 1950 in Jonesville, Virginia ) is a prolific American author who has written a number of Lovecraftian short stories. These have appeared in periodicals including S. T. Joshi 's Black Wings and Robert M. Price 's Crypt of Cthulhu , as well as anthologies such as The Children of Cthulhu (2002, Del Ray/Ballantine), Weird Shadows Over Innsmouth (2005, Fedogan &amp; Bremer ), Lovecraft's Monsters (2014, Tachyon Publications), Innsmouth Nightmares ( PS Publishing , 2015), and The Madness of Cthulhu: Volume Two (2015, Titan Books). Selected Bibliography Short Fiction "Embrace of Clay, Embrace of Straw" (1986) "Eggs" (2000) "Outside" (2002) "Waiting at the Crossroads Motel" (2012) "Deep Fracture" (2015) "In the Lovecraft Museum" (2015) "Between the Pilings" (2015) "Mister Ainsley" (2017) External Links at Wikipedia at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Jonesville. Virginia</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://stevetem.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>14 September 1950</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Devil_Reef">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>Devil Reef</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The reef was used by captain obed marsh as a meeting point when contacting the deep ones of y'ha-nthlei from around 1838 . Rumours abound that the rocks are home to a legion of devils and imps or a hidden pirate hoard.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Rocky shoal</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Deep Ones</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Atlantic Ocean</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Offshore of Innsmouth Harbour</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shadow_Over_Innsmouth"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Michael_R._Collings">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Critics"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scholars"/>
    <rdfs:label>Michael R. Collings</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Michael Robert Collings Connection to the Mythos Author Birth Date 29 October 1947 Rupert, Idaho, USA United States Horror Fantasy Science fiction is a prolific American author, poet, literary critic, and former creative writing professor whose works focus on the horror, science fiction and fantasy genres. Alongside his fictional output, he has written several non-fiction works on prominent contemporary authors, with the greatest number of these by far about world-renowned horror writer Stephen King . Collings' Lovecraftian fiction has been published in anthologies such as the World Horror Convention 2012 Souvenir Book and The Cthulhu Mythos Megapack . Selected Bibliography Short Fiction " In the Haunted Darkness " (2012) External Links at Wikipedia at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Idaho Rupert</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Michael Robert Collings</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>29 October 1947</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Invaders_Now%21">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Invaders Now!</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Invaders Now! Christos N. Gage, Alex Ross Caio Reis English Marvel Comics Release Date Nov, 2010 - April, 2011 Media Type Comic book Invaders Now! is a 5-part miniseries written by Christos N. Gage and Alex Ross. Synopsis The World War 2 superhero team, the Invaders are forced to battle both the Great Old One Shuma-Gorath and a World War 2 supervillain team of Nazis, the Uberkommandos.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Alex Ross</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Christos N. Gage</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2011 2010 - April Nov</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Distortion_Out_of_Space">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Distortion Out of Space</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Francis flagg, a.k.a. H.g. Weiss, was a correspondent of h.p. Lovecraft . "history" first appeared in weird tales in august 1934 . Flagg was a self-confessed fan of lovecraft's works .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Francis_Flagg"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Science Fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>August 1934</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>Unknown certainly as early as 1932</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Oztalun">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ElderGods"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Oztalun</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Elder Gods</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Golden</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Shimmering One</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as Golden and Shimmering One Elder Gods First appearance " The Bane of the Byagoona " Created by James Ambuehl is an Elder God . It is symbolized by a seven-pointed star, which is its own seal.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Bane of the Byagoona</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Ambuehl"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dream-Quest_of_Unknown_Kadath_and_Other_Stories">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleComicBookAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath and Other Stories</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft Jason Thompson United States English Media Type Graphic Novel 184 is a collection of four of Lovecraft's tales adapted into comic book format. It is illustrated by Jason Thompson . Contents The book is printed as a limited-edition hardback and contains a map of the Dreamlands along with concept sketches and unused artwork. The four stories included are: The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath The White Ship Celephaïs The Strange High House in the Mist Reception Gavin Lees of website Bleeding Cool said of the book “ Thompson has a clear love and respect for the source material and draws the picaresque fantasy in an ornate and heavily-rendered style ... It’s a faithful adaptation and one that should satisfy even the most ardent of Lovecraft admirers. ”</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>184</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Monks_Hollow">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Monk's Hollow</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Created by henry kuttner is a fictional village invented by kuttner . It's the setting of his stories " the frog " and " the hunt " points of interest include the witch stone, the north swamp, and the wizard's house .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Hunt"/>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Village</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>United States Eastern Seaboard</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Frog"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Wilbur_Whateley">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dunwich"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Wilbur Whateley</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>1928 August 3</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Wilbur is a fictional character created by h. p. lovecraft for his short story " the dunwich horror " . He was born in the rural town of dunwich, massachusetts on february 2, 1913 at 5 a.m. he was immediately recognized for his hideousness, described as a " dark, goatish-looking infant ". He displayed unnaturally rapid physical and mental development: at seven months he could walk unassisted and eleven</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Demonbane Wilbur Whateley Waiting The City at the Two Magnetic Poles</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dunwich_Horror"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gnophkeh">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtinctSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gnophkeh</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Gnophkehs are brutish humanoids are enemies of both the voormis and early hyperborean human tribes . They are described as being covered in coarse, matted hair with large protruding ears and proboscidean noses . They originally lived in hyperborea and worshiped the great old one rhan-tegoth . But rhan-tegoth eventually forgot them when he entered a stone-like hibernation state .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The Scroll of Morloc The Flame in the Ice</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Mound"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Relic_In_The_Egyptian_Gallery_%26_More_Tales_Of_The_Cthulhu_Mythos">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Relic In The Egyptian Gallery &amp; More Tales Of The Cthulhu Mythos</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mark McLaughlin Michael Sheehan, Jr. English Genre(s) Horror Release Date 2017 70 is a collection of short stories from authors Mark McLaughlin and Michael Sheehan, Jr. Overview Consisting of six stories and running to a total of 70 , was released in 2017 as a digital download on Amazon Kindle. Most of the stories were included in the later collection Horrors &amp; Abominations: 24 Tales of the Cthulhu Mythos in December 2018. Contents " The Lurker From The Kelp Forest " " The Nightmare In Delapore Tower " " The Power Of Azalareon " " Another Terrible Old Man " " The Thing From Beyond The Living Door " " The Relic In The Egyptian Gallery "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Michael Sheehan. Jr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>70</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2017</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Amaterasu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CharactersIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ElderGods"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Amaterasu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Elder_God"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Elder god first appearance secrets of japan is part of the shinto cosmology . She was first used by chaosium in 2005 for its call of cthulhu role-playing game as an elder god .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Secrets_of_Japan"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Asenath_Waite">
