  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shuttered_Room_and_Other_Pieces">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Shuttered Room and Other Pieces</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Frank Utpatel Cover Artist Richard Taylor United States English Arkham House Release Date 1959 313 is a collection of works by and about the late American Howard Phillips Lovecraft . Overview Running to a total of 313 , edited by August Derleth , and featuring illustrations by Frank Utpatel , was published in a limited run of 2500 copies by Arkham House in 1959. It has not been reissued. The book contains a selection of works by Lovecraft - including several juvinilia and " posthumous collaborations " -along with a number of memoirs, reminisces and studies by both those who knew him and other, more contemporary, authors. Contents Foreword by August Derleth " The Shuttered Room " by August Derleth and H. P. Lovecraft " The Fisherman of Falcon Point " by August Derleth and H. P. Lovecraft Editor's Note (Juvenilia and Early Tales) by August Derleth " The Little Glass Bottle " by H. P. Lovecraft " The Secret Cave, or John Lees Adventure " by H. P. Lovecraft " The Mystery of the Grave-Yard, or "A Dead Man's Revenge": A Detective Story " by H. P. Lovecraft " The Mysterious Ship " by H. P. Lovecraft " The Alchemist " by H. P. Lovecraft " Poetry and the Gods " by Anna Helen Crofts and H. P. Lovecraft " The Street " by H. P. Lovecraft " Old Bugs " by H. P. Lovecraft " Idealism and Materialism: A Reflection " by H. P. Lovecraft " The Commonplace Book of H. P. Lovecraft " by H. P. Lovecraft " Lovecraft in Providence " by Donald Wandrei " Lovecraft as Mentor " by August Derleth " Out of the Ivory Tower " by Robert Bloch " Three Hours with H. P. Lovecraft " by Dorothy C. Walter " Memories of a Friendship " by Alfred Galpin " Homage to H. P. Lovecraft " by Felix Stefanile " Lines to H. P. Lovecraft " by Joseph Payne Brennan " H. P. L. " by Clark Ashton Smith " Revenants " by August Derleth " H. P. L. " by by Robert H. Barlow " Anniversary " by Robert H. Barlow " Letter for Last Christmas " by Robert H. Barlow " H. P. Lovecraft: The Books " by Lin Carter " H. P. Lovecraft: The Gods " by Lin Carter " Addendum: Some Observations on the Carter Glossary " by T. G. L. Cockcroft " Notes on the Cthulhu Mythos " by George T. Wetzel " Lovecraft's First Book " by William L. Crawford " Dagon " by H. P. Lovecraft " The Strange High House in the Mist " by H. P. Lovecraft " The Outsider " by H. P. Lovecraft</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>313</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1959</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Xuthal_of_the_Dust">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleComicBookAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Xuthal of the Dust</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Fred Van Lente Guiu Vilanova English Dark Horse Comics Release Date April 2015 - June 2015 Media Type Comic book is 3-issue story arc in issues #13-15 of the 1st volume of Conan the Avenger that was published by Dark Horse Comics in 2015. The story is an adaptation of Robert E. Howard 's " The Slithering Shadow " by Fred Van Lente. Synopsis Conan and his companion end up in a drug addicted city called Xuthal, where they encounter the Great Old One known as Thog .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Fred Van Lente</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>April 2015 - June 2015</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_and_Other_Monsters">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu and Other Monsters</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sam Stone Cover Artist David J. Howe United Kingdom English Genre(s) Horror Telos Publishing Release Date April 2017 Media Type Trade Paperback Ebook 271 is an anthology of works by British Sam Stone . Overview Consisting of sixteen short stories - nine of which are set in the greater Cthulhu Mythos - is a thematic collection whose stories are linked by the inclusion of various creatures. It was released by Telos Publishing in both print and ebook versions in April 2017 and runs to a total of 271 . Contents " The Vessel " "Of Gods and Blood" "Fall Out" "Food" "Sonar City" "The Book of the Gods" "The Gold of Roatán" "The Rift" "Tell Me No Lies" "The Jealous Sea" "The Collector" "Urban Wolf "The Promise" "DNA Books" "The Night Bird" "The Puppet Master"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Sam Stone</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United Kingdom</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>271</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>April 2017</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hemoglobin">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleFilmAndTelevisionAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Hemoglobin</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Based on " The Lurking Fear " Peter Svatek Release date 31 July 1997 Canada English CAD8,000,000 (approx.) , originally titled Bleeders , is a B-movie horror feature from Peter Svatek, written by Charles Adair, Dan O'Bannon and Ronald Shusett. It is very loosely based on the H. P. Lovecraft short story " The Lurking Fear ". Synopsis A man and his girlfriend travel to a remote island where his relatives once lived, in search of a cure for an unnamed disease. Instead, he finds a pack of cannibalistic subterranean beasts running loose, and must make a difficult choice.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>Canada</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Lurking_Fear"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>31 July 1997</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Beast_of_Averoigne">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AveroigneCycle"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ClarkAshtonSmithWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Beast of Averoigne</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Averoigne weird tales first appeared in the may 1933 issue of weird tales . It tells the story of an uncanny beast that ravages the province of averoigne . It is set in 1369, in the vicinity of the abbey of périgon .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Averoigne"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>May 1933</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Keith_Herber">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Keith Herber</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>23 March 2009</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Keith Donald "Doc" Herber Connection to the Mythos Game designer Author Birth Date 3 January 1949 Detroit, Michigan, USA Death Date 23 March 2009 Lakeland, Florida, USA United States Keith Donald "Doc" Herber (3 January 1949 - 23 March 2009) was an American game designer, author, editor and musician. Overview A professional bass guitarist who has written both gaming supplements and novels for White Wolf Publishing, 's relevance to the Cthulhu Mythos is his work on the Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game by Chaosium . Already a freelance writer for Chaosium, he joined the staff in 1989 and worked as the Call of Cthulhu line editor for the next five years until his departure in 1994; however, even after leaving the company Herber continued to provide additional material for the game through until 2008. Hwrber was also the co-founder, with Tom Lynch , of the now-defunct Miskatonic River Press , an independent publisher established in 2008 which specialised in Lovecraftian gaming material and fiction. Selected Bibliography Short Fiction "Fortunes" (1995) Gaming Material King of Chicago (1994) Cthulhu Companion (1983) The Fungi from Yuggoth (1984) Cthulhu Now (1987, 1992, contributor) Blood Brothers (1990, contributor) Arkham Unveiled (1990) Tales of the Miskatonic Valley (1991, contributor) 1920s Investigator's Companion H. P. Lovecraft's Dunwich (2002) H. P. Lovecraft's Arkham (2003) New Tales of the Miskatonic Valley (2008) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Michigan Detroit</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Keith Donald Doc Herber</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Game designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>3 January 1949</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
