  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cement_Surroundings">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#BrianLumleyWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cement Surroundings</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Short story in the cthulhu mythos genre by brian lumley first published in 1969 . It was later incorporated into his first novel, the burrowers beneath . An archaeologist's nephew pieces together his uncle's account of the threat .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tales_of_the_Cthulhu_Mythos"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>1969</ex:isPublishedAsW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#R._Boerem">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonFictionAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>R. Boerem</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Writer Non-fiction is an author who has written a handful of non-fiction essays and reviews on topics related to the works of Howard Phillips Lovecraft and the Cthulhu Mythos . These have appeared in collections such as Essays Lovecraftian (1976, T-K Graphics), Discovering H. P. Lovecraft (2001, Wildside Press ), and H. P. Lovecraft: Four Decades of Criticism (1980, Ohio University Press), and periodicals including Crypt of Cthulhu , Nyctalops , Lovecraft Annual , and Lovecraft Studies . Selected Bibliography Non-Fiction "The First Lewis Theobald (1976) " A Lovecraftian Nightmare " (1976) " The Continuity of the Fungi from Yuggoth " (1980) "Lovecraft's "The City"" (2011) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Writer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Circle_of_Darkness">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crossovers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Circle of Darkness</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story begins with michelangelo saving a blind man from getting hit by a truck . The man offers him a room so he can spend some time away from his brothers and focus on writing a crime fiction novel . During his time at the new apartment, he is unable to work on his book after being distracted by the blind man's suspicious activity . Shortly afterwards, its revealed that the man is tiresias the immortal prophet, who uses ludvig prinn 's</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Bill Moulage</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Stephen Murphy</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>December 2006</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Virgil_Finlay">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSFriendsAndAcquaintances"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsCorrespondents"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Virgil Finlay</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>18 January 1971 1971</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>One of the great weird artists of his time and a prolific contributor of artwork to the pulps; late correspondent of Lovecraft. [A Virgil Finlay Bibliography features much of Finlay’s art.]</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Virgil finlay was an american illustrator who specialized in fantasy, science fiction, and horror artwork . He began his career at the classic pulp magazine weird tales . Finlay's depiction of lovecraft as an 18th century gentleman, "the old gentleman from providence" (1937) has become iconic .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>New York USA Rochester</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1914 23 July 1914</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Yomagntho">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OuterGods"/>
    <rdfs:label>Yomagn'tho</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Outer Gods</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Feaster_from_the_Stars"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>That Which Relentlessly Waits Outside</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Description is an outer god described as being equal to cthugha in terms of power . The entity was sealed away in a world circling the star pherkard . When summoned to earth, it takes the form of a flaming circle with three inner-petals and a dark interior with chaotic shapes .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Thing in the Library</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crispin_Burnham"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>E. P. Berglund</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Decorative_Water_Feature_of_Nameless_Dread">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Decorative Water Feature of Nameless Dread</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Historywas first published in the anthology whispers from the abyss by 01publishing in november 2014 . Also appeared in the collection of brogden's own works evocations in july 2015 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>James Brogden</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Humour</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Whispers_from_the_Abyss"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2014</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cult_of_the_Darkhold">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Organisation"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cult of the Darkhold</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesO>Darkholders Cthonic Brotherhood</ex:hasAlternativeNamesO>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The , also known as the chthonic brotherhood and the darkholders, is a fictional organization in the marvel universe . It is made up of men and women of power and position, who joined to acquire power and/or wealth, and stay in the organization to keep what they've acquired . The organization has access to secret bases, all types of vehicles (land, air, and sea), and cutting edge technology (e.g. Laser blasters) it employs heavily armed security teams (with body armor),</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNotableMemberO>Thulsa Doom Morgan le Fay</ex:hasNotableMemberO>
    <ex:hasTypeO>Cult Chthon Dracula</ex:hasTypeO>
    <ex:wasCreatedBynO>Mark Gruenwald Steven Grant David Michelinie John Byrne</ex:wasCreatedBynO>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Monomaniacal_Pursuit_of_Glanvills_Will">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Monomaniacal Pursuit of Glanvill's Will</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>G. Large English Lovecraftiana Vol 5, Issue 4 date 2021  " " is a Lovecraftian short story by G. Large, published in Vol 5, Issue 4 of the Lovecraftiana zine in 2021. Synopsis The story tells the tale of a 17th century man named Pennington through later discussion by biographers. Pennington develops an obsession with a woman who is likely a figment of his own imagination, and he attempts to prove the strength of his will in order to gain her affections. This quest ultimately leads him to defeat death through sheer strength of will, but leaves him as a broken and slowly degenerating corpse. The story suggests that he may still be alive somewhere in the world.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>G. Large</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Issue 4</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Lovecraftiana Vol 5</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2021</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Tales">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Periodicals"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Arkham Tales</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A Magazine of Weird Fiction Nathan Shumate Nathan Shumate English Cold Fusion Media Empire Quarterly November 2008 Final Publication November 2009 : The Magazine of Weird Fiction was a short-lived digital periodical which ran for five issues between 2008 and 2009. Released on a quarterly schedule, it was edited by Nathan Shumate and published by Cold Fusion Media Empire. Issues November 2008 February 2009 May 2009 August 2009 November 2009 Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#From_the_Parchments_of_Pnom">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ClarkAshtonSmithWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>From the Parchments of Pnom</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The tsathoggua cycle is a text created by combining excerpts of two letters written by clark ashton smith to r. h. barlow in 1934 . In these letters, smith genially pretends to have consulted occult sources such as the fictional parchments of pnom . In addition to his own stories, smith also references the works of h.p. Lovecraft , lord dunsany, and james branch cabell .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Pseudofiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu Fiction</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Tsathoggua_Cycle"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2005</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>1934</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Joseph_S._Lovecraft">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSRelatives"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Joseph S. Lovecraft</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>28 March 1850</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lovecraft’s paternal great-grandfather. Joseph left Newton-Abbot, Devonshire, England with his wife, Mary, sometime between 1827 and 1831 to move to the United States, eventually settling in Rochester, New York. Joseph and two of his sons (Joseph Jr. and William) worked there as coopers, making barrels for flour.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isRelativeToR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1775</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#CharactersIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Goblin">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SpeciesIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdfs:label>Goblin</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Goblins are deformed humanoid fairies of short stature . They are known for stealing children, playing cruel pranks, and selling magic food to unwary humans .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ye_Booke_of_Monstres"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Illyana_Rasputina">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Illyana Rasputina</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Magik is a superhero in the fictional marvel universe, who was created in 1975, a b-list hero with a few comic book series . In her pre-teen years she was kidnapped by the sorcerer belasco and turned into a demonic sorceress . Within the dimension of limbo she is the sorceress supreme .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Chutes</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Ladders Magik (Illyana</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Storm Limited Series) Curse of the Mutants</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Giant-Sized X-Men Vol 1 1</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Len Wein</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Unholy_Dimensions">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Unholy Dimensions</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Jeffrey Thomas English Mythos Books Release Date October 2005 288  : Lovecraftian Tales is a collection of Cthulhu Mythos and Lovecraftian fiction and poetry by American Jeffrey Thomas . It was published by Mythos Books in October 2005, and received an ebook release with new cover art through Forma Street Press in May 2014. Overview Contents " The Bones of the Old Ones " " The Avatars of the Old Ones " " The Young of the Old Ones " "Red Glass" " I Married a Shoggoth " "The Ice Ship" " Servile " " Conglomerate " "Book Worm" " Through Obscure Glass " "The Servitors" " The Doom in the Room " " Out of the Belly of Sheol " "Ascending to Hell" " The Third Eye " " The Face of Baphomet " " Cells " "The House on the Plain" "The Fourth Utterance" " The Writing on the Wall " " Corpse Candles " " Yoo-Hoo, Cthulhu " " Lost Soul " " Pazuzu's Children " "The Boarded Window" "What Washes Ashore" " The Cellar Gods " Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jeffrey_Thomas"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>288</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 2005</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#You_Will_Never_Be_the_Same">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>You Will Never Be the Same</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Erica Satifka United States English Genre(s) Science fiction 01Publishing Whispers from the Abyss date November 2014 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos story by American Erica Satifka. Synopsis A junior officer aboard a ship which crosses the stars by travelling through dimensions attempts to assist when the pilot and crew are driven insane by something they have seen during the course of their journey. Although successful in his endeavour, he is shown not only a truth - that the realities are unraveling in the face of the Great Old Ones ' return - but a name: Cthulhu . History "" was published in the Kat Rocha -edited anthology Whispers from the Abyss by 01Publishing in November 2014. Behind the Mythos The name of the starship, the Wong-Danforth , appears to be a nod to one of the two surviving characters in H. P. Lovecraft 's novella At the Mountains of Madness , Danforth .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Erica Satifka</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Science fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Whispers_from_the_Abyss"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2014</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gerrit_Martense">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gerrit Martense</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Created by lovecraft is a fictional character created by h. p. lovecraft in his story "the lurking fear" he is the progenitor of the martense family whose hideous devolution is the subject of the story.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Lurking_Fear"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Blair_Reynolds">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Blair Reynolds</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Blair E. Reynolds Connection to the Mythos Artist Author Gaming supplement contributor United States Blair E. Reynolds (died 12 August 2021) was an American artist who also contributed material to at least one roleplaying game supplement, and wrote at least one short Cthulhu Mythos story. His output mostly appeared in supplements for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu horror roleplaying game produced by Pagan Publishing , and related material including periodicals, fiction anthologies, and comic books. Reynolds died on 12 August 2021 of congenital heart failure . Selected Works Short Fiction "Operation LOOKING GLASS" (1998) Gaming Material The Mysteries of Mesoamerica (2008, contributor) Artwork The Unspeakable Oath (1991 - ?, covers) Courting Madness (1992, cover) Tales of the Miskatonic Valley (1991, interior) The Resurrected Volume One: Grace Under Pressure (1993, interior) Walker in the Wastes (1994, cover) The Realm of Shadows (1997, cover and interior) The Resurrected III: Out of the Vault (2002, interior) The Mysteries of Mesoamerica (2008, cover and interior) See Also Images by External Links at Wikipedia at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads at RPGGeek References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Blair E. Reynolds</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Gaming supplement contributor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Vessel_%28Henry_Snider%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Vessel (Henry Snider)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Vessel (Henry Snider) Henry Snider English Genre(s) Weird fiction Lovecraftiana Vol 5, Issue 1 date September 2011  " The Vessel " is a Lovecraftian short story by Henry Snider. Synopsis On live television, a faith healer enacts a ritual involving the breaking of the Ten Commandments, resulting of the tearing of her body into a portal to some other hellish dimension. As the crew flee the scene, things begin to emerge… History Help! Wanted: Tales of On-the-Job Terror (2011) Horrific Beginnings (2015) Lovecraftiana Vol 5, Issue 1 (2020)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Henry Snider</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Issue 1</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Lovecraftiana Vol 5</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>September 2011</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Darkhold:_Pages_from_the_Book_of_Sins">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Darkhold: Pages from the Book of Sins</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Chris Cooper Richard Case, Mark McKenna, Rurik Tyler, Bob Downs, Mike Esposito English Marvel Comics Release Date Oct 1992- Jan 1994 Media Type Comic book is a 16 issue series written by Chris Cooper. Synopsis The Darkhold Redeemers opposed the Elder God Chthon 's plans by stopping those who are using the pages of the Darkhold and thereby unleashing its evil on the world.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Chris Cooper</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>Oct 1992- Jan 1994</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
