    <rdfs:label>John J. Weir</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1977</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Late correspondent of Lovecraft and editor of the fanzine Fantasmagoria.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1922</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jacques_Roulet">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CharactersIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdfs:label>Jacques Roulet</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>1598</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>16th century 1598 France is a historical figure from 16th century France who is mentioned in H. P. Lovecraft 's story " The Shunned House "; the story implies that Jacques is the ancestor of the story's fictional villains, Etienne and Paul Roulet . The story's narrator, discussing the history of Providence 's Roulet family, recalls an "ominous item in the annals of morbid horror": Compare this to the nonfiction account given in a blog on social history in central France: The narrator notes the "queer significance as to name and place", since the story's Roulet family also comes from Caude. He speculates that if the "Providence gossips" had "generally known of it...the coincidence of names would have brought some drastic and frightened action." References</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Acolyte_of_the_Flame">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Acolyte of the Flame</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>" (a translation of fragment mxi of the pnakotic manuscripts) " is a short cthulhu mythos story by the late american lin carter . It was first published in the yuletide 1985 issue of crypt of cthulhu by cryptic press .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lin_Carter"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Fantasy short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Yuletide_1985"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 1985</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Duane_W._Rimel">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsCoAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsCorrespondents"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Duane W. Rimel</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1996 September 30</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Rex weldon was an american science fiction, fantasy and erotica writer most well known for his collaborations with american horror writer howard phillips lovecraft . Lovecraft was a close friend who mentored him in his early writings as early as 1934 . After lovecraft's death, rimel continued to write mythos stories, such as " music of the stars " and the first glossary of the cthulhu mythos .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>Washington Asotin</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Rex Weldon</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1915 February 21</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Der_dunkle_Fremde">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Der dunkle Fremde</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>W. H. Pugmire Cover Artist Björn Craig German H. P. Lovecraft's Schriften des Grauens Genre(s) Lovecraftian fiction Blitz Release Date November 2018 233 ( The Dark Stranger ) is a German- collection of Lovecraftian horror by the late American W. H. Pugmire, published by Blitz in November 2018 as part of their H. P. Lovecraft's Schriften des Grauens ( Writings of Horror ) . The book is a translation of Pugmire's collection The Strange Dark One: Tales of Nyarlathotep (2012, Miskatonic River Press ) . Contents "Das Fenster zwischen den Welten" (trans. of "The Strange Dark One") "Das Tal aus Sand und Wind" ("Immortal Remains") "Hinter dem Tor der tiefen Träume" ("Past the Gates of Deepest Dreaming") "Das Zeichen des Gesichtslosen Gottes" ("One Last Theft") "Der Kuss des Nyarlathotep" (" The Hands That Reek and Smoke ") "Die Lauschende Leere" ("The Audient Void") "Die tausend Säulen von Irem" ("Some Bacchante of Irem") "Der Blick hinter die Welt" ("To See Beyond") References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#W._H._Pugmire"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Lovecraftian fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>233</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>German</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>H. P. Lovecraft's Schriften des Grauens</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>November 2018</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#ceasedOnC">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
