  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Challenge_from_Beyond">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RobertEHowardWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Challenge from Beyond</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>This is the second of two round-robin stories named, both commissioned by editor julius schwartz and published in the same issue of fantasy magazine, in 1935 . It was written by howard phillips lovecraft , frank belknap long , robert e. howard , c. l. moore , and abraham merritt . A man named george campbell was camping out in a canada forest when he heard a faint noise and attempted to throw a rock at the creature .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Howard Phillips Lovecraft C. L. Moore A. Merritt Robert E. Howard Frank Belknap Long</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_True_Telling_of_the_Terror_That_Came_to_Red_Hook">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A True Telling of the Terror That Came to Red Hook</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story tells of two previously unknown participants of the events recounted in h. p. lovecraft's short story . They discover the shocking truth of robert suydam 's occult activities and work towards bringing about his downfall . This involves spiriting him away from the ship aboard which his wife was murdered and killing the creature he had become in the catacombs beneath new york .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#William_Meikle"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Heroes_of_Red_Hook"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2016</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Orrori_e_meraviglie_a_Sesqua_Valley">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Orrori e meraviglie a Sesqua Valley</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>W. H. Pugmire Italy Italian Dagon Press Release Date June 2022 320 ( Horrors and wonders in Sesqua Valley ) is an Italian- collection of Lovecraftian fiction by the late American W. H. Pugmire . Running to a total of 320 , it was published in a hardcover format by Dagon Press (an Italian not to be confused with the now-defunct British small press of the same name) in June 2022. Contents Introduzione by Adriano Monti-Buzzetti "Resti immortali" (translation of "Immortal Remains") "Il fiore del sacrificio" (translation of "The Bloom of Sacrifice") "La prole intessuta" (translation of " The Woven Offspring ") "Il vuoto che ascolta" (translation of "The Audient Void") "L'ultimo furto" (translation of "One Last Theft") "Lo strano oscuro" (trans. of "The Strange Dark One") "Oltre le porte del sogno più profondo" (translation of "Past the Gates of Deepest Dreaming") "Ricerca onirica" (translation of "A Quest of Dream") "Estasi di paura" (translation of "An Ecstasy of Fear") "Lo splendore del capro" (translation of "This Splendor of the Goat") "Il mi amico, Wilum Pugmire" by S. T. Joshi</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#W._H._Pugmire"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>Italy</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>320</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Italian</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>June 2022</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Yuletide_1988_1/2">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu Yuletide 1988 1/2</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Ralph Rayburn Phillips Cover Artist Mike T. Cherry English Cryptic Publications Release Date December 1988 40 Preceded by Yuletide 1988 Followed by Candlemas 1988 is the second of two issues released as the 61st instalment of Robert M. Price 's long-running fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . It was published by Cryptic Publications in December of that year. The majority of this shorter-than-usual edition is devoted to reviews and letters. Contents Editorial Shards by Robert M. Price "Why Abdul Al Hazred Went Mad" by D. R. Smith Reviews by Stefan Dziemianowicz and Robert M. Price Letters by Susan Michaud, Darrell Schweitzer , Ros Calverley, David F. Godwin, Robert H. Knox, Mark Rainey , Paul A. Roales, Charles Garofalo , Dennis Lien, David Barker, Kevin A. Ross , Stefan R. Dziemianowicz, Peter Cannon , Donald R. Burleson, Ph.D. , Tani Jantsang , Russell Hanson, James Rockhill, Kenneth W. Faig, Jr., Wilum Pugmire , Rah Hoffman, and Sean Branney</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>40</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>December 1988</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Copping_Squid">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Copping Squid</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Perilous press and other mythos tales is a short story by the late american michael shea . Late-night liquor store clerk ricky is blackmailed by one of his customers, a man named andre, into witnessing his communion with a dark god . What ricky sees changes him in a fundamental way, and he is left to decide whether he wishes to follow the same path which andre trod .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Michael_Shea"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Other Mythos Tales</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2009</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Rue_dAuseil">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Rue d'Auseil</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a fictional street in an unnamed french city that is generally assumed to be paris . It appears as the of erich zann 's apartment in h. p. lovecraft's short story " the music of erich zann "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>City street</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Erich_Zann"/>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#France"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Music_of_Erich_Zann"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Regnar_Lodbrugs_Epicedium">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Regnar Lodbrug's Epicedium</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The acolyte is a poem by the late american howard phillips lovecraft . It describes in brief the career of regnar lodbrug, the legendary viking warrior more commonly known in english as ragnar lodbrok . The spelling that lovecraft uses is closer to the danish spelling of his name: regnar lodbrog .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The Acolyte</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>June 1944</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>1914</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sodatha">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sodatha</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as the slithering god and the hidden one . A spawn of the outer god yog-sothoth . Lived within the walled city of hanghrara in the kingdom of zamora during the hyborian age .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tyler_Omichinski">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tyler Omichinski</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Games designer Winnipeg, Canada Canadian Horror http://www.omichinski.com/ is a games designer. He has written a number of sourcebooks for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game as well as Conan the Barbarian , and has contributed material to several publications devoted to Cthulhu Mythos gaming. Omichinski's works include publications from Stygian Fox , a former third-party content provider for Call of Cthulhu , and gaming articles for the roleplaying periodical Bayt al Azif . Selected Works Author Autophagia (2019, Stygian Fox ) The Wild Hunt (2019, Stygian Fox) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads at BoardGameGeek</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>Canada Winnipeg</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>Canadian</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://www.omichinski.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Castle_of_Circles">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Castle of Circles</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mike Lee English Rogue Planet Press Swords Against Cthulhu II: Hyperborean Nights date January 2017 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos story by American Mike Lee . It was published in the sword-and-sorcery anthology Swords Against Cthulhu II: Hyperborean Nights by Rogue Planet Press in January 2017. Synopsis Petty criminals Law Pferzonne and Valzarum find themselves indebted to the powerful sorcerer Umma Xatha, a former apprentice to the great wizard Eibon , who enlists the pair in a scheme to retrieve a complete and annotated copy of his former master's greatest work, the fabled Book of Eibon . After travelling to the tower containing their prize Xatha betrays the thieves, attempting to sacrifice them to the minor god Kathatana which dwells within. Fortunately for the two men, Kathatana bears them no ill will, having only designs for Xatha, and they are able to escape while the vengeful deity consumes their treacherous partner.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mike_Lee"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Swords_Against_Cthulhu_II:_Hyperborean_Nights"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>January 2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Demon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Demon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Chris Claremont John Byrne John Byrne Terry Austin English Marvel Comics Release Date March 1981 Media Type Comic book " " is a one-off story featured in Marvel Comics ' Uncanny X-Men . Synopsis Shadowcat finds herself alone with a N'Garai in the Xavier Mansion, the last to make it through the now-sealed dimensional portal. Although it nearly succeeds in killing her several times, Kitty manages to finally destroy it using the Blackbird's jet engines. Publication History "" was published in issue #143, Vol 1 of Uncanny X-Men in March 1981 by Marvel Comics. It was written by Chris Claremont and John Byrne, with artwork supplied by Byrne and Terry Austin.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Chris Claremont</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>John Byrne</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>March 1981</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sam_Raimi">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPeople"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sam Raimi</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Samuel Marshall "Sam" Raimi Connection to the Mythos Director Birth Date 29 October 1959 Royal Oak, Michigan, USA United States  Samuel Marshall "Sam" Raimi (born 29 October 1959) is an American director, screenwriter, and producer who began his career in the low-budget film industry but has risen to direct blockbuster productions such as the Spider-Man trilogy (2002-2007) and Doctor Strange in the Multiverse of Madness (2022) . For the purposes of this wiki, Raimi is notable for his creation of the multimedia Evil Dead franchise, which follows the (mis)adventures of bumbling store clerk Ash Williams as he battles various forces thrown into his path by the Necronomicon Ex-Mortis , an evil grimoire possessed by a demon . During the course of his escapades, Williams has crossed paths with many elements of the Cthulhu Mythos . Selected Works Film The Evil Dead (1981) The Evil Dead II (1987) Army of Darkness (1992) Television Ash vs Evil Dead (2015-2018) External Links at Wikipedia at Britannica at iMDB at Rotten Tomatoes References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Michigan Royal Oak</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Samuel Marshall Sam Raimi</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Director</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>29 October 1959</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mlok">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Mlok</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>First appearance "The Priest of " Created by Gary Myers is a creature, possibly a Great Old One who landed on Earth in ancient times somewhere near the Bnazic desert (EXP: "The Priest of " ) known to devour human victims for sustenance. A bloody cult was created around and it became strong in the city of Drinen . (EXP: "The Gods of Drinen", "The Tomb of Neb" )</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Priest of Mlok</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gary_Myers"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Queen_of_Hearts">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Queen of Hearts</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The was created by lewis carroll in 1865, first appearing in alice's adventures in wonderland . In 2008, the character was reimagined for zenescope entertainment's grimm fairy tales fictional universe .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>1st ( ADJ ): Grimm Fairy Tales: Return to Wonderland Vol 1 0</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Alice's Adventures in Wonderland</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Lewis Carroll</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Haunter_of_the_Dark:_Collected_Short_Stories_Volume_Three">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Haunter of the Dark: Collected Short Stories Volume Three</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft Cover Artist Nichola Trayler-Barbrook United Kingdom English Genre(s) Fantasy, Horror, Science fiction short stories Wordsworth Editions Limited Release Date 2011 Media Type Print (Paperback) Preceded by The Horror in the Museum: Collected Short Stories Volume Two Followed by The Lurking Fear: Collected Short Stories Volume Four is a collection of fantasy, horror and science fiction short stories by the American H. P. Lovecraft . Contents Introduction by M. J. Elliott " The Haunter of the Dark " " The Doom That Came to Sarnath " " The Statement of Randolph Carter " " The Cats of Ulthar " " Celephaïs " " The Other Gods " " Herbert West--Reanimator " " The Unnamable " " The Shunned House " " The Horror at Red Hook " " Pickman's Model " " The Silver Key " " The Strange High House in the Mist " " The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath " " The Colour Out of Space " " History of the Necronomicon " " Fungi from Yuggoth " " The Shadow Over Innsmouth " " The Dreams in the Witch House " " The Thing on the Doorstep " " The Shadow Out of Time " " Supernatural Horror in Literature "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United Kingdom</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Fantasy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Science fiction short stories</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2011</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Josh_Reynolds">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Josh Reynolds</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Joshua Reynolds Connection to the Mythos Author Editor South Carolina, United States https://joshuamreynolds.co.uk/ is an author and editor who specialises in horror, science fiction and fantasy. Born and raised in South Carolina, United States, Reynolds has since moved to Sheffield, England. He has written over thirty novels and a multitude of shorter works, including several pieces set in the Cthulhu Mythos of H. P. Lovecraft , perhaps most notably a trilogy of novels for Fantasy Flight Games ' Arkham Horror Fiction line. Reynolds' non-Mythos works include tie-in fiction for properties such as Legend of the Five Rings , and extensive writing for Black Library, Games Workshop 's fiction imprint for their various Warhammer settings. Selected Bibliography Short Fiction " The Dunwich Father " (2003) " Big Nasty " (2003) "The Far Deep" (2011) " Eliza " (2012) " Dead Man's Tongue " (2014) " The Yoth Protocols " (2014) " Squatter's Rights " (2017) " Those Folk Below " (2017) Novels Wrath of N'Kai (2020) Shadows of Pnath (2023) Song of Carcosa (2024) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>United States South Carolina</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Joshua Reynolds</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://joshuamreynolds.co.uk/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Feast_with_a_Few_Strangers">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Feast with a Few Strangers</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Edward p. berglund is an american and editor of the cthulhu mythos . It was first published in the 1974 anthology spoor anthology by fort necessity press . A slightly altered version was also included in abortive zine in lovecraft's shadow .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Edward_P._Berglund"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Spoor_Anthology"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1974</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pickmans_Press">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SmallPress"/>
    <rdfs:label>Pickman's Press</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Current Status Active United States Edgewood, New Mexico http://www.pickmanspress.com/index.html is a publisher of Lovecraftian horror , weird fiction, and dark fantasy, based in Edgewood, New Mexico, USA. Specialising in weird fantasy, they have a particular focus on works related to those of H. P. Lovecraft 's. Sselected Publications Anthologies Corporate Cthulhu: Twenty Tales of Bureaucratic Nightmare , Edward Stasheff , editor (2018) Collections The Averoigne Legacy: Tribute Tales in the World of Clark Ashton Smith , Edward Stasheff, editor (2019) Psychopompos , by H. P. Lovecraft (2020) Sorcery Against Caesar: The Complete Simon of Gitta Short Stories , by Richard L. Tierney (2020) Fungi from Yuggoth , by H. P. Lovecraft (2021) Reassuring Tales , by T. E. D. Klein (2021) The Drums of Chaos , by Richard L. Tierney (2021) The Averoigne Archives: The Complete Averoigne Tales of Clark Ashton Smith , by Clark Ashton Smith (2021) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://www.pickmanspress.com/index.html</ex:hasWebsiteR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Providence_%28comic_book%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Providence (comic book)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Providence Alan Moore Jacen Burrows Cover Artist Jacen Burrows English Avatar Press Release Date May 2015 - March 2017 Media Type Comic book Providence is a twelve-issue comic book limited series, written by Alan Moore and illustrated by Jacen Burrows, published by Avatar Press. It is set in the same continuity as the " The Courtyard " and the 4-issue Neonomicon series, both also written by Moore. The premise of Providence is that Lovecraft 's stories are loosely based on the experiences of Robert Black, a queer Jewish journalist who is exploring New England in hopes of garnering material for a novel based on occult outsiders. This conceit allows Moore to rework Lovecraft's familiar fictions in his own unique style. Issues "The Yellow Sign" "The Hook" "A Lurking Fear" "White Apes" "In the Walls" "Out of Time" "The Picture" "The Key" "Outsiders" "The Haunted Palace" "The Unnamable" "The Book" External Link Facts in the Case of Alan Moore's Providence , annotations</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Alan_Moore"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 2015 - March 2017</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jesse_Dedman">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonFictionAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Jesse Dedman</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Pseudonym(s) Deadman Connection to the Mythos Editor Artist Author , who also goes under the pseudonym Deadman , is an editor, artist and author who has produced several works centred on H. P. Lovecraft and the greater Cthulhu Mythos . Most of these works have been issued through the small-press publishing house Deadman's Tome, a company which Dedman owns and operates. Selected Works Anthologies Cthulhu Christmas Special and Other Lovecraftian Yuletide Tales (2017, with William C. Marchese ) Ancient Ones II (2018) Deep Fried Horror Cthulhu Cheese Burger (2020) Short Fiction " Cthulhu Gets A Job " (2020) Non-Fiction "Your Favorite Author Is Racist, Now What?" (2018) Cover Art Cthulhu Christmas Special and Other Lovecraftian Yuletide Tales (2017, with William C. Marchese ) Ancient Ones II (2018) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database Deadman at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Deadman</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
