  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Stuart_Gordon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPeople"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Stuart Gordon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>24 March 2020</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Stuart Alan Gordon Connection to the Mythos Director Screenwriter Birth Date 11 August 1947 Chicago, Illinois, USA Death Date 24 March 2020 Los Angeles, California, USA United States Horror Science Fiction Film Noir Stuart Alan Gordon (11 August 1947-24 March 2020) was an American film director, screenwriter, producer and self-confessed fan of H. P. Lovecraft . He filmed a number of works - both for the big screen and television - either based on or influenced by Lovecraft's fiction; Gordon frequently collaborated with screenwriter Dennis Paoli and actors Jeffrey Combs and Barbara Crampton on these productions. Selected Works Screen Re-Animator (1985) From Beyond (1986) Castle Freak (1995) Dagon (2001) Masters of Horror Episode " Dreams in the Witch-House " (2005) " From the Dark " (2009) Theatre Re-Animator: The Musical (2011 onwards)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Chicago Illinois</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Stuart Alan Gordon</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Director</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Screenwriter</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>11 August 1947</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Warm">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Warm</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The madness of cthulhu: volume 1 was first published in the anthology the madness of cthulhu: volume 1 in october 2014 . It is a retelling of lovecraft's 1927 short story " pickman's model " told from the perspective of the ghoul . The ghoul is something of an outcast from its own society and first inspired richard upton pickman's controversial works .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Darrell_Schweitzer"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The Madness of Cthulhu: Volume 1</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2014</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gate_of_the_Gods">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gate of the Gods</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Oliver Smith English Rogue Planet Press Lovecraftiana Vol 5, Issue 4 date 2021  " " is a Lovecraftian poem by Oliver Smith. It was published in Vol 5, Issue 4 of digital zine Lovecraftiana by Rogue Planet Press in 2021. Synopsis The story tells of a monstrous creature known as the Dreamer who falls to earth amidst the "ruins of another world". It then goes on to detail how the nascent human civilisation bound the being to its will before it rose against them and crushed its former jailors. Behind the Mythos Cthulhu is often referred to as "The Dreamer" in Mythos fiction; however, primitive humans besting a creature such as this and taking "his crown" would not seem to fit with the most common interpretation of a Great Old One .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Oliver Smith</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Issue 4</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Lovecraftiana Vol 5</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2021</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shoggoth_Issue_2_%28March_1993%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Shoggoth Issue 2 (March 1993)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Shoggoth issue 2 was the second and final issue of chris a. Masters' short-lived fanzine . It ran to a total of 92 in an a5 format and was self-published in march 1993 . The poem "the r'lyeh rap" was credited as being written by deejay beejay and grandmaster sequeira .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>92</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shoggoth"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>March 1993</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
