  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#John_Langan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonFictionAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>John Langan</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name John Paul Langan Connection to the Mythos Author Birth Date 6 July 1969 United States United States https://johnpaullangan.wordpress.com/ John Paul Langan (born 6 July 1969) is an Bram Stoker Award winning American horror, fantasy and science fiction author, as well as a university professor. He has written several Lovecraftian short stories: these have appeared in anthologies such as The Book of Cthulhu , Autumn Cthulhu , New Cthulhu: The Recent Weird , Innsmouth Nightmares , Through a Mythos Darkly , and Black Wings II . In addition to these, he has also written a handful of non-fiction works on the genre which have appeared in periodicals including Lovecraft Studies and Lovecraft Annual . Langan was also one of the founders of the Shirley Jackson Awards, given to works of fantasy, horror and suspense, and served on the judge's panel for their first three years . Selected Bibliography Short Fiction " Mirror Fishing " (2017) External Links at Wikipedia at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>United States</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>John Paul Langan</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://johnpaullangan.wordpress.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>6 July 1969</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Houndwife">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CaitlinRKiernanWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Houndwife</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Caitlin R. Kiernan English Genre(s) Cthulhu Mythos Sirenia Digest date March 2010 "" is a Cthulhu Mythos short story by Caitlín R. Kiernan that first appeared in the March 2010 issue of Sirenia Digest (#52). Synopsis The story is a sequel to H. P. Lovecraft 's " The Hound " and " The Haunter of the Dark ," with the amulet from the former tale being found in a Savannah curiosity shop by a young woman, who subsequently becomes a willing sacrifice for the Church of Starry Wisdom . It's also a continuation of Kiernan's story " Les Fleurs Empoisonnées ," which is set in Savannah. History The story was republished in the anthology Black Wings II ( PS Publishing , 2012). It later appeared in Beneath an Oil-Dark Sea: The Best of Caitlín R. Kiernan (Volume Two) ( Subterranean Press , 2015), and in Kiernan's collection Houses Under the Sea: Mythos Tales (Subterranean Press, 2019).</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Caitlin_R._Kiernan"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sirenia_Digest"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 2010</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scott_R._Jones">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonFictionAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Scott R. Jones</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Editor Canadian Horror https://scottrjoneswriter.com/ is a Canadian author and editor who has written several works set in the greater Cthulhu Mythos inspired by the works of H. P. Lovecraft ; these have appeared in anthologies such as The Children of Gla'aki and Return of the Old Ones (both 2017, Dark Regions Press ). In addition to his works of fiction, he has also written several non-fiction pieces focusing on the Mythos. Jones's work has been highly praised by accomplished British author Ramsey Campbell . Selected Bibliography Short Fiction "Across a River of Stars" (2016) " The Spike " (2017) " Shout / Kill / Revel / Repeat " (2017) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>Canadian</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://scottrjoneswriter.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Exo-Parasite">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonSapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Exo-Parasite</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The exo-parasites are a fictional race in the marvel universe which first appeared in 2015 in issue #0 of the 6th volume of the avengers comic book series . They are an eldritch parasitic species that lives in exo-space, the space between the realities of the multiverse .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Avengers Vol 6 #0</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>AI Ewing</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Kenneth Rocafort</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Aboleth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Aboleth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The aboleths are an aberrant race from the fictional universes of dnd and pathfinder . They are fish-like amphibious aberrations whom reside in the desolate waters of the far realms and the dark depths of the underdark . They possess incredible psionic abilities, which gives them the ability to manipulate and control lesser beings .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_by_Gaslight">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu by Gaslight</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Second edition was released in 1989, this time in book-only format . In 2012, a third edition was released, edited by kevin ross . A fourth edition was published for chaosium's 7th edition call of cthulhu in 2024 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1986 2012 1989</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Gleaming_Spiral_%28Call_of_Cthulhu_LCG%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Gleaming Spiral (Call of Cthulhu LCG)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Gleaming Spiral Fantasy Flight Games Publication Date 2010 Living Card Game Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game The Gleaming Spiral is an expansion pack for Fantasy Flight Games ' Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game . Overview The fifth Asylum Pack in The Rituals of the Order Cycle series of expansions, The Gleaming Spiral is divided into the standard format of three duplicate sets of 20 unique cards for a total of 60, with the cards contained divided between the existing factions. First released in 2010, it was discontinued in 2015 with the demise of Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game . Contents Trial Judge (Character; The Agency) Catastrophic Explosion (Event; The Agency) While He Sleeps (Event; Cthulhu ) Slave to the Undivided Mind (Event; Cthulhu) Moonbound Byakhee (Character; Hastur ) Unhealthy Fixation (Support; Hastur) Scalpel (Support; Hastur) Jamie Winthrop, Whiz Kid (Character; Miskatonic University ) Lecturing Historian (Character; Miskatonic University) Full Ride Scholarship (Event; Miskatonic University) Shudde M'ell , The Burrower Beneath (Character; Shub-Niggurath ) Telepathic Chthonian (Character; Shub-Niggurath) Ground Tremors (Event; Shub-Niggurath) Meticulous Scribe (Character; Silver Twilight) Unscrupulous Acquisitionist (Character; Silver Twilight) Disturbing Auction (Event; Silver Twilight) Degenerate Gambler (Character; Syndicate) Scandal (Event; Syndicate) Many-angled Thing (Character; Yog-Sothoth ) Hermetic Text (Support; Yog-Sothoth)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2010</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Deepest,_Darkest_Eden">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HyperboreanCycle"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Deepest, Darkest Eden</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cody Goodfellow , editor Cover Artist Mark E. Rogers English Hyperborean Cycle Miskatonic River Press Release Date August 1, 2013 229 : New Tales of Hyperborea is a collection of stories set in Clark Ashton Smith 's Hyperborea , edited by Cody Goodfellow and published by Miskatonic River Press in 2013. With two exceptions, the pieces included were original to the volume. Contents "Wider Than the World: A Door to Hyperborea" introduction by Cody Goodfellow "Hostage" by Nick Mamatas "To Walk Night... Alone..." by Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. "In Old Commoriom" by Darrell Schweitzer "Yhoundeh Fades" (poem) by Ann K. Schwader "Coils of the Ouroboros: The History of Avasquiddoc the Apprentice" by Cody Goodfellow (2010) " Daughter of the Elk Goddess" by John R. Fultz "The Darkness Below" by Brian M. Sammons "The Conquest of Rhizopium" by Dieter Meier "Zolamin and the Mad God" by Lisa Morton "Having Set Out to Be Vanquished" by Garrett Cook "The Lost Archetype" by Brian Stableford "One Last Task for Athammaus" by Ran Cartwright "The Beauties of Polarion" by Don Webb "The Frigid Ilk of Sarn Kathool" by Marc Laidlaw "The Debt Owed Abhoth" by Robert M. Price "Return of the Crystal" by Charles Schneider "Rodney LaSalle Has a Job Waiting in Commoriom" by John Shirley "The Winter of Atiradarinsept" by Zak Jarvis "The Door from Earth" by Jesse Bullington (2011) "Weird of the White Sybil" (poem) by Ann K. Schwader</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cody_Goodfellow"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>editor</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>229</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hyperborean_Cycle"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2013 August 1</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lee_Gibbons">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lee Gibbons</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Artist Horror is an artist who’s main interest to this wiki is his work for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game, for which he has produced numerous covers, particularly during the game's earlier decades. Selected Works Cover Art Nightmare in Norway (1985) Green and Pleasant Land (1987) Cthulhu Classics (1989) Blood Brothers (1990) Arkham Unveiled (1990) Horror on the Orient Express (1991) Call of Cthulhu Core Rulebook (1992) Blood Brothers 2 (1992) The Compact Arkham Unveiled (1995) The Complete Masks of Nyarlathotep (1996) H. P. Lovecraft's Arkham (2003) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Sister_City">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#BrianLumleyWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Sister City</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Brian Lumley English Genre(s) Cthulhu Mythos Appears in Tales of the Cthulhu Mythos Arkham House 1969 "" is a short story by Brian Lumley in the Cthulhu Mythos genre. One of Lumley's first stories, it was written in 1967 and first published in Arkham House 's 1969 collection Tales of the Cthulhu Mythos . Lumley later incorporated this story into his novel Beneath the Moors . Synopsis The story is mainly narrated by Robert Krug, an archaeologist with peculiar physical abnormalities who claims to have discovered the ruins of Sarnath and Ib --and, beneath the moors of Yorkshire, Ib's sister city, Lh-yib. Behind the Mythos The story contains the first references to people and places that were to recur frequently in Lumley's Mythos fiction--including the archaeologists Sir Amery Wendy-Smith and Gordon Walmsley , the lost city of G'harne in Africa, and the Oakdeene Sanitarium .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tales_of_the_Cthulhu_Mythos"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>1969</ex:isPublishedAsW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Klarkash-Ton">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Klarkash-Ton</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>The royal court of Atlantis</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Zhothaqquah</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The hierophant is a character created by the late american author h. p. lovecraft as a fictional version of his close friend clark ashton smith . The name also appears in " the battle that ended the century " (1934), a parody of boxing reporting set in the then-distant year 2000 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Incubus_of_Atlantis"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Roodmas_1983">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu Roodmas 1983</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Thomas Brown United States English Crypt of Cthulhu Release Date May 1983 52 Preceded by Eastertide 1983 Followed by St. John's Eve 1983 is the thirteenth issue of Robert M. Price 's long-running Lovecraftian fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Edited by Price, it ran to a total of 52 in an A5 format and was self-published in May 1983. Cover art was supplied by Thomas Brown. Contents Editorial Shards by Robert M. Price "The Inklings, Lovecraft and the Kalems" by Peter H. Cannon "Middle-Earth, Narnia and Lovecraft's Dream World: Comparative World-Views in Fantasy" by Leigh D. Blackmore "Lovecraft's Theology of Fear and Williams' Theology of Romance" by Edward T. Babinski "Kadath and Mordor: The Quest in Lovecraft and Tolkien" by Ben P. Indick "The Dueling Cosmoses of H. P. Lovecraft and G. K. Chesterton" by Edward T. Babinski "Surprised by Horror: The Fantasy Short Stories of C. S. Lewis" by S. T. Joshi "Williams, Waite, and the Golden Dawn" by Robert C. Carey "A "Lovecraftian" Scene in Perelandra" by Charles Garofalo "Screwtape's Letter to Cthulhu" by Robert M. Price Letters by David Drake, Ramsey Campbell , Brian Lumley , Gahan Wilson , Robert Bloch , Darrell Schweitzer , S. T. Joshi, Carolyn Lee Boyd, Roger Bryant, Ken Faig, Steve Behrends , Robert Finnan, Jim Boisclair, Bob Finnan, Roger G. Knuth, Michael H. Cline, and Daniel Gobbett Reviews by Robert M. Price and C. D. Whateley</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>52</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 1983</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#hasDescendantR">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
