  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weirder_Shadows_over_Innsmouth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Weirder Shadows over Innsmouth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Stephen Jones , editor English Fedogan &amp; Bremer Titan Books Release Date 2013 480 Preceded by Weird Shadows over Innsmouth , published in 2013, is the third in a series of anthologies collecting stories inspired by H. P. Lovecraft 's " The Shadow Over Innsmouth "; like its predecessors, Shadows Over Innsmouth and Weird Shadows Over Innsmouth , it was edited by Stephen Jones . It was published in hardcover by Fedogan &amp; Bremer and in paperback by Titan Books. Contents The contents include (with date of original publication): "Introduction: Weirder Shadows..." by Stephen Jones (2013) " Fish Bride ", by Caitlín R. Kiernan (2009) "Innsmouth Bane" by John S. Glasby (2005) " Innsmouth Clay " by August Derleth and H. P. Lovecraft (1971) "Into the Water" by Simon Kurt Unsworth (2013) " On the Reef " by Caitlín R. Kiernan (2011) "Richard Riddle, Boy Detective in 'The Case of the French Spy'" by Kim Newman (2005) "Rising, Not Dreaming" by Angela Slatter (2011) "The Archbishop's Well" by Reggie Oliver (2013) "The Chain" by Michael Marshall Smith (2013) "The Hag Stone" by Conrad Williams (2013) "The Long Last Night" by Brian Lumley (2012) " The Port " by H. P. Lovecraft (poem) (1930) "The Same Deep Waters as You" by Brian Hodge (2013) "The Song of Sighs" by Angela Slatter (2013) " The Transition of Elizabeth Haskings " by Caitlín R. Kiernan (2013) "The Winner" by Ramsey Campbell (2005) "You Don't Want to Know" by Adrian Cole (2013) "Afterwords: Contributors' Notes" by Stephen Jones (2013)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Stephen_Jones"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>editor</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>480</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2013</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Frank_Festa">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Frank Festa</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Pseudonym(s) Full Force Frank Connection to the Mythos Editor Author Germany is a German author, editor and publisher who works primarily in the Lovecraftian and weird fiction genres. The majority of his editorial output is focused on bringing German-language translations of English horror works to print: to this end, he founded the Festa publishing house in April 2001. Perhaps his most relevant works to this wiki is the H. P. Lovecraft's Bibliothek des Schreckens line, which translates many Cthulhu Mythos stories for German consumption. Selected Works Editor Der Cthulhu-Mythos: 1976 - 2002 (2003) H. P. Lovecrafts Necronomicon (2018) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>Germany</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Full Force Frank</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ialdagorth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OuterGods"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ialdagorth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Dark Devourer</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as The Dark Devourer First appearance "Servants of the Coils" Created by Kevin Eric Sheridan is a cousin and servitor of Azathoth . He appears as a black, shapeless and malevolent mist. The sight of the monstrousity is said to be unnerving if not traumatizing.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Servants of the Coils</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Kevin Eric Sheridan</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dagon_%28Fred_Chappell%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dagon (Fred Chappell)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The novel blends the southern gothic and lovecraftian elements . Whether the evil abundantly displayed in the book is supernatural or not is left to the reader's imagination . Dagon was named best foreign book of the year by the french academy in 1971 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fred_Chappell"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>German</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1968</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#King_of_Tears">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>King of Tears</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is the god of the subtle realms. Dimensions beyond those which man perceives . He was summoned to earth by johnny sorrow, which the justice society of america battled . Years later it was once again summoned to earth by johnny sorrow and the injustice society, who this time planned to enslave the .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dennis_Paoli">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPeople"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dennis Paoli</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Scriptwriter Horror is a scriptwriter specialising in the horror . He has written a number of works both based on and inspired by the fiction of H. P. Lovecraft , and frequently collaborated with director Stuart Gordon before Gordon's death in 2020. Selected Works Screen Re-Animator (1985) From Beyond (1986) Pulse Pounders (1988) Castle Freak (1995) Dagon (2001) Suitable Flesh (2023) Masters of Horror Episode " Dreams in the Witch-House " (2005) Theatre Re-Animator: The Musical</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Scriptwriter</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Whispers_from_the_Abyss">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Whispers from the Abyss</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Josh Finney English Genre(s) Weird fiction Flash fiction 01Publishing Followed by Vol. 2: The Horrors That Are &amp; Shall Be  : An Anthology of H. P. Lovecraft Inspired Short Fiction is a collection of short stories which are either connected to or inspired by the works of Howard Phillips Lovecraft and the Cthulhu Mythos . Overview Edited by Kat Rocha and published by 01Publishing , contains a total of twenty-nine flash fiction stories. Both trade paperback and ebook editions were released, the latter through Amazon's digital Kindle platform. Contents Introduction by Alasdair Stuart " Iden-Inshi " by Greg Stolze " Pushing Back " by J. C. Hemphill " Nation of Disease: The Rise &amp; Fall of a Canadian Legend " by Jonathan Sharp " When We Change " by Mason Ian Bundschuh " Nutmeat " by Martin Hill Ortiz " The Last Tweet " by Charles Black " Secrets in Storage " by Greg van Eekhout and Tim Pratt " The Well " by Tim Jeffreys " The Neon Morgue " by Nathan Wunner " Fear and Loathing in Innsmouth: Richard Nixon's Revenge " by Jason Andrew " My Friend Fishfinger by Daisy, Age 7 " by David Tallerman " Chasing Sunset " by A. C. Wise " I Do the Work of the Bone Queen " by John R. Fultz " Suck It Up, Get It Done " by Brandon Barrows " The Substance in the Sound " by W. B. Stickel " Stone City, Old As Immeasurable Time " by Kelda Crich " Hideous Interview with Brief Man " by Nick Mamatas " The Sea, Like Broken Glass " by Silvia Moreno-Garcia " The Decorative Water Feature of Nameless Dread " by James Brogden " Henry " by Lance Axt " My Stalk " by Aaron J. French " Give Me That Old Time Religion " by Lee Finney " Afraid of Dobermans " by Chad Fifer " Leviathan " by Nicholas Almand " Horrorscope " by Charles Black " The Jar of Aten-Hor " by Kat Rocha " The Floor " by Jeff Provine " Waiting " by Dennis Detwiller " Other People's Houses " by Sarena Ulibarri " You Will Never Be the Same " by Erica Satifka " Death Wore Greasepaint " by Josh Finney</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Flash fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chris_A._Masters">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonFictionAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Chris A. Masters</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>5 January 2022</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Chris Anagnostopoulos Pseudonym(s) C. A. Masters Connection to the Mythos Editor Birth Date 1961 Melbourne, Victoria, Australia Death Date 5 January 2022 Australia (real name Chris Anagnostopoulos, 1961 - 5 January 2022) was an Australian author and editor who worked on a number of weird fiction projects during the course of his career. He is perhaps best known for his editorial work on the Lovecraftian fanzines Shoggoth (1992 - 1993) and EOD (1991 - 1994), both of which he founded and edited for their complete run. In addition to these, Masters also wrote a handful of short stories and non-fiction pieces around the genre, the majority of which were published in the aforementioned zines. Selected Bibliography Editor EOD (1991 - 1994) Shoggoth (1992 - 1993) Non-Fiction " Blasphemous Tomes: A Guide to Lovecraftiana in the Small Press " (1992, with Leigh Blackmore and Bryce Stevens ) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Austlitz</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>http://example.org/lovecraft#Australia Melbourne. Victoria</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Chris Anagnostopoulos</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>http://example.org/lovecraft#Australia</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>C. A. Masters</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1961</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dagons_Bell">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#BrianLumleyWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dagon's Bell</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Brian Lumley English Genre(s) Cthulhu Mythos Weirdbook 23/24 Date of writing 1983 date 1988 "" is a Cthulhu Mythos story by Brian Lumley , written in 1983 and first published in Weirdbook 23/24 (1988). Synopsis Moving into an ancient farm on the North Sea coast has a deleterious effect on a young couple's health and sanity. History Following its initial appearance in Weirdbook, "" was reprinted in the anthology Shadows Over Innsmouth ( Fedogan &amp; Bremer , 1994) and served as the title story of Lumley's collection and Other Discords (New English Library, 1994). It also appeared in Lumley's Beneath the Moors and Darker Places (Tor, 2002) and Haggopian and Other Stories (Subterranean Press, 2008). References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>24</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Weirdbook 23</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1988</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>1983</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shadow_Over_Innsmouth_%28Dynamite_Comics%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Shadow Over Innsmouth (Dynamite Comics)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The shadow is a 2014 one-shot comic book published by dynamite entertainment . It is inspired by the original story of the same name by h.p. Lovecraft . There are basic plot similarities to the earlier lovecraft's book .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Ron Marz</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>The Shadow</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2014</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gleeth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gleeth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Blind God of the Moon</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as The Blind God of the Moon First appearance EXP : Mad Moon of Dreams Created by Brian Lumley is an eyeless and deaf lunar entity and is worshipped by the residents of the ancient continent of Theem'hdra as well as in the Dreamlands . He is very similar to Mnomquah , though they are not related.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Mad Moon of Dreams</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecrafts_Untold_Stories">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lovecraft's Untold Stories</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Action-adventure is an action-adventure video game developed and published by blini games . Released on 29 january 2019, the game is inspired by the works of h.p. Lovecraft and draws heavily from the cthulhu mythos . The game is also accompanied with a artbook dlc, a collection of the most beautiful images and artworks in hi resolution of the game .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>29 January 2019</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Leech_of_Yoh-Vombis">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonSapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Leech of Yoh-Vombis</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sociocultural Characteristics Mars Subterranean Real World Information First appearance CIRCLE : " The Vaults of Yoh-Vombis " Created by Clark Ashton Smith The Leeches of Yoh-Vombis are a subterranean species of Martian parasitic lifeforms which inhabit the underground ruins of Yoh-Vombis, built by the extinct Yorhis , and are believed to have been responsible for their extinction.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Vaults_of_Yoh-Vombis"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Black_Wings_V">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Black Wings V</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The stories are original to this volume, with the exception of caitln r. kernan's "far from any shore" edited by world-renowned lovecraft scholar s. t. joshi, contains a total of twenty stories . It was re-released by titan books as a paperback in january 2018 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Black_Wings"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Spaces_Between">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Spaces Between</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A library assistant helps a famous former musician suffering from a hereditary genetic disorder to research her family’s musical history . As they get to the bottom of the mystery, the musician discovers that her condition allows her to play in a way that others cannot .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Peter_Rawlik"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Lovecraft_eZine"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2016</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fall_of_Cthulhu_Volume_1:_The_Fugue">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Fall of Cthulhu Volume 1: The Fugue</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Fall of cthulhu volume 2: the gathering is a comic book omnibus collecting the first five issues of the series . First published by boom! Studios in february 2008, both trade paperback and digital editions were released .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Michael_Alan_Nelson"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>136</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>February 2008</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jedidiah_Mortonson">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Jedidiah Mortonson</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>Early 21st century</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mortonson is a character created by author c. j. henderson as an antagonist in his frank nardi series of short stories . He was intended as the first antagonist for his hard-boiled private detective frank nardi, and featured in two of the nardi stories . He was eventually thwarted and killed by the intervention of the arkham detective agency .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Nest_of_Pain"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Idea_of_Fear"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#C._J._Henderson"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scion_of_Chaahk">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Scion of Chaahk</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A pair of explorers stumble across an underground chamber filled with offerings . As they explore the chamber, it becomes obvious that the carving is more than it appears to be . The two men attempt to escape, only to find themselves trapped by an ancient cult dedicated to the dark god's worship .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tom_Lynch"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Children_of_Glaaki"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_House_Up_in_the_Hills">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The House Up in the Hills</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Robin D. Laws English Cthulhu Confidential Genre(s) Roleplaying game Pelgrane Press 44 is a scenario supplement for Pelgrane Press 's Cthulhu Mythos roleplaying game Cthulhu Confidential , written by Robin D. Laws , one of the creators of the core game. Initially released solely as a digital download, it was later included in the omnibus collection Even Death Can Die . Overview</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robin_D._Laws"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>44</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Confidential"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Steven_M._Vance">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Steven M. Vance</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Birth Date 16 December 1965 Palm Springs, California, USA United States Horror https://www.facebook.com/StevenMVance3/ is an American author, medical technician, and former soldier based in Portland, Oregon, USA . He has written a handful of stories set in the greater Cthulhu Mythos ; these have been published in collections such as Cthulhu Christmas Special and Other Lovecraftian Yuletide Tales and Pickman's Gallery (both released in 2017). His other writing projects include a digital chapbook, published through Amazon's digital Kindle platform. Selected Bibliography Short Fiction " The Pickman Revival " (2017) " Secret Santa " (2017) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA http://example.org/lovecraft#California Palm Springs</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://www.facebook.com/StevenMVance3/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>16 December 1965</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Still_More_Shadows_Over_Rlyeh">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Still More Shadows Over R'lyeh</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Don D'Ammassa English Managansett Press Release Date March 2019 167 is a collection of six Lovecraftian short stories by the American Don D'Ammassa . Published by Managansett Press in March 2019, both paperback and digital editions were released. Overview Contents "The Thin Spot" "The Chaos Business" "The Effigy" "Twisted" "The Three Pale Men" "The Custodian"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Don_DAmmassa"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>167</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>March 2019</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Child_of_Yig">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Child of Yig</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Real World Information First appearance " The Curse of Yig " Created by H. P Lovecraft and Zealia Bishop The Children of Yig is the name for snakes that are sacred to Yig . Description The Children of Yig can be any variety of snake, but all have a white marking on their foreheads denoting their status. Venom from a cannot be cured with any known methods, and disturbing the Children of Yig could transform one into a Spawn of Yig .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Curse_of_Yig"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Zealia_Bishop"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>H. P Lovecraft</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tales_of_the_Sleepless_City">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tales of the Sleepless City</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Miskatonic River Press Release Date 2013 160 is a scenario supplement for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu horror roleplaying game. Running to a total of 160 , it was published in a paperback format by independent press and third-party content provider Miskatonic River Press in 2013. Overview Contents "To Awaken What Never Sleeps" by Dan Harms "The Terror from the Museum" by Brian Sammons "The Fishers of Men" by Charles Michael Hurst "The Tenement" by Oscar Rios "A Night at the Opera" by Mikael Hedberg "Értóng hé Kūqì de Mǔqīn (The Child and the Weeping Mother)" by Tom Lynch &amp; Scott David Aniolowski</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>160</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2013</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sentinel_Hill">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dunwich"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sentinel Hill</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The hill, located near the whateley farm, features in the story as the site of the whateley family's may eve and halloween rituals . There are also "deposits of skulls and bones," thought by dunwich residents to be the buried remains of the pocumtuck indians .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Hill</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Massachusetts"/>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Dunwich</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dunwich_Horror"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cat_from_Uranus">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cat from Uranus</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cats from uranus are a fictional species created by chaosium for the role-playing game call of cthulhu . Like the other two species, the cats from uranus can transport themselves between worlds by jumping . Their physical form is more "substantial", compared to the cats from saturn .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecrafts_Dreamlands"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Rhogog">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Rhogog</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Bearer of the Cup of the Blood of the Ancients</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as The Bearer of the Cup of the Blood of the Ancients First appearance "Sacristans of " A Study in Emerald Call of Cthulhu Gaslight Created by Micheal Saint-Paul was said to be created when Cthulhu and Hastur fought above the British Isles and Cthulhu shed blood, which several Star-Spawn put an 'entity' into for unknown purposes. This 'entity' lay dormant, growing in power until it became . is a great, black oak tree, hot to the touch, and a being of darkness, and unimaginable power. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>A Study in Emerald Call of Cthulhu Gaslight</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Sacristans of Rhogog</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Micheal Saint-Paul</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Pool">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Pool</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A construction worker bears witness to the surreptitious summoning of a shoggoth by an occultist masquerading as a contractor . The man begins to receive mental messages exhorting him to revisit the site of the incantation - a newly-installed private swimming pool .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Michael_Shea"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>February 2007</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lillian_Delora_Phillips">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSRelatives"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lillian Delora Phillips</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>29 August 1852</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lovecraft’s maternal aunt.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isRelativeToR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1803</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Kythanil">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Planets"/>
    <rdfs:label>Kythanil</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Kythamil is a double planet that once revolved around the star arcturus . The planet was in some catastrophe, but not before the voormi moved .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Dual Planetary Bodies</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Voormi (formerly) Sfatlicllp (possibly)</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Arcturus System</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Through_the_Gates_of_the_Silver_Key"/>
    <ex:wasDestroyedOnL>B.C. Millions of years</ex:wasDestroyedOnL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Vulthoom">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Vulthoom</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Great Old Ones Outer Gods</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Gsarthotegga</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Sleeper of Ravermos</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as the sleeper of ravermos gsarthotegga . Introduced in the short story of the same name and later incorporated into the cthulhu mythos .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ugghiutu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ugghiutu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Outsider"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ugghiatu is an extra-dimensional entity created by american author jeffrey thomas for his dystopian science fiction/ lovecraftian horror setting of punktown . The only known avatar of , which has not been given its own title, is a being grown from a seed birthed from the sacrifice of four highly-trained kalian dancers .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isLocatedAtC>Outside the Prime Reality</ex:isLocatedAtC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Drawing_No._8"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jeffrey_Thomas"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_from_the_Hills">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Horror from the Hills</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Weird tales was serialized in weird tales magazine in 1931 in the january and february-march issues . It was published by arkham house in 1963 in an edition of 1,997 copies . It has come to form an integral part of the cthulhu mythos .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Frank_Belknap_Long"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>January-March 1931</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lamp-eft">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonSapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lamp-eft</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lamp-efts are floating salamander-like creatures with webbed feet, laterally flattened tails and luminous bodies . They naturally inhabit the upper atmosphere of the earth's dreamlands, and descend to the ground only at night . The light generated by lamp-efts contributes to their popularity among occultists by providing a line of defense against mythos entities .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecrafts_Dreamlands"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sandy_Petersen"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pete_Von_Shollys_Lovecraft_Illustrated">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Pete Von Sholly's Lovecraft Illustrated</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Pete Von Sholly Cover Artist Pete Von Sholly United States English Genre(s) Art book Clover Press, LLC Release Date December 2020 208 is an art book which collects the Lovecraftian imagery of artist, filmmaker and author Pete Von Sholly . Running to a total of 208 , it was published by Clover Press, LLC in December 2020 . Gallery References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Art book</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>208</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>December 2020</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Steve_Proposch">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Steve Proposch</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Stephen Robert Proposch Connection to the Mythos Editor Birth Date 1967 Box Hill, Victoria, Australia Australia Melissa Proposch Stephen Robert "Steve" Proposch (born 1967) is an Australian author and editor who has worked on a number of Cthulhu Mythos anthologies, including the Cthulhu Deep Down Under series, most of which have been published through IFWG Publishing . In 1993 he co-founded the horror fiction periodical Bloodsongs , which ran for four years , and was also the founder, in partnership with his wife Melissa, of international art zine Trouble , established in 2004 and running through until 2020 . Selected Works Editor Cthulhu Deep Down Under: Volume 1 (2015, with Christopher Sequiera and Bryce Stevens ) Cthulhu Deep Down Under: Volume 2 (2018, with Christopher Sequiera and Bryce Stevens) Cthulhu: Land of the Long White Cloud (2018, with Christopher Sequiera and Bryce Stevens) Cthulhu Deep Down Under: Volume 3 (2021, with Christopher Sequiera and Bryce Stevens) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>http://example.org/lovecraft#Australia Box Hill. Victoria</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Stephen Robert Proposch</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>http://example.org/lovecraft#Australia</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1967</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Octavio_Cariello">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Octavio Cariello</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Artist Recife, Brazil Weird fiction Superhero Years Active 1986-present is a Brazilian artist best known for his work on titles for comic book giants Marvel and DC. He has also worked on several adaptations of the short stories of H. P. Lovecraft written by Steven Philip Jones and published by Caliber Comics . Selected Works The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: The Alchemist (1997) The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: The Lurking Fear (1997) The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: The Tomb (1997) The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: Beyond the Wall of Sleep (1998) H. P. Lovecraft's Worlds: Volume 1</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>Brazil Recife</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasYearsActiveR>1986-present</ex:hasYearsActiveR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chorazos_Cult">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cults"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Organisation"/>
    <rdfs:label>Chorazos Cult</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The (erroneously called " choronzon cult ") is a religious cult dedicated to the worship of yog-sothoth . It appears in the encyclopedia cthulhiana , a supplement to the role-playing game call of cthulhu .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Tower_of_Zhaal">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CrossroadPress"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Tower of Zhaal</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu armageddon followed by the tree of azathoth is a cthulhu mythos novel by c.t. Phipps . It is the second in his series of cthulhu armageddon novels . A group of mercenaries and sorcerers have a unique task: stop a mad descendant of the whateley family from unleashing the last of the great old ones still asleep .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>CT Phipps</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Action adventure</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Post-apocalypse</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Armageddon"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Karthis">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Planets"/>
    <rdfs:label>Karthis</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Rigel System Notable Features Global ocean Nioth-Korghai (Space Vampires) First appearance " The Space Vampires " Created by Colin Wilson is the homeworld of the Nioth-Korghai , in the Rigel System . It is a world covered by ocean.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>Global ocean</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Nioth-Korghai (Space Vampires)</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>The Rigel System</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>The Space Vampires</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Castro">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Castro</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Cult"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Old Castro</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu is a fictional character created by h. p. lovecraft . He makes his first appearance in the 1928 short story " the call of cthulhu " was arrested on november 1, 1907 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Call_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clash_of_Queens">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Clash of Queens</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Raven Gregory Manuel Preitano English Zenescope Entertainment Release Date Feb 2014-June 2014 Media Type Comic book is a five-issue comic book mini-series written by Raven Gregory in 2014 which was published by Zenescope Entertainment. Synopsis For a long time, an uneasy peace existed between the rival Queens. Which changed with the arrival of Keres, the goddess of death. Now the Queens battle each other for control of Wonderland . Part of the Grimm Fairy Tales' multiverse crossover event, "Age of Darkness".</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Raven Gregory</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>Feb 2014-June 2014</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Blacked_Out">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RamseyCampbellWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Blacked Out</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A man named lamb travelling across the german countryside takes a room in a picture-perfect village with all of the lights on . As night begins to descend, the villagers stage a honey trap to lure lamb out into the street . Soon lamb sees a blanket of darkness extinguishing the lights, and travelling in his direction .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ramsey_Campbell"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United Kingdom</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Cold Print</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1985</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Second_Coming_of_Shuma-Gorath">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Second Coming of Shuma-Gorath</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Roy Thomas Mike Docherty Ricardo Villagran English Marvel Comics Release Date July, 1992 - Sept, 1992 Media Type Comic book is a 3-part story arc written by Roy Thomas which is set during the Hyborian Age. Synopsis Two sorcerer priests attempt to bring Shuma-Gorath , the lord of the Old Ones to Earth, and the Cimmerian barbarian Conan stands in their way.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Roy_Thomas"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1992 1992 - Sept July</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Puppeteer">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Puppeteer</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Young boy develops obsession with reclusive puppeteer ridwan, a tenant on his father's property . Both terrified and fascinated by what he has seen, he waits until ridwan has left his home before breaking in . Inside, he discovers hundreds of shrunken creatures inhabiting a miniature town, but is caught red-handed .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Gary Budgen</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Swords_Against_Cthulhu_II:_Hyperborean_Nights"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>January 2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ghouls_%28poem%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ghouls (poem)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ghouls Full story online here Ron Shiflet United States English Publication date 1987 " Ghouls " is a Cthulhu Mythos poem by American Ron Shiflet . Synopsis The work is a short piece describing the nature of ghouls , and the dangers they pose to the human race. Publication History Making its first appearance in Grim Grafiks in 1987, "Ghouls" was later published in the fanzines Cthulhu Cultus #8 (March 1997) and Nightscapes (August 1998).</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1987</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Howard_Lovecraft_%28Free_Comic_Book_Day_2018_story%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Howard Lovecraft (Free Comic Book Day 2018 story)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Howard Lovecraft English Howard Lovecraft Arcana Studio Publication date 2018 " Howard Lovecraft " is a children’s Cthulhu Mythos story. It was published by Arcana Studio in a short anthology collection as part of 2016's Free Comic Book Day. Neither author nor artist are credited in the book. Overview Synopsis Despite being billed as an "all-new adventure", the story does nothing more than recap the events of the two earlier films in the Howard Lovecraft as a plug for the (then) upcoming movie.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Howard Lovecraft</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2018</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Peter_Cannon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scholars"/>
    <rdfs:label>Peter Cannon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Peter hughes cannon is an editor, scholar and author of cthulhu mythos fiction . His fiction includes pulptime, in which lovecraft, long and sherlock holmes team up to solve a mystery . He was also a member of the providence pals, an informal group of lovecraft enthusiasts .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA http://example.org/lovecraft#California Palo Alto</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Peter Hughes Cannon</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>H. P. G. Wodehouse</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>H.P. Lovecraft Scholar</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Mythos Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>19 October 1951</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Don_Webb">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonFictionAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Don Webb</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Birth Date 30 April 1960 Amarillo, Texas, USA United States Science Fiction Mystery Weird Fiction Non-Fiction Years Active 1985-present is a prolific American author, occultist and teacher. His output includes science fiction, mystery and weird fiction, as well as non-fiction focusing on his work in occultism. A number of his pieces are set in the greater Cthulhu Mythos and many more were inspired by this shared universe. As an occultist, Webb acted as the High Priest of the Temple of Set, an esoteric religious organisation established in 1975, from 1996 to 2002 . Selected Bibliography Collections Through Dark Angles: Works Inspired by H. P. Lovecraft (2014) Short Fiction " To Mars and Providence " (1996) " Like Comment Share " (2014) " Tick Tock " (2017) " She Wore a Trench Coat " (2017) External Links at Wikipedia at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Texas Amarillo</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasYearsActiveR>1985-present</ex:hasYearsActiveR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>30 April 1960</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cat_from_Saturn">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cat from Saturn</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Real World Information First appearance HPL : The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath EXP : Elder Sign Created by Howard Phillips Lovecraft In the Dreamlands , the planet Saturn is populated by a race of strange cats, who occasionally travel to the dark side of Earth 's moon. They are enemies of the cats of Earth. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Elder_Sign"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dream-Quest_of_Unknown_Kadath"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dorieb">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dorieb</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cathuria king created by h. p. lovecraft is a legendary king who rules the fabled land of cathuria according to the beliefs of many who live in the dreamlands . He is even said by some to be a god or demigod .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_White_Ship"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Halloween_in_a_Suburb">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Hallowe'en in a Suburb</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"in a suburb" was published in the national amateur in march 1926 . It was later published in the collection beyond the wall of sleep in 1943 . The "hounds of time" are evocative of the hounds of tindalos .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_United_Amateur"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 1926</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Wyckd_Secrets_Of_The_Infernal_Beaste_Ghattambah">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosBooksFictional"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdfs:label>Wyck'd Secrets Of The Infernal Beaste Ghattambah</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Azmael Mondrago United States English Publication Date 1700 (approximately) Real World Information Created by Mark McLaughlin Michael Sheehan, Jr. is an occult tome created by Mark McLaughlin and Michael Sheehan, Jr. for their Cthulhu Mythos stories. Overview Whilst it’s exact contents are unknown, it has been stated to be written on the cult of Ghattambah . It was written sometime around 1700 by the High Priest of Ghattambah Azmael Mondrago, a member of the Mondrago bloodline which has been in service to Ghattambah for centuries, and who still retain a copy in their chapel (EXP: " Shoggoth Apocalypse "). Appearances " In The Chapel Of Ghattambah " " Shoggoth Apocalypse "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasCreatedByA rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByA>Michael Sheehan. Jr.</ex:wasCreatedByA>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chthonic_Island">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>Chthonic Island</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAccessibilityL>by ship</ex:hasAccessibilityL>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a fictional island in the marvel universe which first appeared in 2016 in the 2nd carnage comic book series in issue #10 . It's located in one of the world's busiest sea lanes, yet it's not on any map, doesn't show up on radar, nor an any satellite photo . For centuries, human sailors have been drawn to the island where they were raped to propagate the species and then sacrificed to chthon .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>beach</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>jungle</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>mountain</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasTypeL>island</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Broodlings of Chthon</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Earth"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>Carnage Vol 2 10</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#In_Lovecrafts_Shadow_%28Ron_Shiflet_zine%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>In Lovecraft's Shadow (Ron Shiflet zine)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>In lovecraft's shadow was an extremely short-lived zine published by american author and ron shiflet through his tenoka press label . Despite containing stories by a number of popular contemporary cthulhu mythos authors, it ran for only a single issue . In december 2017, a digital edition was issued through amazon's kindle platform .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pharol">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Pharol</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Pharol the Black</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A black, fanged, cycloptic demon with arms like swaying serpents . The entity normally dwells in another dimension . The wizard eibon of hyperborea occasionally summons him for arcane information .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hydra"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shaggai"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Shambleau</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#C._L._Moore"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_in_the_Gallery">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Horror in the Gallery</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story is presented as a police testimony given by arthur wilcox hodgkins in 1929, after a night watchman is found murdered in the south gallery of the sanbourne institute of pacific antiquities in santiago, california . Hodgkins maintains that there were actually two bodies, the second being the body of the night watchman's murderer . At the time of his mental collapse, curator henry stephenson blaine had been cataloging the collection of files and artifacts belonging</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lin_Carter"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Disciples_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 1976</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Leaves_of_a_Necronomicon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Leaves of a Necronomicon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Leaves of a necronomicon is a collection of short stories, edited by joseph s. pulver, sr. . It tells the interconnected story of a single copy of h. p. lovecraft 's necronomicon as it passes from hand to hand over decades . It was published in 2022 by chaosium .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>339</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2022</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lost_in_Memory">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lost in Memory</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>John Ostrander Patrick Olliffe, Phyllis Novin English DC Comics Media Type Comic book is a story in the 1st issue of the 3rd volume of Spectre Annual written by John Ostander. Synopsis The supernatural superhero Spectre remembers how during World War 2, the Spectre and the Fate fought against Koth-Shugoth , an Elder God attempting to cross over to Earth.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>John Ostrander</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Treasure_of_Horemkhu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Treasure of Horemkhu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Simon of gitta is a sword-and-sorcery hero from the pulp adventure series . The story is a prequel to " under the pyramids ," which h.p. Lovecraft ghost-wrote for harry houdini .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_L._Tierney"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Pulse Pounding Adventure Stories #2</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 1987</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Ancient_Track">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Ancient Track</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The two fairly lengthy stanzas describe the narrator's journey along a sinister and seemingly-abandoned pathway . It has since been reprinted a multitude of times, and has even been translated into french .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 1930</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dee_%28Michael_Shea%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dee (Michael Shea)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Dee is a recurrent character in the works of the late american author michael shea . She often carries a heavy walking stick, although she acknowledges that this is for aesthetic and self-defence purposes . First encountered the forces of the cthulhu mythos after she was accidentally given cookies laced with cannabis by her former partner .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dagoniad"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tsathoggua"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Michael_Shea"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecrafts_Bibliothek_des_Schreckens">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>H. P. Lovecrafts Bibliothek des Schreckens</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Frank Festa German Blitz Festa 1999 Final Publication Ongoing  , which translates to English as H. P. Lovecraft's Library of Terror , is a German- series of Lovecraftian and Cthulhu Mythos anthologies published by Blitz from 1999 until Festa took up the mantle in 2000. Although the series primarily consists of translations of English- works, it also contains a percentage of original works. Frank Festa , the founder of Festa, has acted as the for the series' entire run. Works (incomplete) Lovecrafts dunkle Idole (1999) Die Xothic-Legenden (2004) Namenlose Kulte (2006) Das schleichende Chaos (2006) Cthulhu (2009) Die Offenbarungen des Glaaki (2014) Der Wahnsinn aus der Gruft (2014) Tiefenrausch (2022)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>German</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#HorrorBabbles_Dream_Cycle">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleAudioAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>HorrorBabble's Dream Cycle</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft English HorrorBabble Release Date August 2018 is a complete collection of the Dream Cycle works of late American H. P. Lovecraft . It was issued by HorrorBabble in August 2018, with both digital and audiobook versions receiving a release. Contents " Polaris " " The White Ship " " The Doom That Came to Sarnath " " The Cats of Ulthar " " Celephaïs " " Ex Oblivione " " Nyarlathotep " " The Quest of Iranon " " The Other Gods " " Azathoth " " Hypnos " " What the Moon Brings " The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath " The Outsider " " The Silver Key " " The Strange High House in the Mist " " Through the Gates of the Silver Key "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 2018</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dark_Mind,_Dark_Heart">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dark Mind, Dark Heart</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>249 is a horror anthology containing a number of stories set in the expanded cthulhu mythos . It is perhaps most notable for containing the publication of famous british ramsey campbell 's first mythos story . Edited by lovecraft contemporary august derleth and released in a run of 2,493 copies .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror Fantasy Short Fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>249</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1962</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Annie_Emeline_Phillips">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSRelatives"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Annie Emeline Phillips</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>29 August 1852</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lovecraft’s maternal aunt. In October 1916 Annie and Edward were separated when she took their son, Phillips, to Roswell, Colorado in the hopes of arresting his tuberculosis (it is possible that they separated earlier than this). When Phillips died on 31 December 1916 Annie returned not to Edward in Cambridge, but to Providence where she spent most of the rest of her life with her nephew, Howard Phillips Lovecraft. Edward and Annie were never legally divorced.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isRelativeToR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1803</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Schmidt">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Schmidt</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>1917 June 20th</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Imperial German Navy</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>June 20th, 1917 Germany Navy Seaman (Submariner) Imperial German Navy Human First appearance HPL : " The Temple " Created by H. P. Lovecraft Seaman is a fictional character in the story " The Temple " by H. P. Lovecraft . He served aboard the German submarine U-29 under Lieutenant-Commander Karl Heinrich during the First World War. As a result of the supernatural events experienced by the crew, both and Seaman Bohm became sick and lapsed into violent insanity. Their constant ravings concerning a "terrible curse" upon the submarine led Heinrich to execute them in order to maintain discipline.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Temple"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Kgilmnon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dimensions"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdfs:label>K'gil'mnon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Created by j. b. lee is a fictional alternative reality or dimension . Appeared in the short story " his hour upon the stage " by j. b. lee . Home to and ruled by the outer god kaalut .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#His_Hour_Upon_the_Stage"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Terror_of_Toad_Lake">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Terror of Toad Lake</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A man stumbles across an ancient cult of toad-men and degenerate hillbillies . Enlisting the assistance of his childhood best friend, the two tackle the cult . However, the sole survivor discovers that the toad-men may have passed on some vile condition .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Ambuehl"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>German</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>June 1997</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Deaf,_Dumb,_and_Blind">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Deaf, Dumb, and Blind</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>" deaf, dumb and blind " is a short story by . H. p. lovecraft and c.m. Eddy, jr. it was first published in the . April 1925 edition of pulp magazine weird tales .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Howard_Phillips_Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>C. M. Eddy. Jr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#X-Men:_Apocalypse_vs_Dracula">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>X-Men: Apocalypse vs Dracula</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The 3,000 year old mutant apocalypse the eternal one battles the demonic n'garai and the lord of vampires, dracula .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Frank Tieri</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>April 2006-July 2006</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Barnabas_Marsh">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Barnabas Marsh</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Old Marsh</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Barnabas is a fictional character, the head of the semi-human marsh family in the sinister town of innsmouth . Due to his deep one ancestry, he is functionally immortal, though he may still be killed through violent means . He is married to a human woman from the nearby town of ipswich, a choice he made against the wishes of the town's hybridized power structure .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shadow_Over_Innsmouth"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ida_C._Haughton">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSFriendsAndAcquaintances"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ida C. Haughton</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>unknown</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Amateur journalist with whom Lovecraft had a bitter feud in the early 1920s. He directed the pungent satirical poem “Medusa: A Portrait” (1922) at her.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1922</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tales_of_H._P._Lovecraft">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tales of H. P. Lovecraft</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft English Ecco Press Release Date 1997 328 is a collection of Lovecraft 's horror fiction, selected and introduced by Joyce Carol Oates . It was first published by in 1997 by Ecco Press, who reissued it in 2000. Harper Perennial Modern Classics published a third edition in 2007, featuring cover art by comic book creator Mike Mignola . Contents The stories included in the volume are: " The Outsider " " The Music of Erich Zann " " The Rats in the Walls " " The Shunned House " " The Call of Cthulhu " " The Colour Out of Space " " The Dunwich Horror " At the Mountains of Madness " The Shadow Over Innsmouth " " The Shadow Out of Time " Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>328</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1997</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dead_Reckonings">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dead Reckonings</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various United States English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Chaosium Release Date January 1998 80 : New Lovecraft Adventures for Call of Cthulhu is a scenario supplement for the Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game, designed by J. Todd Kingrea , Kevin Ross , John Snyder, and Richard Watts . Running to a total of 80 in a paperback format, it was published by Chaosium in January 1998. Overview Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>80</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>January 1998</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Turret">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Turret</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"made in goatswood" is a short story by the late american author, richard a. lupoff . It was first published in the scott david aniolowski -edited made in goatswood in 1995 . The story is set in the severn valley milieu of england's severn valley . Lupoff's characters include a computer systems troubleshooter and a distant cousin .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_A._Lupoff"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Made_in_Goatswood"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>July 1995</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Providence_Pals:_Memories_and_Miscellany">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Providence Pals: Memories and Miscellany</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Jason C. Eckhardt United States English Genre(s) Non-fiction Ulthar Press Release Date July 2015 126 is a selected collection of the works of the Providence Pals , an informal group of H. P. Lovecraft enthusiasts active from the late 1970s through to the 1990s. Edited by Sam Gafford , who was a member of the Pals from 1982 onwards, and featuring cover art by Jason C. Eckhardt , another member, it was published by Ulthar Press in July 2015. Gafford states in his introduction that he "spread the word far and wide and asked for a contribution from each and, when possible, a short memorial of the group and what it meant to them. What follows are their memories and also some samples of the fine work that each have done in this field." Back Cover Text</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>126</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 2015</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Study_in_Emerald">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crossovers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Study in Emerald</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story first appeared in shadows over baker street, a collection of stories that combine the worlds of sherlock holmes and the cthulhu mythos . The story won the hugo award for best short story in 2004.was reprinted in gaiman's 2006 collection fragile things .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Neil_Gaiman"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shadows_Over_Baker_Street"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>2003</ex:isPublishedAsW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Fate_of_the_Jaquiths">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Fate of the Jaquiths</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>R. c. mulhare's short story first appeared in the zine lovecraftiana . It was voted for by the fanbase on facebook for inclusion in a best of... collection (2023, rogue planet press) a number of characters share surnames with those from h. p. lovecraft's fiction .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#R._C._Mulhare"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraftiana"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Escape_From_Wonderland">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Escape From Wonderland</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Joe C. Brusha, Raven Gregory, Ralph Tedesco Andrew Mangum, Anthony Spay, Daniel Leister, Dave Hoover, Mike DeBalfo English Zenescope Entertainment Release Date June 2009 - April 2010 Media Type Comic book is a seven-issue comic book mini-series written by Joe C. Brusha, Raven Gregory, and Ralph Tedesco in 2009-2010 which was published by Zenescope Entertainment. Synopsis Wonderland took Calie Liddle 's infant daughter, Violet Liddle . Forcing Calie to go back to the realm of madness to rescue her baby.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Joe C. Brusha</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Ralph Tedesco</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Raven Gregory</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>June 2009 - April 2010</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Eastertide_1986">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu Eastertide 1986</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Allen Koszowski Lance Brown Cover Artist Chris Pelletiere United States English Crypt of Cthulhu Cryptic Publications Release Date April 1986 56 Preceded by Candlemas 1986 Followed by Roodmas 1986 is the 38th issue of long-running fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Running to a total of 56 , it was edited by Robert M. Price , featured cover art by Chris Pelletiere, interior art by Allen Koszowski and Lance Brown, and was published in an A5 format by Cryptic Publications in April 1986 . Contents Editorial Shards by Robert M. Price ""The Hound"—A Dead Dog?" by Steven Mariconda ""The Tomb" &amp; "Dagon": A Double Dissection" by William Fulwiler "The Sources for "From Beyond"" by S. T. Joshi "Spawn of the Moon-Bog" by Will Murray "Exploring "The Temple"" by David E. Schultz "On "Beyond the Wall of Sleep"" by M. Eileen McNamara, M.D. "The Little Tow-Head Fiend; Or, The Problem of Herbert West" by Will Murray "HPL's Style" by Ralph E. Vaughan "Fun Guys from Yuggoth: Lovecraft the Man, Lovecraft the Fan" by Will Murray "Three Who Died" by uncredited "Advice to the Lovecraft-Lorn" by Donna Death Reviews by Marc A. Cerasini and Stefan Dziemianowicz Letters by Kevin A. Ross , Leslie Skingle, Stefan R. Dziemianowicz, Thomas Owen, Jim Cort, Charles Garofalo , Richard L. Tierney , Wilum Pugmire , and Will Murray References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>56</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>April 1986</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Red_Knight">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Red Knight</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Ben</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ben was a child who was inflicted with severe combined immunodeficiency, forcing him to live behind a germ-free barrier . His family were murdered by his disturbing drawings which came to life, after which he ended up in wonderland .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Through the Looking-Glass</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>What Alice Found There</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Wonderland Annual 2009</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Lewis Carroll</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Red_Sonja">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Red Sonja</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sonja was the daughter of a hyrkanian chief, and was the only survivor of a vicious raid on her village . She was captured by slavers and for three years was forced to fight in the arena . Afterwards she became an adventurer, a lusty and hotblooded warrior who loves battle . Eventually becoming the queen of sogaria after liberating it from the oppressive emoran empire .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Conan the Barbarian Vol 1 23</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Roy_Thomas"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Alice_Liddle">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Alice Liddle</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Lacie</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Alice was created by lewis carroll in 1865, first appearing in alice's adventures in wonderland . In 2008, she first appeared in the highly sexualised grimm fairy tales fictional universe, in the issue #0 of the 1st volume of grimm fairy tales: return to wonderland .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>ADJ Return to Wonderland Alice in Wonderland</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Alice's Adventures in Wonderland</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Lewis Carroll</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft:_An_Appreciation">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>H. P. Lovecraft: An Appreciation</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Thomas O. Mabbott (as T. O. Mabbott) United States English Genre(s) Non-fiction Arkham House Marginalia date 1944 " " is a short non-fiction work by American scholar and Weird Tales contributor Thomas O. Mabbott (as T. O. Mabbott). History Marginalia (1944) H. P. Lovecraft: Four Decades of Criticism (1980, 1981) Lovecraft Remembered (1998)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Thomas O. Mabbott</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Marginalia"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1944</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Reanimator">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Reanimator</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Keith Davidson Randy Valiente English Dynamite Entertainment Release Date April 2015 - July 2015 Media Type Comic book is a 4-issue mini-series written by Keith Davidson and published by Dynamite Entertainment in 2015. It is a pastiche that expands on the original Herbert West character created by H. P. Lovecraft . Synopsis Working in New Orleans, Dr. Herbert West continues his experiments in reanimating the dead via chemical methods. The pharmacologist Susan Greene assists the doctor in his ghoulish experiments, while he finances his research in the narcotics trade by selling zombie brain fluid: the reagent from " Herbert West-- ". Soon he ends up in a confrontation with drug gangs, Haitian Voodoo and the followers of the Elder Gods .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Keith Davidson</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>April 2015 - July 2015</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Zug">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Zug</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Monster was worshipped by the priests of thugra khotan in stygia during the hyborian age . Inside the dome was a huge amount of gold . Itself was dozens of feet in diameter and had a great maw full of sharp teeth .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Children_of_Lovecraft_Country">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Children of Lovecraft Country</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various English Golden Goblin Press Release Date February 2020 is a thematic anthology of Cthulhu Mythos fiction. Edited by Brian M. Sammons and Oscar Rios , it was published by Golden Goblin Press in February 2020. Overview Contents "Ghosts and Monsters" by Peter Rawlik "Dreams of Dunwich" by Glynn Owen Barrass "Witchlights" by Christine Morgan "Luck Be With You" by Brian M. Sammons "George Weedon and the Mystery of Emily Keane" by Lee Clark Zumpe "Blood and the Deep Blue Sea" by Oscar Rios</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>February 2020</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_House_of_the_Seven_Gables">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The House of the Seven Gables</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Nathaniel Hawthorne United States English Genre(s) Gothic romance Ticknor and Fields Release Date March 1851 : A Romance is a gothic romance novel by the late American Nathaniel Hawthorne , first published by Ticknor and Fields in March 1851. According to Lovecraft scholars S. T. Joshi and David E. Schultz , it is a possible inspiration for " The Case of Charles Dexter Ward " . Synopsis The plot concerns a struggling family, the history of their home, and a malicious family member who will stop at nothing to retrieve missing land deeds. The story also makes hints to a curse laid upon the bloodline many years previously. External Links at Wikipedia References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nathaniel_Hawthorne"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Gothic romance</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>March 1851</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#wasCreatedByC">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label>wasCreatedByC</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <owl:inverseOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#created"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gemma_Files">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ContentCreators"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gemma Files</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Her work has appeared in numerous anthologies of lovecraftian fiction . She is the author of over 100 short stories, five short-story collections, and five novels . Married the science fiction and fantasy writer stephen j. barringer in 2002 . They have one son .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>http://example.org/lovecraft#England London</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFatherR>, Gary Files</ex:hasFatherR>
    <ex:hasMotherR>Elva Mai Hoover</ex:hasMotherR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>Canadian</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasSpouseR>Stephen J. Barringer</ex:hasSpouseR>
    <ex:hasYearsActiveR>1993-present</ex:hasYearsActiveR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1968 April 4</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Zon_Mezzamalech">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Zon Mezzamalech</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mhu Thulan Wizard First appearance CIRCLE : " Ubbo-Sathla " Created by Clark Ashton Smith is a fictional character created by Clark Ashton Smith for his Cthulhu Mythos story " Ubbo-Sathla ". Biography An ancient Hyperborean sorcerer, lived in Mhu Thulan and possessed a mysterious crystal that allows one to experience visions of the past, all the way down to the beginning of all things. The Book of Eibon records that Mezzamalech disappeared mysteriously without a trace, and the crystal was lost afterwards. In truth, Mezzamalech used the crystal to send his mind back in time to the source of all life on Earth, the amorphous Ubbo-Sathla , hoping to study the stone tablets guarded by this unbegotten entity, and gain the knowledge of a race of gods who had died or abandoned the material universe "before the Earth was born". ( CIRCLE : " Ubbo-Sathla ")</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ubbo-Sathla"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Loved_Dead_and_Other_Tales">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Loved Dead and Other Tales</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>C. M. Eddy, Jr. Cover Artist David Moen English Fenham Publishing Release Date February 2008 257 is a collection of horror stories by the late American C. M. Eddy, Jr. , including the four stories ghostwritten for Eddy by H. P. Lovecraft . It wa published in a paperback format in February 2008 by Fenham Publishing. Contents " The Loved Dead " "With Weapons of Stone" "The Red Cap of Mara" "An Arbiter of Destiny" "The Cur" "The Better Choice" " Ashes " "Eterna" "Arhl-a of the Caves" " The Ghost-Eater " " Deaf, Dumb and Blind " "Souls &amp; Heels" "Sign of the Dragon"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>C. M. Eddy. Jr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>257</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>February 2008</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Frank_Nardi">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Frank Nardi</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Detective_Agency"/>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>New York Police Department</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Franklin "frank" nardi is a character who appears in the expanded cthulhu mythos . He was originally created by author c. j. henderson . He has since been expanded upon by others including josh reynolds and robert m. price .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Idea_of_Fear"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#C._J._Henderson"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Leviathan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Avatars"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CharactersIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Leviathan</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Serpent White Death</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a colossal shark-like or serpentine creature, more than fifty feet in length . It is regarded as an offspring or avatar of cthulhu . In an alternate timeline, creatures known as leviathans have been trained to attack ships during world war two .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The Arctic Stone Malleus Monstrorum</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Hebrew Bible</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Ross F. Bagby</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Xo_Tlmi-go">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Xo Tl'mi-go</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Real World Information First appearance EXP : " Children of the Kingdom " Created by T. E. D. Klein The are a subterranean race that reproduce by attacking and impregnating human woman. Description The are a race of cave-dwelling humanoids with pale skin, webbed hands, and tapeworm-like heads. They reproduce by producing a semen-like fluid and manually inserting it into a human woman. A tribe of Native Americans called the Chibcha believe that the Xo Tl'mi are the offspring of their gods cursed with barrenness for their sins.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Children of the Kingdom</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#T._E._D._Klein"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_One_That_Got_Away">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The One That Got Away</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu mythos short story first published in 2000/2001 issue of dark legacy . Was included in the collection they thrive in darkness: mythos and madness in 2018 . Takes place in tenoka, texas, a frequent setting for the mythos works of american author ron shiflet .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Black Comedy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>January 2001</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecrafts_Dreamlands">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>H. P. Lovecraft's Dreamlands</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Roleplaying Beyond the Wall of Sleep Sandy Petersen et al Tom Sullivan Mark Roland Cover Artist Raymond Bayless United States English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Horror Roleplaying game Chaosium Release Date 1986 : Roleplaying Beyond the Wall of Sleep , also published in 1997 as The Complete Dreamlands , is a sourcebook for Chaosium ’s Mythos -based roleplaying game Call of Cthulhu . Overview Containing six scenarios which allow investigators to explore the extra-dimensional realm of the Dreamlands , a bestiary of inhabitants, comprehensive background information, player handouts, additional skills and colour maps of the world, was first released in 1986 and subsequently updated to accommodate later rules changes. It was written by Sandy Petersen with contributions from many others including Keith Herber , Richard T. Launius and Lynn Willis , and artwork by Raymond Bayless, Tom Sullivan and Mark Roland. Scenarios "To Sleep, Perchance to Dream", an introductory adventure. "Captives of the Two Worlds" "Pickman's Student" "Season of the Witch" "Lemon Sails" "The Land of Lost Dreams" Gallery References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Sandy Petersen et al</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1986</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cult_of_Glaaki">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cults"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Organisation"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cult of Glaaki</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Glaaki"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Glaaki is a fictional group of humans and undead created by ramsey campbell for his 1964 short story " the inhabitant of the lake " the group has been mentally enslaved by the great old one glaaki in order to have them do his bidding . Their main appears to be the recording of the sum wisdom of their master in the collected volumes the revelations of glaaki .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNotableMemberO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Thomas_Lee"/>
    <ex:hasPurposeO>The service of Glaaki</ex:hasPurposeO>
    <ex:hasTypeO>Cult</ex:hasTypeO>
    <ex:isLocatedAtO>The Severn Valley</ex:isLocatedAtO>
    <ex:isLocatedAtO>United Kingdom</ex:isLocatedAtO>
    <ex:wasCreatedBynO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ramsey_Campbell"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#An_Inhabitant_of_Carcosa">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>An Inhabitant of Carcosa</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ambrose bierce's story first appeared in the san francisco newsletter of december 25, 1886 . It was later reprinted in bierce's collections tales of soldiers and civilians . Elements of the story, including the setting of carcosa, were later incorporated into the cthulhu mythos .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ambrose_Bierce"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Fantasy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>California Advisor</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>San Francisco Newsletter</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1886 December 25</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mythos_%28card_game%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Mythos (card game)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mythos was a collectible card game for two or more players published by chaosium . It was first published in 1996, but discontinued in 1997 after a slump in sales . It was succeeded in 2004 by call of cthulhu collectible card game, a licensed ccg from fantasy flight games .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Charlie Crank</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>sci-fi</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Surprising_Sweetness_Of_Their_Blood">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Surprising Sweetness Of Their Blood</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The house of the ocelot &amp; more lovecraftian nightmares date 2019is a short cthulhu mythos story by authors mark mclaughlin and michael sheehan, jr.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Michael Sheehan. Jr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>More Lovecraftian Nightmares</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The House of the Ocelot</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2019</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#October">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>October</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here H. P. Lovecraft United States English Arkham House Beyond the Wall of Sleep Date of writing 1920 date 1943 " " is a poem by the late American Howard Phillips Lovecraft . Written in 1920, it did not see until its inclusion in the 1943 Arkham House collection Beyond the Wall of Sleep , six years after Lovecraft's death. It has since been reprinted several times in various collections . Overview The poem, as may be gathered from its title, is an ode to the month of , in which it was written. History Beyond the Wall of Sleep (1943) Collected Poems (1963) The World of H. P. Lovecraft (1971) Fungi from Yuggoth and Other Poems (1971) Medusa and Other Poems by H. P. Lovecraft (1986) The Ancient Track: The Complete Poetical Works of H. P. Lovecraft (2001, 2013) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Beyond_the_Wall_of_Sleep"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1943</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>October 1920</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraftian:_The_Shipwright_Circle">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lovecraftian: The Shipwright Circle</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>121 is a short novel by american and comic book creator steven philip jones . It was published by caliber comics in august 2019; both paperback and digital editions were released . The story transposes several characters from h.p. Lovecraft into a contemporary setting .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Steven_Philip_Jones"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>121</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 2019</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ym-bhi">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Y'm-bhi</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>K'n-yanians</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The corpses are mechanically reanimated with atomic energy and thought power by their k'n-yanian masters . Most are taken from the mortal slave-class but some are the remains of disgraced freemen and captured humans .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Mound"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dormammu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dormammu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Background is an eldritch inter and extra-dimensional demonic entity from the fictional universe of marvel by marvel comics . He is a billion years old and is one of the faltines, a race of beings of pure magic who was exiled from their dimension . His power eclipses that of mephisto and other demonic entities, though his own power is vastly eclipsed by multiversal demonic entities . He commands armies of demons, which guard his dimension, are</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Necronomicon:_Selected_Stories_and_Essays_Concerning_the_Blasphemous_Tome_of_the_Mad_Arab">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Necronomicon: Selected Stories and Essays Concerning the Blasphemous Tome of the Mad Arab</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The necronomicon: selected stories and essays concerning the blasphemous tomes of the mad arab was released by chaosium as part of their call of cthulhu fiction line . Edited by prolific mythos contributor robert m. price, it contains 58 short pieces (60 in the second edition) from a variety of authors, although lin carter 's works dominate the collection at 41 entries .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>334 543</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu Fiction</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>November 1996</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shadow_Over_Innsmouth_and_Other_Stories_of_Horror">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Shadow Over Innsmouth and Other Stories of Horror</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft Cover Artist Alan Magee English Scholastic Books Release Date 1971 255 is a collection of H. P. Lovecraft stories published in 1971 by Scholastic Books for the direct-to-school market. Sold for 75 cents, it featured an introduction by Margaret Ronan and cover art by Alan Magee. Cody Goodfellow credits the book with introducing him to the Cthulhu Mythos . Contents "A Word to the Reader..." (introduction by Margaret Ronan) " The Colour Out of Space " " The Outsider " " Imprisoned with the Pharaohs " (as Harry Houdini ) " The Transition of Juan Romero " " In the Walls of Eryx " (with Kenneth Sterling ) " The Festival " " The Shadow Over Innsmouth " References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>255 pages</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1971</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Christmas_Special_and_Other_Lovecraftian_Yuletide_Tales">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Christmas Special and Other Lovecraftian Yuletide Tales</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Jesse Dedman and William C. Marchese English William C. Marchese and Jesse Dedman Release Date November 2017 80  is a short anthology of Lovecraftian fiction first published in November 2017. Edited by Jesse Dedman and William C. Marchese , it was independently published by those two. Both trade paperback and digital editions were released. Overview Contents " 'Twas the Night Before Madness " by S. Alessandro Martinez " Next Stop: R'lyeh " by Shadrick Beechem " A Christmas Carnage " James Dorr "Red Christmas" by Tony Evans "Mausoleum 13" by Gerri R. Gray "The Christmas Creature" by William Marchese "The Thing in Crimson" by S. Alessandro Martinez " The Horror in the Stable " by R. C. Mulhare " Christmas Day " by DJ Tyrer " Secret Santa " by Steven M. Vance "Hark, the Herald!" by Aaron Vlek</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>80</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>November 2017</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lazarus">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lazarus</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>It was first published by alvin michel, and was later translated into english . Ironically, given its 's extreme anglophobia , it is more famous in its english translation than in its native language .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Henri_B%C3%A9raud"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#France"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>French</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1924</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pflegmwad">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Pf'legmwad</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>'s subconscious mind manifests itself as a pair of humanoid figures known as c'rruso and d'mmingo . While dreaming, 's subconscious mind also manifests itself as a pair of humanoid figures known as 'c'rruso and d'mmingo . While dreaming, 's subconscious mind also manifests itself as a pair of humanoid figures known as 'c'rruso and d'mmingo</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Call of C'rruso</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Laura Shaw</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Mark</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Graeme_Davis">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Graeme Davis</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>British games designer, novelist, and editor graeme davis was born 29 august 1958 in isleworth, england . He has penned a number of works for the cthulhu mythos, including material for the achtung! Cthulhu roleplaying game . Cited lovecraft's friend and long-time correspondent robert e. howard as one of his influences .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>http://example.org/lovecraft#England Isleworth</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United Kingdom</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://graemedavis.wordpress.com</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Contributor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1958</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Testament_of_Athammaus">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ClarkAshtonSmithWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Testament of Athammaus</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Wtis a short fantasy story by the late american and lovecraft contemporary clark ashton smith . It forms part of his hyperborean cycle of tales . Athammaus relates how his once-great city came to be abandoned overnight .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Fantasy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hyperborean_Cycle"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 1932</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Testament_of_Cthulhu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Testament of Cthulhu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A man who believed his mother to have died in an accident stumbles across the small town of sea dream . There he discovers an enclave of deep ones in the process of transitioning from land to sea . Together they travel to the city of y'ha-nthlei to pursue their worship of cthulhu .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Michael Sheehan. Jr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Abominations: 24 Tales of the Cthulhu Mythos</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Horrors</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 2018</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Thomas_Malone">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Thomas Malone</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>New York police department</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Thomas f. malone is an irish- new york police detective . At the age of 42, he investigates mysterious disappearances in the red hook neighbourhood . At one point he questions the elderly recluse robert suydam, whom he suspects of skullduggery .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_at_Red_Hook"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Aspirations">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Aspirations</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>44 is a gaming supplement for chaosium 's call of cthulhu horror roleplaying game . Created and published by third-party content provider, and boasts an ensemble cast of designers . Both softcover and digital editions were released .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>44</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2019</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Codex_Eastertide_1999">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Codex Eastertide 1999</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Allen Koszowski English Necronomicon Press Release Date April 1999 60 Preceded by Hallowmas 1998 Followed by Winter 2000 is the penultimate issue of the long-running but irregularly-released fanzine Chronicles of the Cthulhu Codex . Edited by Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. - the only one of seventeen issues for which he filled this role - and featuring cover art by Allen Koszowski , it was published by Necronomicon Press in April 1999. Contents Editorial: From the Scriptorium by Pierre Comtois "Sesqua Bud" by Chad Hensley "What the Moon Brings" by Robert A. Lovely "The Easiest Case" by Jason Kuhl "The Nightmare of the Spheres" by R. Michael Burns "The Dungeon of Self" by C. J. Henderson "III. Do Not Calle Up" by Ann K. Schwader "Ishtar" by Michael Fantina "After the Wheel Turned" by Joseph S. Pulver, Sr.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>60</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>April 1999</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dark_Giant">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dark Giant</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Elder Gods</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Many Sided Ones</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The dark giants are a fictional race of extra-dimensional entities in the dc comics multiverse . They appear as the primary antagonists in the latter half of the 14-issue secret six vol 4 comic run . They were given the name "dark giants" as entities like m'nagalah were referred to as elder gods in earlier comics .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Secret Six Vol 4 8</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Dale Eaglesham</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Gail Simone</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Midnight_Sons">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Organisation"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OrganisationsOriginatingFromMythosAdjacentWorks"/>
    <rdfs:label>Midnight Sons</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The (aka, children of midnight) is a supernatural team of superheroes . They have dedicated themselves to protect humanity from supernatural threats . They have clashed repeatedly with the elder god chthon and its minions . Recently they pursued the serial killer carnage .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNotableMemberO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Stephen_Strange"/>
    <ex:hasNotableMemberO>Ghost Rider</ex:hasNotableMemberO>
    <ex:hasTypeO>team</ex:hasTypeO>
    <ex:wasCreatedBynO>Andy Kubert</ex:wasCreatedBynO>
    <ex:wasCreatedBynO>Howard Mackie</ex:wasCreatedBynO>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Roodmas_1985">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu Roodmas 1985</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Jason C. Eckhardt Cover Artist Stephen Fabian United States English Crypt of Cthulhu Cryptic Publications Release Date May 1985 56 Preceded by Eastertide 1985 Followed by St. John's Eve 1985 is the 31st issue of leading H. P. Lovecraft scholar Robert M. Price 's long-running Lovecraftian fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Edited by Price with a cover by Stephen E. Fabian and interior art by Jason C. Eckhardt (who provided an accompanying image for each story), it ran to a total of 56 in an A5 format, and was published by Cryptic Publications in May 1985 . Contents Editorial Shards by Robert M. Price "The Tomb of Neb" by Gary Myers "Discovery Time" by Frank Belknap Long "The Monument" by Carl Jacobi "Chief White Cloud" by Duane Rimel "Mnomquah" by Lin Carter "Nemesis of the Unfinished" by Don Carter and Clark Ashton Smith "Golnor the Ape" by Robert E. Howard "Fun Guys from Yuggoth: Howard Lovecraft and Bobby Fischer" by Peter H. Cannon "Advice to the Lovecraft-Lorn" by Donna Death Letters by Dan Gobbett, Brian Lumley , Raymond T. Funamoto, Charles Garofalo , Steve Behrends , Kathy Corcoran, Kenneth W. Faig, Jr., Chris Beekman, Armando D Marini, Jr., Robert M. Jefts, Kenneth M. Humphreys, and Ramsey Campbell References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>56</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 1985</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
