  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Stuart_Boon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Stuart Boon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Games Designer Edmonton, Alberta, Canada is a Canadian-born games designer, author and university lecturer currently living in Scotland. His breakthrough work in the gaming industry was the highly-acclaimed Shadows Over Scotland in 2011 for Cubicle 7 Entertainment 's Cthulhu Britannica line of Call of Cthulhu supplements, after which he assumed the Line Developer role for those publications. Boon has also produced Call of Cthulhu content for Golden Goblin Press . Selected Bibliography Gaming Supplements Shadows Over Scotland (2011) The Ballad of Bass Rock (2012) Folklore (2013, with James Desborough and others) World War Cthulhu: The Darkest Hour (2013) De Horrore Cosmico (2015, contributor) Britannia &amp; Beyond (2023, with Oscar Rios , Richard Watts and others) External Links at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>Alberta Canada Edmonton</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games Designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Call_for_Cthulhu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Storybooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Call for Cthulhu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Young cthulhu is a children's picture book which was inspired by h. p. lovecraft 's short story " the call of cthulhu " written by j. j. savage with illustrations by jennifer singer</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#J._J._Savage"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Young Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Herbert_West--Reanimator">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Herbert West--Reanimator</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story was written between october 1921 and june 1922 . It was first serialized in february through july 1922 in the homebrew magazine . Lovecraft claimed to be unhappy with the work, writing it only because he was being paid five dollars for each installment .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Home_Brew"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>February–July 1922</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Anne_Phillips">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSRelatives"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Anne Phillips</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>29 August 1852</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Wife of Gardner Lyon, whom she married 17 March 1823. She gave birth to eight children: Annie, Albert, Jason, Alden, Hannah, Susan, Esther, and Samantha.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isRelativeToR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1803</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Return_of_the_Deep_Ones_and_Other_Mythos_Tales">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#BrianLumleyWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CrossroadPress"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Return of the Deep Ones and Other Mythos Tales</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Brian Lumley United Kingdom English Genre(s) Horror Roc UK Release Date February 1994 Media Type Anthology is a collection of Cthulhu Mythos works by British Brian Lumley . It contains two Titus Crow tales (one short story and one novella) and two stand-alone novels. Contents " Inception " (1987) " Lord of the Worms " (1983) Beneath the Moors (1974) The Return of the Deep Ones (1984) Publication History was published by Roc UK in February 1994, and has since received an ebook release. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United Kingdom</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>February 1994</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Devilarium:_The_Mythos_Merchant">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Devilarium: The Mythos Merchant</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Devilarium is a new mythos creature which will trade information or items of use to investigators in return for something precious to the investigators . "zgrozy" is an old polish word of archaic usage, meaning "horrible" or "terror".</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hypnos">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CharactersIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ElderGods"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Hypnos</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Elder Gods</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>In the mythos is the greek/roman god of dreams and appears in lovecraft's short story, where he meets a nameless man who would soon be his friend by chance . The two travel further beyond, going further and deeper each time they transcend the previous plateau of existence . His friend gives up and awakens from his sleep and waits for him to wake up as well, only to be driven insane by what he experienced afterwards .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nag%C3%A4ae">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonSapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ServitorRaces"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nagäae</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cy%C3%A4egha"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cyäegha First appearance EXP : " Darkness, My Name Is " Created by Eddy C. Bertin The are a fictional race of toad-like servitors of Cyäegha , created by Eddy C. Bertin in the 1976 story " Darkness, My Name Is ". Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Darkness"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>My Name Is</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Eddy_C._Bertin"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Lurking_Fear:_Collected_Short_Stories_Volume_Four">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Lurking Fear: Collected Short Stories Volume Four</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft Cover Artist Nichola Trayler-Barbrook United Kingdom English Genre(s) Fantasy, Horror, Science fiction short stories Wordsworth Editions Limited Release Date 2013 Media Type Print (Paperback) ~272 pp Preceded by The Haunter of the Dark: Collected Short Stories Volume Three is a collection of fantasy, horror and science fiction short stories by the American H. P. Lovecraft . Contents The Lurking Fear : Collected Short Stories Volume Four contains these texts: " The Lurking Fear " " The Beast in the Cave " " The Alchemist " " The Tomb " " Beyond the Wall of Sleep " " Memory " " The Transition of Juan Romero " " The White Ship " " The Terrible Old Man " " Facts concerning the Late Arthur Jermyn and His Family " " The Street " " The Temple " " The Tree " " From Beyond " " Nyarlathotep " " The Picture in the House " " Ex Oblivione " " The Quest of Iranon " " The Moon-Bog " " The Outsider " " The Music of Erich Zann " " Hypnos " " Azathoth " External links summary</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United Kingdom</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Fantasy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Science fiction short stories</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>~272 pp</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2013</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Three_Enchantments">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Three Enchantments</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The house of the worm is a short story by american author gary myers . It tells the story of a carpet salesman named dlareb and his apprentice, lir . Dlareb had gone to hide the silver ball and ended up undergoing a horrific flabby transformation .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gary_Myers"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The House of the Worm</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1975</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Susan_Esther_Phillips">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSRelatives"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Susan Esther Phillips</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>28 July 1851</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation></ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isRelativeToR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1827</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Kalanp%C3%A4inen">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Kalanpäinen</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Iku-Turso"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dagon"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as dagon "mahtavat ammoiset" is a fictional great old one connected with finland . Created by the finnish writer tuomas saloranta in the 2012 short story " mahtavat ammoiset " ( means "fish-head" or "fish-headed" in finnish)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tuomas_Saloranta"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Henry_Kuttner">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsCorrespondents"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Henry Kuttner</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1958 February 3</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>W. t. kuttner (1915-1958) was a prolific american author of science fiction, fantasy, and horror . Kuttner contributed several stories to the cthulhu mythos invented by lovecraft and smith . Kuttner's first published story, " the graveyard rats," caught lovecraft's eye .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA http://example.org/lovecraft#California Los Angeles</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>American</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Edward J. Bellin Paul Edmonds Noel Gardner Will Garth James Hall Keith Hammond Hudson Hastings Peter Horn Kelvin Kent Robert O. Kenyon C. H. Liddell Hugh Maepenn Scott Morgan Lawrence O'Donnell Lewis Padgett Woodrow Wilson Smith Charles Stoddard</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1915 April 17</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Baby_Downstairs">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Baby Downstairs</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Chad Fifer English 01Publishing Whispers from the Abyss Vol. 2: The Horrors That Are &amp; Shall Be date October 2015  " " is a tongue-in-cheek short story by Chad Fifer , first published in the Kat Rocha -edited anthology of Lovecraftian fiction Whispers from the Abyss Vol. 2: The Horrors That Are &amp; Shall Be by 01Publishing in October 2015. Synopsis A man is threatened by a seemingly-intelligent baby living alone in the downstairs apartment, one which makes the police officers which he calls to deal with the situation disappear… The story culminates with the man finding a bloodstained rattle outside his front door along with a note which reads "Don’t make me come up here again".</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chad_Fifer"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Shall Be</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Whispers from the Abyss Vol. 2: The Horrors That Are</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu:_Death_May_Die">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu: Death May Die</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A group of players (up to 5) work together to stop a group of old one worshiping cultists who are attempting to awaken an elder god . To achieve this, they will need to fight through various monsters and cultists, disrupt the ritual of the cultists, and deal with the elder god when it inevitably awakens . The game has a series of episodes, each one providing a different map, set of monsters, and methods of disrupting the</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2019</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Laundry">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Organisation"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OrganisationsOriginatingFromMythosInspiredWorks"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Laundry</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationO>U.K. government</ex:hasAffiliationO>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>General Information Government agency National defence U.K. government Real World Information The Atrocity Archives Created by Charles Stross  is a fictional British secret agency dedicated to fighting extradimensional threats, invented by author Charles Stross for his Laundry Files series. Agents Bob Howard: The main character and narrator of most Laundry Files stories, Howard is an IT consultant recruited into after stumbling onto one of the equations that open a pathway for dangerous extradimensional entities. The name he uses in the books is not his real name or his actual cover name, but a pseudonym used in his work diaries derived from Robert E. Howard . Mo O'Brien: Bob's love interest and later wife, she wields a sentient violin made of bone once owned by " Erich Zahn " as a weapon of mass destruction. James Angelton: Bob's boss and a senior official at , Angelton (a pseudonym taken from a famously paranoid historical CIA officer) is the vessel for a powerful glad-he's-on-our-side entity known as the Eater of Souls.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasPurposeO>National defence</ex:hasPurposeO>
    <ex:hasTypeO>Government agency</ex:hasTypeO>
    <ex:wasCreatedBynO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Charles_Stross"/>
  </rdf:Description>
