  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lexicromicon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lexicromicon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mark Hayes English Genre(s) Non-fiction Steampowered Books Release Date March 2022 305 : A Bluffers Guide to the Writings of H. P. Lovecraft is a literary guidebook to the works of Howard Phillips Lovecraft written by British Mark Hayes. Running to a total of 305 , it was published by Steampowered Books in March 2022; both paperback and digital editions were released. Overview Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Mark Hayes</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>305</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>March 2022</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Laundry_Files">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#StoryCycles"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Laundry Files</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The series combines the science fiction elements of the cthulhu mythos with the british spy thriller genre . In the world of , there are two primary espionage groups dealing with the paranormal--the british laundry and the united states' black chamber . As humanity increases in number and computational power, the many-angled ones will be able to filter through the universe's walls .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Charles_Stross"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2001 - 2022</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Giant_Bee">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonSapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Giant Bee</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shub-Niggurath"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sociocultural Characteristics Harag-Kolath Shub-Niggurath Physical Characteristics Skin colour Black Real World Information First appearance EXP : " The Seed of the Star-God " "The Lords of Pain" Created by Richard L. Tierney Giant Bees are a fictional species created by the late American author Richard L. Tierney for his Cthulhu Mythos contributions. Overview The Giant Bees are servants of the Great Old One Shub-Niggurath , inhabiting the underground city of Harag-Kolath . References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The Lords of Pain</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Seed_of_the_Star-God"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_L._Tierney"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jeffrey_Moeller">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Jeffrey Moeller</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Traditional games designer Horror Jeffrey "Jeff" Moeller is a games designer and editor who has contributed material to a number of supplements for Chaosium 's seminal Call of Cthulhu Mythos roleplaying game, particularly through licensed third-party content provider Golden Goblin Press . Selected Bibliography Gaming Supplements Mortal Coils (1998, contributor) The Resurrected III: Out of the Vault (2002, contributor) Island of Ignorance: The Third Cthulhu Companion (2013, contributor) De Horrore Cosmico (2015, contributor) Fronti Nulla Fides (2019, contributor) Aspirations (2019, editor, contributor) An Inner Darkness (contributor) Tails of Valor (contributor) External Links Jeff Moeller at Goodreads Jeff Moeller at RPGGeek</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Traditional games designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Detective:_A_C._J._Henderson_Tribute">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Detective: A C. J. Henderson Tribute</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Anthology Various English Self-published Release Date April 2015 394 is a thematic anthology of Lovecraftian fiction dedicated to the late American C. J. Henderson . Edited by David Conyers , David Kernot and Konstantine Paradias , it was issued in ebook format and published by the authors themselves. Overview Contents Introduction: Just the Facts by Robert M. Price "A Large and Rattling Stick" by C. J. Henderson "Unsatisfied" by Brian M. Sammons "Black Wind" by Cody Goodfellow " The Avatars of the Old Ones " by Jeffrey Thomas "The Glass Jaw" by Glynn Owen Barrass and Ron Shiflet "The Forth Protocol" by William Meikle "The Violation of Anne Newman" by Peter Rawlik "Dreams of Destruction" by Shane Jiraiya Cummings "The Search for Giselle" by David Kernot "The Prince and the Power of the Air" by Robert M. Price "The Folding Mansion" by Konstantine Paradias "The Temporal Deception" by David Conyers and C. J. Henderson</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>394</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>April 2015</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Eltdown_Shards">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosBooksFictional"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdfs:label>Eltdown Shards</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The are a fictional archeological discovery, depicted as a collection of pottery fragments that preserve pre-human writing . They were invented by richard f. searight in his story " the sealed casket " (1935), and incorporated into the cthulhu mythos through h. p. lovecraft's contribution to the round-robin story " the challenge from beyond " (1935) they are named for the fictional village in england where they were discovered .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeA>Book</ex:hasTypeA>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnA rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Sealed_Casket"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByA>Om Oris</ex:wasCreatedByA>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Errol_Undercliffe:_A_Tribute">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RamseyCampbellWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Errol Undercliffe: A Tribute</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ramsey Campbell English Arkham House Demons by Daylight date 1973 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos piece by British Ramsey Campbell , first published by Arkham House in the 1973 collection Demons by Daylight . It is a companion piece to " The Franklyn Paragraphs ", a story which first saw print in the same book. Overview Written in the style of a newspaper obituary, "" notes Undercliffe 's hometown of Brichester , his bibliography, an upcoming screen series based on his writing by a Korean director, and his mysterious disappearance.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ramsey_Campbell"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Demons_by_Daylight"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1973</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Musketeers_vs._Cthulhu_in_the_Court_of_King_Louis">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Musketeers vs. Cthulhu in the Court of King Louis</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various English Genre(s) Lovecraftian horror Black Ink Fiction Release Date May 2022 346 is an anthology of Lovecraftian fiction edited by Tim Mendees and published by Black Ink Fiction in May 2022; both paperback and digital editions were released . Overview Contents Foreword by Tim Mendees "The Man in the Golden Mask" by Rob Poyton "The Esuugat Tablets" by Claudia Christian and Chris McAuley "The Disappearance of Sergent Frederick Chambois" by Stephen Herczeg "The Cult of Ghouls" by Gavin Chappell "The Stone, the Serpent, and the Man of God" by Tim Mendees "The Secret of the Templars" by Jasiah Witkofsky "The Powers of Cthulhu" by E. W. Farnsworth "Brief Moment in Time" by Carlton Herzog "Porthos in Peril" by Chris Soucy "The Tower" by Shelley De Cruz "The Mirror of Madame Fortier" by Mark Rankin "Judicium Aquae Frigidae" by J. M. Faulkner "A Shadow Over Paris" by Mike Adamson "The Thing in the Iron Mask" by John Houlihan "The Red Eminence &amp; the Abyss" by Nicholas Wilkinson About the Authors References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Lovecraftian horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>346</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 2022</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Henry_S._Whitehead">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsCoAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsCorrespondents"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Henry S. Whitehead</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1932 November 23</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Henry st. clair whitehead was an american writer of horror fiction and fantasy . He co-wrote two short stories with h. p. lovecraft , " the trap " (published in 1931) and " bothon " (1946) whitehead was a correspondent of h. p. lovecraft .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>United States http://example.org/lovecraft#New_Jersey Elizabeth</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>American</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1882 March 5</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tales_of_the_Lovecraft_Mythos">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tales of the Lovecraft Mythos</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various fedogan &amp; bremer release date 1992 is an anthology of cthulhu mythos fiction . Edited by robert m. price, it covers the pulp era, but extends no further . Price's subsequent anthology the new lovecraft circle serves as a sequel of sorts to .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1992</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Tryout">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AmateurPress"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Tryout</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Charles w. smith english 1914 final publication 1946 was an amateur publication, part of the national amateur press association . The monthly was the first outlet for several lovecraft stories: "the cats of ulthar" (november 1920), "the terrible old man" (july 1921), "the tree" (october 1921), and "in the vault" (november 1925) lovecraft also published numerous poems and articles in smith's journal under a variety of pseudonyms, including lawrence appleton, alexander ferguson</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dythalla">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dythalla</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Lord of Lizards</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as Lord of Lizards First appearance " Lord of Lizards " Created by James Ambuehl , also known as the Lord of Lizards , is a gigantic reptilian creature similar to Bokrug , but terrestrial and with a mane of feelers.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Lord of Lizards</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Ambuehl"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#That_Tantalizing_Taste_You_Will_Never_Forget">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>That Tantalizing Taste You Will Never Forget</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Story was published in the 2018 collection horrors &amp; abominations: 24 tales of the cthulhu mythos . It was written by mark mclaughlin and michael sheehan, jr.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Michael Sheehan. Jr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Abominations: 24 Tales of the Cthulhu Mythos</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Horrors</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 2018</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scarlet_Obeisance">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Scarlet Obeisance</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. English Publication date August 1999 " " is a Cthulhu Mythos poem by the late American Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. Synopsis The eight fairly long verses in essence sing the praise of the Great Old One Chaugnar Faugn . Publication History "" was first printed in the online zine Nightscapes in August 1999, and then in the Hallowmas 2000 issue of Crypt of Cthulhu . In 2008 it was included in the Robert M. Price -edited anthology The Tindalos Cycle , and finally in the the collection of Pulver's own work, SIN &amp; Ashes in 2010.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Joseph S. Pulver. Sr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>August 1999</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tim_Kirk">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tim Kirk</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Artist Birth Date 30 October 1947 Long Beach, California, USA United States is a multi-award winning American artist who has contributed imagery to the Cthulhu Mythos inspired by the works of Howard Phillips Lovecraft . He specialised in fantasy and science fiction works, but is now retired. In 2019, Kirk created a map of Clark Ashton Smith's fictional French province of Averoigne; this was published in the Pickman's Press collection The Averoigne Archives: The Complete Averoigne Tales of Clark Ashton Smith . Selected Works Cover Art Awaiting Strange Gods (2015) Interior Art Awaiting Strange Gods (2015) The Averoigne Archives: The Complete Averoigne Tales of Clark Ashton Smith (2019) See Also Images by External Links at Wikipedia at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA http://example.org/lovecraft#California Long Beach</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>30 October 1947</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#RhThulla">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Rh'Thulla</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Rh'Thulla of the Wind</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as of the Wind Relatives M'nagalah (brother) Kag'Naru (sister) First appearance ADJ : Challengers of the Unknown #83 Created by Gerry Conway is a fictional deity created by DC Comics for their comic series Challengers of the Unknown . Background is the brother of M'nagalah , who was shut off from the cosmos by the Golden Ones. was enlisted to corrupt the minds of men so that he and his kind could rule the universe again.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Challengers of the Unknown #83</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Gerry Conway</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dark_Eidolon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ClarkAshtonSmithWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Dark Eidolon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Eidolon is a short story by clark ashton smith , set in zothique , "the last continent on earth" it was first published in weird tales in january 1935 . It has since appeared in numerous collections of smith's work .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>January 1935</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Green_and_Pleasant_Land">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Green and Pleasant Land</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A regional sourcebook for the call of cthulhu horror roleplaying game . Produced by american roleplaying game manufacturer chaosium . Includes work from ensemble cast of writers including brian lumley .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United Kingdom</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>80</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1987</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#When_the_Stars_Are_Right">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>When the Stars Are Right</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Dreams from the strangers' café is a short story by british author . It is told in the format of a series of message transcripts from a woman to her husband . When humanity discovers that the manifestation of mythos creatures releases staggering amounts of energy, they develop technology capable of storing and utilising this source .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#William_Meikle"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Science Fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Dreams from the Strangers' Café #2</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>April 1994</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Thing_in_the_Woods">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Thing in the Woods</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>It was first published in the united kingdom by gerald duckworth &amp; co. in 1913 . Behind the mythos has been speculated by some to be a possible inspiration for h. p. lovecraft 's 1929 short story " the dunwich horror "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Margery_Williams"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>Great Britain</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Children's</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1913</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Basilisk">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SpeciesIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdfs:label>Basilisk</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A species of extra-dimensional entity created by american games manufacturer chaosium for their call of cthulhu roleplaying game . It was inspired by the real-world legends of the , a creature known in european mythology since at least the beginning of the first century ad . Their toxin is so potent that any area they inhabit soon becomes a desolate, inhospitable wasteland . An unusual capability of the is its ability to transfer its venom to its prey</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_Studies">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lovecraft Studies</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>S. T. Joshi Marc Michaud Non-fiction English Necronomicon Press 1979 Final Publication 2005 was an academic journal focused on the work of H. P. Lovecraft , launched in 1979 by Marc Michaud , of Necronomicon Press , and Lovecraft scholar S. T. Joshi . A semi-annual, the journal was a leading outlet for Lovecraftian scholarship. After it stopped publishing in 2005, its work was continued by Lovecraft Annual starting in 2007. The H. P. Lovecraft Archives has a list of issues of , an online index and links to selected articles found on the Web. Issues 9-21 are available online on Archives.org.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Peaslee_Papers">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Peaslee Papers</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A Lovecraftian Chronicle Peter Rawlik Cover Artist Raven Daemorgan English Lovecraft eZine Press Release Date November 2017 297 : A Lovecraftian Chronicle is a collection of Cthulhu Mythos stories by American Peter Rawlik which traces the history of the Peaslee family across the ages, and documents their long-running conflict with the Great Race of Yith . It was published by Lovecraft eZine Press in 2017. Overview Contents "In the Hall of the Yellow King" "Operation Starfish" " The Pestilence of Pandora Peaslee " "The Crucifixion of Yig" "Tempus Edax Rerum" "The Lost Treasure of Cobbler Keezar" "Professor Peaslee Plays Paris" "Professor Peaslee's Pandemonium" "Pr. Peaslee's Price" "Letter Found on a Dead Sailor" "The Temporary Chronologist" "The Time Traveler's Ex-Wife" "Hannah and Her Mother Take a Very Long Lunch" "The Statement of Lincoln Robinson" "Operation Switch" " Cold War, Yellow Fever " "Operation Alice" "The Watchmaker's Lament" "The Prognosis of Pandora Peaslee" "The Setting of the Sine" "A Sense of Time" "Seki"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Peter_Rawlik"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>297</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>November 2017</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#In_Lovecrafts_Shadow">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AugustDerlethWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>In Lovecraft's Shadow</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The cthulhu mythos stories of august derleth is a collection of short works by the late american august derleth . Published in hardcover by arkham house in 1998, runs to a total of 351 not including introductions .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#August_Derleth"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>351 + xiv</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1998</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Titus_Crow">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dreamers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Titus Crow</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>The Elder Gods</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>The Wilmarth Foundation</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Crow is a fictional character created by brian lumley for the eponymous cthulhu mythos series . Originally an occultist with a vast knowledge of the in-universe mythos . Later rebuilt to be better in every way after a catastrophic accident . Became a persistent nemesis to the cthulhu cycle deities, and ithaqua in particular .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>See Appearances</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasCharacterMateC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tiania"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Billys_Oak"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Bibliographies_%28George_T._Wetzel%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Bibliographies (George T. Wetzel)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Bibliographies Various United States English Genre(s) Non-fiction SSR Publications Release Date 1955 43 Bibliographies is a short non-fiction chapbook collection focusing on the works of Howard Phillips Lovecraft . Overview Containing two essays and running to only 43 , Bibliographies was published by SSR Publications in 1955 in a limited run of just 75 numbered copies. It was edited by the late H. P. Lovecraft scholar George T. Wetzel . Contents " Amateur Press Works " by George T. Wetzel " Professional Works and Miscellany " by Robert E. Briney</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>43</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1955</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Ring_of_Eibon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Ring of Eibon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here Ron Shiflet United States English Publication date 1998 " " is a poem by American Ron Shiflet . Synopsis The work describes the magical properties of a demon-possessed ring owned by the mighty sorcerer Eibon the Unfathomable . Publication History "" was published in the James Ambuehl -edited collection The Sorcerer's Apprentices: New Tales in the Tradition of Clark Ashton Smith.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Comte_Antoine_de_C--">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Comte Antoine de C--</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Antoine's family was cursed after his ancestor wrongly accused and murdered the reclusive alchemist michel mauvais . Antoine never knew his parents, for his father died from the curse, while his mother died in childbirth . Antoine was able to avoid his fate after the murderer, the long-lived charles le sorcier, botched antoine's assassination .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Alchemist"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Silent_Legions">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Silent Legions</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>is a traditional pen-and-paper Lovecraftian tabletop role-playing game with the emphasis on sandbox adventures. It was published by Sine Nomine Publishing in 2015. External links Review of at Geek Native page on DriveThruRPG.com</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lucy_%28BhYhlun%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lucy (Bh'Yhlun)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lucy is a character created by author ian davey as one of the protagonists in his bh'yhlun series of cthulhu mythos stories . She made her first appearance in 1997 in " black letter day " .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Black Letter Day (1997)</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ian_Davey"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Conan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Conan</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cimmerian is a sword and sorcery barbarian character, created by robert e. howard and first published in the pulp magazines weird tales . He has been a slave, a thief, a pirate, a mercenary, an adventurer, a hero, a general, and finally a king . He has encountered black sorcerers, hellish cults, ancient gods, demons, and</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Various Marvel Comics Demonbane</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Phoenix on the Sword</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_E._Howard"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shadow_From_the_Abyss">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleComicBookAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Shadow From the Abyss</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft (original story) United States English Genre(s) Horror Last Gasp Skull Comics date August 1972 " " is an American comic book strip. Overview An adaptation of the Howard Phillips Lovecraft novella The Shadow Out of Time , "" was drawn by cartoonist Larry Todd . It appeared in Skull Comics #5 in August 1972, and was issued by small press comic Last Gasp. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Skull Comics</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>August 1972</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_House_of_the_Ocelot_%26_More_Lovecraftian_Nightmares">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The House of the Ocelot &amp; More Lovecraftian Nightmares</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mark McLaughlin Michael Sheehan, Jr. English Release Date January 2019 220 (print editions) 222 (digital edition) is a collection of stories by authors Mark McLaughlin and Michael Sheehan, Jr. Overview contains 18 stories, runs to a total of 220 (222 as an ebook) and was released in paperback (January 2019), hardcover (December 2021) and digital editions. It is separated into five thematic sections. Contents Denizens of the Dreamlands " The House Of The Ocelot " " Queen Of The Vultures " " Another Terrible Old Man " " The Foul One Lives To Destroy: A Tale Of The Dreamlands " Worshippers of Ghattambah " In The Chapel Of Ghattambah " " The Hungry One Must Feed " " The Spawn Of Ghattambah " Shoggoths &amp; Hybrid Horrors " Shoggoth Apocalypse " " The Slimy Ones " " The Surprising Sweetness Of Their Blood " " The Glorious Return Of Herbert West " Magic &amp; Monstrosities "Aunt Paloma" (non-Lovecraftian monster horror) " The Last Witch-House " " Uncle Caesar " "Nobody Goes To Paw-Paw Town Any More" (non-Lovecraftian mythological horror) " Stainless Steel Sarcophagus " Doomsday " The Arrival Of Our New Master: A Lovecraftian Armageddon "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Michael Sheehan. Jr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>220 222</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>January 2019</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Delta_Green:_Alien_Intelligence">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Delta Green: Alien Intelligence</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various English Armitage House Release Date March 1998 189 is an anthology of Lovecraftian fiction based in the Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game sub-setting Delta Green . Edited by Bob Kruger and John Tynes , it was published in March 1998 by Pagan Publishing under their imprint Armitage House; it is currently available in digital and paperback print-on-demand formats through DriveThruRPG. Overview Contents Introduction by Bob Kruger Preface: A Word of Explanation by John Tynes "The Dark Above" by John Tynes "Drowning in Sand" by Dennis Detwiller "Pnomus" by Ray Winninger "Climbing the South Mountain" by Bruce Baugh "Potential Recruit" by Greg Stolze "An Item of Mutual Interest" by Adam Scott Glancy "Identity Crisis" by Bob Kruger "Operation LOOKING GLASS" by Blair Reynolds References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>189</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>March 1998</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Enchantress_of_Sylaire">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AveroigneCycle"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ClarkAshtonSmithWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Enchantress of Sylaire</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Anselme is a romantic young man who has become a hermit in averoigne . After 13 months of solitude, he meets a beautiful woman and an enormous wolf . The "wolf" reveals that he is the sorcerer malachie du marais, now a werewolf . The story first appeared in the july 1941 issue of weird tales .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Fantasy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Averoigne"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>July 1941</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Unicron">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Unicron</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lord of chaos is a multiversal mechanical eldritch entity and cosmic horror element from the fictional universe of the transformers comics and cartoons . He is the eternal foe of primus, his twin-brother (cybertron) and has one goal which is to consume the multiverse . His planet-sized form is powered by the energy of the moons, planets, and stars he has consumed . So far has consumed 22.56% of known realities .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Tindalos_Cycle">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Tindalos Cycle</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various United States English Hippocampus Press Release Date December 2008 Media Type Paperback 364 is a Cthulhu Mythos-dedicated anthology edited by Robert M. Price . Overview Comprising 27 works thematically linked by Frank Belknap Long 's short story " The Hounds of Tindalos " (which is included), the contents include works from Long himself, his influences such as Robert W. Chambers and Ambrose Bierce , and authors whose works have in turn been influenced by Long. Contents " The Maker of Moons " (1896) by Robert W. Chambers " The Death of Halpin Frayser " (1891) by Ambrose Bierce " The Space-Eaters " (1928) by Frank Belknap Long " The Hounds of Tindalos " (1929) by Frank Belknap Long " The Letters of Halpin Chalmers " (1994) by Peter Cannon " The Death of Halpin Chalmers " by Perry M. Grayson " The Madness Out of Time " (1986) by Lin Carter " The Hound of the Partridgevilles " (1999)" by Peter Cannon " Through Outrageous Angles " (1986) by David C. Kopaska-Merkel and Ronald McDowell " The Firebrands of Torment " (2007) by Michael Cisco " The Shore of Madness " by Ann K. Schwader " Gateway to Forever " (1984) by Frank Belknap Long " The Gift of Lycanthropy " (1979) by Frank Belknap Long " The War Among the Gods " by Adrian Cole " The Ways of Chaos " (1996) by Ramsey Campbell " Juggernaut " (2000) by C. J. Henderson " Scarlet Obeisance " (1999) by Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. " The Horror from the Hills " (1931) by Frank Belknap Long " Pompelo's Doom " by Ann K. Schwader " Confession of the White Acolyte " by Ann K. Schwader " When Chaugnar Wakes " (1932) by Frank Belknap Long " The Elephant God of Leng " (2002) by Robert M. Price " Death Is an Elephant " (1939) by Robert Bloch " The Dweller in the Pot, By Frank Chimesleep Short " (1990) by Robert M. Price " But It's A Long Dark Road " by Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. " Nyarlatophis, A Fable of Ancient Egypt " (2002) by Stanley C. Sargent " Mind-Pilot " (2003) by William Laughlin Publication History was published by Hippocampus Press in December 2008, and was reissued in May 2010. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>364</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>December 2008</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Will_of_Stanley_Brooke">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RamseyCampbellWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Will of Stanley Brooke</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Stanley brooke is diagnosed with terminal cancer and turns to ancient books of lore in an attempt to find a cure . He summons his lawyer, ernest bond, in order to dictate a new will, which states that his four siblings and niece will no longer be beneficiaries of his substantial estate . When william collier arrives at brooke’s house for the reading of the will, the family and bond all remark on the uncanny resemblance collier bears to the dead man . Bond takes</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ramsey_Campbell"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United Kingdom</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror Short Fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Inhabitant_of_the_Lake"/>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Less Welcome Tenants Cold Print</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1964</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nick_Mamatas">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nick Mamatas</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Birth Date 20 February 1972 Long Island, New York, USA United States Horror Science Fiction Fantasy Non-fiction World Fantasy Award http://www.nick-mamatas.com/index.php (20 February 1972-present) is an American author and editor of horror, science fiction, fantasy and non-fiction. Overview The winner of a Bram Stoker award and nominee for both World Fantasy and Hugo , Mamatas has written several works in and around the greater Cthulhu Mythos including the novel I Am Providence , an occult murder mystery set around the fictional H. P. Lovecraft -focused 'Summer Tentacular' fan convention. Selected Bibliography Novels I Am Providence (2016) Short Fiction " Hideous Interview with Brief Man " (2013) " Country Mouse, City Mouse " (2017)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>New York USA Long Island</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://www.nick-mamatas.com/index.php</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>20 February 1972</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Encyclopedia_Cthulhiana/Contents_F-L">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Encyclopedia Cthulhiana/Contents F-L</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>This page lists the contents from f-l of the chaosium reference book the encyclopedia cthulhiana by daniel harms . It is based on the work of daniel harms, author of the encyclopedia .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Book_of_Cthulhu_II">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Book of Cthulhu II</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Book of Cthulhu 2 Various Claudia Noble Cover Artist Claudia Noble English Night Shade Press Release Date October 2012 The Book of Cthulhu 2 is an anthology of Lovecraftian fiction edited by Ross E. Lockhart and published by Night Shade Books in October 2012. A digital edition was also released. Overview Contents Introduction by Ross E. Lockhart " Shoggoth's Old Peculiar " by Neil Gaiman " Nor the Demons Down Under the Sea (1957) " by Caitlín R. Kiernan " This Is How the World Ends " by John R. Fultz " The Drowning at Lake Henpin " by Paul Tobin "The Ocean and All Its Devices" by William Browning Spencer " Take Your Daughters to Work " by Livia Llewellyn "The Big Fish" by Kim Newman " Rapture of the Deep " by Cody Goodfellow " Once More, from the Top " by A. Scott Glancy " The Hour of the Tortoise " by Molly Tanzer "I Only Am Escaped Alone to Tell Thee" by Christopher Reynaga " Objects from the Gilman-Waite Collection " by Ann K. Schwader " Of Melei, of Ulthar " by Gord Sellar " A Gentleman from Mexico " by Mark Samuels " The Hands That Reek and Smoke " by W. H. Pugmire "Akropolis" by Matt Wallace " Boojum " by Elizabeth Bear and Sarah Monette " The Nyarlathotep Event " by Jonathan Wood " The Black Brat of Dunwich " by Stanley C. Sargent " The Terror from the Depths " by Fritz Leiber " Black Hill " by Orrin Grey " The God of Dark Laughter " by Michael Chabon " Sticks " by Karl Edward Wagner "Hand of Glory" by Laird Barron</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 2012</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_F_Morton">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSFriendsAndAcquaintances"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>James F Morton</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1941</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Amateur journalist, author of many tracts on race prejudice, free thought, and taxation, and longtime friend of Lovecraft.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1870</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhus_Vault">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu's Vault</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Richard Launius Jim Dietz Jolly Roger Games Publication Date 2015 Cooperative card game is a cooperative card game set in the Cthulhu Mythos . Created by world-renowned games Richard Launius , author of the first edition of Arkham Horror , and Jim Dietz, it was published in 2015 by Jolly Roger Games. Artwork was supplied by Mark Behm, William Bricker, and Jacob Walker . Overview Gallery References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Freshman">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Freshman</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story centers on roderick desmond, a 60-year-old writer of occult novels . He longs to escape the control of his domineering mother, but knows she will follow him even if he enters college . The hills to the west of arkham are named for the tamsiqueg indians .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Philip_Jos%C3%A9_Farmer"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>French</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Italian</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Science Fiction</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The Magazine of Fantasy</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>May 1979</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#At_the_Mountains_of_Madness_%281992_ARTC_audio_drama%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleAudioAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>At the Mountains of Madness (1992 ARTC audio drama)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>At the Mountains of Madness (1992 ARTC audio drama) Based on At the Mountains of Madness Thomas E. Fuller Henry Howard Production Company The Atlanta Radio Theatre Company Release date 1992 United States English At the Mountains of Madness is an audio drama adaptation of the H. P. Lovecraft novella of the same name produced by the Atlanta Radio Theatre Company . It was first performed live on stage at Pine Mountain, Georgia's World Horror Convention in 1992.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#At_the_Mountains_of_Madness"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1992</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Haunter_of_the_Graveyard">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Haunter of the Graveyard</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Tv presenter and scriptwriter has morbid fascination with sprawling graveyard . Takes to reading and, eventually, sleeping atop the graves . Eventually disappears, his badly decomposed and partially eaten remains being discovered in the area of the cemetery .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#J._Vernon_Shea"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>French</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tales_of_the_Cthulhu_Mythos"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1969</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Of_Melei,_of_Ulthar">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Of Melei, of Ulthar</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Gord Sellar English Genre(s) Cthulhu Mythos Wyrm Publishing Clarkesworld Magazine date October 2009 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos story by Malawian Gord Sellars, first published in the October 2009 edition of Clarkesworld Magazine . Synopsis Young Melei, a peasant serving girl from the city of Ulthar in the Dreamlands , discovers that when she sleeps, she has certain abilities which she doesn’t possess in her waking life. She uses her newfound power of flight to explore the farthest reaches of the new world laid bare beneath her. History Clarkesworld Magazine (2011) The Book of Cthulhu II (2012, 2022) Clarkesworld: Year Four (2013) Far-Fetched Fables (2014) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Gord Sellar</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Clarkesworld Magazine</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2009</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Rising_with_Surtsey">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#BrianLumleyWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Rising with Surtsey</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Short story was first published in the 1971 anthology dark things . It has appeared in several other collections by the author .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dark_Things"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1971</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nights_of_Wundagore">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nights of Wundagore</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mark Gruenwald, David Michelinie, Steven Grant John Byrne, Dan Green English Marvel Comics Release Date September 1979-July 1979 Media Type Comic book is a 3-part story in issues #185-185 of the 1st volume of the Avengers written by Mark Gruenwald, Steven Grant, and David Michelinie. Synopsis The Elder God Chthon made the sorcerer Modred his thrall and had him capture the Scarlet Witch as part of his plan to use her as his vessel on Earth.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>David Michelinie</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Mark Gruenwald</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Steven Grant</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>September 1979-July 1979</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Eastertide_1984">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu Eastertide 1984</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Jim Garrison United States English Crypt of Cthulhu Cryptic Publications Release Date April 1984 56 Preceded by Candlemas 1984 Followed by Saturnalia and Other Poems by H. P. Lovecraft is the twentieth issue of leading H. P. Lovecraft scholar Robert M. Price 's long running Lovecraftian fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Edited by Price, who published it through his Cryptic Publications label, it featured cover art by Jim Garrison, and ran to a total of 56 in an A5 format. This was the first issue of Crypt of Cthulhu to be issued through Cryptic Publications, and only the second publication to be released by the press. The focus of the issue was the poetry of Lovecraft, his friends and contemporaries. Contents Editorial Shards by Robert M. Price "H. P. Lovecraft's Fungi from Yuggoth" by David E. Schultz "Two Discarded Drafts of "The Well"" by H. P. Lovecraft "The Story in Fungi from Yuggoth" by Ralph E. Vaughan "Preference" by Clark Ashton Smith "The Lack of Continuity in Fungi from Yuggoth" by David E. Schultz "Nepenthe" by Samuel Loveman "Illuminating "The Elder Pharos"" by Will Murray "Wings of Dream" by Duane Rimel ""St. Toad's" Revisited" by Robert M. Price "Poet of the Unknown" by Dirk W. Mosig "Quatrain" by Samuel Loveman "Two Spurious Lovecraft Poems" by S. T. Joshi "The First Cthulhu Mythos Poem" by Will Murray "An Errata List to A Winter Wish" by S. T. Joshi "The Frozen Waterfall" by Clark Ashton Smith Untitled poem by Clark Ashton Smith "Above the Loud Sea" by Clark Ashton Smith "The Ship" by Duane Rimel "The Questioner" by R. H. Barlow "To Heine" by Samuel Loveman "The Lake of Enchanted Silence" by Clark Ashton Smith Reviews by uncredited contributors Letters by Daniel Gobbett, L. D. Blackmore, Will Murray, Steve Behrends , and M. Eileen McNamara, M.D.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>56</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>April 1984</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Punishment_of_Igharta">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Punishment of Igharta</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"the fleece of yaggar" is a pseudo-factual cthulhu mythos work by and editor f. c. adams . It tells of how the offspring of shub-niggurath , a creature named igharta, is cursed by hastur the unspeakable for the rape of one of his priestesses . He then goes on to sire the line of creatures known as satyrs .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>F. C. Adams</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Translations_from_the_Book_of_Yng"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Nightscapes #9</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>September 1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Neonomicon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Neonomicon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Alan Moore Jacen Burrows English Avatar Press Release Date July 2010 - February 2011 Media Type Comic book is a 4-part mini-series written by Alan Moore. Synopsis Two FBI question the former FBI agent Aldo Sax due to a copycat killer and their investigation leads them to a sex orgy that results in the death of one and the capture of the female agent Brears. Brears is repeatedly violated by a fishman , destined to become the mother of the infant Cthulhu .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Alan_Moore"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 2010 - February 2011</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#L._F._Falconer">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>L. F. Falconer</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Leanna Fay Falconer Connection to the Mythos Author United States https://www.lffalconer.com/ Leanna Fay Falconer is an American author whose works tend to be described as dark fantasy and supernatural, although she categorises herself as a character rather than genre writer. Selected Bibliography Short Fiction " Tuama " (2019)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Leanna Fay Falconer</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://www.lffalconer.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Best_of_H._P._Lovecraft:_Bloodcurdling_Tales_of_Horror_and_the_Macabre">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Best of H. P. Lovecraft: Bloodcurdling Tales of Horror and the Macabre</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft Cover Artist Michael Whelan English Genre(s) Horror Del Rey/Ballantine Release Date October 1982 375 is a collection of short stories by the late American H. P. Lovecraft . Overview First published in October 1982 by Del Rey/Ballantine, contains a total of 16 of Lovecraft's most prominent works and is prefaced by an introduction from one of Lovecraft’s protégés, the late American Robert Bloch , with cover art supplied by Michael Whelan. It was reprinted in September 1988 and April 1995, and received an ebook release in November 2002. Contents Introduction: "Heritage of Horror" by Robert Bloch " The Rats in the Walls " " The Picture in the House " " The Outsider " " Pickman's Model " " In the Vault " " The Silver Key " " The Music of Erich Zann " " The Call of Cthulhu " " The Dunwich Horror " " The Whisperer in Darkness " " The Colour Out of Space " " The Haunter of the Dark " " The Thing on the Doorstep " " The Shadow Over Innsmouth " " The Dreams in the Witch-House " " The Shadow Out of Time " Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>375</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 1982</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Viburnia">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Viburnia</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Relatives See image above Human First appearance HPL : " Family Tree of Azathoth " Created by H. P. Lovecraft is a character created by the late American author Howard Phillips Lovecraft for his tongue-in-cheek " Family Tree of Azathoth ". Overview Little is known of other than that she was a Roman noblewoman descended from Nyarlathotep . She mated with the being known as Ghoth the Burrower , itself a descendant of Cthulhu , a coupling which Lovecraft described as a "hellish and nameless tragedy" and eventually resulted in the birth of himself and Owen Gwynedd , amongst many others.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Family Tree of Azathoth</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Key_and_the_Gate_%28Call_of_Cthulhu_LCG%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Key and the Gate (Call of Cthulhu LCG)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Key and the Gate Fantasy Flight Games Publication Date 2013 Living Card Game Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game The Key and the Gate is a deluxe expansion for Fantasy Flight Games ' Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game . Overview Released in 2013 and containing a total of 165 cards (55 individual cards in sets of three), The Key and the Gate has at its core the Yog-Sothoth faction, although as with other deluxe expansions, cards are included for all factions as well as non-specific cards usable by all players. After Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game was replaced by Arkham Horror: The Card Game (likely due to copyright considerations), The Key and the Gate was discontinued. Contents Jeanne D'Ys, The Demoiselle (Character; Yog-Sothoth ) Claude Owen, Trying to Conquer Death (Character; Yog-Sothoth) Nadine Eskiy, The Namer (Character; Yog-Sothoth) Nikola Tesla , Man Out of Time (Character; Yog-Sothoth) Wilbur Whateley , Scion of Yog-Sothoth (Character; Yog-Sothoth) Professor Nathaniel Peaslee , Alien Intelligence (Character; Yog-Sothoth) Fthaggua , Regent of the Fire Vampires (Character; Yog-Sothoth) Yog-Sothoth, All-In-One (Character; Yog-Sothoth) Lost Oracle (Character; Yog-Sothoth) Wentshukumishiteu (Character; Yog-Sothoth) Hungry Star Vampire (Character; Yog-Sothoth) Quantum Theorist (Character; Yog-Sothoth) Decrepit Wizard (Character; Yog-Sothoth) Hand of Aforgomon (Character; Yog-Sothoth) Yithian Scout (Character; Yog-Sothoth) Keeper of the Great Library (Character; Yog-Sothoth) Scholar from Yith (Character; Yog-Sothoth) Scientist from Yith (Character; Yog-Sothoth) Displaced (Support; Yog-Sothoth) One-In-All (Support; Yog-Sothoth) Elder Chasm, Abyssal Rift (Support; Yog-Sothoth) Frozen Time (Support; Yog-Sothoth) Rite of the Silver Key, Ritual of the Profane (Support; Yog-Sothoth) Ys, Swallowed By The Sea (Support; Yog-Sothoth) Rite of the Silver Gate, Rending Space-Time (Support; Yog-Sothoth) De Vermis Mysteriis , Signs of the Black Stars (Support; Yog-Sothoth) Lost City of Pnakotus , Source of Forgotten Lore (Support; Yog-Sothoth) Dark Passenger (Support; Yog-Sothoth) Song of the Spheres (Support; Yog-Sothoth) Summon Spectral Hunter (Support; Yog-Sothoth) Temporal Slip (Event; Yog-Sothoth) Mists of Lethe (Event; Yog-Sothoth) Calling the Williwaw (Event; Yog-Sothoth) Vortex of Time (Event; Yog-Sothoth) Studying the Void (Event; Yog-Sothoth) Return to Yith (Event; Yog-Sothoth) Interstellar Migration (Event; Yog-Sothoth) Prepared Alienist (Character; Neutral) Visions of the Future's Past (Event; Neutral) Will of Azathoth , The Edicts of Madness (Support; Neutral) Eidolon (Event; Neutral) Norman Blackwood, Sr. Crafty Veteran (Character; The Agency) Franklin Automobile (Support; The Agency) Castro , Immensely Aged Mestizo (Character; Cthulhu ) Cyclopean Stone, Horrifying Monolith (Support; Cthulhu) Sieur Piriou Louis, Hastur 's Huntsman (Character; Hastur) The Tattered Cloak, Regalia Dementia (Support; Hastur) Andrew-Chapman, Boon Companion (Character; Miskatonic University ) Nepenthe (Support; Miskatonic University) Slenderman, Feaster Upon the Innocent (Character; Shub-Niggurath ) The Festival, Bringer of Strange Joy (Support; Shub-Niggurath) B. Ramsdale Brown, Well Connected (Character; Silver Twilight) The Black Stone, Leyline Nexus (Support; Silver Twilight) Arsène Renard, Gentleman Thief (Character; Syndicate) Burglar Tools (Support; Syndicate) Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2013</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Search_for_the_Silver_Key_%28Call_of_Cthulhu_LCG%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Search for the Silver Key (Call of Cthulhu LCG)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Search for the Silver Key Fantasy Flight Games Publication Date 2008 Living Card Game Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game Search for the Silver Key is an expansion pack for Fantasy Flight Games ' Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game . Overview The fourth Asylum Pack in the Dreamlands Cycle series of expansions, Search for the Silver Key contains 60 cards in the standard distribution of three sets of 20 unique cards. First released in 2008 and later revived through Fantasy Flights' in-house print-on-demand service, it was discontinued with the demise of Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game in 2015. Contents John Henry Price, Cold and Paternal (Character; The Agency) Price Manor (Support; The Agency) Randolph Carter , King of Ilek-Vad (Character; Miskatonic University ) Arcane Translation (Event; Miskatonic University) King Kuranes , Ruler of Celephais (Character; Syndicate) The Back Room (Support; Syndicate) Cerenerian Deep One (Character; Cthulhu ) Dark Quagmire (Support; Cthulhu) Victoria's Protege (Character; Hastur ) Devolution (Event; Hastur) Guardian of the Key (Character; Yog-Sothoth ) The Silver Key, Unlocking the way... (Support; Yog-Sothoth) Ghoulish Hag (Character; Shub-Niggurath ) The Vale of Pnath (Support; Shub-Niggurath) Halflings of D'haz (Character; Neutral) Curious Zoog (Character ; Neutral) Cryptic Writings (Support; Neutral) Guardian Pillar Restricted (Support; Neutral) The Book of Black Stones (Support; Neutral) Dreamlands Eclipse (Support; Neutral) Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2008</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
