  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Doom_That_Came_to_Innsmouth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Doom That Came to Innsmouth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Behind the mythos is a short story by american brian mcnaughton . It is set in the fictional world of the cthulhu mythos of h.p. Lovecraft .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_McNaughton"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Caroline_Dombrowski">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Caroline Dombrowski</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Pseudonym(s) C. Dombrowski Connection to the Mythos Editor Horror , who often goes by the professional name of C. Dombrowski, is an editor who has worked a number of works of Lovecraftian and Cthulhu Mythos fiction, frequently in collaboration with Scott Gable . These include the Miskatonic University series of anthologies, as well as Tomorrow's Cthulhu , a futuristic look at Lovecraftian horror. Selected Bibliography Editor Tomorrow's Cthulhu (2016, with Scott Gable ) Ride the Star Wind (2017, with Scott Gable) Welcome to Miskatonic University (2019, with Scott Gable) It Came from Miskatonic University (2020, with Scott Gable) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>C. Dombrowski</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Peter_F._Guenther">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Peter F. Guenther</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Years Active 1997 - 2000 is a qualified educator, computer programmer, author, game designer and classroom teacher. He previously wrote Mythos fiction in the form of short stories published online as part of the Nightscapes online Lovecraft 'zine. Selected Bibliography " Tentacles " (1997) External Links at The Internet Speculative Fiction Database</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasYearsActiveR>1997 - 2000</ex:hasYearsActiveR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Long_Island_Weird_-_The_Lost_Interviews">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Long Island Weird - The Lost Interviews</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story offers a series of vignettes on a long-running and secret war between humanity and a race of undersea creatures . It is told through eight recovered interviews conducted by a man who is writing a book on the subject . Most of the characters are not named, and are only referred to by their titles .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Charles_Christian"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>August 2014</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Phorcys">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CharactersIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Phorcys</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Olympians (Greek Gods)</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Jeff parker is a monster of greek mythology . First appeared in 2010 in the 2nd issue of the comic book miniseries, x-men vs agents of atlas .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>X-Men vs Agents of Atlas Vol 1 #2</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Gabriel Hardman</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Jeff Parker</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Egyptica">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dimensions"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalLocations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdfs:label>Egyptica</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAccessibilityL>Magical means</ex:hasAccessibilityL>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Extra-dimensional plane of existence created by mark mclaughlin and michael sheehan, jr. was said to have been created by the outer god nyarlathotep for his followers and their descendants, who dwell there to this day . Access to it is protected by a thick, swirling green fog shot through with bolts of lightning .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>Blood-red mountains</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>Carnivorous forests</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>Obsidian city</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasOriginL>Creation of Nyarlathotep</ex:hasOriginL>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Extra-dimensional plane of existence</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Egypticans</ex:isInhabitedByL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Old_Bugs">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Old Bugs</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The piece was written after lovecraft's friend alfred galpin 's suggestion that he wanted to try alcohol before prohibition went into effect . Lovecraft, a teetotaler, wrote a tale of an old derelict known as , who turns out to be galpin himself .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shuttered_Room"/>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Other Pieces</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Singers_of_Strange_Songs">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Singers of Strange Songs</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist H. E. Fassl English Call of Cthulhu Fiction Chaosium Release Date August 1997 238 is an anthology of short stories celebrating the Lovecraft -inspired works of Brian Lumley , edited by Scott David Aniolowski and published by Chaosium in August 1997. Contents "City Out of Time" (poem) by Brian Lumley "Cement Surroundings" by Brian Lumley "Bad Soil" by Don D'Ammassa "The Temple of Yig" by Donald R. Burleson "Not to Force the Rhymes" by Benjamin Adams "In His Daughter's Darkling Womb" by Tina L. Jens "The Reliable Vacuum Company" by James Robert Smith "The Nullity of Choice" by John Tynes "Where I Go, Mi-Go" by Lois H. Gresh "Subway Accident" by Gregory Nicoll "The High Rollers" by Benjamin Adams &amp; James Robert Smith "A Forty Share in Innsmouth" by C.J. Henderson "Shudder Wyrm" by Stephen Mark Rainey "Spaghetti" by Brian Lumley</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>238</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu Fiction</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 1997</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Footprints_in_the_Snow">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Footprints in the Snow</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>June Violette English Simian Publishing Apotheosis: Stories of Human Survival After the Rise of the Elder Gods date October 2015  " " is a Lovecraftian short story by June Violette. It was published in the Jason Andrew -edited thematic anthology Apotheosis: Stories of Human Survival After the Rise of the Elder Gods by Simian Press in October 2015. Synopsis In a degenerate future world where the night is ruled by alien abominations, an aging trader witnesses the arrival of a strange young girl in her town. The girl explains that she is on her way to the nearby mountains in search of a hidden valley, where rests a being who might make all right with the world again. Although the trader wishes to join the girl on her quest, she is too old to make the trip, and the girl's fate remains unknown.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>June Violette</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Apotheosis:_Stories_of_Human_Survival_After_the_Rise_of_the_Elder_Gods"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Noble_Endeavor">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Noble Endeavor</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Is a short story by american lucy a. snyder . Set in an alternate timeline where clockwork men are replacing human labourers .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lucy_A._Snyder"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>January 2018</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Elder_Things">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Elder Things</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here Ron Shiflet United States English Publication date October 2000 " " is a poem written by American Ron Shiflet . It was inspired by H. P. Lovecraft 's novella At the Mountains of Madness . Synopsis The nine verses offer a potted history of the alien Elder Things which were introduced into the Mythos in At the Mountains of Madness . Publication History "" was first published in Dark Legacy #8 in October 2000, and has also appeared in the E. P. Berglund -edited online zine Nightscapes .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2000</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Courtemanche">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Brian Courtemanche</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Games Designer United States is an American games designer and librarian. Overview A New England native, 's Cthulhu Mythos work to date has centred around gaming supplements for Chaosium 's seminal roleplaying game, Call of Cthulhu . Selected Bibliography Terrors From Beyond (2009, contributor) Arkham Now (2010) Atomic-Age Cthulhu (2013)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games Designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#To_Hold_Ye_White_Husk">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>To Hold Ye White Husk</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Two sailors whose ship has been sunk wash ashore on an island which feels intrinsically wrong . Stumble across what appears to be some kind of dark fane, and the corpse of one of their former crewmates . The two eventually lapse into madness as something evil circles the temple .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#W._H._Pugmire"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2014</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Statement_of_Andrew_Doran">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CrossroadPress"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Statement of Andrew Doran</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>190 is a cthulhu mythos novel by american and editor matthew davenport . It was first published independently through the createspace platform in february 2013 . The protagonist, dr. andrew . Doran, is called upon to recover the necronomicon . Which has been stolen by nazis to be used in their plans for world domination .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Matthew_Davenport"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>190</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Andrew Doran</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>February 2013</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Atlachnaphobia">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Atlachnaphobia</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A man with a lifelong fear of spiders visits a psychiatrist with a very peculiar set of skills . Despite receiving a certain degree of help, he eventually falls prey to the depredations of atlach-nacha .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Ambuehl"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Black Comedy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Cthulhu Cultus #9</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>January 1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Wendy_Adams">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Wendy Adams</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Unnamed street gang</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Urchin</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Wendy is a fictional character who appears in the expanded cthulhu mythos . She was created by fantasy flight games for their second edition release of the arkham horror board game . Has appeared in several of their other games and novels .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Kingsport Horror Deluxe Expansion</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Music_of_the_Stars">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Music of the Stars</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A man named rambeau visits his friend, frank baldwyn, who shares his interests in both occultism and music . Baldwyn claims that he has come across disturbing combinations of tones, which he plays in his piano . When the music is finished, they hear the rustling sound of agitated rats moving inside the wall . Baldwyn believes he might be close to unveiling "the ", a song that would literally drive the listener mad .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_F_Searight">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSFriendsAndAcquaintances"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Richard F Searight</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1975</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sporadic contributor of weird and science fiction tales to the pulp magazines. He corresponded with Lovecraft from 1933 to 1937.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1902</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Sussex_Manuscript">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Sussex Manuscript</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Text created by fred pelton in the 1940s, presented as a transcription of an occult text from the renaissance called cultus maleficarum . Text is dedicated to the fictional frederick i, baron of sussex, and is dated 1598 . Pelton submitted the manuscript to august derleth at arkham house, who planned to publish it .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Fred Pelton</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Pseudo-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Eastertide_1989"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>April 1989</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gurathnaka">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gurathnaka</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Eater of Dreams Shadow of the Night</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as Eater of Dreams Shadow of the Night The Dreamlands First appearance " Parting the Veil " Created by Matthew Grau is a shadowy incorporeal entity dwelling in the Dreamlands .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Parting the Veil</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Matthew Grau</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nyctelios">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OuterGods"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nyctelios</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Elder Gods</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Outer Gods</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Current location Beneath the Mediterranean Sea Outer Gods Elder Gods (formerly) First appearance "Le Regard Dans L'Abime" Created by Patrick Bosquet , was originally an Elder God , but was punished by Nodens for transgressions against his brethren by being imprisoned under the Mediterranean Sea and is now an Outer God . He takes the form of a grotesque, muscular cyclops-like humanoid, whose body is infested with hideous worms. One day he will rise from the abyssal citadel he was ensnared in and seek revenge upon the Elder Gods.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isLocatedAtC>Beneath the Mediterranean Sea</ex:isLocatedAtC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Le Regard Dans L'Abime</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Patrick Bosquet</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Uluath">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Uluath</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Background (aka the destroyer and the dread lord) is a cephalopod god in the fictional universe of marvel by marvel comics . Which has been sleeping in the pacific ocean, in the city below the seas . A cult founded by mctavish attempted to awaken it via an ultrasonic transmitter .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Henri_B%C3%A9raud">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsInspirationsAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Henri Béraud</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>24 October 1958</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Béraud was a popular french author and journalist, as well as a nazi collaborator during the second world war . His novel lazarus (1925) has been cited as an inspiration for h. p. lovecraft 's novella the shadow out of time .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>http://example.org/lovecraft#France Lyon</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>French</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasYearsActiveR>1903-1944</ex:hasYearsActiveR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Possible inspiration</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>21 September 1885</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Cold_Comes_South">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Cold Comes South</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>In lovecraft's shadow is a short story by american matthew baugh . It was first published in the first and only issue of ron shiflet's fanzine in lovecraft's shadow . The story is set in the old west, when a group of buffalo hunters flees from a storm .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Matthew Baugh</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#In_Lovecrafts_Shadow"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 2007</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Summoning_the_Outer_Gods">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Summoning the Outer Gods</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Josh Maybrook English Genre(s) Cthulhu Mythos Rogue Planet Press Lovecraftiana date Unknown " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos poem by American-born Josh Maybrook , first published in Gavin Chappell 's Lovecraftiana horror magazine (date unknown, Rogue Planet Press ). In 2023, it was voted by the fanbase on social media platform Facebook to be included in the anthology The Best of Lovecraftiana (2023, Rogue Planet Press), where it appeared as the opening piece . Synopsis As can be ascertained from the title, the fourteen lines give a bullet point list on the steps to take when summoning the Azathoth and his cohort. References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Josh_Maybrook"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraftiana"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>Unknown</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Darrell_Schweitzer">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonFictionAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Darrell Schweitzer</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Darrell Charles Schweitzer Connection to the Mythos Mythos Contributor Editor Birth Date 27 August 1952 Woodbury, New Jersey, United States United States Fantasy Horror Science Fiction Years Active 1968-present 𝐓 is an American editor, literary critic and author of fantasy, science fiction and horror. He has written many pieces set in the expanded Cthulhu Mythos , and his editorial credits include two lengthy stints working on the revival of the classic pulp magazine Weird Tales , as well as a number of Lovecraftian anthologies. Selected Bibliography Editor Weird Tales (1987-1994, 1998-2007) Cthulhu's Reign (2010) That Is Not Dead (2015) Tales from the Miskatonic University Library (2017, with John Ashmead) Mountains of Madness Revealed (2019) Author The Innsmouth Tabernacle Choir Hymnal (2004) The Arkham Alphabet Book (2006) Awaiting Strange Gods: Weird and Lovecraftian Fictions (2015) " Those of the Air " (with Jason Van Hollander ) (1994) " Envy, the Gardens of Ynath, and the Sin of Cain " (2002) " The Eater of Hours " (2009) " Stragglers from Carrhae " (2014) " The Hutchison Boy " (2016) Nonfiction Discovering H. P. Lovecraft (1976, originally published as Essays Lovecraftian ) (1976) Lovecraft in the Cinema (1975) The Dream Quest of H. P. Lovecraft (1978)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>United States http://example.org/lovecraft#New_Jersey Woodbury</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Darrell Charles Schweitzer</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasYearsActiveR>1968-present</ex:hasYearsActiveR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Mythos Contributor Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>27 August 1952</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Heroes_and_Heroines_of_Britannia">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Heroes and Heroines of Britannia</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Oscar Rios Alberto Guerra Dan Smith English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Golden Goblin Press is a supplementary source book for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game. Overview The investigators Included are: Carnucus, the Archer Regalis, the Bard Eisia, the Druid Brituenda, the Cavalry Officer Vodenos, the Gaesatae Olondus, the Sailor Maireenn, the Barbarian Segi, the Miner Vindiorix, the Publican Taloric, the Criminal Collegium Member Chretien of Cenomanus, the Physician Behind the Mythos Written by Oscar Rios with art provided by Alberto Guerrilla and Dan Smith, was published by licensed third-party content provider Golden Goblin Press .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Oscar_Rios"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sentient_polyhedron">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sentient polyhedron</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sociocultural Characteristics The 4th Dimension (possible) Real World Information First appearance HPL : " The Dreams in the Witch-House " Created by Howard Phillips Lovecraft The Sentient Polyhedrons are a species created by the late American author H. P. Lovecraft for his short story " The Dreams in the Witch-House ". Little was revealed about them, but it is known that Brown Jenkin took the form of a polyhedron kaleidoscope while travelling in the 4th dimension.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dreams_in_the_Witch-House"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#20,000_Leagues_Into_Madness:_Chapter_1">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosComicBookAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>20,000 Leagues Into Madness: Chapter 1</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>27 is the first issue of an abortive comic book series by writer and artist brian del rio . It was published through his independent label mobili comics in august 2021 . Based on an audio podcast, also created by del rio and issued through his mobili comics audio label .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Brian del Rio</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>27</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 2021</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Zhar_and_Lloigor">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Zhar and Lloigor</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Great Old Ones</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Star-Treader (Lloigor)</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Twin Obscenity (Zhar)</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Zhar and lloigor are twins from the cthulhu mythos . They first appeared in the short story " the lair of the star-spawn " by august derleth and mark schorer . Their mountainous ropy bodies lie in vast lightless caverns beneath the "lake of dread" on the plateau of sung .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Demonbane Haiyore! Nyaruko-san</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:isLocatedAtC>Under the Isle of the Stars on the Plateau of Sung</ex:isLocatedAtC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Lair_of_the_Star-Spawn"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#August_Derleth"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_Schorer"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Gift_from_the_Stars">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Gift from the Stars</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sheldon Woodbury English James Ward Kirk Publishing Lovecraft after Dark date November 2015 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos story by Sheldon Woodbury. It was first published in the thematic anthology Lovecraft after Dark by James Ward Kirk Publishing in November 2015. Synopsis Told in the form of a letter from H. P. Lovecraft to Clark Ashton Smith , "" describes how Lovecraft 's mother slowly devolved into insanity, whispering the secrets her broken mind had learned to her son during his formative years. It culminates with Lovecraft as an adult attending his mother's deathbed in an insane asylum , where Lovecraft himself is exposed to the horrific truth of her ravings, and his belief that he is to suffer the same fate as she. History Lovecraft after Dark (2015) Ancient Ones II (2018)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Sheldon Woodbury</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_after_Dark"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Photograph_From_Life">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Photograph From Life</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Detective thomas malone is assigned to investigate the disappearance of the eccentric bostonian artist richard upton pickman . For an unexplained reason, pickman transforms himself into a ghoul and kills thurber, though malone manages to escape and presumably make his way back to the waking world . Behind the mythos malone is possibly the character created by h. p. lovecraft for his 1927 short story " the horror at red hook "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_M._Price"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pickmans_Gallery"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2018</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dygra">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dygra</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Great Old Ones</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Stone-Thing</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as The Stone-Thing Great Old Ones First appearance EXP : " Correlated Contents " Created by James Ambuehl , The Stone-Thing , is a jewel-covered, geode-like horror with tentacles made of minerals. As was only ever made reference to in James Ambuehl 's short story " Correlated Contents ", virtually nothing is known of it. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Correlated_Contents"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Ambuehl"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dho-spawn">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dho-spawn</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Real World Information First appearance " Rigid Air " Created by Marion Anderson &amp; Phil Anderson The is a type of Great Old One created for the Call of Cthulhu role-playing game. Description are the larval stages of the Great Old Ones , which are invisible and dwell in another dimension.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Rigid Air</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Marion Anderson</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Phil Anderson</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Maine">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsIncorporatedFromTheRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>Maine</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The state was the last of the new england states to be admitted to the union, in 1820 . Its population in 1930 was 797,423, and 1,362,359 in 2020, making it the least densely populated state east of the mississippi river . The most notable reference to in lovecraft's fiction is in " the thing on the doorstep "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Ghost-Eater"/>
    <ex:hasTypeL>State</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>United States</ex:isLocatedAtL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Werewolf">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SpeciesIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdfs:label>Werewolf</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lycanthropes are creatures that can turn themselves from human to wolf, or into a wolf/human hybrid . Werewolves are part of the mythology and folklore of several cultures . Many of the werewolves in marvel comics have a mythos-connected origin .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Malleus_Monstrorum"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Only_the_End_of_the_World_Again"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Marvel Mystery Comics #14</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Ghost-Eater"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Evil_Dead_2:_Revenge_of_the_Martians">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Evil Dead 2: Revenge of the Martians</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Frank Hannah Michael Montenat, Vero Gandini English Space Goat Publishing Release Date 2016 Media Type Comic book is a one-shot comic book written by Frank Hannah in 2016 which was published by Space Goat Publishing. Synopsis For over a century, the evidence of the Martian invasion has been kept in storage by governments around the world. The Deadites bring the Martians and their war machines back.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Frank Hannah</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Black_Galley">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#TechnologicalArtefacts"/>
    <rdfs:label>Black Galley</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Black galleys are ominous trading ships that sail the southern sea of the dreamlands . Crew are men of leng , almost human slaves disguised with turbans to cover their two horns . At ports such as dylath-leen they remain below decks for weeks without buying any provisions .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasFunctionA>Transportation</ex:hasFunctionA>
    <ex:hasPlaceOfOriginA>http://example.org/lovecraft#Dreamlands likely on that dimension's moon</ex:hasPlaceOfOriginA>
    <ex:hasTypeA>Vehicle</ex:hasTypeA>
    <ex:isUsedByA>Men of Leng</ex:isUsedByA>
    <ex:isUsedByA>Moon-beasts</ex:isUsedByA>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnA rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dream-Quest_of_Unknown_Kadath"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Raymond_Thulask">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Raymond Thulask</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Powers and Abilities Magic Manipulation Occult Psychiatrist Human First appearance EXP : " Atlachnaphobia " EXP : " One Rainy Afternoon Spent in the Company of Dr. Thulask " Created by James Ambuehl Dr. is a character created by American author James Ambuehl for his Cthulhu Mythos fiction. Overview Little is known of Dr. Thulask other than that he is a psychiatrist who showed up in the town of Braving, Minnesota after treating several big-name stars in Hollywood, California. He does, however, have an extensive knowledge of the creatures of the Cthulhu Mythos, as demonstrated when he dealt with the case of one Jim Campbell and the spider-goddess Atlach-Nacha . Thulask also possesses a large occult library, and appears immune to at least some of the madness-inducing effects of witnessing the Mythos creatures. " Atlachnaphobia " (1998) " One Rainy Afternoon Spent in the Company of Dr. Thulask " (2005)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#One_Rainy_Afternoon_Spent_in_the_Company_of_Dr._Thulask"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Atlachnaphobia"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Ambuehl"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Wish-Demon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Wish-Demon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a fictional demon in the prime reality of the . Marvel universe of comics . It was created in 1993 by . Chris cooper, first appearing in issue #8 of the 1st volume of darkhold: pages from the book of . Sins .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>1993</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Darkhold: Pages from the Book of Sins Vol 1 8</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Chris Cooper</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Byron_Craft">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Byron Craft</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Screenwriter Cthulhu Mythos https://www.byroncraftbooks.com/ is an American author, filmmaker and screenwriter who has written numerous books set in the greater Cthulhu Mythos inspired by the works of H. P. Lovecraft . His writing credits include a screenplay for a never-completed Mythos film project, a four-novel series titled The Mythos Project , and The Arkham Detective , a series of eight novellas. Selected Works Collections The Arkham Detective Collection Volume 1 (2017) The Arkham Detective Collection Volume 2 (2016) The Mythos Project Cry of Cthulhu (formerly The Alchemist's Notebook ) (2014) Shoggoth (2016) Shoggoth 2: Return of the Elders (2018) R'lyeh: The Lost Realm (2021) The Arkham Detective series Cthulhu's Minions (2015) The Innsmouth Look (2016) The Devil Came to Arkham (2017) The Dunwich Dungeon (2017) Death on the Arkham Express (2019) Who Stole the Necronomicon? (2020) Arkham After Dark The Comatose Man Screenplays The Cry of Cthulhu (unrealised project) External Links at the internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://www.byroncraftbooks.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Screenwriter</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_as_a_Poet">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lovecraft as a Poet</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Winfield Townley Scott United States English Genre(s) Non-fiction Grant-Hadley Rhode Island on Lovecraft date 1945 " " is a short non-fiction work by the late American critic and newspaperman Winfield Townley Scott . It was published in the scholarly collection Rhode Island on Lovecraft in 1945.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Winfield_Townley_Scott"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
