  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Perilous_Trials_%28Call_of_Cthulhu_LCG%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Perilous Trials (Call of Cthulhu LCG)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Perilous Trials Fantasy Flight Games Publication Date 2010 Living Card Game Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game The Twilight Beckons is an expansion pack for Fantasy Flight Games ' Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game . Overview The second Asylum Pack in The Rituals of the Order Cycle series of expansions, Perilous Trials is divided into the standard format of three duplicate sets of 20 unique cards for a total of 60, with the cards contained divided between the existing factions. First released in 2010, it was discontinued in 2015 with the demise of Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game . Contents Educated Officer (Character; The Agency) Cover Up (Event; The Agency) Deep One Stowaway (Character; Cthulhu ) Disguised Serpent Man (Character; Cthulhu) Sinister Clerk (Character; Hastur ) Twisted Choreographer (Character; Hastur) Horrifying Daydreams (Event; Hastur) Medical Student (Character; Miskatonic University ) Dean of Admissions (Character; Miskatonic University) Flux Stabilizer (Support; Miskatonic University) Predator of the Night (Character; Shub-Niggurath ) One of the Thousand (Character; Shub-Niggurath) Rich Widow (Character; Silver Twilight) Master Artificer (Character; Silver Twilight) The Silver Lance, Hallowed Weapon (Support; Silver Twilight) Spirit Dagger (Support; Silver Twilight) Thomas Bannano, "Tommy Bananas" (Character; Syndicate) Gang Warfare (Event; Syndicate) Stalking Hound (Character; Yog-Sothoth ) A Gate Opens (Event; Yog-Sothoth)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2010</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crossovers">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Herald:_Lovecraft_and_Tesla">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Herald: Lovecraft and Tesla</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>John C. Reilly Colin Dyer English Action Lab Release Date Dec 2014-ongoing Media Type Comic book is an ongoing comic book series written by John C. Reilly which is published by Action Lab, with 12 issues so far printed as of Oct 2019. Synopsis A dangerous prototype engine is stolen by Amelia Earhart, and Nikola Tesla asks H. P. Lovecraft 's help to save his fiancée. Lovecraft in turn has been conducting an investigation to find out who is Cthulhu 's Herald.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>John C. Reilly</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>Dec 2014-ongoing</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Migou_Mecha">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#TechnologicalArtefacts"/>
    <rdfs:label>Migou Mecha</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Vehicle Weapon system Migou Mechas are war machines used by the Migou alien race in the CthulhuTech roleplaying game by Sandstorm Productions. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasFunctionA>Weapon system</ex:hasFunctionA>
    <ex:hasTypeA>Vehicle</ex:hasTypeA>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nutmeat">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nutmeat</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Martin Hill Ortiz English Genre(s) Weird fiction Whispers from the Abyss date 2013  " " is a short Lovecraftian story by American and university professor Martin Hill Ortiz. Synopsis A parasitologist investigating an alleged infestation in rural California realises too late that whatever is infecting the local walnut crop has already made the jump to more mobile lifeforms… Characters Doctor Hiram Lerner , a plant parasitologist working out of California's Davis University. Baskin , a country bumpkin who has become infected by some kind of intelligent body-snatching organism. History "" was published in the Kat Rocha -edited Lovecraft -inspired anthology Whispers from the Abyss in 2013.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Martin Hill Ortiz</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Whispers_from_the_Abyss"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2013</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#What_Belongs_in_Verse">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>What Belongs in Verse</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft English Genre(s) Non-fiction Donald M. Grant To Quebec and the Stars Date of writing 1935 date 1976 " " is a short non-fiction work by the late American Howard Phillips Lovecraft . Although it was written in 1935, it did not see until its inclusion in August Derleth 's 1976 collection The Dark Brotherhood and Other Pieces . History The Dark Brotherhood and Other Pieces (1966) To Quebec and the Stars (1976) Howard Phillips Lovecraft 1 (1991) Miscellaneous Writings (1995) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>To Quebec</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>the Stars</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1976</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>1935</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Ancient_Track_%28magazine%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Ancient Track (magazine)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Ancient Track Melanie E. Fischer Todd H. C. Fischer English Annual October 1998 Final Publication October 1999 The Ancient Track was a limited-run publication with a focus on the Cthulhu Mythos inspired by American author H. P. Lovecraft . Overview Running to only two issues, which were published in the October of 1998 and 1999, The Ancient Track was edited by Melanie E. Fischer and Todd H. C. Fischer with cover art supplied by Allen Koszowski . Issue Guide 1998 October 1998 1999 #2</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Moths_to_a_Pallid_Flame">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Moths to a Pallid Flame</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>They thrive in darkness: mythos and madness is a short story by ron shiflet and glynn barrass . A horrific murder in rural texas follows a stage adaptation of the play the king in yellow . A sheriff's deputy and a specialist from england are brought together to put a stop to the spread of the incursion .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Glynn Barrass</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Madness</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>They Thrive in Darkness: Mythos</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2018</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Slayer_of_Souls">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Slayer of Souls</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>301 is a fantasy novel by robert w. chambers originally published in 1920 . It mentions the mystical city of yian , which would be incorporated into the cthulhu mythos by h. p. lovecraft in the whisperer in darkness .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_W._Chambers"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>301</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1920</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Bill_Sweetan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Bill Sweetan</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Ace</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>William "bill" sweetan is a recurrent character in the cthulhu mythos fiction of american author ron shiflet . He made his first appearance in the short story " the scales of justice ", published in the 2008 collection looking for darla: stories of mythos noir . During the second world war, sweetan served in the armed forces, although in what capacity is unknown .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Looking_for_Darla"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Scales_of_Justice"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Abominations_of_Nephren-Ka_%26_Three_More_Tales_of_the_Cthulhu_Mythos">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Abominations of Nephren-Ka &amp; Three More Tales of the Cthulhu Mythos</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mark McLaughlin Michael Sheehan, Jr. English Genre(s) Horror Release Date September 2015 60 is a collection of short stories by Mark McLaughlin and Michael Sheehan, Jr. Overview Running to a total of 60 , was released as a digital download on Kindle in September 2015. The titular story, " The Abominations Of Nephren-Ka ", is written as a prequel to H. P. Lovecraft 's " The Haunter of the Dark ". All of the stories were included in the later collection Horrors &amp; Abominations: 24 Tales of the Cthulhu Mythos in December 2018. Contents " That Tantalizing Taste You Will Never Forget " " Deathless Bride Of The Crawling Chaos " " Toadface " " The Abominations Of Nephren-Ka "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Michael Sheehan. Jr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>60</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>September 2015</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Fungi_from_Yuggoth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Fungi from Yuggoth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Basic role-playing is a campaign supplement for chaosium 's call of cthulhu roleplaying game, written by keith herber . First published in 1984, a second edition was published in 1987 . "the dreamer" involves the search for a missing psychic .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1987 1984</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dark_Regions_Press">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DefunctRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SmallPress"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dark Regions Press</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Dark regions was a small press publisher which focused on horror and dark fiction . Management of the company later passed to morey's son chris . On 21 july 2024, scottish author william meikle announced its closure .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://darkregions.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Room_in_the_Castle">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RamseyCampbellWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Room in the Castle</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The inhabitant of the lake and less welcome tenants was first published in 1964 . It has since been reprinted in several volumes of campbell's work, including cold print (1985) and die offenbarungen des glaaki .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ramsey_Campbell"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>German</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Inhabitant_of_the_Lake"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Less Welcome Tenants</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1964</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#United_States_of_America">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsIncorporatedFromTheRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>United States of America</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lovecraft was born into a nation with 44 states and saw it grow to 48 states . Over the same time period, average life expectancy advanced from 45 to nearly 63 years . In his lifetime, the us fought in two major wars, the spanish-american war and world war i .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Country</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>North America</ex:isLocatedAtL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Last_Tweet">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Last Tweet</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Charles Black English Genre(s) Weird fiction Flash fiction Whispers from the Abyss date 2013 " " is an extremely short Cthulhu Mythos flash fiction piece by Charles Black. Synopsis The nineteen words are, as the title might suggest, a partial tweet describing how the tweeter found the Necronomicon in Arkham , conducted an occult ritual, and awakened the Great Old Ones . History "" was published in the Lovecraftian anthology Whispers from the Abyss in 2013.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Charles Black</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Flash fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Whispers_from_the_Abyss"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2013</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label>A place or setting, real or fictional, associated with events or entities from the mythos.</rdfs:label>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brichester">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Brichester</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAccessibilityL>Road</ex:hasAccessibilityL>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>City road created by ramsey campbell is a fictional town invented by him . It is the main community of his severn valley environment . It is the setting of several of his mythos -related short stories .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>City</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#England"/>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>United Kingdom</ex:isLocatedAtL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Zoth_Syra">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Zoth Syra</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Empress of the Abyss</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Queen of the Green Abyss</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as queen of the green abyss or empress of the abyss . Ruler of a species of amorphous creatures that dwell deep within the earth's oceans . Enchants humans with her hypnotic singing and can somehow convert them into scaly gilled humanoids .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Spawn_of_the_Green_Abyss"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#C._Hall_Thompson"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhurotica">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhurotica</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Kirsten Brown Galen Dara Stephen Stanley Cover Artist Oliver Wetter English Dagan Books Release Date January 2011 334 is an anthology of erotic Lovecraftian fiction which includes a smattering of non-fiction works regarding the relationships between sex and Lovecraftiana. Edited by Carrie Cuinn and published by independent press Dagan Books, it runs to a total of 334 , and was released in January 2011; both paperback and digital editions were released. Contents " Astrophobos " by H. P. Lovecraft "Introduction by Carrie Cuinn "Descent of the Wayward Sister" by Gabrielle Harbowy "The C-Word" by Don Pizarro "Infernal Attractors" by Cody Goodfellow "Daddy's Girl" by Madison Woods "Victim of Victims" by Anonymous "The Cry in the Darkness" by Richard Baron "Riemannian Dreams" by Juan Miguel Marin "Turning On, Tuning In, and Dropping Out at the Mountains of Madness" by Ahimsa Kerp "Song of the Catherine Clark" by Maria Mitchell "Between a Rock and an Elder Goddess" by Mae Empson "The Fishwives of Sean Brolly" by Nathan Crowder "Flash Frame" by Silvia Moreno-Garcia "Transfigured Night" by K. V. Taylor "The Lake at Roopkund" by Andrew Scearce "Ipsa Scientia" by Constella Espj "Amid Disquieting Dreams" by Leon J. West "The Dreamlands of Mars" by Travis King "The Assistant from Innsmouth" by Steven J. Scearce "The Summoned" by Clint Collins "Sense" by Matthew Marovich "Optional on the Beach at the Festival of Shug Niggurath" by Gary Mark Bernstein "Le Ciél Ouvert" by Kirsten Brown " Cthulhu's Polymorphous Perversity " by Kenneth Hite "The Sexual Attraction of the Lovecraftian Universe" by Jennifer Brozek ", Female Empowerment, and the New Weird" by Justin Everett External Links Interview with Carrie Cuinn on the development of at Lovecraftian review site Deep Cuts in a Lovecraftian Vein</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>334</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>January 2011</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Elder_God">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ElderGods"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Elder God</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Elder Deity</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The elder gods are fictional extraterrestrial entities featured in the cthulhu mythos . They are originally described as looking like huge pillars of light or fire, which dwell in the vicinity of the star betelgeuse . In the expanded mythos, they originate from another dimension, have mostly human-like appearance and are benevolent in nature .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dawning_of_His_Dreams">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Dawning of His Dreams</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Thana Niveau United States English Genre(s) Horror Dark Regions Press The Children of Gla'aki date 2017 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos story by American Thana Niveau . Synopsis The story describes the coming of the Great Old One Gla'aki to an inhabited world. The peaceful creatures there are shown the meaning of fear, corrupted, and forced to build a great city for their new master and launch it through space to the planet Earth before being discarded and destroyed as Gla'aki finds more innocent prey to feed upon. Characters Gla'aki , a malevolent and eternally-hungry Great Old One. History "" was published in the thematic anthology The Children of Gla'aki by Dark Regions Press in 2017.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Thana Niveau</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Children_of_Glaaki"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Knot">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Knot</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Is a short story by american dennis detwiller first published in the collection of lovecraftian fiction whispers from the abyss vol. 2: the horrors that are &amp; shall be .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dennis_Detwiller"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Shall Be</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Whispers from the Abyss Vol. 2: The Horrors That Are</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Squatters_Rights">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Squatter's Rights</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The children of gla'aki is a short story by american josh reynolds . It was published in the thematic anthology the children of gla'aki in 2017 . It tells the story of an amateur occultist who is attacked by the supernatural servants of the great old one .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Josh_Reynolds"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Children_of_Glaaki"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Whispers_in_the_Dark_%28Call_of_Cthulhu_LCG%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Whispers in the Dark (Call of Cthulhu LCG)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Whispers in the Dark Fantasy Flight Games Publication Date 2010 Living Card Game Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game Whispers in the Dark is an expansion pack for Fantasy Flight Games ' Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game . Overview The first Asylum Pack in The Yuggoth Contract Cycle series of expansions, Whispers in the Dark contains 60 cards in the standard division of three sets of 20 unique cards. First released in 2010, it was discontinued with the demise of Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game . Contents James Logan, Ready for Anything (Character; The Agency) Flanking Maneuver (Event; The Agency) Backwater Deep One (Character; Cthulhu ) Brood of Yig (Character; Cthulhu) Crazed Arsonist (Character; Hastur ) Nightstalker (Character; Hastur) The Sirens of Hell (Event; Hastur) Focused Art Student (Character; Miskatonic University ) Perpetual Silence (Event; Miskatonic University) The Locked Door (Conspiracy; Neutral) The Red-Gloved Man, He Was Never There (Character; Neutral) The Yuggoth Contract, Nefarious Pact (Support; Neutral) Ferocious Dark Young (Character; Shub-Niggurath ) Empty Sockets (Support; Shub-Niggurath) Tyler Scindere, Bounty Hunter (Character; Syndicate) Want Ads (Support; Syndicate) The Laundromat, More Than It Seems (Support; Syndicate) Hunting Nightgaunt (Character; Yog-Sothoth ) Casting off the Skin (Event; Yog-Sothoth) Speak to the Dead (Event; Yog-Sothoth)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2010</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Descent_into_the_Abyss">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ClarkAshtonSmithWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Descent into the Abyss</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>This is a short story by lin carter and clark ashton smith . It was first published in the 1980 anthology weird tales #2 . Functions as a prequel to smith's " the seven geases " .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lin_Carter"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Kensington Publishing Corp.</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Zebra Books</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 1980</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chad_Bowser">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Chad Bowser</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Games designer United States Horror Fantasy http://www.chadbowser.com/index.html Chad J. Bowser is an accomplished American freelance games designer based in Kernersville, North Carolina . Overview has written for a number of roleplaying games and systems, not least of which is Chaosium 's popular Call of Cthulhu system. He has written material for nearly every time period which that game supports, but appears to have a particular affinity for the Cthulhu Invictus setting, and has worked on the game for not only Chaosium, but also third-party content providers Cubicle 7 Entertainment and Modiphius Entertainment . Selected Bibliography Cthulhu Invictus (2009) The Cthulhu Invictus Companion (2011) Red Eye of Azathoth (2011) Cold Harvest (2014) Achtung! Cthulhu: Guide to the Eastern Front (2014) De Horrore Cosmico (2015, contributor) Malleus Monstrorum (unknown edition) External Links at RPGGeek References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://www.chadbowser.com/index.html</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Norbert_Gora">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Norbert Gora</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Poland Polish is a Polish poet and author who has written several pieces around the expanded Cthulhu Mythos . These works have been included in publications such as Swords Against Cthulhu , Swords Against Cthulhu II: Hyperborean Nights , Lovecraft after Dark , and the Lovecraftiana zine. Selected Works Poetry " I Love You, Hastur " (2015) " The Last Moments of Lomar " (2017) " When He Arrived " (2018) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads Interview with at The Poet</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>Poland</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>Polish</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Cultus_Issue_4">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Cultus Issue 4</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu Cultus #4 Various Cover Artist Tani Jantsang United States English P. Marsh Release Date January 1997 62 Preceded by Cthulhu Cultus #3 Followed by Cthulhu Cultus #5 Cthulhu Cultus #4 is an issue of sporadic American fanzine Cthulhu Cultus , edited by Tani Jantsang and James Ambuehl and published by P. Marsh in April 1996. Contents "Sculpture" by James Ambuehl "Aklo Tablets" by Tani Jantsang "Dawn of the New Aeon" by Allen Mackey "The Sand Castle" E. P. Berglund "Consciousness III: Requiem" by E. P. Berglund "The Horror in the Oval Office" by M. J. P. Smith "Once Upon a Time" by Casey Ley "Obed and Obadiah Marsh - And More" by Tani Jantsang</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>62</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>January 1997</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nightscapes_December_1999">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nightscapes December 1999</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The 12th issue of now-defunct online fanzine nightscapes was released in 1999 . Edited by the late american scholar and edward p. berglund . Featured fiction by john w. patterson, poetry by franklyn searight and games by matt sanborn .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>December 1999</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#M._Stern">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>M. Stern</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Weird fiction Fantasy Science fiction https://msternauthor.com/ is an author of weird fiction, fantasy, and science fiction who has written a handful of stories set in the Cthulhu Mythos begun with the works of H. P. Lovecraft . These have appeared in zines such as Lovecraftiana ( Rogue Planet Press ) and anthologies including Necronomi-RomCom: Dark Edition (2024, Transformations by Obsidian Butterfly LLC). Selected Bibliography Short Fiction " After the After Party " (2021) " Something Missing " (2023) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://msternauthor.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_World_of_H._P._Lovecraft">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The World of H. P. Lovecraft</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft August Derleth Cover Artist Gervasio Gallardo English Ballantine Adult Fantasy Genre(s) Horror Fantasy Ballantine Books Release Date 1971 is a boxed set collection of Lovecraft ’s work, and includes the controversial " posthumous collaborations " with August Derleth . Overview Published by Ballantine Books and consisting of four volumes (all part of the Adult Fantasy ) which were also released individually, the collection was issued in a sleeve featuring art by Gervasio Gallardo , who also supplied the covers for the individual volumes. Constituent Volumes The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath The Doom That Came to Sarnath The Survivor and Others Fungi from Yuggoth and Other Poems Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#August_Derleth"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Fantasy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Ballantine Adult Fantasy</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1971</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Doom_that_Came_to_Sarnath_%281971_collection%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Doom that Came to Sarnath (1971 collection)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The doom that came to sarnath and other stories is a collection of fantasy and horror stories by h. p. lovecraft . It was first published in paperback by ballantine books as the twenty-sixth volume of its celebrated ballantine adult fantasy in february 1971 . The stories were written between 1919 and 1935, and originally published in various fantasy magazines, notably weird tales .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Fantasy short stories</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>208 pp</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Ballantine Adult Fantasy</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1971</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
