  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Vision">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Vision</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Kieran Dacey Boylan English Rogue Planet Press Lovecraftiana Vol 5, Issue 2 date July 2020  " " is a Lovecraftian poem by Australian Kieran Dacey Boylan . It was published in Vol 5, Issue 2 of Lovecraftiana by Rogue Planet Press in July 2020. Synopsis The eight-lines describe an undiscovered cave where a strange idol is carved from running water, and an abstract concept displays its unhappiness.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Kieran_Dacey_Boylan"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Issue 2</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Lovecraftiana Vol 5</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>July 2020</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dorothy_C._Walter">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Critics"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonFictionAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dorothy C. Walter</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1967</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Dorothy Charlotte Walker Connection to the Mythos Critic Birth Date 27 April 1889 St. Johnsbury, Vermont, USA Death Date 1967 United States Non-fiction Dorothy Charlotte Walter (27 April 1889 - 1967) was, amongst other things, a teacher, researcher, editor, and archivist. Spending much of her time between Vermont and Providence, Rhode Island , she was an alumni of Brown University ; she subsequently spent some time working the Brown archives, including those regarding Howard Phillips Lovecraft . Walter also wrote a small number of non-fiction pieces on Lovecraft, which have been published in collections such as The Shuttered Room and Other Pieces , Rhode Island on Lovecraft , and Lovecraft Remembered . Selected Bibliography Non-Fiction Lovecraft and Benefit Street (1943) " Lovecraft and Benefit Street " (1943) " Three Hours with H. P. Lovecraft " (1959) External Links at The Internet Speculative Fiction Database at the Vermont Historical Society</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA St. Johnsbury. Vermont</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Dorothy Charlotte Walker</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Critic</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>27 April 1889</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Temple">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Temple</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story is narrated as a "found manuscript" penned by a lieutenant-commander in the german navy . It documents his untimely end at the bottom of the ocean in the days of world war ii . The story ends with a mysterious explosion irreparably damaging the u-boat's engines .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>September 1925</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Uncle_Caesar">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Uncle Caesar</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A young boy notices a drastic improvement in a strange man's health . When the boy grows up and has a family of his own, he must consider whether to ask for the man's help in order to save his own child .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Unclean_One">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cults"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ServitorRaces"/>
    <rdfs:label>Unclean One</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>First appearance " The Descent into the Abyss " Created by Lin Carter Unclean Ones worship Abhoth and Yeb is their leader.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Descent_into_the_Abyss"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lin_Carter"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mr._Cannyharme">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Mr. Cannyharme</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Is a novel by the late american michael shea . It is a loose retelling of h. p. lovecraft 's short story " the hound " .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Michael_Shea"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 2021</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Charles_Garofalo">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonFictionAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scholars"/>
    <rdfs:label>Charles Garofalo</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Charles William Garofalo Connection to the Mythos Author Scholar Birth Date 1954 Weird fiction Charles William Garofalo (born 1954 ) is an author who has written a number of Lovecraftian short stories as well as several scholarly pieces on the . In his works have appeared in anthologies including Eldritch Blue: Love &amp; Sex in the Cthulhu Mythos (Lindisfarne Press, 2004), Black Forbidden Things (Starmont Press, 1992), and The Yog-Sothoth Cycle ( Chaosium , 2022), as well as Robert M. Price 's long-running fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu , for which Garofalo was an avid letter-writer. Selected Works Short Fiction "Writer's Bloch" (1986) "Midnight in Providence" (1987) "The Sending" (1994) "Family Recipe" (2004) Non-fiction " Chariots of the Old Ones? " (1982, with Robert M. Price ) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Charles William Garofalo</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Scholar</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1954</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Starry_Wisdom">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crossovers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Starry Wisdom</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu mythos / thomas carnacki crossover story published in 2021 . Story is a rewrite of meikle's earlier story " into the black " .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#William_Meikle"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Carnacki: Starry Wisdom</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Other Stories</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 2021</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Charles_D_Hornig">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSFriendsAndAcquaintances"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Charles D Hornig</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1999</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Youthful editor of the Fantasy Fan (1933–1935). He was hired by Hugo Gernsback to edit Wonder Stories in 1933.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1916</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lemuria">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsIncorporatedFromTheRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsInspirationsWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lemuria</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A lost continent that supposedly existed in the indian ocean before sinking beneath the waves . Occultists like helena blavatsky asserted that it was the homeland of ancestral human forms . The notion was adopted into the cthulhu mythos by h. p. lovecraft and other writers .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Houses_Under_the_Sea:_Mythos_Tales">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CaitlinRKiernanWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Houses Under the Sea: Mythos Tales</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Caitlin R. Kiernan Cover Artist Marcela Bolivar English Genre(s) Cthulhu Mythos Subterranean Release Date 2019 488  is a collection of the best of Caitlin R. Kiernan 's short Lovecraftian fiction, released by Subterranean Press in 2019, though a limited edition was published by Centipede Press in 2018. "Lovecraft and I", a keynote address they gave at the 15th annual HPL Film Festival in Portland, Oregon, in 2010, serves as an introduction. Many of the stories combine the Cthulhu Mythos with Kiernan's background in paleontology and their interest in deep time. Contents Contents of Houses Under the Sea , with date of first publication: "Lovecraft and I" (2011) " Valentia (1994) " (2000) " So Runs the World Away " (2001) " From Cabinet 34, Drawer 6 " (2005) " The Drowned Geologist (1898) " (2003) " The Dead and the Moonstruck " (2004) " Houses Under the Sea " (2007) " Pickman’s Other Model (1929) " (2008) " The Thousand-and-Third Tale of Scheherazade " (2009) " The Bone’s Prayer " (2009) " The Peril of Liberated Objects, or the Voyeur's Seduction " (2012) " At the Gate of the Deeper Slumber " (2012) " Fish Bride " (2009) " The Alchemist’s Daughter (A Fragment) " (2009) " Houndwife " (2010) " Tidal Forces " (2011) " John Four " (2010) " On the Reef " (2010) " The Transition of Elizabeth Haskings " (2012) " A Mountain Walked " (2012) " Love Is Forbidden, We Croak and Howl " (2012) " Pushing the Sky Away (Death of a Blasphemer) " (2013) " Black Ships Seen South of Heaven " (2015) " Pickman’s Madonna " (2013) " The Peddler’s Tale, or Isobel’s Revenge " (2013) " The Cats of River Street " (2014) " M Is for Mars " (2019) The Dandridge Cycle : " A Redress for Andromeda (2001) " (2000) " Nor the Demons Down Under the Sea (1957) " (2002) " Study for The Witch House (2013) " (2013) " Andromeda Among the Stones " (2003) Publication History and Acknowledgments</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Caitlin_R._Kiernan"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>488 pages</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2019</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Zyhum%C3%A9">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Zyhumé</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Unknown, possibly Hyperborea Offspring Yhoundeh First appearance Letter to Robert H. Barlow , 10 September 1934 Created by Clark Ashton Smith is an androgynous animal archetype who spawned Yhoundeh and is her sole parent. is still located in the archetype cavern in the Hyperborean underworld and was once visited by Ralibar Vooz. Its physical appearance is described as "a sort of nebulous and more or less spheroid elk". ( CIRCLE : Clark Ashton Smith , letter to Robert H. Barlow , 10 September 1934)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>10 September 1934</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Letter to Robert H. Barlow</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Silver_Key">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MysticalArtefacts"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdfs:label>Silver Key</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is an ancient magical artifact in the fiction of h. p. lovecraft that unlocks gates of space and time . It was last in the possession of randolph carter, who used it to go back in time, reverting from his adult self in 1928 to his child self in 1883 . The was created in prehistoric hyperborea, and is attuned to "the personal consciousness-angles of human beings alone"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasFunctionA>Travel through time</ex:hasFunctionA>
    <ex:hasFunctionA>other dimensions</ex:hasFunctionA>
    <ex:hasPlaceOfOriginA>http://example.org/lovecraft#Hyperborea</ex:hasPlaceOfOriginA>
    <ex:hasTypeA>Magical item</ex:hasTypeA>
    <ex:isUsedByA rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Randolph_Carter"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnA rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Silver_Key"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#hasYearsActiveR">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label>hasYearsActiveR</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Unspeakable_Oath">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Periodicals"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Unspeakable Oath</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The unspeakable oath is a magazine dedicated to traditional games based on the cthulhu mythos . The initial run of lasted from 1990 to 2001, with these issues proclaiming the magazine to be dedicated to chaosium's call of cthulhu roleplaying game . After the first issue (#18), it shifted its focus from solely call of cthulhu material to all games based on the mythos</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_White_People">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The White People</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story marks the debut of elements that would later be incorporated into the cthulhu mythos . The story opens up with a conversation between three men, one of which, named ambrose, defends that the "great sinners" must be as far removed from the mundane aspects of man's social sphere as the great saints are .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arthur_Machen"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>The House of Souls</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1906</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Castle_in_the_Window">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Castle in the Window</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A fantasy short story set in the cthulhu mythos by steffan aletti . The narrator visits an old bookstore and buys a diary of an alchemist . The diary tells of how he made a glass window baptized "with the terrible rites of r'lyeh ", and claimed that by looking through the window, he could see the past .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Enter,_the_Dragon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crossovers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Enter, the Dragon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Leverett Butts Dacre Stoker English Various Classic Monsters Unleashed date July 2022 " " is a short story by authors Leverett Butts and Dacre Stoker which blends the gothic horror of iconic movie vampire Dracula with the universe of the Cthulhu Mythos . Synopsis When a mysterious Eastern European organisation reawakens the powerful and ancient vampire Dracula, it falls to the agents of a shadowy U.S. government agency to contain the threat, which they do by exploiting Dracula’s lack of knowledge of modern technology, including his intolerance for crossing running water. Behind the Mythos The unnamed U.S. department has resources scattered across the globe, including assets in Arkham and Miskatonic University ; a character named Armitage is also featured. History "" was published in the thematic anthology Classic Monsters Unleashed by Black Spot Books (paperback), Crystal Lake Publishing (ebook), and Blackstone Publishing (audiobook) in July 2022. The following month, Black Spot also released a hardcover edition.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Dacre Stoker</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Leverett Butts</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Classic Monsters Unleashed</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>July 2022</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Marvel_Super_Heroes">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Marvel Super Heroes</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The game was first released in 1984 and game supplements were last published in 1993 . It incorporates elements of the mythos ( shuma-gorath and chthon ) but it was specific to certain campaign types, otherwise it's rare the characters will encounter the mythos . The game was designed to be easy to understand and use, and the focus was on superhero fights .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Steve Winter</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Science Fantasy role-playing game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Super Heroes</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Futurama">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MediaAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Futurama</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Is an american animated science fiction sitcom created by matt groening of the simpsons fame . It has spawned a series of spin-off comics , numerous direct-to-dvd movies, and endless merchandise .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Comedy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Science Fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gtuhanai">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gtuhanai</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A destructive entity manifesting as a ravenous field of unknown force . It is unknown whether it is sentient; it does not interact with human beings . It is a half-brother of cthulhu and is also related to the slug-like gla'aki .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Forces of Change</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#J._B._Lee"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Scroll_of_Thoth_%28story%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Scroll of Thoth (story)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"the scroll of thoth" is a short story by richard l. tierney . It pits his sword-and-sorcery hero simon of gitta against the most notorious villain of ancient rome--the emperor caligula . The insane emperor acquires the ancient and malevolent scroll of thoth . The story was reprinted as the title story of the simon of gitta collection the scroll of thoth .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_L._Tierney"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Swords Against Darkness II</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>September 1977</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Blandot">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Blandot</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>Early 20th century</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Early 20th century France Landlord Human First appearance HPL : " The Music of Erich Zann " Created by H. P. Lovecraft was a landlord who ran a boarding house on the liminal street known as the Rue d'Auseil in an unnamed French city, from the story " The Music of Erich Zann " by H. P. Lovecraft . The musician Erich Zann and the unnamed narrator of the story both resided at the boarding house before the entire street and its inhabitants mysteriously vanished as though they never existed, including . The narrator himself only barely escaped.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Music_of_Erich_Zann"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Masdevallia">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Masdevallia</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mark Brandon Allen English Bull Spec date December 2010  " " is a Lovecraftian poem by Mark Brandon Allen . It was first published in the December 2010 edition of ezine Bull Spec . Overview Told in seven four-line verses, the poem offers a dream-like vignette of a strange land in which flying ships are brought low by hungry wraiths. History Bull Spec (2010) Lovecraft after Dark (2015)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_Brandon_Allen"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Bull Spec</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 2010</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Old_One">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Old One</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Professor sheldon has an interest in the theory that intelligent races inhabited the earth long before the coming of man . He uncovers references to an ancient city named yuth which may be located somewhere near the bimini islands in the bahamas . Later, his university decides to mount an expedition to the bimini islands to study the work already taking place there . They equip two boats - one carrying a bathysphere and several sets of diving gear - with sheldon, his superior professor dorman and two others comprising</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#John_Glasby"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Short Fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1st December 2002</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Edward_L_Sechrist">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSFriendsAndAcquaintances"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Edward L Sechrist</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1953</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Amateur journalist and beekeeper. Lovecraft met him on several occasions, especially during visits to Washington, DC.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1873</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Albert_Wilmarth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Albert Wilmarth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Albert n. wilmarth is a character created by h. p. lovecraft for his short story the whisperer in darkness . He has since made in the expanded mythos . He is a folklorist and professor of english at miskatonic university in massachusetts .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#At_the_Mountains_of_Madness"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Terror_from_the_Depths"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Whisperer_in_Darkness"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Book_of_Cthulhu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Book of Cthulhu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Obrotowy United States English Night Shade Books Release Date September 2011 is a collection of Cthulhu Mythos fiction edited by Ross E. Lockhart and published by Night Shade Press. The first edition was released in September 2011 in both physical and digital formats, with a second edition making its appearance in May 2022. Contents " Andromeda Among the Stones " by Caitlin R. Kiernan " The Tugging " by Ramsey Campbell " A Colder War " by Charles Stross " The Unthinkable " by Bruce Sterling "Flash Frame" by Silvia Moreno-Garcia "Some Buried Memory" by W. H. Pugmire "The Infernal History of the Ivybridge Twins" by Molly Tanzer " Fat Face " by Michael Shea " Shoggoths in Bloom " by Elizabeth Bear " Black Man with a Horn " by T. E. D. Klein "Than Curse the Darkness" by David Drake "Jeroboam Henley's Debt" by Charles Saunders "Nethescurial" by Thomas Ligotti "Calamari Curls" by Kage Baker "Jihad over Innsmouth" by Edward Morris "Bad Sushi" by Cherie Priest "The Dream of the Fisherman's Wife" by John Hornor Jacobs " The Doom That Came to Innsmouth " by Brian McNaughton "Lost Stars" by Ann K. Schwader "The Oram County Whoosit" by Steve Duffy "The Crawling Sky" by Joe R. Lansdale " The Fairground Horror " by Brian Lumley "Cinderlands" by Tim Pratt "Lord of the Land" by Gene Wolfe "To Live and Die in Arkham" by Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. "The Shallows" by John Langan "The Men from Porlock" by Laird Barron</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>September 2011</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nicholas_Roerich">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AlterEgosCharactersIncorporatedFromTheRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nicholas Roerich</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Икола ери (1874 - 1947) was a russian painter, archaeologist, stage scenographer, writer, and spiritual philosopher . He is known for his landscape paintings, his interest in mysticism, hypnotism and buddhism, his extensive travels through asia . After the revolution of 1917, he and his family left russia and spent time in several countries, including finland, england, the usa, and finally india .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cult_of_the_Third_Moon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cults"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Organisation"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cult of the Third Moon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Varcolac"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The was founded sometime before the birth of christ. It's a cult with a mysterious origin and history . The murderous cult performed human sacrifices to varcolac, as recently as the late 20th century .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeO>cult</ex:hasTypeO>
    <ex:wasCreatedBynO>Paul Jenkins</ex:wasCreatedBynO>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Thing_About_Cars%21">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#BrianLumleyWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Thing About Cars!</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"a thing about cars!" Is a cthulhu mythos -connected short story by brian lumley . It first appeared in his collection the caller of the black in 1971 . The story is set in a midlands village noted for its "tall, gnarled oaks"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Caller_of_The_Black"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1971</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Trail_of_Cthulhu_%28series%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AugustDerlethWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Trail of Cthulhu (series)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Trail of Cthulhu August Derleth United States English The Trail of Cthulhu Genre(s) Horror Publication date 1944 - 1952 𝐓 The Trail of Cthulhu is a of five linked short stories by American and Lovecraft contemporary August Derleth . Synopsis The stories chart the actions of Professor Laban Shrewsbury of Miskatonic University and his friends in their battles against the forces of the Great Old Ones . Stories " The House on Curwen Street " (1944) " The Watcher from the Sky " (1945) " The Gorge Beyond Salapunco " (1949) " The Keeper of the Key " (1951) " The Black Island " (1952) Publication History The individual stories were first printed in pulp magazine Weird Tales , and were later collected in the volume The Trail of Cthulhu , by Arkham House in 1962. It has since been reprinted several times, and has been translated into a number of other languages, including German, French and Italian.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#August_Derleth"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Trail_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1944 - 1952</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ptar-Axtlan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ptar-Axtlan</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Leopard That Stalks the Night</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as The Leopard That Stalks the Night First appearance EXP : " Confessions in Darkness " Created by Gerald W. Page is a mysterious entity that is related to zoomorphic shapeshifers, mostly werecats.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Confessions in Darkness</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gerald_W._Page"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Comte_Henri_de_C--">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Comte Henri de C--</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Comte henri de c-- (no surname given) was a thirteenth-century french count in the story " the alchemist " by h. p. lovecraft . He was the father of godfrey and the ancestor of comte antoine .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Alchemist"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scott_Gable">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Scott Gable</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Editor Publisher United States Speculative fiction is a Seattle, Washington-based American editor and publisher who has worked on a number of Lovecraftian and Cthulhu Mythos publications, frequently in collaboration with Caroline Dombrowski . These include the Miskatonic University series of anthologies, as well as Tomorrow's Cthulhu , a futuristic look at Lovecraftian horror. Gable is also the founder of Broken Eye Books , a small press publisher specialising in "the odd, strange, and offbeat side of speculative fiction " Selected Bibliography Editor Tomorrow's Cthulhu (2016, with Caroline Dombrowski ) Ride the Star Wind (2017, with Caroline Dombrowski) Welcome to Miskatonic University (2019, with Caroline Dombrowski) It Came from Miskatonic University (2020, with Caroline Dombrowski) See Also Broken Eye Books External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Publisher</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_McNaughton">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Brian McNaughton</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>13th May 2004</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Brian john mcnaughton was an american author of both novels and short fiction . His writing is characterized by black humor, considerable gore, and frank sexuality . He wrote a series of sexually explicit "romance" novels under the pen name .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>http://example.org/lovecraft#New_Jersey Red Bank</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Brian John McNaughton</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Contributor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>23rd September 1935</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Web_of_Easter_Island">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Web of Easter Island</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Is a sci-fi horror novel by . Donald wandrei, first published in 1948 by arkham house . It concerns a gigantic race of extradimensional "titans" that will descend to the earth when the stars are in the right configuration . The novel is an homage to the . Cthulhu mythos and is dedicated to lovecraft's friend and . Mythos creator h.p. Lovecraft .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Donald_Wandrei"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1948</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Swords_v._Cthulhu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Swords v. Cthulhu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Jason Morningstar English Stone Skin Press ( Pelgrane Press imprint) Release Date July 2016 368 is an anthology of Lovecraftian fiction edited by Jesse Bullington and Molly Tanzer . It was published by Pelgrane Press through their Stone Skin Press imprint in July 2016; both paperback and digital editions were released. Overview Contents Introduction by Jesse Bullington and Molly Tanzer "The Savage Angela in: The Beast in the Tunnels" by John Langan "Non Omnis Moriar" by Michael Cisco "The Lady of Shalott" by Carrie Vaughn "Trespassers" by A. Scott Glancy "The Dan no Uchi Horror" by Remy Nakamura "St. Baboloki's Hymn for Lost Girls" by L. Lark "The Children of Yig" by John Hornor Jacobs "The Dreamers of Alamoi" by Jeremiah Tolbert "Two Suns over Zululand" by Ben Stewart "A Circle That Ever Returneth In" by Orrin Grey "Ordo Virtutum" by Wendy N. Wagner "Red Sails, Dark Moon" by Andrew S. Fuller "The Thief in the Sand" by M. K. Sauer "Without Within" by Jonathan L. Howard "Daughter of the Drifting" by Jason Heller "A Matter of Aude" by Natania Barron "The Living, Vengeant Stars" by E. Catherine Tobler "The Argonaut" by Carlos Orsi "Of All Possible Worlds" by Eneasz Brodski "The Final Gift of Zhuge Liang" by Laurie Tom "The King of Lapland's Daughter" by Nathan Carson "Bow Down Before the Snail King!" by Caleb Wilson</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>368</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dockside_Dogs">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dockside Dogs</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Paul Fricker John Sumrow English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Release Date 2012 28 (first edition) 40 (second edition) is a short scenario supplement for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game, written by Paul Fricker with artwork supplied by John Sumrow. First published in 2012, a second revised edition was later published through DriveThruRPG as a part of their Miskatonic Repository initiative; both print and digital editions were released. Overview Trivia takes obvious inspiration from Quentin Tarantino's seminal 1992 indie crime movie Reservoir Dogs with many elements clearly incorporated from the film, from the title, to the warehouse setting, to the characters given anonymity by being named for colours. Gallery References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Paul_Fricker"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>40 28</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2012</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecrafts_Worlds:_Volume_2">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleComicBookAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>H. P. Lovecraft's Worlds: Volume 2</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: Volume 2 H. P. Lovecraft (original stories) Steven Philip Jones (adaptations) Various United States English The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft Genre(s) Weird fiction Caliber Comics 166 Preceded by H. P. Lovecraft's Worlds: Volume 1 is a collection of comic book adaptations of the works of H. P. Lovecraft . Overview Running to a total of 166 in black and white with a full colour cover, The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: Volume 2 was printed by indie comic book Caliber Comics . It contains all five stories from the first run of The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft . Adapted Stories " Dagon " " Facts Concerning the Late Arthur Jermyn and His Family " " The Picture in the House " " The Statement of Randolph Carter " " The Music of Erich Zann " Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Steven_Philip_Jones"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>166</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Space_Eldritch">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Space Eldritch</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Carter Reid English Cold Fusion Media Empire Release Date December 2012 246 Followed by II: The Haunted Stars is an anthology of Lovecraftian fiction edited by Nathan Shumate and published by Cold Fusion Media Empire in December 2012. Cover art was supplied by Carter Reid. Overview Contents Foreword by Larry Correia "Arise Thou Niarlat from Thy Rest" by D. J. Butler "Space Opera" by Michael R. Collings "The Menace Under Mars" by Nathan Shumate "Gods in Darkness" by David J. West "The Shadows of Titan" by Carter Reid and Brad R. Torgersen "The Fury in the Void" by Robert J Defendi "Flight of the Runewright" by Howard Tayler Contributors</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>246</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>December 2012</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Feast_of_Eihort">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Feast of Eihort</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here James Gruetzmacher English Nightscapes #6 date January 1998 " " is a Cthulhu Mythos poem by James Gruetzmacher . Synopsis The five four-line verses read as a devotional to the Great Old One Eihort created by Ramsey Campbell for his 1973 short story " The Franklyn Paragraphs ". History Nightscapes #6 (1998) Cthulhu Cultus #12 (1998, as "The ")</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Gruetzmacher"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Nightscapes #6</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>January 1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Eliza_Ann_Randall">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSRelatives"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Eliza Ann Randall</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>28 January 1899</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Wife of William Lovecraft, whom she married in February 1825. She gave birth to two children, Arthur Joseph and Laura Maria.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isRelativeToR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1836</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Perchance_to_Dream">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Perchance to Dream</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Anton zarnak is an occult investigator who investigates nightmares . His character, parker winfield, is a wealthy man who inherited artifacts from his grandfather . The story was first published in 1988 in the magazine crypt of cthulhu .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lin_Carter"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Anton_Zarnak"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>May 1988</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sticks">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sticks</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story received first in the march 1974 issue of whispers . It was reprinted in tales of the cthulhu mythos , revised edition (arkham house , 1990)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Karl_Edward_Wagner"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Whispers</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 1974</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lesser_Old_One">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lesser Old One</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Greater Servitors ( Call of Cthulhu )</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Lesser Gods ( Demonbane )</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lesser old ones is a designation coined by lin carter to refer to a category of beings who serve the great old ones and lead their servitor races . The term appears in several other works, but is not always used .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Watcher_from_the_Sky"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#August_Derleth"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lin_Carter"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Kthulhu_Reich">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Kthulhu Reich</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ken Asamatsu Japanese (translated into English by Jim Rion) Kurodahan Press Release Date January 1, 2019 is an anthology of Lovecraftian fiction written by Ken Asamatsu and published by Kurodahan Press in January 2019. Overview Contents "The Corporal's Self-Portrait" "The Mask of Yoth-Tlaggon" "In the Wasteland of Madness" "April 20th, 1889" "A Feast for the Children of the Night" "Gigantomachia 1945" "Dies Irae"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ken_Asamatsu"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Japanese (translated into English by Jim Rion)</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2019 January 1</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gnorri">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gnorri</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sociocultural Characteristics The Dreamlands Real World Information First appearance HPL : " The Silver Key " HPL : " Through the Gates Of the Silver Key " Created by H. P. Lovecraft The (singular: Gnorr ) are a species created by H. P. Lovecraft for his short story " The Silver Key ". Description The are an aquatic race of finned and bearded creatures who live in the Dreamlands . Its members have 2-4 arms and a torsos that ends in a 15-20 ft long tentacle-like tail. The race is known for building labyrinths in the seas. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Through the Gates Of the Silver Key</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Silver_Key"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dweller_in_the_Pot">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pastiches"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Dweller in the Pot</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>" ; or, the pasta out of space eaters " is a short story by american robert m. price . First published in crypt of cthulhu #74 in august 1990 . Later included in the collection of price's own works blasphemies &amp; revelations .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_M._Price"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Humour</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Crypt of Cthulhu #74</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>August 1990</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Demon_Spawn">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Demon Spawn</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The dark one is a fictional species created by craig digregorio in 2018 for the starz series ash vs. evil dead . When summoned they need to inhabit human host bodies . Their bodies are extremely flexible which gives them remarkable maneuverability .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Episode 10)</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Season 1</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Dark One ( Ash vs. Evil Dead</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Craig Digregorio</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Born_Again%3F">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Born Again?</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>" born again? " Is a story in issue #90 of the long-running superhero comic doctor strange, sorcerer supreme . Written by j. m. dematteis, it was published in may 1996 . The human/demon hybrid is about to give birth to the elder god chthon .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>J.M. DeMatteis</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>June 1996</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_Upton_Pickman">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Richard Upton Pickman</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Ghouls</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A fictional character created by h. p. lovecraft for his short story " pickman's model " pickman was a renowned boston painter notorious for his graphic works . His work depicts the ghouls as having the habit of abducting human children and replacing them with "changelings"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath History of the Necronomicon The Misplaced Masterpiece of Richard Upton Pickman</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pickmans_Model"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_on_the_Beach">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Horror on the Beach</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu was in california when a family moves into an old house . The house triggers a series of manifestations by the entity cthulhu . The story was first published as a chapbook in 1978 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Alan_Dean_Foster"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>July 1978</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Camorin">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalLocations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>Camorin</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Dreamlands Country First appearance HPL : " The White Ship " Created by H. P. Lovecraft is a region in the Dreamlands briefly mentioned in " The White Ship " by H. P. Lovecraft . When Basil Elton spoke of the wonders of the fabled land of Cathuria , he compared its forests of aloe and sandalwood to the "fragrant groves" of .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Country</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>The Dreamlands</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_White_Ship"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Kara_Centell-Dunk">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Kara Centell-Dunk</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Games designer https://www.karacentelldunk.com/ is a games developer who designs for Fantasy Flight Games . In addition to numerous expansions for the award-winning dungeon crawler Descent , she has worked on several releases for the Mansions of Madness Cthulhu Mythos board game. Selected Works Expansion Sets Recurring Nightmares (2016) Suppressed Memories (2016) Beyond the Threshold (2017) Streets of Arkham (2017) Horrific Journeys (2018) Sanctum of Twilight (2018) Path of the Serpent (2019) External Links designer profile at BoardGameGeek</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://www.karacentelldunk.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Bride_of_Re-Animator">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleFilmAndTelevisionAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Bride of Re-Animator</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Second in brian yuzna's 're-animator' series . Loosely based on lovecraft short story " herbert west--reanimator " . Starred jeffrey combs , bruce abbot and david gale . Ran to a total of 96 minutes, and was filmed on a of $2.5 million .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Herbert_West--Reanimator"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>8 September 1990</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Erotinomicon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Erotinomicon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Alex S. Johnson English James Ward Kirk Publishing Lovecraft after Dark date November 2015  " " is a Lovecraftian poem by Alex S. Johnson . It was published in the James Ward Kirk Publishing thematic anthology Lovecraft after Dark in November 2015. Synopsis A woman prepares herself to be the mate of inscrutable beings from beyond. After their carnal visitation, she becomes the birth-womb for their agents on this world.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Alex_S._Johnson"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_after_Dark"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Bgnu-Thun">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>B'gnu-Thun</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Soul-Chilling Ice-God</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as The Soul-Chilling Ice-God Unknown Relatives Ruhtra Dyoll (sibling) First appearance " Correlated Contents " Created by James Ambuehl ( The Soul-Chilling Ice-God ) is the twin brother of Ruhtra Dyoll . He appears as humanoid figure made of ice followed by a eerie blizzard. Notes As was only briefly mentioned in the James Ambuehl short story " Correlated Contents ", almost nothing is known of it.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Correlated_Contents"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Ambuehl"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft:_Four_Decades_of_Criticism">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>H. P. Lovecraft: Four Decades of Criticism</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various United States English Genre(s) Non-fiction Ohio University Press Release Date 1980 247 is a collection of non-fiction pieces focused on the works of late American Howard Phillips Lovecraft . Overview Edited by leading Lovecraft scholar S. T. Joshi , who also contributed some of the essays, was published by Ohio University Press in 1980 (hardcover) and 1981 (paperback). It runs to a total of 247 . Contents Preface by S. T. Joshi " H. P. Lovecraft: His Life and Work " by Kenneth W. Faig, Jr. and S. T. Joshi " Lovecraft Criticism: A Study " by S. T. Joshi " A Chronology of Selected Works by H. P. Lovecraft " by S. T. Joshi " H. P. Lovecraft: An Appreciation " by T. O. Mabbott " Tales of the Marvellous and Ridiculous " by Edmund Wilson " A Literary Copernicus " by Fritz Leiber " From The Supernatural in Fiction " (excerpt) by Peter Penzoldt " The Cthulhu Mythos: A Study " by George T. Wetzel " Some Notes on Cthulhuian Pseudobiblia " by Edward Lauterbach " H. P. Lovecraft: Myth-Maker " by Dirk W. Mosig " On the Literary Influences Which Shaped Lovecraft's Works " by J. Vernon Shea " The Influence of Vathek on H. P. Lovecraft's The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath " by Peter Cannon " Poe and Lovecraft " by Robert Bloch " H.P. Lovecraft in Hawthornian Perspective " by Peter Cannon " Through Hyperspace with Brown Jenkin: Lovecraft's Contribution to Speculative Fiction " by Fritz Leiber " Facts in the Case of H. P. Lovecraft " by Barton L. St. Armand " "The White Ship": A Psychological Odyssey " by Dirk W. Mosig " Lovecraft and the Cosmic Quality in Fiction " by Richard L. Tierney " Dystopia as Utopia: Howard Phillips Lovecraft and the Unknown Content of American Horror Literature " by Paul Buhle " A Parenthesis on Lovecraft as Poet " by Winfield Townley Scott " A Lovecraftian Nightmare " by R. Boerem " The Continuity of the Fungi from Yuggoth " by R. Boerem " To Howard Phillips Lovecraft " by Clark Ashton Smith Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>247</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1980</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Wade_Jermyn">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Wade Jermyn</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>1768</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>White apes</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sir philip jermyn is the ancestor of the jermyn family who mated with a white ape princess . Created by h.p. Lovecraft sir is the ancestor of the jermyn family who mated with a white ape princess . Created by h.p. Lovecraft sir is the ancestor of the jermyn family who mated with a white ape princess .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCharacterMateC>Lady Jermyn</ex:hasCharacterMateC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Facts Concerning the Late Arthur Jermyn</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>His Family</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Four_Tales_of_Cthulhu:_H._P._Lovecraft_for_Young_Readers">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Four Tales of Cthulhu: H. P. Lovecraft for Young Readers</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft Matthew MacDonald Matthew MacDonald English Genre(s) Children's ProseTech Release Date October 10, 2018 111 is a collection of Cthulhu Mythos short stories, published by ProseTech. The first three stories are abridged adaptations of H. P. Lovecraft 's works, adapted by Matthew MacDonald and geared towards younger readers. The fourth story is an original work, written by MacDonald for this anthology. Contents " The Outsider " " The Cats of Ulthar " The Shadow Out of Time " A Gift From the Deep "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Matthew MacDonald</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Children's</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>111</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2018 October 10</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Entropy-reversing_machine">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#TechnologicalArtefacts"/>
    <rdfs:label>Entropy-reversing machine</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesA>The time-space machine</ex:hasAlternativeNamesA>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a fictional device featured in the cthulhu mythos novel the horror from the hills . It was invented by roger little, who also refers to it as "the time-space machine" the device emits a blinding greenish beam that reverses entropy . Even the briefest exposure causes organized matter to dissipate .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeA>Technological artefact</ex:hasTypeA>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnA rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_from_the_Hills"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByA rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Roger_Little"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Servitor_race">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Servitor race</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Servitor races are species that collectively serve another entity or entities . Examples of servitor races can be found in the works of the earliest cthulhu mythos contributors, such as h. p. lovecraft . In the role-playing game call of cthulhu, most species are classified as either servitor races, independent races or fabulous creatures .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Zokkar">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Zokkar</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Olden King</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The olden king was a king of sarnath who is remembered for the gardens of exquisite beauty that he made for the city during his reign . A description of the gardens is provided in " the doom that came to sarnath " by h. p. lovecraft .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Doom_That_Came_to_Sarnath"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Lovecraft_Movie/Television_Show_to_Be_Proud_of">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Lovecraft Movie/Television Show to Be Proud of</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here Patrick Crocker English Genre(s) Non-fiction Edward P. Berglund Nightscapes March 1999 date March 1999 " " is a short non-fiction piece by Patrick Crocker. It was published in the Edward P. Berglund -edited online fanzine Nightscapes in March 1999 . Overview The work describes the 's view of a perfect film adaptation of one of H. P. Lovecraft 's stories, complete with ideas on cast and director, and uses " The Call of Cthulhu " as a case study. It then moves on to question why Lovecraftian elements are not more common in television shows, with a particular emphasis on why The X-Files , one of the most-watched shows in the world at the time of , is a prime example of how easy it would be to integrate the Cthulhu Mythos into popular television.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Patrick Crocker</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nightscapes_March_1999"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 1999</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._E._Fassl">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>H. E. Fassl</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>12 October 2008</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Harry E. Fassl Connection to the Mythos Artist Birth Date 26 June 1952 Georgia, USA Death Date 12 October 2008 United States Harry E. Fassl (26 June 1952-12 October 2008) was an American artist who provided both interior and cover illustrations for a wide variety of publications, including several Cthulhu Mythos books and magazines. Selected Works Cover Art Made in Goatswood (1995) The Cthulhu Cycle: Thirteen Tentacles of Terror (1996) The Dunwich Cycle: Where the Old Gods Wait (1995) The Ithaqua Cycle (1998) Nameless Cults: The Cthulhu Mythos Fiction of Robert E. Howard (2001) See Also Images by External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Georgia</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Harry E. Fassl</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>26 June 1952</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tekeli-li,_They_Cry">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tekeli-li, They Cry</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Tomorrow's cthulhu is a short story by canadian author a.c. wise . It was first published in the broken eye books anthology tomorrow's cthulhu in january 2016 . Despite containing no direct references to either lovecraft or the greater cthulhu mythos, the title is a reference to the vocalisation made by the shoggoth in at the mountains of madness .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#A._C._Wise"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tomorrows_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>January 2016</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Last_Feast_of_Harlequin">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Last Feast of Harlequin</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A university professor with a lifelong fascination with clowns, their history, and their social evolution heads to a small town after hearing about a festival centred around clown culture . He infiltrates the group, only to find a cult dedicated to a subterranean creature called "the worm" the cult leader's final words remain with him forever after: that he is one of them, and has always been one of them .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Thomas_Ligotti"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Science Fiction</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The Magazine of Fantasy</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>April 1990</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Atomic-Age_Cthulhu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Atomic-Age Cthulhu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mythos horror in the 1950s is sourcebook by chaosium for their call of cthulhu roleplaying game . Offers background and tips for playing in a post-world war iii civilisation . Also contains new investigator skills and professions suitable for the times .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Courtemanche"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_M._Sammons"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Christopher_Smith_Adair"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Michael_Dziesinski"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Oscar_Rios"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tom_Lynch"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Matt Sanborn</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>230</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>February 2013</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Rough_Night_in_Innsmouth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Rough Night in Innsmouth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Story is about a seaman fleecing a little money from the locals by betting on himself in a local boxing competition . When he goes down, the referee attempts to stop the fight, only for the mangler to push him out of the way again . Luckily for the sailor, his friends come to his rescue, but this only serves to enrage the mangler: the barman blows a small bone whistle, summoning a fish-like humanoid</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nightscapes_December_1999"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 1999</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Terror_of_Octobernomicon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Terror of Octobernomicon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Golden Goblin Press Basic Role-Playing is a scenario supplement book for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game. Edited by Mike Wilson and Lisa Padol , it was published by licensed third-party content provider Golden Goblin Press ; both print and digital editions were released. Overview Scenarios "Currency of Grief" by Daniel Purcell "Suspended Pending Resolution" by Francesca McMahon "Accounts of Violence" by Marek Golonka "The Hounds of Sekhmet" by Helen Yau "Sun and Shadow" by Walter Attridge "He’s a Lowdown, Dirty Owlhoot" by William Adcock "The Expedition" by Morgan Llewellyn Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Henry_Kuttners_Cthulhu_Mythos_Tales:_An_Overview">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Henry Kuttner's Cthulhu Mythos Tales: An Overview</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Shawn Ramsey English Genre(s) Non-fiction Cryptic Publications Crypt of Cthulhu Hallowmas 1987 date November 1987 " " is a short non-fiction piece by , poet, and editor Shawn Ramsey . It appeared in the Hallowmas 1987 edition of Robert M. Price 's long-running Lovecraftian fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shawn_Ramsey"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Hallowmas_1987"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 1987</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ire_of_the_Void">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ArkhamHorrorFiction"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ire of the Void</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Arkham horror files 96 (plus 16-page insert) is a novella written by richard lee by fantasy flight games . The story revolves around one of the setting's recurrent characters, norman withers . Renowned physicist professor . Claus schmidt visited arkham in 1926 as part of his research . He was befriended by university lecturer professor norman withers .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_Lee_Byers"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror Mystery Action Adventure</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>96 (plus 16-page insert)</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Horror_Fiction"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>February 2018</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ex_Oblivione">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ex Oblivione</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The nameless city is a prose poem by american horror fiction writer h.p. Lovecraft . It is written in first person and tells of the dreams of a presumably dying man . It was first published in the united amateur in march 1921, under the pseudonym .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Howard Phillips Lovecraft as Ward Phillips</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Fantasy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror prose poem</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lunch_Trade">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosFilmAndTelevision"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lunch Trade</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Release date 2022 Running time 30 seconds Canada English "" is a 2022 television commercial for Black Diamond's Cheestrings. Synopsis During her lunch break, an office worker is offered various trades for her Cheestrings. Amongst the things her co-workers had offered include a bagel, an ant farm, pirate's treasure, a cloning machine, a mystical orb and Cthulhu the World Eater. She ends up sticking with the Cheestrings. External Links " Cheestrings - " at YouTube</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>Canada</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2022</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Antediluvian_Dreams_%28Call_of_Cthulhu_LCG%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Antediluvian Dreams (Call of Cthulhu LCG)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Antediluvian Dreams Fantasy Flight Games Publication Date 2008 Living Card Game Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game The Antediluvian Dreams is an expansion pack for Fantasy Flight Games ' Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game . Overview The third Asylum Pack in the Summons of the Deep Cycle , The Antediluvian Dreams contains 20 unique cards in sets of three for a total of 60, and focuses on the Combat Struggle sub-phase of the Story Phase . First issued in 2008, it was discontinued with the demise of Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game . Contents Repo Man (Character; The Agency) Dynamite (Support; The Agency) Howard Ludvinski, Visiting Artist (Character; Miskatonic University ) Academic Obfuscation (Support; Miskatonic University) Brazen Hoodlum (Character; Syndicate) Blackball Jim, Pool Hustler (Character; Syndicate) Vengeful Mob (Event; Syndicate) Nocturnal Scavenger (Character; Cthulhu ) Muddy Waters (Support; Cthulhu) Long-Dead Prince, Ancient Royalty (Character; Hastur ) Infernal Obsession (Support; Hastur) The Sleepwalker, Trapped by Nightmare (Character; Yog-Sothoth ) Across Dimensions (Event; Yog-Sothoth) Gibbering Soul (Character; Shub-Niggurath ) Ghoulish Thrill Seeker (Character; Shub-Niggurath) Eat the Dead (Support; Shub-Niggurath) The Cornered Man, "Like an Animal" (Character; Neutral) Secret Stiletto (Support; Neutral) Antediluvian Dreams (Event; Neutral) Ask Questions Later (Conspiracy; Neutral)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2008</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Cellar_Gods">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Cellar Gods</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Short story is loosely connected to the cthulhu mythos . Narrator develops a crush on a young woman who belongs to a group of labourers at a secretive warehouse, apparently all of asian or eskimo descent .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jeffrey_Thomas"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>New Mythos Legends</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>September 1999</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Correlated_Contents_%28anthology%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Correlated Contents (anthology)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Correlated Contents James Ambuehl United States English Genre(s) Horror Mythos Books Release Date December 1998 Media Type Print 97 Correlated Contents: Six Tales of the Cthulhu Mythos is an anthology of Lovecraftian works by American James Ambuehl . Overview Containing six Cthulhu Mythos shorts by Ambuehl and a fairly long introduction by Robert M. Price , Correlated Contents was first published in December 1998 by Mythos Books and runs to 97 . Although supposedly a collection of fan fiction and the negative connotations which that term usually brings to a work, its publication would seem to afford Correlated Contents the same degree of legitimacy in the Mythos as any other piece. Contents Introduction by Robert M. Price "Sculpture" "The Snake Farm" " The Stalker in the Snows " " The Terror of Toad Lake " "The Deep-Lord Awakens" " Correlated Contents "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Ambuehl"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>97</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>December 1998</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tommy_Muldoon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tommy Muldoon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Boston Police Department Arkham Sheriff's Department</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Rookie Cop</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Tommy is a character created by fantasy flight games for inclusion in their arkham horror files range of cthulhu mythos -related products . He was originally created as a playable investigator for their adventure board game arkham horror . Since then, he has made appearances in other related products including eldritch horror , mansions of madness and arkham horror: the card game .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Innsmouth Horror Deluxe Expansion</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
  </rdf:Description>
