  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Francis_Flagg">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsCorrespondents"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Francis Flagg</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>17 May 1946</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Birth Date 4 July 1898 Death Date 17 May 1946 Canada 𝐓 Henry George Weiss (1898 - 1946), better known by his pen name , was a Canadian-American author of speculative fiction, and a correspondent of H. P. Lovecraft . His poem "To Howard Phillips Lovecraft", written in honour of his late friend, was published in the March 1938 issue of Weird Tales . He also co-authored a handful of stories with Forrest J Ackerman . External links George Henry Weiss at Wikipedia at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>Canada</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>4 July 1898</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft_%28Starmont_Readers_Guide%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>H. P. Lovecraft (Starmont Reader's Guide)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft S. T. Joshi United States English Genre(s) Non-fiction Starmont House Release Date 1982 1984 1987 83 H. P. Lovecraft is a non-fiction study of the works of Lovecraft by prominent scholar and critic S. T. Joshi . Overview Running to a total of 83 , H. P. Lovecraft was first published by Starmont House in 1982 as part of their Starmont Reader's Guide series. It was reissued twice, in 1984 and 1987. Contents Chronology Introduction - Life and Thought The Dunsanian Tales The New England Tales The Lovecraft Mythos Other Tales and Revisions Essays, Poetry, Letters Conclusion Annotated Primary Bibliography Annotated Secondary Bibliography Index Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#S._T._Joshi"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>83</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1987 1984 1982</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dream_Cycle">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#StoryCycles"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dream Cycle</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a group of stories written by H.P. Lovecraft between 1918 and 1932 that deal with a " Dream Realm ," a vast, alternate reality that mortals can access through dreams. Stories in the Dream Lands " Polaris " (1918) " The White Ship " (1919) " The Doom That Came to Sarnath " (1919) " The Cats of Ulthar " (1920) " Celephaïs " (1920) " Ex Oblivione " (1920) " Nyarlathotep " (1920) " The Quest of Iranon " (1921) " The Other Gods " (1921) " Azathoth " (1922) " Hypnos " (1922) " What the Moon Brings " (1922) The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath (1926) " The Outsider " (1926) " The Silver Key " (1926) " The Strange High House in the Mist " (1926) " The Thing in the Moonlight " (Based on a letter written to Donald Wandrei. Written by J. Chapman Miske) (1927. Published 1941) " Through the Gates of the Silver Key " (with E. Hoffmann Price) (1932) Reference only " The Nameless City " (1921) " The Hound " (1922) The Case of Charles Dexter Ward (1927) At the Mountains of Madness (1931) " The Dreams in the Witch House " (1932)</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Venice_Burning">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Venice Burning</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story was first published in the innsmouth free press anthology future lovecraft in november 2011 . It has also been included in the thematic anthology .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#A._C._Wise"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Future_Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2011</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Void_%28League_of_Legends%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dimensions"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalLocations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsOriginatingFromMythosInspiredWorks"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Void (League of Legends)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The void is a malevolent, extradimensional realm in the fictional universe of league of legends . It is a place of cosmic horror, devoid of light, order, and any semblance of life as it is known . The exact origins of the void are shrouded in mystery, but it is believed to be a primordial force .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dead_and_the_Moonstruck">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CaitlinRKiernanWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Dead and the Moonstruck</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Caitlin R. Kiernan English Gothic! Ten Original Dark Tales date 2004 "" is a short story by Caitlin R. Kiernan that first appeared in the 2004 collection Gothic! Ten Original Dark Tales . Set like several of Kiernan's stories in the supernatural underworld of Providence , Rhode Island , "" tells the tale of a changeling--a human child raised by ghouls --facing a coming-of-age ordeal. Behind the Mythos The title comes from a passage from H. P. Lovecraft 's Supernatural Horror in Literature : History "" was reprinted in Kiernan's collections To Charles Fort With Love (2005), Two Worlds and In Between: The Best of Caitlin R. Kiernan (2011), and Houses Under the Sea: Mythos Tales (2018).</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Caitlin_R._Kiernan"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Gothic! Ten Original Dark Tales</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2004</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#China">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Locations"/>
    <rdfs:label>China</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Plateau of leng and plateau of tsang have both been suspected of being in . In the cthulhu mythos, it is presented as a center of cult activity, particularly that of cthulhu . In " the call of cthulhu ", the cultist castro gives information about the cult from the "undying leaders of the cult in the mountains of .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>Plateau of Leng Plateau of Tsang</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Country</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:hasTypeL>region</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Humans Kuen-Yuin (Deathless Sorcerers) The Mighty Children Tcho-Tcho Serpent People Ghouls Madam Yi</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Earth"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Billys_Oak">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#BrianLumleyWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Billy's Oak</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"darghud's doll "is a short story by brian lumley, first published in 1970 issue of the arkham collector . It marks the print debut of the character, although the 1987 short " inception " saw his earliest in-universe timeline appearance . Occultist titus crow is a collector of obscure and eldritch volumes .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Arkham_Collector"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1970</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Massachusetts">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsIncorporatedFromTheRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>Massachusetts</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>United States East Coast State Colonial British Empire The Commonwealth of is a state in the New England region of the United States . It is a very common Cthulhu Mythos setting, for both real-world and invented locations. Essex County, the northeastern corner of the state, forms the heart of Lovecraft Country . Locations The towns and villages invented by H. P. Lovecraft for his fiction include: Arkham Innsmouth Dunwich Kingsport Aylesbury Dean's Corners Other real-world locations mentioned in Lovecraft's fiction include Boston , Salem , Newburyport , and Cambridge . Bolton is the name of an actual town in that Lovecraft seems to place in a different .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasOriginL>Colonial British Empire</ex:hasOriginL>
    <ex:hasTypeL>State</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>United States East Coast</ex:isLocatedAtL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shadow_Out_of_Time_%28Lovecraft_Illustrated%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Shadow Out of Time (Lovecraft Illustrated)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Shadow Out of Time Pete Von Sholly Cover Artist Pete Von Sholly English Lovecraft Illustrated PS Publishing Release Date February 2015 114 Preceded by The Dunwich Horror Followed by The Shadow Over Innsmouth The Shadow Out of Time is a short anthology edited by S. T. Joshi and released by PS Publishing as a hardcover in February 2015. It features the titular novella by H. P. Lovecraft , illustrated by Pete Von Sholly , as well as three non-fiction works regarding the piece. Overview Contents Introduction by S. T. Joshi The Shadow out of Time by H. P. Lovecraft " The Vanity of Existence in "The Shadow out of Time" " by Paul Montelone " The Shadow out of Plastic " by Pete Von Sholly " "The Shadow out of Time" as Lovecraftian Horror " by W. H. Pugmire</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>114</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Lovecraft Illustrated</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>February 2015</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nyogtha">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nyogtha</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Great Old Ones</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Haunter of the Red Abyss The Thing That Should Not Be The Sanguine The Blood Thirsty Nugoozah Nyokzhah</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The character was originally created by henry kuttner for his short story " the salem horror " . The only way that it can be cast back to its own dimension once summoned is through the use of a ritual combining an ankh, the tikkoun elixir and the vach-viraj incantation .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The Stairs in the Crypt Path of Corruption The Anatomy Lesson</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Salem_Horror"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Henry_Kuttner"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Four_Against_the_Great_Old_Ones">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Four Against the Great Old Ones</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Marco Arnaudo Andrea Sfiligoi Italian English Genre(s) Roleplaying game Ganesha Games Release Date 2020 81 is a Cthulhu Mythos table top role-playing game written by Marco Arnaudo and published by Ganesha Games in 2020. Overview Synopsis A group of heroic investigators must gather their powers, carry out research and hope that favorable astral alignments will help them survive as they race to stop a cult which, when the stars are right, will meet to summon eldritch horror to Earth in the form of the Great Old Ones, plunging the world into chaos and ultimately leading to the destruction of the human race. Real World Information uses the Four Against the Darkness game mechanics, and is able to support solitaire, cooperative (no Game Master) or RPG-lite (one player acts as Game Master but all players help tell the story) styles of play. It was published in softcover perfect bound book form in Italy by MS Edizione and in English as a digital file for download by Ganesha Games. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Marco Arnaudo</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>81</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Italian</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2020</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Taker_of_Souls">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Taker of Souls</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Shaitan</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The demon is a non-corporeal entity which is summoned by the ex-mortis . It is extremely aggressive and has a tendency to horrifically mutilate those it possesses . The demon is able to possess humans and physically manifest itself as a twisted version of a human .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Evil Dead (2013)</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Rodo Sayagues</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Walk_in_the_Shadows">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Walk in the Shadows</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>" clear the air" is a hardboiled/supernatural short story by american joseph s.pulver, sr. it forms part of the arkham detective agency created by c.j. Henderson . Characters are former miskatonic university alumni dedicated to the occult .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Joseph S. Pulver. Sr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Detective_Agency"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nathan_Wunner">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosInspiredAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nathan Wunner</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Lovecraftian fiction  is an author who has written a number of pieces inspired by the Cthulhu Mythos first envisioned in the works of Howard Phillips Lovecraft . His Lovecraftian works have appeared in the anthologies Whispers from the Abyss (2014) and Whispers from the Abyss Vol. 2: The Horrors That Are &amp; Shall Be (2015). Selected Bibliography " The Neon Morgue " (2014) " Death May Die " (2015) External Links at The Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gregory_Nicoll">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gregory Nicoll</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Voice artist Birth Date 22 April 1958 Concord, New Hampshire, USA United States (22 April 1958 ) is an American author and voice performer who has had a hand in a number of Cthulhu Mythos works. His fiction has appeared in anthologies such as Singers of Strange Songs and Song of Cthulhu (1997 and 2001 respectively, Chaosium), and he has narrated several audio adaptations of H. P. Lovecraft 's works for the Atlanta Radio Theatre Company . Selected Works Short Fiction "The Man Who Collected Lovecraft" (1977) "Subway Accident" (1997) "How Nyarlathotep Rocked Our World" (2001) Vocal Talent The Dunwich Horror (1988) At the Mountains of Madness (1992) The Shadow Over Innsmouth (1992) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Concord New Hampshire</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Voice artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>22 April 1958</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pandora_Peaslee">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Pandora Peaslee</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Pandora is an unspecified descendant of the peaslee line, which includes nathaniel wingate peaslee and wingate peaslee . A former central intelligence agency operative, she later came to work as a government liaison to mister ys, a member of the great race of yith . In the aftermath of a calamitous event, pandora found purpose in becoming a resistance fighter against the yithians .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Peter_Rawlik"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Paula_R._Stiles">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Paula R. Stiles</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Editor Author United States http://thesnowleopard.net/ is an American author and editor most notable for her work on the Innsmouth Free Press and Innsmouth Magazine titles. She is a frequent collaborator with Silvia Moreno-Garcia . Selected Bibliography Editor Innsmouth Free Press (2009-2011) Historical Lovecraft (2011, with Silvia Moreno-Garcia ) Future Lovecraft (2011, with Silvia Moreno-Garcia) Innsmouth Magazine (2011-2014) She Walks in Shadows (2015) Fiction " Old Time Religion " (2016) " Light a Candle, Curse the Darkness " (2017) External Links at The Internet Speculative Fiction Database</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://thesnowleopard.net/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Autopsy_and_Other_Tales">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Autopsy and Other Tales</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Michael Shea Various United States English Centipede Press Release Date 2008 593 is a collection of both Mythos and non-Mythos works by the late American Michael Shea . Overview Consisting of 21 short stories, an introduction by Laird Barron , and an afterword by the , runs to a total of 593 . It was published in hardcover format by Centipede Press in 2008, and has yet to be reissued. Contents "The Angel of Death" "The Horror on the #33" "Fast Food" "Grunt-12 Test Drive" "Salome" " Fat Face " "Uncle Tuggs" "Fill It with Regular" " Nemo Me Impune Lacessit " "Polyphemus" "That Frog" "The Extra" "The Growlimb" "The Autopsy" "The Rebuke" "Delivery" "For Every Tatter in Its Mortal Dress" "The Pearls of the Vampire Queen" "I, Said the Fly" " Tsathoggua " "The Color Out of Time"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Michael_Shea"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>593</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2008</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Severn_Valley">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>Severn Valley</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The fictional milieu is arguably the most detailed mythos setting outside of lovecraft country itself . By the mid-60s, campbell acknowledges that "my invented town of brichester , originally intended as the equivalent of lovecraft's arkham , was liverpool by now in all but name" "campbell country" is a reflection of the english countryside that he knew personally .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Gloucester</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>United Kingdom</ex:isLocatedAtL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsOriginatingFromMythosAdjacentWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>Mu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>In the real world is the name of a lost continent whose concept and name were proposed by 19th-century traveler and writer augustus le plongeon . This concept was popularized and expanded by james churchward, who asserted that was a different lost continent than atlantis; he located in the pacific ocean . Appears in numerous cthulhu mythos stories, including many written by lin carter .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Sunken continent</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pacific_Ocean"/>
    <ex:wasDestroyedOnL>Prehistory</ex:wasDestroyedOnL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Exo_III">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsOriginatingFromMythosAdjacentWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Planets"/>
    <rdfs:label>Exo III</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Created by robert bloch is a fictional planet created for the episode "what are little girls made of?" Of the science fiction television series star trek . Contains references to an ancient humanoid race known as the "old ones" was subsequently incorporated into the doctor who universe, and referenced in the mythos crossover novel white darkness .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>Caves</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>Icy plains</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Planet</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Androids</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Milky Way Galaxy</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>What Are Little Girls Made Of</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#May_Skies">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>May Skies</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft English Genre(s) Non-fiction Donald M. Grant To Quebec and the Stars Date of writing May 1917 date 1976 " " is a short non-fiction work by the late American Howard Phillips Lovecraft . Although it was written in May 1917, it did not see until its inclusion in L. Sprague de Camp 's 1976 collection To Quebec and the Stars . It does not appear to have been republished in the years since . References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>To Quebec</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>the Stars</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1976</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>May 1917</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Gods_of_H._P._Lovecraft">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Gods of H. P. Lovecraft</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Overview contains twelve stories, each of which focuses on a different entity or species from the works of h. p. lovecraft . Despite the title, not all of the featured monstrosities are actually gods in their original appearances . A follow-up titled azathoth: ordo abchao was published in 2023 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>454</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>December 2015</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Walter_C._DeBill,_Jr.">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosAdjacentAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Walter C. DeBill, Jr.</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>16 December 2023</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Walter C. DeBill, Jr. Birth Date 21 January 1939 Ancòn, Panama Death Date 16 December 2023 Austin, Texas, USA United States Walter C. DeBill, Jr. (21 January 1939 - 16 December 2023) was an author of horror and science fiction short stories and a long-time contributor to the Cthulhu Mythos . DeBill also created the Mlandoth Cycle , a story cycle intended to run parallel to Lovecraft's Cthulhu Mythos, but which has, as with so many other writings of weird fiction, been retroactively incorporated into the Mythos by Chaosium as part of their Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game. Selected Bibliography Collections The Black Sutra (2006) Short Fiction "In 'Ygiroth" (1975) " Where Yidhra Walks " (1976) "What Sort of Man" (2004) Creations Mlandoth Mril Thorion Ayi'ig Ngyr-Korath 'Ymnar Yidhra Xothra Y'hath External Links Walter C. DeBill, Jr. at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database Walter C. DeBill, Jr. at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>Ancòn Panama</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>21 January 1939</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Worlds_of_H._P._Lovecraft:_The_Statement_of_Randolph_Carter">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleComicBookAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: The Statement of Randolph Carter</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft (original story) Steven Philip Jones (adaptation) Christopher Jones David DeVera United States English The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft Genre(s) Weird fiction Caliber Comics Release Date 1996 52 is a comic book adaptation of the Howard Phillips Lovecraft story of the same name . Overview Adapted by Steven Philip Jones and featuring artwork by Christopher Jones and David DeVera, The Statement of Randolph Carter runs to 55 . The cover is in full colour, while the interiors are rendered in black and white. It was first published as a stand-alone comic by Caliber Comics in 1996 and was later included in the omnibus collection H. P. Lovecraft's Worlds: Volume 2 . Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Steven_Philip_Jones"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>52</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1996</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Best_of_Dark_Legacy,_Volume_1">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Best of Dark Legacy, Volume 1</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Dark legacy, volume 2 is a collection of lovecraftian fiction from the now-defunct fanzine dark legacy . Edited by american ran cartwright, it runs to a total of 212 . Features cover art by konstantin vohwinkel .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>212</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dark_Legacy"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chris_Klepac">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Chris Klepac</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Christian "Chris" Klepac Connection to the Mythos Traditional games designer Roleplaying games Horror Christian "Chris" Klepac is a traditional games designer who has produced several roleplaying game works in the Cthulhu Mythos . His material has for the most part been released through Pagan Publishing 's gaming magazine The Unspeakable Oath , but has also been seen in non-English publications including the magazines Cthulhus Ruf and Labirynt , and has even been included in the convention-exclusive collection Courting Madness . Selected Works Courting Madness (1992, contributor) The Resurrected Volume Two: Of Keys &amp; Gates (1994, with John H. Crowe III and John Tynes ) The Resurrected III: Out of the Vault (2002, contributor) Cthulhus Ruf (2014) External Links at RPGGeek References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Christian Chris Klepac</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Traditional games designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#William_Frederick_Anger">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSFriendsAndAcquaintances"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>William Frederick Anger</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>unknown</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Weird fiction fan and late correspondent of Lovecraft.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1921</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkya">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Arkya</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A god-like eidolon which observes the history of its land, also known as , until its destruction at the hands of the vorklai (deep ones?) After this he becomes a vampiric night-creature guarding the</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Annals of Arkya</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_A._W._Lowndes"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Hills_Rise_Wild%21">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Hills Rise Wild!</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The hills rise wild! Was a tabletop combat game loosely based on the greater cthulhu mythos . The premise was that old wizard whateley has died and left his occult possessions to be looted . As a result, four clans have gathered in the small town of dunwich to battle it out for whateley's copy of the necromicon .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2000</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Under_the_Keeper_of_the_Key">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Under the Keeper of the Key</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Jaap Boekestein English James Ward Kirk Publishing Lovecraft after Dark date November 2015 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos story by Dutch Jaap Boekestein . It was published in the James Ward Kirk Publishing anthology Lovecraft after Dark in November 2015. Synopsis After many years of corresponding with the mysterious Machteld, Gerald Voss is finally invited to a meeting where he will learn the secrets of Great Cthulhu and the Old Ones. Participating in a ritual designed to heighten his consciousness through pain and pleasure, he is ultimately transformed by Machteld into an amphibious creature like herself, and ventures forth to claim the knowledge which he has earned.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jaap_Boekestein"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_after_Dark"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tani_Jantsang">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonFictionAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tani Jantsang</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Pseudonym(s) Phyllis Rose Connection to the Mythos Editor Author Artist Horror Non-fiction is an author, editor and one-time artist who works primarily in the horror and occult non-fiction genres. Her editorial credits include the complete run of the Cthulhu Cultus fanzine, and her works have been included in publications such as Eldritch Tales and the Robert M. Price -edited Crypt of Cthulhu . Selected Works Editor Cthulhu Cultus (1995-2001) Artist Cthulhu Cultus (1995-2001) See Also Images by External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Phyllis Rose</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#An_Incarceration_in_BhYhlun">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#BhyhlunSeries"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>An Incarceration in Bh'Yhlun</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Short story forms second part of his bh'yhlun series of post-apocalyptic mythos fiction . First published in online zine nightscapes #3 in september 1997 . Received a printed release in dark legacy #5 in may 1995 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ian_Davey"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Post-apocalyptic horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Nightscapes #3</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>September 1997</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#In_The_Chapel_Of_Ghattambah">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>In The Chapel Of Ghattambah</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A pair of rich siblings sacrifice their new gold-digging stepmother to the monstrous high priest of ghattambah . Historyhas been included in the collections horrors &amp; abominations: 24 tales of the cthulhu mythos and the house of the ocelot .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Abominations: 24 Tales of the Cthulhu Mythos</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Horrors</ex:isPublishedAsW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Season_in_Carcosa">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Season in Carcosa</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Daniele Serra English Miskatonic River Press Release Date September 2012 282 is an anthology of Yellow Mythos fiction, stories based on Robert W. Chambers ' The King in Yellow , which has become firmly entrenched in H. P. Lovecraft 's Cthulhu Mythos . Edited by Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. and featuring cover art by Daniele Serra, it runs to a total of 282 and was published by Miskatonic River Press in September 2012; both digital and hardcover editions were released. Overview Contents Introduction by Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. "My Voice Is Dead" by Joel Lane "Beyond the Banks of the River Seine" by Simon Strantzas "Movie Night at Phil's" by Don Webb "MS Found in a Chicago Hotel Room" by Daniel Mills "it sees me when I'm not looking" by Gary McMahon "Finale, Act Two" by Ann K. Schwader "Yellow Bird Springs" by Kate Gardner "The Theatre and Its Double" by Edward Morris "The Hymn of the Hyades" by Richard Gavin "Slick Black Bones and Soft Black Stars" by Gemma Files "Not Enough Hope" by Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. "Whose Hears are Pure Gold" by Kristin Prevallet "April Dawn" by Richard A. Lupoff "King Wolf" by Anna Tambour "The White-Face at Dawn" by Michael Kelly "Wishing Well" by Cody Goodfellow "Sweetums" by John Langan "The King in Yellow" by Pearce Hansen "D T" by Laird Barron "Salvation in Yellow" by Robin Spriggs "The Beat Hotel" by Allyson Bird</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>282</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>September 2012</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chris_A._Jackson">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Chris A. Jackson</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author United States Fantasy Science Fiction Horror https://www.jaxbooks.com/ is an American author best known for his stories of nautical piracy and roleplay games tie-in fiction; he has written for several gaming franchises including Pathfinder , Shadowrun and Arkham Horror . Selected Bibliography The Deep Gate (2018)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://www.jaxbooks.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gah_Lak_Tus">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gah Lak Tus</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Brandon peterson is an eldritch mechanical entity in the fictional ultimate universe (earth-1610) of marvel by marvel comics . Background is a vast collective of city-sized robotic drones that spanned 100,000 miles . It traveled the cosmos for millennia, destroying every life-bearing world in its path.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>January 2006</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Ultimate Extinction #1</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Brandon Peterson</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Warren Ellis</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Last_Quest">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Last Quest</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Historywas first published in the anthology through a mythos darkly . It also appeared in the collection of meikle's own works into the black: tales of lovecraftian terror in 2019 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#William_Meikle"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Alternate History</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Through_a_Mythos_Darkly"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>June 2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Bokrug">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Bokrug</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Great Old Ones</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Lesser Old Ones</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Great Water Lizard</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The great water lizard is a fictional deity created by h. p. lovecraft for his 1920 short story " the doom that came to sarnath " the deity sleeps beneath the calm waters of a lake that bordered ib and the city of sarnath . In the expanded mythos, is sometimes portrayed as a great old one ( exp : call of cthulhu ) or as a lesser old one subservient to the great old one</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Doom_That_Came_to_Sarnath"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#D-Void">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>D-Void</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a malevolent undead entity that lives in the dead universe . It appears to be at least partially inspired by some entities from the cthulhu mythos . It is able to control the minds of sentient robots and feed on cybertronians .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#In_the_Dark">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>In the Dark</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Various Cover Artist Christian Wildgoose English Genre(s) Horror Rachel Deering Tiny Behemoth Press IDW Publishing Release Date May 2014 373 is an anthology collection of short comic book horror stories from a wide variety of authors and artists. Published by Rachel Deering, Tiny Behemoth Press, and IDW Publishing, it was released in May 2014; both physical and digital editions were issued. Overview Although not a Lovecraftian anthology, contains several references to Lovecraftiana, including: " The Unseen ", a contemporary adaptation of H. P. Lovecraft 's short story " From Beyond " An unrelated cover plate for At the Mountains of Madness by Phil Murphy A reference in F. Paul Wilson 's "Doc Johnson" to Lumens in the Pine Barrens of New Jersey . This is a nod to Wilson's short Cthulhu Mythos story " The Barrens " Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>373</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 2014</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ascent_into_Hell">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ascent into Hell</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Peter B. Gillis Chris Warner, Randy Emberlin English Marvel Comics Release Date April, 1987 - May, 1988 Media Type Comic book is a 14-part story arc in issues #1-14 of the 2nd volume of Strange Tales written by Peter B. Gillis. Synopsis Due to the actions of Doctor Strange , the mystical barriers protecting Earth from the Old Ones have been weakened and Shuma-Gorath and his minions threaten Earth .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Peter B. Gillis</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1987 - May 1988 April</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shuy-Nihl">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Shuy-Nihl</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Devourer in the Earth</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as The Devourer in the Earth First appearance " Madness in Haiti " appears as a dark orb or blob of darkness endowed with tentacles.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Madness in Haiti</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Baleful_God">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Baleful God</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>W. H. Pugmire United States English Necronomicon Press Cthulhu Codex May 1996 " ", which has also seen print under the title " Yon Baleful God " is a very short Cthulhu Mythos story by the late American W. H. Pugmire , writing as Wilum Pugmire. It was first published by Necronomicon Press in the May 1996 edition of fanzine Cthulhu Codex . Synopsis In the Sesqua Valley , a man introduces his lover to the worship of the Great Old One Tsathoggua before the two share an unexpected sacrifice. History Cthulhu Codex May 1996 Dreams of Lovecraftian Horror (1999) Gathered Dust and Others (2011, as "Yon Baleful God") Trivia The fictional Sesqua Valley is a recurrent setting in the Cthulhu Mythos fiction of W. H. Pugmire, having been created by the for this very purpose.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#W._H._Pugmire"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Codex_May_1996"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lloyd_Arthur_Eshbach">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSFriendsAndAcquaintances"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lloyd Arthur Eshbach</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>2003</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Science fiction writer and publisher who edited the Galleon and published some of Lovecraft’s poems and stories in the magazine.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1910</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Encyclopedia_Cthulhiana/Contents_A-E">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Encyclopedia Cthulhiana/Contents A-E</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>This page lists the contents from a-g of the chaosium reference book the encyclopedia cthulhiana by daniel harms . It is based on the work of george goodenough (1901–1971) and henry whentworth (1871–1928?)</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jeff_Barber">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Jeff Barber</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Jeffrey "jeff" barber is an american games designer and occasional artist . Has worked on several cthulhu mythos projects through his involvement with third-party content provider pagan publishing . Best known for his science fiction eco-fantasy blue planet roleplaying game .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Missouri Columbia</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Jeffrey Jeff Barber</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Traditional games designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Raandeese">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Raandeese</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The are monstrous shark-people who worship the god raandaii-b'nk . Once every two years the females give birth to three young (two females and one male) at birth they resemble sharks, but in days of birth arms and legs develop . At 20 years old they have grown to resemble humans in shape .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>From the Deep</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gregory_Nicoll"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sirenia_Digest">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CaitlinRKiernanWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ezines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sirenia Digest</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Caitlin R. Kiernan English Monthly November 2005 http://www.caitlinrkiernan.com/sirenia/home.php is a monthly e-zine published by Caitlin R. Kiernan , billed as a "monthly journal of the weirdly erotic." First appearing in November 2005, it is the first venue for much of Kiernan's short fiction. Kiernan's calls the journal In addition to the vignettes, often features short fiction by Kiernan and previews of novels. Stories that appeared for the first time in include: “ Pickman’s Other Model (1929) ” (March 2008, #28), “ The Thousand-and-Third Tale of Scheherazade ” (January 2009, #38), “ The Bone’s Prayer ” (February 2009, #39) “ Fish Bride (1970) ” (May 2009, #42) “ The Alchemist’s Daughter (A Fragment) ” (June 2009, #43) “ Houndwife ” (March 2010, #52) “ Tidal Forces ” (June 2010, #55) “ John Four ” (September 2010, #58) “ On the Reef ” (October 2010, #59) “ The Transition of Elizabeth Haskings ” (January 2012, #74) “ Love Is Forbidden, We Croak &amp; Howl ” (May 2012 #78) “ A Mountain Walked ” (September 2012, #82) “ Pushing the Sky Away (Death of a Blasphemer) ” (August 2013, #91) “ Study for The Witch House ” (September 2013, #92) “ Pickman’s Madonna ” (October 2013, #93) “ The Peddler’s Tale, or Isobel’s Revenge ” (December 2013, #95) “ The Cats of River Street (1925) ” (July 2014, #102) External Link home page References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Servitor_of_Haiogh-Yai">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Servitor of Haiogh-Yai</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Haiogh-Yai"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Haiogh-Yai First appearance EXP : " The Outsider " Created by James Ambuehl The Servitors of Haiogh-Yai are a race of worm or lizard-like servitors of Haiogh-Yai and dwell with the entity in Vix'ni-Aldru . The Servitors made their first appearance in James Ambuehl 's Cthulhu Mythos poem " The Outsider ", published in the December 1990 issue of Eldritch Tales .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Outsider"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Ambuehl"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Bobby_Derie">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Bobby Derie</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Robert "Bobby" Derie Connection to the Mythos Editor Author Genre blogger https://deepcuts.blog/ Robert "Bobby" Derie is a historian of pulp and weird fiction, editor, one-time author, and the creator of the review Deep Cuts in a Lovecraftian Vein . Selected Bibliography Editor Sex and the Cthulhu Mythos ( Hippocampus Press , 2015) Weird Talers: Essays on Robert E. Howard and Others (Hippocampus Press, 2019) The Collected Letters of Robert E. Howard: Index and Addenda (Robert E. Howard Press, 2014) Cross Plains Pilgrimage (2022) External Links Robert Derie at The Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://deepcuts.blog/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
