    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Will of Claude Ashur</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story is set in a town on the "lifeless, atlantic-clawed stretches of the northern new jersey coast" the main social establishment is the tavern at inneswich . The story also takes place in part at miskatonic university .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#C._Hall_Thompson"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>July 1947</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#J._B._Lee">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>J. B. Lee</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Years Active 1998-2000 is an author and occasional artist who has written a number of pieces set in the greater Cthulhu Mythos established by the works of H. P. Lovecraft and many others. These have for the most part been published in fanzines such as Nightscapes , Cthulhu Cultus , Dark Legacy , and Imelod . Selected Bibliography Short Fiction " Not One the Same " (1998) "Dies Irae" (1998) " Genuine Article " (1998) " His Hour Upon the Stage " (1998) "Nemesis" (1999) " With Eyes That See " (1999) "Forces of Change" (1999) Artwork The Sorcerer's Apprentices: New Tales in the Tradition of Clark Ashton Smith (1998, Sunken Citadel/Tenoka Press) Dark Legacy (1999, Tenoka Press) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasYearsActiveR>1998-2000</ex:hasYearsActiveR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hecuba">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Hecuba</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Great_Old_One"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"the queen of time" is a pre-universe great old one created by big finish . Created by brian hayles is a great old one created by big finish productions . Background is the sister of the celestial toymaker who ensnares sentient beings .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Queen of Time</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Brian Hayles</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Margery_Williams">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsInspirationsAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Margery Williams</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>4 September 1944</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Bianco (born Margery Winifred Williams) Pseudonym(s) Harper Williams Connection to the Mythos Possible inspiration Birth Date 22 July 1881 London, England Death Date 4 September 1944 New York City, New York, USA Children's Bianco (22 July 1881 – 4 September 1944), born Margery Winifred Williams, was an award-winning American children’s author best known for her popular 1921 novel The Velveteen Rabbit . Williams' 1913 novel The Thing in the Woods (which was reprinted in the United States under the pseudonym Harper Williams) a story about lycanthropy in Pennsylvania, has been speculated to have been an influence on H. P. Lovecraft 's short story " The Dunwich Horror " ; Lovecraft was known to have read the book, and wrote the poem " On The Thing in the Woods by Harper Williams ", a piece which remained unpublished until its inclusion in the 2001 Night Shade Books collection The Ancient Track: The Complete Poetical Works of H. P. Lovecraft . Selected Bibliography Novels The Thing in the Woods (1913) External Links at Wikipedia at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>http://example.org/lovecraft#England London</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Margery Williams Bianco</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Harper Williams</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Possible inspiration</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>22 July 1881</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Troy_Young">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Troy Young</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Canada Horror Fantasy Science Fiction is an author based in Toronto, Canada. He writes in the fantasy, science fiction, and horror genres, and is perhaps best-known for his series of Cthulhu Mythos stories contained in the Nyarlathotep Cycle . Selected Bibliography Collections The Other: Encounters with the Cthulhu Mythos Book 1 The Other: Encounters with the Cthulhu Mythos Book 2 (2020) The Other: Encounters with the Cthulhu Mythos Book 3 (2021) The Meph'hibb'anauth Cycle: The Other, Series Two, Book 1 (2022) The Nyarlathotep Cycle: The Other Omnibus (2023) Short Stories " It Came from the Sea " (2020) " It Slumbers Beneath the Ice " (2020) " It Lives in the Woods " (2020) " It Hides in the Village " (2020) " It Lurks in the Basement " (2020) " It Ends Where It Began " (2020) " The Happenings of December 13th, 2012 " (2020) " The Rat King " (2020) " The Alien Gardener " (2020) " The Pyramid of the Black Pharaoh " (2020) " The Druid of Castle Rock " (2020) " The Viking's Doom " (2020) " The Tattered Lady " (2020) " The Thing in the Customs House " (2020) " The House at the Edge of Time and Space " (2021) " The Shade of Pierre Girault " (2021) " The Misplaced Masterpiece of Richard Upton Pickman " (2021) " The Curse of the Windsor Witch " (2021) " The Bride of the Yellow King " (2021) " In the Depths of R'lyeh " (2021)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>Canada</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Melanie_E._Fischer">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonFictionAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Melanie E. Fischer</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Editor Author Horror Non-Fiction is an editor and author who has written both fiction and non-fiction works relating to the H. P. Lovecraft -inspired Cthulhu Mythos . Selected Bibliography Editor The Ancient Track (1998-1999) Writings Imelod (1997-1999)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Thomas_Lovecraft">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSRelatives"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Thomas Lovecraft</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1826</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lovecraft’s paternal great-great-grandfather. Lovecraft claims that Thomas sold “his family seat,” Minster Hall, in 1823 due to financial difficulties.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isRelativeToR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1745</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Trail_of_Cthulhu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Trail of Cthulhu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Gumshoe is an investigative tabletop role-playing game designed by kenneth hite using the gumshoe designed by robin d. laws . The game uses a rules-light approach where points are spent to obtain clues to the adventure's resolution and for combat . Since its inception, a wealth of supplements for have been released including: the murderer of thomas fell (2008) rough magicks (2009) shadows over filmland (2009) .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 2008</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Joseph_Hanley">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>James Joseph Hanley</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>The Braving Bulletin</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>J.J.</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as J.J. Journalist The Braving Bulletin Human First appearance EXP : " Wrath of the Wind-Walker " EXP : " One Rainy Afternoon Spent in the Company of Dr. Thulask " Created by Robert M. Price James Ambuehl James Joseph "J.J." Hanley is a character created by American authors James Ambuehl and Robert M. Price for their Braving, Minnesota contributions to the greater Cthulhu Mythos . Overview Hanley is an intelligent man who works as a reporter for the Braving Bulletin , a local Minnesotan newspaper. He appears to cover primarily human-interest pieces (and has his own column titled "Meet the People of Braving") but also claims to have unearthed a number of stories which have been deemed "ultimately unprintable", hinting that they may have been supernatural in nature. " Wrath of the Wind-Walker " (1998) " One Rainy Afternoon Spent in the Company of Dr. Thulask " (2005)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#One_Rainy_Afternoon_Spent_in_the_Company_of_Dr._Thulask"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Wrath_of_the_Wind-Walker"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Ambuehl"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_M._Price"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dilution_Solution">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dilution Solution</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Adrian Simmons English Simian Publishing Apotheosis: Stories of Human Survival After the Rise of the Elder Gods date October 2015  " " is a Lovecraftian short story by Adrian Simmons. It was published in the Jason Andrew -edited thematic anthology Apotheosis: Stories of Human Survival After the Rise of the Elder Gods by Simian Publishing in October 2015. Synopsis In a subterranean city on an Earth besieged by the forces of madness and chaos, a Patroller - men and women designated to travel the world in search of vital supplies for the colony - is brought to a rehabilitation centre suspected of being infected by the pervasive insanity he has witnessed in the world above. Over the following weeks, he is treated by a genius doctor, one who came from outside and is reputed to know everything about the cause of the madness. He and his fellow inmates are slowly indoctrinated into the doctor's cult, before leaving the city to spread their unique form of madness to the next colony in line…</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Adrian Simmons</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Apotheosis:_Stories_of_Human_Survival_After_the_Rise_of_the_Elder_Gods"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Home_From_the_Sea">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Home From the Sea</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Daylight dims: volume 1 (2013) : tales of lovecraftian terror (2017) .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#William_Meikle"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Daylight Dims: Volume 1</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2013</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Zombie">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SpeciesIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdfs:label>Zombie</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Zombies are undead or walking dead creatures which are part of the mythology and folklore of several of mankind's cultures . Chaosium first made zombies part of the mythos in 2007 when they were added to the malleus monstrorum sourcebook . Zombies vary from those created by witch doctors and sorcerers to those that are uncontrollable ravenous flesh-eating zombies .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Cast a Deadly Spell White Darkness</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Witch Hunt Malleus Monstrorum</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Daring Mystery Comics #1</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Alberto_Breccia">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Alberto Breccia</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>10 November 1993</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Artist Birth Date 15 April 1919 Montevideo, Uruguay Death Date 10 November 1993 Buenos Aires, Argentina Argentinian (15 April 1919-10 November 1993) was a South American comic book artist. He has provided artwork for a number of adaptations of H. P. Lovecraft stories, most of which have yet to be translated into English. Selected Bibliography The Shadow Over Innsmouth (1973) The Nameless City (1974) The Haunter of the Dark (1975) The Dunwich Horror (1979) Collections Los mitos de Cthulhu</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>Montevideo Uruguay</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>Argentinian</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>15 April 1919</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
