    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Namenlose_Kulte">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Namenlose Kulte</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Germany German H. P. Lovecraft's Bibliothek des Schreckens Festa Release Date April 2006 303 , which translates as Nameless Cults , is a German- collection of H. P. Lovecraft 's fiction, which also includes a handful of scholarly works by other authors. It was published as a hardcover by Festa as part of their H. P. Lovecraft's Bibliothek des Schreckens in April 2006. Edited by Frank Festa , it runs to a total of 303 . Contents "Der Ruf des Cthulhu" by Howard Phillips Lovecraft (" The Call of Cthulhu ") "Die Katzen von Ulthar" by Howard Phillips Lovecraft (" The Cats of Ulthar ") "Pickmans Modell" by Howard Phillips Lovecraft (" Pickman's Model ") "Das Grauen von Dunwich" by Howard Phillips Lovecraft (" The Dunwich Horror ") "Celephais" by Howard Phillips Lovecraft (" Celephaïs ") "Aus dem Jenseits" Howard Phillips Lovecraft (" From Beyond ") "Das weiße Schiff" by Howard Phillips Lovecraft (" The White Ship ") "Der Tempel" by Howard Phillips Lovecraft (" The Temple ") "Jenseits der Mauer des Schlafes" by Howard Phillips Lovecraft (" Beyond the Wall of Sleep ") "Herbert West – Reanimator" by Howard Phillips Lovecraft (" Herbert West--Reanimator ") "Die Farbe aus dem All" by Howard Phillips Lovecraft (" The Colour Out of Space ") "Erinnerungen an einen Aufenthalt H. P. Lovecrafts in Florida" by Robert H. Barlow ("The Wind That Is in the Grass: A Memoir of H. P. Lovecraft in Florida") "Vermischte Eindrücke von H. P. L." by Marian F. Bonner (" Miscellaneous Impressions of H.P.L. ") "Ein flüchtiger Blick auf H. P. L." by Mary V. Dana ("A Glimpse of H. P. L.") "H. P. Lovecraft aus der Sicht einer Schülerin" by Zealia Bishop ("H. P. Lovecraft: A Pupil's View")</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>Germany</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>303</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>German</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecrafts_Bibliothek_des_Schreckens"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>April 2006</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#At_the_Altar_of_the_Goat_God">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>At the Altar of the Goat God</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Jim Owsley Gary Kwapisz Cover Artist Thomas Kidd English Marvel Comics Release Date June, 1986 Media Type Comic book " " is a Cthulhu Mythos -connected Conan the Barbarian comic book story. Synopsis The mercenary Conan finds himself in the middle of a feud between a cult and a wealthy landowner in Zamora, and encounters the 'Sahb Delanzar, the Great Goat God, the God with a Thousand Young' (in actuality Shub-Niggurath , one of H. P. Lovecraft 's most well-known Outer Gods ). Behind the Mythos "" was published in issue #125 of fantasy publication The Savage Sword of Conan by Marvel Comics in June 1986. It was written by Jim Owsley with artwork provided by Gary Kwapisz. External Links " " at the Marvel Database.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Jim Owsley</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1986 June</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Cthulhu_Mythos_%28August_Derleth_collection%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AugustDerlethWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Cthulhu Mythos (August Derleth collection)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Cthulhu Mythos (August Derleth collection) August Derleth Cover Artist Leah Lococo English Genre(s) Horror Barnes &amp; Noble Release Date February 1997 448 The Cthulhu Mythos is a collection of Mythos tales from the late American August Derleth . Overview Published in February 1997 by Barnes &amp; Noble, The Cthulhu Mythos is a 448 page hardcover book edited by Jim Turner with an introduction by Ramsey Campbell and cover art from Leah Lococo. It is largely a reprint of In Lovecraft's Shadow: The Cthulhu Mythos Stories of August Derleth . Contents Introduction by Ramsey Campbell " The Dweller in Darkness " " Beyond the Threshold " " The Thing That Walked on the Wind " " Ithaqua " " The Passing of Eric Holm " " Something from Out There " Original Introduction to The Mask of Cthulhu " The Return of Hastur " " The Whippoorwills in the Hills " " Something in Wood " " The Sandwin Compact " " The House in the Valley " " The Seal of R'lyeh " " The House on Curwen Street " " The Watcher from the Sky " " The Gorge Beyond Salapunco " " The Keeper of the Key " " The Black Island " " A Note on the Cthulhu Mythos "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#August_Derleth"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>448</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>February 1997</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Thalarion">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalLocations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Thalarion</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAccessibilityL>Via the stone gate Akariel</ex:hasAccessibilityL>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesL>City of a Thousand Wonders</ex:hasAlternativeNamesL>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lathi - an eidolon who rules the city and reduces those who see it to skeletons . City is surrounded by grey stone walls that hide most of the frieze covered buildings and alluring sculptures . Streets are littered with unburied bones .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>The Dreamlands</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_White_Ship"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dylath-Leen">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dylath-Leen</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ulthar is a bustling trading port and one of the largest cities in the dreamlands . It is famous for the comings and goings of the mysterious black galleys . The city was created by h.p. Lovecraft and is one of the largest cities in the dreamlands .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Humans</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>The Dreamlands</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dream-Quest_of_Unknown_Kadath"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Arctic_Stone">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Arctic Stone</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu mythos short story first published in 1980 issue of eldritch tales . It tells the story of two anthropologists who discover a tribe's camp . The tribe is dedicated to worshiping their tornasuk cthulhu and his servant dagon .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Ross F. Bagby</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Eldritch Tales No. 7</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1980</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Spawn_of_Othuyeg">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Spawn of Othuyeg</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Real World Information First appearance " The Seven Cities of Gold " Created by Crispin Burnham The are creatures created by Crispin Burnham for his short story " The Seven Cities of Gold " and later used by Chaosium for the Call of Cthulhu role-playing game. Description The appear as miniature versions of their creator Othuyeg , who they live with in the Seven Cities of Gold. They are most commonly seen flooding out of the ground after earthquakes.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Seven Cities of Gold</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crispin_Burnham"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Michael_Leigh">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Michael Leigh</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Leigh is an occultist with a deep understanding of mystical practices . Appears in " the black kiss ", by kuttner and robert bloch . Carson, the main character of "the salem horror", finds himself liking leigh .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Black_Kiss"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Salem_Horror"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Henry_Kuttner"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Urhag">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonSapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Urhag</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Real World Information First appearance " The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath " Created by H. P. Lovecraft</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dream-Quest_of_Unknown_Kadath"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Irem">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsOriginatingFromMythosAdjacentWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Irem</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesL>Irim</ex:hasAlternativeNamesL>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesL>The City of Pillars</ex:hasAlternativeNamesL>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Behind the mythos is inspired by and based on the real life legend of iram, "the atlantis of the sands" one possible real life for iram / is shisr in oman, at a site that amateur archaeologists have discovered a lost city .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Desert</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Nameless_City"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>Quran</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Henry_%28Lance_Axt%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Henry (Lance Axt)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>" henry " is a short story by lance axt . It was published in the anthology of h.p. Lovecraft-inspired fiction whispers from the abyss in november 2014 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Lance Axt</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Whispers_from_the_Abyss"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2014</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tsathoggua_%28poem%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tsathoggua (poem)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Tsathoggua (poem) Full story online here Ron Shiflet United States English Publication date 1998 " Tsathoggua " is a short Mythos poem by Ron Shiflet . Synopsis The two verses offer a potted history and brief description of the Great Old One Tsathoggua . Publication History "Tsathoggua" was published in the James Ambuehl -edited anthology The Sorcerer's Apprentices: New Tales in the Tradition of Clark Ashton Smith in 1998.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Highway_97">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CaitlinRKiernanWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Highway 97</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>" bainbridge "is a short story by caitlin r. kiernan that features their recurring character . The story takes place just before the events of " bainbridge ." It was later included in an expanded edition of alabaster published by dark horse .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Caitlin_R._Kiernan"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dancy_Flammarion"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2006</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>December 2005 January 2006</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_2_%28Jack_Ryan%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu 2 (Jack Ryan)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Crypt of Cthulhu 2 Jack Ryan United States English Release Date October 2019 143 Crypt of Cthulhu 2 is a collection of horror fiction by American Jack Ryan , consisting of six short stories tied together by a framing narrative. Running to a total of 143 , it was independently published in October 2019; both paperback and digital editions were released. Overview Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jack_Ryan"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>143</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 2019</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Envy,_the_Gardens_of_Ynath,_and_the_Sin_of_Cain">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Envy, the Gardens of Ynath, and the Sin of Cain</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Envy, the gardens of ynath and the sin of cain is a short story by american writer and critic darrell schweitzer . Written from the point of view of the narrator, one brian simmons, it is a follow-up to h.p. Lovecraft's story the whisperer in darkness . Climax concerns simmons' last visit to see noyes in his home at the old akeley place, where noyes has discovered the artefact .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Darrell_Schweitzer"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Short Fiction Science Fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>April 2002 Worlds of Cthulhu Awaiting Strange Gods The Cthulhu Mythos Megapack</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Interzone</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>April 2002</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Eastertide_1985">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu Eastertide 1985</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Chris Gross United States English Crypt of Cthulhu Cryptic Publications Release Date April 1985 64 Preceded by Candlemas 1985 Followed by Roodmas 1985 is the 30th issue of leading H. P. Lovecraft scholar Robert M. Price 's long-running Lovecraftian fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Edited by Price with a cover by Chris Gross, it ran to a total of 64 in an A5 format, and was published by Cryptic Publications in April 1985 . Contents Editorial Shards by Robert M. Price "Believers in Lovecraft" by Ralph E. Vaughan "Truth Is Stranger Than Lovecraft" by Jim Shoffner "The Brotherhood of Cthulhu?" by Richard L. Tierney "Lovecraft's New York Exile: Its Influence on His Life and Writing" by David E. Schultz "The Innsmouth Look" by Bert Atsma "On the Revision of "Dreams of Yith"" by Edward P. Berglund "Charnal Knowledge" by Joel Lane "Some Ancestors of Vathek: The "Eastern Stories" of John Hawksworth in The Adventurer, 1752-54" by Darrell Schweitzer "Weird Tales on Television" by Graeme Flanagan and William Fulwiler "Yet More Lovecraft in the Cinema" by Chester Malon "Lovecraft in the Comics" by Will Murray "Lovecraft in Rock Music" by John Stanton "On the Achievements of a Popular Writer" by H. P. Lovecraft "The Members of the Men's Club of the First Universalist Church of Providence, R. I., to Its President, About to Leave for Florida on Account of His Health" by H. P. Lovecraft "Theobaldian Aestivation" by H. P. Lovecraft "Beyond the Rose-Arbor" by Clark Ashton Smith "Offspring of the Grave" by Clark Ashton Smith "The Pilgrim of Eternity" by Clark Ashton Smith "The Transformation of Athanor" by Clark Ashton Smith "A Gift from the Beloved" by Clark Ashton Smith "Between Two Worlds" by Clark Ashton Smith "The Entity of the Sands" by Clark Ashton Smith "The Thing from the Antarctic" by Clark Ashton Smith "Prisoners of the Black Dimension" by Clark Ashton Smith "The Destination of Gideon Balcoth" by Clark Ashton Smith "The After-Men" by Clark Ashton Smith "The Embassy to Tilrath" by Clark Ashton Smith "The Lunar Brain" by Clark Ashton Smith "The Vapor from the Void" by Clark Ashton Smith "A "Vintage" from Atlantis" by Clark Ashton Smith "The Cairn" by Clark Ashton Smith "The River of Mystery" by Clark Ashton Smith "Men of the Macrocosm" by Clark Ashton Smith "The Lunar Path" by Clark Ashton Smith "The Scarab" by Clark Ashton Smith ""Vulthoom" " by Clark Ashton Smith "The Demon "from" Alphard" by Clark Ashton Smith "The Inverse Avatar" by Clark Ashton Smith "Hecate" by Clark Ashton Smith "The Dome in the Ice" by Clark Ashton Smith Reviews by Stephen Mariconda, Will Murray, and Robert M. Price Letters by Robert Bloch , Alan Ziebarth, Roger G. Knuth, Don Herron, David T. Pudelwitts, Jean-Luc Buard, and L. Sprague de Camp References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>64</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>April 1985</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Wars">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Wars</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A group of players take control of a great old one and their cults, and battle for power over a map . The game is usually won by completing the "doom track" each faction is composed of a great old one, eight to ten monsters, and six cultists .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sandy_Petersen"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Strategy miniature board game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Yorhi">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtinctSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Yorhi</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The were a species of native martian which appeared in the h. p. lovecraft -inspired cthulhu mythos . The possessed three flared nostrils, a skin like tough leather, and long limbs with recurved, satyr-like legs . The technology of the was of a far greater level than that of their descendants, although some still possess artefacts .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Vaults_of_Yoh-Vombis"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Necronomicon_Press">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SmallPress"/>
    <rdfs:label>Necronomicon Press</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Current Status Active 1976 Marc A. Michaud United States West Warwick, Rhode Island, USA Distribution Method Print Horror Fantasy https://necropress.com/ is a small-press publisher of H. P. Lovecraft -related content based in the United States. Overview Established in 1976 by Marc A. Michaud as an outlet for the writings of H. P. Lovecraft, over the years have expanded their scope to include other Cthulhu Mythos contributors, as well as critical works from many other authors (generally published in journal form), and at least one biography of Lovecraft's life. Selected List of Authors Published by H. P. Lovecraft Robert E. Howard Clark Ashton Smith Richard F. Searight Ramsey Campbell Hugh B. Cave Brian Lumley Brian Stableford S. T. Joshi Selected Publications Periodicals Midnight Shambler (1988-1999) Crypt of Cthulhu (1990-1999) Cthulhu Codex (1996-1999) Chapbooks The Innsmouth Heritage (1992) Ex Libris Miskatonici (1993) The Hoard of the Wizard-Beast and One Other (1994) Collections Uncollected Prose &amp; Poetry Volume 2 (1980) The Fantastic Poetry (1990) Non-Fiction An Index to the Selected Letters of H. P. Lovecraft (1980) The Chronology Out of Time (1986, Peter Cannon ) Clark Ashton Smith: Letters to H.P. Lovecraft (1987) Selected Papers on Lovecraft (1989) On the Emergence of 'Cthulhu' and Other Observations (1995) H. P. Lovecraft: A Life (1996, 2004)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://necropress.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Dice">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Dice</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Steve Jackson Games Dice Game  is a tabletop dice game by Steve Jackson Games inspired by H. P. Lovecraft 's Cthulhu Mythos . Overview A generic dice game with a Mythos skin, players take turns rolling the dice in an effort to remove all of their opponents' sanity, represented by glass beads. The game came packaged with a custom-designed 12-sided dice, 18 glass tokens and a rules sheet. Available accessories included dice in a variety of designs, materials and sizes, and a plush dice bag, but all of these - including the core set - have been discontinued. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Kim_Newman">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonFictionAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Kim Newman</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Kim James Newman Pseudonym(s) Jack Yeovil Connection to the Mythos Author Editor Birth Date 31 July 1959 London, England United Kingdom Horror Science Fiction Fantasy https://johnnyalucard.com/ Kim James Newman is a prolific British author, editor and film critic whose works fall for the most part into the science fiction, fantasy and horror genres. He has written several full-length novels under the pen name Jack Yeovil, the majority of these as tie-ins for British wargames manufacturer Games Workshop , and frequently collaborates with Stephen Jones in editorial projects. He is perhaps best known for his Anno Dracula series of novels, which imagines an alternative fictional history in which Dracula succeeded in conquering England . The series is known for featuring several characters from literature and films, including Herbert West , who appears in the second and third books, The Bloody Red Baron and Dracula Cha Cha Cha . Selected Bibliography Short Fiction "A Quarter to Three" (1988) "The Big Fish" (1993) "Another Fish Story" (2005) "Richard Riddle, Boy Detective in 'The Case of the French Spy'" (2005)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>http://example.org/lovecraft#England London</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Kim James Newman</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United Kingdom</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasPseudonymR>Jack Yeovil</ex:hasPseudonymR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://johnnyalucard.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>31 July 1959</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jacen_Burrows">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Jacen Burrows</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Artist Birth Date 11 September 1972 San Diego, California, USA United States (born 11 September 1972) is an American comic book artist. Beginning his career providing material for roleplaying games, with regards to this wiki he is most notable for the artwork he supplied for various Alan Moore Cthulhu Mythos stories. Selected Works Alan Moore's Yuggoth Cultures and Other Growths (2003) Alan Moore's The Courtyard (2003) Neonomicon (2011) Providence (2016)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA http://example.org/lovecraft#California San Diego</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>11 September 1972</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Down_in_Limbo">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Down in Limbo</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story follows young university lecturer who is finishing his dissertation on a translation of the scroll known as the r'lyeh text . Aided by the discovery of a set of undersea carvings dubbed the bimini glyphs, he visits a past-life regression channeler . There, he is regressed using lines from the original script of the scroll and remembers an existence in a world which she does not recognise . When he leaves,</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_M._Price"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Short Fiction Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1995</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Critics">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lair_of_the_NGarai">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lair of the N'Garai</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>'wolverine' is a mutant superhero comic book story which made its first appearance in wolverine annual vol 1 . Written by larry hama, it was published by marvel comics in august 1995 . 'wolverine' follows the adventures of mutant superhero wolverine .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Larry Hama</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 1995</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Forsaken_Lore">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Forsaken Lore</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Nikki Valens Fantasy Flight Games Publication Date 2014 Board game Eldritch Horror is an expansion set for the Eldritch Horror Cthulhu Mythos board game. Designed by Nikki Valens , it was released by Fantasy Flight Games in 2014 . Overview External Links About at the Fantasy Flight Games website Gallery References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2014</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Joe_Morey">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Joe Morey</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Editor Publishing house founder Chris Morey (son) Years Active 1986 - present is an editor, occasional author, and small press publishing house founder who works primarily in the horror and dark fantasy genres. He has published many Lovecraftian works through his various houses, which have included Dark Regions Press and Weird House Press , and has released works by authors such as Tim Curran , William Meikle , Sam Gafford , Michael Shea , Donald Tyson , W. H. Pugmire , and Ramsey Campbell . Selected Works Editor The Howling Terror and Other Lovecraftian Horror Stories ( Tony Richards , 2022) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasChildrenR>Chris Morey</ex:hasChildrenR>
    <ex:hasYearsActiveR>1986 - present</ex:hasYearsActiveR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Publishing house founder</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hugh_Rankin">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Hugh Rankin</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1956</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Hugh rankin was an american artist who produced illustrations for weird tales . As such, he provided the original art for dozens of stories by h. p. lovecraft . Stories originally illustrated by rankin include " the call of cthulhu ", " the curse of yig ", " the dunwich horror ", " the electric executioner ", " the last test ", " pickman's model ", " the shadow kingdom ", " the silver</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Hugh Doak Rankin</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1878</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Yagothoggua">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Yagothoggua</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Quy Notable Features The Temple of Quyagen The Tanzers, The Y'nthoggyans First appearance EXP : "The Thing in the Library" Created by Crispin Burnham &amp; E. P. Berglund - the sacred city in Quy in which the Temple of Quyagen , and the god himself, stand. It is not clear what became of the city since the Hyborian Age . The city was inhabited by a ruling priesthood, known as the Tanzers , and secretly by the Y'nthoggyans .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNotableFeatureL>The Temple of Quyagen</ex:hasNotableFeatureL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>The Tanzers</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>The Y'nthoggyans</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Quy"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>The Thing in the Library</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sean_Branney">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sean Branney</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Branney is an american actor, filmmaker, occasional author and editor . He is co-founder of the h.p. Lovecraft historical society . He is also credited with coining the name of the group .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Appreciation society founder</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Filmmaker</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#HyperboreanCycle">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#T%E2%80%99yog">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>T’yog</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>148 B.C. 173 1931 December 8</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>T'yog is a fictional character from h. p. lovecraft and hazel heald's story " out of the aeons ", an ancient priest turned into a mummy by the sight of a great old one . In the story, he is described as the high-priest of shub-niggurath, the "guardian of the copper temple of the goat with a thousand young"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Out_of_the_Aeons"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hazel_Heald"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Silent_One">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Silent One</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Deep Ones</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The silent ones were a group of creatures which appeared in mark mclaughlin 's 2004 short story " toadface " . They slept in an ancient ruined temple (said to be reminiscent of a gladiatorial arena) within the aquatic deep one city of y'ha-nthlei .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Toadface"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shrieking_Madness">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MediaAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Shrieking Madness</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The episode features many nods to both lovecraft's real life and the cthulhu mythos . The gang investigate attacks made by a fictional monster named char gar gothakon . They uncover a crazed superfan who is dressing up as one of his favorite characters to exact revenge on those who have been in any way critical of his idol .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>18 October 2010</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Witch_House">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsOriginatingFromMythosAdjacentWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Witch House</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The was located near the town of penmallow in cornwall, whose residents lived in fear of the manor . Its housekeeper was blondine, who was the latest reincarnation of the high priestess of dagoth, of the evil city of kalumesh .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Building</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Blondine</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Earth"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>Marvel Premiere Vol 1 7</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Follower_from_the_Depths">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Follower from the Depths</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Richard King Perkins, II English James Ward Kirk Publishing Lovecraft after Dark date November 2015  " " is a short Lovecraftian poem by Richard King Perkins, II , first published in the James Ward Kirk Publishing anthology Lovecraft after Dark in November 2015. It comprises four verses of five lines each.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>II</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Richard King Perkins</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_after_Dark"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtinctSpecies">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Creighton_Capshaw">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Creighton Capshaw</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Innsport Natural History Museum</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Capshaw is a fictional character created by american author brandon berntson for his lovecraft mysteries trilogy of cthulhu mythos stories . He made his debut appearance in the first book in the series, 2018's the night of dagon . Before the events of the series, capshaw had been a long-time friend of the wealthy antiquarian mordred haddonfield .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Night_of_Dagon"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brandon_Berntson"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gerald_W._Page">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gerald W. Page</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Gerald page is an american writer and editor . His oeuvre has consisted of short stories and articles related to fantasy, science fiction, mystery and horror, particularly weird horror . His first story appeared in the magazine analog in 1963 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jeb_Marsh">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Jeb Marsh</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Jeb is a character created by american author armand rosellia for his keyport cthulhu series of mythos stories . He made his first appearance in the short story " ancient " in 2012 . Upon his change, the keyport branch of the esoteric order of dagon locked him up in his secluded cottage .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ancient"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Armand_Rosamilia"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Aberration">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Aberration</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Aberrations are warped and twisted lifeforms in the fictional universes of the Dungeons &amp; Dragons and Pathfinder pencil-and-paper roleplaying games by Wizards of the Coast and Paizo Inc. Background Aberrations are creatures which are utterly alien to the cosmos and its parallel planes. In the D&amp;D multiverse, they either came from the Far Realm or were touched by it. Creatures with strange abilities, bizarre anatomies, and/or alien mindsets. Which include Lovecraftian-like aberrations like the Gibbering Mouther , the Illithids , the Aboleths , and the Beholders . Gallery of Examples of Aberrations</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Devlin_Macky">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Devlin Macky</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Macky is a private investigator in the east coast city of innsport . He made his debut appearance in the first book in the series, the night of dagon . Macky was married in the past, to a woman named veronica . Veronica was diagnosed with cancer a month after their first meeting . She died two years later, after losing her battle against the disease . Macky and his secretary millie have a complicated relationship .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCharacterMateC>Veronica</ex:hasCharacterMateC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Night_of_Dagon"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brandon_Berntson"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Alabaster_%28collection%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CaitlinRKiernanWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Alabaster (collection)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Alabaster Caitlin R. Kiernan Cover Artist Ted Naifeh English Subterranean Press Release Date August 2006 179 Preceded by Threshold Followed by Comes a Pale Rider Alabaster is a collection of short stories by Caitlin R. Kiernan featuring the character Dancy Flammarion , introduced in the novel Threshold (Roc/New American Library, 2001). It was published by Subterranean Press in 2006. Subterranean Press published a second collection of Dancy Flammarion stories, Comes a Pale Rider , in 2020. Contents "'s Preface" (essay) " Les Fleurs Empoisonnées " (2002) " The Well of Stars and Shadow " (2001) " Waycross " (2003) " Alabaster " (2006) " Bainbridge " (2006) " Highway 97 " (2006) Afterword: On the Road to Jefferson" (essay) In internal chronological order "The Well of Stars and Shadow" "Highway 97" "Bainbridge" "Alabaster" "Waycross" " Les Fleurs Empoisonnées "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Caitlin_R._Kiernan"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>179</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 2006</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
