  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Necronomicon:_Book_of_the_Dead">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleFilmAndTelevisionAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Necronomicon: Book of the Dead</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Running time 96 minutes english $4,000,000 (estimated) is a 1993 horror anthology film based on the works of howard phillips lovecraft . Consists of adaptations of three of lovecraft’s works - " the rats in the walls ", " cool air " and " the whisperer in darkness " each of which featured a different at the helm - connected by an original wraparound story .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasWriterW>http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft</ex:hasWriterW>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cool_Air"/>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Rats_in_the_Walls"/>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Whisperer_in_Darkness"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ntse-Kaambl">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ElderGods"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>N'tse-Kaambl</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Elder Gods</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>The priests of Yuth (chief worshippers)</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Malleus monstrorum is an elder god from the expanded cthulhu mythos . She first appeared in gary myers' short story " the house of the worm " .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Yohk the Necromancer Xiurhn H. P. Lovecraft's Dreamlands The Encyclopedia Cthulhiana Malleus Monstrorum Haiyore! Nyaruko-san</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The House of the Worm</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gary_Myers"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Apocalypse_Codex">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Apocalypse Codex</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"followed by the rhesus chart" is the fourth novel in the laundry files series . It blends elements of the cthulhu mythos with spy fiction and bureaucratic office comedy . Won the locus award for best fantasy novel in 2013 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Charles_Stross"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>336</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 2012</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pallid_Mask">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Pallid Mask</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>First appearance ADJ : The King in Yellow Created by Robert W. Chambers The also known as the Phantom of Truth or The Stranger is a humanoid emissary of Hastur . Background The is a 6 foot tall slender figure in a voluminous yellow robe who walks around with a staff and has a genderless, featureless white mask covering his face. For millennia it has visited cities known for their nihilism and decadence to determine if they should be consumed by Carcosa . The character takes its name from a being referred to in Robert W. Chambers ' The King in Yellow . Behind the Mythos The character was expanded upon in 2017 "Black Stars Beckon" by Jim Groves for Paizo Inc's Pathfinder roleplaying game.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_King_in_Yellow"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_W._Chambers"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#To_H.P.L.:_Christmas">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>To H.P.L.: Christmas</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Richard F. Searight United States English Necronomicon Press The Brain in the Jar and Others date October 1992 " " is a short poem by the late American and correspondent of Lovecraft 's Richard F. Searight . Overview The poem was presumably written as a Christmas message from Richard F. Searight to Howard Phillips Lovecraft. History "" was posthumously published in the collection of Searight's own works The Brain in the Jar and Others by Necronomicon Press in October 1992.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_F._Searight"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Others</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The Brain in the Jar</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 1992</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sally_Compton">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sally Compton</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Grandma Compton</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as grandma compton arkansas offspring clyde compton joe compton human first appearance hpl : " the curse of yig " is a fictional character created by h. p. lovecraft who appears in " the mound " and " the curse of yig "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Mound"/>
    <ex:hasCharacterMateC>Joe Compton</ex:hasCharacterMateC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Curse_of_Yig"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#An_Arkham_Witch">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>An Arkham Witch</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here Ron Shiflet United States English Publication date September 1998 " " is a poem by American Ron Shiflet . Synopsis The nine verses tell the story of a witch named Mattie Stiles who fends off an angry mob of Arkham citizenry using dark magic. Publication History "" was published in Nightscapes in September 1998, and again in Cthulhu Cultus #14 in 1999.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>September 1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sheila">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sheila</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Sheila Williams</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Chosen One</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as the chosen one is a hero in the fictional army of darkness universe who was created in 1992 by sam raimi . First appeared in the 1992 comedy-fantasy film army of darkness .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Army_of_Darkness"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sam_Raimi"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Yarkdao">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Yarkdao</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sociocultural Characteristics The Planet Tond Real World Information First appearance EXP : " The Inhabitant of the Lake " EXP : " A Madness from the Vaults " Created by Ramsey Campbell The are an extraterrestrial species which appear in the expanded Cthulhu Mythos . Biology and Sociocultural Characteristics Of their physical attributes, nothing is known about the save that their bodies are humanoid and they possess a pair of retractable ears. They are an intelligent race, being the rulers of their world - the planet Tond - and are a polytheistic people, their priests of Chig conducting rituals which last for several years. All that is known of the comes from writings in the occult volumes of The Revelations of Gla'aki . Behind the Mythos The were created by Ramsey Campbell and introduced in his short story " The Inhabitant of the Lake ".</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Madness_from_the_Vaults"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Inhabitant_of_the_Lake"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ramsey_Campbell"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Siren">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SpeciesIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdfs:label>Siren</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sirens are a species in greek mythology. The marvel adaptation were created in 1970 by roy thomas and sal buscema . Making their first appearance in issue #29 of the 1st volume of the sub-mariner comic book series .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Sub-Mariner Vol 1 #29</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Roy_Thomas"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Sal Buscema</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Repairer_of_Reputations">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Repairer of Reputations</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Robert w. chambers is a short story published by robert w. chambers in 1895 . The story is set in 1925, 30 years in the future of the story's publication . The protagonist, one hildred castaigne, is a young man whose personality changed drastically .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_W._Chambers"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Mist">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalLocations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsOriginatingFromMythosInspiredWorks"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Mist</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Eerie, impenetrable fog is home to various species of otherworldly, grosteque creatures and deadly predators from another dimension . Novella and film suggest the mysterious mist is from another world that accidentally spilled into ours due to a failed experiment . Inhabitants is home to a diverse array of creatures that rely heavily on their sense of smell to hunt .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Grimoire_of_the_Necronomicon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Grimoire of the Necronomicon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Donald Tyson English Genre(s) Non-fiction Llewellyn Publications Release Date 2008 is a book by Canadian and esotericist Donald Tyson which purports to be a "how-to" guide to performing practical ritual magic using elements derived from the works of H. P. Lovecraft . First published in August 2008 by Llewellyn Publications, both paperback and digital editions were released, and foreign- translations have also been issued . Overview References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Donald_Tyson"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2008</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Groth-Golka">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Groth-Golka</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Fishers from Outside</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Great Old Ones</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Demon Bird-God</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"the gods of bal-sagoth" is a creature invented by robert e. howard . Originally depicted as a giant flightless bird worshipped as a deity . In the expanded mythos, lin carter identified it as the lord of the fishers from outside .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isLocatedAtC>Beneath Antarctica</ex:isLocatedAtC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Gods_of_Bal-Sagoth"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_E._Howard"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Haggopian">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#BrianLumleyWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Haggopian</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story was written in mid-1970 and first published in the june 1973 issue of the magazine of fantasy &amp; science fiction . It was later incorporated as the sixth chapter of his novel the return of the deep ones .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Science Fiction</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The Magazine of Fantasy</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>June 1973</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>1970</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Yule_Horror_%28Nick_OGorman%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleComicBookAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Yule Horror (Nick O'Gorman)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Yule Horror H. P. Lovecraft (original poem) Nick O'Gorman (adaptation) Nick O'Gorman English Target Destroyed Comics Release Date 2023 Yule Horror is a comic book adaptation of the H. P. Lovecraft poem " Festival ". Adapted by Nick O'Gorman who also provided the artwork, it was published through his independent label Target Destroyed Comics in 2023. The comic was later included in the collection HPL Vol. 1: Comic Adaptations of the Works of HP Lovecraft , also by Target Destroyed, in May 2023.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nick_OGorman"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2023</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pagan_Publishing">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DefunctRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Publishers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Pagan Publishing</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Armitage house was a small publishing company whose focus was on cthulhu mythos games and related fiction . As it grew and expanded it began to produce actual sourcebooks for the game, perhaps the most famous being the delta green supplement . As of july 2021 's website is no longer available to access, indicating that the company has ceased trading .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gathering_Forces">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gathering Forces</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>" drawing no. 8wunderwaffe" is a short story set in the dystopian setting of punktown . It was first published in the call of cthulhu roleplaying game supplement punktown by miskatonic river press /chronicle city in 2018 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jeffrey_Thomas"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Punktown"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Punktown"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2018</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Curse_Of_The_Tillinghasts">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Curse Of The Tillinghasts</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A young woman discovers that two scions of the tillinghast family have been continuing the experiments of their ancestor crawford . She is able to turn the tables on the sister and cast her into the beyond with her previous victims . The brother subsequently repents and tries to make amends for his prior actions .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Michael Sheehan. Jr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Abominations: 24 Tales of the Cthulhu Mythos</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Horrors</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 2018</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Of_Gods_%26_Aliens">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Of Gods &amp; Aliens</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ran Cartwright Cover Artist Angela DeRaimo United States English H'chtelegoth Press Release Date October 2011 370 is a collection of short stories by American Ran Cartwright . Overview First published by H'chtelegoth Press in October 2011, contains 30 stories and runs to 370 in total. Included in the collection are the complete contents of Cartwright's 2006 chapbook, Tales of Nephren Ka (" The Circle ", " The Chronicler ", " The Coming ", " The Candidate "). Contents 's Note "Fires" "The Starlet and the Fishman" "Down on the Farm" "Nitiqret's Necklace" " De Marigny's Clock " " The Scuttler in the Dark " "A Fisherman's Tale" " Return to the Mountains of Madness " " Azathoth Awakening " " Arkham Shadows " "The Dia Tesseron Destroyed" "The Spider and the Fly" "The House in the Valley" " Innsmouth Harvest " " Y'ha-nthlei Rising " " " " The Circle " " The Chronicler " " The Coming " " The Candidate " "Hagif" "Damascus" "Shar-i-Gholghola" "Ubar" " Z'toggua " "Shog-e'Yahg" "Ch'g-Ghral" "A Festival of Winds" " Rhan-Tegoth " "Fire and Ice"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ran_Cartwright"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>370</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 2011</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Amblers_Inspiration">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#BrianLumleyWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ambler's Inspiration</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A horror writer visits a fellow and finds that the question "where do you get your ideas?" Has a horrific answer . The story mentions oakdeene sanatorium, which appears repeatedly in lumley's mythos tales . It compares its central character, a writer of weird fiction, to h.p. Lovecraft .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Caller_of_The_Black"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1971</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nyaghoggua">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nyaghoggua</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Kraken Within</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as The Kraken Within First appearance CIRCLE : " The Abyss " CIRCLE : " " Created by Robert A. W. Lowndes is a being served by the priests of Arkya , mentioned in the Song of Yste , a "pre-glacial" text. Some scholars suggest that is merely another name for Tsathoggua (who?) . Behind the Mythos The name was first mentioned in the short story " The Abyss " by Robert A. W. Lowndes . References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Abyss"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_A._W._Lowndes"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosInspiredWorks">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Unbound,_Vol._2">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Unbound, Vol. 2</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Michael Dashow English Permuted Press Release Date July 2009 276 Preceded by Cthulhu Unbound, Vol. 1 Followed by Cthulhu Unbound 3 is an anthology of Lovecraftian fiction edited Thomas Brannan and John Sunseri. Running to a total of 276 , it was published by Permuted Press in July 2009 . Overview Contents "Passing Down" by Inez Schaechterle " The Tenants of Ladywell Manor " by Willie Meikle "The Hunters Within the Corners" by Douglas P. Wojtowicz "Surely You Joust" by Patrick Thomas "References in Cthonic, Eldritch, Roiling Creations Are Recondite" by Warren Tusk "New Fish" by Kiwi Courters "Tomb on a Dead Moon" by Tim Curran "The Long, Deep Dream" by Peter Clines "Santiago Contra el Culto de Cthulhu" by Mark Zirbel "Stomach Acid" by David Conyers and Brian M. Sammons "Sleeping Monster Futures" by Brandon Alspaugh "Nemo at R'lyeh" by Joshua Reynolds "What's a Few Tentacles Among Friends?" by Sheila Crosby "An Incident Occurring in the Huachuca Mountains, West of Tombstone" by Gary Vehar "Abomination with Rice" by Rhys Hughes References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>276</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 2009</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Todd_H._C._Fischer">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Todd H. C. Fischer</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Editor Author Artist Canadian Horror Science Fiction http://todd-fischer.com/ is a Canadian author, editor and artist whose focus lies within the science fiction and horror genres. He has also contributed a number of works to the expanded Cthulhu Mythos inspired by the fiction of H. P. Lovecraft and others. Selected Works Editor Imelod (17 issues, 1996-2000) The Ancient Track (2 issues, 1998-1999) Short Fiction " The Revelations of Alexander Dyer " (1997) Artwork Imelod (2 issues, 1997-2000) Avatars of the Old Ones (1999)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>Canadian</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://todd-fischer.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Spawn_of_Hastur">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Spawn of Hastur</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Real World Information First appearance EXP : Ye Booke of Monstres II Created by Scott David Aniolowski The are a fictional race created by Chaosium in 1995 for the Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game. Background A race knowledgeable in magic, the are octopoidal creatures with hideous faces. They are an amphibious race with a limited ability to fly and sometimes are seen with Hastur in the Lake of Hali . Victims are crushed by their tentacles and/or drawn to their bodies, and swallowed whole. Behind the Mythos The were created by games designer Scott David Aniolowski for the Call of Cthulhu monster manual Ye Booke of Monstres II .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ye_Booke_of_Monstres_II"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scott_David_Aniolowski"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Gods_of_Peg%C4%81na">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Gods of Pegāna</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Followed by time and the gods is a book by the anglo-irish fantasy writer lord dunsany . The book is organized as a series of sketches about an imaginary pantheon . The concept was a key influence on h.p. Lovecraft .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lord_Dunsany"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>Republic of Ireland</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Fantasy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>94</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1905</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
