  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Altuas">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RegionsTerritories"/>
    <rdfs:label>Altuas</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Earth Serpent People First appearance EXP : " The Stalker in the Snows " Created by James Ambuehl is a fictional region created by American author James Ambuehl for his 1997 short story " The Stalker in the Snows ". Overview is an ancient land which was old even when Atlantis itself was young. The villages of Zendosh and Thom-Raballos, and the wind-swept Plateau of Shung all lie in . All in the ancient land of feared Parthias the Conqueror. It seems likely that it is the same as Altuan , and possibly an alternative name for Shuggon although it is not clear that it is a Serpent People colony.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Serpent People</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Earth"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Stalker_in_the_Snows"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Caller_of_The_Black_%28collection%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#BrianLumleyWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CrossroadPress"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Caller of The Black (collection)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The caller of the black is an arkham house collection of short stories by brian lumley . It was followed in 1977 by a second collection of lumley's work, the horror at oakdeene and others . The stories in it are original to the volume, with the exceptions of "the cyprus shell,billy's oak," and "an item of supporting evidence"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1971</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tla-yub">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>T'la-yub</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>K'n-yanian HPL : The Mound Created by H. P. Lovecraft is a fictional character from " The Mound ", a revision story written by H. P. Lovecraft for Zealia Bishop . She is a woman from the fictional underground realm of K'n-yan , who falls in love with Zamacona , the story's protagonist. She is described as "a noblewoman of moderate beauty and of at least average intelligence".</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Mound"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jason_Andrew">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Jason Andrew</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Editor Fresno, California, USA United States https://jasonbandrew.com/ is an American author, editor and games designer who has written several works of Lovecraftian and Cthulhu Mythos fiction, and also edited the anthology Apotheosis: Stories of Human Survival After the Rise of the Elder Gods . His gaming credits include extensive work for White Wolf, including bot tabletop and live-action supplements for Vampire and Werewolf , and Shadowrun . His Mythos fiction has been seen in anthologies such as Chaosium 's Atomic-Age Cthulhu and Frontier Cthulhu . Selected Works Editor Apotheosis: Stories of Human Survival After the Rise of the Elder Gods (2015) Author " Fear and Loathing in Innsmouth: Richard Nixon's Revenge " (2013) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA http://example.org/lovecraft#California Fresno</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://jasonbandrew.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Arkham</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAccessibilityL>Train line to Newburyport Bus Route to Newburyport via Innsmouth</ex:hasAccessibilityL>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Created by h. p. lovecraft is a fictional town set in the us state of massachusetts . It frequently appears in his horror fiction and in the work of other writers in the cthulhu mythos . Is the namesake of house , the publishing house that promoted lovecraft's work after his death .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL>Herbert West--Reanimator The Unnamable The Festival The Silver Key The Colour Out of Space The Dunwich Horror The Whisperer in Darkness The Shadow Over Innsmouth The Dreams in the Witch House Through the Gates of the Silver Key The Thing on the Doorstep The Shadow Out of Time The Shambler from the Stars</ex:hasAppearanceL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Massachusetts"/>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Essex County</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Picture_in_the_House"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dunwich_Horror_%28DART_production%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleAudioAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Dunwich Horror (DART production)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"the dunwich horror" is an audio adaptation of the short story of the same name by american fan organisation the h.p. Lovecraft historical society . Designed to listen like a 1930s radio broadcast, "the dunwich horror" is part of the historical society's extensive dark adventure radio theatre series . Running to a total of 75 minutes, it is released in both compact disc and digital download formats .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dunwich_Horror"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Salton_Sea_Monster">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Salton Sea Monster</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a Lovecraftian reptilian entity from the fictional universe of Hellboy . He was one of the Ogdru Hem , children of the Ogdru Jahad . Originally his being was seen as an herald of the end-times, but it was quickly worshipped as a god by humans . One of these key worshippers was Paul Nedin, a former Christian pastor from Atlanta, who formed the Church of the Living Earth.</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#August_Derleth:_posthumous_collaboration_controversy">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AugustDerlethWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>August Derleth: posthumous collaboration controversy</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>After H. P. Lovecraft 's death, August Derleth wrote and published numerous stories under his own and Lovecraft's bylines, described as "posthumous collaborations" . They were mostly based on story ideas from Lovecraft's Commonplace Book , and occasionally incorporated fragments written by Lovecraft, but overwhelmingly consisted of Derleth's own prose. Lovecraft scholar S. T. Joshi commented: The Sixteen Stories The 16 stories S. T. Joshi has separated apart, which have been in the past uncritically attributed to both Derleth and Lovecraft as co-authors, or even to Lovecraft alone, are The Lurker at the Threshold (1945) " The Survivor " (1954) " The Ancestor " (1957) " The Gable Window " (1957) " The Lamp of Alhazred " (1957) " The Peabody Heritage " (1957) " The Shadow Out of Space " (1957) " Wentworth's Day " (1957) " The Fisherman of Falcon Point " (1959) " The Shuttered Room " (1959) " Witches' Hollow " (1962) " The Shadow in the Attic " (1964) " The Dark Brotherhood " (1966) " The Horror from the Middle Span " (1974) " Innsmouth Clay " (1974) " The Watchers Out of Time " (1974) S. T. Joshi's scholarship confirmed what was known privately for many years, that none of the above works are by H. P. Lovecraft. All of them are properly to be considered works of August Derleth. This has no relevancy to any criticism or praise of the stories on their merits; this is a matter entirely related to authorship.</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Mirror_of_Nitocris">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#BrianLumleyWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Mirror of Nitocris</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"an item of supporting evidence" is a cthulhu mythos story by brian lumley . It introduces the character of occultist and chronicler, henri-laurent de marigny . De marigny acquires a legendary mirror that once belonged to the monstrous egyptian queen .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Titus_Crow"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Caller_of_The_Black"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1971</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Black_Sutra">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Black Sutra</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Walter C. DeBill, Jr. English Mythos Books Release Date September 2006 281 is a collection of Lovecraftian fiction by Walter C. DeBill, Jr . Running to a total of 281 and featuring an introduction by leading H. P. Lovecraft scholar Robert M. Price , it was published by Mythos Books in September 2006. Overview Contents Introduction by Robert M. Price "Prologue: Collector's item" "From the Sea" "Night Sounds" "Ngyr-Khorath" "In 'Ygiroth" "The History of the Great Race" "Red-Litten Yoth" "Blue-Litten K'N-Yan" "Homecoming" "What Lurks Among the Dunes" "R'lyeh" "Predator" "The Lure of Leng" " Where Yidhra Walks " "Fragment from the Necronomicon" "Perilous Legacy" "Heart Attack" "The Horror from Yith" "A Movement in the Grass" "The Oldest Dreamer" "The Barrett Horror" "He Who Comes at the Noontime" "The Feast" "Control" "That Which Devours" "The Bookseller's Second Wife" "Solar Pons &amp; the Cthulhu Mythos" "Lament" "How Nohoch Koos Met the Xtaaby" "Natty" "What Sort of Man" "The Changeling"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Walter C. DeBill. Jr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>281</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>September 2006</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Eibon_and_the_Lord_of_Bala-Norath">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Eibon and the Lord of Bala-Norath</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here Ron Shiflet United States English Publication date July 1998 " " is a poem by American Ron Shiflet . Synopsis The Lord of Bala-Norath calls upon the sorcerer Eibon for aid when his kingdom comes under siege by an evil tyrant-king wishing to steal away the Lord's daughter. Publication History "" was first published in the online zine Nightscapes in July 1998, and later appeared in the fanzine Dark Legacy #6 of April 2000.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>July 1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
