    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Literary Copernicus</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Fritz Leiber United States English (original) German (translation) Genre(s) Non-fiction Arkham House Something About Cats and Other Pieces date 1949 " " is a short non-fiction piece by and correspondent of Howard Phillips Lovecraft 's Fritz Leiber , concerning the work of the former. History Something About Cats and Other Pieces (1949) Essays Lovecraftian (1976) H. P. Lovecraft: Four Decades of Criticism (1980, 1981) Über H. P. Lovecraft (1984) Fafhrd &amp; Me (1990) Lovecraft Remembered (1998) Discovering H. P. Lovecraft (2001) Fritz Leiber and H. P. Lovecraft: Writers of the Dark (2003, 2004)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fritz_Leiber"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>German</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Something_About_Cats"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Other Pieces</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1949</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dont_Monkey_With_a_Monkey">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Don't Monkey With a Monkey</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Looking for darla: stories of mythos noir was published in the collection looking for darla: stories of mythos noir by elder signs press in september 2008 . The story is a tongue-in-cheek cthulhu mythos short story by american author ron shiflet .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Looking_for_Darla:_Stories_of_Mythos_Noir"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>September 2008</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Harold_S._Farnese">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSFriendsAndAcquaintances"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Harold S. Farnese</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1945</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Composer who set some of Lovecraft’s poems to music. He corresponded sporadically with Lovecraft from 1932 to 1933. It was he who provided August Derleth with the spurious “Black Magic” quotation attributed to Lovecraft.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1885</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Death_Wore_Greasepaint">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Death Wore Greasepaint</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Historyis a short cthulhu mythos story by american and comic book creator josh finney . A mentally-unstable clown begins a ritual while performing live on local television before killing himself on air and dooming his town .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Josh_Finney"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Whispers_from_the_Abyss"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2014</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#That_at_Which_Dogs_Howl_and_Other_Lovecraftian_Stories">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>That at Which Dogs Howl and Other Lovecraftian Stories</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Edward M. Erdelac Cover Artist Thomas Brown English Genre(s) Lovecraftian fiction Raven Canticle Press Release Date October 2023 309  is a collection of Lovecraftian fiction by American Edward M. Erdelac . Running to a total of 309 and featuring cover art by Thomas Brown, it was published by Raven Canticle Press in October 2023; both print and digital editions were released . Contents (Note: Contents taken from Amazon's "Read sample" feature, retrieved on 16 August 2024) "The Wood Of Ephraim" "The Lady of the Amorous City" "By Unknown Hands" "Brown Jenkin's Reckoning" "That at Which Dogs Howl" "It Came to Modesto" "Sneak Preview" "The Crawlin' Chaos Blues" "Five To One" " The Boonieman " " Black Tallow " "Anaparagogi" "The Theophany Of Nyx" " The Allclear " Trivia The title is from a quote by H. P. Lovecraft , "We shall see that at which dogs howl in the dark, and that at which cats prick up their ears after midnight." References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Edward_M._Erdelac"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Lovecraftian fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>309</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 2023</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#He">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>He</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Wtis a short story by american horror writer h. p. lovecraft . Written august 1925, it was first published in weird tales , september 1926 . The story tells of an unnamed poet narrator, who has moved to new york city .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_A._Mihalko">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Mark A. Mihalko</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author United States Horror https://markmihalko.wixsite.com/nightstalker is an American author and avionics technician who has written a handful of pieces set in the Cthulhu Mythos inspired by the works of H. P. Lovecraft . These have appeared in collection such as Lovecraft after Dark (2015, James Ward Kirk Publishing) and Weirdbook Annual 2 (2019, Wildside Press ). Selected Bibliography Poems " Hear My Call " (2015) " Ascend " (2019) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://markmihalko.wixsite.com/nightstalker</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hippolyte_Le_Sorcier">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Hippolyte Le Sorcier</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Relatives Gilles Grenier (ancestor) Clark Ashton Smith (descendant) First appearance HPL : Selected Letters of H. P. Lovecraft Created by H. P. Lovecraft is a character created by H. P. Lovecraft , who included him in the family tree of Azathoth in a 1933 letter to James F. Morton . Le Sorcier is depicted as a son or descendant of " Gilles Grenier , Lord of Averoigne ", and an ancestor of Lovecraft's friend Clark Ashton Smith . It's unknown if Lovecraft intended this character to be related to Charles Le Sorcier , the titular villain from his short story " The Alchemist ". In French, "Le Sorcier" means "The Sorcerer", most likely intended as an epithet, as opposed to a surname. In "The Alchemist", Charles Le Sorcier is the son of Michel Mauvais ("mauvais" being a French word for bad or evil), and is specifically noted to have no known descendants.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Selected_Letters_of_H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham:_Reanimated">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Arkham: Reanimated</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>168 preceded by the night of dagon followed by the lurker at the threshold is a cthulhu mythos novel by american brandon berntson . It was independently published in february 2020; both paperback and digital editions were released . Private investigator devlin macky and his secretary/romantic interest millie von clydesburgh decide to take a romantic day trip into the nearby countryside . Unfortunately for them, a storm moves</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brandon_Berntson"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>168</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Lovecraft_Mysteries"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>February 2020</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Great_One">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Great One</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Outer Gods</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>God of Earth</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The great ones are entities created by the late american author howard phillips lovecraft . They are not as powerful as the great old ones and are not even as intelligent as most humans . However, they are protected by the outer gods , particularly nyarlathotep .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dream-Quest_of_Unknown_Kadath"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Other_Gods"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fate_of_Cthulhu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Fate of Cthulhu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Fate is a roleplaying game which was published by evilhat productions in 2019 . It utilises the -agnostic fate roleplaying ruleset, which emphasises roleplaying over rolling dice . Premise involves players taking the roles of characters from the present day as well as time travellers from the future . They must make a stand against the coming apocalypse .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2019</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Vaults_of_Yoh-Vombis">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ClarkAshtonSmithWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Vaults of Yoh-Vombis</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>" the dweller in the gulf " is a sci-fi horror story by . Clark ashton smith . It is set on the planet mars and introduces the . Aihai species, which would appear again in " the dweller in the gulf " and " vulthoom " together, these three tales form a loose trilogy, sometimes referred to as . Smith's "mars cycle"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>May 1932</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
