  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Glynda">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Glynda</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Her blood runs cold is a fictional character created by josh finney for his casefile: arkham mythos stories . She is a beautiful woman whose body has been modified by the outer god shub-niggurath to make her more appealing to the opposite sex . As a former devotee of shub-niggurath, possesses certain magical powers, and her abilities specialise in protective invocations . She is currently in a relationship with private eye hank flynn</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Casefile:_Arkham-_Her_Blood_Runs_Cold"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Casefile:_Arkham-_Nightmare_on_the_Canvas"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Josh_Finney"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#hasChildR">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label>hasChildR</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <owl:inverseOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#hasParent"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Britannica">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Britannica</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>176 is an adventure sourcebook for chaosium 's call of cthulhu horror roleplaying game . Contains five stand-alone scenarios set in england, covering a range of time periods . Each scenario was contributed by a different and comes complete with player handouts and a set of pre-generated characters .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>176</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2009</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#On_the_Sending_Out_of_the_Soul">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosBooksFictional"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdfs:label>On the Sending Out of the Soul</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>United States (presumed) English Publication Date 1793 (presumed) Real World Information First appearance CIRCLE : " Hydra " Created by Henry Kuttner is an occult text created by American author Henry Kuttner for his 1939 short story " Hydra ". Description A 4" by 5" pamphlet running to eight pages, contains seven pages of "banal sophisms of mysticism" with the last page giving instructions on how to project one's astral self. The instructional is deceptive, however, with those who follow the directions causing the beheading of others who are then fed upon by the extra-dimensional entity dubbed the Hydra , their souls forever trapped in a state of extreme anguish. History was published in Salem in 1793 by worshipers of the Hydra, although whether it originated here is unclear. All copies of it are presumed destroyed in the modern age.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnA rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hydra"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByA rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Henry_Kuttner"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Ice-Demon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ClarkAshtonSmithWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HyperboreanCycle"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Ice-Demon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A hunter named quanga, along with two jewelers named . Hoom feethos and eibur tsanth, travels north of his native land of . Iqqua, into the frozen mhu thulan . They find an ice cave in which quanga's late brother, iluac, claimed that he had found the bodies of haalor's men sealed within the translucent walls of ice .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hyperborean_Cycle"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>April 1933</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Maker_of_Moons">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Maker of Moons</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Fantasy short story introduces the mystical city of yian . The city would be incorporated into the cthulhu mythos by h.p. Lovecraft . A goldsmith finds a strange creature crawling out of his pocket .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Laban_Shrewsbury">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Laban Shrewsbury</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Miskatonic_University"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Professor is a character created by august derleth as the main protagonist for his the trail of cthulhu series of short stories . He is a member of the faculty at miskatonic university . For years, he has dedicated himself to the study of the cthulhu mythic cycle, learning astral projection and how to summon the byakhee .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The Watcher from the Sky Something in Wood The Gorge Beyond Salapunco The Keeper of the Key The Black Island October Surprise</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_House_on_Curwen_Street"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#August_Derleth"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Saturn_%28god%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CharactersIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Saturn (god)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>The Roman Pantheon of Gods</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Cronus</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Saturn (god) Also known as Cronus Roman Mythology Relatives Uranus Gee Jove Pluto Multiple Children The Roman Pantheon of Gods Earthly God First appearance HPL : " Simplicity: A Poem " Created by H. P. Lovecraft Saturn (also known as Cronus ) is a god in a human form, first appearing in the H. P. Lovecraft poem " Simplicity: A Poem "'. He is a god in Ancient Roman mythology and is known for devouring his children.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Simplicity: A Poem</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Klosmiebhyx">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Klosmiebhyx</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>First appearance EXP : " October Surprise " Created by Kevin L. O'Brien is the daughter of Cxaxukluth and sister of Zstylzhemghi , Ghizguth and Hzioulquoigmnzhah . Born in a black star in the center of a grand nebula she travelled with Cxaxukluth and most of her relations to Yuggoth . While there she and Hzioulquoigmnzhah produced Ysbaddaden and Scathach . Behind the Mythos made her only appearance in the short story " October Surprise " by Kevin L. O'Brien and was never described.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#October_Surprise"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Kevin_L._OBrien"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Innsmouth_Free_Press">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SmallPress"/>
    <rdfs:label>Innsmouth Free Press</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Current Status Active 2009 Silvia Moreno-Garcia Canada Vancouver, British Columbia Key People Paula R. Stiles (editor) Weird fiction Horror https://innsmouthfreepress.com/ is a Canadian micro-publisher which specialises in the horror and weird fiction genres. Overview First established by Silvia Moreno-Garcia to publish Innsmouth Magazine (at the time simply titled ), it later moved on to a small number of Lovecraftian and horror anthologies and novels. As of 2022, they are no longer accepting books for review. Selected Publications Periodicals Innsmouth Magazine (2009-2014) Anthologies Historical Lovecraft (2011) Future Lovecraft (2011) She Walks in Shadows (2015)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://innsmouthfreepress.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Call_of_Cthulhu_%282005_film%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleFilmAndTelevisionAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Call of Cthulhu (2005 film)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The call of cthulhu is a 2005 independent silent film adaptation . It is the first film adaptation of the famous lovecraft story . The film uses mythoscope, a blend of vintage and modern filming techniques . The narrator dies peacefully in his sleep, rather than being summoned upon the mysterious death of his great-uncle .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sean_Branney"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>H.P. Lovecraft</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2005 06 June</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#diedOnR">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#FunctionalProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label>diedOnR</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chung_Ga">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Chung Ga</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaugnar_Faugn"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Chaugnar faugn is the high-priest of the cult of chaugnar faugn in the plateau of tsang . Has lived in the uk for eight years and graduated in the university of oxford . Has little esteem for the other cultists, whom he refers dismissively as "filthy primitives"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_from_the_Hills"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Frank_Belknap_Long"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tinfoil_Dossier">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CaitlinRKiernanWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#StoryCycles"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tinfoil Dossier</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The series comprises agents of dreamland , black helicopters and the tindalos asset . Each book tells a self-contained story, but they are interlinked by the rival agencies that try to contain or exploit outbreaks of unnatural horror . Certain characters appear in more than one book, particularly the signalman .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Caitlin_R._Kiernan"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chronicles_of_the_Cthulhu_Codex_4">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Chronicles of the Cthulhu Codex 4</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Chronicles of the Cthulhu Codex #4 Various Cover Artist Gregorio Montejo English Fungoid Press Release Date February 1987 60 Preceded by January 1987 Followed by January 1989 Chronicles of the Cthulhu Codex #4 is an issue of the sporadically-released fanzine Chronicles of the Cthulhu Codex , edited by Pierre Comtois and published by Fungoid Press in February 1987. Cover art was supplied by Gregorio Montejo. This issue was the last to be edited by Comtois until the final issue more than thirteen years later in December 2000. Contents " Innsmouth Over the Sea " by Ken Stone "Of Aslab Riddle?" by Ken Stone "What the Sea Gives Up" by Pierre Comtois "The End" by David Daniel "The Kraulshavn Fragment" by uncredited "The Resurrection of Kzadool-Ra" by Henry J. Vester, III "Monster" by R. J. Zimmerman</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>60</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>February 1987</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gates">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dimensions"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gates</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The are incredibly vast levels of existence. The exact number of is not known, only that there is a "multiplicity" of them . Past the ultimate gate lies the ultimate abyss, and is the home of yog-sothoth itself .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Guardians_of_Time">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Guardians of Time</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Great_Old_One"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Six-Fold God Enlighteners The Council of Guardians</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The , are a group of great old ones created by the bbc in 1966 for doctor who . They are masters of reality and embody various aspects of the cosmos . They are a pantheon within a pantheon, the upper echelon of the greater old ones .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Celestial Toymaker</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Brian Hayles</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Ghouls_Portrait">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Ghoul's Portrait</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A talented thief steals a portrait of the god mordiggian . Its priests dispatch amina algol, a woman raised with ghouls, and her adoptive siblings arif and bera to retrieve the piece .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Joshua Reynolds</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pickmans_Gallery"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2018</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fitting_Punishment">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleComicBookAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Fitting Punishment</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft (original story) English The Vault of Horror #16 Media type Comic book date December 1950 " " is a short story which appeared in issue #16 of comic book The Vault of Horror in December 1950. It is an adaptation of H. P. Lovecraft ’s 1925 piece " In the Vault ".</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The Vault of Horror #16</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 1950</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gilles_Grenier">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gilles Grenier</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Gilles grenier is a fictional sorcerer created by clark ashton smith for his short story, " the mandrakes " . In the story, and his wife sabine are 15th century sorcerers who move to the french province of averoigne .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCharacterMateC>Sabine</ex:hasCharacterMateC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Mandrakes"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Xiurhn">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Xiurhn</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A consort named thish arrived after traveling a long way to vornai seeking the dark jewel of mhor . Thish stole the talisman and framed a witch for the crime . He found the dark jewel and met the amorphous who recognized the talisman .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gary_Myers"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The House of the Worm</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1975</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sand-dweller">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sand-dweller</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Real World Information First appearance AWD : " The Gable Window " Created by H. P. Lovecraft &amp; August Derleth A is a fictional creature created by August Derleth in " The Gable Window ", a " posthumous collaboration " with H. P. Lovecraft . They resemble "emaciated, grit-encrusted humans with koala-like heads. They have been featured as a monster in the Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game. Gallery References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Gable_Window"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#August_Derleth"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#J._Alan_Russell">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>J. Alan Russell</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Artist United States https://www.merzo.net/ContactUs.htm is an American artist. Notable works Russell's Guide to Interdimensional Entities (with B. A. Lee ) Russell's Guide to Interdimensional Entities, Artefacts and Incantations (with B. A. Lee) See Also Images by</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://www.merzo.net/ContactUs.htm</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chris_Spivey">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Chris Spivey</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Games designer United States African American is an African American games designer and war veteran. His gaming credits include work on Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu , Pelgrane Press 's Cthulhu Confidential and Trail of Cthulhu , Onyx Path Publishing's Scion , and White Wolf Publishing's World of Darkness roleplaying games. Spivey is an advocate for greater diversity in the roleplaying games community, a culture he has been passionately involved in for nearly three decades . Selected Works Call of Cthulhu Harlem Unbound (2017, 2020) Call of Cthulhu (2018 Starter Set) Trail of Cthulhu Out of the Woods (2017) Cthulhu Confidential Cthulhu Confidential (2017) Even Death Can Die (2023) One For the Money The Shadow Over Washington Scion Masks of the Mythos (2022, developer, contributor) External Links at Goodreads at RPGGeek References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Malachai_Farrow">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Malachai Farrow</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>1926 18th October</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Malachai james farrow was a soldier who served in the pancho villa expedition of 1916-17 alongside his friend mark harrigan . Their squad was separated from the main force and pursued by villistas, they took refuge on a rocky hillside . While looking for a means of escape, the squad came across an unusual opening in the side of the hillside and investigated . While investigating, the group came across a cavern which was home to a bizarre machine attended by</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Feeders_from_Within"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Peter_J._Evans"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dweller_in_the_Gulf_%28short_story%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ClarkAshtonSmithWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Dweller in the Gulf (short story)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The dweller in the gulf, originally titled "dweller in martian depths", is a short story by american and friend of h.p. Lovecraft . It forms the second part of his series of short stories set on the planet mars .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Mars</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Wonder Stories</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 1933</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_White_Tree_%28DART_production%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Audio"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The White Tree (DART production)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The white tree: a tale of inspector legrasse is a dark adventure radio theatre production by the h.p. Lovecraft historical society . Unlike many of the dart productions, it is an original story, rather than an adaptation . It features a set of period-authentic props, including a page from a mystical book and a voodoo art</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Cairo_Guidebook">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
