  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shadows_of_the_Darkest_Jade">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Shadows of the Darkest Jade</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sarah Hans English Innsmouth Free Press Historical Lovecraft date April 2011  " " is a short story by Sarah Hans, first published in the Lovecraftian anthology Historical Lovecraft by Innsmouth Free Press in April 2011. Synopsis Two monks travelling the world to spread the word of Buddha encounter an ancient entity and its inhuman cult lurking deep within the backwoods near the Silk Road. One is cursed with madness and dies soon afterwards, while the other lives a miserable life profoundly changed by the experience. History Historical Lovecraft (2011) Whispers from the Abyss Vol. 2: The Horrors That Are &amp; Shall Be (2015) Digital Horror Fiction Anthology: Volume 1 (2018) Dead Girls Don’t Love (2018)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Sarah Hans</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Historical_Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>April 2011</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Army_of_Darkness:_Furious_Road">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Army of Darkness: Furious Road</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Nancy A. Collins Kewber Baal English Dynamite Entertainment Release Date March 2016 - Aug 2016 Media Type Comic book is a 6-issue mini series written by Nancy A. Collins in 2016 for Dynamite Entertainment. Synopsis Twenty years in the future, in a post-apocalyptic wasteland, the Army of Darkness have taken over the world. Forcing the surviving remnants of the human, witch, vampire, and werewolf species to join forces in an uneasy alliance, in Alive the last free city (what used to be Lansing, Michigan). Among their ranks are Dracula , his daughter Eva , Frankenstein's Monster, and Ash Williams .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Nancy A. Collins</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>March 2016 - Aug 2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jason_Voorhees">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Jason Voorhees</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Jason was a mentally disabled child who had a deformed head and face . His mother later went on a killing spree and was decapitated . Later returned as an undead serial killer with supernatural powers .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>Jason Goes to Hell: The Final Friday Freddy vs. Jason vs. Ash Freddy vs. Jason vs. Ash: The Nightmare Warriors</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Friday the 13th</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Ron Kurz</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Sean S. Cunningham</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Tom Savini</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Victor Miller</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#William_Lovecraft">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSRelatives"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>William Lovecraft</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1910</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>William left Devonshire on 4 May 1831 and married Eliza Ann Randall in Rochester, New York in February 1825. Like his brother, Joseph Jr., he worked as a cooper, and the two opened a manufacturing company, W &amp;amp; J Lovecraft. The company dissolved around 1869 and after that William worked as the owner of a wood yard.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isRelativeToR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1863</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Call_of_Wonderland">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Call of Wonderland</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Grimm Fairy Tales presents: Dan Wickline, Joe Brusha, Ralph Tedesco, Raven Gregory Matt Triano, Nacho Arranz Estevez English Zenescope Entertainment Release Date May 2012 - Sept 2012 Media Type Comic book is a seven-issue comic book mini-series written by Raven Gregory in 2008-2009 which was published by Zenescope Entertainment. Synopsis With both the Jabberwocky and Queen of Hearts defeated, Wonderland has been thrown into chaos. The Red Knight is going to unleash the Old Ones thereby destroying both Wonderland and Earth. Back on Earth, a forgotten secret is discovered by a literary student researching Lovecraft .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Dan Wickline</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Joe Brusha</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Ralph Tedesco</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Raven Gregory</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 2012 - Sept 2012</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Will_Murray">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Will Murray</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>William p. murray was a member of the providence pals, an informal group of h. p. lovecraft enthusiasts and scholars active from the late 1970s through to the 1990s . Since 1979, he has been the literary executor for lester dent, the creator of doc savage . His pulp fiction has appeared in such anthologies as miskatonic university , the cthulhu cycle , disciples of cthulhu ii , cthulhu's re</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>William Patrick Murray</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>28 April 1953</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Elihu_Whipple">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Elihu Whipple</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>1919 June 26</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Whipple is a fictional character created by h. p. lovecraft in his story " the shunned house" he is a physician and antiquarian, determined to confront the house's supernatural evil . He is described in the story as "a sane, conservative physician of the old school"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shunned_House"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#More_Shadows_Over_Rlyeh">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>More Shadows Over R'lyeh</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Don D'Ammassa English Managansett Press Release Date January 2017 166 is a collection of Lovecraftian short stories by American Don D'Ammasso . Published by Managansett Press in January 2017, both paperback and digital editions were released. Contents " The Well Being " " The Bad Neighbor " " Border Town " " Lost Canyon " " The Warden of the Wall " " The Key " " Wendy " " The Old Place " " Shell Games "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Don_DAmmassa"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>166</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>January 2017</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Innsmouth_Heritage">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Innsmouth Heritage</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Geneticist david stevenson travels to innsmouth, new hampshire, to study a strange condition known as the innsmouth look . He meets an old friend, ann eliot, who has been willed much of the land and property in the town by her late father . Eliot agrees to become a study case for stevenson on the condition that he attempt to rid the sufferers of the horrific dreams which accompany the look . He also convinces many of the other</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Stableford"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United Kingdom</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Science Fiction Short Fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 1992</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#hasChildrenR">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label>hasChildrenR</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Sinking_City_%28Video_Game%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Sinking City (Video Game)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Former u.s. navy sailor turned private investigator travels to the largely unknown town of oakmont, massachusetts to investigate the nightmarish visions he has been experiencing . There he discovers the town to be in great tumult, as not only is the town experiencing an influx of refugees from the recently destroyed town of innsmouth . In the course of his investigation, reed discovers that some of the occult factions within oakmont are</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2019 (Nintendo Switch) PlayStation 4 Xbox One) 2019 (Windows 2021 (Play Station 5) 2021 (Xbox Series X April 28 February 19 June 27 S) September 12</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Unexpected_Turbulence">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Unexpected Turbulence</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Daniel Purcell English Rogue Planet Press Lovecraftiana Vol 5, Issue 3 date October 2020  " " is a short Lovecraftian story by British David Purcell, first published in Vol 5, Issue 3 of the Lovecraftiana zine in October 2020. Synopsis In 1927, a young pilot attempts to be the first to make the Atlantic crossing from New York to Paris. Mid-journey, he spies a colossal being striding through the ocean and sending waves of madness through his mind before knocking his small aircraft into the ocean. Swimming to a nearby island, he encounters a cult dedicated to the entity; as the entity approaches the shore, he joins them in their worship, sure that it’s coming means the end of them all.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Daniel Purcell</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Issue 3</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Lovecraftiana Vol 5</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2020</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Key_of_the_Eternal_Gates">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MysticalArtefacts"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdfs:label>Key of the Eternal Gates</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Keys of the Eternal Gates are fictional items in the Dynamite universe which first appeared in 2019 in the 1st issue of 1st volume of the Bettie Page: Unbound comic book series. Background The Keys of the Eternal Gates are powerful sorcerous artefacts, of which there are five, all of which can fit in the palm of a human's hand, and are attached to a necklace. Each is able to open a portal to summon one of the Great Old Ones . Together they can release them from their dimensional prisons or imprison the Old Ones. They are scattered across time, space, and dimensions with one of them on Earth, the Tunguska Star (aka, The Sorcerer's Star).</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dark_One_%28Dynamite_Entertainment%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dark One (Dynamite Entertainment)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Elder Gods</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Great Old Ones</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Great Ones</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Old Ones</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Dark ones are a race or group of demonic-like beings in the army of darkness / evil dead / reanimator multiverses . They are the primary antagonists in the story, the power behind the necromancer kaya and the deadites . In the mainstream (canon) reality, the dark ones appear to possess far less power than the versions shown in the comics .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Army of Darkness vs. Re-Animator Vol 1 4</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>James Kuhoric</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Curse_of_the_Crocodile">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Curse of the Crocodile</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Crypt of cthulhu is a short story by richard l. tierney . It is a prequel to robert bloch's story " the secret of sebek " . The story is set in 38 a.d., in memphis, egypt .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_L._Tierney"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>May 1987</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Haiyore%21_Nyaruko-san">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosFilmAndTelevision"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Haiyore! Nyaruko-san</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A series of light novels, manga, and anime published in japan that incorporates elements from the cthulhu mythos . A flash-animated straight-to-video adaptation titled haiyoru! Nyaruani was released from october 2009 to march 2010 . An animated tv series was released in two seasons from 2012 to 2013, with 12 half-hour episodes per season .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lynne_Hardy">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lynne Hardy</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lovecraftian horror is an award-winning roleplaying games designer and occasional author based in the united kingdom . Her gaming credits include the core books for modiphius entertainment's achtung! Cthulhu setting . Her fiction has appeared in anthologies such as cthulhu lives! (ghostwoods books, 2014) and it came from miskatonic university ( broken eye books, 2020)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United Kingdom</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Feed_the_Shoggoth%21">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Feed the Shoggoth!</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Feed the Shoggoth! Badger McInnes Squamous Studios Publication Date 2015 Card Game Feed the Shoggoth! is a card-based game published by Squamous Studios in 2015, after a successful Kickstarter campaign. Overview</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Aunt_Hester">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#BrianLumleyWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Aunt Hester</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Hester lang tells her favorite but long-separated nephew peter about times when she and her twin brother exchanged bodies . When they were 12, she unconsciously switched with george in order to rescue their younger sister from a swimming mishap . A year or two later, when george was anxious about an exam, they again switched identities without meaning to .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The Satyr's Head</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1975</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>December 1971</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Doorway_of_the_Elder_Ones">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MysticalArtefacts"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdfs:label>Doorway of the Elder Ones</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a fictional item in the g.i.joe universe which first appeared in 2012 in the 1st issue of the 1st volume of infestation 2: g.i. Joe .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fractal_Creature">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonSapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Fractal Creature</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Yog-Sothoth"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Creatures from the Fractal Dimension</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Fractal creatures are a fictional race created by chaosium in 1995 for their call of cthulhu roleplaying game . They live in the fractal dimension which can only be accessed via a gate created by a computer . They manifest in the material plane as beings of pure energy which drift in the air in search of energy .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ye_Booke_of_Monstres_II"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scott_David_Aniolowski"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Thomas_V._Powers">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Thomas V. Powers</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Weird fiction Non-fiction is an author who has written a handful of short stories set in the greater Cthulhu Mythos . These have been included in publications such as the anthologies Swords Against Cthulhu II: Hyperborean Nights , Swords Against Cthulhu III: A New Dark Age (2017 and 2018 respectively, Rogue Planet Press ), and the online zine Nightscapes , edited by the late E. P. Berglund . Selected Works Short Fiction "Notes on Cthulhu" (1998) "The Statement of Jackson Palmer" (1999) " The Tale of Nanok the Merchant " (2017) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Force_of_Will">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Force of Will</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Co., Ltd. Collectible Card Game is a collectible card game created by Co., Ltd that was made available to English-speaking countries in 2013. The game mechanics are similar to Magic: The Gathering as each player is given a deck of 40–60 cards featuring various creatures, chants, spells, additions and regalia with the objective of defeating their opponents by reducing their life points to zero. Mythos Connections The Cthulhu are a race of evil creatures from the fictional universe of the game. As their name suggests, they are based on various entities from the Cthulhu Mythos . Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Tales_May_2009">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Periodicals"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Arkham Tales May 2009</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist George Cotronis English Cold Fusion Media Empire Release Date May 2009 104 Preceded by Arkham Tales February 2009 Followed by Arkham Tales August 2009 was the third issue of Arkham Tales: A Magazine of Weird Fiction , a short-lived quarterly magazine edited by Nathan Shumate . It featured cover art by George Cotronis. Contents Editorial: The Great Escape by Nathan Shumate Letters to the Editor "The House on Waterloo Lane" by Nicholas Ozment "Road Rage" by J. C. Koch "The Thirteenth Tree" by J. J. Beazley "Unnatural Blonde" by Rob Brooks "The Old House" by Nandi Ekles "Happy St. Patrick's Day" Eric W. Jepson "The Diet" by Maura McHugh "To Soothe the Savage Beast" by Edward Morris Contributor Notes</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>104</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 2009</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Star_Mother">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OuterGods"/>
    <rdfs:label>Star Mother</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Outer_God"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The was created in 1995 by chaosium for its call of cthulhu roleplaying game . Resembles a yellow-green stone whose shape is that of a huge-breasted faceless plump female figure with dozens of pencil-thin root-like strands .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ye_Booke_of_Monstres_II"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scott_David_Aniolowski"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Bryan_Katz">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Bryan Katz</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Janitor (formerly) human first appearance in the 2020 short story " the dewey mine attack " created by author and artist dean wirth . He made his first appearance in the 2020 short story " the dewey mine attack " .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Randy_Oswald_Story"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dewey_Mine_Attack"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dean_Wirth"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecrafts_The_Dunwich_Horror">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleComicBookAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>H. P. Lovecraft's The Dunwich Horror</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Joe R. Lansdale Peter Bergting English IDW Publishing Release Date Oct 2011 - Jan 2012 Media Type Comic book is a comic book mini-series, written by Joe R. Lansdale and published by IDW Publishing. It ran from 2011 to 2012, with a total of four issues. It is an adaptation of H. P. Lovecraft 's " The Dunwich Horror " and also includes an adaptation of his other short story " The Hound ".</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Joe_R._Lansdale"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>Oct 2011 - Jan 2012</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cards_of_Cthulhu,_A_Deck_Building_Game">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cards of Cthulhu, A Deck Building Game</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cards of cthulhu: a deckbuilding game is a card game from duel grounds . Based on the works of h. p. lovecraft and the greater cthulhu mythos . Designed for between two and five players .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_St._Johns_Eve_1985">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu St. John's Eve 1985</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Lance Brown Allen Koszowski Cover Artist Jim Garrison United States English Crypt of Cthulhu Cryptic Publications Release Date June 1985 56 Preceded by Roodmas 1985 Followed by Lammas 1985 Crypt of Cthulhu Roodmas 1985 is the 32nd issue of leading H. P. Lovecraft scholar Robert M. Price 's long-running Lovecraftian fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Edited by Price with a cover by Jim Garrison and interior art by Lance Brown and Allen Koszowski , it ran to a total of 56 in an A5 format, and was published by Cryptic Publications in June 1985 . Contents Editorial Shards by Robert M. Price "An Autopsy on the Old Ones" by Bert Atsma "Lovecraft's Alien Civilizations: A Political Interpretation" by S. T. Joshi "Lovecraft's POElar Adventure" by Ben P. Indick "At the Mountains of Madness as a Sequel to Arthur Gordon Pym" by Peter Cannon "The Trouble with Shoggoths" by Will Murray "Fun Guys from Yuggoth: Analects from the Mainstream #1: Of Lovecraft and Sam Loveman" by Don Herron "Advice to the Lovecraft-Lorn" by Donna Death Reviews by Will Murray and Robert M. Price Letters by Richard L. Tierney , Jessica Amanda Salmonson, Nelson Bridwell, Darrell Schweitzer , Laurence C. Bush, John Pelan , Richard M. Jefts, Peter H. Gilmore, Duane Rimel , Tani Jantsang , and Pierre Comtois References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>56</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>June 1985</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Beneath_the_Temple_of_Outlandish_Idols">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Beneath the Temple of Outlandish Idols</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu mythos short story published in fantasy anthology in 2017 . Three brothers of a noble family hatch a plot to regain their fortune . They break into the catacombs beneath the palace of the lord hierophant . They attempt to loot the grave goods buried with their owners .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gavin_Chappell"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Swords_Against_Cthulhu_II:_Hyperborean_Nights"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>January 2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Yug-Siturath">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Yug-Siturath</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The All-Consuming Fog</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as The All-Consuming Fog First appearance "The Devil in You" is a vampiric entity manifesting as an all-consuming fog that absorbs vital forces/souls.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Devil in You</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Elizabeth_Allen">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Elizabeth Allen</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Red Queen</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as red queen powers and abilities healing (wonderland), leadership, reality manipulation (wonderland), swordsmanship, unarmed combat, weapon master ruler human . First appeared in 1871, first appearing in "through the looking glass"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>1st ( ADJ ): Tales from Wonderland: Queen of Hearts Vol 1 1</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Through the Looking Glass</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Lewis Carroll</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Facts_in_the_Case_of_H._P._Lovecraft">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Facts in the Case of H. P. Lovecraft</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Barton L. St. Armand English Genre(s) Non-fiction Ohio University Press H. P. Lovecraft: Four Decades of Criticism date 1980 " " is a short non-fiction piece by and scholar Barton L. St. Armand. Overview Running to only eight pages in length, "" was published in the S. T. Joshi -edited collection H. P. Lovecraft: Four Decades of Criticism by Ohio University Press in 1980.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Barton L. St. Armand</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft:_Four_Decades_of_Criticism"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1980</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chronicles_of_the_Cthulhu_Codex_5">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Chronicles of the Cthulhu Codex 5</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Chronicles of the Cthulhu Codex #5 Various Cover Artist Allen Koszowski English Fungoid Press Release Date January 1989 70 Preceded by February 1987 Followed by January 1991 Chronicles of the Cthulhu Codex #5 is an issue of the sporadically-released fanzine Chronicles of the Cthulhu Codex . Edited by Henry J. Vester, III and featuring cover art by Allen Koszowski , it was published by Fungoid Press in January 1989. This issue was the first not to have Pierre Comtois as editor, and the last to be titled Chronicles of the Cthulhu Codex until the final issue, some eleven years later (future issues had the title cut down to The Cthulhu Codex , and later simply Cthulhu Codex ). Contents "The Dreams of Yig" by Pierre Comtois and Henry J. Vester, III "CAS - A Reminiscence" by Robert B. Elder "The Oracle of Sàdoqua" by Ron Hilger A Bit of the Dark World: An Interview with Fritz Leiber by Gregorio Montejo "The Thing in the Glen" by Wilum H. Pugmire "An Opener of the Gate" by Margaret L. Carter "Yuggoth Comes to Providence" by L. Sprague de Camp "Visions of Golconda" by Richard L. Tierney "Brooding Tyre" by Richard L. Tierney "The Sorcerer" by Shawn Ramsey "The Witch" by Shawn Ramsey "To Clark Ashton Smith" by Shawn Ramsey</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>70</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>January 1989</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#When_the_Dweller_Awakes">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crossovers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>When the Dweller Awakes</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A crossover with the x-men superhero team and the cthulhu mythos . It is a story featured in the adventures of the x-men issue #4 by marvel comics .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Ralph Macchio</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1996 May 15</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Eldritch_Tales:_A_Miscellany_of_the_Macabre">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Eldritch Tales: A Miscellany of the Macabre</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft Les Edwards Cover Artist Les Edwards English Gollancz Release Date July 2011 is a collection of works by the late American H. P. Lovecraft . First published by Gollancz in hardcover, paperback, and digital formats in July 2011, a hardcover edition was reissued in March 2020. In both editions, cover and exterior artwork was provided by Les Edwards . Contents " The Tomb " " Polaris " " Beyond the Wall of Sleep " " The White Ship " " The Picture in the House " " The Temple " " The Terrible Old Man " " The Tree " " The Moon-Bog " " The Other Gods " " The Quest of Iranon " " Hypnos " " The Festival " " He " " The Evil Clergyman " " The Beast in the Cave " " The Alchemist " " Poetry and the Gods " (with Anna Helen Crofts ) " The Street " " The Transition of Juan Romero " " Azathoth " " The Book " " The Thing in the Moonlight (with Jack Chapman Miske ) " A Reminiscence of Dr. Samuel Johnson " " Memory " " Nyarlathotep " " Ex Oblivione " " What the Moon Brings " " The Horror at Martin's Beach (with Sonia H. Greene ) " The Very Old Folk " " History of the Necronomicon " " The Descendant " " The Trap " (with Henry S. Whitehead ) " The Messenger " " The Ancient Track " " The Wood " " The House " " Hallowe'en in a Suburb " " Nathicana " " Supernatural Horror in Literature " " Facts Concerning the Late Arthur Jermyn and His Family " " Two Black Bottles " (with Wilfred Blanch Talman ) " The Challenge from Beyond " (with Robert E. Howard , Frank Belknap Long , A. Merritt , and C. L. Moore ) " Celephaïs " " Ibid " " The Nightmare Lake " " Despair " " The Poe-et's Nightmare " " The Last Test " (with Adolphe de Castro ) " The Electric Executioner " (with Adolphe de Castro) " Psychopompos: A Tale in Rhyme " " Festival " " In a Sequester'd Providence Churchyard Where Once Poe Walk'd " " The Green Meadow " (with Winifred V. Jackson ) " The Crawling Chaos " (with Winifred V. Jackson) " Fungi from Yuggoth " Afterword: Lovecraft in Britain by Stephen Jones Other Collaborations and Revisions by uncredited References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 2011</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Eternal_Darkness:_Sanitys_Requiem">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Eternal Darkness: Sanity's Requiem</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Eternal darkness: sanity's requiem is a psychological horror action-adventure video game . The game features a "sanity meter" that represents the protagonist's mental stability . The game is known for its innovative approach to the horror genre .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Black_Rapture">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Black Rapture</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A young loner murders his unloving parents during the opening stages of the return of the great old ones . He believes this act will grant him favour with the earth's new masters . But even death is not enough to stop his parents from finishing the scheme they have spent years nurturing .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nightscapes"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>September 1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Golothess">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Golothess</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Great Old Ones</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Great Old Ones is a Great Old One that is said to reside in the same domain as the Greek god Bacchus. was cut into ten pieces by Yig during a great battle. One of these pieces became an alabaster dish in Egypt.</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Wilson_Shepherd">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSFriendsAndAcquaintances"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Wilson Shepherd</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>unknown</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Weird fiction editor and publisher who assisted Donald A. Wollheim in editing the Phantagraph and Fanciful Tales. He corresponded briefly with Lovecraft in 1936–1937. He published Lovecraft’s “A History of the Necronomicon” in 1937.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1917</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Imprisoned_with_the_Pharoahs_%28DART_production%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleAudioAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Imprisoned with the Pharoahs (DART production)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Imprisoned with the Pharoahs (DART production) Based on " Imprisoned with the Pharaohs " Production Company The H. P. Lovecraft Historical Society Running time 75 minutes (approx.) United States English Imprisoned with the Pharaohs is an audio drama adaptation of the short story of the same name by H. P. Lovecraft . Overview Produced by the H. P. Lovecraft Historical Society as part of their Dark Adventure Radio Theatre series, Imprisoned with the Pharaohs runs to a total of 75 minutes and is available in both physical (compact disc) and digital download formats. Both come with a set of period-authentic props: a postcard from Harry Houdini, a letter from the Cairo Museum, a newspaper clipping, and a show poster from one of Houdini's performances are all included. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Imprisoned_with_the_Pharaohs"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#I_am_Providence_%28G._O._Clark%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>I am Providence (G. O. Clark)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>I am Providence (G. O. Clark) G. O. Clark English Genre(s) Cthulhu Mythos Rogue Planet Press Lovecraftiana date Unknown " I am Providence " is a Cthulhu Mythos poem by American G. O. Clark, first published in Gavin Chappell 's Lovecraftiana horror magazine (date unknown, Rogue Planet Press ). In 2023, it was voted by the fanbase on social media platform Facebook to be included in the anthology The Best of Lovecraftiana (2023, Rogue Planet Press), where it appeared as the final piece . Synopsis The twenty-five lines describe how the tombstone of Howard Phillips Lovecraft bears witness to the return of the Elder Gods . History Lovecraftiana (date and issue unknown, Rogue Planet Press ) The Best of Lovecraftiana (2023, Rogue Planet Press) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>G. O. Clark</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraftiana"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>Unknown</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Delta_Green:_Dark_Theatres">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Delta Green: Dark Theatres</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist John T. Snyder English Armitage House Release Date 2001 299 , which features the tagline " Cthulhu Mythos stories of modern horror and conspiracy " is an anthology of Lovecraftian fiction based in the Delta Green sub-setting of Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu horror roleplaying game. Edited by Bob Kruger and John Tynes , it runs to a total of 299 and was released by Pagan Publishing through their Armitage House imprint in 2001; it is currently available in both digital and print-on-demand editions through DriveThruFiction. Overview Contents Introduction on Delta Green, Majestic-12 and the Katorechia by John Tynes " Once More, from the Top " by A. Scott Glancy "Night and Water" by Dennis Detwiller "Russian Dolls" by Robert E. Furey "As I See It" by Greg Stolze "Suicide Watch" by Arinn Dembo "The Corn King" by John Tynes "Good Night, Bach Ma, Good-Bye" by Benjamin Adams "The Fast Track" by Martin E. Cirulis References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>299</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2001</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_One">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>The One</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nodens"/>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Poseidon"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Poseidon is a fictional character created by h. p. lovecraft in the short story " the strange high house in the mist ". He is a hermit who has dealings with mysterious supernatural forces . He lives in an "abnormally antique grey cottage in the sky" atop a crag overlooking kingsport .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Strange_High_House_in_the_Mist"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#HorrorBabble">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Publishers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>HorrorBabble</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Overview by ian and jennifer gordon, produces audio readings of both classic stories and more contemporary ones . Output includes many of lovecraft's original stories, as well as connected tales by his contemporaries . More recent stories tend to lean towards the weird fiction, and the weird tales - era style in particular .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://www.horrorbabble.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Substance_in_the_Sound">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Substance in the Sound</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A father and son fishing crew discover a strange lump of protoplasm floating off of america's east coast . They take it to an expert to be analysed, only to discover many more of the masses . The scientist then reveals that he is of innsmouth stock, and recites a chant which invigorates the creatures .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>W. B. Stickel</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Whispers_from_the_Abyss"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2013</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Miskatonic_University_%28anthology%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Miskatonic University (anthology)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Miskatonic University Martin H. Greenberg &amp; Robert E. Weinberg , eds. Cover Artist Jim Warren English Genre(s) Cthulhu Mythos DAW Books Release Date November 1996 352 Miskatonic University is an anthology of Cthulhu Mythos stories focused on Lovecraft's fictional college , edited by Martin H. Greenberg and Robert E. Weinberg , and published by DAW Books in 1996. The stories are original to the collection. Contents "Preface: A Letter from the President to Incoming Students" (essay) by Stefan Dziemianowicz "Kali Yuga Comes" by Tina L. Jens "Teachers" by Mort Castle "Her Misbegotten Son" by Alan Rodgers "Scavenger Hunt" by Brad Linaweaver "Black Celebration" by Jay Bonansinga "To Be as They" by Stephen Mark Rainey "Second Movement" by Benjamin Adams "A Dreaming of Dead Poets" by Jane M. Lindskold "Mandelbrot Moldrot" by Lois H. Gresh "The Smile of a Mime" by Billie Sue Mosiman " The Sothis Radiant " by Will Murray "The Play's the Thing" by Christie Golden " Ghoulmaster " by Brian McNaughton</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Martin H. Greenberg</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Robert E. Weinberg</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>eds.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>352</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>November 1996</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dirk_W._Mosig">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scholars"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dirk W. Mosig</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Yōzan Connection to the Mythos Scholar Birth Date 1943 Germany United States Non-fiction Dr. Yōzan (born 1943) is, amongst many other things, a leading scholar of Howard Phillips Lovecraft . Born in Germany, he emigrated to the United States where he attended the University of Florida; he currently teaches psychology at the University of Nebraska. S. T. Joshi , to whom Mosig has offered a degree of mentorship in the field, has said that "Dirk Mosig is the key transitional figure in Lovecraft studies; and if the history of this field is ever written, he will have to occupy a central role." Selected Works " H. P. Lovecraft: Myth-Maker " (1976) " "The White Ship": A Psychological Odyssey " (1980) Trivia The Lammas 1985 edition of Robert M. Price 's long-running Lovecraftian fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu was devoted to the works of , and included some complementary works by fellow scholars including S. T. Joshi and Peter Cannon . See Also Images by External Links at Wikipedia at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>Germany</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Yōzan Dirk W. Mosig</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Scholar</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1943</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Only_the_End_of_the_World_Again">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crossovers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Only the End of the World Again</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>First published in the thematic anthology shadows over innsmouth in 1994 . Concerns a man named . Lawrence talbot, an "adjustor" who also happens to be a lycanthrope . His would-be murderers do not count on the ferocity of his canine nature, giving him the opportunity to kill them .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Neil_Gaiman"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Shadows Over Innsmouth</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1994</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Algernon_Blackwood">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsInspirationsAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosAdjacentAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Algernon Blackwood</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>10 December 1951</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Algernon blackwood was an english author, journalist and radio broadcaster . 's work has been described as "more consistently meritorious than any weird writer's except dunsany 's " he has had an enormous influence on the development of the cthulhu mythos .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>http://example.org/lovecraft#England Kent</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Algernon Henry Blackwood</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>Great Britain</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Inspiration</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>14 March 1869</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Mask_of_Cthulhu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AugustDerlethWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Mask of Cthulhu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>August Derleth United States English (original) French German Genre(s) Weird fiction Arkham House Release Date 1958 is an anthology of Cthulhu Mythos stories by August Derleth , first published by Arkham House in 1958 and reprinted several times, including in French and German translations. Most of the stories had previously appeared in Weird Tales between 1939 and 1953. Contents The stories in the collection include: " The Return of Hastur " (1939) " The Whippoorwills in the Hills " (1948) " Something in Wood " (1948) " The Sandwin Compact " (1940) " The House in the Valley " (1953) " The Seal of R'lyeh " (1957) Publication History (1958, Arkham House) (1961, Consul Books) (1971, Beagle Boxer) Le masque de Cthulhu (1972, Christian Bourgois, French-) (1974, Neville Spearman) Le masque de Cthulhu (1975, Francis Loisirs, French-) (1976, Panther) (1976, Ballantine Books) Le masque de Cthulhu (1985, Presses Pocket, French-) (1988, Grafton) Le masque de Cthulhu (1991, Presses Pocket, French-) (1996, Carroll &amp; Graf) Die Masken des Cthulhu (2001, Festa, German-) Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#August_Derleth"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>French</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>German</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1958</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Flame_Creatures_of_Cthugha">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Flame Creatures of Cthugha</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthugha"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Minions of Cthugha</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The are extraterrestrial entities made of living flame which dwell in the star fomalhaut with their master, the fire elemental known as cthugha . They can be summoned to earth when the star is visible in the night sky by the repetition of the sentence " ph'nglui mglw'nafh cthugha fomalhaut n'gha- ghaa naf'l thagn. Iä! Cthugha! "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dweller_in_Darkness"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#August_Derleth"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Achtung%21_Cthulhu:_Three_Kings">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Achtung! Cthulhu: Three Kings</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Three Kings Sarah Newton Dim Martin Cover Artist Dim Martin English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Modiphius Entertainment Chronicle City Release Date 2013 is a scenario supplement for Modiphius Entertainment 's Achtung! Cthulhu setting for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu horror roleplaying game. The first book in the Achtung! Cthulhu , it was written by Sarah Newton , featured artwork by Dim Martin, and was published by Modiphius and Chronicle City in 2013 , as the result of a successful Kickstarter campaign. Overview Gallery References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sarah_Newton"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2013</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Haggopian_and_Other_Stories">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#BrianLumleyWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CrossroadPress"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Haggopian and Other Stories</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Brian Lumley Cover Artist Bob Eggleton English Subterranean Press , Crossroad Press (audiobook) Release Date May 2008, December 2019 (audiobook) is a collection of Cthulhu Mythos short stories by Brian Lumley . It was published by Subterranean Press in May 2008. The audiobook version was narrated by Joshua Saxon and released by Crossroad Press in 2019. Contents " The Caller of The Black " (1971) " Haggopian " (1973) " Cement Surroundings " (1969) " The House of Cthulhu " (1973) " The Night Sea-Maid Went Down " (1971) " Name and Number " (1982) " Recognition " (1981) "Curse of the Golden Guardians" (1984) " Aunt Hester " (1975) " The Kiss of Bugg-Shash " (1978) " De Marigny's Clock " (1971) " Mylakhrion the Immortal " (1977) " The Sister City " (1969) " What Dark God? " (1975) " The Statement of Henry Worthy " (1977) " Dagon's Bell " (1988) " The Thing from the Blasted Heath " (1971) " Dylath-Leen " (1971) " The Mirror of Nitocris " (1971) " The Second Wish " (1980) " The Hymn " (2006) " Synchronicity or Something " (1988) " The Black Recalled " (1983) "The Sorcerer's Dream" (1979)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 2008 December 2019 (audiobook)</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#My_Life_in_Miniature">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>My Life in Miniature</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The all-new atom is a four-part story featured in issues #1-4 of the series . It is written by gail simone and published by dc comics in 2006 . The story follows the adventures of a young man named ryan choi . He attempts to continue the legacy of his father, professor ray .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Gail Simone</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>September - December 2006</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Stocking">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RamseyCampbellWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Stocking</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Tandem horror 2 date 1968is a short story by british ramsey campbell which is connected to the greater cthulhu mythos . First published in the anthology tandem horror 2 in 1968 by tandem . Has also been translated into german .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ramsey_Campbell"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>German</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Tandem Horror 2</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1968</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Cultus_Issue_3">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Cultus Issue 3</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu Cultus #3 Various Cover Artist Tani Jantsang United States English P. Marsh Release Date December 1995 58 Preceded by Cthulhu Cultus #2 Followed by Cthulhu Cultus #4 Cthulhu Cultus #3 is an issue of sporadic American fanzine Cthulhu Cultus , edited by Tani Jantsang and James Ambuehl and published by P. Marsh in December 1995. Contents "The Inner Gateway" by E. P. Berglund "The Poet" by Tani Jantsang "Humor by Phenomenologists Plus None" by Tani Jantsang "The Answer Is in the Plate" by Lasonya Nella Faccia "Father Figure" by Alex Guillotte and Jeffrey Thomas "Lovecraftian Last Words" by James Ambuehl "The Skeptic" by James Ambuehl "Foetid Earth" by James Ambuehl "Bugg-Shash Sucks" by James Ambuehl "The Doom of Othuum" by James Ambuehl "The Call of Kthanid" by James Ambuehl "Xothic Bare Facts: Lin's Naked Tales Retold" by A. D'Fender "Interviews with Other Nations" by Tani Jantsang "The Precambian Fossil Record" by Sy Chloe and P. Dias "Beast of Love" by James Ambuehl and Tracy Ambuehl "Zann's Reprise" by Peter H. Gilmore "The Colour Out of Space" by E. P. Berglund "The Nameless City" by E. P. Berglund "The Dunwich Horror" by E. P. Berglund "The Haunter of the Dark" by E. P. Berglund "Dream-Sendings" by E. P. Berglund "Orca's Leap" by Casey Ley</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>58</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>December 1995</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Acquired_Through_Deaccession">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Acquired Through Deaccession</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lee Clark Zumpe English Rogue Planet Press Lovecraftiana Vol 5, Issue 2 date July 2020 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos story by American Lee Clark Zumpe . It was published in the Lammas Eve edition of Lovecraftiana by Rogue Planet Press in July 2020. Synopsis When head of Collections Management Norton Schubert is appointed to the Miskatonic University library under somewhat dubious circumstances, many of the senior faculty are troubled, believing his cost-cutting measures to be less than genuine. Using a meeting to discuss these concerns as cover, the former head librarian and members of the campus security break in to Schubert's house and discover that he has been stealing works of great occult significance. In retribution, some of the more enlightened faculty members enact a ritual to summon a pack of Hunting Horrors , ensuring that Schubert will never be able to unleash unspeakable horrors on the planet, and that the authorities will never ask any awkward questions about his disappearance.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lee_Clark_Zumpe"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Issue 2</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Lovecraftiana Vol 5</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>July 2020</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Secrets_in_Storage">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Secrets in Storage</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Greg van Eekhout Tim Pratt United States English Genre(s) Weird fiction Whispers from the Abyss date 2013  " " is a Lovecraftian short story by American authors Greg van Eekhout and Tim Pratt. Synopsis A man who buys the contents of old storage lockers for a living finds an ancient chest amongst the contents of his latest purchase. Inside, he finds a magical tunnel which descends into the bowels of the Earth , and there, a cave which houses several altars, a multitude of dead bodies, and a malignant entity which dwells in a pool. Fleeing the chest, he destroys it, although he is forever haunted by what he witnessed within. History "" was published in the anthology of Lovecraft -inspired works Whispers from the Abyss in 2013.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Greg van Eekhout</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Tim Pratt</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Whispers_from_the_Abyss"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2013</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Rakshassa">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Rakshassa</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Azathoth"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Rakshassi are fictional creatures featured in andy lane's novel all-consuming fire . They are winged crustacean-like creatures with crimson red armour and a long segmented tail ending in a spiked club . They are the servants of a massive slug-like alien who identifies herself as "azathoth"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#All-Consuming_Fire"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Andy Lane</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_500">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu 500</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Jeff Tidball Atlas Games  : Mythos Motorsports Madness is a Cthulhu Mythos -inspired card game. Overview A generic card game with a racing car/Cthulhu Mythos skin shoehorned on, was designed by Jeff Tidball and published by Atlas Games in August 2004. The box contains two 55-card decks and a rules sheet and is intended to be played by 3-8 players in under an hour.</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#wasDestroyedOnL">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#FunctionalProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label>wasDestroyedOnL</rdfs:label>
