  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Church_in_High_Street">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RamseyCampbellWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Church in High Street</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Campbell's first published story, " the tomb herd ", appeared in the 1962 arkham house anthology dark mind, dark heart . It is set in campbell's fictional severn valley , england, the setting for many of his later mythos stories .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ramsey_Campbell"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror Mystery Short Fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1962</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Spawn_of_Cthulhu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Spawn of Cthulhu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Gervasio Gallardo English Ballantine Adult Fantasy Genre(s) Weird fiction Ballantine Books Release Date October 1971 274 is a 1971 horror anthology edited by Lin Carter , part of the Ballantine Adult Fantasy . Its 12 stories include works that both influenced and were influenced by H. P. Lovecraft 's Cthulhu Mythos . Contents "Introduction: About and H. P. Lovecraft" ( Lin Carter ) " The Whisperer in Darkness " ( H. P. Lovecraft ) " An Inhabitant of Carcosa " ( Ambrose Bierce ) " The Yellow Sign " (from The King in Yellow ) ( Robert W. Chambers ) "Cordelia’s Song from The King in Yellow " (Vincent Starrett) " The Return of Hastur " ( August Derleth ) "Litany to Hastur" (from Dreams from R'lyeh ) (Lin Carter) " The Children of the Night " ( Robert E. Howard ) " K’n-yan " ( Walter C. DeBill, Jr. ) " The Tale of Satampra Zeiros " ( Clark Ashton Smith ) " The Hounds of Tindalos " ( Frank Belknap Long ) " The Curse of Yig " ( Zealia Bishop ) " The Mine on Yuggoth " ( Ramsey Campbell )</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>274</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Ballantine Adult Fantasy</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 1971</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Joe_R._Lansdale">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Joe R. Lansdale</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Joe Richard Harold Lansdale Connection to the Mythos Author Birth Date 28 October 1951 Gladewater, Texas, USA United States https://www.joerlansdale.com/ Joe Richard Harold Lansdale (born 28 October 1951) is a multi award-winning American author, screenwriter and comic book creator who has written a number of Lovecraftian works during a career spanning more than four decades. These have appeared in anthologies such as The Book of Cthulhu (2011, Night Shade Press) and Lovecraft's Monsters (2014, Tachyon Publications). Lansdale has cited Ray Bradbury, Stephen King , H. P. Lovecraft , and Edgar Allan Poe as amongst his major influences Selected Bibliography Short Fiction "The Crawling Sky" (2009) "The Bleeding Shadow" (2011) Comic Books H. P. Lovecraft's The Dunwich Horror (2011-2012) External Links at Wikipedia at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Texas Gladewater</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Joe Richard Harold Lansdale</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://www.joerlansdale.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>28 October 1951</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Beyond_the_Veil_of_Pretty_Pink_Lies">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Beyond the Veil of Pretty Pink Lies</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Rebecca J. Allred English Ulthar Press Pickman's Gallery date 2018  " " is a Lovecraftian short story by Rebecca J. Allred. It was published in the Matthew Carpenter -edited thematic anthology Pickman's Gallery by Ulthar Press in 2018. Synopsis A woman who paints pictures of the deceased to assuage the grief of bereaved families meets a rich and enigmatic woman who offers her a commission. However, the strain of painting this final composition proves too much for the artist and she suffers a mental breakdown, destroying her previous works which, she reveals, showed the subjects not embraced in heavenly bliss, but sent to a hellish afterlife inhabited by cruel monstrosities.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Rebecca J. Allred</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pickmans_Gallery"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2018</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Turner">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>James Turner</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>28 March 1999</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name James Allen Turner Connection to the Mythos Editor Birth Date 19 March 1945 St. Louis, Missouri, USA Death Date 28 March 1999 St. Louis, Missouri, USA United States Weird Fiction James Allen "Jim" Turner was an American editor who worked on several anthologies of Cthulhu Mythos content amongst other works. Overview Working as the editorial director for Mythos publishing house Arkham House throughout the 1970s and 80s, Jim Turner took over the post from August Derleth after the latter's death in 1971. Whilst there, he worked on several volumes of Mythos-related content with S. T. Joshi , but left in 1996, citing creative differences with co-owner April Derleth, August's daughter. After his departure, Turner formed his own publishing house, Golden Gryphon Press, where he worked on similar material to that at Arkham House, and published more Lovecraft -related content. Selected Bibliography Editor Cthulhu 2000: A Lovecraftian Anthology (1995) Eternal Lovecraft: The Persistence of H. P. Lovecraft in Popular Culture (1998) External Links Jim Turner at Wikipedia Jim Turner at The Internet Speculative Fiction Database</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Missouri St. Louis</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>James Allen Turner</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>19 March 1945</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mister_Misery">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Mister Misery</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Background (aka, the thing in the cellar) is a magical being that was created by doctor stephen strange . It was kept in the basement of his mansion in manhattan, ny city . During the empirikul's assault on earth in their attempt to purge the world of magic, his mansion was razed .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#ZothiqueStoryCycle">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Hoard_of_the_Wizard-Beast">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Hoard of the Wizard-Beast</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The slaying of the monster is a short story by h. p. lovecraft and r. h. barlow . It was written in 1933 but not published until 1994, in and one other . It was the second collaboration between lovecraft and the sixteen-year-old barlow .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Howard_Phillips_Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#R._H._Barlow"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>One Other</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Zadok_Allen">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Zadok Allen</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>1831 Human First appearance HPL : The Shadow Over Innsmouth EXP : " The Transition of " Call of Cthulhu: Dark Corners of the Earth Created by H. P. Lovecraft is a character in H. P. Lovecraft 's The Shadow Over Innsmouth , one of the few remaining humans in Innsmouth . After relating the history of Innsmouth to traveller Robert Olmstead , he was captured and presumably sacrificed to the Deep Ones by the Esoteric Order of Dagon . Expanded Mythos In Robert M. Price 's short story " The Transition of " (1995), Allen is depicted as having in fact been taken below the waves and transformed into a Deep One, where he presumably still lives. Allen also appeared as an important non-player character in Bethesda Softworks' Call of Cthulhu: Dark Corners of the Earth video game in 2005.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The Transition of Zadok Allen Call of Cthulhu: Dark Corners of the Earth</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shadow_Over_Innsmouth"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Hallowmas_1987">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu Hallowmas 1987</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Grey Ginter United States English Crypt of Cthulhu Cryptic Publications Release Date November 1987 64 Preceded by Michaelmas 1987 Followed by Yuletide 1987 Crypt of Cthulhu Hallowmas 1985 is the 51st issue of long-running fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Running to a total of 64 , it was edited by Robert M. Price , featured cover art by Grey Ginter, interior work by Richard L. Tierney and Ottos Bumberger, and was published by Cryptic Publications in November 1987 in an A5 format . Contents Editorial Shards by Robert M. Price "Lovecraft and Blackwood: A Surveillance" by Mike Ashley "Innsmouth Spawn" by Randall Larson "Did Lovecraft Revise "The Curse of Alabad and Ghinu and Aratza"?" by Will Murray " Cthaat Aquadingen : A Guide to Further Research " by Carl T. Ford " Henry Kuttner's Cthulhu Mythos Tales: An Overview " by Shawn Ramsey "The True History of the Tcho-Tcho People" by Tani Jantsang and Robert M. Price "Still More Limericks from Yuggoth" by Lin Carter "The Benevolence of Yib" by Lin Carter "The Mystics of Muelenburg" by Thomas Ligotti "From the Vaults of Yoh-Vombis" by Lin Carter Reviews by Stefan Dziemianowicz Letters by Jessica Amanda Salmonson, Robert M. Price, T. E. D. Klein , Philip Obed Marsh , Abner Mozingo, Lin Carter, Richard L. Tierney , Stefan Dziemianowicz, L. Sprague de Camp , Wilum Pugmir e, Brett Rutherford, Charles Garofalo , Michael J. Lotus, Brian Lumley , Will Murray, Shawn Ramsey, Phinas Kornegay , Paul R. Wilson, Peter H. Cannon , and Dan Gobbett References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>64</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>November 1987</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Curse_Whistle">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MysticalArtefacts"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdfs:label>Curse Whistle</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesA>Bone Owl</ex:hasAlternativeNamesA>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesA>Bone Pipe</ex:hasAlternativeNamesA>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesA>Hoot Pipe</ex:hasAlternativeNamesA>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesA>Instrument of Doom</ex:hasAlternativeNamesA>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a fictional artefact in the cthulhu mythos . It was created by chaosium for their call of cthulhu rpg . It is described as a magical musical pipe that can induce horror and madness through sound . Otherworldly tunes can be played from the pipe, causing nearby creatures within earshot to go insane .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasFunctionA>Induce madness through sound</ex:hasFunctionA>
    <ex:hasTypeA>Weapon</ex:hasTypeA>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnA rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Grand_Grimoire_of_Cthulhu_Mythos_Magic"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Subterranean_Press">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Publishers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Subterranean Press</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Subterranean press specializes in horror, science fiction, and fantasy . It has been a primary publisher for caitlin r. kernan , brian lumley , thomas ligotti , elizabeth bear , and poppy z. brite . It has also published numerous other mythos-related authors, including robert bloch , robert e. howard , hugh b. cave , and peter cannon .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://subterraneanpress.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#E._F._Bleiler">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Critics"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>E. F. Bleiler</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>13 June 2010</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Bleiler was an american editor, bibliographer, and critic . He wrote many pieces on the works of the late howard phillips lovecraft . Many of his pieces have drawn unfavourable conclusions .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA http://example.org/lovecraft#Massachusetts</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Everett Franklin Bleiler</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Critic</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>30 April 1920</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
