  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Basil_Elton">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dreamers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Basil Elton</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Relatives Nathaniel Elton (grandson) Lighthouse Keeper Human First appearance HPL : " The White Ship " EXP : The Complete Dreamlands Created by H. P. Lovecraft is the narrator and protagonist of H. P. Lovecraft 's Dunsanian story " The White Ship ". He is the keeper of the North Point lighthouse, and once a regular visitor to the Dreamlands . His visits to the Dreamlands ended when he was killed in a shipwreck there, leaving him stranded in the Waking World. ( HPL : " The White Ship ") The Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game, in which Elton appears as a non-player character, locates the North Point in Kingsport , Massachusetts . ( EXP : The Complete Dreamlands ) This is likely a reference to The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath , in which Randolph Carter mentions "a lighthouse-keeper in ancient Kingsport" heavily implied to be .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The Complete Dreamlands</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_White_Ship"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#I_Am_Providence:_The_Life_and_Times_of_H._P._Lovecraft">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>I Am Providence: The Life and Times of H. P. Lovecraft</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cover of Volume 1 S. T. Joshi United States English Genre(s) Biography Release Date 2010 is a non-fiction work by Lovecraft scholar and critic S. T. Joshi . Overview Joshi has described this comprehensive biography as his most significant achievement in his long career of studying Lovecraft. I Am Providence is a significantly expanded edition of one of his earlier works, H. P. Lovecraft: A Life , containing an addition 150,000 words which had been cut from the original publication due to length considerations, has been revised to incorporate recent information on Lovecraft, and was released in two separate volumes. Publication History I Am Providence was released by Hippocampus Press in 2010. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#S._T._Joshi"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Biography</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2010</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraftiana_Vol_1,_Issue_3">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Periodicals"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lovecraftiana Vol 1, Issue 3</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Antonio Espinosa Mihail Bila Maria Mitchell Mario Zuccarello Cover Artist Dave Oliver English Rogue Planet Press Release Date October 2016 58  is an issue of the periodical Lovecraftiana , edited by Gavin Chappell and published by Rogue Planet Press in October 2016. Contents "Last Will of a Providence Man" by Mathew Wilson "The Roach God" by Neal Privett "The Chief in Yellow" by B Michael Stevens "In the Shadow of Shattered Kadath" by Maria Mitchell "The Globe in the Satellite" by Francis Erdman "Gorillas" by Stephen Hernandez "The Shekels of Blood Field" by Jeff C Stevenson</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>58</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Henry_Fuseli">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Critics"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsInspirationsAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Henry Fuseli</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>17 April 1825</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Johann heinrich füssli was a swiss-born artist, draughtsman and artistic commentator and critic . His paintings were highly influential in the british art scene of the time, and later made an impact on american horror writer howard phillips lovecraft . In " the colour out of space " lovecraft describes the titular alien entity in the line " it was a scene from a vision of fuseli"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>Switzerland Zurich</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Johann Heinrich Füssli</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>Swiss</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Lovecraft's inspiration</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>7 February 1741</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Squire">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Squire</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"he" is a fictional character from h. p. lovecraft's 1926 short story . He is depicted as a wizard who "flouted the sanctity of things as great as space and time" the narrator of the story--as stand-in for lovecraft himself--describes his first impression upon meeting a stranger in greenwich village . The stranger leads him to his home, a private estate incongruously located in the heart of</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#He"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nor_the_Demons_Down_Under_the_Sea_%281957%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CaitlinRKiernanWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nor the Demons Down Under the Sea (1957)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"nor the demons down under the sea (1957)" is a short story by caitlin r. kiernan . It first appeared in the anthology the children of cthulhu (del rey, 2002) it is part of kiernan's dandridge cycle , a of interconnected stories about a mysterious house .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Caitlin_R._Kiernan"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dandridge_Cycle"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Children_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2002</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ken_Asamatsu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ken Asamatsu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A prolific japanese horror writer and editor who played a key role in popularizing the cthulhu mythos with a japanese audiences . Asamatsu encountered the legacy of h.p. Lovecraft when he visited ri as a teenager and read the case of charles dexter ward . Asamatsu has published two anthologies, hishin (1999) and hishinkai (2002), which also includes essays on lovecraftian topics .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>Hokkaido Japan Sapporo</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>Japanese</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://uncle-dagon.g.dgdg.jp/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>10 April 1956</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Paul_R._McNamee">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Paul R. McNamee</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author United States http://paulmcnamee.blogspot.com/ is an American speculative fiction author, based in Massachusetts , USA , who has written a handful of stories set in and around H. P. Lovecraft 's Cthulhu Mythos , as well as at least one in the occasionally-intersecting Thomas Carnacki series. These works have appeared in anthologies such as Pickman's Gallery , Lovecraftian Tales: Volume 1 , and Carnacki: The Lost Cases . Selected Works Short Fiction " One Night South of the Border " (2018) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://paulmcnamee.blogspot.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Asylum">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Asylum</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Institutions for the treatment of the mentally ill play a prominent role in cthulhu mythos fiction . In lovecraft's fiction, arkham sanitarium is committed here after a breakdown . Huntingdon madhouse sir wade jermyn is placed there in 1765 because of his wild tales of africa .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Mental health facility</ex:hasTypeL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#hasGrandChild">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label>hasGrandChild</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <owl:inverseOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#hasGrandFatherR"/>
    <owl:inverseOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#hasGrandMotherR"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dreams_in_the_Witch_House_%28Cabinet_of_Curiosities%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleFilmAndTelevisionAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Dreams in the Witch House (Cabinet of Curiosities)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"the dreams in the witch house" is an episode of the netflix television series . It is billed as an adaptation of the h.p. Lovecraft short story of the same name . Although billed as an adaptation, it has been so heavily altered as to be virtually unrecognisable .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dreams_in_the_Witch-House"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>27 October 2022</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_of_It_All">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Horror of It All</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"encrusted gems from the "crypt of cthulhu" is a collection of essays (along with selected fiction) on h.p. Lovecraft . "the title is not , as one might expect, 'the best of the crypt of cthulhu'," price noted in introduction . A second collection of crypt of cthulhu material, "black forbidden things," was published in 1992 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1990</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Henry_R._Flynn">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Henry R. Flynn</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>United States Marine Corps</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Henry r. "hank" flynn is a character created by american writer josh finney for his mythos/noir casefile:arkham comic book stories . He made his first appearance in the graphic novel casefile: arkham- nightmare on the canvas in february 2016 . A former marine, he sustained an injury amidst ancient ruins on an unspecified island, seems to have given him some connection to a mystical force which he can tap into .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Casefile:_Arkham-_Her_Blood_Runs_Cold"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Casefile:_Arkham-_Nightmare_on_the_Canvas"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Josh_Finney"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Peter_A._Worthy">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Peter A. Worthy</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Editor Artist Horror is an artist, editor and author. Overview Working mostly within the horror , and the Cthulhu Mythos in particular, has edited the fanzines Al-Azif (for which he also provided interior artwork) and The Black Book , and the anthology Rehearsals for Oblivion (Act I) ; he has also written a number of short stories and poems set within the Mythos. Selected Works Editor Al-Azif (1998) The Black Book (2002-2003) Ancient Exhumations (1999, with David Wynn) Rehearsals for Oblivion (Act I) (2006) Fiction " Sweet Despise " (1997) "Oh, Bury Me Not in Y'ha-nthlei" (1997) "The Taint of Lovecraft" (2002) "Stacked Actors" (2004) " Family Snapshots " (2007, with Ron Shiflet ) "Among the Thousand Young" (1997) "St. Toad Pays a Call" (1997) " Weak " (1997) Artwork Al-Azif (1998) Ancient Exhumations (1999) The Taint of Lovecraft (2002)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pete_Von_Sholly">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonFictionAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scholars"/>
    <rdfs:label>Pete Von Sholly</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Von sholly is an artist, filmmaker, and author who has worked on a number of lovecraftian and cthulhu mythos works . These include a wealth of artwork for ps publishing 's lovecraft illustrated series, as well as numerous non-fiction works on the . He has also illustrated works for leading authors including stephen king , joe r.lansdale, and ramsey campbell . Von sholly's history of monsters mural features over 1100 monster</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://vonshollywood.net/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Filmmaker</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu:_Le_mythe">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu: Le mythe</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft Cover Artist Goomi (first edition) Loïc Muzy (second edition) France French Bragelonne Release Date February 2012 (first edition) October 2015 (second edition) 430 Followed by - Livre II (English: Cthulhu: The Myth ) is a French- collection of the works of H. P. Lovecraft . It was published by Bragelonne in February 2012 and reissued in October 2015; the main differences appear to be different covers, Goomi in 2012, and Loïc Muzy in 2015, with Muzy supplying an additional interior art piece in the latter . Contents Introduction by Jérôme Bouscaut "La cité sans nom" (" The Nameless City ") "Le festival" (" The Festival ") "L'appel de Cthulhu" (" The Call of Cthulhu ") "L'horreur à Dunwich" (" The Dunwich Horror ") "Celui qui chuchotait dans le noir" ( The Whisperer in Darkness ) "Le cauchemar d'Innsmouth" ( The Shadow Over Innsmouth ) "La maison de la sorcière" (" The Dreams in the Witch-House ") "Le monstre sur le seuil" (" The Thing on the Doorstep ") "Celui qui hante les ténèbres" (" The Haunter of the Dark ") Gallery References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#France"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>430</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>French</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>February 2012 October 2015</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Strange_Stories">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#PulpMagazines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Strange Stories</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>February 1941 was a short-lived pulp magazine intended to be a competitor to the established leader in weird fiction, weird tales . It was edited by mort weisinger, assisted by julius schwartz, neither of whom were credited .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Michaelmas_1983">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu Michaelmas 1983</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Crypt of cthulhu 62 was the sixteenth issue of the long-running fanzine . Edited by lovecraft scholar price, it ran to a total of 62 in an a5 format . Cover was styled in the same manner as the comic book tales from the crypt .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>62</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>September 1983</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Seven_Gates_of_Slumber">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Seven Gates of Slumber</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here Ron Shiflet United States English Publication date July 1997  " " is a Cthulhu Mythos -inspired poem by American Ron Shiflet . Synopsis The seven verses - one for each "gate" - describe a series of nightmares suffered by the , culminating in his awakening, screaming and soaked in sweat. Publication History "" was first published in Imelod #7 in July 1997, and appeared in the online fanzine Nightscapes the following month.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>July 1997</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Night_of_the_Hopfrog">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Night of the Hopfrog</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The children of gla'aki is a short story by american author tim curran . It is written in the style of a video transcript and is set in the severn valley . The children of gla'aki was published in the thematic anthology the children of gla'aki in 2017 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tim_Curran"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Found-footage horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Children_of_Glaaki"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Negotium_Perambulans">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Negotium Perambulans</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>E. f. benson is a short story by english writer e. f. benson . The story details the tale of a man, who, as a child, comes to an isolated fisher village in west cornwall and is enchanted with it . When he grows up he eventually moves to london and only when his education is complete, he returns, intending never to leave again .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#E._F._Benson"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Green_God">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Green God</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Horror Under Warrendown</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The , also known as the horror under warrendown , is a great old one created by author ramsey campbell . It appears as a giant statue of a head similar to those found on easter island; however, it is composed entirely of vegetable matter . An unusual feature of the is its ability to transmute those who eat its flesh into a disgusting, rabbit-like creature .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Horror Under Warrendown</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ramsey_Campbell"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Realm_of_Shadows">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Realm of Shadows</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>John H. Crowe III Blair Reynolds Cover Artist Blair Reynolds English Call of Cthulhu Genre(s) Roleplaying game Pagan Publishing Release Date 1997 is a campaign supplement for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu horror roleplaying game. Written by John H. Crowe III and featuring artwork from Blair Reynolds , it was published by third-party content provider Pagan Publishing in 1997 . Overview Gallery References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#John_H._Crowe_III"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Roleplaying game</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1997</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#An_H._P._Lovecraft_Encyclopedia">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>An H. P. Lovecraft Encyclopedia</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Release date 2001 is a reference work written by s. t. joshi and d. e. schultz . It covers the life and work of american horror fiction writer h. p. lovecraft . It provides entries on all of lovecraft's stories, complete with synopses, publication history and word counts .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#David_E._Schultz"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#S._T._Joshi"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2001</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mormo">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CharactersIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Mormo</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Thousand-Faced Moon</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>In the trail of cthulhu roleplaying game, kenneth hite says is diana, mab, lamia, lilith, medusa, and/or several other gods, titans, and great old ones . He links her to vampires, werewolves, and dogs, and says she's an enemy of bast . In ancient greek mythology, gorgo and are names for the gorgons, of whose form the originally very beautiful medusa is given the likeness</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Innsmouth_Tabernacle_Choir_Hymnal">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Innsmouth Tabernacle Choir Hymnal</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Darrell Schweitzer Allen Koszowski Cover Artist Allen Koszowski English Zadok Allen Release Date 2004 22 is a chapbook collection of the poetry of American Darrell Schweitzer . It was published by Zadok Allen in 2004, and features interior and cover art by Allen Koszowski . Contents "Cthulhu Loves His Loyal Minions" "Give Me That Old One Religion" "Eldritch Gifts" "What a Friend We Have in Dagon" "An Eldritch Horror Is Our God" "Yes, They'll Take Our Brains to Yuggoth" "Crawling Chaos Comes for Me" "Hymn to Yog-Sothoth" "I Have a Little Idol (for children)" "Onward Dagon's Minions"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Darrell_Schweitzer"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>22</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2004</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Call_of_Cthulhu_%28DART_production%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleAudioAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Call of Cthulhu (DART production)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The call of cthulhu is an adaptation from the h. p. lovecraft historical society . It is presented in the style of a 1930's radio broadcast and runs to a total of 77 minutes . As with all of the dart productions, it is presented in the style of a 1930's radio broadcast.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Call_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ghroth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OuterGods"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ghroth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Outer_God"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nemesis"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Death Star</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Harbinger</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Maker of the Doom of Worlds</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as nemesis, death star, harbinger, maker of the doom of worlds . Created by ramsey campbell for his short story " the tugging " destroys planets as well and has destroyed the home of baoht z'uqqa-mogg .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Tugging"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ramsey_Campbell"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Huitloxopetl_%28short_story%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Huitloxopetl (short story)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Huitloxopetl was a round robin novella administered by the nightscapes online magazine in 1998 . It was contributed to by many authors, most of them members of the esoteric order of dagon amateur press association .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Call_of_Cthulhu:_Destinys_End">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Call of Cthulhu: Destiny's End</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Headfirst Publication Date N/A Survival-Horror was one of two planned follow-ups to Call of Cthulhu: Dark Corners of the Earth , the other being Call of Cthulhu: Beyond the Mountains of Madness . Overview Announced in November 2002, Destiny's End was developed by Headfirst in parallel with their Dark Corners of the Earth release. It was ultimately cancelled in 2006 after Headfirst were unsuccessful in securing a ; this cancellation was a major contributing factor in the bankruptcy of the company. Somewhat unusually for the survival-horror , it was designed to be played as a two-person cooperative game, although it could still be played by a single player. Premise The story involved two characters, Emily and Jacob, searching for clues to their past in the degenerate town of Innsmouth , solving problems and fighting Mythos monsters along the way.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>Unknown</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brotherhood_of_the_Black_Pharaoh">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cults"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Organisation"/>
    <rdfs:label>Brotherhood of the Black Pharaoh</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nyarlathotep"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The brotherhood is a cult of wealthy egyptians and sudanese . They worship nyarlathotep, specifically his avatar of the black pharaoh . They meet at irregular intervals to perform rituals in the desert near the pyramids in saqqara and in the network of caves below the sphinx of giza .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNotableMemberO>Audrey Penhew the Dark Sorcerer</ex:hasNotableMemberO>
    <ex:hasNotableMemberO>Children of the Sphinx</ex:hasNotableMemberO>
    <ex:hasNotableMemberO>The Brotherhood cultists who operates in the darkest corners of the Earth</ex:hasNotableMemberO>
    <ex:hasPurposeO>Prepare Earth for the return of Great Old Ones .</ex:hasPurposeO>
    <ex:hasPurposeO>Silence any who attempts to expose them.</ex:hasPurposeO>
    <ex:hasTypeO>Cult</ex:hasTypeO>
    <ex:isLocatedAtO>Bent Pyramid</ex:isLocatedAtO>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_House_of_the_Worm_%28Gary_Myers_short_story%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The House of the Worm (Gary Myers short story)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"the house of the worm" is a short story by american author gary myers . It tells of a house resting in the five black pillars of a round hill in vornai in the plain of kaar . The narrator tells that it is an abandoned house which at sunset comes alive as the windows open red like eyes . The man who built the house was the last to live in a banquet of the house and has since gone mad .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gary_Myers"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Italian</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Arkham_Collector"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Summer 1970</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>Summer 1970</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Older_Gods">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MediaAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Older Gods</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Is a low british lovecraftian horror film . It contains strong cosmic horror motifs, such as an ancient cult . Filming was squeezed in between lockdowns during the covid-19 pandemic .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United Kingdom</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasWriterW>David A. Roberts</ex:hasWriterW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2023</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Fishers_from_Outside">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Fishers from Outside</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story was first published in 1988 in the magazine crypt of cthulhu . It reappeared in the anthology the new lovecraft circle . In the latter, the identity of the story's great old one is changed to groth-golka .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lin_Carter"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Crypt of Cthulhu #54</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>April 1988</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Aaron_Polson">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Aaron Polson</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Aaron A. Polson Connection to the Mythos Author Birth Date 15 March 1975 Clay Center, Kansas, USA United States Years Active 2008 - 2015 http://aaronpolson.blogspot.com/ Aaron A. Polson (born 15 March 1975) is an American author and teacher who has written at least one short story set in the Cthulhu Mythos established with the works of Howard Phillips Lovecraft . Selected Works Short Fiction " Ngiri's Catch " (2011) External Links at The Internet Speculative Fiction Database</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Clay Center Kansas</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Aaron A. Polson</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://aaronpolson.blogspot.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:hasYearsActiveR>2008 - 2015</ex:hasYearsActiveR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>15 March 1975</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pickmans_Model_%28Max_D._Stanton%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Pickman's Model (Max D. Stanton)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>" pickman's model " is a short story by american max d. stanton . Published in the ulthar press thematic anthology pickman's gallery in 2018 . Functions as something of a prequel to h.p. Lovecraft's 1927 short story .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Max_D._Stanton"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pickmans_Gallery"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2018</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
