  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shadows_of_the_Evening">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Shadows of the Evening</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>'magdalena schön' is a shy young woman from buffalo, new york . She goes to live with her wealthy, stroke-ridden great-aunt in edmundston, massachusetts . Magdalena hears and becomes obsessed by a young man with a beautiful voice .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Joyce_Carol_Oates"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>The Collector of Hearts: New Tales of the Grotesque</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1998 November 2</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Idh-yaa">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Idh-yaa</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Outer space Offspring Ghatanothoa Ythogtha Zoth-Ommog Cthylla Cthulhu Great Old Ones First appearance " The Xothic Legend Cycle " Created by Lin Carter , The Mighty Mother , is a deity in the Cthulhu Mythos , who gave birth to the Star-Spawn of Cthulhu on a planet in the Xoth Star system, after mating with Cthulhu . She specifically produced the sons of Cthulhu: Ghatanothoa , Ythogtha , and Zoth-Ommog ; and a daughter of Cthulhu: Cthylla . Also once called Quum-yaa. was created by Lin Carter . Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCharacterMateC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Xothic Legend Cycle</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lin_Carter"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Averoigne_Legacy">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Averoigne Legacy</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Tribute tales in the world of clark ashton smith is an anthology edited by edward stasheff . It features stories and poetry connected to smith's pseudo-historical setting of averoigne . It was published by pickman's press in 2019, with the permission of casiana enterprises, smith's literary executors .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Edward_Stasheff"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>editor</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>424</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2019 December 9</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#On_a_Grecian_Colonnade_in_a_Park">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>On a Grecian Colonnade in a Park</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here H. P. Lovecraft United States English Arkham House Beyond the Wall of Sleep Date of writing September 1920 date 1943 " " is a poem by the late American Howard Phillips Lovecraft . Although it was written in September 1920, it did not see until its inclusion in the 1943 Arkham House collection Beyond the Wall of Sleep . Synopsis The ten four-line verses describe an uncanny episode experienced by the narrator regarding Ancient Greece. History Beyond the Wall of Sleep (1943) Collected Poems (1963) The World of H. P. Lovecraft (1971) Fungi from Yuggoth and Other Poems (1971) The Ancient Track: The Complete Poetical Works of H. P. Lovecraft (2001, 2013) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Beyond_the_Wall_of_Sleep"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1943</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>September 1920</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Yuletide_Mementos">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Yuletide Mementos</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mark McLaughlin Michael Sheehan, Jr. English Nightmares &amp; Tentacles: 13 Tales Of The Cthulhu Mythos " : A Tale of Lovecraftian Horror " is a short story by authors Mark McLaughlin and Michael Sheehan, Jr. Synopsis History ": A Tale Of Lovecraftian Horror" was published in the collections Nightmares &amp; Tentacles: 13 Tales Of The Cthulhu Mythos and The Prisoner of Carcosa &amp; More Tales Of The Bizarre (2020).</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Michael Sheehan. Jr.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Nightmares</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Tentacles: 13 Tales Of The Cthulhu Mythos</ex:isPublishedAsW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#David_Grilla">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>David Grilla</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Illustrator , sometimes David J. Grilla, is an artist. Overview Providing both interior and cover art for a multitude of publications, with regards to the Cthulhu Mythos has worked on a number of publications for Chaosium 's popular horror roleplaying game Call of Cthulhu . Selected Works Terrors From Beyond (2009) Arkham Now (2010) Atomic-Age Cthulhu (2013) See Also Images by</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Illustrator</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Madeline_Marshs_Midlife_Crisis">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Madeline Marsh's Midlife Crisis</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>K. Z. Morano English James Ward Kirk Publishing Lovecraft after Dark date November 2015 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos story by K. Z. Morano. It was published in the James Ward Kirk Publishing anthology Lovecraft after Dark in November 2015. Synopsis After his wife Madeline returns home to Innsmouth to be with her love Dagon , a man nicknamed Mister Snow travels to the coastal town to win her back from what he believes to be her small-town lover. When he gets there, he is captured by a group of degenerate Deep Ones attempting to restore their genetic code and used as breeding stock. Although Snow is almost successful in escaping with the help of a young girl, his attempt to rescue his wife results only in him bearing witness to her final transformation and his recapture by the locals.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>K. Z. Morano</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_after_Dark"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#David_Conyers">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>David Conyers</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Games Designer Editor Birth Date 30 May 1971 Sydney, Australia Australia Horror Science Fiction https://david-conyers.com/ is an Australian author, editor, games designer, and occasional artist who works primarily in the Lovecraftian horror and science fiction genres. He has written extensively for games manufacturer Chaosium , with his work there including both roleplaying game supplements and related fiction. Much of his Cthulhu Mythos fiction revolves around the character Harrison Peel , a military intelligence officer who has spent his career battling eldritch horrors. Selected Bibliography Editor Cthulhu's Dark Cults (2010) Author The Yuggoth Island of Doctor Moreau (2022) The Nightmare Dimension (2013) Nanofabrica (2020) Cthulhu Reloaded (2021) Cthulhu Resurgent (2021) Cthulhu Remorseless (2021) Cthulhu Unmasked (2021) "Regrowth" (2006) "Outside Looking In" (2006) "Made of Meat" (2005) "Impossible Object" (2005) "False Containment" (2005) "Stomach Acid" (2006, with Brian M. Sammons ) "The Spiraling Worm" (2007, with John Sunseri) "The Masked Messenger" (2011, with John Goodrich ) "The Weaponized Puzzle" (2013) "Driven Underground" (2013) "The Road to Afghanistan" (2013) "The R'lyeh Singularity" (2013, with Brian M. Sammons) "The Eye of Infinity" (2014) "The Elder Codex" (2014) "War Gods of Men" (2014, with David Kernot ) " The Bullet and the Flesh " (2014, with David Kernot) "The Temporal Deception" (2015, with C. J. Henderson) "Tears in Yellow" (2021) "The Gravity Museum" (2021) "Weapons Grade" (2021) "The Mouth of Jupiter" (2021) "Supersymmetry Blues" (2021) "Invisible Demons" (2021, with Brian M. Sammons) Devil's Children (1993) A Handful of Dust (2003) Secrets of Kenya (2008) Terrors From Beyond (2009, contributor) See Also Images by External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>http://example.org/lovecraft#Australia Sydney</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>http://example.org/lovecraft#Australia</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://david-conyers.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games Designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>30 May 1971</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Moon-beast">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Moon-beast</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The moon-beasts were toadlike creatures living in the dreamlands ' version of earth's moon . They were created by howard phillips lovecraft in " the dream-quest of unknown kadath " they are pale, squat, toadlike creatures, superhumanly strong and noxious-smelling . They communicate using flutes, being otherwise silent even in death .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dream-Quest_of_Unknown_Kadath"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Green">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Green</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>1925</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>First Mate Green</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as First Mate 1925 Human First appearance HPL : " The Call of Cthulhu " Created by H. P. Lovecraft is a fictional character created by H. P. Lovecraft , who makes his only appearance in the 1928 short story " The Call of Cthulhu ". Overview was the first mate of the Emma of Auburn. En route to Callao, the Emma encountered the heavily armored steam yacht Alert , who ordered the schooner to turn back. Captain Collins refused, leading the Alert to fire upon the Emma using a brass cannon. In retaliation, the crew of the Emma managed to board the Alert and slay all of its members, although and Collins were killed in the battle, leaving Second Mate Gustaf Johansen in command of the crew.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Call_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arthur_C._Clarke">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosInspiredAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Arthur C. Clarke</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>19 March 2008</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sir arthur charles clarke was a british author, inventor, explorer and television personality . He is widely regarded as one of the most influential science fiction writers of all time . His rama and space odyssey series are considered classics of the genre .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>http://example.org/lovecraft#England Minehead Somerset</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Sir Arthur Charles Clarke</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United Kingdom</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>16 December 1917</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#From_Beyond">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>From Beyond</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>" celephasnyarlathotep" is a short story by writer h. p. lovecraft . It was written in 1920 and was first published in the fantasy fan in 1934 . The story is told from the first person perspective of an unnamed narrator . It details his experiences with a scientist named crawford tillinghast .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Fantasy_Fan"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>June 1934</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Necrophosis">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Necrophosis</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The game promises a chilling and atmospheric experience set in a nightmarish world filled with grotesque forms and eerie landscapes . Is being developed by the indie studio dragonis ares and adonis brosteanu .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Taken_by_the_Scholars_Choice_Miskatonic_State_University_Edition_of_Shakespeare:_The_Official_Drinking_Game">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Taken by the Scholar's Choice Miskatonic State University Edition of Shakespeare: The Official Drinking Game</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Taken by the Scholar’s Choice Miskatonic State University Edition of Shakespeare: The Official Drinking Game White Jack Hunter Garrett Martin-Churchill Publication Date October 2015 Party game Taken by the Scholar’s Choice Miskatonic State University Edition of Shakespeare: The Official Drinking Game is a humorous Cthulhu Mythos party game created by White Jack Hunter Garrett Martin-Churchill and self-published through Amazon's digital Kindle platform in October 2015. Overview As given away in the title, Taken by the Scholar's Choice … is a drinking game which in which participants must take a drink each time a particular motif from Lovecraftian fiction or Shakespearean play appears in a story. Oddly enough, this would seem to imply that the game is designed for only a single player, unless the story is being relayed to the players via audiobook.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tentacled_Dreams">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tentacled Dreams</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here Ron Shiflet United States English Publication date March 1999 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos poem by American Ron Shiflet . Synopsis Recounting of black dreams experienced by mortals and influenced by the Great Old Ones , possibly even Great Cthulhu himself. Publication History "" was published in the E. P. Berglund -edited online fanzine Nightscapes in March 1999.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 1999</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crossroad_Press">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CrossroadPress"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Publishers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crossroad Press</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>2009 David Niall Wilson United States Horror Science Fiction Fantasy Macabre Press https://crossroadpress.com/ is a United States based horror and science fiction publisher formed in 2009 as Macabre Ink Digital by David Niall Wilson . It publishes re-releases of out of print horror classics, audiobooks of individuals like Clive Barker and Brian Lumley , plus also new releases of independent authors. It has done numerous Cthulhu Mythos and Lovecraftian works over its history including the Titus Crow audiobooks, CT Phipps ' Cthulhu Armageddon , The Harry Stubbs series, Andrew Doran books, and the Books of Cthulhu anthologies. Its non- Lovecraft related works include re-publishing the Stargate SG-1 series and Vampire: The Masquerade novels. Selected Publications Anthologies Tales of the Al-Azif (2019) Tales of Yog-Sothoth (2021) Time Loopers (2020) The Book of Yig (2021) Tales of Nyarlathotep (2023) Eldritch Prisoners (2023) The Book of Hastur (upcoming) Collections The Adventures of Andrew Doran: Box Set (2015, Matthew Davenport ) The Compleat Crow (2015, Brian Lumley ) Dagon's Bell and Other Discords (2017, Brian Lumley) Return of the Deep Ones and Other Mythos Tales (2017, Brian Lumley) The Second Wish and Other Exhalations (2017, Brian Lumley) Fruiting Bodies and Other Fungi (2018, Brian Lumley) Haggopian and Other Stories (2019, Brian Lumley) The Taint and Other Novellas (2019, Brian Lumley) Brian Lumley's Freaks (2019, Brian Lumley) The Caller of the Black (2019, Brian Lumley) The Horror at Oakdeene and Others (2020, Brian Lumley) Andrew Doran Omnibus: Volume 1 (2021, Matthew Davenport) The Nonesuch and Others (2021, Brian Lumley) The Call of Distant Shores (2013, David Niall Wilson) The Best of the Rest (2023, Brian Lumley) Short Tall Tales (2023, Brian Lumley) Novels The Statement of Andrew Doran (2013) Andrew Doran at the Mountains of Madness (2018) Andrew Doran and the Scroll of Nightmares (2021) Cthulhu Armageddon (2016) The Tower of Zhaal (2017) The Tree of Azathoth (2023) The Elder Ice (2018) Broken Meats (2018) Alien Stars (2018) Hide and Seek (2023) The Fly-By-Nights (2011, Brian Lumley ) Beneath the Moors (2019, Brian Lumley ) Star-Spawned: Lovecraftian Horror (2022, Mark Howard Jones ) Letters to the Editor of Cthulhu Sex Magazine (2019, Oliver Baer) Audiobooks Hero of Dreams (2022) Ship of Dreams (2022) Mad Moon of Dreams (2022) Iced on Aran (2022) The Burrowers Beneath (2015) The Transition of Titus Crow (2015) The Clock of Dreams (2015) Spawn of the Winds (2015) In the Moon of Borea (2015) Elysia (2015) The Compleat Crow (2015) Haggopian and Other Stories: A Cthulhu Mythos Collection (2019, Brian Lumley ) The Taint and Other Novellas (2019, Brian Lumley)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://crossroadpress.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gharoide">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gharoide</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Gharoides are small, carnivorous creatures with three eyes, three fur-covered antennae and a long proboscis . Their only known appearance to date is in the short story " genuine article " by j. B. lee in which a christian evangelical, shana mcewan, summons one to murder a new age bookseller .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Genuine_Article"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#J._B._Lee"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Elder_Signs_Press">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DefunctRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SmallPress"/>
    <rdfs:label>Elder Signs Press</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Current Status Defunct 2003 William Jones United States Lake Orion, Michigan Distribution Method Independent Publishers Group Dimension Books is an American independent publisher with a focus on Cthulhu Mythos and Lovecraftian fiction. Based in Lake Orion, Michigan, it was by William Jones in 2003. Elder Sign has published works by a number of popular names in contemporary Lovecraftiana, including Ron Shiflet , C. J. Henderson , James Ambuehl , Tim Curran , Richard A. Lupoff and Stanley C. Sargent . issued its last confirmed release in July 2018; as of October 2023, its website was no longer extant. Selected Publications Periodicals Book of Dark Wisdom (2003-2006) Collections Terrors (2005, Richard A. Lupoff ) Looking for Darla: Stories of Mythos Noir (2008, Ron Shiflet ) Anthologies Hardboiled Cthulhu (2006) High Seas Cthulhu (2007) Other The Cthulhu Mythos Encyclopedia (2012) External Links at Wikipedia at The Internet Speculative Fiction Database</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Audio">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Elder_Worm">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdfs:label>Elder Worm</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The elder worms are a malevolent race of slug-like bipedal creatures . They communicate via telepathy and can borrow through rock as fast as they run on the surface . They worship qlipothic entities who in exchange for the sacrifice of sentient beings, grant them gifts of power .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sun_and_Sentinel">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sun and Sentinel</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Avery Kit Malone English Rogue Planet Press Lovecraftiana Vol 5, Issue 2 date July 2020  " " is a Lovecraftian short story by Avery Kit Malone. It was published in Vol 5, Issue 2 of the Lovecraftiana zine by Rogue Planet Press in July 2020. Synopsis Told in the second person, the story relates how the reader might travel to a small town near Pittsburgh and, while exploring the dilapidated buildings, discover a kinship with the townsfolk, who may not be exactly what they seem. Furthermore, the reader might take it upon themselves to descend into the abandoned mines, where they will meet an ancient sleeping sentinel, a leviathan whose time to awaken may be near…</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Avery Kit Malone</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Issue 2</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Lovecraftiana Vol 5</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>July 2020</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scathach">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Scathach</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Created by kevin l. o'brien is a character from gaelic mythology introduced into the cthulhu mythos in the story " october surprise " by kevin l. o'brien . Her name means "the shadowy one" in gaelic .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#October_Surprise"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Kevin_L._OBrien"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brood_of_Ubbo-Sathla">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonSapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Brood of Ubbo-Sathla</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ubbo-Sathla"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Spawn of Ubbo-Sathla</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The are a fictional race created by chaosium in 1994 for its call of cthulhu rpg . They are the outer god 's primal spawn which look like giant one-celled creatures, no two of which are alike .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ubbo-Sathla"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dunwich_Horror_%28Suspense%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleAudioAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Dunwich Horror (Suspense)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Dunwich Horror Based on " The Dunwich Horror " Ronald Coleman Brett Morrison Production Company CBS Radio Release date November 1945 United States English " The Dunwich Horror " is an abridged radio adaptation of the H. P. Lovecraft short story of the same name . It was created as part of long-running American radio drama series Suspense and first broadcast on 1 November 1945. External Links " The Dunwich Horror " at the Internet Archive</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dunwich_Horror"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>November 1945</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sherlock_Holmes_and_the_Mystery_at_Miskatonic_University">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sherlock Holmes and the Mystery at Miskatonic University</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>46is a short story by american writer zebulon zachariah . It was independently published as a digital chapbook in june 2023 . A paperback edition was released in august that year .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Zebulon Zachariah</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>46</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>June 2023</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Paul_Roulet">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Paul Roulet</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>1730s</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Paul is part of the roulet family that moved from caude, in france, to rhode island in 1686 . The story suggests that the family be related to jacques roulet of caude . It's thought that he "probably provoked" a riot in the 1730s that "wiped out the family"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shunned_House"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Old_One_%28Wonderland%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Old One (Wonderland)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The old ones are a race or group of demonic-like godlike beings in the grimm fairy tales multiverse . They were first identified as old ones in issue #2 of the 1st volume of "call of wonderland" in 2012 . The first old one to appear was the jabberwocky in issue #16 of the 1st volume of the grimm fairy tales in 2007 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>( ADJ ): Call of Wonderland Vol 2 2</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Dan Wickline</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Joe Brusha</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Ralph Tedesco</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Raven Gregory</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Pickman_Original">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Pickman Original</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Pickman's gallery date 2018is a short story by american logan noble . It was published in the matthew carpenter -edited anthology pickman's gallery . Richy art lover walter learns that a previously-unseen collection of pickman's works has come up for sale .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Logan Noble</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pickmans_Gallery"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2018</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Supernatural_Horror_in_Literature">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Supernatural Horror in Literature</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The essay was first published in august 1927 in the sole issue of the amateur press magazine the recluse . Portions of a revised and expanded version were published in the fantasy fan from 1933-35 . The complete revised version appeared in the arkham house collection the outsider and others in 1939 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The Recluse</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1927</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>1926-1933</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ayiig">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ayi'ig</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Serpent Goddess</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as The Serpent Goddess Current location A deep canyon in Texas, USA Relatives Yig (parent) Yidhra (parent) First appearance EXP : "A Movement in the Grass" Created by Walter C. DeBill Jr. ( The Serpent Goddess ) is a fictional entity created by Walter C. DeBill, Jr. for his short story "A Movement in the Grass". Overview The daughter of the Great Old One Yig and the Outer Goddess Yidhra , appears as an octopus-like monstrosity with serpentine eyes and detachable tentacles that move independently. She lives in a cavern within a deep canyon somewhere in the state of Texas, USA . In Other Media In the mobile game Crash Fever , the character Snakeskin Moonwhite Ig was based on Ay'ig. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isLocatedAtC>A deep canyon in Texas</ex:isLocatedAtC>
    <ex:isLocatedAtC>USA</ex:isLocatedAtC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>A Movement in the Grass</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Walter C. DeBill Jr.</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mythos_Books">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DefunctRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SmallPress"/>
    <rdfs:label>Mythos Books</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>1996 United States LLC was an American small press publisher which specialises in the works of H. P. Lovecraft , the Cthulhu Mythos , horror, supernatural, weird and fantastic fiction. Amongst the authors whose works it has published are C. J. Henderson , Stanley C. Sargent , Robert M. Price , Jeffrey Thomas and Richard L. Tierney . does not appear to have published any material since May 2011, and must unfortunately be considered to be defunct . Selected Publications Periodicals Crypt of Cthulhu (1999 - 2001) Anthologies Mythos Tales &amp; Others (1996) Collections Correlated Contents (1998, James Ambuehl ) Ancient Exhumations (1999, Stanley C. Sargent ) The Taint of Lovecraft (2002, Stanley C. Sargent) Shards of Darkness (2000, E. P. Berglund ) The Tales of Inspector Legrasse (2005, C. J. Henderson ) Unholy Dimensions (2005, Jeffrey Thomas ) The Black Sutra (2006, Walter C. DeBill, Jr. ) Dark Wisdom: New Tales of the Old Ones (2007, Gary Myers ) Sesqua Valley &amp; Other Haunts (2008, W. H. Pugmire ) Blasphemies &amp; Revelations (2008, Robert M. Price ) Novels The Lovecraft Chronicles (2004, Peter Cannon ) The Drums of Chaos (2008, Richard L. Tierney ) Chapbooks In the Yaddith Time (2007, Ann K. Schwader ) Non-Fiction The Rise and Fall of the Cthulhu Mythos (2008, S. T. Joshi ) Other Works The Lovecraft Tarot (2002, second edition) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#One_Night_South_of_the_Border">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>One Night South of the Border</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Illegitimate descendant of controversial artist travels to mexico in search of one of his forebears' paintings . Tracks it to the lair of local drug lord cortez before summoning his monstrous family of ghouls to dispose of both the footsoldiers and cortez himself .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Paul_R._McNamee"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pickmans_Gallery"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2018</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
