  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Orrin_Grey">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosInspiredAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Orrin Grey</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Lovecraftian fiction https://orringrey.com/  is an author who has contributed a number of stories to the Cthulhu Mythos begun with the works of Howard Phillips Lovecraft . He has also written tie-in pieces for traditional games such as Warmachine and Iron Kingdoms . His Lovecraftian works has appeared in anthologies such as Historical Lovecraft (2011, Innsmouth Free Press ), The Book of Cthulhu II (2012, Night Shade Books), and The Children of Gla'aki (2017, Dark Regions Press ). Grey cites E. F. Benson , Fritz Leiber , and Mike Mignola as amongst his major influences . Selected Bibliography Short Fiction " The Labyrinth of Sleep " (2011) " Black Hill " (2011) " Invaders of Gla'aki " (2017) External Links at The Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://orringrey.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Kitsune">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SpeciesIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdfs:label>Kitsune</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>hu-ching</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Multi-tailed fox spirits are part of chinese and japanese folklore . First appeared in china and over time migrated to korea and then japan . Can live up to 900 years and upon dying either reincarnate as a different being or return to the spirit world .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Secrets_of_Japan"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Broodling_of_Chthon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Broodling of Chthon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Daughters of Chthon</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The broodlings of chthon first appeared in 2016 in issue #11 of the 2nd volume of the carnage comic book series . They are a fictional race in the marvel universe which first appeared in 2016 in issue #11 of the 2nd volume of the carnage comic book series .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Carnage Vol 2 #11</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Gerry Conway</ex:wasCreatedByC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Mike Perkins</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#On_Icy_Kinarth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>On Icy Kinarth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The planet kynarth (spelled with a "y") was incorporated into the cthulhu mythos in the short story " through the gates of the silver key ", by h.p. Lovecraft . The poem describes a dream the narrator has, in which they find themselves standing at the edge of a cliff on "icy kinarth" a predatory "fleshless thing" is drawn to and attacks</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Frank_Belknap_Long"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Weird_Tales"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>April 1930</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Glyph">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Glyph</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Created by ian davey (real name unknown) is a primary character and main protagonist in ian davey 's bh'yhlun series . Made her first appearance in " an incarceration in bh'yhlun " published in the september 1997 edition of edward p. berglund 's online fanzine nightscapes .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#An_Incarceration_in_BhYhlun"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ian_Davey"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hippocampus_Press">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Publishers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Hippocampus Press</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Hippocampus is an american publishing house focusing on the horror . Founded in 1999 by derrick hussey, it publishes the works of lovecraft . They also produce periodicals and non-fiction works related to the horror .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://hippocampuspress.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Roby_Alzada_Place">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSRelatives"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Roby Alzada Place</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>28 January 1899</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lovecraft’s maternal grandmother.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isRelativeToR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1836</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#White_polypous_thing">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonSapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>White polypous thing</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sociocultural Characteristics Presumably aquatic Physical Characteristics Eye colour Glowing Possibly amorphous Real World Information First appearance HPL : " The Call of Cthulhu " Created by H. P. Lovecraft The was a creature created by H. P. Lovecraft for his short story " The Call of Cthulhu ". Description Apparently existing somewhere in the swamplands south of the city of New Orleans, Lousiana, "The Call of Cthulhu" gives a very brief description in a single passage: From this, and the fact that the degenerate cult encountered by the police in the bayous were known to be worshippers of the Great Old Ones , it may be speculated that the belonged to that ancient race, although this theory cannot be confirmed with the information to hand. One counterpoint could be that the Great Old Ones are stated by the cult to be lying dead "inside the earth and under the sea", whereas the was quite possibly awake and active (see below). Possible Sighting In "The Call of Cthulhu", in the aftermath of the police's battle with the cult, the narrator interviews one of the officers, "an excitable Spaniard" named Joseph D. Galvez, who hints that he bore witness to However, the narrator is unsure whether this is something which Galvez had actually experienced, or the product of a fertile imagination overstimulated from the horrors of the evening. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasDistinctionC>Possibly amorphous</ex:hasDistinctionC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Call_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Luveh-Keraphf">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Luveh-Keraphf</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Priest of Bast Human First appearance CIRCLE : " The Suicide in the Study " Created by Robert Bloch is a fictional character invented by Robert Bloch as an homage to his friend H. P. Lovecraft . He was said to be a priest of Bast from the time of Egypt's 13th Dynasty; his worship of the cat-headed goddess is an in-joke referring to Lovecraft's love of cats . The character is first mentioned in Bloch's " The Suicide in the Study ", published in the June 1935 issue of Weird Tales . The story refers to "the Black Rites of Luveh-Keraph, priest of Bast ". " The Grinning Ghoul ", in the June 1936 Weird Tales , cites "the grotesque Black Rites of mystic , the priest of cryptic Bast".</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Suicide_in_the_Study"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_Bloch"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Deep_Fried_Horror_Cthulhu_Cheese_Burger">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Deep Fried Horror Cthulhu Cheese Burger</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Becky Narron English Deadman's Tome Release Date January 2020 58  is a tongue-in-cheek anthology of Lovecraftian fiction first published in January 2020 by Deadman's Tome. Edited by Jesse Dedman , both trade paperback and digital editions were released. Overview Contents " The Extraordinary Lineage of Ethan McKay " by James Carlson " Cthulhu Gets A Job " by Mr. Deadman " The Reading: Three Variants on a Theme " by James Dorr " The Dorm Mate Out of Dunwich " by R. C. Mulhare " The Macabre and Blasphemous Horror of Chocolate Cake Twinkles " by M. Kelly Peach " The Way Things Are, the Way Things Will Be " by John Wolf</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>58</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>January 2020</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Copyright_status_of_works_by_H._P._Lovecraft">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Copyright status of works by H. P. Lovecraft</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>In the united states, the copyright on works published before 1978 expires after 95 years . This means that as of 2024, all works published before 1928 are in the public domain . Questions center on who exactly owns or owned the copyrights .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#JamesAllisonCycle">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Cyprus_Shell">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#BrianLumleyWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Cyprus Shell</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A british officer explains his seafood phobia by recounting the story of a deadly encounter with a mysterious mollusc . The discovery of murex hypnotica occurs in cyprus, near "cephos, between cephos and kyrenia on the coast" this is the first mention in print of the monstrous cthaat aquadingen .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Arkham_Collector"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>Summer 1968</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brood">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Brood</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The are a reptilian-like demonic insectoid race which reproduces by infecting sentient lifeforms with their eggs . Known for enslaving races and working them to their death (including the sentient, miles long, faster-than-light whale ships called acanti) possess conventional technology including faster-than-light conventional starships, directed energy weapons, and computers .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Derek_Adams">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Derek Adams</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Glasgow, Scotland Private investigator Human First appearance The Amulet Created by William Meikle is a character created by Scottish author William Meikle for his Midnight Eye Files series of occult detective stories. He made his first appearance in the 2005 novel The Amulet , and has since appeared in at least fifteen different stories. Overview Adams is a hard-drinking, heavy-smoking private detective based in Glasgow, Scotland, he is a man with an open mind and a certain experience in dealing with the supernatural. His encounters with the bizarre include a run-in with brain-snatching Mi-Go , and a team-up with Arkham private dick Frank Nardi while on the case of mysterious signals from the great beyond. Selected Appearances " Call and Response " (2017) " Eeny Meeny Miney Mi-Go " (2019)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Amulet</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#William_Meikle"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPL:_The_History">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>HPL: The History</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lin Carter United States English Genre(s) Non-fiction SSR Publications Howard Phillips Lovecraft: Memoirs, Critiques &amp; Bibliographies date 1955 " " is a short non-fiction piece by the late and Howard Phillips Lovecraft scholar Lin Carter . It first saw print in George T. Wetzel 's collection of scholarly works Howard Phillips Lovecraft: Memoirs, Critiques &amp; Bibliographies in 1955. History Howard Phillips Lovecraft: Memoirs, Critiques &amp; Bibliographies (1955) Sword &amp; Fantasy #7 (2006)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lin_Carter"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Bibliographies</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Critiques</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Howard Phillips Lovecraft: Memoirs</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1955</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tangled">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tangled</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A. Henry Keene English James Ward Kirk Publishing Lovecraft after Dark date November 2015  " " is a Lovecraftian poem by A. Henry Keene . It was published in the James Ward Kirk Publishing thematic anthology Lovecraft after Dark in November 2015. Synopsis The fourteen lines seem to tell of two beings whose love has caused both their minds and bodies to become intermingled.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#A._Henry_Keene"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraft_after_Dark"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#W_Paul_Cook">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSFriendsAndAcquaintances"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>W Paul Cook</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1948</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Publisher of the Monadnock Monthly, the Vagrant, and other amateur journals; a longtime amateur journalist, printer, and life-long friend of Lovecraft. In 1927 Cook issued the Recluse, containing Lovecraft’s “Supernatural Horror in Literature”.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1881</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jack_Russell">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Jack Russell</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Werewolf by night is a superhero in the fictional marvel universe, who was created in 1972, a c-list hero with a few comic book series . He is the son of a transylvanian nobleman and baron, whose family had been cursed by the elder god chthon .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Werewolf_by_Night_Vol_2"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Marvel Spotlight Vol 1 2</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Gerry Conway</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Born_of_the_Winds">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#BrianLumleyWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Born of the Winds</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story is a sequel to august derleth's " the thing that walked on the wind" it is a story that was nominated for a world fantasy award . It relates the efforts of a meteorologist to help a woman find her son .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Science Fiction</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The Magazine of Fantasy</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 1975</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>1972-1973</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Weird_Writer_in_Our_Midst:_Early_Criticism_of_H._P._Lovecraft">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Weird Writer in Our Midst: Early Criticism of H. P. Lovecraft</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>S. T. Joshi United States English Genre(s) Non-fiction Hippocampus Press Release Date December 2010 263 is a non-fiction work by leading Lovecraft scholar S. T. Joshi . Overview A diverse collection of early critical pieces on Lovecraft along with analyses and obituaries from the 1930s and 1940s, A Weird Writer in Our Midst features pieces from not only his contemporaries but also those who knew him. Compiled by S. T. Joshi and running to a total of 263 , it was published by Hippocampus Press in December 2010.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#S._T._Joshi"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>263</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>December 2010</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Black_Rituals_of_Koth-Serapis">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosBooksFictional"/>
    <rdfs:label>Black Rituals of Koth-Serapis</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Koth-serapis hyperborea is an ancient compendium of evil magic written by the hyperborean sorcerer koth-serapis during the days of acheron . The majority of its contents are unknown, but its study was frowned upon during the hyperborean age, and still is in modern times .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Russells_Guide_to_Interdimensional_Entities,_Artefacts_and_Incantations">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Russell's Guide to Interdimensional Entities, Artefacts and Incantations</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Russell's guide to interdimensional entities , artefacts and incantations is a fictional "field guide" to the cthulhu mythos . Like the predecessor, this book also includes articles on locations such as r'lyeh . Each article is accompanied by brand-new artwork .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#B._A._Lee"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>92</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Publishers">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nathaniel_Elton">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dreamers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nathaniel Elton</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Relatives Basil Elton (grandfather) Lighthouse keeper Human First appearance EXP : The Complete Dreamlands Created by Chaosium is a fictional character created by Chaosium for their horror-roleplaying game Call of Cthulhu , making his first appearance in the supplement The Complete Dreamlands Overview is the grandson of Basil Elton , the protagonist of H. P. Lovecraft 's 1919 short story " The White Ship ." In the expanded Cthulhu Mythos of Call of Cthulhu Elton, with the help of fellow dreamer Ward Phillips , was able to navigate the one safe path through the Basalt Pillars of the West to reach Cathuria . ( EXP : The Complete Dreamlands ).</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Complete Dreamlands</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Littlest_Shoggoth:_A_Holiday_Tale_of_the_Cthulhu_Mythos">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Storybooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Littlest Shoggoth: A Holiday Tale of the Cthulhu Mythos</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>56 is a children's book written by stan! And published by otherworld creations in 2013 . It tells the story of kaiju-sized monster, squammy, who was bullied by the other mythos monsters in the undersea city of r'lyeh .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Stan!</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Children's</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>56</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2013 June 1</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jonathan_Thomas">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Jonathan Thomas</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Providence, Rhode Island, USA United States is a horror writer who was born in Providence , Rhode Island . He has written several stories with Lovecraftian themes, including the Cthulhu Mythos novel The Color Over Occam (Arcane Wisdom, 2013). Lovecraft scholar S. T. Joshi includes Thomas with Ramsey Campbell , T. E. D. Klein , Thomas Ligotti , and Caitlín R. Kiernan as writers who have "used Lovecraft’s mythos as a springboard for their own views on humanity’s relations to the cosmos." Selected Bibliography Novels The Color Over Occam (2013) Collections Midnight Call and Other Stories (2008) Tempting Providence and Other Stories (2010) 13 Conjurations (2013) Dreams of Ys and Other Invisible Worlds (2015) Naked Revenants and Other Fables of Old and New England (2017) Avenging Angela and Other Uncanny Encounters (2021) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads at the H. P. Lovecraft Film Festival References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA http://example.org/lovecraft#Rhode_Island Providence</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hchtelegoth_Press">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DefunctRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SmallPress"/>
    <rdfs:label>H'chtelegoth Press</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Current Status Defunct 2000 R. S. Cartwright United States was a small-press publisher by author R. S. Cartwright which focused on works set in the greater Cthulhu Mythos . Issuing its first publication in 2000, as of 2011 it appears to be closed for business. The publisher derived its name from the Great Old One H'chtelegoth , an entity created by Cartwright for his 2000 short story "The Stranger &amp; H'chtelegoth". Selected Publications Periodicals Dark Legacy (2000-2001) Collections Innsmouth Tales - 2000 (2000) Of Gods &amp; Aliens (2011) External Links at The Internet Speculative Fiction Database</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Wilhelmshaven">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsIncorporatedFromTheRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Wilhelmshaven</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Germany Town Humans First appearance HPL : " The Temple " is a real world town located on the northern coast of Germany, mentioned in the story " The Temple " by H. P. Lovecraft . The German submarine U-29 under Lieutenant-Commander Karl Heinrich had intended to return there at the conclusion of its mission before it was lost at sea.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Town</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Humans</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Germany</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Temple"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shape_of_a_Snake">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Shape of a Snake</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A group of american cavalrymen encounter the half-breed cult of the eternal serpent who are anticipating the return of their gods . One sergeant manages to turn the tables on the cult and stop its fell masters from manifesting .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cody_Goodfellow"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#World_War_Cthulhu"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Elizabeth_Toldridge">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSFriendsAndAcquaintances"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Elizabeth Toldridge</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1940</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Poet living in Washington, DC, who corresponded with Lovecraft from 1928 to 1937. Robert Barlow visited her while traveling from Florida to Providence.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1861</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Alabaster_%28story%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CaitlinRKiernanWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Alabaster (story)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"alabaster" is a short story by caitlin r. kiernan . It features their recurring character, albino monster-hunter dancy flammarion . The story is set five days after the events of " bainbridge .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Caitlin_R._Kiernan"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dancy_Flammarion"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2003</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
    <ex:wasWrittenOnW>2003 March 18</ex:wasWrittenOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#StoryCycles">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Carcosa_%28One_Free_Elephant%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Carcosa (One Free Elephant)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Carcosa Nigel Kennington One Free Elephant Publication Date 2017 Horror board game Carcosa is a Lovecraftian board game for 1-4 players designed by Nigel Kennington with artwork by Colin Briggs, Diana Franco Campos, and Augustinas Raginskis. It was launched via a Kickstarter campaign - which easily reached its initial and stretch goals - and was published as a retail product by One Free Elephant in 2017. Overview Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Seven_Blasphemies_of_Ghattambah">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosBooksFictional"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Seven Blasphemies of Ghattambah</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Adrian Mondrago Belgium English Greek German Martian Publication Date 1417 Real World Information Created by Mark McLaughlin Michael Sheehan, Jr. is an occult book created by Mark McLaughlin and Michael Sheehan, Jr. for their Cthulhu Mythos stories. Description Written in 1417 by Belgian warlock Adrian Mondrago, ' s contents are written in English, Greek, German and, inexplicably, ancient Martian, a Rosetta Stone-like feature which would allow humans to understand the Martian . Although it’s precise contents are unknown, it was said to contain information which would assist in bringing about the return of Nyarlathotep ( EXP : " Shoggoth Apocalypse ", " The Relic In The Egyptian Gallery "). It is likely the same book which the Dr. Xorthonos was attempting to steal from the Arkham Public Museum although that book was simply named The Blasphemies of Ghattambah ( EXP : " The Relic In The Egyptian Gallery "). A copy of The Seven Blasphemies is in the possession of the Mondrago family , a bloodline with a long history of Ghattambah worship, while a Cthulhu -worshipping Deep One cult in the town of Sea Dream has another ( EXP : " In The Chapel Of Ghattambah ", " The Testament of Cthulhu ").</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasCreatedByA rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByA>Michael Sheehan. Jr.</ex:wasCreatedByA>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#hasDescendant">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label>hasDescendant</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <owl:inverseOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#hasAncestorR"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#I_Walk_the_Worlds_Black_Rim">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>I Walk the World's Black Rim</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The novella recounts the adventures of a deep one descendant warrior . He quests to destroy an enormous deep one-type monster called nindhoggir . Behind the mythosis notable for its deliberate parallelism of (fictional) folklore .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mike_Minnis"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Historical</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nightscapes"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1997</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Apollo">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Apollo</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Erik von Wodtke Douglas A. Sirois English AAM/Markosia Release Date Oct 2013 Media Type Comic book 80 is a comic book which reimagines classical Greek mythology within the Cthulhu Mythos universe. Written by Erik von Wodtke with artwork by Douglous A. Sirois, it was published in October 2013 by AAM/Markosia. Overview Synopsis Angered by the infidelity of her husband Zeus, a vengeful Hera unleashes monsters on the Earth to destroy the woman he committed adultery with and her child. The heroes battle various classical monsters and Cthulhu himself, the greatest of the monsters unleashed.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Erik von Wodtke</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>80</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>Oct 2013</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#New_York_City">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsIncorporatedFromTheRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>New York City</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lovecraft first visited the city in april 1922, invited by his future wife to stay with her for six days in her flatbush apartment . He returned to the city in july 1922 and again in august 1922 . Lovecraft moved to 169 clinton street in cobble hill, brooklyn after his marriage to sonia greene . He wrote three stories set in new york during his time there: " the horror at red hook ", written on august 1-2, 1925 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cool_Air"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#He"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_at_Red_Hook"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_from_the_Hills"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_King_in_Yellow"/>
    <ex:hasTypeL>City</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>New York state</ex:isLocatedAtL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dark_Legacy_-_The_Poetry">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dark Legacy - The Poetry</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist David Salazar English Dark Legacy Frogtown Press Release Date October 2015 106 is a collection of Lovecraftian poetry from the of the now-defunct fanzine Dark Legacy , edited by American Ran Cartwright . Running to a total of 106 and featuring cover art by David Salazar, it was published by Frogtown Press in October 2015. Overview Contents Introduction by Ran Cartwright "The Lesson" by Ron Shiflet " Eibon and the Lord of Bala-Norath " by Ron Shiflet "In Praise of Yig" by Ron Shiflet "Cynothoglys" by Ron Shiflet "The Mausoleum" by Ron Shiflet "Spawn of Cthulhu" by Ron Shiflet " The Elder Things " by Ron Shiflet "Beneath Darkened Skies" by Ron Shiflet "The Art of Pickman" by Ron Shiflet "Changing" by Ron Shiflet "Dunwich" by Ron Shiflet "West of Dunwich" by Ron Shiflet "Return to Arkham" by Ron Shiflet "The Wind-Walker" by Ron Shiflet " Commoriom Vengeance " by Ron Shiflet "Maal-Dweb's Maze" by Ron Shiflet "The Lord of Xiccarph" by Ron Shiflet " Cthulhu Lies Dreaming " by Ron Shiflet " Forbidden Knowledge " by Ron Shiflet " Summoning the Dream-Sender " by Ron Shiflet "In the Asylum" by Ron Shiflet Lovecraftian Cliff Notes for Ech-Pi-El 's: "Cool Air", "The Horror at Red Hook", "The Shunned House", "Arthur Jermyn", and "The Shadow out of Time" by Franklyn Searight Folks of Innsmouth : "Zadok Allen", "Attorney", "Old Man Marsh's Chauffeur", "Capt. Obed Marsh", "Innsmouth Yacht Club", "Stranger on the Street", "Priest of Dagon" by Franklin Searight "There Is a Pond" by Franklyn Searight "Abdul Alhazred - The Mad Poet of Arabia" by Franklyn Searight "Ithaqua" by Franklyn Searight "His Name Is Howard" by Franklyn Searight "A Birthday Tribute" by Franklyn Searight "The Marsh House" by Franklyn Searight "The Terrible Old Man" by Franklyn Searight "The Dreams in the Witch-House" by Franklyn Searight "The Innsmouth Bar and Grill" by Franklyn Searight "Damascus, 738 AD" by Lee Clark Zumpe "Nyarlathotep in Florence" by Lee Clark Zumpe "The Willow on Meadow Hill" by Lee Clark Zumpe "Unspeakable Cults" by Lee Clark Zumpe "Tunguska" by Lee Clark Zumpe "Theodorus Philetas" by Lee Clark Zumpe "The Hollow of Old Oaks" by Lee Clark Zumpe "Composition de Mort" by Lee Clark Zumpe "A Stranger in Smithville" by Lee Clark Zumpe "Ring of Spiderwort" by Lee Clark Zumpe "Under Darkening Skies" by James Livesay "Homoflora" by Frederick J. Mayer "Xeethra Fruit" by Frederick J. Mayer "Dark Legacy" by Frederick J. Mayer "Scarlet Succubus Shrine" by Frederick J. Mayer "The Abominations of Yoni" by Frederick J. Mayer " Algol " by Adrian Kleinbergen "From Beyond" by Adrian Kleinbergen "The Colour Out of Space" by Adrian Kleinbergen "Desert Ghouls" by Victoria Felix "The Ghouls All Know" by Victoria Felix "The City Beneath the Sands" by Victoria Felix "Pickman" by Victoria Felix Untitled poem by Victoria Felix "Hide Away" by Victoria Felix "The Dreamer Sleeps Tonight" by Victoria Felix "The Lay of Roshiel's Last Stand" by Victoria Felix The Villanelle of the Desert" by Victoria Felix</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>106</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dark_Legacy"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 2015</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Black_Mirror">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MysticalArtefacts"/>
    <rdfs:label>Black Mirror</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a common item seen both in weird horror and sword and fantasy stories . Whoever looks into the is able to see into other dimensions and if used correctly, the user can travel through time . Behind the mirror was a page of the darkhold , the most powerful tome of dark magic on earth .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Hoard_of_the_Wizard-Beast_and_One_Other">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Hoard of the Wizard-Beast and One Other</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>R. H. Barlow and H. P. Lovecraft United States English Necronomicon Press Release Date October 1994 Media Type Print collects together two short stories by R. H. Barlow as revised by Howard Phillips Lovecraft . It was first published in October 1994 by Necronomicon Press and has not been reissued. Contents " The Hoard of the Wizard-Beast " " The Slaying of the Monster "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#R._H._Barlow"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>October 1994</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hound_%28creature%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Hound (creature)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The titular creature from the short story " the hound " is not known whether the hound is one of a race of hounds or a unique mutation of another race . The narrator links it to the plateau of leng in central asia .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Hound</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Littlest_Lovecraft:_The_Call_of_Cthulhu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Storybooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Littlest Lovecraft: The Call of Cthulhu</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft (original story) Tro Rex (adaptation) Eyona Bella English Genre(s) Children's Esoteric Order of Publishing Release Date November 23, 2013 60 is a children's book, published by the Esoteric Order of Publishing in 2013. It was written by Tro Rex and illustrated by Eyona Bella, as an adaptation of H. P. Lovecraft 's " The Call of Cthulhu ". Overview</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Tro Rex</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Children's</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>60</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2013 November 23</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lawrence_Watt-Evans">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lawrence Watt-Evans</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lawrence Watt Evans (born 1954) is an American science fiction and fantasy author and editor who has also written commissioned works in "franchise" properties such as Star Trek . He also wrote as a critic at one point in his career including film reviews and a non-fiction critical work on Terry Pratchett. He has often written under pseudonyms including and Nathan Archer . The majority of his work has nothing to do with the Cthulhu Mythos and consists in large part of science fiction or fantasy books series. Selected Bibliography Short Fiction " Pickman's Modem " (1992) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Mystery_of_April_Snow">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Mystery of April Snow</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Oscar Rios Golden Goblin Press Horror Call of Cthulhu is a scenario supplement for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game. Overview Running to a total of 30 pages and including all of the necessary maps and player handouts, contains a complete, stand-alone scenario. Written by Oscar Rios with artwork provided by David Lee Ingersoll, it is published by licensed third-party content provider Golden Goblin Press as a digital download.</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_King_in_Yellow">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The King in Yellow</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The stories could be categorized as early horror fiction or victorian gothic fiction . The work also touches on mythology, fantasy, mystery, science fiction and romance . Chambers' book borrows elements from the supernatural stories of ambrose bierce .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_W._Chambers"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Short Fiction Horror Weird Fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brotherhood_of_the_Beast">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cults"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Organisation"/>
    <rdfs:label>Brotherhood of the Beast</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The brotherhood was founded by two men in the 12th century with the intention of making nephren-ka's prophecy come true . Hauptman established the brotherhood's headquarters in europe and began to create a network of agents drawn from among the descendants of nephren-ka . The original plans of the brotherhood were to establish a kingdom in eastern europe, but both attempts were made in failure .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeO>Cult</ex:hasTypeO>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#New_Cthulhu:_The_Recent_Weird">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>New Cthulhu: The Recent Weird</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Paula Guran , editor English Genre(s) Cthulhu Mythos Prime Books Release Date November 22, 2011 528 Followed by New Cthulhu 2: More Recent Weird is an anthology of Cthulhu Mythos fiction edited by Paula Guran , and published in 2011 by Prime Books. It reprints Lovecraftian horror stories that appeared in the first decade of the 21st century. In 2015, Prime Books published a sequel, New Cthulhu 2: More Recent Weird , also edited by Guran. Contents Introduction by Paula Guran " Pickman's Other Model (1929) " by Caitlín R. Kiernan "Fair Exchange" by Michael Marshall Smith "Mr. Gaunt" by John Langan "The Vicar of R'lyeh" by Marc Laidlaw "The Crevasse" by Dale Bailey and Nathan Ballingrud "Bad Sushi" by Cherie Priest "Old Virginia" by Laird Barron "The Dude Who Collected Lovecraft" by Nick Mamatas and Tim Pratt "The Oram County Whoosit" by Steve Duffy "The Fungal Stain" by W. H. Pugmire " A Study in Emerald " by Neil Gaiman "Buried in the Sky" by John Shirley "Bringing Helena Back" by Sarah Monette "Take Me to the River" by Paul J. McAuley "The Essayist in the Wilderness" by William Browning Spencer "The Disciple" by David Barr Kirtley " Shoggoths in Bloom " by Elizabeth Bear "Cold Water Survival" by Holly Phillips "The Great White Bed" by Don Webb "Lesser Demons" by Norman Partridge " Grinding Rock " by Cody Goodfellow " Details " by China Miéville "Another Fish Story" by Kim Newman "Head Music" by Lon Prater "Tsathoggua" by Michael Shea " Mongoose " by Elizabeth Bear and Sarah Monette " A Colder War " by Charles Stross</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Paula_Guran"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>editor</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>528</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2011 November 22</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pilot_Demon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Pilot Demon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sociocultural Characteristics Extra-dimensional Real World Information First appearance EXP : The Alchemist's Notebook EXP : Cthulhu's Minions Created by Byron Craft Pilot Demons are a species of extra-dimensional being created by American author and screenwriter Byron Craft for his 2014 novel The Alchemist's Notebook , and subsequently expanded upon in 2015's Cthulhu's Minions . Overview Described as "ethereal puffs of smoke that sometimes take shape and solidify" , Pilot Demons can take a multitude of forms when they materialise, but are always diminutive in stature. Occasionally winged and always fast and vicious, they act as heralds and pathfinders when greater beings wish to worm their way into our plane of existence. They are highly intelligent creatures, being able to perform complex tasks such as constructing mechanical devices to enable them to operate automobiles. The Alchemist's Notebook (2014) Cthulhu's Minions (2015) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhus_Minions"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Alchemist's Notebook</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Byron_Craft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Pratt">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>James Pratt</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Lovecraftian horror Years Active 2013-2018 is an author who has written a number of short stories set in the Cthulhu Mythos . These have been published in collections such as Swords Against Cthulhu II: Hyperborean Nights ( Rogue Planet Press , 2017) and Corporate Cthulhu: Lovecraftian Tales of Bureaucratic Nightmare (Pickman's Press, 2018). Selected Bibliography Short Fiction " Coffin of Glass " (2017) "Welcome to the R'lyeh Corporation" (2018) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasYearsActiveR>2013-2018</ex:hasYearsActiveR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Riddle_of_Jeremy_Roman">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Riddle of Jeremy Roman</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Dean Wirth English Rogue Planet Press Lovecraftiana Vol 5, Issue 3 date October 2020 ← Previous Next → " The Dewey Mine Attack " " The Randy Oswald Story "  " " is a Lovecraftian short story by Canadian Dean Wirth , and is the second in a series begun with " The Dewey Mine Attack ". It was published in the Lammas Eve edition of Lovecraftiana by Rogue Planet Press in 2020. Synopsis Told through a series of letters, news reports, medical charts, and government memorandums, "" begins by telling of the rehabilitation of Bryan Katz , the sole survivor of the previous story who had been driven to insanity by his experiences. It then goes on to describe the experiences of two brothers, Joshua and Jeremy Roman, seamen serving aboard the battleship USS Connecticut in 1919. These men were the sole survivors of an attack by the star-creatures encountered by Katz decades earlier, although they later died from bizarre and horrific complications as a result of physical contact with the beings.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dean_Wirth"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Issue 3</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Lovecraftiana Vol 5</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2020</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#House_of_the_Rising_Sun:_Innsmouth,_USA">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>House of the Rising Sun: Innsmouth, USA</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here Ken Ichigawa English Nightscapes #13 date May 2000 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos story by Ken Ichigawa. Synopsis A pair of sexually-jaded brothers from Providence, Rhode Island are made aware of a bordello in Innsmouth which will give them everything they desire. Travelling there, the two unknowingly bed Deep One -hybrid prostitutes in the rundown brothel. Whilst one is disgusted by the experience, the other finds a deep satisfaction in the act, and perhaps even a hint as to his own heritage. History "" was published in the E. P. Berglund -edited Mythos zine Nightscapes in May 2000.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Ken Ichigawa</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Nightscapes #13</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>May 2000</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Harjit_Singh">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Harjit Singh</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>2019 in the South Pacific</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>The Department of Extraordinary Phenomena</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Rupinder</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as rupinder ( exp : " it lives in the woods") is a character created by troy young for his nyarlathotep cycle series of cthulhu mythos stories . Made his first appearance in the short story " it slumbers beneath the ice "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCharacterMateC>Neha</ex:hasCharacterMateC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#It_Slumbers_Beneath_the_Ice"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Troy_Young"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#John_P._McCann">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>John P. McCann</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Birth Date 1952 Elmhurst, Illinois, USA is an American producer, scriptwriter, voice actor and occasional author. While his television works focus on children’s animation, his literature focuses on horror and dark humour. His Lovecraftian works have appeared in anthologies such as The Cthulhu Mythos Megapack ( Wildside Press , 2012). Selected Bibliography Short Fiction " Dagon and Jill " (2011) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Illinois Elmhurst</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1952</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Silas_Marsh">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Silas Marsh</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Sailor</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The sailor is a character who features in many of fantasy flight games' cthulhu mythos -related games and who has also appeared in some of their fiction . He first appeared as an investigator for the second edition expansion of arkham horror , innsmouth horror . In the novella the deep gate by chris a. jackson, he is the main branch of the marshes and was born and raised in innsmouth .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Deep_Gate"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>by Chris A. Jackson</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Innsmouth Horror Deluxe Expansion</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Eric_Vogt">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Eric Vogt</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Artist Roleplaying games is an author and artist known for his work on Chaosium 's seminal horror roleplaying game Call of Cthulhu , whose credits include cover art on a number of supplements, as well as assorted additional material. Selected Works Cover Art In the Shadows (1995) Ye Booke of Monstres II (1995) The Cairo Guidebook (1995) Interior Art Arkham Sanitarium (1997, additional material) Other Works Utatti Asfet (1996, production staff) External Links at Goodreads at RPGGeek</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mister_Ys">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Mister Ys</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Created by h.p. Lovecraft is a character who appears without a name in the fiction of h.p. Lovecraft . He is the alien entity who transfers minds with wingate peaslee in the novella the shadow out of time (1936) he is given the name ys in peter rawlik 's cthulhu mythos novel the weird company (2014), and plays a significant role in rawlik's mythos stories involving the pea</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC>The Weird Company The Pestilence of Pandora Peaslee Time Flies</ex:hasAppearanceC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Shadow_Out_of_Time"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Saucers_from_Yaddith">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Saucers from Yaddith</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Esoteric order of dagon apais a short story by robert m. price . It is suggested that the nug-soth have visited earth repeatedly to abduct humans and perform medical experiments upon the abductees . The experiments range from the swapping of a man's blood type from o to a to the swapping of eyes and hands to more nightmar</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_M._Price"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Science Fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Esoteric Order of Dagon APA</ex:isPublishedAsW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tsan-Chan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Organisation"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tsan-Chan</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The empire is a fictional future terran empire created by the late american author h. p. lovecraft . It is first mentioned in his 1919 short story " beyond the wall of sleep " whether lovecraft intended to refer to an individual emperor, a dynasty, or even a geographical region is uncertain .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeO>Empire</ex:hasTypeO>
    <ex:isLocatedAtO>Asia</ex:isLocatedAtO>
    <ex:wasCreatedBynO rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nyarlathoteps_Way">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nyarlathotep's Way</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Vagrant serge seeks a boon from a priest of nyarlathotep at a remote and hidden temple . However, once the man leaves the priest is revealed to be nyarlathotep itself, who has over the decades engineered serge's descent into destitution .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Tom Pinchuk</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Shall Be</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Whispers from the Abyss Vol. 2: The Horrors That Are</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Streets_of_Arkham">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Streets of Arkham</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Kara Centell-Dunk Tony Fanchi Grace Holdinghaus Fantasy Flight Games Publication Date 2017 Mansions of Madness is an expansion set for Fantasy Flight Games ' Cthulhu Mythos board game Mansions of Madness . Designed by Kara Centell-Dunk , Tony Fanchi and Grace Holdinghaus , it was published in 2017. Overview Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Paul_Mellon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSRelatives"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Paul Mellon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1910</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lovecraft’s paternal uncle and husband of Mary Louisa Lovecraft.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isRelativeToR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1863</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hugh_B_Cave">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSFriendsAndAcquaintances"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Hugh B Cave</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>2004</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Prolific author of stories for the pulp magazines. He lived for a time near Lovecraft in Pawtucket, Rhode Island. They corresponded briefly but never met.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1910</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shuggob">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Shuggob</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>In the vale of pnath is a fictional character created by . Lin carter for his cthulhu mythos short story " in the vale of . Pnath ". Description is an elderly ghoul sage who lives alone in a stone house surrounded by a garden of moss and lichens .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#In_the_Vale_of_Pnath"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lin_Carter"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tony_Fanchi">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tony Fanchi</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Games designer is a games developer who designs work for Fantasy Flight Games . Their credits include Fantasy Flight original games, tie-ins with franchises such as Star Wars and Marvel Comics , and a number of Arkham Horror Files Cthulhu Mythos releases. Selected Works Core Games Unfathomable (2021) Expansion Sets Streets of Arkham (2017) External Links designer profile at BoardGameGeek</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
