  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraftiana_Vol_8,_Issue_2">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Periodicals"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lovecraftiana Vol 8, Issue 2</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Jim Pitts Dean Wirth Cover Artist Pietro Rotelli English Lovecraftiana Rogue Planet Press Release Date July 2023 237  is an issue of the Rogue Planet Press periodical Lovecraftiana , published in July 2023. It was edited by Gavin Chappell , featured cover art by Pietro Rotelli, and interior work from Jim Pitts and Dean Wirth ; both print and digital editions were released . Contents (Note: Dean Wirth's short story "Flight of the Battle-Axe" does not appear on the contents list for the digital edition of the magazine, although it remains included) " Something Missing " by M Stern " Sarnath Centre " by Tony Daly " Seeds from the Blasted Heath " by Brian Barnett "Above Sleeping Pharaohs" by Matthew Wilson "Among the Stones" by Lamont Turner "Blended Kaleidoscopic Rotation" by Tony Daly "The Last Feast of Blodeuwedd" by Carlton Herzog "Stay Awhile" by Benjamin DeHaan "Voices Through the Walls" by Lee Clark Zumpe " Rise of Thalhulla " by Joshua Robinson "The Daylight Horror" by Russell Epp-Leppel "The Triumph of Ages" by A Fiddlestix "The Oval Mirror" by Rick Hudson " The Twilight People " by Christopher Pate "Flight of the Battle-Axe" by Dean Wirth " Thick as a Brick " by Craig Brownlie "The Specter of Eliphas Levi" by EW Farnsworth "Performance" by Gerry Saracco "The Night of the Feast" by Logan McConnell Reviews ( Nancy Kilpatrick 's Thirteen Plus-1 Lovecraftian Narratives and Edgar Allen Poe: Mysterium, Incubus et Terror ) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>237</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecraftiana"/>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 2023</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecrafts_Dagon_for_Beginning_Readers">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Storybooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>H. P. Lovecraft's Dagon for Beginning Readers</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft (original story) R. J. Ivankovic (adaptation) R. J. Ivankovic English Genre(s) Children's Chaosium Release Date February 15, 2019 80 is a children's book, written and illustrated by R. J. Ivankovic. It was originally posted on Ivankovic's DeviantArt account, as a serialized story from 2012 to 2013. In 2017, Ivankovic began working on a revision for Chaosium , which was published as a hardcover book in 2019. The revised version features new art, with both versions being faithful adaptations of H. P. Lovecraft 's " Dagon ", done in the style of a Dr. Seuss book. Overview References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>R. J. Ivankovic</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Children's</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>80</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2019 February 15</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gambrel_Roof">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gambrel Roof</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A is an architectural feature frequently cited in the fiction of H. P. Lovecraft . A roof with a steeply sloping section below a section with a gentler slope--think of the profile of the classic barn--the style is characteristic of 17th and 18th century architecture, an era for which Lovecraft had a great fondness. In Lovecraft " The Unnamable " (1923): " The Festival " (1923): " The Ghost-Eater " (1924, with C. M. Eddy, Jr. ) "He" (1925): " The Silver Key " (1926): " The Strange High House in the Mist " (1926): " Pickman's Model " (1926): The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath (1927): " The Colour Out of Space " (1927): The Case of Charles Dexter Ward (1927): " The Dunwich Horror " (1928): " The Shadow Over Innsmouth " (1931): " The Dreams in the Witch-House " (1932): " Through the Gates of the Silver Key " (1932-33): " The Thing on the Doorstep " (1933):</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Book_of_Iod:_Ten_Tales_of_the_Mythos">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Book of Iod: Ten Tales of the Mythos</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various United States English Genre(s) Horror Chaosium Release Date 1995 Media Type Print is an anthology of Cthulhu Mythos works, the majority of which were penned by Lovecraft contemporary Henry Kuttner , although Robert M. Price and Lin Carter each contributed a story. Overview First published by Chaosium in 1995 as part of their Cthulhu Cycle Books , The Book of Iod was edited by noted Mythos contributor Robert M. Price. Contents " The Secret of Kralitz " (1936) " The Eater of Souls " (1937) " The Salem Horror " (1937) " The Black Kiss " by Robert Bloch and Henry Kuttner (1937) " The Jest of Droom-Avesta " (1937) " Spawn of Dagon " (1938) " The Invaders " (1939) " The Frog " (1939) " Hydra " (1939) " The Bells of Horror " (1939) " The Hunt " (1939) " Beneath the Tombstone " by Robert M. Price (1984) " Dead of Night " by Lin Carter (1988) Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1995</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Zarr">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Zarr</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Zathog"/>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Zarrians</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The are a fanatical, ruthless race who had conquered most of their home galaxy, xentilx . In the distant past, the god zathog made a pact with the , granting them the power to travel through time and space . Their method of conquest is to first subdue their target planet through use of nuclear bombardment, then recruit indigenous collaborators to form a puppet government .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Winds of Zarr</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_L._Tierney"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#isConnectedToMythosR">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label>isConnectedToMythosR</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Unknown_Abomination">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Unknown Abomination</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is an eldritch abomination from the fictional universe of marvel by marvel comics . It resided in the cavern of a mountain in the swamp of mists in atlantis during the thurian age . It was controlled by lieutenant khornah via a gem and</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Locations">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fish_Bride">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CaitlinRKiernanWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Fish Bride</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Caitlin R. Kiernan English Sirenia Digest date May 2009 " (1970)" is a short story by Caitlin R. Kiernan that first appeared in the May 2009 issue of Sirenia Digest (#42). Synopsis "" tells the story of a relationship between a human man and a human- Deep One hybrid woman in an unnamed town. History The story was republished in The Weird Fiction Review #2 (Centipede Press, 2011). It later appeared in Kiernan's collection Houses Under the Sea: Mythos Tales ( Subterranean Press , 2019).</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Caitlin_R._Kiernan"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sirenia_Digest"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>May 2009</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Cruel_Empire_of_Tsan_Chan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CallOfCthulhuRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Cruel Empire of Tsan Chan</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Call of cthulhu is a horror fiction role-playing game based on h.p. Lovecraft's cthulhu mythos . The game is set in a nightmarish earth, approximately 3000 years in the future . Most of mankind has gone insane and worshipped the great old ones .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInCountryL>United States</ex:isInCountryL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Army_of_Darkness:_Ash_Vs._The_Classic_Monsters">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Army of Darkness: Ash Vs. The Classic Monsters</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>James Kuhoric Nick Bradshaw English Dynamite Entertainment Release Date Sept 2006 - Jan 2007 Media Type Comic book (aka, Ash Vs. Dracula ) is a 4-issue story arc written by James Kuhoric. Which appeared in 2006-2007 in issues #8-11 of the 1st volume of Army of Darkness by Dynamite Entertainment. Synopsis Dracula plans to use the Necronomicon to bring about Eternal Night, starting with New York City. Ash Williams allied with Dracula's daughter Eva and Frankenstein's Monster battle Dracula and his army of vampires, werewolves, and mummies.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>James Kuhoric</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>Sept 2006 - Jan 2007</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#John_ODonnel">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>John O'Donnel</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#John_Kirowan"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>O'donnel is a sometime-companion of occultist john kirowan . Revealed to possess knowledge of a past life, one in which he was a pictish warrior in the tribe of the aryara . Prompted him to exile himself and dedicate the rest of his life to tracking down and killing the perpetrators .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Children_of_the_Night"/>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Haunter_of_the_Ring"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_E._Howard"/>
  </rdf:Description>
