  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Cacophony_%28Call_of_Cthulhu_LCG%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamingSupplements"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Cacophony (Call of Cthulhu LCG)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Cacophony Fantasy Flight Games Publication Date 2010 Living Card Game Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game The Cacophony is an expansion pack for Fantasy Flight Games ' Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game . Overview The sixth and final Asylum Pack in The Yuggoth Contract Cycle series of expansions, The Cacophony contains 60 cards in the standard division of three sets of 20 unique cards. First released in 2010, it was discontinued with the demise of Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game . Contents Out of the Darkness (Event; The Agency) Bloodbath (Event; The Agency) Springfield M1903 (Support; The Agency) Disguised Threat (Event; Cthulhu ) Feeding Frenzy (Event; Cthulhu) The Cave on the Hill, Local Landmark (Support; Cthulhu) Lost to the Madness (Event; Hastur ) Fledgling Byakhee (Character; Hastur) Stone Calendar (Support; Miskatonic University ) Uneasy Translator (Character; Miskatonic University) Harry Houdini, Escape Artist (Character; Miskatonic University) Behind the Door (Conspiracy; Neutral) Forgiveness? (Event; Neutral) Shub-Niggurath , Dark Mistress of the Woods (Character; Shub-Niggurath) Examining the Optic Nerve (Event; Shub-Niggurath) Safe House (Support; Syndicate) Tattooed Thug (Character; Syndicate) The View from Within (Event; Syndicate) Reckless Elder Thing (Character; Yog-Sothoth ) Bird Demon (Character; Yog-Sothoth)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2010</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#This_Is_How_the_World_Ends">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>This Is How the World Ends</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu's reign is a short story by american author john r. fultz . It was first published in the darrell schweitzer-edited anthology cthulhu's reign (daw books, 2010) the story is set in a post-apocalyptic world where the human race has been overrun .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#John_R._Fultz"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhus_Reign"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>April 2010</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Zimmer">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Zimmer</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>1917 June</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Imperial German Navy</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>June, 1917 Germany Navy Seaman (Submariner) Imperial German Navy Human First appearance HPL : " The Temple " Created by H. P. Lovecraft Seaman is a fictional character in the story " The Temple " by H. P. Lovecraft . He served aboard the German submarine U-29 under Lieutenant-Commander Karl Heinrich during the First World War. During the supernatural events experienced by the crew, both and Boatswain Müller went missing. They were presumed by Heinrich to have committed suicide by jumping overboard while the U-29 was surfaced, victims of the fear that overcame them.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Temple"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Confidential">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Confidential</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Robin D. Laws Chris Spivey Ruth Tillman Pelgrane Press Publication Date May 2017 Roleplaying game GUMSHOE (adapted from) is a Cthulhu Mythos roleplaying game created by Robin D. Laws , Chris Spivey , and Ruth Tillman , and published by Pelgrane Press in May 2017; both print and digital editions were released. Overview Designed using Robin D. Laws' GUMSHOE as a starting point, is a game for two players, with one filling the role of gamesmaster and the other taking control of one of three pre-generated characters. Full rules are included, as are three complete scenarios and background information on the characters. The game proved popular enough to spawn a series of single-scenario adventure supplements, initially released as digital downloads then later collected into the volume Even Death Can Die . List of Supplements Ex Astoria High Voltage Kill One For the Money The House Up in the Hills The Howling Fog The Shadow Over Washington Even Death Can Die Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>May 2017</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lee_Brown_Coye">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lee Brown Coye</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>W. t. coye (1907 - 1981) was an american artist who illustrated several stories for weird tales starting in 1945 . He also produced the cover art for a number of arkham house books, including the original tales of the cthulhu mythos . Stories that coye illustrated for weird tales include " the hog ", " the will of claude ashur ", " the whippoorwills in the hills ", and</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Henry_Hasse">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Henry Hasse</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1977 May 20</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full Name Henry Louis Hasse Birth Date February 7, 1913 Indiana, USA Death Date May 20, 1977 𝐓 Henry Louis Hasse (February 7, 1913 – May 20, 1977) was a science fiction writer who co-authored Ray Bradbury's first professionally published story, "Pendulum," which appeared in the November 1941 issue of Super Science Stories . His early Cthulhu Mythos story, " The Guardian of the Book ," appeared in the March 1937 issue of Weird Tales . External Links at Internet Speculative Fiction Database stories at Project Gutenberg</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Indiana</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Henry Louis Hasse</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1913 February 7</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ta-Vho-Shai">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ta-Vho-Shai</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Vulthoom"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Vulthoom is a fictional character created by clark ashton smith for his short story " vulthoom ". They are servants of vulthoom, a powerful creature from a world outside the solar system whose spaceship crashed on mars in prehistory . The powers of vulthoom grant its servants immortality in return for their loyalty .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Vulthoom"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
  </rdf:Description>
