  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crimson_King">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crimson King</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a demonic entity in the fictional universe of the dark tower horror novels by stephen king . His goal is to destroy the multiverse by taking down the dark tower, so he can remake the multiverse in his image . He had a variety of powers, including telekinesis, shapeshifting, necromancy, elemental powers, dark magic,</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Treader_of_the_Dust_%28short_story%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ClarkAshtonSmithWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Treader of the Dust (short story)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The Treader of the Dust " is a short story by Clark Ashton Smith . It was published in August 1935 in the Magazine of Fantasy and Science Fiction . Synopsis Antiquarian John Sebastian obtains the Testament of Carnamagos , a tome of forbidden knowledge, from a book seller and in the process is exposed to the horror and hazard of Quachil Uttaus . Returning to his house, Sebastian is captivated by the Testament and held in place until Quachil Uttaus slowly advances towards him across a gulf of time and space, ultimately- turning the man to dust.</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Waiting">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Waiting</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Dennis Detwiller United States English Genre(s) Weird fiction 01Publishing Whispers from the Abyss date November 2014  " " is a short story by American and games designer Dennis Detwiller . It is inspired by the works of the late Howard Phillips Lovecraft . Synopsis A man working a lonely job in a desert bus station suffers a revelation on the nature of time and space, and his own place in the grand scheme of things. History "" was published in the anthology of H. P. Lovecraft-related fiction Whispers from the Abyss by 01Publishing in November 2014.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dennis_Detwiller"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Weird fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Whispers_from_the_Abyss"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>November 2014</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Midnight_Massacre">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Midnight Massacre</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>D.G. Chichester, Howard Mackie, Chris Cooper, Gregory Wright Kirk van Wormer, Mark Badger, Ron Wagner, Mike Witherby, Rurik Tyler, Bob Downs, Ron Garney, Chris Ivy, Adam Kubert, Joe Kubert, Dan Green, Klaus Janson, Frank Spinks English Marvel Comics Release Date August 1993 Media Type Comic book is a crossover story written by multiple writers involving various supernatural superheroes that appeared in issue #10 of the 1st volume of Nightsalkers , issue #40 of the 3rd volume of Ghost Rider , issue #11 of the first volume of Darkhold , issue #12 of the 1st volume of Morbius: The Living Vampire , and issue #13 of the 1st volume of Ghost Rider/Blaze: Spirits of Vengeance . It notably involves the Darkhold grimoire by Chthon as it corrupts the vampire / human hybrid superhero and vampire hunter Blade . External Links at the Marvel Database wiki</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Chris Cooper</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>D.G. Chichester</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Gregory Wright</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Howard Mackie</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 1993</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arthur_Leeds">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSFriendsAndAcquaintances"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Arthur Leeds</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>unknown</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>An associate of Lovecraft in New York and member of the Kalem Club. He was the author (with J. Berg Esenwein) of Writing the Photoplay (Springfield, MA: The Home Correspondence School, 1913; rev. ed. 1919).</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>unknown</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Black_Hill">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Black Hill</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A successful american oilman purchases a plot of land believed by the indigenous people to be the home of something dark and sinister . Unfortunately for the man, the colossal entity tears open a rip in the bedrock, swallowing him whole before sealing itself back in its subterranean lair .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Orrin_Grey"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Historical_Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>April 2011</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#R._Layamon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>R. Layamon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Miskatonic_University"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Professor Wizard Miskatonic University Human First appearance EXP : " The Freshman " Created by Philip José Farmer is a fictional character who appears in Philip José Farmer 's short story " The Freshman ". Layamon is a professor at Miskatonic University , the chairman of the Committee of the Occult Arts and History Department and a powerful wizard. He is affiliated with the Lam Kha Alif fraternity. Farmer provides a description of Layamon: Throughout the story, Layamon is pushing the new student Roderick Desmond to cross a moral line, to eliminate the "defects of character" that would keep him from fulfilling his arcane potential.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Freshman"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Philip_Jos%C3%A9_Farmer"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Eldritch_Blue:_Love_%26_Sex_in_the_Cthulhu_Mythos">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Eldritch Blue: Love &amp; Sex in the Cthulhu Mythos</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Susan McAdam United States English Lindisfarne Press Release Date March 2004 Media Type Print 318 is a thematic anthology set in the Mythos established by American H. P. Lovecraft . Overview Containing a total of 21 short works from a wide range of authors, the pieces included in Eldritch Blue deal with the relationship between love and carnal desire within the context of the greater Mythos. It was first published in March 2004 by Lindisfarne Press, was edited by Kevin L. O'Brien with an introduction by Robert M. Price and cover art by Susan McAdam, and runs to 318 . Contents Prologue by Kevin L. O'Brien Introduction: The Thing in the Underwear by Robert M. Price "Promethes: Where Walks Istasha" by James Ambuehl " The Tale of Toad Loop " by Stanley C. Sargent "Goat-Mother" by Pierre Comtois "The Spawn of Y'lagh" by Randall D. Larson (as Randall Larson) "Mail Order Bride" by Ann K. Schwader "Family Recipe" by Charles Garofalo "Cat's-Paw" by E. P. Berglund "Beast of Love" by James Ambuehl and Tracy Ambuehl " The Faces at Pine Dunes " by Ramsey Campbell "Dagon's Mistress" by Neil Riebe " The Thing on the Doorstep " by H. P. Lovecraft "The Prodigies of Monkfield Cabot" by Mike Minnis " Seduced " by Ron Shiflet (as Ronald Shiflet) "Stacked Actors" by Peter A. Worthy "Have You Found Him" by Jean Ann Donnel "The Violet Princess" by Stephen Mark Rainey "What Sort of Man" by Walter C. DeBill, Jr. "The Obsession of Percival Cairstairs" by Charles Black "A Mate for the Mutilator" by Robert M. Price "To Cast Out Fear" by C. J. Henderson "The Surrogate" by Kevin L. O'Brien Afterword: Epimethes: Love (and Sex), Mythos Style by Kevin L. O'Brien</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>318</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>March 2004</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nameless_City">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nameless City</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a ruined city created by h. p. lovecraft for his short story of the same name . It was built in prehistory by a species of quadrupedal reptilian creatures . The city is located somewhere in the arabian desert, possibly modern-day yemen . It has been suggested that the real-world abandoned city of irem was the inspiration for the .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceL>The Descendant At the Mountains of Madness</ex:hasAppearanceL>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Derelict city</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isInhabitedByL>Unnamed species of reptilian creatures</ex:isInhabitedByL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Arabian Desert ( HPL )</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Modern-day Yemen ( AWD )</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Nameless_City"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Bettie_Page">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Bettie Page</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Secret Agent, Model Human First appearance ADJ : Comics EXP : " The Crisis on Infinite Betties " is a comic book hero, based on the historical pin-up model of the same name. She first appeared in the Comics one-shot by Dark Horse Comics in 1996, and later came back in 2017 for her own series by Dynamite Entertainment. Background is a model who works as a secret agent for a secretive American spy agency in the early 20th century which deals with various threats including extraterrestrials, monsters, the supernatural and mad scientists. During one of her missions, she had to save Earth and the entire multiverse from the Great Old Ones , who were breaking out of their dimensional prison. ( EXP : " The Crisis on Infinite Betties ") Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Crisis_on_Infinite_Betties"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Bettie Page Comics</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gustaf_Johansen">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gustaf Johansen</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>1925</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Second Mate Johansen</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu is a fictional character created by h. p. lovecraft . He makes his first appearance in the 1928 short story " the call of cthulhu " johansen was a norwegian sailor "of some intelligence" he was the second of the emma of auburn .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCharacterMateC>Unnamed wife</ex:hasCharacterMateC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Call_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Static">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Static</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A psychic on staff at the cees is sent to antarctica to decipher mysterious inscriptions . While there, he unwittingly unleashes a cosmic horror . Story was reprinted in murray's collection the wild adventures of cthulhu .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Will_Murray"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Beyond_the_Mountains_of_Madness"/>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Cryptic Events Evaluation Taskforce</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2015 April 30</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A._Henry_Keene">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosInspiredAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>A. Henry Keene</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Horror  is an author, poet and editor who has written a handful of works inspired by those of Howard Phillips Lovecraft and the Cthulhu Mythos . These have appeared in anthologies such as 2015's Lovecraft after Dark by James Ward Kirk Publishing. Selected Works Short Fiction " Claire " (2015) Poetry " Tangled " (2015) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#AveroigneCycle">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hell-Plant">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonSapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Hell-Plant</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Hell-plants are writhing slimy masses of branches and creepers that are covered with lipless fanged mouths and bulging eyeballs . Seeds are carried within living hosts, planted by mythos beings or powerful sorcerers . When it germinates it rips through the body, instantly killing its host .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ye_Booke_of_Monstres_II"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Scott_David_Aniolowski"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Unseen">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleComicBookAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Unseen</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story is a loose adaptation of h.p. Lovecraft's short story " from beyond " it was first published in the idw horror anthology in the dark in may 2014 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Justin Jordan</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#In_the_Dark"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>May 2014</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nichola_Trayler-Barbrook">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nichola Trayler-Barbrook</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Artist United Kingdom is an artist, writer, editor and home renovation owner-builder. She lives with her husband Alex in Ware, England . Trayler-Barbrook has created the covers for several Mythos anthologies. She has spent more time however as illustrator and editor of children's books and providing artwork for DVD releases. Selected Works Cover Art The Whisperer in Darkness: Collected Stories Volume One The Horror in the Museum: Collected Short Stories Volume Two The Haunter of the Dark: Collected Short Stories Volume Three The Lurking Fear: Collected Short Stories Volume Four</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United Kingdom</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Artist</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Xcthol">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Xcthol</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Great Old Ones</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Goat God</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as The Goat God Relatives Shub-Niggurath (Possible Relative) Great Old Ones First appearance " Ravenstone Sanitarium " (RPG Material) is a sadistic, mind-controlling faun-like entity who was theorized to be related to Shub-Niggurath .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Ravenstone Sanitarium (RPG Material)</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Home_Brew">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#PulpMagazines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Home Brew</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>English final publication 1924 was an american humour and horror fiction pulp magazine . The magazine was set up by george julian houtain and his wife e. dorothy maclaughlin . It was known for its slightly racy nature and was dedicated to challenging gender roles, moral codes and prohibition .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mother_Shub">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Mother Shub</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shub-Niggurath"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Shub-niggurath is a character created by american author ron shiflet . At the time of her death, lived in a ramshackle, single-room hut in the west virginia forests known to the locals as shub's woods . She plied her trade as a local witch doctor, providing the hillbilly families with basic medical care and potions with a variety of effects .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Out_of_the_Frying_Pan"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ezines">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tokyo_Afterschool_Summoners">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tokyo Afterschool Summoners</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Housamo is a japanese f2p turn-based card game rpg app available on android and ios that features characters based on the cthulhu mythos . Developed by lifewonders, it was released in december 2016 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 2016</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#S._Albert_Kivinen">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>S. Albert Kivinen</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>13 August 2021</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Seppo albert kivinen was a finnish author and doctor of philosophy and docent emeritus of university of helsinki, known for his occult interests . He is a onetime winner of the atorox award for best finnish short story, for his lovecraftian pastiche "the midnight worm in ikaalinen" his stories often take place in his childhood home county of satakunta and particularly the municipality of ikaalinen .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>Finland Ikaalinen</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Seppo Albert Kivinen</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>Finland</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>19 October 1933</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#New_Maps_of_Dream">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>New Maps of Dream</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Marcelo Gallegos English Genre(s) Lovecraftian fiction PS Publishing Release Date July 2021 276  is an anthology of Lovecraftian fiction edited by Cody Goodfellow and Joseph S. Pulver, Sr. and featuring cover art by Marcelo Gallegos. Running to a total of 276 , it was released by PS Publishing in a hardcover format in July 2021 , a little over a year after Pulver, Sr.'s death in April 2020 . Overview Contents Introduction by Cody Goodfellow "Siren Song" by Michael Cisco "Rust Red in Moonlight" by Kaaron Warren "The Sweetest Little Girl in the World" by Damien Angelica Walters "If I Could Be Any Animal I Would Be" by S. P. Miskowski "If the Cat Were Around, He Would Have Eaten the Fish" by Mehitobel Wilson "Inhata" by Jayaprakash Satyamurthy "Over 1,000,000 Copies in Print" by Philip Fracassi "Blacktongue Blues" by Zak Jarvis "Oil of Cat" by Nathan Carson "At the Crossroads" by Christine Morgan "The Dankness Over Dylath-Leeny" by Scott R. Jones "The Malls of Ulthar" by Matthew M. Bartlett "Pandora" by Orrin Grey "From Moon to Darkling Moon" by Anna Tambour "The Onionland Tingle" by Nick Mamatas "The Sea Witch" by Rios De La Luz .Fearful is the Ancient Evil of Their Faces" by Christopher Slatsky "Ruby Soul, Bone Moon" by Lucy A. Snyder " Drunk on Dream " by Jeffrey Thomas References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Lovecraftian fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>276</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 2021</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gifts">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gifts</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu mythos story by robert stahl, published in the anthology whispers from the abyss vol. 2: the horrors that are &amp; shall be .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Robert Stahl</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Shall Be</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Whispers from the Abyss Vol. 2: The Horrors That Are</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#A_Madness_from_the_Vaults">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RamseyCampbellWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>A Madness from the Vaults</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Two different versions of the story are known to exist . The first version was written in 1962, but ended up in a fanzine instead . In 1971, when asked if he could contribute a story to the lovecraft anthology hpl, he felt it contained an excessive amount of "sadism"</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Old_Whateley">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dunwich"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Old Whateley</rdfs:label>
    <ex:ceasedOnC>1924 August 2</ex:ceasedOnC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Wizard whateley was a major character in lovecraft's " the dunwich horror " created by h. p. lovecraft . He was the "aged and half-insane father of lavinia whateley" he died of natural causes on august 2, 1924 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dunwich_Horror"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#madeFirstAppearanceOnC">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label>madeFirstAppearanceOnC</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Call_of_Cthulhu:_The_Card_Game_Core_Set">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Call of Cthulhu: The Card Game Core Set</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Call of cthulhu: the card game is the basic set required to play fantasy flight games ' deckbuilding mythos game . Contains 155 cards (165 in the second edition), all of the requisite tokens and a playmat . Was discontinued in 2015, likely due to licensing considerations . Replaced by arkham horror: the card game .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Elder_Things_that_Fell_To_Earth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Elder Things that Fell To Earth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Mike Raicth Pere Perez English Dynamite Entertainment Release Date April 2007 - Aug 2007 Media Type Comic book is a 3-part story arc written by Mike Riacth in 2007 which appeared in issues #1-3 of the 1st volume of Savage Tales by Dynamite Entertainment. Synopsis An alien ship crashes on a primitive island in the Pacific Ocean. The survivors of the crash are Elder Things carrying a prisoner, Cthulhu , who must be contained at all costs. His madness spreads to the islanders as the Elder Things fight to keep it imprisoned.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Mike Raicth</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>April 2007 - Aug 2007</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#ContentCreators">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lord_Dunsany_and_His_Work">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lord Dunsany and His Work</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here Flub United States English (original) French Genre(s) Non-fiction Arkham House Marginalia date 1944 " " is a short non-fiction work by the late American Howard Phillips Lovecraft . Although it was written in 1922, it did not see until its appearance in the 1944 Arkham House collection Marginalia , seven years after Lovecraft’s death. Overview The piece is an appreciation of the writings of Lord Dunsany , a prolific Anglo-Irish whose work was a major influence on Lovecraft. This influence can be most clearly seen in Lovecraft's more fantastical works, such as the Dream Cycle . History Marginalia (1944) Howard Phillips Lovecraft 1 (1991) Miscellaneous Writings (1995) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Flub</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Non-fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English French</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Marginalia"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>1944</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#hasOccupationR">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label>hasOccupationR</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sonia_Haft_Greene">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSFriendsAndAcquaintances"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sonia Haft Greene</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1972</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Russian Jewish immigrant and amateur journalist who became Lovecraft’s wife in 1924. When Lovecraft returned to Providence in 1926, she continued to work in the millinery trade in New York. In early 1929 she forced Lovecraft to pursue divorce proceedings. Some years later she moved to California and married Nathaniel Davis. Her memoir of Lovecraft, first published in 1948, has been published in its complete form as The Private Life of H. P. Lovecraft (1985).</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1883</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalLocations">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Wonderland_Annuals">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Wonderland Annuals</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>various various English Zenescope Entertainment Release Date May 2009 - July 2012 Media Type Comic book Wonderland: Annuals were one-shot comic book from 2009-2012 which were published by Zenescope Entertainment. One-shots stories involving Wonderland , the Liddle family, and their home.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>May 2009 - July 2012</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Danforth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Danforth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Miskatonic_University"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>History is a graduate student at miskatonic university . He first appeared in the 1936 novel at the mountains of madness . He is described as "a great reader of bizarre material"</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#At_the_Mountains_of_Madness"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleAuthors">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosFilmAndTelevision">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Wizard_of_Lemuria">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pastiches"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Wizard of Lemuria</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Thongor and is a novel by Lin Carter . Publisher's Summary "The famous first adventure of the mightiest warrior-hero since Conan . The ancient dragon kings have deemed that the hour is right to regain their dominance over Earth and only Thongor and his companions, aided by the science and magic of the wizard Sharajsha, stand between mankind and the night of doom."</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#I._N._J._Culbard">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>I. N. J. Culbard</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ian Culbard Connection to the Mythos Comic book adapter Greenwich, London, United Kingdom United Kingdom British Fantasy Award (Best Comic/Graphic Novel, 2011) Ian Culbard , who goes by the professional name of , is an award-winning British comic book artist. Overview Although beginning his career as an animator, Culbard is best known for his comic book works, and of these his adaptations are perhaps his most famous. Working with publisher SelfMadeHero, he has produced adaptations of several pieces, including those of H. P. Lovecraft , Robert W. Chambers , and Arthur Conan Doyle. Selected Works At the Mountains of Madness: A Graphic Novel (2010) The Case of Charles Dexter Ward: A Graphic Novel (2012) The Shadow Out of Time (2013) The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath (2014) The King in Yellow (2015) See Also Images by</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>London United Kingdom Greenwich</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United Kingdom</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Comic book adapter</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsCorrespondents"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Clark Ashton Smith</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1961 August 14th</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Carol jones dorman wt was a poet, sculptor, painter and author of fantasy, horror and science fiction short stories . He was a friend and correspondent of h.p. Lovecraft from 1922 until his death in 1937 . He is best known for his short stories and for his friendship with lovecraft .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>http://example.org/lovecraft#California Long Valley</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1893 January 13th</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Weird_Company">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Weird Company</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Easter eggs features characters from various lovecraft stories working together to defeat eldritch forces . The bulk of the novel takes the form of an account written by robert martin olmstead . Individual chapters are written from the points of view of other characters .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Pete Rawlik</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Cthulhu Mythos</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>288</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2014 October 7</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hyperborean_Cycle">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#StoryCycles"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Hyperborean Cycle</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a series of short stories that take place in the fictional prehistoric setting of hyperborea (present-day greenland) various elements in smith's cycle have been borrowed by h.p. Lovecraft , most notably the "toad-god" tsathoggua . The rapidly approaching ice age threatens to wipe out all life on the hyperborean continent .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Allan_and_the_Sundered_Veil">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crossovers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Allan and the Sundered Veil</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The league of extraordinary gentlemen is a horror-fantasy short story written by alan moore and illustrated by kevin o'neill, set in their league of extraordinary gentlemen universe . It features the character allan quatermain, from the works of h. rider haggard, joining forces with three other heroes . They are: the time traveler from h.g. Wells 'the time machine', and the dreamer from lovecraft's cthulhu mythos</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fate_of_Cthulhu_%28core_rulebook%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ebooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Fate of Cthulhu (core rulebook)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Fate of Cthulhu core rulebook Evil Hat Productions Publication Date 2019 Roleplaying game Fate The Fate of Cthulhu core rulebook is the first book in the Fate of Cthulhu supernatural horror roleplaying series, introducing the basic rules and premise for the game. It was published by Evil Hat Productions in 2019 after a successful funding campaign on Kickstarter ; both hardcover and digital editions were released. Overview Design Team Designers Leonard Balsera Stephen Blackmoore Misha Bushyager Sophie Lagacé PK Sullivan Lara Turner Artists Sarah Dahlinger Fred Hicks Kurt Komoda Tyler Walpole Gallery References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2019</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#I_Miti_di_Cthulhu_%28Italian-language_series%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>I Miti di Cthulhu (Italian-language series)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>I Miti di Cthulhu Italian Fanucci Editore March 1985 Final Publication September 1991 I Miti di Cthulhu is an Italian- series of collections of Cthulhu Mythos-related horror published by Fanucci Editore between March 1985 and September 1991. The books consist largely of translations of English- works from a variety of authors, including those of the Lovecraft circle, expanded mythos authors, and writers from whom Lovecraft took inspiration . Series Entries (incomplete) L'orrore di Cthulhu (1986) Il richiamo di Cthulhu (1987) References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>Italian</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_Weinberg">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonFictionAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Robert Weinberg</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>2016 September 25</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Robert e. weinberg (1946-2016) was an american author and editor . He wrote horror, science fiction, fantasy, and superhero comic books . He edited or co-edited dozens of anthologies, including lovecraft's legacy and miskatonic university .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>http://example.org/lovecraft#New_Jersey Newark</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasFullNameR>Robert Edward Weinberg</ex:hasFullNameR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>American</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://www.robertweinberg.net/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:hasYearsActiveR>1967-2016</ex:hasYearsActiveR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1946 August 29</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Man_of_Stone">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Man of Stone</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The horror in the museum is a short story by h.p. Lovecraft and hazel heald . It is one of five stories lovecraft revised for heald in october 1932 . The story describes the narrator's search for the missing sculptor arthur wheeler, who disappeared in the upper adirondacks of new york state .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hazel_Heald"/>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Howard_Phillips_Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Science Fiction short story</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dositheus">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dositheus</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Dositheos is a fictional character, based on the historical figure dositheos . Appears in the simon of gitta stories of richard l. tierney .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Sword_of_Spartacus"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_L._Tierney"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mind_parasite_%28disambiguation%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Disambiguations"/>
    <rdfs:label>Mind parasite (disambiguation)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The term Mind Parasites may refer to one of the following: Creatures Doels Lloigor Nyghan Grii Flying Polyps Nioth-Korghai The unnamed Mind Parasites that appear in Colin Wilson 's 1967 novel The Mind Parasites . Fictional Books The Mind Parasites , a Mythos monograph of the same name written by the character Professor Gilbert Austin. Novels The Mind Parasites , a 1967 novel by British author Colin Wilson .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Aquarium">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Aquarium</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A short story by carl jacobi that first appeared in the collection dark mind, dark heart . It is notable for its introduction of several marine-themed eldritch tomes . The story follows a painter and her friend who lease a house in knightsbridge, london .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Carl_Jacobi"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Dark Heart</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Dark Mind</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>1962</ex:isPublishedAsW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lovecrafts_Legacy">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Lovecraft's Legacy</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Robert E. Weinberg &amp; Martin H. Greenberg, eds. Cover Artist Duncan Eagleson English Tor Release Date November 1990 334 is an anthology of Lovecraftian horror edited by Robert E. Weinberg and Martin H. Greenberg, and published by Tor in 1990. All the stories were original to the collection, which featured an introduction by Robert Bloch . Contents "Introduction: An Open Letter to H. P. Lovecraft" (essay) by Robert Bloch "A Secret of the Heart" by Mort Castle "The Other Man" by Ray Garton "Will" by Graham Masterton "Big 'C'" by Brian Lumley "Ugly" by Gary Brandner "The Blade and the Claw" by Hugh B. Cave "Soul Keeper" by Joseph A. Citro "From the Papers of Helmut Hecker" by Chet Williamson " Meryphillia " by Brian McNaughton " Lord of the Land " by Gene Wolfe "H.P.L." by Gahan Wilson "The Order of Things Unknown" by Ed Gorman " The Barrens " by F. Paul Wilson</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Martin H. Greenberg</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Robert E. Weinberg</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>eds.</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>334</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>November 1990</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cave_beast">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cave beast</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sociocultural Characteristics Earth Real World Information First appearance " The Beast in the Cave " Created by Howard Phillips Lovecraft Cave beasts are a species of humanoid creatures in the Cthulhu Mythos . They are believed to be the inbred descendants of cannibalistic cave-dwelling humans . They are described as white semi-humanoid creatures with long clawed nails.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Beast_in_the_Cave"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Aphra_Marsh">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Aphra Marsh</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Innsmouth"/>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Federal Bureau of Investigation</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Aphra Yukhl Marsh</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Aphra is the protagonist of the innsmouth legacy books by ruthanna emrys . She is the leader of a small magical coven, following the aeonist teachings she was raised in . She is assumed to be of a white ethnicity despite the mixed heritage of the deep ones .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Innsmouth_Legacy"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ruthanna_Emrys"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Henry_J._Vester,_III">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Henry J. Vester, III</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Editor California, USA United States is an American author, poet and occasional editor who has contributed several works to the greater Cthulhu Mythos . These have appeared in periodicals such as Chronicles of the Cthulhu Codex , Tales of Lovecraftian Horror , and Fungi , as well as the the anthologies The Innsmouth Cycle (1997) , The Tsathoggua Cycle (2003), The Averoigne Legacy (2019), and The Yig Cycle (2021). Selected Works Editor Chronicles of the Cthulhu Codex (1989-1994) External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads at the H. P. Lovecraft Film Festival website</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA http://example.org/lovecraft#California</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Editor</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Congregation_of_the_Earth_Mother">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cults"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Organisation"/>
    <rdfs:label>Congregation of the Earth Mother</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a fictional organization in the cthulhutech pencil-and-paper roleplaying game by wildfire llc . It's an ancient cult, that has existed for millennia and wielded great power . Recently it has been decimated by the neg and is a shadow of what it once was .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu_Codex_Lammas_1998">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu Codex Lammas 1998</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Cover Artist Allen Koszowski English Necronomicon Press Release Date August 1998 44 Preceded by Eastertide 1998 Followed by Hallowmas 1998 is the fourteenth issue of fanzine Cthulhu Codex . Edited by Robert M. Price and featuring cover art from Allen Koszowski , it was published by Necronomicon Press in August 1998. Contents Editorial: From the Scriptorium by Robert M. Price "Simply, the Sea" by Michael Gillis "Stomach Pains" by Franklyn Searight "No Answer" by D. F. Lewis "The Shadow of the Sleeping God" by James Ambuehl " The Return of Rhan-Tegoth " by Laurence J. Cornford</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>44</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>August 1998</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Yivo">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Yivo</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Shkle is a sentient, extraterrestrial being from another universe . Communicated with universe "gamma" (the futurama main universe) through the space-time hole called "the anomaly" appears in the second direct-to-dvd movie, the beast with a billion backs .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Harold_Clemmens">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Harold Clemmens</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Miskatonic_University"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>University president The Arkham Detective Agency Miskatonic University Human First appearance " Cruelty " Created by C. J. Henderson is a character created by C. J. Henderson as part of his Arkham Detective Agency series. Overview At the time the stories are set, is the president of Miskatonic University , Arkham 's premier higher-learning institution. C. J. Henderson describes him as: Despite his appearance, he is portrayed as being something of a visionary, making such decisions as retaining the services of a private detective agency: not only as physical security, but to monitor Arkham's underworld for rumours of any criminal activities directed towards the university. Highly intelligent, he also appears to have at least a rudimentary knowledge of the gods and monsters of the Mythos , as shown in the short story " Cruelty ". Appearances " Cruelty " ( C. J. Henderson , 2017) " Those Folk Below " ( Josh Reynolds , 2017) " A Pleasure in Madness " (C. J. Henderson, 2017)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cruelty"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#C._J._Henderson"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gate_of_Truth">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalLocations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LocationsOriginatingFromMythosInspiredWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Gate of Truth</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The the is a metaphysical structure that appears in the fictional universe of the fullmetal alchemist manga and anime . In the anime, within the gate are shadowy entities (the gate children) whom are the owners of the ethereal limbs . In the cosmos, all living things possess their own gates, giving them the ability to see truth .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Curse_of_the_Chthonians">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chaosium"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Curse of the Chthonians</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sandy Petersen United States English Call of Cthulhu Chaosium Release Date 1984 88 (first edition) 160 (second edition) is a scenario sourcebook for the Call of Cthulhu horror roleplaying game by Chaosium . Overview Containing a selection of four subterranean-themed, ready-to-play scenarios (two of which are linked to form a mini-campaign of sorts), along with a background section on Kabbalistic Science, runs to 88 (first edition) and 160 (second edition). It was written by renowned games designer Sandy Petersen . Contents "The City Without a Name" by William Hamblin "The Curse of Chaugnar Faugn" by William A. Barton "Dark Carnival" by David A. Hargrave "Thoth's Dagger" by William Hamblin Publication History First released in 1984, was later expanded and reissued in 2011. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sandy_Petersen"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>160 88</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW>Call of Cthulhu</ex:isPartOfSeriesW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>1984</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Zeboyd_Games">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Publishers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Zeboyd Games</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>2009 Robert Boyd William Stiernberg United States Lake Arrowhead, California, USA https://zeboyd.com/  is an indie video games company which specialises in retro-style side-scrolling roleplaying games. by Robert Boyd and William Stiernberg, who are also the lead developers, it has produced two games inspired by the Cthulhu Mythos established through the works of H. P. Lovecraft . Selected Releases Cthulhu Saves the World (2010) Cthulhu Saves Christmas (2019)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://zeboyd.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#isInLanguageW">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label>isInLanguageW</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sorcery_Against_Caesar">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sorcery Against Caesar</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The complete simon of gitta short stories is a collection of richard l. tierney 's simon of gitta stories, published by pickman's press in 2020 . The book is an expanded edition of tierney's collection the soul of thoth . Includes two collaborations wrote with robert m. price and glenn rahman .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_L._Tierney"/>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>433</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>December 2020</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nycrama">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nycrama</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Elder Gods</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Zombifying Existence</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>A cosmic entity first mentioned by clarkashton smith in his unfinished story "the infernal star" and expanded upon by john w. patterson . Made its first actual appearance in his cthulhu mythos story " priestess of ", published in the december 1999 edition of edward p. berglund's online fanzine nightscapes .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Priestess_of_Nycrama"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#John_W._Patterson"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Don_Diego_de_la_Vega">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Don Diego de la Vega</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Zorro</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Zorro is a wealthy landowner who takes up the masked identity of a hero to "to avenge the helpless, to punish cruel politicians, to aid the oppressed" in 2019, he became the protagonist in the lovecraftian pastiche zorro: rise of the old gods by american mythology productions .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAppearanceC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Zorro:_Rise_of_the_Old_Gods"/>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Curse of Capistrano</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Johnston McCulley</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Periodicals">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Creature_Cops:_Special_Varmint_Unit">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Creature Cops: Special Varmint Unit</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Rob Anderson Fernando Melek, Novo Malgapo English IDW Publishing Release Date Jan 2015 - March 2015 Media Type Comic book is a 3-issue miniseries written by Rob Anderson in 2015 which was published by IDW Publishing. Synopsis In a world in which spliced hybrid animals is commonplace, the Special Varmint Unit deals with handling the hybrid animals. They have to deal with a cult trying to bring about the return of the Outer Gods .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Rob Anderson</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>Jan 2015 - March 2015</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Edward_Pretorius">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedInMythos"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Edward Pretorius</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Dr. Inventor is a fictional character created for the 1986 film adaptation of h. p. lovecraft's short story " from beyond ". He is the main antagonist in the film, with most of the action taking place in his mansion. He is played by actor ted sorel .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#From_Beyond"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Chimaera">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CharactersIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Chimaera</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Relatives Parents: Echidna (mother), Typhon (father) Siblings: Angerboda , Orthrus , Ladon , Cerberus , Lernean Hydra , Teumessian Fox , and the Snakes of Many Colors The is a monster in Greek mythology, a fire breathing creature that merges elements of three different animals (which depending on the version is lion/snake/goat or lion/dragon/goat). Background Marvel The is a descendant of the Elder God Set . It's the offspring of Set's granddaughter Echidna who mated with Typhon, one of the Titans. Notes In Witches and Other Night-Fears , Charles Lamb mentioned the Gorgons , Hydras, and Chimaeras, as "dire stories of Celaeno and the Harpies ". The excerpt is quoted as a preface in " The Dunwich Horror " ( HPL ).</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Voltiyig">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Voltiyig</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC>Great Old Ones</ex:hasAffiliationC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Yig's Terrifying Son</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Also known as Yig's Terrifying Son Relatives Yig (Father) Great Old Ones First appearance EXP : "The Thing in the Library" is an entity described as a feathered winged serpent with flaming nostrils, similarly to the Aztec god Quetzalcoatl and is imprisoned within a dark tower topped with a giant five-pointed star. He is a spawn of Yig .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Thing in the Library</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Debt_of_the_Summoner">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Debt of the Summoner</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"the temple of bonesthe punishment of igharta" is a pseudo-factual cthulhu mythos work by and editor f. c. adams . It forms part of his translations from the book of yng .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>F. C. Adams</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Translations_from_the_Book_of_Yng"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Nightscapes #9</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>September 1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dark_Young_of_Shub-Niggurath">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ServitorRaces"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dark Young of Shub-Niggurath</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAffiliationC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Shub-Niggurath"/>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The dark young are a fictional species first mentioned by h. p. lovecraft in the short story " the whisperer in darkness " they are horrifying, pitch-black monstrosities, seemingly made of ropy tentacles . They are usually called upon to preside over cult ceremonies . One means for summoning them is found in the book of eibon and requires a blood offering .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Whisperer in Darkness Notebook Found in a Deserted House</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>H. P. Lovecraft Robert Bloch</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Arkham_Horror:_The_Card_Game">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Arkham Horror: The Card Game</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Arkham horror is a card-based investigative adventure game . Instead of being a player-versus-player game, players take on the roles of investigators attempting to find out more about the supernatural events which plague arkham . Each investigator comes with a small number of unique cards which they are required to incorporate into their decks .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>Late 2016</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft:_Tales_of_Horror_%28Leather-Bound_Classics%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#HPLovecraftWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>H. P. Lovecraft: Tales of Horror (Leather-Bound Classics)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>H. P. Lovecraft: Tales of Horror H. P. Lovecraft United States English Canterbury Classics Release Date October 1, 2017 768 H. P. Lovecraft: Tales of Horror is a collection of H. P. Lovecraft 's stories, published by Canterbury Classics in 2017. It is part of their Leather-Bound Classics series. Contents Introduction by Ken Mondschein, PhD " Dagon " " The Statement of Randolph Carter " " The Cats of Ulthar " " The Temple " " The Picture in the House " " The Outsider " " The Music of Erich Zann " " Herbert West--Reanimator " " The Lurking Fear " " The Rats in the Walls " " The Festival " " The Shunned House " " In the Vault " " Cool Air " " The Call of Cthulhu " " Pickman's Model " The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath The Case of Charles Dexter Ward " The Colour Out of Space " " The Dunwich Horror " The Whisperer in Darkness At the Mountains of Madness The Shadow Over Innsmouth " The Dreams in the Witch House " " The Thing on the Doorstep " The Shadow Out of Time " The Haunter of the Dark "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasPageCountW>768</ex:hasPageCountW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>2017 October 1</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Events">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Organisation"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Bernard_Austin_Dwyer">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSFriendsAndAcquaintances"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Bernard Austin Dwyer</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1943</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Weird fiction fan and would-be writer and artist, living in West Shokan, NY; correspondent of Lovecraft.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1897</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Marinus_Bicknell_Willett">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MagicUsers"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Marinus Bicknell Willett</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Willett is the family physician of the protagonist of his novel the case of charles dexter ward . He grows increasingly worried about ward's obsession with his ancestor joseph curwen . Willett eventually uses the reverse of the resurrection spell to turn curwen back into dust .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Case_of_Charles_Dexter_Ward"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Book_of_Dzyan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ArtefactsOriginatingFromMythosAdjacentWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosBooksFictional"/>
    <rdfs:label>Book of Dzyan</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The book of dyzan tibet senzar is a text that theosophist helena petrovna blavatsky claimed to have seen in tibet . The book relates, among other things, the origins of humanity on the lost continents of hyperborea, lemuria, and atlantis . The word "dzyan" is pronounced "djan" and signifies "to reform one's self by meditation and knowledge"</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Swan_Point_Cemetery">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Swan Point Cemetery</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Lovecraft's gravestone reads "i am providence" swan point was started in 1846 as a 60-acre tract of land . It has now grown to 200 acres, and is still accepting internments . The second most famous horror writer buried there is a friend of lovecraft's .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasTypeL>Graveyard</ex:hasTypeL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Rhode_Island"/>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Providence</ex:isLocatedAtL>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tunneler_Below">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tunneler Below</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The tunnelers below are a fictional race created by fritz leiber for his short story " the terror from the depths " they are a race of worm-like creatures which are as long as humans and as thick as their thighs . They possess great psionic abilities, especially telepathy, which they use to invade dreams, steal the memories and thoughts of the unwilling, and communicate with spirits .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Terror_from_the_Depths"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fritz_Leiber"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Grimoire">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artefact"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosBooksFictional"/>
    <rdfs:label>Grimoire</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Grimoires are books of great importance or power and are the source of much of the forbidden knowledge that illuminates the darkest recesses of the world . They contain forbidden knowledge and discoveries about the great old ones or of many occult topics . Often times these discoveries of forbidden truths end up driving the reader insane or leading to their ruin .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Blade_of_the_Slayer">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Blade of the Slayer</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story first appeared in pulse-pounding adventure stories #1 , published in 1986 . A significantly different version appeared in tierney's collection the scroll of thoth . The identity of the slayer is different in the two published versions .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Richard_L._Tierney"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Pulse Pounding Adventure Stories #1</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 1986</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Steven_Philip_Jones">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Editors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonFictionAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Steven Philip Jones</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Birth Date 1960 Lincoln, Nebraska, USA United States Weird fiction Non-fiction http://stevenphilipjones.com/ is an American author and editor whose genres include non-fiction and weird fiction, although he is best known for his comic book scripts and adaptations. He has adapted a number of stories by H. P. Lovecraft for the comic book format and written several more, most of which has been published through indie publishing house Caliber Comics . Selected Works Novels Lovecraftian: The Shipwright Circle (2019) Short Fiction " The Empty House on Harley Street " (2020) Comic Books The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: Arthur Jermyn (1993) The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: Dagon (1993) The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: The Music of Erich Zann (1993) The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: The Picture in the House (1993) The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: The Statement of Randolph Carter (1996) The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: The Alchemist (1997) The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: The Lurking Fear (1997) The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: The Tomb (1997) The Worlds of H. P. Lovecraft: Beyond the Wall of Sleep (1998) H. P. Lovecraft's Worlds: Volume 1 H. P. Lovecraft's Worlds: Volume 2 External Links at the Internet Speculative Fiction Database at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>USA Nebraska Lincoln</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>http://stevenphilipjones.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1960</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Inception">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#BrianLumleyWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Inception</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"lord of the worms" is a short story by brian lumley . It is the first story in his collection of short stories entitled "titus crow" .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brian_Lumley"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPartOfSeriesW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Titus_Crow"/>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Compleat_Crow"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>April 1987</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Menoptera">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraterrestrialSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Menoptera</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The are a humanoid insectoid race which have human-like faces . They have four large wings that give them the ability to fly . The secret to their flight was the anti-gravitic power of isocryte .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Web Planet</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Bill Strutton</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sebastian_Greaves">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sebastian Greaves</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Horror is an author who has written a number of stories set in the greater Cthulhu Mythos inspired by the works of H. P. Lovecraft and many others. These works have, as of May 2022, all been collected in the anthology Cthulhu Mythos Anthology . Selected Bibliography Collections Cthulhu Mythos Anthology Short Fiction " Have You Seen the Yellow Sign? " " The Eye of Horus " " Great Cthulhu " " The Blind Idiot Godhead " " Nyarlathotep: The Man With No Face "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Stephen_Hickman">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Artists"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Stephen Hickman</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Hickman illustrated over 425 covers for ace, baen, ballantine, bantam, doubleday, doubleday, phage press, doubleday, del rey, doubleday, and others over a 44 year period beginning in 1976 . Hickman won a world science fiction convention's hugo award, 6 chesley awards from the association of science fiction and fantasy artists and 2 spectrum gold awards .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Patrice_Hathaway">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Patrice Hathaway</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Violinist</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The violinist was created by fantasy flight games as a playable character for several of their arkham horror files lines of games . Originally conceived as a character for the innsmouth horror expansion of arkham horror second edition . She has also made appearances in several of fantasy flight's companion games including eldritch horror , and elder sign .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Innsmouth Horror Deluxe Expansion</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Bank_Job">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Bank Job</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>'looking for darla: stories of mythos noir' is a short story by american author ron shiflet . It was first published in his collection looking for darla: stories of mythos noir in september 2008 . Despite having no direct connections to the works of h.p. Lovecraft or the cthulhu mythos, dorffman mentions that he studied the arab's book .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Looking_for_Darla:_Stories_of_Mythos_Noir"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>September 2008</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Dark_One_%28disambiguation%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Disambiguations"/>
    <rdfs:label>Dark One (disambiguation)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The term " Dark Ones " has multiple meanings within the Cthulhu Mythos . The wizard John Carnby claimed that his brother and fellow sorcerer Helman Carnby was "more favored by the Dark Ones". As he identifies himself and his brother as being "in the service of Satan and of Those who were before Satan", it's possible that these beings include the Dark Ones. ( CIRCLE : " The Return of the Sorcerer ") In the R'lyeh Text , as translated by Bryant Hoskins, the Dark Ones are the servitor race that serves Ghatanothoa , under the leadership of Yeb ( EXP : " Behind the Mask ") Therefore, it's possible that they might be the Lloigor species , which have likewise been identified as Ghatanothoa's minions ( EXP : " The Return of the Lloigor "). Other uses of the term: Dark One (Cthulhu Dark Ages) Dark One (Dynamite Entertainment)</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Millie_Von_Clydesburgh">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Millie Von Clydesburgh</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Millie is a fictional character created by author brandon berntson for his lovecraft mythos trilogy . She made her debut appearance in the first book in the series, 2018's the night of dagon . Millie has a deep-seated affection for devlin macky, but he seems reluctant to commit to any kind of meaningful relationship .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Night_of_Dagon"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Brandon_Berntson"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nemean_Lion">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CharactersIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nemean Lion</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The monster is a descendant of the elder god set . It's the offspring of set's granddaughter echidna who mated with typhon, one of the titans .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Thor</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>the Warriors Vol 1 #1</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Colleen Coover</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ynathogguan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Y'nathogguan</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Real World Information First appearance " The Temple of the Demon " Created by Crispin Burnham Y'nathogguans are the Servitors of Quyagen who dwell in Quy . Legend says that they are humanoid reptiles with heads like tigers, save that they have tentacles instead of whiskers. They hunt men down and when these tentacles touch bare flesh they drain the youth and energy from them. This may have given rise to the Rakshasa of Indian folklore. They are said to have been created to battle the minions of the Great Race and those jealous gods of Glyh-Vo (Glyu-uho), and to have been given charge of man by Quyagen.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Temple of the Demon</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crispin_Burnham"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Eilor">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GreatOldOnes"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Ei'lor</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>The Star-Seed The Plant-God</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The star-seed is a great old one which features in the cthulhu mythos established by h.p. Lovecraft . It was created by james ambuehl for his short story " correlated contents " .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Correlated_Contents"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#James_Ambuehl"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#When_in_Leng...">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>When in Leng...</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>"when in leng... " is a short story by american ron shiflet . Contains extensive name-dropping of mythos stalwarts such as dagon . First published in the fanzine the ancient track in october 1998 .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Ancient_Track"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthuloide_Welten">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Periodicals"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthuloide Welten</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>German Pegasus Spiele 2001 Final Publication 2011 , which translates into English as Cthulhu Worlds , was a long-running German- gaming magazine which focused on Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu horror roleplaying game, which was published by Pegasus Spiele over the course of a decade . Overview Published by traditional games manufacturer and distributor Pegasus Spiele, ran for 21 regular issues and one special between 2001 and 2011 before being discontinued . The special was dedicated to chief editor Frank Heller who left in 2011, seemingly precipitating the magazine's demise, and was sold exclusively at CthulhuCon 2011; this issue had a print run of only 200 copies . After its discontinuation, the vacuum left by was taken up by its fan base who the zine Cthulhus Ruf ( Cthulhu's Call ), a publication created by many of its predecessors' contributors, and which would become the official publication for the Deutsche Lovecraft Gesellschaft (German Lovecraft Society) in 2014 . References</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>German</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Young_Goodwife_Doten">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Young Goodwife Doten</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Short story was first published in the 1995 anthology 100 wicked little witch stories . It was later included in a 2008 collection of price's own works . It is an expansion on events referenced in the novel thaumaturgical prodigies .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Robert_M._Price"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>September 1995</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Wonderland_%28series%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Wonderland (series)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Wonderland (series) Raven Gregory V Ken Marion English Zenescope Entertainment Release Date July 2012 - Aug 2016 Media Type Comic book Wonderland is a fifty-issue comic book series written by Raven Gregory in 2012-2016 which was published by Zenescope Entertainment. Synopsis After escaping from Wonderland , Calie Liddle believed that she was done with the realm of madness. Only to discover that Wonderland wasn't done with her, forcing her and her daughter Violet to go on the run.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Raven Gregory</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 2012 - Aug 2016</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Teumessian_Fox">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CharactersIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Teumessian Fox</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a monster of greek mythology, a gigantic fox sent by the gods to prey on the children of thebes . In the marvel fictional universe it is mentioned as being one of the offspring of echidna but it has not yet made an appearance in marvel comics .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
