  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Sphinx">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#CharactersIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>Sphinx</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The great of giza was patterned after the true , but the face was later replaced with the human one of the pharoh khephren . The real 's paws were covered in yellow fur, and each toe was equipped with a retractable claw .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Cats_of_Ulthar"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#S._Ansky">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftsInspirationsAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>S. Ansky</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1920 8 November</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Shloyme zanvl rappoport (1863, vitebsk – 1920, otwock) was a scholar who documented jewish folklore and mystical beliefs . He is best known for authoring the play the dybbuk or between two worlds .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasBirthplaceR>Czaśniki. Vitebsk Governorate Russian Empire (now Belarus)</ex:hasBirthplaceR>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>Russian</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Inspiration to H.P. Lovecraft</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isKnownForR>The Dybbuk</ex:isKnownForR>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1863</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Artifact">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Books"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Artifact</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Brian Hill English Brian Hill Publication date March 2023  " " is the first book of a mythos-inspired mystery/horror series by American Brian Hill. Synopsis 'Rev' Parata is a down-on-his-luck PI scraping by in the shadow of 1980's New Orleans . He receives a job from a mysterious British aristocrat to recover a stolen canopic jar, a jar that supposedly contains the earthly remains of the mythical "Black Pharoah" of Egypt 's third dynasty. During his investigation, Rev finds that neither the Jar nor his client are what they seem, and he is propelled headlong into powers beyond understanding. External Links at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Brian Hill</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 2023</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Wolf_Man">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Wolf Man</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Wolf Men</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>Wolf People</ex:hasAlternativeNamesC>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The wolf men are a fictional race in the marvel universe . They first appeared in 1971 in issue #2 of the 1st volume of the kull the conqueror comic book series .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>Kull the Conqueror Vol 1 #2</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Roy_Thomas"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Marie Severin</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Astounding_Stories">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#PulpMagazines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Astounding Stories</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Astounding was the leading science fiction magazine of the pulp magazine era . It published two of h. p. lovecraft's most highly regarded works: at the mountains of madness and the shadow out of time . Astounding was bought in 1933 by the street &amp; smith publishing company after clayton went bankrupt . Street &amp; smith hired f. orlin tremaine as editor .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Avatars">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Eldritch_Realms_%28Age_of_Wonders_4%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Eldritch Realms (Age of Wonders 4)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Eldritch realms is a 4th content pack for age of wonders 4 that is strongly inspired by the cthulhu mythos . Players are immersed in a world corrupted by the umbral abyss, a malevolent dimension filled with cosmic horrors . New map layer filled with unique locations, tactical combat maps, and independent factions of umbral dwellers .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nyarlathoteps_aliases">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OuterGods"/>
    <rdfs:label>Nyarlathotep's aliases</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Nyarlathotep is known as the god of a thousand forms . Although he is known to visit planets across the cosmos, it is likely that he employs far more forms than a mere thousand . Some of his aliases were dedicated to specific avatars .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Wolf-Like_Mountain">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonSapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Wolf-Like Mountain</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Real World Information First appearance The Dream-Quest of Unknown Kadath Created by H. P. Lovecraft</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dream-Quest_of_Unknown_Kadath"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Notebook_Concerning_the_Class_Struggle_in_Dunwich,_Found_in_the_Ruins_of_a_Construction_Site">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Notebook Concerning the Class Struggle in Dunwich, Found in the Ruins of a Construction Site</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Kevin Wetmore English 01Publishing Whispers from the Abyss Vol. 2: The Horrors That Are &amp; Shall Be date October 2015 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos story by American Kevin Wetmore , first published in the anthology of Lovecraftian fiction Whispers from the Abyss Vol. 2: The Horrors That Are &amp; Shall Be by 01Publishing in October 2015. Synopsis In the 1980s, a wealthy social activist studying at Miskatonic University attempts to build housing for the poor on the old Whateley property in Dunwich , Massachusetts , despite the warnings from locals. Although the rest of his crew flees at the sound of something monstrous moving through the nearby woods, he decides to remain, and his body is later found by police, seemingly crushed under the ruins of the home he was working on.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Kevin_Wetmore"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Shall Be</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Whispers from the Abyss Vol. 2: The Horrors That Are</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2015</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Chant_of_Dagon">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Chant of Dagon</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here Ron Shiflet United States English Publication date March 1998 " " is a short Cthulhu Mythos poem by American Ron Shiflet . It runs to a total of fourteen lines. Synopsis A searcher of forbidden knowledge narrates his own sacrifice, culminating in the summoning of an unknown beast- but likely not Dagon , as the title suggests. Publication History "" was published in issue #11 of the zine Cthulhu Cultus in March 1998.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Crypt_of_Cthulhu_Yuletide_1985">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Fanzines"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Crypt of Cthulhu Yuletide 1985</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Various Guy Cowlishaw Cover Artist Robert M. Price English Cryptic Publications Release Date December 1985 Preceded by Hallowmas 1985 Followed by Candlemas 1986 is the 36th issue of long-running fanzine Crypt of Cthulhu . Running to a total of 52 pages, it was edited by Robert M. Price , who also provided the cover art, and published by Cryptic Publications in December 1985. Much of the contents of this issue were devoted to the works of Lin Carter . Contents Editorial by Robert M. Price " The Doom of Yakthoob " by Lin Carter Review: The Year's Best Horror Stories XIII by Stefan Dziemianowicz "The City of Pillars" by Lin Carter " The Acolyte of the Flame " by Lin Carter "The Stone from Mnar" by Lin Carter "H. P. Lovecraft: The History" by Lin Carter "Under the Eaves" by Lin Carter "High Atlantis" by Lin Carter "The Carter-Smith "Collaborations"" by Steve Behrends "A Response" by Lin Carter Interview with Lin Carter "Advice to the Lovecraft-Lorn" by Donna Death Review: Discovering Modern Horror Fiction 1 by Stefan Dziemianowicz Review: The Collector's Index to Weird Tales by Robert M. Price Review: Etchings and Odysseys #6 by Stefan Dziemianowicz Letters by Jim Cort, Stefan R. Dziemianowicz, Pierre Comtois , Robert Bloch , Charles Garofalo , Carl Ford , Richard L. Tierney , Tani Jantsang , Matt Rogers, Steve Behrends, Will Murray , and Joel Shea</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Various</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>December 1985</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Four_Sealed_Jars">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Four Sealed Jars</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>is a 1975 short story made by Gary Myers in his collection of stories The House of the Worm .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Drunk_on_Dream">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Drunk on Dream</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The unnamed narrator turns to alcohol in an effort to forget about his former lover . After a while it dawns on him that, while passed out, his consciousness visits another plane of existence in which he can control the landscape . During one of these nighttime excursions, he believes he glimpses his ex and, upon waking, resolves to seek her out . However, in a drunken stupor one evening, accidentally causes a car crash which results in his death .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jeffrey_Thomas"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Lovecraftian fiction</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#New_Maps_of_Dream"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>July 2021</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosFilmAndTelevisionAdaptations">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Devils_Bathtub">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Devil's Bathtub</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Iris is a neglected ten-year-old girl who lives on a remote antarctic research station with her workaholic father . When her dog goes missing, she discovers that it has been assimilated by creatures from the nearby, beings known as shoggoths .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Lois_H._Gresh"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Madness_of_Cthulhu"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>October 2014</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#hasSeriesPart">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label>hasSeriesPart</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <owl:inverseOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#isPartOfSeriesW"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Wendigo">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SpeciesIncorporatedFromFolkloreMythAndReligion"/>
    <rdfs:label>Wendigo</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The is a supernatural creature originating from algonquian myth and folklore . It is typically described as a gaunt, ghoulish humanoid associated with a foul stench and an insatiable craving for human flesh . In popular culture, wendigos have been depicted in various ways, often as monstrous humanoids, associated with the northern winds and/or cannibalism . In some depictions, humans can be possessed by or transformed</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#MGubgub">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdfs:label>M'Gubgub</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Background is an eldritch cosmic entity and cosmic horror element in nth man's fictional universe (earth-8908) it spans 9 million light years across, which is x4.5 larger than the largest known galaxy in the cosmos (ic 1101) or nearly x90 as large as the milky way galaxy . It has a variety of abilities including the ability to alter its size at will, fire energy blasts, communicate telepathically, and was possessed cosmic awareness .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Incubus">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AugustDerlethWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Incubus</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>is a poem by August Derleth that appeared in his collection In Lovecraft's Shadow: The Cthulhu Mythos Stories of August Derleth .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_House_of_the_Worm_%28collection%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pastiches"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The House of the Worm (collection)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The house of the worm is a collection of short stories by american author gary myers . It was published by arkham house in 1975 as a limited-edition hardcover with a run of 4,144 copies . All ten of the stories contained in this volume were reprinted in a subsequent collection of mythos work, the of the worm .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Gary_Myers"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror Fantasy</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Averoigne_%28poem%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AveroigneCycle"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ClarkAshtonSmithWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Averoigne (poem)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Averoigne (poem) Clark Ashton Smith United States English Arkham House The Dark Chateau and Other Poems date December 1951 "Averoigne" is a poem by Clark Ashton Smith about Averoigne , the French province he invented for his historical fantasy stories. The poem first appeared in the Spring 1951 issue of the magazine Challenge , and in the Arkham House collection The Dark Chateau and Other Poems (1951). The poem depicts Averoigne as a place where "the enchantress weaves/Weird spells," where "abides the mage", and where "the lamia sings". External Link "Averoigne" complete text at Eldritch Dark</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Clark_Ashton_Smith"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>Other Poems</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW>The Dark Chateau</ex:isPublishedAsW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>December 1951</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Obyrith">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherSupernaturalBeings"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Obyrith</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The obyriths are ancient demons which predated all other demonic races and even the gods . As time passed, some of the lords assumed humanoid forms . There are only twelve lords remaining, the greatest being the queen of chaos and the pale night .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#April_Moon_Books">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SmallPress"/>
    <rdfs:label>April Moon Books</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>2014 Neil Baker Canada Ontario, Canada Horror Fiction https://www.aprilmoonbooks.com/ is a small press publisher which specialises in the horror fiction and has released several books about and inspired by the works of Howard Phillips Lovecraft . It was in 2014 by author, editor and artist Neil Baker . Selected Output Anthologies The Dark Rites of Cthulhu (2014, edited by Brian M. Sammons ) The Call of Poohthulhu (2022, edited by Neil Baker ) Chapbooks A Picnic at the Mountains of Madness (2015, Neil Baker )</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://www.aprilmoonbooks.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#George_Kirk">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftSFriendsAndAcquaintances"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>George Kirk</rdfs:label>
    <ex:diedOnR>1962</ex:diedOnR>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Member of the Kalem Club. He published Twenty-one Letters of Ambrose Bierce (1922) and ran the Chelsea Bookshop in New York.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasRealPersonMateR rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
    <ex:wasBornOnR>1898</ex:wasBornOnR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Bubble_creature">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#NonSapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Bubble creature</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Sociocultural Characteristics Extra-dimensional Real World Information First appearance HPL : " The Dreams in the Witch-House " Created by H. P. Lovecraft Keziah Mason took the form of a bubble agglomerate while traveling in the fourth dimension .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Dreams_in_the_Witch-House"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#H._P._Lovecraft"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Jack_Ryan">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Jack Ryan</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author United States is an American author and newspaper columnist who has written a handful of works set in the greater Cthulhu Mythos . In addition to these, he has also released numerous non-fiction works on such diverse topics as travel destinations and franchise tie-in colouring books. Ryan currently resides in New England, the setting for some of his mythos works. Selected Works Novellas Crypt of Cthulhu (2018) Crypt of Cthulhu 2 (2019) Crypt of Cthulhu 3 (2023) External Links at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasNationalityR>United States</ex:hasNationalityR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Tindalosian_Hybrid">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#EarthNativeSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Tindalosian Hybrid</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Tindalosian hybrids are creatures from roger zelazny's 1981 novel the changing land . They are not a natural species, and are instead created whenever someone consumes the blue ichor that may be left by hounds of tindalos .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>The Changing Land</ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Roger_Zelazny"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Pastiches">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Phredd_Groves">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Phredd Groves</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Games designer Roleplaying games is a traditional games designer. He is perhaps most relevant to this wiki for his work on third-party content provider Golden Goblin Press 's supplements for Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game. Selected Works De Horrore Cosmico (2015) Tales of the Caribbean (2016) External Links at Goodreads</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#wasPublishedOnW">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#FunctionalProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label>wasPublishedOnW</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Tomb_Herd">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RamseyCampbellWorks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Tomb Herd</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>is a Mythos short story written by Ramsay Campbell when he was 15, albeit published in 1986. It appeared in Crypt of Cthulhu #43. Synopsis A visitor to Kingsport finds it impossible to leave, as physical space is bent around, meaning the roads out of town lead back in. The town itself is an overwhelmed ruin, haunted by and lich-like horrors in the church graveyard. Behind the Mythos The story is notable for its welter of Mythos deities namechecked in almost a laundry list approach and for Campbell's great dedication to attempting to ape the literary style and affectations of Lovecraft himself.</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Michael_Dziesinski">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Michael Dziesinski</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Games Designer Author Horror is a games designer and author who has worked on Chaosium 's Call of Cthulhu roleplaying game, and contributed a short story to the anthology Arkham Tales: Legends of the Haunted City , also by Chaosium. Selected Bibliography Short Fiction " Burnt Tea " (2006) Gaming Supplements Secrets of Japan (2005) Strange Aeons II (2010) Atomic-Age Cthulhu (2013)</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Games Designer</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Hsieh_Ho">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Humans"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosCharacters"/>
    <rdfs:label>Hsieh Ho</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Ho is a character created by frank belknap long for his cthulhu mythos novel the horror from the hills . In his dream, saw " big green animals " descending from mountains and devouring people, and was ordered by gautama siddhartha to head to the museum of fine arts . He also saw the museum's curator of archaeology, algernon harris, and somehow also knew that that man would help him .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_from_the_Hills"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Frank_Belknap_Long"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_at_Red_Hook_%28DART_production%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#LovecraftCircleAudioAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Horror at Red Hook (DART production)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The horror at red hook is an audio play of the story of the same name by the h. p. lovecraft historical society . It is a lively dramatization of one of hpl 's most controversial works . Running to a total of 66 minutes, the play is available in both physical (compact disc) and digital downloads.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isBasedOnW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Horror_at_Red_Hook"/>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Soul_Worm">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Soul Worm</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Soul worms are a malevolent race of alien intelligences . A race of manipulators and enslavers . Range in size from 25-30 ft in length and 5-7 ft in height .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnC rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Rifts"/>
    <ex:wasCreatedByC>Palladium Games</ex:wasCreatedByC>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Revenge_of_Wonderland">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ComicBooks"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Revenge of Wonderland</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Raven Gregory Allan Otero English Zenescope Entertainment Release Date July 2018 - Jan 2019 Media Type Comic book is a six-issue comic book mini-series written by Raven Gregory in 2018-2019 which was published by Zenescope Entertainment. Synopsis Wonderland is now ruled by the Duchess. She's kidnapping people from the world and re-shaping them to serve as her playthings. Calie Liddle and her daughter Violet fight to prevent the madness of Wonderland from invading the world.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW>Raven Gregory</ex:hasAuthorW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasReleasedOnW>July 2018 - Jan 2019</ex:wasReleasedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#hasNotableFeatureL">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label>hasNotableFeatureL</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Hounds_of_Tindalos">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Hounds of Tindalos</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The story's narrator, "frank", appears to be the same character from long's previous short story, " the space-eaters " . The story follows unconventional researcher, . Halpin chalmers, who believes that he can view the entirety of time and . Space through the use of a mysterious eastern narcotic called . Liao .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Frank_Belknap_Long"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Short Fiction Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>March 1929</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#%C3%89liphas_L%C3%A9vi">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#AlterEgosCharactersIncorporatedFromTheRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#DeceasedRealWorld"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Éliphas Lévi</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Alphonse Louis Constant , better known as (1810 - 1875), was a French occultist and ceremonial magician who wrote several influential books on the subject. Lévi is mentioned in H. P. Lovecraft 's novel The Case of Charles Dexter Ward , in which he is described as a "cryptic soul who crept through a crack in the forbidden door and glimpsed the frightful vistas of the void beyond". The words of the magic formulae that Ward uses to bring his ancestor Joseph Curwen back from the dead are compared to Lévi's "mystic writings".</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#hasTypeO">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label>hasTypeO</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Organisation"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Neflord">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Character"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExtraDimensionalSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#SapientSpecies"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Species"/>
    <rdfs:label>Neflord</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The neflords are a race of dark gods in the fictional universe of the hack/slash comic book by devil's due publishing . They rule the dead dimension of nef and its demonic inhabitants, including the humanoid nefspawn . Despite being powerful gods they cannot create, as they are gods of death and suffering .</ex:hasAnnotation>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cabot_Museum">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Location"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Structures"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cabot Museum</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>The of archaeology is a fictional institution located in boston , created by h. p. lovecraft and hazel heald in the story " out of the aeons ". Originally designed as a mansion by bullfinch in 1919, the small museum specialises in objects from ancient and unknown civilisations . Before interest in the ancient pacific mummy in 1932 it held little public interest, but had widespread recognition in scientific circles .</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Boston"/>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Beacon Hill District</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Mass.</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:isLocatedAtL>Mt Vernon Street</ex:isLocatedAtL>
    <ex:madeFirstAppearanceOnL rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Out_of_the_Aeons"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#The_Neon_Morgue">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>The Neon Morgue</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Nathan Wunner English Genre(s) Horror 01Publishing Whispers from the Abyss date 2013  " " is a short Lovecraftian story by Nathan Wunner . Synopsis In order to survive, a man living in a hellish future tries to barter with the creatures which now rule the world. Unfortunately, they see through his attempt to trick them with a dishonest trade, and the beings descend on him in a savage fury. History "" was published in the Lovecraft -inspired anthology Whispers from the Abyss by 01Publishing in 2013.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Nathan_Wunner"/>
    <ex:hasGenreW>Horror</ex:hasGenreW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:isPublishedAsW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Whispers_from_the_Abyss"/>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2013</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhu:_The_Great_Old_One_%28card_game%29">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#GamesAndAdaptations"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu: The Great Old One (card game)</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Cthulhu: The Great Old One Dann Kriss Dann Kriss Games Publication Date 2014 2019 (Deluxe Edition) Card game Old Maid Cthulhu: The Great Old One is a Cthulhu Mythos -based card game from Dann Kriss Games. Overview Designed by Dann Kriss with artwork supplied by Ian Daniels, Cthulhu: The Great Old One is a party card game for 2-10 players. At its core, it is a version of the popular Old Maid game, with a handful of minor mechanics added to better represent the Cthulhu Mythos aspect of the game. First published in 2014 through a kickstarter campaign, a Deluxe Edition was released in 2019, also funded via kickstarter. Gallery</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>2019 2014</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Cthulhus_Dark_Spawn">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#MythosLiterature"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#OtherAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Work"/>
    <rdfs:label>Cthulhu's Dark Spawn</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Full story online here Ron Shiflet United States English Publication date April 1998 " " is a short poem by American Ron Shiflet . Synopsis An abbreviated story of the overly-confident narrator who has summoned a being over which he finds, to his horror, he has no control. Publication History "" was first published in the April 1998 edition of Nightscapes , and appeared in The Ancient Track in October of the same year.</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasAuthorW rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#Ron_Shiflet"/>
    <ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>United States</ex:hasCountryOfOriginW>
    <ex:isInLanguageW>English</ex:isInLanguageW>
    <ex:wasPublishedOnW>April 1998</ex:wasPublishedOnW>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/lovecraft#Mark_McLaughlin">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#ExpandedMythosAuthors"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/lovecraft#RealWorldPerson"/>
    <rdfs:label>Mark McLaughlin</rdfs:label>
    <ex:hasAnnotation>Connection to the Mythos Author Horror Fantasy The 2002 Bram Stoker Award https://bmoviemonster.com/ is a writer of Mythos works both as a solo writer and frequently in collaboration with Michael Sheehan, Jr. The Outer God Nyarlathotep and feline entities as devices in McLaughlin’s Mythos stories are common, the latter likely due to his great love for cats. Overview Selected Bibliography Collections The Abominations of Nephren-Ka &amp; Three More Tales of the Cthulhu Mythos (2015) The Horror In The Water Tower &amp; Five More Tales Of The Cthulhu Mythos (2016) The Blasphemy in the Canopic Jar &amp; More Tales Of The Cthulhu Mythos (2016) Shoggoth Apocalypse &amp; More Tales Of The Cthulhu Mythos (2017) The Creature in the Waxworks: Tales of Weird &amp; Lovecraftian Horror (2017) The Relic In The Egyptian Gallery &amp; More Tales Of The Cthulhu Mythos (2017) The Testament Of Cthulhu: Tales Of Weird Fantasy &amp; Horror (2018) Stainless Steel Sarcophagus: Tales Of Lovecraftian Fantasy &amp; Horror (2018) Horrors &amp; Abominations: 24 Tales of the Cthulhu Mythos (2018) Nightmares &amp; Tentacles: 13 Tales Of The Cthulhu Mythos The House of the Ocelot &amp; More Lovecraftian Nightmares (2019) City Of Living Shadows &amp; More Lovecraftian Tales (2019) The Prisoner of Carcosa &amp; More Tales Of The Bizarre (2020) Short Fiction " Toadface " (2004) " The House of the Ocelot " (2019) " The Ambassador from Ulthar " (2019, with Michael Sheehan, Jr. ) " The Prisoner of Carcosa " " Horrors of the Trash Island " " Yuletide Mementos "</ex:hasAnnotation>
    <ex:hasWebsiteR>https://bmoviemonster.com/</ex:hasWebsiteR>
    <ex:isConnectedToMythosR>Author</ex:isConnectedToMythosR>
  </rdf:Description>
